/*
 * AQA A-Level Economics (7136) — Exam Practice Question Bank
 * Shape: { topicId: { name, questions: [{ q, marks, command, markScheme, diagram? }] } }
 * 40 questions per topic; 14 topics; ids match economics-aqa.js and topics-config.js.
 *
 * WHY THESE TARIFFS. Every question here uses a tariff that actually appears on a
 * 7136 paper — no invented mark values. Established from the June 2022 and June 2023
 * question papers and mark schemes on AQA's filestore; the same papers are indexed
 * in this repo's papers-config.js.
 *
 *   Papers 1 (Markets and Market Failure) and 2 (National and International
 *   Economy), 80 marks in 2 hours, are the same shape:
 *     Section A — one of two data-response contexts:
 *        2 marks  calculation from the data, to a stated number of decimal places
 *        4 marks  "Explain how the data in Extract A (Figure 1) show that…"
 *        9 marks  "…With the help of a diagram, explain/analyse…"
 *       25 marks  "…Using the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate…"
 *     Section B — one of three essays, each in two parts:
 *       15 marks  "Explain the determinants of…" / "Explain the main causes of…"
 *       25 marks  "Assess / Evaluate / Discuss the view that…"
 *   Paper 3 (Economic Principles and Issues), 80 marks in 2 hours:
 *     Section A — 30 one-mark multiple choice (those live in economics-aqa.js)
 *     Section B — an investigation on a set of extracts:
 *       10 marks  "To what extent, if at all, do the data suggest…?"
 *       15 marks  "Explain possible reasons for…"
 *       25 marks  "…would you recommend that…? Justify your recommendation."
 *
 * MIX PER TOPIC: 6 x 2, 6 x 4, 10 x 9, 2 x 10, 6 x 15, 10 x 25, in that order.
 *
 * MARK SCHEMES. AQA marks the 9, 10, 15 and 25-mark questions by LEVELS OF
 * RESPONSE, not by ticking points, so each markScheme gives indicative content plus
 * the ladder an examiner applies. Indicative content is NOT a checklist — a response
 * that makes other valid points well still reaches the top band.
 *
 *    4-mark: point-marked 4/3/2/1 on how fully the data is used AND explained.
 *    9-mark: L3 (7-9) well organised, develops the key issues, sound knowledge,
 *            good application and analysis; L2 (4-6) relevant issues, reasonable
 *            knowledge, some analysis; L1 (1-3) brief, limited, little analysis.
 *   10-mark: L3 (8-10) / L2 (4-7) / L1 (1-3).
 *   15-mark: L3 (11-15) good response developing a selection of the key issues;
 *            L2 (6-10) focused on relevant issues, satisfactory knowledge;
 *            L1 (1-5) one or more relevant issues identified, limited knowledge.
 *   25-mark: L5 (21-25) sound focused analysis and well-supported evaluation;
 *            L4 (16-20) sound focused analysis, some supported evaluation;
 *            L3 (11-15) some reasonable analysis, generally unsupported evaluation;
 *            L2 (6-10) fairly weak, limited understanding; L1 (1-5) very weak.
 *
 * Extracts and data tables labelled "written for this practice question" are
 * practice material with internally consistent figures, not claims about the world.
 * Dated statistics outside those extracts are real and carry their date.
 * The student self-marks against the mark scheme: Full / Partial / Missed.
 */

const ECONOMICS_AQA_PRACTICE = {
'3.1.1': {
    name: 'Economic Problem & Methodology',
    questions: [
      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nCountry X: two production possibilities for capital goods and consumer goods, 2025\n  Combination P:  capital goods = 40 ; consumer goods = 100\n  Combination Q:  capital goods = 50 ; consumer goods = 82\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the opportunity cost of producing one extra unit of capital goods as Country X moves from combination P to combination Q.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: consumer goods forgone divided by extra capital goods = (100 - 82) / (50 - 40) = 18 / 10.\nAnswer: 1.80 units of consumer goods per extra unit of capital goods.\n2 marks for 1.80; 1 mark for the correct method (subtract, then divide) spoiled by an arithmetic slip. No marks for an answer with no working and no correct figure.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nCountry Y: government spending on healthcare\n  2024 = £182bn\n  2025 = £194bn\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the percentage increase in Country Y's government healthcare spending between 2024 and 2025.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: (194 - 182) / 182 × 100 = 12 / 182 × 100.\nAnswer: 6.59% (6.5934...% rounded to two decimal places).\n2 marks for 6.59%; 1 mark for the correct method, including an unevaluated 12/182 × 100, or for 6.6% / 6.593% (right method, wrong rounding).` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nMaximum annual output if each country used all its resources on one good\n  Country A:  wheat = 60 tonnes   OR  cars = 30\n  Country B:  wheat = 20 tonnes   OR  cars = 20\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the opportunity cost to Country A of producing one car, measured in tonnes of wheat.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: maximum wheat output divided by maximum car output = 60 / 30.\nAnswer: 2.00 tonnes of wheat per car.\n2 marks for 2.00; 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for the inverted ratio 0.50 (the opportunity cost of wheat, not of cars).\nFor reference in later parts: Country B gives up 20/20 = 1.00 tonne of wheat per car, so Country B has the comparative advantage in cars.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nCountry Z, manufacturing sector, 2025\n  Total output = 4 860 000 units\n  Workers employed = 3 200\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, output per worker in Country Z's manufacturing sector in 2025.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: total output / number of workers = 4 860 000 / 3 200.\nAnswer: 1 518.75 units per worker.\n2 marks for 1 518.75; 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip. Accept the answer written without the thousands space.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nCountry W: land use, 2025\n  Total land area = 24.5 million hectares\n  Land in agricultural use = 17.2 million hectares\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the percentage of Country W's total land area that is in agricultural use.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: 17.2 / 24.5 × 100.\nAnswer: 70.20% (70.2040...% to two decimal places).\n2 marks for 70.20%; 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic or rounding slip (e.g. 70.2% or 70.204%).` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nCountry V: estimated potential (full-employment) output\n  2020 = £620bn\n  2025 = £646bn\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the percentage increase in Country V's potential output between 2020 and 2025.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: (646 - 620) / 620 × 100 = 26 / 620 × 100.\nAnswer: 4.19% (4.1935...% to two decimal places).\n2 marks for 4.19%; 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic or rounding slip.\nContext note: a rise in potential output of this kind is what an outward shift of the production possibility frontier represents.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nCountry X: production possibilities, 2025\n  Combination P:  capital goods = 20 ; consumer goods = 120\n  Combination Q:  capital goods = 30 ; consumer goods = 112\n  Combination R:  capital goods = 40 ; consumer goods = 100\n  Combination S:  capital goods = 50 ; consumer goods = 82\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that Country X faces an increasing opportunity cost of producing capital goods.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Each successive 10 extra units of capital goods costs more consumer goods: 8 units (P to Q), then 12 (Q to R), then 18 (R to S).\n• Per unit of capital goods the opportunity cost therefore rises from 0.8 to 1.2 to 1.8 consumer goods — a rising, not constant, ratio.\n• Explanation: factors of production are not homogeneous and are not perfectly substitutable between uses. The resources best suited to capital-goods production are transferred first; each later transfer moves resources that were comparatively more productive in consumer goods.\n• Consequence: the production possibility frontier is concave to the origin, its slope steepening as capital-goods output rises.\nLevels: 4 = correct figures quoted AND the rising ratio explained using imperfect substitutability of factors; 3 = figures used with some explanation; 2 = figures identified, explanation thin; 1 = one relevant figure identified only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nMaximum annual output if each country used all its resources on one good\n  Country A:  wheat = 60 tonnes   OR  cars = 30\n  Country B:  wheat = 20 tonnes   OR  cars = 20\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that, although Country A has an absolute advantage in both goods, Country B has a comparative advantage in cars.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Absolute advantage: Country A can produce more of both goods from the same resources (60 > 20 tonnes of wheat; 30 > 20 cars).\n• Opportunity cost of one car: Country A gives up 60/30 = 2 tonnes of wheat; Country B gives up 20/20 = 1 tonne.\n• Because Country B's opportunity cost per car is lower, Country B has the comparative advantage in cars; symmetrically Country A gives up 0.5 cars per tonne of wheat against Country B's 1 car, so Country A has the comparative advantage in wheat.\n• Explanation of the distinction: absolute advantage compares output levels, comparative advantage compares opportunity costs — which is why a country that is worse at everything can still gain from trade.\nLevels: 4 = both opportunity costs calculated correctly AND the absolute/comparative distinction explained; 3 = correct calculation with partial explanation; 2 = absolute advantage identified only, or one opportunity cost calculated; 1 = a relevant figure quoted with no explanation.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nCountry Z, 2025\n  Estimated potential (full-employment) output = £646bn\n  Actual output = £598bn\n  Unemployment rate = 7.4%\n  Manufacturing capacity utilisation = 71%\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that Country Z is producing at a point inside its production possibility frontier.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Actual output (£598bn) is £48bn, or about 7.4%, below potential output (£646bn), so output could rise without any new resources.\n• Unemployment of 7.4% means labour — a factor of production — is available but unused; capacity utilisation of 71% means nearly three-tenths of the capital stock is idle.\n• Explanation: a point on the frontier requires all factors to be fully and efficiently employed. Idle labour and idle capital place the economy at an interior point.\n• Implication: moving to the frontier involves no opportunity cost in the usual sense — more of both goods can be produced by putting unemployed resources to work.\nLevels: 4 = at least two indicators used precisely AND the interior-point idea fully explained; 3 = data used with some explanation; 2 = data identified, explanation thin; 1 = one relevant figure identified only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nCountry W, 2025\n  General government spending = 41% of GDP\n  Share of firms in private ownership = 78%\n  Healthcare: provided by the state, free at the point of use\n  Prices of most goods and services: determined by supply and demand\n  Minimum wage, competition law and environmental regulation: in force\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that Country W is best described as a mixed economy.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Market features: 78% of firms privately owned and most prices set by supply and demand, so the price mechanism — signalling, incentive and rationing — allocates the majority of resources.\n• State features: government spending equal to 41% of GDP, state provision of healthcare free at the point of use, and regulation of wages, competition and the environment.\n• Explanation: a pure free market would have no state provision and no regulation; a pure command economy would have state ownership of the means of production and administered prices. Country W has both public and private sectors, which is the definition of a mixed economy.\n• Development: the state's role here is typically justified by market failure (public goods, externalities, inequity), not by a rejection of markets.\nLevels: 4 = market AND state evidence quoted from the data AND the mixed-economy conclusion explained; 3 = both sides identified with some explanation; 2 = one side of the data used; 1 = a relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA furniture workshop before and after reorganising production into specialised tasks\n  Before:  12 workers ; output = 240 chairs per day\n  After:   12 workers ; output = 420 chairs per day\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that the division of labour has raised labour productivity in this workshop.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Output per worker rises from 240/12 = 20 chairs to 420/12 = 35 chairs per day, an increase of 15 chairs or 75%.\n• The labour force is unchanged at 12 workers, so the rise cannot be explained by employing more labour — output per unit of the factor has risen, which is the definition of a rise in labour productivity.\n• Explanation of the mechanism: splitting production into specialised tasks raises dexterity through repetition, removes time lost moving between tasks, and allows workers to be matched to the task they do best; it also makes task-specific capital worth buying.\n• Link: higher productivity from the same resources is one of the causes of an outward shift of the economy's production possibility frontier.\nLevels: 4 = productivity figures calculated correctly AND the division-of-labour mechanism explained; 3 = figures used with some explanation; 2 = the rise in output identified without the per-worker calculation; 1 = a relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: `Extract B — written for this practice question\nThree statements made in a newspaper article about Country X:\n  Statement 1: "Country X's government spent £182bn on healthcare in 2024 and £194bn in 2025."\n  Statement 2: "The government ought to spend more on healthcare and less on defence."\n  Statement 3: "When the price of cigarettes rose by 10%, the quantity demanded fell by 4%."\n\nExplain how it can be determined that Statement 2 is a normative statement while Statements 1 and 3 are positive statements.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Statements 1 and 3 are positive: they are factual claims about what is, and can in principle be tested against data — spending figures can be checked in the public accounts, and the price and quantity change can be checked against market data.\n• Statement 2 is normative: "ought to" expresses a value judgement about what should be. No amount of data can prove or disprove it, because it depends on how the speaker ranks health against defence.\n• Explanation of the test: a statement is positive if evidence could in principle show it to be false; it is normative if disagreement rests on values rather than on evidence.\n• Development: positive statements may still be wrong (a positive statement is testable, not necessarily true), and normative statements usually rest on positive premises — which is why the distinction matters in policy debate rather than settling it.\nLevels: 4 = correct classification of all three AND the testability/value-judgement criterion explained; 3 = correct classification with some explanation; 2 = correct classification with thin explanation; 1 = partial classification only.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 3-4) states that "every extra operation the health service performs costs the economy more than the last one did". With the help of a production possibility frontier diagram, explain why the opportunity cost of producing more healthcare rises as healthcare output expands.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: PPF with healthcare on the horizontal axis and all other goods and services on the vertical, drawn concave to the origin. Mark equal increments along the horizontal axis and show the progressively larger vertical falls.\n• Chain of reasoning: moving along the frontier requires resources to be transferred out of other production. The first resources moved are those best suited to healthcare and least productive elsewhere, so little other output is lost.\n• As healthcare output expands, the resources still available are progressively less suited to healthcare and more productive elsewhere, so each equal increase in healthcare costs more other output. The slope of the frontier — the marginal rate of transformation — steepens.\n• Underlying assumption: factors of production are not homogeneous and not perfectly substitutable between uses. If they were, the PPF would be a straight line and opportunity cost would be constant.\n• Application: an extra operation requires theatre space, consultant time and capital equipment that could have produced something else; specialist surgeons in particular are a very poor substitute for workers in other sectors, which is exactly why the cost of the marginal operation rises.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate concave PPF, clearly labelled, with a developed chain of reasoning linking factor heterogeneity to the steepening slope; L2 (4-6) reasonable knowledge and some analysis, diagram present but partially flawed or under-used; L1 (1-3) limited, largely descriptive, diagram missing or incorrect.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 8-9) states that "net inward migration of working-age people added 0.6% to the country\'s labour force last year". With the help of a production possibility frontier diagram, analyse the likely effect of this migration on the economy\'s production possibilities.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: PPF for two goods, showing an outward (rightward) shift of the whole frontier from PPF1 to PPF2.\n• Chain of reasoning: labour is a factor of production. A larger working-age population increases the quantity of one factor available, so the maximum output of both goods rises — the frontier shifts outwards, which is a rise in productive potential.\n• Development: the shift need not be parallel. If migrants are concentrated in particular occupations — construction, health and social care, agriculture — the frontier shifts further out along the axis of the good those sectors produce, pivoting rather than shifting evenly.\n• Further analysis: migration also raises aggregate demand for goods and for housing, so the economy may move closer to the frontier as well as outward; and if the capital stock does not grow in step, capital per worker falls and output per worker may not rise even though total output does.\n• Distinction that earns L3: this is a shift of the frontier (more resources), not a movement along it (reallocation) or a movement towards it (better use of existing resources).\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram showing an outward shift, developed analysis distinguishing shift from movement, and a qualifying point about composition or capital dilution; L2 (4-6) diagram and some analysis; L1 (1-3) limited or descriptive, or confuses a shift with a movement along the frontier.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 12-13) states that "unemployment in the region fell from 9.1% to 5.4% over two years". With the help of a production possibility frontier diagram, explain how this change affects the economy\'s position relative to its frontier.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: PPF with a point A inside the frontier and a point B on the frontier, with the arrow from A towards B. The frontier itself does not move.\n• Chain of reasoning: unemployment means a factor of production is available but idle, so the economy produces less than it could — an interior point. Re-employing that labour raises output of both goods simultaneously.\n• Key insight: the move from A to B involves no opportunity cost in terms of the other good, because the extra output comes from previously unused resources rather than from a transfer. This is what makes reducing unemployment economically attractive as well as socially desirable.\n• Contrast for L3: once the economy reaches B, any further increase in one good must come from a movement along the frontier (and so has an opportunity cost) or from an outward shift (more or better resources).\n• Qualification: a fall in the measured unemployment rate can also reflect people leaving the labour force altogether, in which case the labour supply has fallen and productive potential has not been restored; the measured rate alone does not prove the economy has reached the frontier.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram with interior and frontier points, developed explanation of the zero-opportunity-cost move, and a qualification; L2 (4-6) diagram and some explanation; L1 (1-3) limited, or wrongly shifts the frontier outwards.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 18-19) states that "the government plans to raise public investment while holding current spending flat". With the help of a production possibility frontier diagram, analyse how a decision to devote more resources to capital goods today may affect the economy\'s production possibilities in the future.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: PPF with capital goods on one axis and consumer goods on the other. Show a movement along today's frontier from a low-capital point A to a higher-capital point B, then a second frontier PPF2 lying outside PPF1 to represent the future.\n• Chain of reasoning: choosing B means giving up consumer goods today — that is the opportunity cost. Capital goods are not consumed; they are inputs. A larger capital stock raises future productive capacity, shifting the frontier outwards in later periods.\n• Development: this is the trade-off between present and future consumption. The country that invests more sacrifices current living standards to raise the ceiling on future ones; the country at A enjoys more now but its frontier shifts out more slowly.\n• Qualification: the gain depends on the productivity of the investment. Capital spending on projects with low returns, or capital that depreciates quickly, shifts the frontier little. Investment must also exceed depreciation for the net capital stock to grow at all.\n• Application: this is the logic behind government capital budgets being treated separately from current budgets, and behind the argument that borrowing to invest differs from borrowing to consume.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) two-period diagram used accurately, with a developed chain linking current sacrifice to a future outward shift plus a qualification about investment quality or depreciation; L2 (4-6) diagram and some analysis; L1 (1-3) limited or descriptive.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 21-22) states that "a breakthrough in automated harvesting has transformed farm yields, leaving manufacturing unaffected". With the help of a production possibility frontier diagram, explain the effect of a technological improvement in one industry only.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: PPF with agricultural output on the horizontal axis and manufactured output on the vertical. The frontier pivots outwards along the agriculture axis only: the maximum agricultural output rises while the vertical intercept (maximum manufacturing output) is unchanged.\n• Chain of reasoning: technology raises the output obtainable from a given quantity of resources in farming alone, so every combination that includes some agriculture becomes more productive, but a point of complete specialisation in manufacturing is unaffected.\n• Consequence for opportunity cost: the slope of the frontier changes, so the opportunity cost of manufactured goods in terms of agricultural goods rises — the economy now sacrifices more potential food to produce the same manufactures.\n• Development: the economy can now consume more of both goods if it chooses a point on the new frontier between the old point and the agriculture axis, even though only one industry improved.\n• Further analysis: over time resources are likely to be reallocated out of farming as fewer workers are needed there, which raises structural unemployment in the short run — a movement inside the frontier — even while potential output has risen.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) correctly pivoted frontier with the unchanged intercept explained, plus developed analysis of the change in opportunity cost or the reallocation of resources; L2 (4-6) diagram with some explanation, may shift the whole frontier instead of pivoting; L1 (1-3) limited or inaccurate.` },

      { q: 'With the help of a production possibility frontier diagram, explain how the concepts of scarcity, choice and opportunity cost are illustrated by the frontier.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: a concave PPF with an unattainable point beyond the frontier, an inefficient point inside it, and two attainable efficient points on it.\n• Scarcity: the frontier itself is the boundary of what finite resources and current technology allow. Points beyond it are unattainable — that is scarcity made visible, and it exists however rich the economy is.\n• Choice: every point on the frontier is a different combination, and society must select one. The model says nothing about which is best; that requires a normative judgement about the value of the two goods.\n• Opportunity cost: moving between efficient points requires giving up some of one good to gain the other. The slope of the frontier measures this cost, and its concavity shows the cost rising at the margin.\n• Developed point: the interior point adds a fourth idea — inefficiency. There the economy is not on the boundary at all, so more of both goods is available without any sacrifice, which is why unemployment and idle capital are treated as waste rather than as a choice.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate fully-labelled diagram with all three concepts explained and tied explicitly to features of the diagram; L2 (4-6) diagram and two concepts explained, or three concepts asserted with thin linkage; L1 (1-3) limited, descriptive, diagram absent or wrong.` },

      { q: 'With the help of a production possibility frontier diagram, analyse how an extension of the division of labour across an economy can raise its productive potential.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: PPF shifting outwards from PPF1 to PPF2, with the same quantity of resources.\n• Chain of reasoning: dividing production into specialised tasks raises output per worker through practice and dexterity, by removing time lost switching tasks, by matching workers to the task in which their opportunity cost is lowest, and by making specialised capital worth installing. More output from the same resources means the frontier moves out.\n• Development: specialisation also supports trade. Because specialised workers produce far more than they consume of one good, exchange becomes essential, which is why the division of labour and the growth of markets advance together.\n• Analysis of limits: Adam Smith's own qualification is that the division of labour is limited by the extent of the market — in a small market specialised output cannot be sold. So the outward shift depends on market size and on transport and transaction costs falling.\n• Costs: repetitive work can cause boredom, higher labour turnover and falling quality, and specialised workers are occupationally immobile when demand shifts, so some of the theoretical gain is not realised.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate outward-shift diagram with a developed chain from specialisation to productivity to potential output, plus a limit or cost; L2 (4-6) diagram with some analysis; L1 (1-3) listed advantages with little analysis.` },

      { q: 'With the help of production possibility frontier diagrams, explain how specialisation and trade based on comparative advantage can allow two countries to consume at points beyond their own production possibility frontiers.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: two linear PPFs, one per country, with different slopes to represent different opportunity costs, plus a consumption point for each country lying beyond its own frontier once trade takes place.\n• Numerical anchor (may use the Table 1 data): Country A gives up 2 tonnes of wheat per car, Country B gives up 1 tonne. Country B has the comparative advantage in cars, Country A in wheat.\n• Chain of reasoning: if each country specialises where its opportunity cost is lower, world output of both goods rises. Trading at any rate between the two domestic opportunity cost ratios — here between 1 and 2 tonnes of wheat per car — leaves both countries able to consume combinations outside their own production frontiers.\n• Key point: the consumption possibility frontier lies outside the production possibility frontier once trade is possible. Production still occurs on the frontier; it is consumption that escapes it.\n• Qualification: the gains depend on the terms of trade lying strictly between the two opportunity cost ratios, on transport costs and tariffs being small enough not to swallow the gain, and on the assumption of constant opportunity costs implied by linear frontiers.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagrams with different slopes, correct comparative advantage identified, and the consumption-beyond-the-frontier result explained with a terms-of-trade condition; L2 (4-6) some accurate work, terms of trade omitted or asserted; L1 (1-3) confuses absolute with comparative advantage, or omits diagrams.` },

      { q: 'Analyse why economists frequently disagree about economic policy even when they agree about the data.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `• Value judgements: policy advice is normative. Two economists may agree that a carbon tax reduces emissions by a given amount and still disagree about whether the cost to low-income households is acceptable, because they weight efficiency and equity differently.\n• Different models: the same data are interpreted through different theoretical frameworks. Whether a minimum wage costs jobs depends on whether the labour market is modelled as competitive or as monopsonistic, and both models are internally consistent.\n• Empirical uncertainty about magnitudes: economists often agree about the direction of an effect and disagree about its size. If the elasticity estimates differ, the policy conclusion differs even though the theory does not.\n• Causation versus correlation: non-experimental data rarely identify causation cleanly. Reverse causality, omitted variables and time lags leave room for honest disagreement about what the data show.\n• Time horizons: a policy that is costly in the short run and beneficial in the long run will be assessed differently by economists who apply different discount rates to future gains.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) at least three reasons developed, with a clear distinction between disagreement about values and disagreement about models or magnitudes; L2 (4-6) two or three reasons with some development; L1 (1-3) a list with little analysis.` },

      { q: 'Analyse how the price mechanism in a free market economy answers the three fundamental questions of what to produce, how to produce it and for whom to produce.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `• What to produce: prices signal consumer preferences. Rising demand for a good raises its price, raising profitability; the signalling and incentive functions of price draw resources into that use, so consumer sovereignty determines the pattern of output.\n• How to produce: firms face factor prices and compete on cost. A rise in the relative price of labour makes capital-intensive methods cheaper, so firms substitute towards them. Competition forces productively efficient methods because high-cost producers are driven out.\n• For whom to produce: output goes to those with both the willingness and the ability to pay. Incomes are determined in factor markets by the marginal revenue product of the factors owned, so the distribution of output follows the distribution of factor endowments.\n• Analysis of the mechanism: price performs three functions at once — signalling, incentive and rationing — with no central direction. Hayek's argument is that this economises on information, because no planner needs to know why a price moved for the response to be correct.\n• Limits that a strong answer raises: the mechanism answers "for whom" on ability to pay, not on need, and fails where there are externalities, public goods or significant market power, which is why every real economy is mixed.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) all three questions answered with developed chains of reasoning and explicit use of the signalling, incentive and rationing functions; L2 (4-6) two or three questions covered with some analysis; L1 (1-3) descriptive account of markets.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) and Extract B — written for this practice question\nCountry X, selected indicators\n                              2020      2025\n  Potential output           £620bn    £646bn\n  Actual output              £601bn    £598bn\n  Net capital stock          £1 480bn  £1 613bn\n  Labour force               28.1m     28.7m\n  Unemployment rate          4.1%      7.4%\n\nExtract B states: "Investment has been strong, but a downturn in export markets has left factories idle."\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data suggest that Country X's production possibility frontier shifted outwards between 2020 and 2025?`,
        marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `• Evidence for an outward shift: potential output rose from £620bn to £646bn, an increase of 26/620 × 100 = 4.19%. The net capital stock rose by 133/1480 × 100 = 8.99% and the labour force by 0.6m, or 2.14%. More of two factors of production is exactly what shifts a frontier outwards.\n• Consistency check: the two resource increases are independent evidence supporting the potential-output estimate, which strengthens the conclusion beyond a single indicator.\n• Evidence that must not be misread: actual output fell from £601bn to £598bn and unemployment rose from 4.1% to 7.4%. That is a movement further inside the frontier, not an inward shift of it. Extract B's reference to idle factories confirms that resources exist but are unused.\n• Limitations of the data: potential output is an estimate, not an observation, and is revised; the capital stock figure is in money terms and may not be adjusted for depreciation or prices; no data are given on productivity, technology or the quality of the migrant or new labour, which also determine potential output.\n• Judgement: the data support an outward shift of the frontier over the period, on two independent measures of resources plus the potential-output estimate, while showing that the economy moved further away from that frontier. The extent is limited by the estimated nature of potential output and the absence of productivity data.\nLevels: L3 (8-10) data used precisely with calculation, the shift/movement distinction handled correctly, limitations of the data discussed and a supported judgement reached; L2 (4-7) relevant data used with some evaluation, or the distinction blurred; L1 (1-3) data described with no judgement.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — written for this practice question\nSix regions of Country X, 2025\n  Region    Education spending per head    Output per hour worked\n    1              £1 120                        £31.40\n    2              £1 260                        £33.10\n    3              £1 340                        £36.80\n    4              £1 410                        £35.90\n    5              £1 580                        £40.20\n    6              £1 720                        £43.60\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data suggest that higher education spending per head causes higher labour productivity?`,
        marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `• The association: as education spending per head rises across the six regions, output per hour generally rises too, from £31.40 in Region 1 to £43.60 in Region 6 — a rise of about 39% for a rise in spending of about 54%. The relationship is positive and fairly strong.\n• The exception matters: Region 4 spends more per head than Region 3 (£1 410 against £1 340) but has lower productivity (£35.90 against £36.80), so the relationship is not monotonic and something else is clearly at work.\n• Correlation is not causation: the causation may run the other way. High-productivity regions generate higher tax revenue and higher incomes, which can fund more education spending, so productivity may be causing the spending.\n• Omitted variables: capital per worker, industrial structure, infrastructure, migration of graduates between regions and the pre-existing skill level are all unobserved here and plausibly drive both variables.\n• Time lags: education spending in 2025 cannot affect the 2025 workforce. Any causal effect operates over decades, so same-year data cannot identify it.\n• Sample and measurement: six observations is a very small sample; spending per head says nothing about how well the money is spent; no confidence intervals or statistical tests are given.\n• Judgement: the data are consistent with a causal link but fall well short of demonstrating one. They suggest at most that the two move together, and the direction of causation cannot be settled without longitudinal data or a natural experiment.\nLevels: L3 (8-10) data used precisely including the exception, correlation/causation handled explicitly with at least two distinct challenges, and a supported judgement; L2 (4-7) relationship described with some evaluation; L1 (1-3) description of the data only.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main determinants of an economy\'s productive potential.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Quantity of factors: the size and participation rate of the labour force (affected by demography, migration, retirement age and childcare availability), the stock of capital net of depreciation, the availability of land and natural resources, and the supply of enterprise.\n• Quality of factors: human capital built by education and training; the vintage and technological sophistication of the capital stock. A given number of workers and machines can support very different output levels.\n• Technology and innovation: the state of technical knowledge determines the maximum output obtainable from any bundle of inputs. Research and development spending and the diffusion of existing best practice both matter, and diffusion is often the larger effect.\n• Efficiency of resource allocation: institutions that move resources to their highest-value use — competitive product markets, flexible labour markets, well-functioning capital markets, secure property rights and the rule of law — raise potential output without adding a single resource.\n• Specialisation and trade: the extent of the market determines how far the division of labour can be pushed, and openness to trade allows a country to specialise according to comparative advantage.\n• Infrastructure and the public capital stock: transport, energy networks and digital connectivity raise the productivity of private capital and labour.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several determinants explained with developed chains of reasoning and appropriate use of the PPF framework, clear and well organised; L2 (6-10) some determinants explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list of factors with little or no explanation.` },

      { q: 'With the help of a diagram, explain the main causes of an outward shift in a country\'s production possibility frontier.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: PPF shifting outwards from PPF1 to PPF2; a second diagram or the same one used to show a pivot where only one industry improves.\n• Investment in physical capital: net investment above depreciation raises the capital stock, so more output is obtainable from the same labour force. The opportunity cost is consumption forgone today, shown as a movement along today's frontier towards the capital-goods axis.\n• Growth in the labour supply: natural population growth, net inward migration, higher participation rates or a later retirement age all add to the quantity of labour.\n• Improvements in human capital: education, vocational training and learning by doing raise output per worker, shifting the frontier out without any change in the number of workers.\n• Technological progress: new production methods raise output per unit of input. If the advance is confined to one industry the frontier pivots rather than shifting evenly, changing relative opportunity costs.\n• Discovery of natural resources and better use of land, and institutional reform that improves allocative efficiency — competition policy, trade liberalisation, secure property rights.\n• Important distinction: all of these shift the frontier. Reducing unemployment or raising capacity utilisation moves the economy towards an existing frontier and is not a cause of a shift.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) accurate diagram integrated into the explanation, several causes developed with chains of reasoning, and the shift/movement distinction made explicitly; L2 (6-10) diagram present, some causes explained; L1 (1-5) list of causes, diagram absent or inaccurate.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main determinants of the gains a country makes from specialisation and exchange.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• The difference in opportunity cost ratios: the gain from trade exists only where opportunity costs differ. The wider the divergence between the two countries' ratios, the larger the potential gain; identical ratios mean no comparative advantage and no gain.\n• The terms of trade: gains are shared according to where the trading ratio settles between the two domestic opportunity cost ratios. A ratio close to one country's own opportunity cost gives that country almost none of the gain, which is why the distribution of gains, not their existence, is what trade disputes are about.\n• Transport, tariff and transaction costs: these subtract from the gain and can eliminate it for low-value, high-bulk goods. Falling container-shipping and communication costs are a large part of why trade has grown.\n• Economies of scale: specialisation enlarges the market for each producer, allowing long production runs and lower long-run average costs, so the gain can exceed the static comparative-advantage calculation.\n• The extent of the market and factor mobility: Smith's limit still applies. Gains are realised only if resources can actually be moved into the export sector; occupational and geographical immobility leave structural unemployment and reduce the net gain.\n• Dynamic effects: exposure to foreign competition raises productivity and encourages innovation, while over-specialisation leaves a country exposed to a collapse in demand or price for its single export.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several determinants explained with developed reasoning, correct use of opportunity cost ratios and the terms of trade; L2 (6-10) some determinants explained, terms of trade thin; L1 (1-5) assertion that trade is beneficial with little explanation.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main reasons why the basic economic problem exists in every type of economic system.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• The core proposition: resources are finite while human wants are unlimited, so not every want can be satisfied. This is a statement about the relationship between wants and resources, not about poverty, and it does not disappear as a country grows richer.\n• Why wealth does not solve it: wants expand with income. A richer economy faces choices over healthcare, environmental quality and leisure rather than over food, but the necessity of choosing is unchanged — which is why the problem persists in the richest economies.\n• Consequence 1 — choice: every economic system must answer what to produce, how to produce it and for whom. A market system answers through prices; a command system through administrative allocation; a mixed system through both. The mechanism differs; the questions do not.\n• Consequence 2 — opportunity cost: because resources used one way cannot be used another, every choice has a next-best alternative forgone. This applies equally to a planner's decisions and to a consumer's.\n• Consequence 3 — rationing: some mechanism must decide who gets what. Price rations by ability to pay; a command system rations by queue, quota or administrative priority. Neither abolishes scarcity; they distribute its burden differently.\n• The role of the margin: because of scarcity, rational decisions are taken at the margin — comparing the marginal benefit and marginal cost of a little more — rather than in all-or-nothing terms.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) the problem defined precisely and its three consequences explained with developed reasoning and examples from more than one system; L2 (6-10) definition plus some consequences explained; L1 (1-5) definition with little development.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main differences between positive and normative statements, and why the distinction matters when economists advise government.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Definition and test: a positive statement is a claim about what is, was or will be, and is in principle testable against evidence. A normative statement is a claim about what ought to be and rests on a value judgement, so evidence alone cannot settle it.\n• Positive statements need not be true: testability, not truth, is the criterion. "A minimum wage always reduces employment" is positive and, under monopsony, false.\n• Why the distinction matters in advice: an economist can establish the positive consequences of a policy — who gains, who loses, by how much — without being able to establish that the policy should be adopted. That final step requires a ranking of the gains and losses, which is a political judgement.\n• Hidden value judgements: apparently positive language can smuggle in values. Describing an outcome as "efficient" assumes efficiency is the objective; using GDP as the measure of success embeds a judgement about what counts.\n• Practical implication: government economists therefore present options with their costs and benefits rather than a single recommendation, and cost-benefit analysis makes its value judgements explicit — for example in the discount rate applied to future costs and the monetary value placed on a life or on carbon.\n• Limits of the distinction: positive economics is not value-free in practice, because the questions economists choose to study and the models they select already reflect judgements, so the distinction is a discipline on argument rather than a guarantee of objectivity.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) distinction explained precisely with examples, and its consequences for policy advice developed including the limits of the distinction; L2 (6-10) distinction explained with some application; L1 (1-5) definitions only.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main ways in which resources are allocated in a mixed economy.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• The price mechanism for most goods: consumer demand raises price, price signals scarcity and profitability, the incentive function draws resources in, and the rationing function clears the market. In a mixed economy this governs the bulk of output.\n• Direct state provision: goods with public-good characteristics (non-rival, non-excludable — defence, flood defences) will not be supplied adequately by markets because of the free-rider problem, so the state provides and funds them from taxation.\n• Provision of merit goods: education and healthcare are underconsumed relative to the socially optimal level because of information failure and positive externalities, so the state provides them free at the point of use or subsidises them.\n• Taxation, subsidy and regulation: rather than replacing markets, the state alters the prices and rules they operate under — indirect taxes on demerit goods such as the UK Soft Drinks Industry Levy introduced in April 2018, subsidies for goods with external benefits, minimum wages such as the UK National Living Wage of £12.71 an hour from April 2026, and competition law enforced by the Competition and Markets Authority.\n• Redistribution: because the market answers "for whom" on ability to pay, the state uses progressive taxation and transfer payments to alter the distribution that factor markets produce.\n• The balance between the two: the mix differs by country and over time, and the case for each state role rests on a specific market failure rather than on a general preference for planning.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several allocation mechanisms explained with developed reasoning and accurate, dated examples; L2 (6-10) some mechanisms explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) description of public and private sectors.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that a free market economy always allocates scarce resources more efficiently than a mixed economy.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `• Case for the market: the price mechanism's signalling, incentive and rationing functions coordinate millions of decisions with no central direction. Hayek's argument is informational — prices summarise dispersed knowledge no planner could assemble.\n• Efficiency claims: competition drives firms to minimum average cost (productive efficiency) and, where price equals marginal cost, output reflects consumers' valuation (allocative efficiency). The profit motive also rewards innovation, generating dynamic efficiency over time.\n• Evidence: the sustained output and productivity gap that opened between centrally planned and market economies in the second half of the twentieth century, and the productivity gains that followed liberalisation in telecoms and airlines, are the strongest empirical support.\n• Evaluation — the "always" is what fails: efficiency claims rest on assumptions that frequently do not hold. Externalities mean private and social costs diverge, so the market equilibrium is not the welfare-maximising one; public goods are undersupplied because of non-excludability; information failure and monopoly power break the link between price and marginal cost.\n• Evaluation — equity and efficiency are distinct: the market answers "for whom" by ability to pay. An allocation can be Pareto efficient and still leave people without healthcare. Judging a system requires a normative choice the efficiency criterion cannot make.\n• Evaluation — the counterfactual: the real comparison is not market versus plan but different mixes. Government intervention can also fail, through information gaps, unintended consequences, administrative cost and regulatory capture, so the case for intervention is not automatic either.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that markets are usually the more efficient allocator where the competitive assumptions approximately hold, that "always" is indefensible because market failure is pervasive, and that the real question is which mix, decided market by market on the size of the market failure weighed against the risk of government failure.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, well-organised evaluation, both sides developed with a supported judgement reached at the end; L4 (16-20) good analysis with evaluation that is present but less consistently developed; L3 (11-15) sound knowledge and some analysis, evaluation limited or asserted; L2 (6-10) descriptive with isolated relevant points; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that the production possibility frontier is too simple a model to be of use to policymakers.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `• The simplifications: two goods, a fixed state of technology, a fixed quantity of resources, full information, and no money, prices, distribution or time. Real economies produce millions of goods and choices are made under uncertainty.\n• Case that it is still useful: the model isolates the ideas that matter most — scarcity as a boundary, choice as selection along it, opportunity cost as its slope, and growth as a shift. A model's value lies in what it omits; adding realism would obscure exactly these relationships.\n• Direct policy uses: the distinction between moving towards the frontier (demand-side policy to close an output gap) and moving the frontier (supply-side policy) is the single most important organising idea in macroeconomic policy, and it comes straight from this model.\n• Further uses: the capital-versus-consumer-goods version formalises the present-versus-future trade-off that underlies investment policy and public-sector capital budgets, and the "guns versus butter" version frames defence spending debates.\n• Evaluation — what it cannot do: the model says nothing about which point should be chosen. That is normative and requires a social welfare judgement. It is also silent on distribution, on how the economy reaches a chosen point, and on the institutions that determine whether it does.\n• Evaluation — misleading features: the smooth concave curve implies a precision about potential output that does not exist. Estimates of the output gap are revised heavily after the event, so policy based on a supposed distance from the frontier can be badly wrong in real time.\n• Evaluation — the alternative standard: models should be judged against other models, not against reality. There is no richer model of the same relationships that is equally teachable, and richer growth models are used alongside rather than instead of it.\n• Judgement: a supportable conclusion is that its simplicity is the source of its usefulness for framing choices, but that it is a conceptual device rather than a quantitative tool, and policymakers err when they treat measured "potential output" with the precision the diagram suggests.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained evaluation of the model's assumptions and uses with a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) sound knowledge, limited evaluation; L2 (6-10) descriptive account of the PPF; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 20-21) states that "Country X saves and invests a smaller share of its output than any comparable economy, and its growth has suffered as a result".\n\nUsing the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that Country X should shift a substantially larger share of its resources into capital goods.`,
        marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `• The mechanism in favour: capital goods are inputs, not consumption. Net investment above depreciation raises the capital stock, raises capital per worker and shifts the production possibility frontier outwards, raising the ceiling on future consumption. Use a two-period PPF diagram.\n• Supporting reasoning from the extracts: a low investment share is consistent with slow growth in potential output; the data on the capital stock and potential output can be used to show the link quantitatively.\n• Compounding: even a small sustained difference in the growth rate of potential output produces a large difference in living standards over a generation, which is the strongest argument for accepting the present sacrifice.\n• Evaluation — the opportunity cost is real: the resources come from current consumption, which falls now. For households already on low incomes that cost is immediate and certain while the benefit is delayed and uncertain, so the case depends on the discount rate applied to future consumption.\n• Evaluation — quality of investment matters more than quantity: capital spending on low-return projects, or in the presence of weak institutions and poor project appraisal, raises the capital stock without raising output much. Diminishing returns to capital mean the marginal return falls as the capital stock grows.\n• Evaluation — complements: capital raises productivity only alongside skills, infrastructure and effective management. Without human capital and absorptive capacity the new machinery is underused, so an investment-only strategy may fail.\n• Evaluation — how the shift happens: the extract implies a policy decision, but in a mixed economy investment is largely a private decision responding to expected returns, interest rates and confidence. Government can alter incentives through capital allowances, public investment and stable policy, but cannot simply reallocate resources.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that raising the investment share is likely to raise long-run living standards if the return on the marginal project exceeds its opportunity cost, but that "substantially larger" needs a limit — set by the quality of available projects and the tolerable fall in current consumption — rather than being a target in itself.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) extracts used throughout, sustained two-sided evaluation and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good use of extracts and developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) some use of extracts, evaluation asserted; L2 (6-10) largely descriptive, little extract use; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Discuss the view that specialisation and the division of labour always raise a country\'s standard of living.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `• The case in favour: specialisation raises output per worker through dexterity, time saved switching tasks, matching workers to their lowest-opportunity-cost task and the use of task-specific capital. More output from the same resources shifts the PPF outwards and raises real income per head.\n• At national level: specialisation according to comparative advantage raises world output of all goods and allows consumption beyond each country's own production frontier, provided the terms of trade lie between the domestic opportunity cost ratios.\n• Supporting mechanism: specialisation enlarges markets, which allows economies of scale and lowers long-run average cost, so consumers face lower prices as well as higher incomes.\n• Evaluation — costs to workers: repetitive tasks reduce job satisfaction, raise turnover and absenteeism and can lower quality, offsetting part of the productivity gain. Highly specialised workers are occupationally immobile, so a shift in demand produces structural unemployment rather than smooth reallocation.\n• Evaluation — risk from over-specialisation: a country dependent on one export is exposed to a collapse in its price or in world demand, and to exchange-rate shocks. Specialisation raises expected output but also its variance, and living standards depend on both.\n• Evaluation — interdependence and supply chains: specialisation across borders creates dependence on other countries for essentials, as shortages of semiconductors and of medical equipment illustrated. Resilience has a value the standard model omits.\n• Evaluation — distribution: average living standards can rise while particular groups lose. The gains from specialisation and trade accrue to consumers and to expanding sectors; the losses fall on specific regions and occupations, and "standard of living" measured as a national average conceals that.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that specialisation raises average living standards reliably in the long run, that the word "always" fails because of adjustment costs, distributional losses and vulnerability to shocks, and that whether any particular country gains depends on the terms of trade and on the policies used to redistribute the gains.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained two-sided discussion with a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis and developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) sound knowledge with limited evaluation; L2 (6-10) descriptive list of advantages and disadvantages; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that free trade based on comparative advantage benefits all trading countries.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `• The theory: where opportunity cost ratios differ, complete specialisation raises world output of both goods, and trade at a ratio between the two domestic opportunity costs leaves both countries consuming beyond their production possibility frontiers. Illustrate with linear PPFs and a numerical example.\n• Extension beyond the static model: trade enlarges markets, allowing economies of scale; exposure to foreign competition raises domestic productivity; and access to imported capital goods and technology raises potential output.\n• Evaluation — the terms of trade decide the distribution: if the trading ratio sits close to one country's own opportunity cost, that country captures almost none of the gain. Both may gain while one gains overwhelmingly, so "benefits all" can be true and still misleading.\n• Evaluation — assumptions that fail: the model assumes constant opportunity costs, perfect factor mobility within countries, no transport costs and full employment. With increasing opportunity costs specialisation is incomplete; with immobile factors the losers do not move into the expanding sector and structural unemployment persists.\n• Evaluation — winners and losers within a country: gains accrue to consumers and exporters, losses to import-competing industries and their regions. Unless the gains are redistributed, a country can gain in aggregate while large groups are permanently worse off — which is why free trade is politically contested even where the aggregate case is strong.\n• Evaluation — dynamic and development arguments: the infant industry argument holds that comparative advantage is not fixed and that a country specialising in low-value primary products according to current advantage may lock itself into slow growth and volatile export prices. Against that, protection risks entrenching inefficiency and inviting retaliation.\n• Evaluation — non-economic considerations: strategic dependence on imports of food, energy or essential technology carries a security cost outside the model.\n• Judgement: a supportable conclusion is that free trade raises world output and, on plausible terms of trade, raises aggregate real income in both countries, but that "benefits all" fails at the level of groups within countries and depends on assumptions — mobility, full employment and reasonable terms of trade — that frequently do not hold.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained evaluation with the model used accurately and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) theory explained, evaluation limited; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that the assumption of rational economic decision making makes economic models unreliable guides to policy.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `• The assumption: agents have stable, consistent preferences, full information and unlimited computational capacity, and choose the option that maximises utility or profit at the margin.\n• The behavioural critique: bounded rationality means limited information and limited processing capacity; heuristics such as anchoring and availability produce systematic, not random, errors; present bias and loss aversion generate choices inconsistent with the model. Kahneman (Nobel, 2002) and Thaler (Nobel, 2017) established that these departures are predictable.\n• Policy consequences of the critique: if choices are predictably biased, policies that rely on consumers responding to information alone will underperform. The success of automatic enrolment into workplace pensions is the clearest UK illustration — participation among eligible employees reached 90% in 2025, a level that voluntary opt-in never approached, because the default was changed rather than the incentive.\n• Assessment — the assumption is often good enough: models are judged by predictive accuracy, not descriptive realism. Demand curves slope downwards, firms do respond to costs, and the aggregate predictions of the rational model hold well in markets with repetition, feedback and significant stakes.\n• Assessment — errors may not aggregate: individual departures from rationality can offset one another, and arbitrage by well-informed participants can drive market outcomes towards the rational prediction even when many individuals are biased.\n• Assessment — the behavioural alternative is not complete: there is no single unified behavioural model to replace utility maximisation. A framework with many biases can rationalise any observed behaviour after the event, which makes it weaker as a predictive tool, and nudge effects are often small and sometimes fail to replicate.\n• Assessment — the honest position is domain-specific: the rational model performs worst for infrequent, complex, high-stakes decisions with delayed consequences — pensions, insurance, mortgages, health behaviour — and best for repeated, low-complexity market choices.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that the assumption does not make models unreliable in general, but that it makes them unreliable in identifiable domains; the appropriate response is to select the model to suit the decision, and to design policy using both price incentives and choice architecture.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained two-sided assessment with accurate behavioural evidence and a domain-specific judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) the critique described, assessment limited; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Discuss the view that economics cannot be regarded as a science because economists are rarely able to conduct controlled experiments.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `• The charge: the natural sciences isolate variables in controlled conditions. Economists usually observe non-experimental data in which everything changes at once, so competing explanations of the same events cannot be decisively separated.\n• Consequences: the ceteris paribus assumption is a modelling device rather than an experimental condition; correlation is hard to convert into causation; reverse causality, omitted variables and time lags leave persistent disagreement about magnitudes.\n• Discussion — the scientific method does not require laboratories: astronomy, geology, epidemiology and evolutionary biology also rely on observation rather than controlled trials and are not denied scientific status. What matters is whether hypotheses are formulated so that evidence could refute them.\n• Discussion — economics does now run experiments: randomised controlled trials are standard in development economics and in behavioural policy, where the UK Behavioural Insights Team, set up in 2010, tests interventions against control groups. Laboratory experiments test game-theoretic predictions directly.\n• Discussion — quasi-experimental methods: difference-in-differences, instrumental variables, regression discontinuity and natural experiments recover causal estimates from observational data, and their development has substantially raised the standard of empirical work.\n• Discussion — genuine obstacles remain: human beings respond to being studied and to the policies predicted by the models, so relationships can break down once exploited — the Lucas critique and Goodhart's law. This is a real difference from the natural sciences.\n• Discussion — the normative element: economics is used to make policy recommendations, which requires value judgements that no scientific method can supply. That does not make its positive branch unscientific, but it does mean the discipline is not only a science.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that the positive branch of economics meets the criterion that matters — testable hypotheses confronted with evidence — and increasingly does so experimentally, while the reflexivity of human behaviour and the normative use of its conclusions make it a social science rather than a natural one, which is a different claim from "not a science".\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained two-sided discussion with accurate examples and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) the issue described with limited evaluation; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that the failure of centrally planned economies demonstrates that governments should play no part in allocating resources.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `• The evidence relied on: centrally planned economies suffered chronic shortages and surpluses, low product quality, weak innovation and a widening output gap against market economies over the second half of the twentieth century.\n• The explanation: planners lacked the dispersed information that prices summarise, so they could not know relative scarcities; without profit and loss there was no incentive to minimise cost or to innovate; and administered prices could not perform the rationing function, so queues and black markets emerged instead.\n• Evaluation — the conclusion does not follow: the failure of comprehensive central planning of all production is evidence against comprehensive central planning, not against any government role. No mainstream economist proposes replacing the price mechanism, but the case for targeted intervention rests on specific market failures.\n• Evaluation — the market failure case: public goods are non-rival and non-excludable, so free-riding means private provision is inadequate; externalities drive a wedge between private and social cost; information failure and monopoly power break the efficiency conditions. Each identifies a market outcome that is not welfare-maximising.\n• Evaluation — the equity case: even an efficient market allocation distributes output by ability to pay. The state's role in redistribution rests on a value judgement the efficiency criterion cannot address.\n• Evaluation — markets need the state to function: secure property rights, contract enforcement, competition law and financial regulation are preconditions for markets, not interferences with them. The Competition and Markets Authority's designation of Google and of Apple as having strategic market status in October 2025 under the Digital Markets, Competition and Consumers Act 2024 is an example of intervention intended to make a market work better rather than to replace it.\n• Evaluation — government failure is real: information gaps, unintended consequences, administrative costs, regulatory capture and short political horizons mean intervention can worsen outcomes. The correct comparison is between an imperfect market and an imperfect government, not between an imperfect market and an ideal planner.\n• Judgement: a supportable conclusion is that the historical record shows comprehensive planning to be inferior to markets as a general allocation mechanism, but that it says nothing against intervention justified by identifiable market failure; the question is which mix, judged case by case against the risk of government failure.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained evaluation distinguishing comprehensive planning from targeted intervention, with a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) sound knowledge, evaluation limited; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the significance of opportunity cost as an argument against further increases in government spending on healthcare.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `• The argument: resources devoted to healthcare cannot be used elsewhere. The opportunity cost is the next best alternative forgone — education, infrastructure, defence or private consumption if the spending is tax-financed. Illustrate with a PPF with healthcare on one axis and all other goods on the other, and note that the cost rises at the margin as healthcare output expands.\n• Strength of the argument: it forces the right question. The issue is never whether healthcare is valuable but whether the marginal pound produces more benefit in healthcare than in its best alternative use. This is why health economics values interventions per quality-adjusted life year rather than in absolute terms.\n• Supporting point: skilled clinical staff, land and specialised capital are poor substitutes for inputs elsewhere, so the concavity of the frontier is real and the marginal cost of further expansion is high.\n• Assessment — opportunity cost is an argument for appraisal, not against spending: it identifies a cost to be weighed, not a reason to stop. If the marginal return in healthcare exceeds the marginal return elsewhere, opportunity cost argues for more spending, not less.\n• Assessment — healthcare has returns beyond consumption: better health raises labour supply, participation and productivity, so part of health spending is investment that shifts the frontier outwards rather than a movement along it. Counting it purely as consumption overstates its net cost.\n• Assessment — the position relative to the frontier matters: if the economy has spare capacity, extra spending can employ otherwise idle resources, and the short-run opportunity cost in terms of forgone alternative output is much smaller than the model assumes.\n• Assessment — equity and the counterfactual: the alternative use of the resources may itself be low-value private consumption, and a purely efficiency-based comparison ignores the distributional case for provision free at the point of use.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that opportunity cost is indispensable as a discipline on the healthcare debate, because it rules out the claim that health spending is costless, but that it is neutral on direction — it argues for allocating to the highest marginal return, which may well mean more health spending rather than less.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained assessment using opportunity cost precisely at the margin, with a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) opportunity cost explained, assessment limited; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: `After considering Extract D, and the original evidence in Extracts A, B and C, would you recommend that the government of Country X should reallocate resources from consumer-goods industries towards capital-goods industries? Justify your recommendation.\n\nExtract D — written for this practice question\n"A further review finds that Country X's existing capital stock is only 68% utilised, that two-fifths of its investment over the last decade went into commercial property rather than productive equipment, and that 22% of its workforce lacks a qualification at level 2 or above."`,
        marks: 25, command: 'Justify',
        markScheme: `• Restate the case from the earlier evidence: a low investment share, slow growth in potential output and a rising capital stock are consistent with the argument that more capital goods would shift the frontier outwards and raise future living standards. Use a two-period PPF diagram.\n• What Extract D changes — utilisation: if the existing capital stock is only 68% utilised, the binding constraint is not the quantity of capital. Adding capital to an economy that is not using what it has raises measured investment without raising output, and the immediate gain would come from moving towards the existing frontier instead.\n• What Extract D changes — composition: two-fifths of investment going into commercial property rather than productive equipment means the recorded investment figure overstates the addition to productive capacity. The problem is identified as the quality and direction of investment, not its level.\n• What Extract D changes — complements: with 22% of the workforce below level 2, human capital is plausibly the tighter constraint. Capital and skills are complements, so the marginal return to new equipment is low where the workforce cannot operate it, and spending on training may shift the frontier further per pound.\n• Weighing the evidence: Extracts A to C establish that potential output is growing slowly; Extract D reinterprets the cause. The two are not contradictory — they point to different remedies for the same symptom.\n• The recommendation and its conditions: a defensible recommendation is a qualified no to a blanket reallocation, and yes to a targeted one — address utilisation and demand first, redirect investment incentives from property towards productive equipment, and pair any increase in capital spending with training, because the return to capital depends on the complementary factors.\n• Counter-argument that must be addressed: low utilisation may itself be cyclical, in which case it will reverse and the capital constraint will reassert itself; and delaying investment is costly because the capital stock takes years to build. A candidate who addresses this and still reaches a reasoned recommendation should be credited at the top.\n• Uncertainties in the evidence: all the figures are point estimates from a single review, there is no time series on utilisation, and the extracts give no information on the rate of return to the investment being proposed.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) all four extracts used, evidence weighed against evidence, a clear recommendation justified with conditions and the main counter-argument addressed; L4 (16-20) extracts used well with a justified recommendation, weighing less consistent; L3 (11-15) some use of extracts, recommendation asserted; L2 (6-10) descriptive use of extracts; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` }
    ]
  },
  '3.1.2': {
    name: 'Individual Economic Decision Making',
    questions: [
      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA consumer's total utility from slices of pizza, measured in utils\n  1 slice = 40 ; 2 slices = 72 ; 3 slices = 96 ; 4 slices = 111 ; 5 slices = 118\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the marginal utility the consumer gains from the fourth slice of pizza.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: marginal utility = total utility of 4 slices - total utility of 3 slices = 111 - 96.\nAnswer: 15.00 utils.\n2 marks for 15.00; 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for dividing total utility by quantity (average rather than marginal utility).\nNote: the falling sequence 40, 32, 24, 15, 7 is the law of diminishing marginal utility.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA consumer choosing between two goods\n  Good A: price = £4.00 ; marginal utility of the next unit = 26 utils\n  Good B: price = £2.50 ; marginal utility of the next unit = 18 utils\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the marginal utility per pound the consumer gains from Good A.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: marginal utility divided by price = 26 / 4.00.\nAnswer: 6.50 utils per pound.\n2 marks for 6.50; 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip.\nFor reference in later parts: Good B gives 18 / 2.50 = 7.20 utils per pound, so the equi-marginal condition MUa/Pa = MUb/Pb is not met and the consumer should switch spending towards Good B.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA randomised trial of a pension communication, 2 000 employees in each group\n  Control group (opt-in form):        412 employees joined the scheme\n  Treatment group (automatic entry):  1 806 employees joined the scheme\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the percentage of the treatment group that joined the scheme.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: 1 806 / 2 000 × 100.\nAnswer: 90.30%.\n2 marks for 90.30%; 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic or rounding slip.\nFor reference: the control group rate is 412/2 000 = 20.60%, a difference of 69.70 percentage points.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nAn experiment on the endowment effect, 120 participants\n  Average amount buyers were willing to pay for a mug:              £3.20\n  Average amount owners were willing to accept to sell the same mug: £7.04\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the ratio of the willingness-to-accept figure to the willingness-to-pay figure.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: 7.04 / 3.20.\nAnswer: 2.20 (owners demand 2.2 times what buyers will pay for the identical good).\n2 marks for 2.20; 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for the inverted ratio 0.45.\nNote: under the traditional model the two figures should be almost identical, since the mug's value does not depend on who holds it.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA supermarket trial of a "healthy checkout" layout, 8 weeks\n  Confectionery units sold at checkouts, standard layout:  24 500\n  Confectionery units sold at checkouts, healthy layout:   17 885\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the percentage fall in confectionery units sold at the checkouts under the healthy layout.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: (24 500 - 17 885) / 24 500 × 100 = 6 615 / 24 500 × 100.\nAnswer: 27.00%.\n2 marks for 27.00%; 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for using the new figure as the denominator.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA consumer's marginal utility from cups of coffee per week, in utils\n  1st cup = 30 ; 2nd cup = 22 ; 3rd cup = 15 ; 4th cup = 9 ; 5th cup = 4\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the consumer's total utility from four cups of coffee per week.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: total utility = sum of the marginal utilities = 30 + 22 + 15 + 9.\nAnswer: 76.00 utils.\n2 marks for 76.00; 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for including the fifth cup (80.00).\nNote: total utility is still rising because every marginal utility figure is positive; it is the increments that are falling.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA consumer's total utility from slices of pizza, in utils\n  1 slice = 40 ; 2 slices = 72 ; 3 slices = 96 ; 4 slices = 111 ; 5 slices = 118 ; 6 slices = 118\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that this consumer experiences diminishing marginal utility.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Marginal utility for each successive slice is 40, 32, 24, 15, 7 and 0 — positive but falling throughout.\n• Diminishing marginal utility means the satisfaction from each additional unit consumed within a given period falls as consumption rises, which is exactly the pattern shown.\n• Total utility continues to rise while marginal utility is positive, and reaches its maximum at the sixth slice where marginal utility is zero. Rising total utility is therefore entirely consistent with diminishing marginal utility.\n• Significance: because the consumer values each extra slice less, they will only buy more at a lower price, which is the utility-based explanation of the downward-sloping demand curve.\nLevels: 4 = marginal utilities correctly derived AND the falling pattern explained, with the total/marginal relationship handled correctly; 3 = correct derivation with some explanation; 2 = the falling pattern identified without correct derivation; 1 = a relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA consumer with a fixed weekly budget, currently buying both goods\n  Good A: price = £4.00 ; marginal utility of the next unit = 26 utils\n  Good B: price = £2.50 ; marginal utility of the next unit = 18 utils\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that this consumer is not maximising utility from the given budget.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Marginal utility per pound: Good A gives 26/4.00 = 6.50 utils per pound; Good B gives 18/2.50 = 7.20 utils per pound.\n• The equi-marginal principle states that utility is maximised where MUa/Pa = MUb/Pb. Here the two are unequal, so the allocation of the budget is not optimal.\n• Explanation of the adjustment: transferring one pound of spending from A to B gains 7.20 utils and loses 6.50, a net gain of 0.70 utils. The consumer should keep switching towards B.\n• Why the adjustment stops: as more of B is consumed, its marginal utility falls (and A's rises as less is consumed), so the ratios converge until they are equal and no further gain is available.\nLevels: 4 = both ratios calculated correctly AND the equi-marginal condition and the direction of adjustment explained; 3 = ratios calculated with some explanation; 2 = one ratio calculated, or the principle stated without calculation; 1 = a relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA randomised trial of pension enrolment methods, 2 000 employees per group\n  Control group, opt-in form provided:            412 joined (20.6%)\n  Group 2, opt-in plus an information leaflet:    509 joined (25.5%)\n  Group 3, automatic entry with the right to opt out: 1 806 joined (90.3%)\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that changing the default option was more effective than providing information.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• The leaflet raised participation by only 4.9 percentage points (from 20.6% to 25.5%), while the change of default raised it by 69.7 percentage points (from 20.6% to 90.3%) — an effect more than fourteen times larger.\n• Both treatments are compared against the same control group and the groups are of equal size, so the comparison is a like-for-like one.\n• Explanation: under the traditional rational model the default should be irrelevant, because the same options are available at negligible cost in every group. The information treatment is the one the rational model predicts should work.\n• Behavioural explanation: inertia, present bias and the status quo bias mean the default is taken as an implicit recommendation and the effort of switching is deferred indefinitely, so choice architecture dominates information.\nLevels: 4 = both percentage-point changes calculated AND the default-versus-information contrast explained; 3 = data used with some explanation; 2 = data identified, explanation thin; 1 = one relevant figure identified only.` },

      { q: `Extract B — written for this practice question\nTwo groups of 400 shoppers were shown the same minced beef with different labels.\n  Label 1: "75% lean"    — 268 shoppers rated the product as good quality\n  Label 2: "25% fat"     — 116 shoppers rated the product as good quality\n\nExplain how the data in Extract B show that the framing of information affects consumer decisions.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• 67.0% of shoppers rated the product highly when it was framed as "75% lean" against 29.0% when framed as "25% fat" — a difference of 38 percentage points.\n• The two labels are logically identical descriptions of the same product, so the underlying good and its objective characteristics are unchanged.\n• Explanation: under the traditional model preferences are defined over outcomes, so logically equivalent descriptions must yield identical valuations. The observed difference contradicts that.\n• Behavioural explanation: framing works because people evaluate outcomes relative to a reference point and are loss averse, so a description that highlights the negative attribute ("fat") is weighted more heavily than one that highlights the positive attribute ("lean").\nLevels: 4 = both percentages calculated or the difference quantified AND the logical-equivalence point explained; 3 = data used with some explanation; 2 = the difference identified, explanation thin; 1 = one relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: `Extract B — written for this practice question\nAn online retailer advertised a driving course at £199. At the checkout, after customers had entered their details, compulsory booking, administration and card fees of £41 were added.\n  Customers who completed the purchase at the advertised price of £199: not applicable\n  Customers shown the full £240 price from the start:       31% completed the purchase\n  Customers shown £199 and charged £240 at the checkout:    58% completed the purchase\n\nExplain how the data in Extract B show that the way a price is presented can affect consumer decisions.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• The completion rate is 58% when the fees are revealed late against 31% when the full price is shown from the start — 27 percentage points higher for the identical total price of £240.\n• The good and the amount paid are identical in both conditions, so a fully informed, rational consumer should make the same decision in each.\n• Explanation: this is drip pricing. The headline price sets an anchor; by the time the additional charges appear the consumer has invested time and attention in the transaction, and the sunk effort plus the small relative size of each added fee make abandoning the purchase feel costly.\n• Real context: the Competition and Markets Authority fined the AA £4.2 million in April 2026 over drip pricing in driving lessons and ordered refunds of more than £760 000 to over 80 000 learner drivers, treating the practice as a breach of consumer protection law.\nLevels: 4 = the percentage-point difference quantified AND the anchoring/sunk-effort mechanism explained; 3 = data used with some explanation; 2 = the difference identified, explanation thin; 1 = one relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — written for this practice question\nA survey of 1 000 gym members\n  Members who said in January that they intended to attend at least twice a week:  740\n  Members who actually attended at least twice a week over the following year:     193\n  Members paying a monthly rolling contract who said they intended to cancel but had not done so after six months: 288\n\nExplain how the data in Table 2 show evidence of bounded self-control.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Only 193 of the 740 members who intended to attend twice a week did so — 26.1% — leaving 547 whose behaviour did not match their own stated intention.\n• A further 288 members continued paying for a contract they had already decided to cancel, so the failure runs in both directions: under-consumption of the thing they valued and continued spending on something they did not.\n• Explanation: bounded self-control means consumers know what is in their long-term interest but fail to act on it, because the cost of exercising is immediate and certain while the benefit is delayed and uncertain.\n• Development: this is present bias, and it is systematic rather than random, which is why the traditional model's assumption that stated preferences and revealed choices coincide breaks down here.\nLevels: 4 = both figures used precisely AND bounded self-control / present bias explained; 3 = data used with some explanation; 2 = data identified, explanation thin; 1 = one relevant figure identified only.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 4-5) states that "each additional unit a consumer buys within a given period gives them less satisfaction than the one before". With the help of a diagram, explain the relationship between total utility and marginal utility.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: two vertically aligned panels, or one diagram with two curves — total utility rising at a decreasing rate and reaching a maximum, and beneath it a downward-sloping marginal utility curve cutting the horizontal axis at exactly the quantity where total utility peaks.\n• Definitions: total utility is the overall satisfaction from consuming a given quantity; marginal utility is the addition to total utility from one more unit, so marginal utility is the slope of the total utility curve.\n• Chain of reasoning: while marginal utility is positive, each extra unit adds something, so total utility rises — but because marginal utility is falling, it rises by less each time, giving the concave shape.\n• The turning point: where marginal utility equals zero the consumer gains nothing from another unit, so total utility is at its maximum. This is the single most examinable feature of the relationship.\n• Beyond that point marginal utility is negative — the extra unit actively reduces satisfaction (the sixth cup of coffee, the last slice of cake) — and total utility falls.\n• Application: because the consumer values each extra unit less, they will only purchase more at a lower price, so the marginal utility schedule underlies the individual demand curve.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram with the TU maximum aligned to MU = 0, developed explanation of the slope relationship; L2 (4-6) diagram present with some explanation, alignment may be inaccurate; L1 (1-3) definitions only, diagram missing or wrong.` },

      { q: 'With the help of a diagram, explain how the law of diminishing marginal utility can be used to derive an individual\'s downward-sloping demand curve.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: a downward-sloping marginal utility curve with quantity on the horizontal axis, and alongside it a demand curve with price on the vertical axis, showing that the two schedules have the same shape.\n• The decision rule: a rational consumer buys an extra unit while the marginal utility of that unit, expressed in money terms, is at least equal to the price. Consumption stops where marginal utility equals price.\n• Chain of reasoning: at a high price, only the first few units have a marginal utility high enough to justify purchase, so quantity demanded is low. When price falls, units further down the marginal utility schedule now clear the threshold, so quantity demanded rises.\n• Conclusion: the quantity at which MU = P for each price traces out the demand curve. The demand curve is, on this interpretation, the marginal utility curve measured in money.\n• Extension: consumer surplus is the area between the demand curve and the price, because every unit before the last was worth more to the consumer than they paid.\n• Qualification worth making: this requires marginal utility to be measurable in money terms and the marginal utility of money itself to be constant — assumptions modern theory replaces with indifference analysis and revealed preference.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram, the MU = P rule applied to two different prices, clear derivation; L2 (4-6) some accurate derivation, diagram thin; L1 (1-3) assertion that demand slopes downwards with no derivation.` },

      { q: 'Analyse how a rational consumer with a fixed budget allocates spending between two goods, and how that allocation changes when the price of one good falls.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `• The condition: utility is maximised where the marginal utility per pound is equal across all goods, MUa/Pa = MUb/Pb, with the whole budget spent.\n• Why it must hold: if MUa/Pa exceeds MUb/Pb, the last pound spent on A yields more satisfaction than the last pound spent on B, so transferring spending from B to A raises total utility. Reallocation continues until no such transfer is available.\n• Why the process converges: diminishing marginal utility means that as more A is bought its marginal utility falls, and as less B is bought B's marginal utility rises, so the two ratios move towards each other.\n• Effect of a fall in Pa: MUa/Pa immediately rises above MUb/Pb, so the consumer substitutes towards A until diminishing marginal utility restores equality — the substitution effect, and the source of the downward-sloping demand curve.\n• Second channel: the price fall also raises real income, so the consumer can afford more of both. For a normal good the income effect reinforces the substitution effect; for an inferior good it works against it.\n• Worked illustration: with Pa = £4.00 and MUa = 26, A yields 6.50 utils per pound against B's 18/2.50 = 7.20. A fall in Pa to £3.00 raises A to 8.67 utils per pound, reversing the direction of the profitable switch.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) the condition stated correctly, the adjustment process analysed with a numerical or algebraic chain, and both effects of the price change distinguished; L2 (4-6) condition stated with some analysis; L1 (1-3) the rule asserted with no reasoning.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 11-12) states that "people demand far more to give up something they own than they would pay to acquire it". With the help of a diagram, explain the concept of loss aversion.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: a value function with gains and losses relative to a reference point on the horizontal axis and subjective value on the vertical. The curve is concave in the gains domain, convex in the losses domain, and noticeably steeper below the reference point than above it.\n• Definition: loss aversion means a loss of a given size reduces wellbeing by more than an equivalent gain increases it — experimental estimates put the ratio at roughly two to one.\n• The reference point is the key departure from the traditional model: outcomes are evaluated as changes from a starting position, not as final states of wealth. Two people with identical final wealth can evaluate the same outcome differently.\n• Consequence — the endowment effect: ownership shifts the reference point, so giving up the good is coded as a loss. The practice data show owners demanding £7.04 for a mug buyers would pay only £3.20 for, a ratio of 2.20 — close to the standard loss-aversion estimate.\n• Further consequences: status quo bias in default choices, the disposition to hold losing investments rather than realise a loss, and the effectiveness of policies framed as avoiding a charge rather than earning a reward.\n• Contrast that earns L3: under the traditional model the willingness to pay and the willingness to accept for the same good should be nearly identical, since the good's value is independent of who currently holds it.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate asymmetric value function with the reference point marked, definition precise, and at least one developed consequence; L2 (4-6) definition with some explanation, diagram symmetric or missing detail; L1 (1-3) definition only.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 16-17) states that "the seller of a used car knows things about it that the buyer cannot discover". Analyse how asymmetric information in the second-hand car market can lead to market failure.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `• Definition: asymmetric information exists where one party to a transaction has more or better information than the other, so the terms of trade do not reflect the true characteristics of the good.\n• The mechanism (Akerlof's "market for lemons", 1970): buyers cannot distinguish good cars from bad ones before purchase, so they will only pay a price reflecting average quality.\n• Chain of reasoning: that average price is below what a good car is worth, so owners of good cars withdraw from the market. Average quality of the cars remaining therefore falls, buyers revise the price they will pay downwards, and more good cars are withdrawn.\n• Outcome: adverse selection. In the limit only the worst cars are traded, and mutually beneficial transactions between honest sellers and willing buyers fail to happen. This is a misallocation of resources, so it is market failure rather than merely an unfair outcome.\n• Distinction that earns L3: the failure is not that buyers are cheated on average — the price adjusts for that — but that the market unravels and welfare-improving trades do not occur.\n• Private and public responses: warranties, independent inspections, full service histories, manufacturer-approved used schemes and dealer reputation all work by making quality credible; regulation requiring disclosure of write-off or mileage history does the same. Their existence is evidence of the problem, since a market with symmetric information would not need them.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) the unravelling mechanism analysed as a chain, market failure correctly identified as misallocation, with at least one remedy explained; L2 (4-6) asymmetric information explained with partial analysis; L1 (1-3) definition with an example only.` },

      { q: 'Analyse how the use of heuristics such as anchoring and availability can lead consumers to make choices that the traditional model of rational decision making would not predict.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `• Context: heuristics are mental shortcuts that economise on the cost of deciding. They are a rational response to bounded rationality — limited information and limited processing capacity — but they produce systematic, not random, errors.\n• Anchoring: an initial figure disproportionately influences a subsequent judgement, even when it is arbitrary. "Was £80, now £45" makes the second figure appear cheap, and a recommended serving or default quantity anchors how much is consumed. Under the traditional model an irrelevant number should not affect valuation at all.\n• Availability: the probability of an event is judged by how easily an example comes to mind. Heavily reported risks such as air crashes or shark attacks are over-weighted while common but undramatic risks are under-weighted, so insurance is bought against the wrong things.\n• Why this matters analytically: because the errors are predictable in direction, they do not cancel out across consumers. Aggregate demand can therefore be systematically different from what the rational model predicts, and firms can and do design pricing to exploit the bias.\n• Policy consequence: if biases are systematic they can also be corrected systematically — by regulating reference pricing, by mandating standardised unit prices that neutralise the anchor, or by presenting risks in frequency terms that make them easier to imagine.\n• Qualification: heuristics are usually efficient. Deliberating fully over every purchase would itself be irrational given the time cost, so the correct conclusion is that shortcuts fail in identifiable circumstances rather than that consumers are irrational in general.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) both heuristics explained with examples and a developed analysis of why systematic bias matters; L2 (4-6) both defined with some analysis; L1 (1-3) definitions or examples only.` },

      { q: 'Analyse how social norms and herding behaviour can influence an individual consumer\'s decisions.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `• Definition: a social norm is a shared expectation about how people behave; herding is the tendency to copy the observed behaviour of others rather than rely on one\'s own information.\n• Departure from the traditional model: utility is assumed to depend on the individual\'s own consumption bundle. Norms make utility depend on what others do, so the individual demand curve is no longer independent of other consumers\' choices.\n• Informational mechanism: where quality is hard to judge, others\' behaviour is genuinely informative. Choosing the busy restaurant is a rational inference from a credible signal, not an error.\n• Social mechanism: conformity carries direct utility and deviation carries a cost in disapproval, which is why norm-based messages such as "nine out of ten people in your area pay their tax on time" raise compliance where an appeal to duty does not.\n• Consequences for markets: herding produces bandwagon effects, viral demand and, in asset markets, self-reinforcing price movements that detach prices from fundamentals — since each person infers value from others who are inferring it from them.\n• Policy use and limits: the UK Behavioural Insights Team, established in 2010, has used descriptive norms in tax and health communications. The limit is that telling people most others do the undesirable thing can entrench it, so norm messages can backfire when the desirable behaviour is the minority one.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) both mechanisms analysed, with the departure from independent preferences made explicit and a consequence or policy application developed; L2 (4-6) definitions with some analysis; L1 (1-3) examples with little analysis.` },

      { q: 'Analyse how a government can use choice architecture, including default choices, to change consumer behaviour.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `• Definition: choice architecture is the design of the environment in which choices are made — the order of options, what happens if nothing is chosen, how information is framed and how easy each option is to take.\n• Default choices: the option that applies if the individual does nothing. Automatic enrolment into workplace pensions, introduced from October 2012, made saving the default; participation among eligible employees reached 90% in 2025, with 22.6 million saving, a level voluntary opt-in never approached.\n• Why defaults are so powerful: inertia and present bias mean the effort of switching is deferred; the default is read as an implicit recommendation; and loss aversion makes moving away from the status quo feel like giving something up.\n• Other tools, in ascending order of coerciveness: framing and simplification; changing the salience or physical position of options, as in supermarket checkout layouts; mandated choice, which forces an active decision without dictating it; and restricted choice, which removes options altogether.\n• The design principle: these interventions preserve freedom of choice and are cheap relative to taxation or regulation, which is why they are described as "nudges" rather than as mandates.\n• Analytical qualification: defaults transfer power to whoever sets them, and their effect depends on the cost of opting out remaining genuinely low. Opt-out rates of around 11 to 12 per cent among automatically enrolled employees show the choice remains real.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) several tools analysed with the behavioural mechanism explained and an accurate dated example developed; L2 (4-6) defaults explained with some analysis; L1 (1-3) list of nudges with little analysis.` },

      { q: 'Analyse two ways in which bounded self-control can lead consumers to outcomes that they themselves regret.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `• Definition: bounded self-control means the consumer knows which action serves their long-term interest but fails to carry it out, because the cost falls now and the benefit later.\n• Mechanism — present bias: future costs and benefits are discounted more steeply in the near term than the model of consistent discounting allows, so preferences reverse as the moment approaches. The plan made in January to exercise is abandoned each individual evening.\n• Way one — under-saving and under-investment in health: the immediate cost of a pension contribution or of exercise is certain while the benefit is distant, so consumers persistently do less than their own stated plans. The practice data show only 193 of 740 members who intended to attend the gym twice weekly actually doing so.\n• Way two — inertia in cancelling and switching: once a subscription, tariff or insurance policy is running, the effort of switching is always postponable, so consumers keep paying for things they have already decided to leave. Firms respond by designing sign-up to be easy and cancellation to be hard.\n• Why regret is the right test: the consumer\'s own later assessment, not the economist\'s, establishes the failure. This makes the case for intervention weaker than for externalities — the harm is largely internal — but distinguishes it from simply disapproving of the choice.\n• Responses: commitment devices such as fixed-term savings products and pre-committed contribution increases, cooling-off periods, and defaults that put the long-term interest in the no-action position.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) two distinct mechanisms analysed with developed chains of reasoning and a response or the regret criterion discussed; L2 (4-6) two ways identified with some analysis; L1 (1-3) assertion that people lack willpower.` },

      { q: 'With the help of a diagram, explain how imperfect information can cause a merit good to be under-consumed relative to the socially optimal level.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: marginal private benefit (MPB) and marginal social benefit (MSB) curves with MSB above MPB, and a marginal cost curve. Market equilibrium at MPB = MC gives Q1; the social optimum is at MSB = MC giving Q2 > Q1. Shade the welfare loss triangle between Q1 and Q2.\n• Definition: a merit good is one the consumer undervalues at the point of consumption, typically because the benefits are long-term, uncertain or poorly understood — vaccination, further education, preventive dental care.\n• Chain of reasoning: with imperfect information the consumer\'s perceived private benefit lies below the true benefit, so the demand curve is drawn too low. Equating perceived benefit with price gives Q1, below the socially optimal Q2.\n• Welfare consequence: every unit between Q1 and Q2 would have delivered a social benefit greater than its marginal cost, so the shaded triangle measures the welfare lost by the under-consumption. This is allocative inefficiency, which is what makes it market failure.\n• Reinforcing behavioural factor: present bias compounds the information problem, because the costs of consuming a merit good are immediate while the benefits are delayed — which is why information campaigns alone often move consumption very little.\n• Remedies and their logic: subsidy or free provision shifts the effective price down to align private with social optimum; information provision shifts perceived MPB towards MSB; compulsion removes the choice where the under-consumption is severe.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate MPB/MSB diagram with Q1, Q2 and the welfare loss identified, and a developed explanation; L2 (4-6) diagram with some explanation, welfare loss omitted or misplaced; L1 (1-3) definition of a merit good only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) and Extract B — written for this practice question\nA randomised trial run by a large employer, 2 000 employees in each group\n  Control, opt-in form:                  412 joined (20.6%)\n  Group 2, opt-in plus information:      509 joined (25.5%)\n  Group 3, automatic entry, opt-out available: 1 806 joined (90.3%)\n  Group 3 average monthly contribution:  £94\n  Control group average monthly contribution among those who joined: £171\n\nExtract B states: "Group 3 employees were disproportionately those on the lowest salaries."\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data suggest that changing the default option raised the total amount saved?`,
        marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `• Evidence for: participation rose from 20.6% to 90.3%, an extra 1 394 savers out of 2 000. Total monthly saving in Group 3 is 1 806 × £94 = £169 764, against 412 × £171 = £70 452 in the control group — more than double, so on these figures the default raised total saving substantially.\n• Strength of the design: the groups are randomly allocated and equally sized, so the difference can be attributed to the treatment rather than to differences between the people. Group 2 acts as a further control isolating the default effect from the information effect.\n• Evidence against, or complicating the conclusion: the average contribution in Group 3 is £94 against £171 in the control group. The default appears to anchor people at the minimum contribution rate, so participation is bought at the cost of depth of saving.\n• Extract B matters: if Group 3 joiners are disproportionately low earners, the lower average contribution may reflect lower salaries rather than an anchoring effect of the default, and the comparison of averages is not like-for-like.\n• Further limitations: the data cover one employer and one period; there is no information on whether the extra saving is new saving or is offset by reduced saving elsewhere or by higher borrowing, which is the central question for total household saving; and no long-run persistence data are given.\n• Judgement: the data support a large increase in the amount saved into these schemes, but "total saving" is a broader concept and the data cannot settle whether household saving as a whole rose. The anchoring of contributions at a low rate is a real qualification, though Extract B suggests it is partly compositional.\nLevels: L3 (8-10) figures calculated and used precisely, the anchoring and composition issues both handled, and a supported judgement reached; L2 (4-7) relevant data used with some evaluation; L1 (1-3) data described without judgement.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — written for this practice question\nSugar content of soft drinks sold in Country X, grams per 100ml, sales-weighted average\n  Year before the levy was announced:   6.8\n  Year the levy was announced:          6.1\n  Year the levy took effect:            4.4\n  Two years after the levy took effect: 3.9\nOver the same period, sugar content in non-levied confectionery rose from 52.1 to 53.4 grams per 100g.\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data suggest that the levy caused manufacturers to reformulate their drinks?`,
        marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `• Evidence for: the sales-weighted sugar content fell by 2.9g per 100ml, or 42.6%, from 6.8 to 3.9. The largest single fall, from 6.1 to 4.4, coincides with the levy taking effect, and reformulation began as soon as the levy was announced — exactly what a forward-looking producer response predicts.\n• The comparison group strengthens the inference: sugar content in confectionery, which was not levied, rose slightly over the same period. That makes a general change in consumer taste or in ingredient costs a less plausible explanation of the fall in drinks.\n• Real-world corroboration: the UK Soft Drinks Industry Levy, introduced in April 2018, is designed as a tax on the producer with a threshold, so the incentive is to reformulate below the threshold rather than to pass the tax on. A 2024 government review found a 46% average reduction in the sugar added to soft drinks.\n• Limitations of the data: a sales-weighted average conflates two different effects — manufacturers changing recipes, and consumers switching between existing products. The data cannot separate them.\n• Further limitations: confectionery is an imperfect control because it faces different demand conditions; there is no data on what was happening to sugar content before the announcement, so the pre-existing trend is unknown; and other policies such as labelling rules may have changed at the same time.\n• Judgement: the data provide strong support for a causal effect on the sugar content of drinks sold — the timing, the size and the behaviour of the untaxed comparison group all point the same way — but they cannot establish how much came from reformulation as opposed to switching, which is precisely what the question asks.\nLevels: L3 (8-10) data calculated and used precisely, the control group and the reformulation-versus-switching distinction both handled, with a supported judgement; L2 (4-7) relevant data used with some evaluation; L1 (1-3) description only.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main assumptions on which the traditional model of rational economic decision making rests.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• The objective: consumers maximise utility and firms maximise profit. Every decision is taken to achieve the best outcome by the agent\'s own criterion.\n• Preferences are complete, transitive and stable: the agent can rank any two bundles, rankings do not contradict each other (if A is preferred to B and B to C then A is preferred to C), and they do not change with irrelevant features of presentation.\n• Full information: agents know the prices, qualities and consequences of all available options, including future ones, so no systematic mistakes occur.\n• Unlimited computational capacity: agents can process all relevant information and identify the optimum at no cost in time or effort.\n• Self-interest: utility depends on the agent\'s own consumption, not on what others receive, and not on fairness.\n• Decisions are taken at the margin, comparing marginal benefit with marginal cost, and the equi-marginal condition MUa/Pa = MUb/Pb determines the allocation of a budget.\n• Consistency over time: future costs and benefits are discounted at a constant rate, so plans made today are carried out tomorrow.\n• What the assumptions buy: determinate, testable predictions — downward-sloping demand curves, responses to price and to income — which is why the model remains the standard starting point even where its descriptive realism is weak.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) the assumptions set out precisely with explanation of why each is needed and what it delivers; L2 (6-10) several assumptions stated with some explanation; L1 (1-5) a list with little explanation.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main ways in which behavioural economics suggests consumers depart from rational decision making.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Bounded rationality: information is costly and processing capacity limited, so consumers satisfice — accepting an option that is good enough — rather than optimise. Heuristics follow from this.\n• Systematic biases from heuristics: anchoring, where an arbitrary initial figure shapes valuation; availability, where the easily recalled event is over-weighted; and framing, where logically equivalent descriptions produce different choices, as in "75% lean" against "25% fat".\n• Reference dependence and loss aversion: outcomes are evaluated as gains or losses relative to a reference point, and losses weigh roughly twice as heavily as equivalent gains. This generates the endowment effect and status quo bias.\n• Bounded self-control and present bias: consumers discount the near future far more steeply than the distant future, so preferences reverse and plans about saving, exercise and diet are not carried out.\n• Social influences: norms, herding, altruism and a concern for fairness mean utility depends on what others do and receive. Ultimatum-game behaviour — rejecting positive but unfair offers — is inconsistent with narrow self-interest.\n• Imperfect and asymmetric information: even a fully rational agent cannot choose well without accurate information, and the second-hand car market shows how asymmetry alone can cause a market to unravel.\n• The common thread that earns L3: these departures are systematic and predictable in direction, not random noise, so they do not cancel out in aggregate — which is what makes them relevant to policy rather than merely to psychology. Kahneman (Nobel, 2002) and Thaler (Nobel, 2017) established this body of evidence.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several categories of departure explained with examples and the systematic-bias point made explicitly; L2 (6-10) several biases described with some explanation; L1 (1-5) a list of biases.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main determinants of whether a nudge will succeed in changing consumer behaviour.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Whether the behaviour is caused by a bias the nudge addresses: a default corrects inertia, so it works where people want the outcome but do not act. It will not work where the behaviour reflects a genuine preference or a binding constraint such as insufficient income.\n• The cost of the alternative: nudges preserve choice, so their effect depends on the effort of opting out remaining low but non-zero. Automatic enrolment retains an opt-out rate of around 11 to 12 per cent precisely because the alternative is genuinely available.\n• Salience and simplicity: nudges work when the desired action is made easy and obvious at the moment of decision. Complexity, paperwork and delay restore the inertia the nudge was designed to overcome.\n• Timing and context: interventions delivered at the moment of choice — at the checkout, at the point of sign-up, on the bill — outperform general information campaigns delivered long beforehand.\n• Whether the norm being invoked is true and majority: a descriptive-norm message works when most people already do the desired thing. Telling people that most people do not comply can entrench non-compliance.\n• Countervailing incentives: a nudge competes with commercial choice architecture designed to pull the other way, and with financial incentives. Where the price signal is strong and opposed, the nudge is likely to be swamped.\n• Scale and persistence: many nudge effects are small and some fade or fail to replicate at scale, so evidence from a randomised trial in one setting does not guarantee an effect elsewhere. The Behavioural Insights Team, set up in 2010, tests interventions against control groups for this reason.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several determinants explained with developed reasoning and accurate dated examples; L2 (6-10) some determinants explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list of nudges rather than determinants.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main forms of choice architecture available to a government seeking to influence consumer behaviour.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Default choices: the outcome that applies if nothing is done. Automatic enrolment into workplace pensions from October 2012 raised participation among eligible employees to 90% by 2025; opt-out organ donation ("deemed consent") took effect in England in May 2020, in Wales in 2015 and in Scotland in 2021.\n• Framing and the presentation of information: identical facts described as a gain or as a loss produce different choices, so the wording of letters, bills and labels is itself a policy instrument. Traffic-light nutrition labelling works this way.\n• Salience and physical positioning: moving products, changing queue layouts or altering the order of options on a screen changes what is chosen without changing what is available — the supermarket checkout is the standard example.\n• Simplification: removing steps, pre-filling forms and shortening letters raises take-up of entitlements and compliance with obligations, because complexity is itself a deterrent.\n• Mandated choice: the individual must make an active decision but is not told which to make. This preserves autonomy more fully than a default while eliminating inertia.\n• Restricted choice: removing options altogether — banning an ingredient, capping a portion size, prohibiting a sales practice. This is no longer a nudge, since choice is not preserved, and needs a stronger justification.\n• Social-norm messaging: telling people what most others do, used in tax compliance and health communications, works through conformity rather than through incentives.\n• The organising point: these tools form a ladder from least to most coercive, and the usual argument is that the least coercive instrument capable of achieving the objective should be used first.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several forms explained with mechanisms and accurate dated examples, and the coerciveness ladder made explicit; L2 (6-10) several forms described with some explanation; L1 (1-5) list with little explanation.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main causes of asymmetric information in markets and the main consequences for the allocation of resources.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Cause — hidden characteristics: the seller knows the quality of the good and the buyer cannot verify it before purchase. Used cars, insurance applicants\' health, and the quality of professional services are the standard cases.\n• Cause — hidden actions: once a contract exists, one party\'s behaviour cannot be observed. An insured driver may take less care; a manager may pursue their own objectives rather than the shareholders\'.\n• Cause — complexity and infrequency: financial products, mortgages and pensions are bought rarely and are hard to evaluate, so the information gap persists even where nothing is deliberately concealed.\n• Consequence — adverse selection: buyers price for average quality, good-quality sellers withdraw, average quality falls and the market can unravel. Akerlof\'s 1970 analysis of the used car market sets out the mechanism; in insurance the same logic leaves the low-risk uninsured.\n• Consequence — moral hazard: hidden action leads to more of the risky behaviour than either party would have agreed to ex ante, raising costs for everyone in the pool.\n• Consequence — the principal-agent problem: where owners cannot monitor managers, objectives diverge, which is why remuneration is tied to performance.\n• Consequence for allocation: in every case mutually beneficial trades fail to occur or occur on the wrong terms, so resources are misallocated. This is market failure, not merely unfairness.\n• Remedies: signalling by the informed party (warranties, qualifications, service histories), screening by the uninformed party (excesses, no-claims discounts, credit checks), and regulation requiring disclosure or setting minimum standards.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) causes and consequences explained with developed chains of reasoning and the distinction between adverse selection and moral hazard made clearly; L2 (6-10) some causes and consequences explained; L1 (1-5) definition with an example.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main reasons why consumers may fail to maximise their utility in practice.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Imperfect information: the equi-marginal condition requires knowledge of prices and of the satisfaction each option will yield. Consumers frequently do not know prices elsewhere, and cannot know the utility of an unfamiliar good before consuming it.\n• Asymmetric information: where the seller knows more than the buyer, the choice is made on the wrong information and can be systematically distorted, as in used cars, financial products and professional services.\n• Bounded rationality: gathering and processing information is costly, so consumers satisfice. Search stops well before the optimum because further search is not worth the time.\n• Systematic biases: anchoring, availability and framing shift valuations away from the true marginal utility, and firms design prices and presentation to exploit this — reference pricing, drip pricing and complex tariffs all work this way.\n• Bounded self-control: present bias means consumers act against their own long-term interest even when they know it, so the consumption path they choose is not the one they would choose in advance.\n• Habit, inertia and social influence: repeat purchase without reassessment, status quo bias in switching, and conformity to what others do all break the assumption that each decision is optimised afresh.\n• Measurement problems with utility itself: utility is ordinal and subjective, so the consumer cannot literally compute utils per pound. The model is a description of the logic of choice rather than of the calculation performed.\n• Balancing point: none of this means consumers choose badly in general. Repetition, feedback and high stakes discipline choices, so the failures are concentrated in infrequent, complex and delayed-consequence decisions.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several reasons explained with developed reasoning and a sense of where the failures are concentrated; L2 (6-10) several reasons stated with some explanation; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the usefulness of the traditional model of rational decision making in explaining the behaviour of consumers.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `• What the model delivers: from utility maximisation, diminishing marginal utility and the equi-marginal condition it generates downward-sloping demand curves, substitution and income effects, and predictions about responses to price and income that are borne out across a huge range of markets.\n• Methodological defence: models are judged by predictive power, not descriptive realism. No one claims consumers compute marginal utility per pound; the claim is that they behave as if they do.\n• Where it works best: repeated, low-complexity purchases with immediate feedback and meaningful stakes — groceries, fuel, everyday services. Here learning and competition discipline behaviour towards the model\'s predictions.\n• Evaluation — systematic departures: framing changes choices between logically identical options; the endowment effect gives willingness-to-accept roughly twice willingness-to-pay; defaults dominate information, as the rise in workplace pension participation to 90% of eligible employees by 2025 shows. None of these is compatible with stable preferences over outcomes.\n• Evaluation — the errors do not cancel: because biases are systematic in direction, aggregate behaviour departs from the prediction too, and firms design pricing to exploit them. The Competition and Markets Authority\'s £4.2 million fine on the AA in April 2026 over drip pricing shows regulators treating the exploitation of bias as a live problem.\n• Evaluation — the alternative is incomplete: behavioural economics offers a catalogue of biases rather than a single tractable model. With enough biases available, any observed behaviour can be explained after the event, which weakens predictive power. Many nudge effects are also small or fail to replicate at scale.\n• Evaluation — domain matters: the traditional model performs worst for infrequent, complex, high-stakes decisions with delayed consequences — pensions, insurance, mortgages, health — and these are exactly the markets where regulation concentrates.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that the model remains the most useful single framework because of its tractability and its accuracy in ordinary markets, but that it should be treated as a benchmark from which predictable deviations occur, with behavioural analysis supplying the corrections in the domains where it fails.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained two-sided evaluation with accurate evidence and a domain-specific judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) both models described, evaluation limited; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess whether nudges are a more effective means of reducing the consumption of demerit goods than indirect taxation.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `• Set up the problem: demerit goods are over-consumed because of negative externalities and because consumers under-weight future private costs. A diagram with MPB above MSB shows the over-consumption at Q1 and the welfare loss.\n• The case for taxation: an indirect tax shifts supply left, raising price and reducing quantity to the social optimum, internalising the externality. It raises revenue that can be hypothecated, and it works through the price mechanism so it does not depend on the consumer noticing anything.\n• Evidence for taxation and for the design subtlety: the UK Soft Drinks Industry Levy, introduced in April 2018, is levied on producers above a sugar threshold. A 2024 government review found a 46% average reduction in the sugar added to soft drinks, and the threshold falls from 5g to 4.5g per 100ml on 1 January 2028 — so its main effect came through reformulation rather than through consumer price response.\n• The case for nudges: they are cheap, preserve freedom of choice, and are effective where the barrier is inertia rather than price. The 10p single-use carrier bag charge in England, extended to all retailers in May 2021, cut bag sales at the main retailers by over 95% — a response far larger than the tiny sum involved can explain through price alone.\n• Assessment — effectiveness depends on elasticity: where demand is price inelastic, as for addictive goods, a tax reduces quantity little and mainly transfers income. Where the behaviour is driven by habit and inertia, a nudge can outperform a price change many times over.\n• Assessment — equity: indirect taxes on demerit goods are regressive, taking a larger share of income from poorer households, and the burden falls on all consumers including those imposing no external cost. Nudges have no direct distributional cost, though restricting choice may bear unevenly.\n• Assessment — scale and durability: taxes are enforceable, permanent and hard to evade domestically; nudge effects are often small, can fade, and sometimes fail to replicate outside the trial. A tax also changes producer behaviour, which a consumer-facing nudge does not.\n• Assessment — they are complements, not substitutes: the bag charge is simultaneously a price and a salience intervention, and the drinks levy works through producers while labelling works through consumers. The strongest answers use this rather than forcing an either/or.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that taxation is the more reliable instrument where an external cost must be internalised and demand is responsive, that nudges dominate where inertia is the binding constraint and the externality is small, and that the effective policies in practice combine the two.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained comparative assessment with accurate dated evidence, diagram used, and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) both policies described with limited assessment; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Discuss the view that behavioural economics has made the concept of utility maximisation redundant.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `• The claim: if consumers are boundedly rational, biased and inconsistent over time, a theory built on maximisation of a stable utility function describes nobody, and should be replaced.\n• Evidence for the claim: framing effects, the endowment effect, preference reversals over time, and the dominance of defaults over incentives are all inconsistent with stable preferences over final outcomes — the assumption on which maximisation rests.\n• Policy evidence: interventions that change nothing about prices or options nonetheless transform outcomes, as automatic enrolment did for workplace pensions, where participation among eligible employees reached 90% in 2025.\n• Discussion — behavioural economics extends rather than replaces: prospect theory is itself a maximisation model. It maximises a value function defined over gains and losses relative to a reference point, with probability weighting. The structure of optimisation survives; what changes is the argument of the function.\n• Discussion — the benchmark is needed to identify the bias: a departure can only be identified as a departure against a standard. Loss aversion is defined as an asymmetry relative to the rational benchmark, so removing the benchmark would remove the finding.\n• Discussion — predictive performance: the maximising model still predicts well in repeated, well-informed, high-feedback markets, and aggregate demand relationships remain the workhorse of applied economics. A theory is not redundant while it outperforms its rivals on most of the domain.\n• Discussion — the weakness of the alternative: a long list of context-dependent biases without a unifying principle is descriptively rich but predictively weak, since it is often unclear ex ante which bias will dominate. Replication problems in the behavioural literature reinforce the caution.\n• Discussion — where the claim has real force: for infrequent, complex, delayed-consequence decisions, treating observed choice as revealing true preference is untenable, and the normative use of utility maximisation — inferring welfare from choice — is what behavioural evidence most seriously undermines.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that behavioural economics has made naive utility maximisation untenable as a description of individual choice and has badly damaged its use as a welfare criterion, but has not made it redundant as a modelling framework, because the corrections are themselves expressed as modified maximisation and the benchmark is needed to state them.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained two-sided discussion showing how the two frameworks relate, with a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) both positions described; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the effectiveness of automatic enrolment as a policy for raising retirement saving in the UK.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `• The policy: from October 2012, employers must automatically enrol eligible workers into a workplace pension, with minimum contributions from employer and employee, and workers may opt out.\n• The behavioural rationale: under-saving reflects present bias, inertia and the complexity of pension choice rather than a considered preference for consumption now. Putting saving in the no-action position uses inertia rather than fighting it.\n• Evidence of success: participation among eligible employees reached 90% in 2025, with 22.6 million saving — an increase of 600 000 on the previous year — and total annual saving by eligible employees of £166.1 billion. Opt-out rates of around 11 to 12 per cent remain low, showing the choice is real but rarely exercised.\n• Cost-effectiveness: the administrative cost to government is small relative to the behaviour change, and no tax rates were altered. Compared with tax relief, which is expensive and benefits higher earners most, the default is a cheap instrument.\n• Evaluation — depth versus breadth: minimum contribution rates anchor many savers at a level well below what an adequate retirement income requires. Participation is close to universal; adequacy is not, and the default may have converted the question "should I save?" into "the minimum must be about right".\n• Evaluation — coverage gaps: the £10 000 earnings trigger has been frozen, so multiple-jobholders and the lowest-paid, disproportionately women and part-time workers, can fall outside eligibility despite total earnings above the threshold. The self-employed are excluded entirely.\n• Evaluation — is it new saving?: a rigorous assessment requires knowing whether pension saving displaced other saving or was financed by borrowing. Where it substitutes for other assets the net effect on retirement resources is smaller than the headline figure.\n• Evaluation — incidence and unintended effects: employer contributions are a labour cost and may be passed back in slower wage growth, so part of the saving is financed by the employee in any case; and for those with expensive debt, saving at the minimum rate may not be optimal.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that automatic enrolment is among the most effective pieces of behavioural policy on record when judged on participation, achieving in a decade what voluntary schemes had failed to achieve in generations, but that it is at best a partial success on adequacy and coverage — so the policy question has moved from getting people into pensions to raising contribution rates and extending eligibility.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained evaluation using accurate dated evidence with a judgement that distinguishes participation from adequacy; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) the policy described with limited evaluation; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the ethical objections to the use of nudges by governments.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `• The objections: nudges work by exploiting the very biases they identify, so they bypass rather than engage a person\'s reasoning; they are often invisible to those affected, which makes consent impossible; they are paternalistic, assuming the state knows the individual\'s interests better than they do; and setting the default transfers real power to whoever designs the architecture, with no obvious accountability.\n• The slippery-slope objection: a technique justified for pensions and organ donation is equally available for objectives the public has not endorsed, and the same tools are used commercially to the consumer\'s disadvantage.\n• Assessment — the libertarian paternalist reply: nudges preserve the option to choose otherwise at low cost, which distinguishes them sharply from bans and from taxes. The opt-out rate of around 11 to 12 per cent under automatic enrolment shows the choice is genuinely available.\n• Assessment — there is no neutral architecture: some default must exist, some order of options must be chosen, some wording must be used. The choice is between architecture designed with the individual\'s interest in mind and architecture designed by commercial interests or by accident.\n• Assessment — the comparison should be with the alternatives: the realistic alternatives to a nudge are taxation, regulation or prohibition, all of which restrict autonomy more. On that comparison the nudge is the least coercive instrument capable of the objective.\n• Assessment — transparency answers part of the objection: a nudge that continues to work when publicly disclosed is not manipulative in the strong sense. Automatic enrolment is publicised and still works; a hidden nudge that would fail if disclosed is much harder to defend.\n• Assessment — where the objection bites hardest: where the nudge serves the government\'s interest rather than the individual\'s (tax compliance, recruitment), where the individual\'s own considered preference differs from the one being promoted, and where the architect is not democratically accountable.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that the manipulation objection is weakened by the impossibility of neutral architecture and by the availability of the alternative, but that it is not dissolved; the defensible position is a transparency test — nudges should be disclosed, directed at outcomes the individual would endorse on reflection, and subject to the same accountability as other policy.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained assessment engaging seriously with both the objection and the reply, with a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) objections described with limited assessment; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that asymmetric information is the most significant cause of market failure in the market for financial products.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `• The case: financial products are bought rarely, have delayed and uncertain consequences and are hard to evaluate. Sellers know the charges, risks and exclusions; buyers frequently do not, so the conditions for adverse selection are unusually strong.\n• Mechanisms: hidden characteristics produce mis-selling and adverse selection in insurance, where the low-risk withdraw and the pool deteriorates; hidden actions produce moral hazard, since insured parties take more risk; and the principal-agent problem arises where advisers are remunerated by product providers rather than by clients.\n• Consequences: mutually beneficial contracts fail to occur, consumers hold unsuitable products, and trust in the market falls — a misallocation of resources rather than merely an unfair distribution.\n• Evaluation — behavioural failures may matter more: even a fully informed buyer of a pension or a mortgage faces present bias, inertia and complexity. The evidence that defaults transform pension participation while information barely moves it — a 4.9 percentage point effect against nearly 70 in the practice trial data — suggests the binding constraint is often not information at all.\n• Evaluation — competing causes: systemic risk is a negative externality, since a failing bank imposes costs on parties outside the transaction, and it is this rather than information asymmetry that motivates capital requirements and deposit insurance. Market power in payment systems and in retail banking is a third distinct source of failure.\n• Evaluation — the causes interact: deposit insurance corrects one failure and creates moral hazard, a second. Regulation intended to reduce asymmetry by mandating disclosure can add to complexity and so worsen the behavioural failure — a long, accurate document nobody reads.\n• Evaluation — test by remedy: disclosure requirements, the classic remedy for asymmetry, have repeatedly underperformed, while product bans, price caps, default fund design and simplification have worked better. That pattern is evidence that information alone is not the core problem.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that asymmetric information is a necessary part of the explanation and is more severe here than in most markets, but that it is not obviously the most significant cause: behavioural failure explains why disclosure remedies fail, and systemic externalities explain the largest welfare costs, so a single-cause account cannot be sustained.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained evaluation weighing competing causes with a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) asymmetric information explained with limited evaluation; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that, because consumers frequently fail to act on information, governments should restrict choice rather than seek to inform it.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `• The premise is well supported: information campaigns typically produce small effects. In the practice trial an information leaflet raised pension participation by 4.9 percentage points while changing the default raised it by 69.7, and consumers rarely read terms and conditions or compare tariffs.\n• The argument for restriction: if information does not change behaviour, and the behaviour imposes costs, removing the harmful option is the only reliable instrument. Bans on particular ingredients, caps on prices and prohibitions on sales practices work regardless of whether anyone reads anything.\n• Supporting evidence: producer-side regulation has outperformed consumer-side information in several cases — the Soft Drinks Industry Levy achieved a 46% average reduction in added sugar according to the 2024 review by changing what was sold, not by persuading consumers.\n• Assessment — the conclusion over-reaches: the failure of information does not establish that restriction is the right response, because intermediate instruments exist. Defaults, mandated choice, simplification and salience all outperform information while preserving choice, and the ladder of coerciveness should be climbed from the bottom.\n• Assessment — heterogeneous preferences: restriction imposes one outcome on everybody. Where preferences genuinely differ, the cost falls on those who would have chosen the restricted option for good reasons, and that cost is invisible in aggregate statistics.\n• Assessment — the internality-externality distinction: restriction is far easier to justify where the harm falls on third parties. Where the harm is to the chooser alone, the paternalism objection is at its strongest and the case must rest on the individual\'s own later regret.\n• Assessment — government failure and unintended consequences: restriction invites substitution into unregulated alternatives and illegal markets, requires enforcement, and can be captured by incumbents who benefit from the rules. It also removes the feedback that would reveal whether the policy was right.\n• Assessment — why information still has a role: it is a precondition for the market working at all, it is cheap, and it is the only instrument that improves the quality of choice rather than replacing it. Standardised unit pricing and traffic-light labelling change behaviour precisely because they reduce the effort of deciding rather than adding to it.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that the evidence justifies abandoning reliance on information alone, but not the leap to restriction; the proportionate response is to use the least coercive effective instrument, reserving restriction for cases where third-party harm is large or where the option has no legitimate use.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained assessment engaging with the intermediate options and the internality/externality distinction, with a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) both sides described; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 18-19) states that "the levy changed what manufacturers put in the bottle, while years of healthy-eating campaigns changed very little about what shoppers put in the basket".\n\nUsing the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that taxing producers is a more effective way of reducing sugar consumption than informing consumers.`,
        marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `• Set up the market failure: sugar-sweetened drinks are over-consumed because of negative externalities in healthcare costs and because consumers under-weight delayed private costs. Use an MPB/MSB diagram to identify the over-consumption and the welfare loss.\n• The mechanism of a producer levy: taxing the producer above a sugar threshold makes reformulation cheaper than paying the tax. This changes the product rather than the price, so it does not rely on any consumer noticing, deciding or caring.\n• Evidence from the extracts and the real world: the practice data show sales-weighted sugar content falling from 6.8g to 3.9g per 100ml, with the largest fall as the levy took effect and no comparable fall in untaxed confectionery. The UK Soft Drinks Industry Levy, in force since April 2018, produced a 46% average reduction in added sugar according to the 2024 government review; the threshold falls from 5g to 4.5g per 100ml on 1 January 2028.\n• Why information underperforms: the behavioural obstacles are present bias, inattention at the point of purchase and the effort of processing nutritional data. Information changes what people know, not the environment in which they decide.\n• Evaluation — the comparison may be unfair: the levy targets producers, who are few, informed, profit-maximising and highly responsive to a clear financial incentive. Information targets millions of consumers deciding in seconds. The success reflects the choice of target as much as the choice of instrument.\n• Evaluation — limits of the producer route: reformulation has a technical floor; substitution into untaxed products (confectionery, juices, milk-based drinks) offsets part of the gain, which is exactly why the exemption for milk-based drinks is being removed; and sweetener substitution has its own contested health effects.\n• Evaluation — equity and incidence: to the extent the levy is passed on, it falls more heavily on lower-income households, and it applies to all consumers including moderate ones. Information imposes no such cost and respects autonomy.\n• Evaluation — information is not a substitute but a complement: labelling and the levy work on different margins, and labelling also sharpened producers\' incentive to reformulate by making sugar content salient and comparable.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that producer-side taxation has been demonstrably more effective at reducing sugar in the products sold, because it acts on a small number of responsive decision-makers rather than on millions of inattentive ones, but that its success is bounded by substitution and reformulation limits, so the effective policy is a producer levy supported by labelling rather than either alone.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) extracts used throughout, diagram integrated, sustained evaluation with a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good use of extracts with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) some extract use, evaluation asserted; L2 (6-10) largely descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Discuss the view that the concept of utility is too subjective to be of any use in economic analysis.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `• The objection: utility cannot be observed, measured in a common unit or compared between people. "Utils" have no physical existence, and one person\'s satisfaction cannot be added to another\'s, so any analysis built on it is unfalsifiable.\n• Strength of the objection: it bites hardest on welfare economics. Judging that a policy raises total welfare requires interpersonal comparison of utility, which cannot be made scientifically — it is a value judgement wearing technical clothing.\n• Further support: behavioural evidence shows that measured valuation depends on framing, on the reference point and on the order of questions, so even an individual\'s own utility is not a stable quantity awaiting measurement.\n• Discussion — economics no longer needs cardinal utility: the theory was rebuilt on ordinal preference and then on revealed preference. Only rankings are required, and rankings are inferred from observed choices, so the unobservability of utils is not the obstacle the objection assumes.\n• Discussion — the concept earns its keep through its predictions: diminishing marginal utility yields the downward-sloping demand curve; the equi-marginal principle yields the response of consumption to relative prices; consumer surplus gives an operational welfare measure. These predictions are testable even though utility is not observable.\n• Discussion — unobservable concepts are normal in science: temperature was used long before it could be given a molecular account. A concept is judged by the strength of the framework built on it, not by whether it can be seen.\n• Discussion — practical proxies exist: willingness to pay, quality-adjusted life years and subjective wellbeing surveys all operationalise utility for policy. Each is imperfect — willingness to pay is bounded by ability to pay, which embeds a distributional judgement — but they are used and they inform real decisions.\n• Discussion — the honest limit: the framework is strongest for positive analysis of how choices respond to prices and incomes, and weakest where it is asked to deliver an aggregate welfare verdict, because that is where interpersonal comparison becomes unavoidable.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that utility is indispensable as an organising concept for positive analysis, where ordinal preference is sufficient and the predictions are testable, but that the subjectivity objection stands against its normative use — so the correct response is to be explicit about the value judgements in welfare analysis rather than to abandon the concept.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained discussion distinguishing positive from normative use, with a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) the objection described with limited discussion; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: `After considering Extract D, and the original evidence in Extracts A, B and C, would you recommend that the government of Country X use an automatic-enrolment default rather than a subsidy to raise saving among low-income workers? Justify your recommendation.\n\nExtract D — written for this practice question\n"A follow-up study of the default scheme found that 71% of automatically enrolled low-income workers were contributing at the statutory minimum two years later, that 24% of them simultaneously held consumer debt at annual interest rates above 30%, and that the previous subsidy scheme had cost £1.90 in public money for every £1.00 of additional saving it generated."`,
        marks: 25, command: 'Justify',
        markScheme: `• Restate the case for the default from the earlier evidence: the trial data show participation rising from 20.6% to 90.3% under automatic entry against 25.5% under information, and real-world automatic enrolment took participation among eligible employees to 90% by 2025. The instrument works on inertia, which is the binding constraint.\n• Cost comparison from Extract D: the subsidy cost £1.90 of public money per £1.00 of extra saving, implying substantial deadweight — much of the subsidy went to saving that would have happened anyway. The default costs the exchequer almost nothing, which is a strong argument in its favour.\n• What Extract D qualifies — anchoring at the minimum: 71% still contributing at the statutory minimum after two years shows the default sets the level as well as the participation decision. Breadth has been achieved; adequacy has not, and the default itself may be causing that.\n• What Extract D qualifies — debt: 24% of these workers hold debt at over 30% a year. For them, saving at a low return while borrowing at a high one is very plausibly a worse outcome than not saving, so a universal default may make a sizeable minority worse off on their own terms.\n• Weighing: the default is clearly the more cost-effective instrument for raising participation, but the subsidy operates on the amount saved rather than on the decision to save, so the two instruments address different margins. The evidence does not support a simple ranking.\n• The recommendation and its conditions: a defensible recommendation is yes to the default as the primary instrument, given its effectiveness and near-zero fiscal cost, but with two conditions — an escalating default contribution rate so the anchor rises over time, and a screening or signposting step so that workers holding high-cost debt are directed to repay it first rather than being silently enrolled.\n• Counter-argument to address: a subsidy is better targeted at the adequacy problem and is visible and politically defensible, whereas an escalating default risks raising opt-out rates among exactly the low-income group the policy is meant to help. A candidate who addresses this and still reaches a reasoned recommendation should be credited at the top.\n• Uncertainties in the evidence: the trial covers one employer; the £1.90 figure is a single estimate with no confidence interval; there is no evidence on whether default saving displaced other saving; and two years is too short to judge persistence.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) all four extracts used, evidence weighed against evidence, a clear recommendation justified with conditions and the main counter-argument addressed; L4 (16-20) extracts used well with a justified recommendation; L3 (11-15) some use of extracts, recommendation asserted; L2 (6-10) descriptive use of extracts; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` }
    ]
  },
  '3.1.3': {
    name: 'Price Determination',
    questions: [
      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nThe market for a branded breakfast cereal\n  Price per box:            2024 = £2.40 ; 2025 = £2.76\n  Boxes sold per week:      2024 = 50 000 ; 2025 = 45 500\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the price elasticity of demand for this cereal.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: percentage change in quantity demanded / percentage change in price.\nQuantity: (45 500 - 50 000) / 50 000 × 100 = -9%. Price: (2.76 - 2.40) / 2.40 × 100 = +15%.\nAnswer: -9 / 15 = -0.60.\n2 marks for -0.60; 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for 0.60 without the negative sign (the sign matters, since it shows the inverse relationship).` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nDemand for a own-label value range of tinned food\n  Average real household income:  rose by 4.5%\n  Quantity of the value range demanded: fell by 1.8%\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the income elasticity of demand for this value range.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: percentage change in quantity demanded / percentage change in income = -1.8 / 4.5.\nAnswer: -0.40.\n2 marks for -0.40; 1 mark for 0.40 without the negative sign or for the correct method with an arithmetic slip.\nInterpretation for later parts: a negative YED identifies the good as inferior, and a value between 0 and -1 means demand is income inelastic.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nTwo hot drinks sold in the same supermarkets\n  Price of Brand A tea:                   rose by 8.0%\n  Quantity of Brand B coffee demanded:    rose by 5.6%\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the cross elasticity of demand for Brand B coffee with respect to the price of Brand A tea.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: percentage change in quantity demanded of B / percentage change in the price of A = 5.6 / 8.0.\nAnswer: +0.70.\n2 marks for +0.70; 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for the inverted calculation 8.0/5.6 = 1.43.\nInterpretation: a positive cross elasticity identifies the two goods as substitutes, and a value below 1 means they are weak substitutes.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nThe market for greenhouse-grown tomatoes\n  Farm-gate price per kg:     before = £1.80 ; after = £2.07\n  Quantity supplied per week: before = 12 000 kg ; after = 13 440 kg\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the price elasticity of supply of these tomatoes.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: percentage change in quantity supplied / percentage change in price.\nQuantity: (13 440 - 12 000) / 12 000 × 100 = +12%. Price: (2.07 - 1.80) / 1.80 × 100 = +15%.\nAnswer: 12 / 15 = 0.80.\n2 marks for 0.80; 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for the inverted calculation 1.25.\nInterpretation: a value below 1 means supply is price inelastic, as is typical for an agricultural product in the short run.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nThe market for a branded breakfast cereal\n  Price per box:            2024 = £2.40 ; 2025 = £2.76\n  Boxes sold per week:      2024 = 50 000 ; 2025 = 45 500\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the percentage change in the producer's weekly total revenue between 2024 and 2025.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: total revenue = price × quantity. 2024: 50 000 × £2.40 = £120 000. 2025: 45 500 × £2.76 = £125 580.\nPercentage change: (125 580 - 120 000) / 120 000 × 100 = 5 580 / 120 000 × 100.\nAnswer: +4.65%.\n2 marks for +4.65%; 1 mark for both revenue figures correct but the percentage wrong, or for the correct method with an arithmetic slip.\nInterpretation: revenue rose when price rose, which is consistent with the price inelastic demand (PED = -0.60) calculated earlier.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nThe market for a soft drink before and after a specific indirect tax of £0.50 per litre\n  Price paid by consumers: before the tax = £2.40 ; after the tax = £2.75\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the percentage of the £0.50 tax that is borne by consumers.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: the consumer burden per litre is the rise in the price paid = 2.75 - 2.40 = £0.35. As a percentage of the tax: 0.35 / 0.50 × 100.\nAnswer: 70.00%.\n2 marks for 70.00%; 1 mark for identifying the consumer burden as £0.35 without converting it to a percentage, or for the correct method with an arithmetic slip.\nInterpretation: the producer bears the remaining £0.15, or 30.00%. The consumer bears the larger share because demand is less price elastic than supply.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nThe market for a branded breakfast cereal\n  Price per box:            2024 = £2.40 ; 2025 = £2.76\n  Boxes sold per week:      2024 = 50 000 ; 2025 = 45 500\n  Producer's weekly revenue: 2024 = £120 000 ; 2025 = £125 580\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that demand for this cereal is price inelastic.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Price rose by 15% while quantity demanded fell by only 9%, so the proportionate change in quantity is smaller than the proportionate change in price.\n• PED = -9/15 = -0.60. The absolute value lies between 0 and 1, which is the definition of price inelastic demand.\n• Corroboration from revenue: total revenue rose from £120 000 to £125 580, an increase of 4.65%. Revenue moving in the same direction as price is the revenue test for inelastic demand.\n• Explanation of why: the cereal is branded, so close substitutes exist but brand loyalty and habitual purchase weaken the response; the item is also a small share of the weekly shopping budget, which reduces the incentive to search.\nLevels: 4 = the elasticity calculated correctly AND the inelastic interpretation explained with the revenue evidence used; 3 = calculation with some explanation; 2 = the direction of the changes identified without calculation; 1 = a relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nSales of a supermarket's own-label value range\n  Average real household income:          rose 4.5%\n  Quantity of the value range demanded:   fell 1.8%\n  Quantity of the premium range demanded: rose 9.0%\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that the value range is an inferior good while the premium range is a normal good.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Value range: YED = -1.8 / 4.5 = -0.40. Demand falls as income rises, which is the definition of an inferior good.\n• Premium range: YED = +9.0 / 4.5 = +2.00. Demand rises more than proportionately with income, so it is a normal good and specifically a luxury (YED greater than 1).\n• Explanation: as income rises consumers substitute away from the value range towards better-quality alternatives, so the value range loses demand despite no change in its own price.\n• Business implication: the two ranges behave in opposite directions over the economic cycle, so stocking both stabilises the retailer\'s revenue when incomes change — the practical reason firms use YED.\nLevels: 4 = both elasticities calculated correctly AND the normal/inferior distinction explained; 3 = both calculated with some explanation; 2 = one calculated, or the distinction stated without calculation; 1 = a relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nEffect of an 8.0% rise in the price of Brand A tea\n  Quantity of Brand B coffee demanded:        rose 5.6%\n  Quantity of Brand A's own-brand biscuits demanded: fell 2.4%\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that Brand B coffee is a substitute for Brand A tea while the biscuits are a complement.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Coffee: XED = +5.6 / 8.0 = +0.70. A positive cross elasticity means the two goods move in opposite directions in quantity — consumers switch to coffee when tea becomes dearer — which defines substitutes.\n• Biscuits: XED = -2.4 / 8.0 = -0.30. A negative cross elasticity means the goods are bought together, so dearer tea reduces biscuit demand: they are complements.\n• Explanation of the magnitudes: both values are below 1 in absolute terms, so both relationships are weak. Tea and coffee are imperfect substitutes because many consumers have a settled preference.\n• Use: a firm uses XED to predict how a rival\'s price change will shift its own demand curve, and to decide whether to match a competitor\'s price cut.\nLevels: 4 = both elasticities calculated correctly AND the substitute/complement distinction explained using the sign; 3 = both calculated with some explanation; 2 = one calculated, or signs interpreted without calculation; 1 = a relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nThe market for unskilled labour in a region, weekly\n  At the free-market wage of £9.80 an hour:  hours demanded = 1 420 000 ; hours supplied = 1 420 000\n  At a legal minimum wage of £12.71 an hour: hours demanded = 1 302 000 ; hours supplied = 1 561 000\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that the minimum wage creates excess supply in this labour market.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• At the minimum wage the quantity of hours supplied (1 561 000) exceeds the quantity demanded (1 302 000), leaving an excess supply of 259 000 hours a week.\n• The minimum wage of £12.71 is set above the market-clearing wage of £9.80, so it is a binding price floor; the market cannot clear at that wage because the wage is not permitted to fall.\n• Explanation of the two responses: the higher wage raises the quantity of labour workers wish to supply (a movement along the supply curve) and reduces the quantity firms wish to hire, because labour is now more expensive relative to capital and to workers elsewhere.\n• Interpretation: this excess supply is the standard competitive-market prediction of unemployment among unskilled workers. The UK National Living Wage was £12.71 an hour from April 2026.\nLevels: 4 = the excess supply calculated AND both the floor and the two behavioural responses explained; 3 = the excess supply identified with some explanation; 2 = the figures compared with thin explanation; 1 = one relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nThe market for train travel on a route, averages per week\n  Period 1: fare = £24.00 ; journeys = 82 000 ; average petrol price = £1.42 a litre\n  Period 2: fare = £24.00 ; journeys = 91 000 ; average petrol price = £1.68 a litre\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that the rise in journeys was caused by a shift of the demand curve and not by a movement along it.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• The fare is unchanged at £24.00 in both periods, yet journeys rose from 82 000 to 91 000, an increase of 10.98%. A movement along a demand curve requires a change in the good\'s own price, and there was none.\n• The change must therefore come from a non-price determinant. The data identify one: the price of petrol, a substitute for rail travel, rose by 18.31% from £1.42 to £1.68.\n• Explanation: dearer motoring makes rail relatively cheaper, so at every fare more journeys are demanded. The whole demand curve shifts to the right.\n• Precision that earns full marks: the fare is a movement variable, the petrol price is a shift variable. Confusing the two is the most common error in this topic.\nLevels: 4 = the constant fare and the substitute price change both used precisely AND the shift/movement distinction explained; 3 = the data used with some explanation; 2 = the rise in journeys noted, explanation thin; 1 = one relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nThe market for a soft drink before and after a specific tax of £0.50 per litre\n  Price paid by consumers:  before = £2.40 ; after = £2.75\n  Price received by producers (net of tax): before = £2.40 ; after = £2.25\n  Litres sold per week:     before = 68 000 ; after = 61 200\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that the greater share of the tax burden fell on consumers.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• The consumer price rose by £0.35 (from £2.40 to £2.75) while the producer\'s net receipt fell by £0.15 (from £2.40 to £2.25). The two shares sum to the £0.50 tax.\n• As percentages: consumers bear 0.35/0.50 = 70.00% and producers 0.15/0.50 = 30.00%.\n• Explanation: the incidence falls more heavily on the side of the market that is less price elastic. Quantity fell by only 10% (68 000 to 61 200) for a 14.58% rise in the consumer price, so PED is about -0.69 — inelastic — and consumers absorb most of the tax rather than reducing purchases.\n• Implication: the tax raises revenue effectively (61 200 × £0.50 = £30 600 a week) but reduces consumption relatively little, which is the standard trade-off for taxes on inelastically demanded goods.\nLevels: 4 = both burdens calculated AND the elasticity explanation of the split given; 3 = burdens calculated with some explanation; 2 = one burden identified; 1 = a relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 5-6) states that "a run of poor harvests across southern Europe has left wholesalers scrambling for supply". With the help of a supply and demand diagram, explain the effect of this on the equilibrium price and quantity of fresh tomatoes in the UK.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: standard supply and demand axes, price and quantity. Supply shifts left from S1 to S2 with demand unchanged; equilibrium moves from P1Q1 to a higher P2 and lower Q2.\n• Chain of reasoning: poor harvests reduce the quantity available at every price, so the supply curve shifts left. At the old price P1 there is now excess demand — quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied — and competition between buyers bids the price up.\n• The rise in price rations the reduced supply to those who value it most, and simultaneously reduces quantity demanded along the demand curve until the market clears at P2Q2.\n• Magnitude depends on elasticity: demand for fresh produce is price inelastic in the short run because there are few close substitutes for a specific ingredient and it is a small part of the weekly budget. With steep demand, a given leftward supply shift produces a large price rise and a small quantity fall.\n• Development: supply is also very inelastic in the short run because the growing cycle is fixed, so the initial price spike is large. Over a longer period higher prices attract supply from other regions and from greenhouse production, so the supply curve shifts partly back and the price falls.\n• Signalling: the high price is not merely a symptom but a signal and incentive that draws resources into tomato growing, which is how the shortage is corrected without any central direction.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate labelled diagram with a leftward supply shift, developed reasoning on the adjustment to the new equilibrium, and elasticity used to explain the magnitude; L2 (4-6) diagram with some explanation; L1 (1-3) diagram missing or incorrect, or a shift of demand drawn instead.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 9-10) states that "the government has announced a specific duty of 50p a litre on sugary drinks". With the help of a supply and demand diagram, analyse how the burden of this tax will be shared between consumers and producers.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: supply shifts vertically upwards by the full 50p from S1 to S1 + tax. Mark the new consumer price P2, the producer net price P2 - tax, the old price P1, and shade the consumer burden (P2 - P1) × Q2 and the producer burden (P1 - net price) × Q2, plus government revenue as the whole rectangle.\n• Chain of reasoning: the tax raises the marginal cost of supplying each unit, so producers require a higher price to supply any given quantity. The market clears at a higher consumer price and a lower quantity.\n• The split: the consumer price rises by less than the full tax because the quantity falls, so producers absorb the remainder in a lower net receipt. The practice data give a 35p/15p split, or 70% to consumers.\n• The determining principle: the burden falls more heavily on whichever side of the market is less price elastic, because that side has fewer alternatives and so adjusts quantity least. With inelastic demand and elastic supply, consumers bear most of the tax.\n• Extreme cases that demonstrate the principle: with perfectly inelastic demand the consumer price rises by the full 50p and quantity is unchanged; with perfectly elastic demand the producer bears all of it.\n• Development: producers have a third option the simple diagram omits — reformulating the product to fall below the tax threshold. The UK Soft Drinks Industry Levy, in force since April 2018, was deliberately designed with a threshold for this reason, and a 2024 government review found a 46% average reduction in the sugar added to soft drinks.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram with the tax wedge and both burdens shaded, elasticity used to explain the split, and a developed point; L2 (4-6) diagram with some analysis, burdens not clearly separated; L1 (1-3) tax shown as a demand shift or diagram absent.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 13-14) states that "ministers are considering a per-unit subsidy for electric van purchases". With the help of a supply and demand diagram, explain the effect of such a subsidy on price, quantity and the cost to the taxpayer.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: supply shifts vertically downwards by the amount of the subsidy from S1 to S1 - subsidy. Mark the new lower consumer price P2, the higher price received by producers P2 + subsidy, the higher quantity Q2, and the total cost to government as subsidy × Q2.\n• Chain of reasoning: the subsidy reduces the effective marginal cost of supply, so producers will supply more at every price. The market clears at a lower price to consumers and a larger quantity.\n• Distribution of the benefit: the consumer price falls by less than the full subsidy, with the remainder captured by producers as a higher net receipt. The split again depends on relative elasticities — the more inelastic side captures more.\n• Taxpayer cost: the total outlay is the subsidy per unit times the new, larger quantity, which is shown as the rectangle between the two supply curves up to Q2. Because the quantity rises, the total cost exceeds the subsidy times the original quantity.\n• Efficiency justification: the subsidy is defensible where the good generates positive externalities, so that marginal social benefit exceeds marginal private benefit and the free market under-provides. The subsidy shifts output towards the social optimum.\n• Analytical qualification: part of the spending is deadweight, going to buyers who would have purchased anyway, and the effect on quantity is small if demand is price inelastic — in which case the subsidy mainly raises producer revenue rather than sales.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram with the subsidy wedge, both prices and the cost rectangle marked, and developed reasoning including the split of the benefit; L2 (4-6) diagram with some explanation; L1 (1-3) subsidy shown as a demand shift or diagram absent.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 17-18) states that "the regulator has capped the price landlords may charge for student accommodation in the city". With the help of a supply and demand diagram, analyse the likely effects of this maximum price.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: supply and demand for rented accommodation with a horizontal maximum price line drawn below the equilibrium. Mark quantity demanded Qd at the cap, quantity supplied Qs, and the excess demand Qd - Qs.\n• Chain of reasoning: a binding price ceiling prevents the price performing its rationing function. At the capped rent more students want accommodation than landlords are willing to supply, so there is a shortage.\n• Consequences of the shortage: some non-price rationing mechanism emerges — queues and waiting lists, first-come-first-served, landlord discretion in selecting tenants, larger deposits or informal side-payments. The gainers are those who secure a tenancy; the losers are those excluded entirely.\n• Supply-side effect over time: the price elasticity of supply of housing is very low in the short run because the stock is fixed, so the immediate shortage is modest. Over a longer period landlords convert properties to other uses or reduce maintenance, supply falls further and the shortage widens.\n• Quality margin: where price is controlled but quality is not, the adjustment occurs through quality. Landlords reduce maintenance and services until the value of what is offered matches the capped rent.\n• Evaluation point worth making: the policy transfers surplus from landlords to those tenants who obtain a property, but it does not raise the quantity of housing — indeed it reduces it — so it addresses affordability for some at the cost of availability for others. Supply-side measures such as planning reform address the underlying shortage more directly.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram with a binding cap below equilibrium and excess demand marked, developed analysis of non-price rationing and the supply response over time; L2 (4-6) diagram with some analysis; L1 (1-3) cap drawn above equilibrium or diagram absent.` },

      { q: 'With the help of a supply and demand diagram, explain what is meant by consumer surplus and producer surplus, and show how each changes when supply decreases.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: demand and supply curves meeting at P1Q1. Consumer surplus is the triangle between the demand curve and P1; producer surplus is the triangle between P1 and the supply curve. Then draw S2 to the left of S1, giving a higher P2 and lower Q2, and shade the new, smaller triangles.\n• Consumer surplus: the difference between the maximum price a consumer would have been willing to pay — read off the demand curve, which reflects marginal utility — and the price actually paid, summed across all units purchased.\n• Producer surplus: the difference between the price received and the minimum a producer would have accepted, read off the supply curve, which reflects marginal cost.\n• Effect of a fall in supply: price rises to P2 and quantity falls to Q2. Consumer surplus falls unambiguously, for two reasons — those still buying pay a higher price, and some consumers leave the market entirely.\n• Producer surplus is ambiguous: remaining producers gain from the higher price but lose the surplus on the units no longer sold. Whether producer surplus rises or falls depends on the price elasticity of demand — with inelastic demand the price gain dominates and producer surplus rises, which is why a poor harvest can raise farm incomes.\n• The welfare conclusion: the sum of the two surpluses falls, and the reduction that is not transferred between the parties is the welfare loss from the reduced quantity traded.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram with both surpluses correctly identified before and after, and the ambiguity of the producer surplus effect explained using elasticity; L2 (4-6) surpluses identified with some explanation of the change; L1 (1-3) definitions only or surpluses misidentified.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 21-22) states that "the agency buys grain when prices are low and sells from store when they are high". With the help of a supply and demand diagram, analyse how a buffer stock scheme is intended to stabilise the price of an agricultural commodity.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: demand curve with two supply curves, a good-harvest supply Sgood to the right and a poor-harvest supply Spoor to the left, plus horizontal lines showing the intervention price floor and ceiling between which the agency operates.\n• Chain of reasoning in a good harvest: supply shifts right and the free-market price would fall below the floor. The agency buys the excess supply — the horizontal distance between the floor price quantity demanded and quantity supplied — adding to the stock and holding the price at the floor.\n• Chain of reasoning in a poor harvest: supply shifts left and the price would rise above the ceiling. The agency releases stock, which shifts effective supply right and holds the price at the ceiling.\n• Why agricultural markets need it: supply is price inelastic in the short run because the growing cycle is fixed, and demand is price inelastic because food has few substitutes and is a small budget share. Two steep curves mean small supply shocks produce very large price swings and volatile farm incomes.\n• Analytical conditions for success: the scheme requires the intervention price band to be set close to the long-run average market price, sufficient finance and storage capacity, and the commodity to be storable at reasonable cost.\n• Why schemes fail in practice: a floor set above the long-run average generates persistent surpluses, ever-rising storage costs and eventual collapse; stocks deteriorate; and a price floor encourages more planting, worsening the surplus it was meant to absorb.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram showing both interventions, developed analysis linking inelasticity to volatility, and the conditions for success discussed; L2 (4-6) mechanism described with some analysis; L1 (1-3) description of buying and selling with no diagram or analysis.` },

      { q: 'Analyse the relationship between the price elasticity of demand for a firm\'s product and the effect of a price change on its total revenue.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `• The relationship: total revenue is price times quantity, and a price change moves the two in opposite directions. Which effect dominates is determined by PED.\n• Inelastic demand (PED between 0 and -1): the proportionate fall in quantity is smaller than the proportionate rise in price, so revenue rises when price rises. The practice data show this — a 15% price rise and a 9% quantity fall raised revenue by 4.65%.\n• Elastic demand (PED more negative than -1): the quantity effect dominates, so a price rise reduces revenue and a price cut raises it. This is why a firm facing many close substitutes cannot raise price without losing revenue.\n• Unit elasticity (PED = -1): the two effects exactly offset and revenue is unchanged. Revenue is therefore maximised where PED = -1, which on a straight-line demand curve is the midpoint.\n• Analysis of the practical complication: PED is not constant along a straight-line demand curve — it is elastic at high prices and inelastic at low ones — so a firm cannot apply a single estimate across a large price change.\n• Further analysis: revenue is not profit. A price cut that raises revenue also raises output and therefore total cost, so the profit-maximising price is not the revenue-maximising one unless marginal cost is zero. This is why digital firms with near-zero marginal cost behave as if maximising revenue.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) all three elasticity cases analysed with a numerical or diagrammatic chain of reasoning and a developed qualification; L2 (4-6) the cases stated with some analysis; L1 (1-3) assertion of the relationship with no reasoning.` },

      { q: 'With the help of a supply and demand diagram, explain why the equilibrium price and quantity change when both demand and supply increase at the same time.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: initial equilibrium at P1Q1. Shift demand right to D2 and supply right to S2; show the new equilibrium at Q2 greater than Q1 with the new price drawn in two alternative positions to make the ambiguity explicit.\n• Quantity is unambiguous: both shifts raise the quantity traded — higher demand raises the quantity bought at every price and higher supply raises the quantity offered at every price — so Q rises.\n• Price is ambiguous: an increase in demand alone raises price; an increase in supply alone lowers it. The net effect depends on which shift is larger.\n• If the demand shift dominates, price rises; if the supply shift dominates, price falls; if they are equal in magnitude, price is unchanged and only quantity rises.\n• Worked application: rising real incomes raise demand for electric vehicles while new production capacity raises supply. Observed prices have fallen where capacity growth outran demand growth, and risen where it did not — the same model explains both.\n• Analytical point that earns L3: this is why an observed price change cannot by itself identify which curve moved. Separating shifts requires quantity data as well, which is the practical reason economists need both series before drawing conclusions.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram showing both shifts and the alternative price outcomes, with the unambiguous/ambiguous distinction clearly explained; L2 (4-6) both shifts drawn with partial explanation; L1 (1-3) one shift only, or an asserted outcome.` },

      { q: 'Analyse the main reasons why the price elasticity of demand for a good is likely to be higher in the long run than in the short run.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `• The general principle: elasticity measures responsiveness, and responsiveness requires the opportunity to adjust. Time is what creates that opportunity, so demand becomes more elastic as the period lengthens.\n• Substitutes take time to find and adopt: after a rise in the petrol price a motorist cannot immediately change car, job or home, so short-run demand is very inelastic. Over years, vehicles are replaced with more efficient or electric models and journeys are reorganised, so the response grows.\n• Habits and information: consumption patterns are habitual and searching for alternatives is costly, so a price change is initially absorbed. As the higher price persists it becomes salient and consumers invest the effort in switching.\n• Durable complementary capital: demand for fuel is derived from a stock of vehicles and boilers that only turns over slowly. The long-run response is embedded in replacement decisions, so it is realised over the life of the capital stock.\n• Contractual and technological lock-in: fixed-term contracts, tariffs and installed technology prevent immediate switching, and these constraints expire over time.\n• Implication for policy and for firms: a tax on an inelastically demanded good raises substantial revenue and cuts consumption little at first, but its behavioural effect grows over time — which is why the short-run and long-run assessments of an environmental tax can differ sharply.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) several reasons analysed with developed chains of reasoning and an implication drawn; L2 (4-6) reasons stated with some analysis; L1 (1-3) a list of factors affecting PED with no time dimension.` },

      { q: 'Analyse how the price mechanism performs its signalling, incentive and rationing functions when demand for a good increases.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `• Set-up: an increase in demand shifts the demand curve right, creating excess demand at the original price, which bids the price up to a new equilibrium.\n• Signalling: the higher price transmits information. Producers do not need to know why demand rose — only that the price has risen — and consumers learn that the good has become relatively scarce. Hayek\'s point is that the price economises on the information anyone needs to hold.\n• Incentive: the higher price raises profitability, so existing firms expand output along the supply curve and new firms enter over time. The incentive function converts the signal into a change in behaviour.\n• Rationing: in the short run, before supply can expand, the higher price allocates the existing quantity to those who value it most, eliminating the excess demand by moving buyers along the demand curve.\n• Analysis of the sequence: the three functions operate over different horizons — rationing is immediate, signalling is simultaneous, and the incentive response takes as long as the price elasticity of supply allows. This is why prices overshoot in markets with inelastic short-run supply and then fall back.\n• Where the mechanism fails: the signal is distorted by externalities, so price does not reflect social cost; rationing by ability to pay may be judged inequitable for necessities; and price controls block the signal entirely, which is why they create shortages.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) all three functions analysed in sequence with a developed chain and a limitation raised; L2 (4-6) the functions described with some analysis; L1 (1-3) the functions listed.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) and Extract B — written for this practice question\nThe market for rental accommodation in City X\n                                    2023      2025\n  Average monthly rent              £940      £1 186\n  Rental properties available       41 200    38 700\n  Households in the city            402 000   428 000\n  Average household income          £34 100   £36 900\n  New dwellings completed in year   1 910     1 240\n\nExtract B states: "Planning approvals in the city fell by a third after the new green-belt designation took effect in 2023."\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data suggest that the rise in rents was caused mainly by a fall in supply rather than by a rise in demand?`,
        marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `• Establish the size of the change: rents rose by (1186 - 940)/940 × 100 = 26.17% over two years.\n• Evidence for a supply cause: the number of rental properties fell by 2 500, or 6.07%, and completions fell by 35.08% from 1 910 to 1 240. Extract B supplies a credible mechanism — a planning restriction — which is exogenous to the rent rise and so a plausible cause rather than a consequence.\n• Evidence for a demand cause: households rose by 26 000, or 6.47%, and average incomes by 8.21%. Housing is a normal good, so both raise demand at every rent. The percentage rise in households actually slightly exceeds the percentage fall in supply.\n• The decisive diagnostic: quantity. If supply alone had fallen, the quantity traded would fall; if demand alone had risen, it would rise. Here properties available fell while the number of households rose, which is consistent with both curves moving — supply left and demand right — and rules out a single-cause explanation.\n• Limitations: the data give stock figures, not the full supply and demand schedules, so no elasticities can be computed and the relative size of the two shifts cannot be established. "Properties available" is an equilibrium quantity, not the supply curve. No data are given on rents elsewhere, so a city-specific cause cannot be separated from a national one.\n• Judgement: the data show both shifts occurring and are therefore consistent with the supply explanation but do not establish that it was the main cause. Extract B strengthens the supply case by identifying a specific exogenous restriction, but with demand growing at a similar percentage rate the honest conclusion is that both contributed and the data cannot rank them.\nLevels: L3 (8-10) calculations used precisely, the quantity diagnostic applied correctly, limitations discussed and a supported judgement reached; L2 (4-7) relevant data used with some evaluation; L1 (1-3) data described without judgement.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — written for this practice question\nThe market for a sugary drink in Country X, before and after a 50p per litre levy\n                              Year before   Year after\n  Average price per litre        £2.40        £2.73\n  Litres sold (millions)         412          369\n  Average household income       £31 200      £32 400\n  Price of bottled water per litre £0.64       £0.61\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data suggest that demand for this drink is price inelastic?`,
        marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `• The headline calculation: price rose by (2.73 - 2.40)/2.40 × 100 = 13.75%; quantity fell by (369 - 412)/412 × 100 = -10.44%. Implied PED = -10.44/13.75 = -0.76, which lies between 0 and -1 and so indicates price inelastic demand.\n• Corroboration: total revenue rose from 412m × £2.40 = £988.8m to 369m × £2.73 = £1 007.4m, an increase of 1.88%. Revenue moving with price is the revenue test for inelastic demand.\n• First limitation — ceteris paribus fails: income rose by 3.85% over the same period, which would raise demand for a normal good and therefore offsets part of the price effect. The true PED is more elastic than -0.76 if the drink is normal.\n• Second limitation — a substitute\'s price moved: bottled water fell from £0.64 to £0.61, a 4.69% fall, which would reduce demand for the drink. This works in the opposite direction and makes the measured response overstate the pure price effect.\n• Third limitation — time period: one year is short. Consumers adjust habits and firms reformulate over longer periods, so long-run PED is likely to be considerably more elastic than this estimate.\n• Fourth limitation — the price change is not pure: part of the price rise is the levy, and producers may have reformulated the product at the same time, so the good being bought after is not identical to the good bought before.\n• Judgement: the data are consistent with price inelastic demand and the revenue test supports it, but the estimate is contaminated by simultaneous changes in income and in a substitute\'s price, so it should be treated as a rough indication rather than a reliable elasticity. The direction of the two contaminating effects is opposite, which limits how far the bias can be signed.\nLevels: L3 (8-10) PED calculated accurately, the revenue test applied, at least two limitations developed and a supported judgement; L2 (4-7) PED calculated with some evaluation; L1 (1-3) description of the data only.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main determinants of the price elasticity of demand for a good.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Availability and closeness of substitutes: the single most important determinant. A branded cereal has many close substitutes and elastic demand; cereal as a category has few, so demand is inelastic. This also explains why a narrowly defined market always has more elastic demand than a broad one.\n• Proportion of income spent on the good: a small budget share means a price change has little effect on real purchasing power and the effort of searching for alternatives is not worth making, so demand is inelastic. Salt and newspapers are inelastic for this reason; holidays and cars are not.\n• Necessity or luxury: goods that satisfy a basic need have inelastic demand because consumption cannot easily be postponed; discretionary purchases can be delayed indefinitely.\n• Addiction, habit and brand loyalty: these narrow the effective set of substitutes for the individual consumer, so demand for tobacco and for established brands is markedly less elastic than product characteristics alone would suggest.\n• Time period: demand becomes more elastic as time passes, because substitutes must be found, habits changed and complementary durable capital replaced. Short-run demand for petrol is very inelastic; long-run demand much less so.\n• Whether the good is a complement in a larger purchase: printer ink and spare parts have inelastic demand because the consumer is locked in by a prior investment, which firms exploit through the razor-and-blades pricing model.\n• Definition of the market and the share of the total cost: an input that is a tiny share of a producer\'s total cost has inelastic derived demand, since even a large percentage price change barely affects the final product\'s cost.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several determinants explained with developed reasoning and accurate examples, and the point about market definition or time made clearly; L2 (6-10) determinants listed with some explanation; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main determinants of the price elasticity of supply of a good.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Time period: the dominant determinant. In the momentary period output is fixed and supply is perfectly inelastic; in the short run only variable factors can be changed, so supply is inelastic; in the long run all factors including plant and capacity are variable, so supply is far more elastic.\n• Spare capacity: a firm operating well below capacity can raise output quickly at little extra unit cost, so supply is elastic. A firm at full capacity cannot, so supply is close to vertical until new capacity is installed.\n• Availability and mobility of factors: supply is elastic where additional labour, materials and capital can be hired quickly. Shortages of specialist skills or of a key raw material make supply inelastic regardless of price.\n• Ease and cost of storing stock: a storable good allows producers to release inventory when prices rise, which raises the elasticity of supply. Perishable and non-storable goods — fresh produce, hotel rooms, live performances — have very inelastic supply.\n• Length of the production process: goods requiring a long lead time, such as crops with an annual cycle, aircraft or buildings, cannot respond within the period, which is why agricultural supply is inelastic in the short run and much more elastic over several seasons.\n• Barriers to entry and exit: where new firms can enter quickly, industry supply is more elastic. Licensing, planning restrictions and large sunk costs reduce it.\n• Factor substitutability and the nature of the cost curve: supply is more elastic where inputs can be substituted for one another, and where marginal cost rises slowly with output rather than sharply.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several determinants explained with developed reasoning and examples, with the central role of time made explicit; L2 (6-10) determinants listed with some explanation; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main causes of a shift in the demand curve for a normal good.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Framing point that must come first: a shift is caused by a change in any determinant of demand other than the good\'s own price. A change in the good\'s own price causes a movement along the curve. Confusing the two is the central error in this topic.\n• Real income: for a normal good demand rises with income, and the size of the shift depends on the income elasticity of demand. Luxuries with YED above 1 shift proportionately more than income changes.\n• Price of substitutes: a rise in the price of a substitute shifts demand right, since consumers switch in. The practice data show rail journeys rising by 10.98% at an unchanged fare after an 18.31% rise in the petrol price.\n• Price of complements: a fall in the price of a complement shifts demand right, because the two are consumed together and the total cost of the package falls.\n• Tastes, preferences and advertising: changing fashion, health information and successful advertising shift demand and can also make it less price elastic by strengthening brand loyalty.\n• Population and its composition: more households, or a change in the age structure, shifts market demand even with individual demand unchanged.\n• Expectations of future prices: an expectation that prices will rise brings purchases forward, shifting current demand right — significant in housing and in commodity markets.\n• Interest rates and credit availability: for goods usually bought on credit, cheaper borrowing lowers the effective cost and shifts demand right.\n• Government policy: subsidies, taxes on substitutes and regulation all shift demand for the affected good.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several causes explained with the mechanism in each case and the shift/movement distinction made explicit; L2 (6-10) causes listed with some explanation; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main causes of price volatility in markets for primary commodities.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• The underlying structural cause: both demand and supply are price inelastic. Two steep curves mean any shift in either produces a large price movement and only a small quantity change, so volatility is built into the market structure rather than being an aberration.\n• Why supply is inelastic: output is determined by planting or extraction decisions taken long in advance, production cycles are long, and capacity cannot be adjusted within the period. For crops the output is then subject to weather, disease and pests that no producer controls.\n• Why demand is inelastic: food and energy are necessities with few close substitutes in aggregate, and in high-income countries they are a modest share of the budget, so quantity demanded barely responds to price.\n• Supply shocks: weather events, disease outbreaks, and geopolitical disruption to extraction or shipping shift supply sharply and unpredictably.\n• The cobweb effect: producers base planting decisions on the current price rather than the price they will receive. A high price this season triggers over-planting, a glut and a low price next season, which triggers under-planting — generating oscillation without any external shock.\n• Speculation and storage: storable commodities attract financial and speculative demand. Speculation can stabilise prices by moving supply from abundant to scarce periods, but can also amplify swings when positions are taken on expected momentum.\n• Income effects on producers: because demand is inelastic, a good harvest reduces farm revenue and a bad harvest raises it — the paradox that producers can be collectively worse off when output rises, which drives the political demand for intervention.\n• Consequences and responses: volatile revenues deter investment and make planning difficult, which is the standard justification for buffer stocks, minimum guaranteed prices, futures and forward contracts, and diversification.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) the inelasticity explanation developed with mechanisms and at least one amplifying factor such as the cobweb effect explained; L2 (6-10) causes listed with some explanation; L1 (1-5) assertion that harvests vary.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main effects of a specific indirect tax on a market, and the main determinants of who bears the burden.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• The mechanism: a specific tax of a fixed amount per unit shifts the supply curve vertically upwards by the amount of the tax, because producers require that much more per unit to supply any given quantity. An ad valorem tax pivots the supply curve instead, since the absolute amount rises with price.\n• Effects on price and quantity: the consumer price rises and the quantity traded falls. The consumer price rises by less than the full tax, and the producer\'s net receipt falls by the remainder.\n• Government revenue: the tax times the new quantity, shown as the rectangle between the two supply curves up to the post-tax quantity. Revenue is highest where demand is inelastic, because quantity falls least.\n• Welfare effect: the fall in quantity below the free-market level creates a deadweight loss — the triangle representing transactions that would have been mutually beneficial but no longer occur. Where the tax corrects a negative externality, this is offset by the welfare gain from reducing over-consumption.\n• Determinant of incidence — relative elasticity: the burden falls more heavily on whichever side of the market is less price elastic, because that side adjusts quantity least and therefore has least ability to avoid the tax. With inelastic demand and elastic supply, consumers bear most of it.\n• Extreme cases: perfectly inelastic demand means the consumer price rises by the full tax; perfectly elastic demand means the producer bears all of it. These bound the possibilities.\n• Other determinants: the degree of competition in the market, whether producers can reformulate the product to escape the tax, whether close untaxed substitutes exist, and the time period — incidence shifts towards producers as demand becomes more elastic over time.\n• Application: the UK Soft Drinks Industry Levy, introduced in April 2018, is levied on producers above a sugar threshold, so the dominant response was reformulation rather than a price rise; the 2024 government review found a 46% average reduction in the sugar added to soft drinks.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) effects explained with a diagram-based chain and the elasticity determinant of incidence developed with cases; L2 (6-10) effects described with some explanation of incidence; L1 (1-5) assertion that a tax raises price.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main ways in which a government can intervene in a market to change the price of a good, and the intended effect of each.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Indirect taxation: shifts supply left, raising price and reducing quantity. Intended where the good is over-consumed because of negative externalities or because consumers under-weight future private costs.\n• Subsidy: shifts supply right, lowering price and raising quantity. Intended where positive externalities mean the market under-provides, or to support producers\' incomes.\n• Maximum price (price ceiling): set below equilibrium to make a necessity affordable. It creates excess demand, so some non-price rationing mechanism — queues, waiting lists, allocation rules — must emerge, and it can reduce supply and quality over time.\n• Minimum price (price floor): set above equilibrium to support producer incomes or to discourage consumption. It creates excess supply, which must be bought up, stored, destroyed or exported if the floor is to hold. The UK National Living Wage, £12.71 an hour from April 2026, is a price floor in the labour market.\n• Buffer stock schemes: combine a floor and a ceiling, with an agency buying into store when prices are low and releasing stock when they are high. Intended to stabilise both prices and producer incomes where supply is volatile.\n• State provision and direct pricing: supplying the good free at the point of use or at a regulated price, used where the good is a public good or a strongly merit good.\n• Regulation and information: minimum standards, licensing, advertising restrictions and mandatory labelling change demand or supply without setting a price directly.\n• The organising judgement: the instrument should match the failure — a tax or subsidy where the problem is a price that does not reflect social cost, a price control where the problem is affordability or income, and information or regulation where the problem is knowledge. Each carries a risk of government failure through unintended consequences, administrative cost and distributional side-effects.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several instruments explained with the mechanism and intended effect of each, and the matching of instrument to failure made explicit; L2 (6-10) instruments described with some explanation; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: 'Assess the effectiveness of maximum prices as a means of helping low-income consumers.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `• The mechanism: a ceiling set below the equilibrium price on a necessity — rent, energy, a staple food — lowers the price for those who obtain the good. Use a diagram showing the cap below equilibrium, quantity demanded Qd, quantity supplied Qs and the excess demand.\n• The case in favour: for consumers who secure the good, real income rises and consumer surplus is transferred from producers. Where the seller has market power, a well-set cap can raise both quantity and welfare, because the monopolist was restricting output — so the standard shortage result does not automatically apply.\n• Speed and political visibility: a cap acts immediately and requires no fiscal outlay, which matters when a price shock is sudden and large.\n• Assessment — the shortage: with a competitive supply curve, a binding cap produces excess demand. Some consumers who would have obtained the good at the market price now get nothing, so the policy helps one group of low-income consumers at the expense of another.\n• Assessment — non-price rationing: with price prevented from rationing, allocation occurs by queueing, waiting lists, seller discretion or informal payments. These mechanisms frequently favour the better-informed and better-connected, so the distributional effect is not reliably progressive.\n• Assessment — the supply response over time: price elasticity of supply rises with time. A cap that produces a small shortage initially produces a larger one as producers exit, reduce maintenance or convert to unregulated uses — the standard finding on long-run rent control.\n• Assessment — quality and the hidden margin: where price is capped but quality is not, adjustment occurs through quality. The consumer pays the capped price for a worse good, so the real gain is smaller than the nominal one.\n• Assessment — better-targeted alternatives: a cash transfer or targeted subsidy raises the purchasing power of the intended group without distorting the price signal, avoids the shortage, and reaches only those who need it. Its costs are fiscal and administrative, and take-up is imperfect.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that maximum prices are effective only in narrow circumstances — where supply is very inelastic in the short run, where the seller has market power, or as a temporary response to a sudden shock — and that as a general instrument for helping low-income consumers they are inferior to income transfers, because they create shortages, ration by non-price means and degrade quality over exactly the horizon on which the poorest are least able to adapt.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained assessment with the diagram integrated, the monopoly case and the time dimension both handled, and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) mechanism explained with limited assessment; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that minimum prices are an effective way of supporting the incomes of agricultural producers.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `• The problem being addressed: agricultural supply and demand are both price inelastic, so harvest variation produces large price swings and volatile incomes. Because demand is inelastic, a good harvest actually reduces total farm revenue — the paradox that motivates intervention.\n• The mechanism: a floor set above the equilibrium guarantees a minimum revenue per unit. Use a diagram showing the floor, Qd, Qs and the excess supply that must be dealt with.\n• The case in favour: the floor raises and stabilises revenue for the units sold, which supports investment by making returns predictable, and it can be justified on the grounds of food security and the preservation of rural communities — arguments outside the efficiency framework.\n• Evaluation — the surplus: at the floor, quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded. The surplus must be purchased and stored, destroyed, exported or converted, and each option carries a cost. Persistent surpluses were the defining failure of guaranteed-price agricultural support.\n• Evaluation — the incentive problem compounds it: a guaranteed price encourages more planting, which enlarges the surplus the scheme must absorb, so the cost of the policy grows over time rather than stabilising.\n• Evaluation — who pays and who gains: consumers pay a higher price for food, which is regressive since food is a larger share of low-income budgets; taxpayers fund the intervention buying. The gains accrue in proportion to output, so most of the support goes to the largest producers rather than to the smallest farms the policy is usually justified by.\n• Evaluation — allocative efficiency: resources are retained in production that the market values below its cost, so output is above the efficient level and there is a welfare loss. Prices no longer signal relative scarcity, so the adjustment the market would have produced is blocked.\n• Evaluation — alternatives: buffer stocks stabilise without permanently raising the price and can be self-financing if the band is set at the long-run average; direct income payments decoupled from output support farmers without distorting production; futures contracts and insurance let farmers hedge volatility privately.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that minimum prices reliably raise farm revenue in the short term but are a poor instrument overall — they are expensive, regressive, badly targeted and self-aggravating through the supply response. If the objective is income stability rather than a permanently higher price, a buffer stock or decoupled income support achieves it at lower cost, which is why support systems have moved in that direction.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained evaluation with the diagram integrated, the surplus and targeting problems developed, alternatives considered and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) mechanism explained with limited evaluation; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the usefulness of price elasticity of demand estimates to a firm setting its prices.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `• The core use: PED determines the effect of a price change on total revenue. Where demand is inelastic a price rise raises revenue; where it is elastic a price cut does. A firm that misjudges this can destroy revenue with a price change intended to increase it.\n• Worked illustration: the practice data show a 15% price rise and a 9% quantity fall, giving PED = -0.60 and a 4.65% rise in revenue — the firm was right to raise price if its objective was revenue.\n• Further uses: PED guides price discrimination, since separating a market into a more elastic and a less elastic segment and charging each differently raises revenue; it informs how much of a cost increase can be passed on; and it indicates the firm\'s degree of market power, since inelastic demand implies weak substitutes.\n• Assessment — estimation problems: PED is estimated from past data in which other things were not equal. The practice data on sugary drinks show income and a substitute\'s price moving at the same time, so the measured value of -0.76 confounds three separate effects.\n• Assessment — PED is not constant: along a straight-line demand curve elasticity varies from elastic at high prices to inelastic at low ones, so a single estimate is valid only for small changes near the observed price. Extrapolating it to a large price change is unsafe.\n• Assessment — it changes over time: short-run demand is far less elastic than long-run demand, so a price rise that raises revenue this year may lose customers permanently as they find substitutes. The relevant elasticity depends on the firm\'s planning horizon.\n• Assessment — competitor reaction is omitted: PED as conventionally measured holds rivals\' prices constant. In an oligopoly a price cut will be matched, so the demand curve the firm actually faces is different from the one estimated. Cross elasticity and game-theoretic reasoning are needed as well.\n• Assessment — revenue is not profit: the profit-maximising price depends on marginal cost as well as on demand. Maximising revenue is only equivalent to maximising profit when marginal cost is zero, which is why the two objectives diverge for most firms.\n• Assessment — other objectives and behavioural pricing: firms pursue market share, satisficing or long-run positioning, and consumers respond to reference prices, anchoring and charm pricing in ways a single elasticity cannot capture.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that PED is indispensable as a directional guide — no firm should change price without knowing whether demand is elastic — but that it is unreliable as a precise instrument, because estimates are contaminated, unstable over time and silent about costs and rivals. Its usefulness is greatest for small changes in stable markets and least where rivals react or the change is large.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained assessment with calculation used, several distinct limitations developed and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) uses described with limited assessment; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 20-21) states that "the levy raised the shelf price of sugary drinks by less than the tax itself, but cut the sugar in the bottle by nearly half".\n\nUsing the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that indirect taxes are the most effective way of reducing consumption of a demerit good.`,
        marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `• Set up the failure: a demerit good is over-consumed because marginal social benefit lies below marginal private benefit, or marginal social cost above marginal private cost. Use an externality diagram to identify the over-consumption and the welfare loss.\n• The mechanism of the tax: shifting supply left raises price and cuts quantity towards the social optimum, internalising the external cost. Revenue can be hypothecated to treatment or to mitigation.\n• Use the extract: the shelf price rose by less than the 50p tax — the practice data show a 35p/15p split, so 70% fell on consumers — and quantity fell by 10.44%. The larger effect came through reformulation, exactly as the UK Soft Drinks Industry Levy was designed to produce; a 2024 government review found a 46% average reduction in the sugar added to soft drinks.\n• Evaluation — elasticity limits the consumption effect: where demand is inelastic, as it is here at about -0.76, a tax reduces quantity little and functions largely as a revenue-raiser and a transfer. For strongly addictive goods the quantity effect is smaller still.\n• Evaluation — regressive incidence: indirect taxes take a larger share of income from poorer households, and the burden falls on moderate consumers who impose little external cost as well as on heavy ones. The welfare gain is concentrated but the cost is spread.\n• Evaluation — setting the rate: internalising an externality requires knowing its monetary value. That value is contested and hard to measure, so the tax is set by judgement and may be too high or too low — a source of government failure.\n• Evaluation — substitution and avoidance: consumers switch to untaxed substitutes, which is why the UK levy\'s exemption for milk-based drinks is being removed and its threshold falls from 5g to 4.5g per 100ml on 1 January 2028. Cross-border shopping and illicit markets reduce effectiveness where the tax is high and the good is portable.\n• Evaluation — the alternatives: regulation and bans act regardless of price but restrict choice and invite substitution; information and labelling respect autonomy but have small effects; nudges are cheap and preserve choice — the 10p carrier bag charge in England, extended to all retailers in May 2021, cut bag sales at the main retailers by over 95%, far more than the price alone explains; minimum unit pricing targets the cheapest, heaviest-consumed products specifically.\n• Evaluation — the key insight from the extract: the levy worked best where it acted on producers rather than on consumers, because producers are few, informed and highly responsive to a clear financial incentive. That suggests the instrument\'s design matters more than its category.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that taxation is the most effective single instrument where demand is reasonably elastic or where the tax is designed to change what producers supply, but that it is neither sufficient nor clearly best where demand is inelastic and substitutes are untaxed — so the strongest policies combine a well-designed producer-facing tax with labelling and restrictions rather than relying on the tax alone.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) extracts used throughout, diagram integrated, sustained evaluation of alternatives and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good use of extracts with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) some extract use, evaluation asserted; L2 (6-10) largely descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Discuss the view that the price mechanism, left to itself, always allocates resources in a way that maximises welfare.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `• The case: price performs three functions simultaneously — signalling relative scarcity, giving producers an incentive to respond, and rationing what exists to those who value it most. No central authority could assemble the information the price summarises.\n• The efficiency result: in a competitive market, equilibrium occurs where marginal private benefit equals marginal private cost, so the sum of consumer and producer surplus is maximised. Show this on a diagram and identify the welfare triangle.\n• Dynamic argument: the profit signal draws resources into expanding sectors and out of declining ones without any instruction, and rewards innovation, so the allocation improves over time as well as being efficient at a point in time.\n• Discussion — externalities: where production or consumption imposes costs on third parties, marginal social cost exceeds marginal private cost and the market over-produces. The price signal is accurate about private costs and wrong about social ones, so the efficiency result fails.\n• Discussion — public goods: non-rival and non-excludable goods cannot be sold profitably because of free-riding, so the market provides too little or none at all, regardless of how much people value them.\n• Discussion — information failure: the efficiency result assumes buyers know what they are buying. Asymmetric information causes adverse selection, and imperfect information causes merit goods to be under-consumed; in both cases the demand curve does not represent true benefit.\n• Discussion — market power: where a firm faces a downward-sloping demand curve it restricts output to raise price, so price exceeds marginal cost and allocative efficiency fails by construction.\n• Discussion — equity: even a perfectly efficient allocation distributes goods by ability to pay. An outcome can maximise the sum of surpluses and still leave people without necessities, and no efficiency criterion can say whether that is acceptable — the judgement is normative.\n• Discussion — the counterfactual: none of this establishes that intervention improves matters. Government failure through imperfect information, unintended consequences, administrative cost and regulatory capture is real, so the comparison is between an imperfect market and an imperfect corrective.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that the price mechanism maximises welfare only under conditions — competition, full information, no externalities and an accepted distribution of income — that hold approximately in many markets and not at all in others. "Always" is therefore indefensible, but the correct inference is targeted intervention where a specific failure is identified and large, not general distrust of markets.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained discussion covering several failures with diagrams and a supported judgement that addresses government failure; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) failures described with limited discussion; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that a national minimum wage inevitably causes unemployment among low-paid workers.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `• The competitive prediction: a wage floor above the market-clearing wage creates excess supply of labour. Use a labour market diagram with the minimum wage above equilibrium; the practice data show hours demanded falling to 1 302 000 and hours supplied rising to 1 561 000, an excess supply of 259 000 hours.\n• Why firms reduce demand: labour demand is derived from the marginal revenue product of labour. A wage above MRP makes the marginal worker unprofitable, and higher relative labour costs encourage substitution towards capital.\n• Assessment — the monopsony case: where a single or dominant employer faces an upward-sloping labour supply curve, it restricts employment below the competitive level to hold the wage down. A minimum wage set between the monopsony wage and the competitive wage raises both the wage and employment. This is not a special case — local labour markets for care, retail and hospitality plausibly have monopsony features.\n• Assessment — elasticity of labour demand: the size of any employment effect depends on how responsive demand is. Where labour is a small share of total costs, where substitution towards capital is hard, and where the product\'s demand is price inelastic, employment falls little.\n• Assessment — margins of adjustment other than employment: firms can respond by reducing hours, cutting non-wage benefits, raising prices, accepting lower profit margins, or raising productivity through better training, lower turnover and improved management — the efficiency wage argument. Each of these absorbs the cost without job losses.\n• Assessment — the empirical record: the UK National Living Wage has risen substantially in real terms, reaching £12.71 an hour in April 2026, without the large employment losses the simple competitive model predicted. Independent review of the rate before each uprating is designed precisely to test for that effect.\n• Assessment — distributional and macroeconomic effects: a higher wage raises incomes at the bottom and can raise aggregate demand, offsetting part of the employment effect; but it can also compress differentials and reduce demand for the least experienced workers specifically, so aggregate stability can conceal displacement of young and low-skilled workers.\n• Assessment — the level matters, not the principle: at a modest level the monopsony and productivity effects can dominate; at a sufficiently high level the competitive prediction must eventually assert itself. The question is empirical and specific to the rate, not resolvable from theory.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that "inevitably" is wrong. Theory gives an unambiguous answer only in a competitive market, monopsony reverses it, and firms have many margins of adjustment; the evidence suggests moderate minimum wages have had small employment effects, while a sufficiently high one would not.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained assessment with both diagrams, the monopsony case handled correctly and a supported judgement about the level; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) competitive case explained with limited assessment; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the effectiveness of buffer stock schemes in stabilising the prices and incomes of primary commodity producers.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `• The problem: inelastic supply and inelastic demand mean harvest variation produces large price swings. Because demand is inelastic, revenue moves inversely with output, so a good harvest reduces farm incomes — which is why stabilising price is not the same as stabilising income.\n• The mechanism: the agency buys into store when the price would fall below a floor and sells from store when it would rise above a ceiling. Use a diagram with two supply curves and the intervention band.\n• The case in favour: price stability allows producers to plan and invest, reduces the risk premium on agricultural lending, and protects consumers from spikes. Unlike a permanent price floor, the scheme is in principle self-financing, buying low and selling high over the cycle.\n• Evaluation — setting the band: the scheme works only if the band brackets the long-run equilibrium price. If the floor is set above it, the agency buys persistently, stocks and storage costs grow without limit, and the scheme collapses — which is what happened to most historical commodity agreements.\n• Evaluation — finance and storage: buying requires working capital precisely when prices are low and revenue is scarce, and storage, insurance and deterioration are continuing costs. Many commodities are perishable, which rules the scheme out entirely.\n• Evaluation — incentive effects: a guaranteed floor encourages planting, shifting supply right and enlarging the surpluses the agency must absorb. The scheme can therefore undermine its own finances.\n• Evaluation — price stability does not guarantee income stability: with a fixed price, a producer\'s income now varies directly with their own output, so a farmer with a poor harvest at a stabilised price is worse off than they would have been at the high market price that a poor harvest would otherwise produce. Stabilising price can destabilise individual incomes.\n• Evaluation — speculation and credibility: if traders doubt the agency\'s reserves or resolve, they will trade against it, and defending the band becomes expensive. International schemes also face free-riding by non-member producers who benefit from the higher price without bearing the cost.\n• Evaluation — alternatives: futures and forward contracts let individual producers hedge without public expenditure; crop insurance targets output risk directly; decoupled income payments support farm incomes without distorting production; and diversification reduces exposure at source.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that buffer stocks are theoretically sound for storable commodities with a well-identified long-run price, but that their record is poor because the band is set politically rather than economically, the finance requirement peaks at the worst moment, and the supply response aggravates the surplus. Market-based hedging and decoupled income support are generally more effective at achieving the actual objective, which is stable incomes rather than stable prices.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained evaluation with the diagram integrated, the price/income distinction developed and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) mechanism explained with limited evaluation; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Discuss the view that subsidies are a more effective way of correcting market failure than regulation.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `• Set up: where positive externalities or information failure mean a good is under-consumed, the market equilibrium lies below the social optimum. A subsidy shifts supply right, lowering price and raising quantity towards the optimum; regulation mandates a minimum standard or level of provision.\n• The case for subsidies: they work through the price mechanism, so they preserve choice and allow the market to determine which producers respond. They are flexible — the rate can be adjusted to the estimated size of the externality — and they reward the desired behaviour rather than punishing its absence.\n• Diagrammatic case: the subsidy internalises the external benefit by making the private price reflect marginal social benefit, achieving the optimum with no loss of consumer choice and generating a welfare gain equal to the previously unrealised surplus.\n• Discussion — the fiscal cost: subsidies must be financed by taxation, which itself distorts other markets, and much of the spending is deadweight, going to buyers who would have acted anyway. Regulation imposes its cost on firms and consumers directly rather than on the budget, which is why it is politically attractive even where a subsidy would be more efficient.\n• Discussion — information requirements: setting the right subsidy requires knowing the monetary value of the externality. That is contested and hard to estimate, so the rate is likely to be wrong; regulation requires only a judgement about a standard, which is a lower informational bar.\n• Discussion — certainty of outcome: regulation delivers the outcome directly. A subsidy delivers it only if the price elasticity of demand is high enough; where demand is inelastic, most of the subsidy raises producer revenue and quantity moves little.\n• Discussion — producer capture and persistence: subsidies create a constituency that defends them long after the justification has gone, and they can be captured as higher producer margins rather than lower consumer prices, particularly where supply is inelastic or competition weak.\n• Discussion — regulation\'s own weaknesses: it is blunt, imposing a uniform requirement on heterogeneous firms; it requires monitoring and enforcement; it can entrench incumbents who can bear the compliance cost; and it provides no incentive to exceed the standard, so it delivers static rather than dynamic improvement.\n• Discussion — the failure determines the instrument: where the problem is a price that fails to reflect social value and responses are heterogeneous, a subsidy or tax is superior. Where the harm is severe, the response needed is uniform, or the price response would be weak, regulation is superior. Hybrid instruments such as tradable permits combine the certainty of a quantity target with the flexibility of a price.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that neither dominates in general — subsidies are more efficient where responses vary and demand is price elastic, regulation more reliable where the outcome must be guaranteed and demand is inelastic, and effective policy typically combines them.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained discussion with diagrams, both instruments evaluated against the type of failure, and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) both described with limited discussion; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that speculation always destabilises commodity markets.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `• Define the terms: speculation is the purchase or sale of a commodity in the expectation of a price change rather than for use or production. Destabilising speculation increases the amplitude of price movements; stabilising speculation reduces it.\n• The case that it stabilises: profitable speculation requires buying when the price is low and selling when it is high. That means adding to demand when supply is abundant and to supply when it is scarce, which moves the commodity from periods of plenty to periods of scarcity and narrows the price range — the same function as a buffer stock, performed privately and at the speculator\'s own risk.\n• Further points in favour: speculators provide liquidity, allowing producers and consumers to hedge through futures contracts so that a farmer can lock in a price at planting; and speculative activity incorporates information about future conditions into current prices faster than physical trading alone would.\n• Assessment — the destabilising case: where traders follow momentum rather than fundamentals, buying because prices are rising, they amplify the swing. Herding means each participant infers value from others who are inferring it from them, which detaches price from underlying supply and demand.\n• Assessment — inelasticity amplifies it: because commodity demand and supply are both price inelastic, a given amount of speculative buying moves price far more than it would in a market with flatter curves, so the destabilising potential is larger here than elsewhere.\n• Assessment — self-fulfilling expectations and hoarding: when storage is used to hold a commodity off the market in anticipation of a rise, the withdrawal itself causes the rise, and food price spikes have been worsened by hoarding and by export bans that acted on the same logic.\n• Assessment — the selection argument: Friedman\'s reply is that destabilising speculators buy high and sell low and so lose money, and are eliminated. The counter is that they can be eliminated only after the damage is done, and that in the meantime they can be joined by others chasing the same trend.\n• Assessment — the distinction that matters: whether speculation is stabilising depends on whether it is informed about fundamentals. Informed speculation smooths; uninformed momentum trading amplifies. Both occur, in varying proportions, in the same market.\n• Assessment — consequences and policy: even temporary amplification has real costs where the commodity is food or energy, falling hardest on low-income consumers. Position limits, transparency requirements and margin rules target the amplification without removing the hedging function.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that "always" is indefensible — the profit motive gives speculators a systematic reason to buy low and sell high, which is stabilising, and the hedging markets they support are valuable. But the claim that speculation is always stabilising is equally wrong: inelastic commodity markets are unusually vulnerable to momentum trading, and the welfare cost of amplified food and energy prices justifies targeted regulation rather than prohibition.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained two-sided assessment with the stabilising mechanism explained properly and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) one side developed, the other asserted; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: `After considering Extract D, and the original evidence in Extracts A, B and C, would you recommend that the government of City X impose a cap on private rents? Justify your recommendation.\n\nExtract D — written for this practice question\n"An independent review of City X found that 34% of private tenants spend more than half their income on rent, that the rental stock has fallen by 6% in two years, that 41% of landlords surveyed said they would sell or convert at least one property if rents were capped 15% below current levels, and that 9 400 dwellings already hold planning permission but have not been started."`,
        marks: 25, command: 'Justify',
        markScheme: `• Restate the problem from the earlier evidence: rents rose 26.17% in two years, the rental stock fell 6.07%, completions fell 35.08%, and Extract B identifies a planning restriction as a cause. Extract D adds that 34% of tenants now spend over half their income on rent, which is an acute affordability problem and a real case for action.\n• The case for a cap: it acts immediately, costs the exchequer nothing, and transfers surplus from landlords to tenants who hold a tenancy. Where supply is very inelastic in the short run, the immediate quantity loss is small, so the transfer is achieved at modest efficiency cost in the first year.\n• What Extract D changes — the supply response: 41% of landlords say they would sell or convert at least one property at a 15% cap. Stated intentions overstate action, but a response of anything approaching that size would shrink a stock that has already fallen 6%, turning an affordability problem into an availability problem.\n• What Extract D changes — the rationing question: with a binding cap, allocation moves to queues, landlord discretion and informal payments. The 34% in acute difficulty are the least likely to win under non-price rationing, so the policy may not reach the group it is designed for.\n• What Extract D changes — the diagnosis: 9 400 dwellings hold permission but are unstarted. That points to a build-out constraint rather than a permission constraint, and identifies an instrument — a build-out incentive or penalty — that raises supply rather than redistributing a shrinking one.\n• Weighing: Extracts A to C establish that supply is the binding constraint; Extract D confirms it and quantifies the risk that a cap would tighten it further. The affordability evidence is the strongest argument for the cap and the supply evidence the strongest argument against.\n• The recommendation and its conditions: a defensible recommendation is no to a general cap, and yes to a package — targeted housing support for the 34% in acute difficulty, which raises their purchasing power without cutting supply; measures to bring the 9 400 permitted dwellings forward; and, if any rent measure is used, a limit on in-tenancy increases rather than a cap on the level, which protects sitting tenants without making new lettings unprofitable.\n• Counter-argument to address: targeted support is partly capitalised into rents where supply is inelastic, so it may enrich landlords rather than tenants, and it takes years while the hardship is immediate. A candidate who addresses this and still reaches a reasoned recommendation should be credited at the top.\n• Uncertainties in the evidence: the 41% figure is a stated intention, not observed behaviour; the survey\'s sample and response rate are unknown; the review is a single snapshot with no comparison city; and no data are given on why the permitted dwellings are unstarted.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) all four extracts used, evidence weighed against evidence, a clear recommendation justified with conditions and the main counter-argument addressed; L4 (16-20) extracts used well with a justified recommendation; L3 (11-15) some use of extracts, recommendation asserted; L2 (6-10) descriptive use of extracts; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` }
    ]
  },
  '3.1.4': {
    name: 'Production Costs & Revenue',
    questions: [
      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA bakery's costs and output, one week\n  Total cost = £10 000\n  Output     = 200 units\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the bakery's average total cost per unit.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: average total cost = total cost / output = 10 000 / 200.\nAnswer: £50.00 per unit.\n2 marks for £50.00; 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for an answer given without the unit where the working is correct.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA component manufacturer's total costs\n  Output = 300 units : total cost = £14 600\n  Output = 310 units : total cost = £15 050\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the marginal cost of production over this range of output.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: marginal cost = change in total cost / change in output = (15 050 - 14 600) / (310 - 300) = 450 / 10.\nAnswer: £45.00 per unit.\n2 marks for £45.00; 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for dividing total cost by output (average rather than marginal cost).\nNote: average cost at 310 units is 15 050/310 = £48.55, so marginal cost is below average cost and average cost is falling over this range.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA bakery's weekly trading\n  Total revenue = £14 000\n  Total cost    = £10 000\n  Output        = 200 units\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the bakery's profit per unit.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: profit = 14 000 - 10 000 = £4 000; profit per unit = 4 000 / 200.\nAnswer: £20.00 per unit.\n2 marks for £20.00; 1 mark for identifying total profit as £4 000 without dividing by output, or for the correct method with an arithmetic slip.\nNote: because normal profit is included in economic cost, this £20.00 is supernormal profit per unit.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA firm that must lower its price to sell an extra unit\n  Price = £18.00 : quantity sold = 40\n  Price = £17.40 : quantity sold = 41\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the marginal revenue from selling the 41st unit.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: marginal revenue = change in total revenue. TR at 40 units = 40 × 18.00 = £720.00; TR at 41 units = 41 × 17.40 = £713.40. MR = 713.40 - 720.00.\nAnswer: -£6.60.\n2 marks for -£6.60; 1 mark for both total revenue figures correct but the sign or subtraction wrong, or for answering £17.40 (the price, not the marginal revenue).\nNote: MR is negative because the price cut applies to all 41 units, not just the extra one, so MR lies below AR for a price maker.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA manufacturer's weekly costs\n  Total fixed cost = £24 000\n  Output           = 3 200 units\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the manufacturer's average fixed cost per unit.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: average fixed cost = total fixed cost / output = 24 000 / 3 200.\nAnswer: £7.50 per unit.\n2 marks for £7.50; 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip.\nNote: because total fixed cost is constant, average fixed cost falls continuously as output rises — the "spreading of overheads".` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA firm's long-run average cost at two scales of production\n  Output = 20 000 units a year : long-run average cost = £42.50\n  Output = 40 000 units a year : long-run average cost = £34.00\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the percentage fall in long-run average cost as output doubles.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: (42.50 - 34.00) / 42.50 × 100 = 8.50 / 42.50 × 100.\nAnswer: 20.00%.\n2 marks for 20.00%; 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for using £34.00 as the denominator (25.00%).\nNote: a fall in long-run average cost as the scale of production rises is the definition of economies of scale.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA farm's output as more workers are added to a fixed area of land\n  Workers: 1 = 12 tonnes ; 2 = 30 ; 3 = 54 ; 4 = 72 ; 5 = 84 ; 6 = 90\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that this farm experiences diminishing marginal returns.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Marginal product of each successive worker is 12, 18, 24, 18, 12 and 6 tonnes. Marginal product rises to a peak at the third worker and falls thereafter.\n• Diminishing marginal returns therefore set in with the fourth worker: total output continues to rise but by progressively less each time.\n• Explanation: land is fixed in the short run, so as more of the variable factor is added each worker has less land and capital to work with. Increasing returns come first because specialisation and teamwork are possible; once the fixed factor is stretched, the additions fall.\n• Cost consequence: because the marginal product of labour is falling while the wage is constant, marginal cost is rising. This is why the short-run marginal cost curve turns upwards, and it is a short-run phenomenon only, since in the long run land is variable too.\nLevels: 4 = marginal products correctly derived AND the turning point identified with the fixed-factor explanation; 3 = marginal products derived with some explanation; 2 = the rise and fall noted without derivation; 1 = a relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA manufacturer's long-run average cost at different scales\n  10 000 units = £42.50 ; 20 000 = £36.00 ; 40 000 = £29.75 ; 80 000 = £29.75 ; 160 000 = £32.20\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that this firm experiences economies of scale up to a minimum efficient scale and diseconomies of scale beyond it.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Long-run average cost falls from £42.50 to £29.75 as output rises from 10 000 to 40 000 units — a fall of 30.00% — which is the definition of economies of scale.\n• Long-run average cost is constant at £29.75 between 40 000 and 80 000 units, so the minimum efficient scale is reached at 40 000 units: this is the lowest output at which the firm attains the minimum long-run average cost.\n• Beyond 80 000 units average cost rises to £32.20, showing diseconomies of scale.\n• Explanation: the fall comes from technical, purchasing, financial, managerial, marketing and risk-bearing economies as scale grows; the later rise comes from coordination and communication difficulties and weakening motivation as the organisation becomes large. All of this is long run, so every factor including plant size is variable.\nLevels: 4 = the three ranges identified with figures AND the minimum efficient scale correctly defined and located; 3 = figures used with some explanation; 2 = the fall and rise noted, MES not identified; 1 = a relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA firm's position at its chosen level of output\n  Output = 9 000 units ; average revenue = £28.00 ; average total cost = £21.00\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that this firm is earning supernormal profit, and calculate its size.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Average revenue of £28.00 exceeds average total cost of £21.00, so the firm earns £7.00 per unit above the cost of production.\n• Total supernormal profit = £7.00 × 9 000 = £63 000.\n• Explanation of why this is supernormal: economic cost includes normal profit, the minimum return required to keep the entrepreneur in this line of business. Any excess of revenue over total cost is therefore profit above that minimum.\n• Significance: supernormal profit is a signal that attracts new entrants. Whether it persists depends on barriers to entry — in a contestable market it will be competed away and AR will fall to AC in the long run.\nLevels: 4 = the per-unit and total figures both calculated AND the normal/supernormal distinction explained; 3 = calculation correct with some explanation; 2 = the comparison made without the total calculated; 1 = a relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA hotel's position during the off-season\n  Output = 5 000 room-nights ; average revenue = £14.00 ; average variable cost = £11.00 ; average fixed cost = £6.00\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that this hotel should continue to operate in the short run despite making a loss.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Average total cost = AVC + AFC = £11.00 + £6.00 = £17.00, which exceeds average revenue of £14.00, so the hotel loses £3.00 per room-night, a total loss of £15 000.\n• However, average revenue of £14.00 exceeds average variable cost of £11.00, so each room-night contributes £3.00 towards fixed costs — a total contribution of £15 000.\n• Comparison of the alternatives: if the hotel shuts down it still pays its fixed costs of £6.00 × 5 000 = £30 000 and earns nothing, so it loses £30 000. Operating loses only £15 000, so operating is the better of two bad options.\n• The rule: a firm should continue in the short run while AR is at least AVC, and shut down only when AR falls below AVC. In the long run all costs are variable, so it must cover average total cost to remain in the industry.\nLevels: 4 = both losses calculated AND the shut-down rule stated correctly with the contribution explained; 3 = the comparison made with some explanation; 2 = the loss identified without the shut-down comparison; 1 = a relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA firm's costs around the minimum of its average cost curve\n  Output = 99 units : total cost = £2 972\n  Output = 100 units : total cost = £3 000\n  Output = 101 units : total cost = £3 032\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that marginal cost cuts average cost at the minimum point of the average cost curve.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Average cost: at 99 units £2 972/99 = £30.02; at 100 units £3 000/100 = £30.00; at 101 units £3 032/101 = £30.02. Average cost is therefore at a minimum at 100 units.\n• Marginal cost: from 99 to 100 units MC = £28.00, which is below average cost, and average cost is falling. From 100 to 101 units MC = £32.00, which is above average cost, and average cost is rising.\n• Explanation of the relationship: whenever the marginal value is below the average, it pulls the average down; whenever it is above, it pulls the average up. The two must therefore be equal exactly where the average stops falling and starts rising.\n• Generalisation: this is an arithmetic necessity rather than an economic assumption, which is why MC always cuts AC and AVC at their minimum points on any cost diagram.\nLevels: 4 = both average and marginal costs calculated AND the pull-the-average-down reasoning explained; 3 = calculations with some explanation; 2 = one side calculated; 1 = a relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA firm's price, output, revenue and cost\n  Q = 8  : P = £22.00 : TR = £176 : TC = £132\n  Q = 10 : P = £20.00 : TR = £200 : TC = £150\n  Q = 12 : P = £18.00 : TR = £216 : TC = £176\n  Q = 14 : P = £16.00 : TR = £224 : TC = £206\n  Q = 16 : P = £13.50 : TR = £216 : TC = £240\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that the output which maximises revenue is not the output which maximises profit.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Profit at each output: 8 units £44; 10 units £50; 12 units £40; 14 units £18; 16 units -£24. Profit is maximised at 10 units.\n• Total revenue is maximised at 14 units, where TR = £224, and falls at 16 units. Revenue maximisation therefore requires an output four units higher than profit maximisation.\n• Explanation: profit is maximised where marginal cost equals marginal revenue; revenue is maximised where marginal revenue is zero. Because marginal cost is positive, MR must also be positive at the profit-maximising output, which lies at a lower quantity.\n• Consequence: a revenue-maximising firm sells more at a lower price than a profit maximiser, which benefits consumers in the short run but leaves less retained profit for investment.\nLevels: 4 = both maximising outputs identified with figures AND the MR = MC versus MR = 0 explanation given; 3 = both identified with some explanation; 2 = one identified; 1 = a relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 4-5) states that "as the plant took on more staff, each extra worker added less to output than the one before". With the help of a diagram, explain the law of diminishing returns and its effect on a firm\'s short-run cost curves.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: an upper panel showing marginal product and average product of labour rising then falling, and a lower panel showing marginal cost and average variable cost falling then rising, with MC at its minimum directly below MP at its maximum.\n• Statement of the law: as successive units of a variable factor are added to at least one fixed factor, the marginal product of the variable factor will eventually fall. It is a short-run law, because it requires a fixed factor.\n• Why marginal product rises first: at low levels of employment the fixed capital is under-used, so additional workers allow specialisation and teamwork. The practice data show marginal product rising from 12 to 18 to 24 tonnes.\n• Why it then falls: as employment continues to rise each worker has less capital and space to work with, so the addition to output falls — 18, then 12, then 6 tonnes.\n• The link to costs: marginal cost is the wage divided by the marginal product of labour. While marginal product rises, marginal cost falls; once marginal product falls, marginal cost rises. This inverse relationship is what gives the short-run MC and AVC curves their U shape.\n• Analytical consequence: the upward-sloping section of MC is the source of the upward-sloping short-run supply curve, so diminishing returns underlie the law of supply itself.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate two-panel diagram with the MP maximum aligned to the MC minimum, and the wage/MP link explained; L2 (4-6) the law explained with some cost linkage, diagram partial; L1 (1-3) the law stated with no cost link or diagram.` },

      { q: 'With the help of a diagram, explain the relationship between a firm\'s marginal cost curve and its average total cost and average variable cost curves.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: U-shaped AVC and ATC curves with ATC above AVC and the gap narrowing as output rises, and a U-shaped MC curve cutting both at their minimum points, cutting AVC first.\n• The arithmetic relationship: when marginal cost is below average cost, it pulls the average down; when it is above, it pulls the average up; so the two are equal precisely where the average is at its minimum. This is a necessity, not an assumption.\n• Numerical illustration: the practice data show MC of £28 below AC of £30.02 while AC is falling, and MC of £32 above AC while AC is rising, with AC at its minimum of £30.00 at 100 units.\n• Why MC cuts AVC before ATC: ATC includes average fixed cost, which is still falling, so the point at which ATC stops falling occurs at a higher output than the point at which AVC does.\n• Why the gap between ATC and AVC narrows: the gap is average fixed cost, which is total fixed cost divided by output, and therefore falls continuously towards zero as output rises.\n• Application: the minimum of ATC is the productively efficient output, and the minimum of AVC is the shut-down point in the short run — the two most examinable points on the diagram.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram with both intersections at the minima, the pulling-the-average reasoning explained and the AFC gap explained; L2 (4-6) relationship stated with partial explanation; L1 (1-3) diagram inaccurate or relationship asserted.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 8-9) states that "the firm believes doubling the size of its plant will cut its unit costs sharply". With the help of a diagram, explain the long-run average cost curve and the concept of minimum efficient scale.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: a long-run average cost envelope curve falling, flattening and then rising, drawn beneath a series of short-run average cost curves each representing a different plant size. Mark the minimum efficient scale at the lowest output achieving minimum LRAC.\n• The long run defined: a period in which all factors of production, including the scale of plant, are variable. The LRAC curve is the envelope of the short-run curves, showing the lowest attainable cost for each output when plant size can be chosen freely.\n• Falling section — economies of scale: technical economies from indivisible capital and the container principle, purchasing economies from bulk buying, financial economies from cheaper borrowing, managerial economies from specialist staff, marketing economies from spreading advertising, and risk-bearing economies from diversification. The practice data show LRAC falling 30.00% from £42.50 to £29.75.\n• Minimum efficient scale: the lowest output at which minimum long-run average cost is achieved — 40 000 units in the practice data. Below it a firm is at a permanent unit-cost disadvantage.\n• Rising section — diseconomies of scale: coordination and communication difficulties, motivational problems from alienation in large organisations, and the cost of managing complexity.\n• Analytical significance: the MES relative to total market demand determines how many firms the market can support at minimum cost. Where MES is a large share of demand, the market supports few firms and natural concentration follows — which is why MES is a structural barrier to entry as well as a cost concept.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate LRAC diagram with MES marked, several economies explained and the market-structure implication drawn; L2 (4-6) diagram with some explanation; L1 (1-3) list of economies with no diagram or MES.` },

      { q: 'Analyse three internal economies of scale that may be available to a large manufacturing firm.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `• Framing: internal economies arise from the growth of the firm itself and reduce long-run average cost. They are distinct from external economies, which arise from the growth of the whole industry.\n• Technical economies: large indivisible capital equipment — a production line, a blast furnace, an automated warehouse — has a high fixed cost that can only be spread over a large output. The container principle reinforces this: doubling the linear dimensions of a vessel raises its surface area, and therefore its material cost, by a factor of four but its capacity by a factor of eight, so unit capacity costs fall with size.\n• Purchasing economies: a large firm buying inputs in bulk obtains discounts, because the supplier\'s own fixed transaction and delivery costs are spread over a larger order and because the buyer has bargaining power. This is why large grocery chains can undercut independents on identical goods.\n• Financial economies: large firms borrow more cheaply because they are lower risk, have collateral and can access bond markets directly rather than bank lending. A lower cost of capital lowers the cost of every unit produced with that capital.\n• Managerial economies: scale allows the division of labour to be applied to management itself, with specialist finance, legal, marketing and logistics functions. A small firm's owner performs all of these less well.\n• Analytical depth that earns L3: these are long-run effects requiring all factors to be variable, and they do not continue indefinitely — beyond minimum efficient scale, coordination costs offset them, which is why LRAC eventually turns upwards.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) three economies analysed with a developed mechanism for each, and the long-run/limits point made; L2 (4-6) three identified with some analysis; L1 (1-3) a list with brief description.` },

      { q: 'Analyse the main causes of diseconomies of scale in a large organisation.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `• Framing: diseconomies of scale are increases in long-run average cost that result from the firm becoming larger. They explain the upward-sloping section of the LRAC curve, and the practice data show LRAC rising from £29.75 to £32.20 beyond 80 000 units.\n• Coordination and control: as the number of activities grows, the effort required to align them grows faster than proportionately. More layers of management are needed, each adding salary cost and delay, and decisions are made further from the information they depend on.\n• Communication failure: information passing through many layers is distorted and slowed. Decisions are taken on out-of-date or filtered information, which raises the cost of mistakes as well as the cost of deciding.\n• Motivation and alienation: workers in a large organisation are further from the outcome of their effort and from the people who judge it, which weakens the link between effort and reward. Monitoring costs rise and productivity falls.\n• The principal-agent problem at scale: divisional managers pursue divisional objectives — budget, headcount, status — rather than the firm's. Internal politics consumes resources that produce nothing.\n• Inflexibility: large firms carry heavy sunk investment in specific processes and routines, so they adapt more slowly to changes in demand or technology. This is a real cost even though it does not appear in a single year's accounts.\n• Analytical qualification: modern information systems, decentralisation into profit centres and performance-linked pay push the onset of diseconomies to higher outputs, which is one reason the LRAC curve for many industries is now flat over a wide range rather than sharply U-shaped.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) several causes analysed with mechanisms and a qualification about how firms mitigate them; L2 (4-6) causes identified with some analysis; L1 (1-3) a list.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 12-13) states that "the hotel stayed open through the winter even though it lost money every week". With the help of a diagram, explain why a firm may continue to produce in the short run while making a loss.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: AR (price) line below ATC but above AVC, with AC, AVC and MC curves drawn and the loss per unit shaded as the vertical gap between AR and ATC at the profit-maximising output.\n• The short-run distinction: fixed costs must be paid whether or not the firm produces, so they are irrelevant to the decision. Only the comparison between revenue and variable cost matters.\n• The rule: continue producing while AR is at least AVC, because every unit then makes a positive contribution towards fixed costs. Shut down when AR falls below AVC, since each unit would then add to the loss.\n• Numerical illustration from the practice data: with AR £14.00, AVC £11.00 and AFC £6.00, operating loses £3.00 per room-night, a loss of £15 000, while closing loses the whole £30 000 of fixed costs. Operating is the better of two bad options.\n• Why the long run differs: in the long run all costs are variable, including the decision to renew the lease or replace the plant, so the firm must cover average total cost to stay in the industry. The long-run shut-down point is at the minimum of LRAC.\n• Further reasons a firm may continue beyond the textbook rule: closing and reopening carry sunk costs, skilled staff and customers are lost, contracts and reputation are damaged, and losses may be expected to be temporary — a seasonal trough or a recession.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram with AR between AVC and ATC, the contribution argument developed and the short-run/long-run distinction made; L2 (4-6) rule stated with some explanation; L1 (1-3) assertion that firms hope things improve.` },

      { q: 'With the help of a diagram, explain the relationship between average revenue, marginal revenue and total revenue for a firm that must lower its price to sell more output.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: a downward-sloping AR (demand) curve with an MR curve below it, twice as steep, cutting the horizontal axis at half the output at which AR does; beneath it, a total revenue curve rising to a maximum directly above the point where MR = 0 and then falling.\n• Average revenue is price: AR = TR/Q = (P × Q)/Q = P, so for a price maker the AR curve is the demand curve.\n• Why MR lies below AR: to sell an extra unit the firm must lower the price on every unit, not only the last one. Marginal revenue is therefore the price of the extra unit minus the revenue lost on all the previous units.\n• Numerical illustration: selling the 41st unit requires cutting the price from £18.00 to £17.40. TR rises from £720.00 to £713.40, so MR is -£6.60 even though the unit sold for £17.40.\n• The link to total revenue: TR rises while MR is positive, is at a maximum where MR = 0, and falls where MR is negative. This is the same relationship as between total and marginal utility.\n• The link to elasticity: demand is price elastic where MR is positive, unit elastic where MR is zero, and inelastic where MR is negative — so a profit-maximising firm always operates on the elastic section of its demand curve, since MC is positive and must equal MR.\n• Contrast that earns L3: for a price taker, price is given, so AR = MR = price and both are horizontal. The divergence between AR and MR is precisely what market power means.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram with MR below AR and TR aligned to MR = 0, the "price cut applies to all units" reasoning developed and the elasticity or price-taker contrast made; L2 (4-6) relationship stated with partial diagram; L1 (1-3) definitions only.` },

      { q: 'With the help of a diagram, explain why a firm maximises profit at the output where marginal cost equals marginal revenue.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: MC and MR curves intersecting at Q*, with AR and AC curves drawn and supernormal profit shaded as (AR - AC) × Q*.\n• The logic below Q*: if MR exceeds MC, the extra unit adds more to revenue than to cost, so producing it raises profit. The firm should expand.\n• The logic above Q*: if MC exceeds MR, the extra unit costs more than it earns, so producing it reduces profit. The firm should contract.\n• Conclusion: profit can only be at a maximum where no further unit changes it, which is where MC = MR. The condition is about the last unit, not the average.\n• The second-order condition: MC must be rising through the intersection. Where MC cuts MR from above, the point is a profit minimum, which is why the relevant intersection is the one on the upward-sloping section of MC.\n• Numerical illustration: the practice data show profit rising from £44 at 8 units to £50 at 10 units and falling to £40 at 12 units, so the maximum is at 10 units — the output where the marginal addition to revenue and to cost are equal.\n• Distinction that earns L3: MC = MR identifies the output, and the demand curve then determines the price that can be charged for it. A price maker does not choose price and quantity independently.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram with profit shaded, the marginal reasoning developed in both directions and the second-order or price-setting point made; L2 (4-6) rule explained with some reasoning; L1 (1-3) the rule asserted.` },

      { q: 'With the help of a diagram, analyse how the output and price chosen by a firm differ when it pursues profit maximisation, revenue maximisation and sales maximisation.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: AR, MR, AC and MC curves on one set of axes, with three outputs marked — Qpm where MC = MR, Qrm where MR = 0, and Qsm where AR = AC — and the corresponding prices read off the AR curve.\n• Profit maximisation at MC = MR: the lowest of the three outputs and the highest price, because the firm stops as soon as the marginal unit ceases to add to profit.\n• Revenue maximisation at MR = 0: a larger output and lower price. The firm continues while any unit adds to revenue, ignoring its cost. The practice data show revenue maximised at 14 units against profit maximised at 10.\n• Sales (output) maximisation subject to a normal profit constraint, at AR = AC: the largest output and the lowest price, since the firm expands until supernormal profit is exhausted.\n• Why a firm might choose each: profit maximisation serves shareholders and funds investment; revenue maximisation may be chosen where managers are paid on turnover, or to deter entry and build market share; sales maximisation is used to establish network effects or to secure long-run dominance.\n• Analysis of the trade-off: the non-profit objectives deliver lower prices and higher output in the short run, which benefits consumers, but leave less retained profit for research and investment, so the dynamic effect may run the other way.\n• Behavioural alternative worth noting: satisficing — pursuing an acceptable rather than a maximum outcome — is common where ownership is divorced from control and managers face competing stakeholder demands.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram with all three outputs identified correctly, developed analysis of the differences and a reason for each objective; L2 (4-6) two objectives analysed with a partial diagram; L1 (1-3) objectives listed.` },

      { q: 'Analyse how the divorce of ownership from control can lead firms to pursue objectives other than profit maximisation.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `• The structure: in a public limited company the shareholders own the firm but salaried managers control day-to-day decisions. The two groups have different objectives and different information.\n• The principal-agent problem: shareholders (principals) cannot observe managers\' (agents\') effort or the quality of their decisions, only outcomes that are also affected by chance. This is asymmetric information about hidden action, so contracts cannot be written on effort directly.\n• Managerial objectives that follow: growth of the firm, because salary, status and career prospects rise with its size; revenue maximisation, where bonuses are linked to turnover; empire-building through acquisitions that raise scale without raising returns; and expenditure on perquisites that raise managers\' utility rather than profit.\n• Satisficing: Simon\'s argument that managers facing bounded rationality and competing stakeholder claims — shareholders, employees, customers, regulators — pursue an acceptable outcome for each rather than maximising any one. Profit then becomes a constraint rather than an objective.\n• Analysis of why shareholders cannot simply prevent it: shareholdings are dispersed, so each individual shareholder has little incentive to monitor and gains only a fraction of any improvement — a free-rider problem that leaves management largely unchecked.\n• Mechanisms that narrow the gap: performance-related pay and share options align incentives; institutional investors have the size to monitor; non-executive directors and corporate governance codes impose oversight; and the market for corporate control means persistent under-performance invites a takeover that removes the managers.\n• Analytical qualification: these mechanisms are imperfect and can create new distortions — share options reward short-term price movements and can encourage excessive risk — so the divergence is constrained rather than eliminated.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) the principal-agent mechanism analysed with several resulting objectives and the disciplining mechanisms evaluated; L2 (4-6) divergence explained with some analysis; L1 (1-3) assertion that managers have different aims.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) and Extract B — written for this practice question\nA manufacturer's costs at four scales of production\n  Annual output      10 000   40 000   80 000   160 000\n  Long-run av. cost  £42.50   £29.75   £29.75   £32.20\n  Units actually sold last year: 38 000\n  Capacity utilisation last year: 76%\n\nExtract B states: "The firm's largest rival produces 90 000 units a year and sells at £31.00."\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data suggest that this firm should expand its output to 160 000 units?`,
        marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `• The cost evidence against expanding to 160 000: long-run average cost there is £32.20, which is 8.24% higher than the £29.75 achieved at 40 000 and 80 000 units. Expanding that far takes the firm into diseconomies of scale, so unit costs would rise, not fall.\n• Where the gain actually lies: the firm currently sells 38 000 units, just below the minimum efficient scale of 40 000. Moving from 10 000 to 40 000 cuts unit cost by 30.00%, so the cost gain from growth is almost entirely captured well before 160 000 units.\n• The competitive comparison: the rival produces 90 000 units and sells at £31.00. At £29.75 the firm could undercut that, so its cost position at 40 000 to 80 000 units is already competitive. The data give no reason to believe scale is the firm\'s problem.\n• Capacity utilisation matters: at 76% the firm has unused capacity, so additional output up to its existing capacity requires no new investment at all and would spread fixed costs further. That is a cheaper route to lower unit costs than building new plant.\n• Limitations of the data: they say nothing about demand. There is no evidence that 160 000 units could be sold, and at a market price of around £31.00 selling four times the current volume would almost certainly require a substantial price cut. Cost data alone cannot settle an expansion decision.\n• Further limitations: the LRAC figures are estimates at four points only, with nothing between 80 000 and 160 000, so the exact onset of diseconomies is unknown; no data are given on the capital cost or the time required to expand; and long-run cost curves shift with technology.\n• Judgement: the data do not support expansion to 160 000 units — that output lies beyond minimum efficient scale and raises unit costs. They do support growth to somewhere between 40 000 and 80 000 units, and most immediately raising utilisation from 76%, but the absence of any demand data means even that conclusion is provisional.\nLevels: L3 (8-10) figures calculated and used precisely, the MES and diseconomies points both handled, the absence of demand data identified and a supported judgement reached; L2 (4-7) relevant data used with some evaluation; L1 (1-3) data described without judgement.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — written for this practice question\nSix firms in the same industry, last financial year\n  Firm   Annual output   Average cost per unit   Directors' pay as % of profit\n    1       12 000            £38.40                     4.1%\n    2       26 000            £33.10                     5.2%\n    3       44 000            £30.20                     9.8%\n    4       61 000            £29.60                    12.4%\n    5       88 000            £29.90                    16.1%\n    6      141 000            £32.80                    21.7%\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data suggest that the largest firms in this industry are being run for the benefit of their managers rather than their shareholders?`,
        marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `• The pattern that supports the claim: directors\' pay as a share of profit rises steadily with firm size, from 4.1% at Firm 1 to 21.7% at Firm 6 — more than a fivefold increase. At the same time, average cost stops falling after Firm 4 and rises to £32.80 at Firm 6, which is 10.81% above the industry minimum.\n• The combination is what matters: the largest firm has both the highest unit costs and the highest managerial share of profit, which is the pattern the managerial-objectives hypothesis predicts if managers pursue growth for its own sake beyond the efficient scale.\n• Evidence against: rising average cost beyond about 61 000 units is fully explained by ordinary diseconomies of scale — coordination, communication and motivation costs — which require no assumption about managerial self-interest at all. A simpler explanation fits the cost data.\n• Alternative explanation of the pay pattern: larger firms are more complex to manage and compete for scarcer executive talent, so higher pay may be a competitive market rate rather than extraction. Expressing pay as a share of profit also mechanically inflates the figure when profit is squeezed by higher costs.\n• Limitations of the data: six firms is a very small sample and the pattern could be coincidental; there is no information on total profit, return on capital or shareholder returns, which is what "benefit of shareholders" actually means; and a single year gives no trend.\n• What would be needed: data on returns to shareholders relative to the cost of capital, on the structure of executive pay (whether performance-linked), on ownership concentration, and on whether the largest firms under-perform over several years.\n• Judgement: the data are consistent with the managerial hypothesis but fall well short of demonstrating it, because diseconomies of scale explain the cost pattern independently and the pay measure is a ratio that rises automatically when profit falls. At most the data raise a question that shareholder-return data would be needed to answer.\nLevels: L3 (8-10) both patterns quantified, the alternative explanation developed, limitations identified and a supported judgement reached; L2 (4-7) relevant data used with some evaluation; L1 (1-3) description only.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main determinants of a firm\'s costs of production in the short run.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• The defining condition: in the short run at least one factor is fixed, so total cost divides into fixed costs that do not vary with output and variable costs that do. This division drives everything else.\n• Factor prices: wages, raw material and energy prices, and rents determine the level of the cost curves. A rise in any of them shifts the whole set of curves upwards without changing their shape.\n• The productivity of the variable factor: marginal cost equals the wage divided by the marginal product of labour, so anything affecting productivity — skills, training, management quality, effort — affects cost directly.\n• The law of diminishing returns: as more of the variable factor is applied to the fixed factor, marginal product eventually falls, so marginal cost eventually rises. This is what gives the short-run MC and AVC curves their U shape and is the central short-run determinant.\n• The level of output relative to capacity: average fixed cost falls continuously as output rises, so unit costs fall sharply at low utilisation. Near full capacity, diminishing returns dominate and unit costs rise.\n• The size of fixed costs and the capital intensity of the process: a capital-intensive firm has high fixed and low variable costs, so its average cost is highly sensitive to utilisation; a labour-intensive firm\'s is not.\n• Technology in use: the technique is given in the short run, so it sets the shape of the cost curves. Changing it is a long-run decision.\n• Taxation and regulation: employer contributions, environmental compliance and licence fees add to either fixed or variable cost depending on whether they vary with output.\n• The organising point: factor prices and technology set the position of the curves; diminishing returns sets their shape; utilisation determines where on them the firm sits.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several determinants explained with developed reasoning, and the fixed/variable and position/shape distinctions made clearly; L2 (6-10) determinants listed with some explanation; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main sources of economies of scale available to a growing firm.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Framing: economies of scale are reductions in long-run average cost that follow from producing at a larger scale. They are a long-run concept, requiring all factors including plant size to be variable, and they are distinct from the short-run spreading of fixed costs.\n• Technical economies: large indivisible capital equipment has a high fixed cost that only a large output can spread. The container principle adds to this, since capacity rises with the cube of linear dimensions while material cost rises with the square, which is why large tankers, warehouses and reactors have lower unit capacity costs.\n• Specialisation and the division of labour: a large workforce can be divided into narrowly specialised roles, raising output per worker, and the same applies to management, where scale supports specialist finance, legal and logistics functions.\n• Purchasing economies: bulk orders attract discounts because the supplier\'s own fixed order-processing and delivery costs are spread, and because a large buyer has bargaining power over price and terms.\n• Financial economies: large firms are lower credit risks with more collateral, and can issue bonds or equity directly rather than borrowing from banks, so their cost of capital is lower — which lowers the cost of every unit produced with that capital.\n• Marketing economies: the cost of a national advertising campaign, a brand, or a distribution network is largely fixed, so unit marketing cost falls as output rises.\n• Risk-bearing economies: a large firm can diversify across products, markets and geographies, which reduces the variance of its returns and therefore the risk premium it must pay.\n• Research and development: R&D is a fixed cost that only a large output can amortise, which is why the minimum efficient scale is very large in pharmaceuticals, semiconductors and aerospace.\n• External economies for contrast: these arise from the growth of the industry rather than the firm — a local pool of skilled labour, specialist suppliers, shared infrastructure and knowledge spillovers.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several sources explained with the mechanism in each case and the internal/external distinction made; L2 (6-10) sources listed with some explanation; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main determinants of a firm\'s total revenue.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• The identity: total revenue is price multiplied by quantity sold, so every determinant works through one or both of these.\n• Price elasticity of demand: because a price change moves price and quantity in opposite directions, PED determines the effect on revenue. Inelastic demand means revenue rises with price; elastic demand means it falls. The practice data show a 15% price rise raising revenue by 4.65% where PED was -0.60.\n• Market structure and the firm\'s demand curve: a price taker faces a horizontal demand curve, so AR = MR = price and revenue is proportional to output. A price maker faces a downward-sloping demand curve, so MR lies below AR and revenue is maximised where MR = 0.\n• Non-price determinants of demand: income and the income elasticity of demand, the prices of substitutes and complements, tastes, population, advertising and expectations all shift the demand curve and therefore the revenue obtainable at any price.\n• Competitors\' behaviour: in an oligopoly the firm\'s revenue depends on rivals\' prices through cross elasticity of demand, and price cuts are typically matched, so the demand curve the firm actually faces differs from the one it would face alone.\n• The firm\'s pricing strategy: price discrimination raises revenue by charging different prices to segments with different elasticities and converting consumer surplus into revenue; bundling, two-part tariffs and loyalty pricing do the same.\n• Product differentiation and branding: successful differentiation shifts demand right and makes it less elastic, allowing a higher price without a proportionate loss of volume.\n• Quality, service and reputation: these raise willingness to pay and the volume sold at any price, and are the main non-price determinants a firm controls directly.\n• The organising point: the firm controls price and product but not the demand curve, so revenue is determined jointly by its own decisions and by conditions in the market.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several determinants explained with developed reasoning, with elasticity and market structure handled accurately; L2 (6-10) determinants listed with some explanation; L1 (1-5) statement that revenue is price times quantity.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main objectives that firms may pursue other than profit maximisation.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Why the question arises: profit maximisation at MC = MR assumes the owner takes the decisions and has the information to identify the marginal condition. In a large company neither holds, because ownership is divorced from control and marginal data are rarely available.\n• Revenue maximisation, at MR = 0: a larger output and lower price than profit maximisation. Managers may prefer it because salary, status and bonuses are often linked to turnover, and because revenue is easier to measure than profit.\n• Sales or output maximisation subject to a normal profit constraint, at AR = AC: the largest output and lowest price. Used to build market share, exploit network effects, establish a standard, or deter entry by making the market look unattractive.\n• Growth maximisation: expanding the size of the firm through investment and acquisition. It raises managers\' salary and security, and may raise long-run profit through economies of scale, but empire-building can push the firm beyond minimum efficient scale into diseconomies.\n• Satisficing: Simon\'s argument that bounded rationality and competing stakeholder claims lead managers to seek an acceptable outcome for each group rather than maximising any one. Profit becomes a constraint — enough to keep shareholders quiet — rather than the objective.\n• Survival: in a recession or a new market, the immediate objective is to cover variable costs and remain in business. This explains behaviour that looks irrational against a profit-maximising benchmark.\n• Social, environmental and ethical objectives: firms pursue them from genuine commitment, because they attract customers and staff, or because they pre-empt regulation. Where they raise long-run profit they are not an alternative objective at all.\n• Price and market-share objectives in oligopoly: limit pricing to deter entry and predatory pricing to remove a rival both sacrifice short-run profit for a long-run position.\n• The organising point: many of these are better understood as profit maximisation over a longer horizon than as rejections of profit as an objective, and distinguishing the two is what separates a strong answer from a list.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several objectives explained with the mechanism and the reason a firm adopts each, and the long-run profit point made; L2 (6-10) objectives listed with some explanation; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main differences between the short run and the long run in the theory of production and costs.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• The definitions: the short run is the period in which at least one factor of production is fixed; the long run is the period in which all factors, including the scale of plant, are variable. The distinction is analytical, not a fixed number of months, and differs by industry — days for a market stall, a decade for a nuclear power station.\n• Fixed and variable costs: fixed costs exist only in the short run. In the long run every cost is variable, which is why there is no long-run equivalent of the average fixed cost curve.\n• The governing law differs: short-run costs are shaped by the law of diminishing returns, which operates because a fixed factor constrains the variable one. Long-run costs are shaped by returns to scale, where all factors change together.\n• The cost curves differ: the short-run MC and AVC curves are U-shaped because of diminishing marginal returns; the LRAC curve is U-shaped because of economies and then diseconomies of scale. They look similar and have entirely different causes, which is the most common confusion in this topic.\n• The relationship between them: the LRAC curve is the envelope of the short-run average cost curves, each representing a different plant size, showing the lowest attainable cost for each output when plant can be chosen freely.\n• The shut-down decision differs: in the short run the firm continues while AR is at least AVC, because fixed costs are unavoidable. In the long run it must cover average total cost, since it can exit and avoid all costs.\n• Entry and exit: only in the long run can firms enter or leave an industry, which is why supernormal profit can persist in the short run but is competed away in the long run where entry is free.\n• Elasticity of supply: supply is more price elastic in the long run, because capacity can be expanded and new firms can enter, which is why price spikes from short-run supply shocks are typically temporary.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) the distinction explained precisely with several consequences developed and the diminishing-returns/returns-to-scale contrast made explicitly; L2 (6-10) the distinction explained with some consequences; L1 (1-5) definitions only.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main determinants of the minimum efficient scale in an industry, and why it varies so widely between industries.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Definition: minimum efficient scale is the lowest output at which a firm achieves the minimum of its long-run average cost curve. In the practice data it is 40 000 units, the point at which LRAC reaches £29.75.\n• Determinant — the size of indivisible fixed costs: the larger the fixed cost that must be spread, the higher the output needed to spread it. A pharmaceutical firm amortising research over a product, or a semiconductor firm amortising a fabrication plant, needs a very large output; a hairdresser needs almost none.\n• Determinant — capital intensity and the technology of production: continuous-flow processes such as steel, chemicals and cement have strong technical economies and the container principle applies, so MES is large. Craft and personal-service production has constant returns over a wide range, so MES is small.\n• Determinant — research and development intensity: R&D is a fixed cost incurred before a single unit is sold, so MES rises with the share of R&D in total cost. This is why aerospace, pharmaceuticals and semiconductors are globally concentrated.\n• Determinant — the reach of marketing and distribution: where a national brand and distribution network are needed, their largely fixed cost raises MES. Where the product is sold locally, it does not.\n• Determinant — network effects and near-zero marginal cost: in digital markets, average cost falls continuously with users and value rises with scale, so there may be no effective minimum efficient scale at all short of the whole market.\n• Determinant — the onset of diseconomies: MES also depends on how soon coordination, communication and motivation costs bite. Better information systems and decentralisation push that point out, flattening the LRAC curve over a wider range.\n• Why it matters: MES relative to total market demand determines how many firms an industry can support at minimum cost, so it is a structural determinant of concentration and a barrier to entry. Where MES exceeds total demand, the industry is a natural monopoly.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) MES defined precisely, several determinants explained with mechanisms, and the link to market structure made explicit; L2 (6-10) determinants listed with some explanation; L1 (1-5) a list, or MES confused with economies of scale generally.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that economies of scale always lead to lower prices for consumers.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `• The mechanism in favour: economies of scale lower long-run average cost. The practice data show LRAC falling 30.00% from £42.50 to £29.75 as output rises to minimum efficient scale. Lower cost shifts the supply curve right, and in a competitive market price falls towards the new minimum average cost.\n• Why competition is the transmission mechanism: with free entry, any firm charging above average cost is undercut, so cost savings must be passed on. The historical fall in the real price of manufactured goods, air travel and electronics is consistent with this.\n• Dynamic reinforcement: scale supports research and development, which is a fixed cost requiring a large output to amortise, so scale can lower future costs as well as current ones.\n• Evaluation — pass-through requires competition: a firm with market power sets price where MC = MR, not at average cost. A fall in cost raises the firm\'s margin and lowers price only partially, so the gain is shared with shareholders rather than passed on in full.\n• Evaluation — scale may create the market power that prevents pass-through: where minimum efficient scale is large relative to market demand, only a few firms can operate efficiently, so the same economies that lower cost also raise concentration. The cost saving and the barrier to entry are the same fact viewed twice.\n• Evaluation — diseconomies: beyond minimum efficient scale, coordination, communication and motivation costs raise unit cost. The practice data show LRAC rising to £32.20 at 160 000 units, so growth beyond a point raises costs and can raise prices.\n• Evaluation — natural monopoly: where LRAC falls over the whole range of market demand, a single firm is the efficient producer, but it will price as a monopolist unless regulated. Here economies of scale lower cost and raise price simultaneously, which is the clearest counter-example.\n• Evaluation — non-price effects: large scale can reduce variety, service quality and local availability, so a lower price may not mean better value; and consumers may face a worse deal even where the headline price falls.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that economies of scale reliably lower costs but only translate into lower prices where competitive pressure or regulation forces the saving to be passed on. The word "always" fails because scale itself is a barrier to entry, so the market structures in which the largest economies are available are often precisely those in which pass-through is weakest.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained evaluation distinguishing cost from price, the market-structure link developed and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) economies explained with limited evaluation; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the significance of the principal-agent problem for the behaviour of large firms.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `• The problem: shareholders own the firm but cannot observe managers\' effort or the quality of their decisions, only outcomes that are also affected by chance. This is asymmetric information about hidden action, so contracts cannot be written on effort.\n• Predicted consequences: managers pursue growth, revenue, perquisites and job security rather than profit; empire-building acquisitions raise size without raising returns; and risk-taking is distorted, since managers bear career risk but not the full financial consequences.\n• Why shareholders cannot simply correct it: dispersed ownership means each shareholder gains only a fraction of any improvement they secure, so monitoring is a public good subject to free-riding. Collective action is weak precisely where ownership is most widely spread.\n• Evidence of significance: the practice data show directors\' pay rising from 4.1% to 21.7% of profit as firm size rises, alongside average cost rising beyond minimum efficient scale — a pattern consistent with growth being pursued past the efficient point.\n• Assessment — the disciplining mechanisms are real: performance-related pay and share options align incentives; institutional investors hold stakes large enough to justify monitoring; non-executive directors and governance codes impose oversight; and the market for corporate control means persistent under-performance invites a takeover that removes the managers.\n• Assessment — competition constrains the problem: in a competitive product market, a firm whose managers indulge themselves loses out on cost and is driven towards exit. The principal-agent problem is therefore most significant where market power provides the slack to absorb it — which is why the two failures compound one another.\n• Assessment — the alignment mechanisms create new distortions: share options reward short-term share-price movements and can encourage excessive risk and earnings management, so the cure can generate its own agency costs. The problem is transformed rather than solved.\n• Assessment — alternative explanations of the same behaviour: rising costs at large scale are fully explained by diseconomies of scale without any assumption about managerial motives, and higher executive pay may reflect a competitive market for scarce managerial talent. Evidence consistent with the hypothesis is not evidence for it.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that the principal-agent problem is a significant and permanent feature of the large corporation, because the information asymmetry that causes it cannot be removed, but that its practical significance is bounded by competition, takeover threat and governance, so it explains departures from profit maximisation at the margin rather than a wholesale abandonment of it.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained assessment with the mechanism analysed, disciplining devices evaluated and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) problem explained with limited assessment; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Discuss the view that profit maximisation is an unrealistic assumption about how firms actually behave.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `• The assumption: firms choose the output at which MC = MR. Illustrate on a diagram and show the supernormal profit.\n• The case that it is unrealistic — information: firms do not know their marginal cost and marginal revenue curves. In practice they use cost-plus pricing, applying a mark-up to average cost, which is not the same rule and need not give the same answer.\n• The case that it is unrealistic — motivation: the divorce of ownership from control means managers pursue growth, revenue or their own security, and the practice data show directors\' pay rising sharply with firm size alongside rising unit costs.\n• The case that it is unrealistic — satisficing and stakeholders: Simon\'s bounded rationality argument, and the competing claims of employees, customers, suppliers and regulators, imply an acceptable outcome for each rather than a maximum of any one.\n• The case that it is unrealistic — other objectives: survival in a downturn, market share in a new market, and social or environmental commitments all produce behaviour that looks wrong against the profit-maximising benchmark.\n• Discussion — the "as if" defence: models are judged by predictive power, not descriptive realism. Firms that do not approximate profit maximisation are driven out by those that do, so competitive selection produces the predicted behaviour even if no manager performs the calculation.\n• Discussion — many alternatives are profit maximisation over a longer horizon: limit pricing, loss-leading, market-share building and investment in reputation all sacrifice current profit for a larger future stream. Treating them as alternatives to profit maximisation confuses the time horizon with the objective.\n• Discussion — the assumption predicts well where it matters: firms do respond to cost increases by raising price, to demand increases by expanding, and to persistent losses by exiting. These are the predictions used in most applied analysis and they hold.\n• Discussion — where it genuinely fails: in firms with substantial market power and dispersed ownership, where slack allows managerial objectives to persist; in state-owned and not-for-profit organisations; and in the short run where survival dominates.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that profit maximisation is unrealistic as a literal description of the decision process but remains the most useful single assumption, because competitive selection and long-run profit considerations bring behaviour close to its predictions; it should be treated as a benchmark whose failures are concentrated in firms shielded from competition.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained two-sided discussion engaging with the "as if" defence and the time-horizon point, with a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) alternatives described with limited discussion; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the importance of economies of scale as a barrier to entry in an industry.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `• The mechanism: where minimum efficient scale is large relative to market demand, an entrant must either enter at large scale, requiring heavy capital investment and adding enough output to depress the market price, or enter small and accept a permanent unit-cost disadvantage. The practice data show an entrant at 10 000 units facing costs of £42.50 against £29.75 at MES — a 42.9% disadvantage.\n• Why this deters entry: the incumbent can profitably price below the small entrant\'s average cost, so entry is unprofitable before it begins. No predatory action is needed; the cost structure alone deters.\n• Where this is strongest: industries with large indivisible capital and heavy fixed research costs — pharmaceuticals, semiconductors, aerospace, steel, rail — and natural monopolies such as water and transmission networks, where LRAC falls across the whole range of demand.\n• Assessment — sunk costs matter more than scale: contestability theory holds that it is the irrecoverability of costs, not their size, that deters entry. Where capital can be leased or resold, hit-and-run entry remains possible even at large scale — the aircraft leasing market being the standard illustration.\n• Assessment — other barriers are often larger: legal barriers such as patents and licences, control of essential inputs or distribution, network effects, switching costs and brand loyalty built by sunk advertising can all exceed scale economies in importance. The CMA\'s designation of Google in search and of Apple and Google in mobile platforms as having strategic market status in October 2025 rested on ecosystem control and network effects rather than on production scale.\n• Assessment — technology can dissolve the barrier: outsourcing, contract manufacturing, cloud computing and digital distribution allow entrants to buy scale rather than build it, so the minimum efficient scale a new firm must achieve internally has fallen sharply in many industries.\n• Assessment — entry at a different point: entrants avoid the barrier by serving a niche where MES is lower, by differentiating so that they do not compete on unit cost, or by entering an adjacent market and expanding, so a large MES delays rather than prevents entry.\n• Assessment — the barrier is not a policy failure in itself: economies of scale are genuinely efficient, so the resulting concentration may deliver lower costs. The competition problem is the exercise of the resulting market power, which is why regulation targets conduct rather than scale.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that economies of scale are a significant barrier where minimum efficient scale is large relative to demand and the capital is sunk, but that their importance is frequently overstated: sunk costs, legal protection and network effects are more often decisive, and technological change has reduced the scale an entrant must achieve for itself.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained assessment comparing scale with other barriers, the sunk-cost point handled correctly and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) the barrier explained with limited assessment; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Discuss the view that a firm making a loss should always cease production.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `• The short-run rule: fixed costs are unavoidable, so only the comparison of revenue with variable cost is relevant. A firm should continue while AR is at least AVC, because each unit contributes towards fixed costs. Illustrate with AR between AVC and ATC.\n• Numerical case: with AR £14.00, AVC £11.00 and AFC £6.00 over 5 000 units, operating loses £15 000 while closing loses the full £30 000 of fixed costs. Continuing is the better of two bad options, so "always" is wrong immediately.\n• The long-run rule: in the long run all costs are variable, so a firm that cannot cover average total cost must exit. A loss that is expected to persist is a signal that resources should be reallocated, and blocking that signal wastes them.\n• Discussion — sunk and re-entry costs: closing destroys value that cannot be recovered. Skilled staff are dispersed, customers and contracts are lost, plant is sold at a discount, and reopening requires recruitment and re-establishment. These costs justify continuing through a loss that closure would not avoid.\n• Discussion — whether the loss is temporary: seasonal businesses, cyclical industries and firms in a recession expect recovery. A hotel closing every winter would forgo the profitable summer, so the relevant comparison is over the whole cycle rather than the current period.\n• Discussion — start-ups and investment phases: firms deliberately run losses to build market share, establish network effects or fund research, expecting a larger future profit stream. This is profit maximisation over a longer horizon, not a failure to maximise.\n• Discussion — interdependence within the firm: a loss-making product may be a loss leader that draws customers to profitable ones, or may share overheads whose burden would simply move elsewhere. Product-level accounting can make a profitable activity look unprofitable.\n• Discussion — non-commercial considerations: a state-owned or subsidised operation may be continued for social reasons — a rural transport route, a strategic industry — where the social benefit exceeds the private loss. That is a different criterion, and it should be stated as such rather than blurred with the commercial one.\n• Discussion — the case for the rule: against all this, persistent subsidy of loss-making activity keeps resources in low-value uses, and the sunk-cost fallacy — continuing because of past investment rather than future prospects — is a genuine and common error that the rule is designed to prevent.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that "always" is indefensible, because in the short run the correct rule compares revenue with variable cost, not with total cost, and because losses expected to be temporary or strategic are consistent with long-run profit maximisation; but a loss that cannot be expected to reverse should lead to exit, and the harder practical problem is distinguishing the two.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained discussion with the shut-down rule applied correctly, several qualifications developed and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) the rule explained with limited discussion; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that the law of diminishing returns is the most important influence on a firm\'s costs.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `• The claim in its strongest form: diminishing returns determines the shape of the short-run cost curves. Marginal cost equals the wage divided by the marginal product of labour, so falling marginal product means rising marginal cost, and this is what gives MC and AVC their U shape and generates the upward-sloping supply curve.\n• Evidence: the practice data show marginal product rising to 24 tonnes at the third worker and falling to 6 at the sixth, with marginal cost necessarily doing the reverse. No other single relationship explains the shape of the short-run curves.\n• Assessment — it applies only in the short run: the law requires a fixed factor. In the long run all factors vary, so returns to scale rather than diminishing returns determine cost, and the LRAC curve\'s shape comes from economies and diseconomies of scale. For any decision with a planning horizon — investment, plant size, entry — the law is simply not the relevant influence.\n• Assessment — factor prices move the curves further and faster: diminishing returns determines the shape of the cost curves; factor prices determine their position. A rise in energy or labour costs shifts every curve upwards immediately, and for most firms in most years this is the larger influence on actual costs. The UK National Living Wage rose to £12.71 an hour in April 2026, a change that affects labour-intensive firms\' costs far more than any movement along a production function.\n• Assessment — technology and productivity: a change in technique shifts the entire production function, so it can more than offset diminishing returns. Long-run cost reductions historically come overwhelmingly from technological change rather than from the position on a given function.\n• Assessment — capacity utilisation and fixed costs: for a capital-intensive firm, average fixed cost falls sharply with output and dominates unit cost over much of the range. A firm at 76% utilisation is affected far more by spreading overheads than by diminishing marginal product.\n• Assessment — management, organisation and scale: efficiency of organisation, supply-chain management and the exploitation of economies of scale produce cost differences between firms using the same technology and facing the same factor prices, which diminishing returns cannot explain at all.\n• Assessment — what the law does uniquely: it is the only explanation of why short-run marginal cost eventually rises, and therefore of why supply curves slope upwards. That is a foundational role no other influence performs.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that the law is the most important influence on the shape of short-run costs and is foundational to supply theory, but is not the most important influence on the level of a firm\'s costs, where factor prices, technology, scale and utilisation matter more. Its importance is theoretical and short-run; the practical determinants of what a firm actually pays lie elsewhere.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained assessment distinguishing shape from level and short run from long run, with a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) the law explained with limited assessment; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 20-21) states that "the firm's directors argue that only by doubling in size can it compete with the industry's largest producers".\n\nUsing the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that this firm should expand its scale of production.`,
        marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `• The case for expansion: long-run average cost falls 30.00% from £42.50 at 10 000 units to £29.75 at 40 000, so a firm below minimum efficient scale faces a permanent unit-cost disadvantage against larger rivals. Use an LRAC diagram with MES marked.\n• Competitive significance: a cost disadvantage of this size cannot be recovered through better management or marketing. It determines whether the firm can match the rival\'s £31.00 price and survive, so the directors\' argument has real force.\n• Dynamic argument: scale also supports research, branding and financial economies, so the cost gap tends to widen over time if the firm does not close it.\n• Evaluation — how far to expand: the case supports growth to minimum efficient scale, not doubling for its own sake. The data show LRAC constant between 40 000 and 80 000 and rising to £32.20 at 160 000, so expansion beyond 80 000 units would raise unit costs. "Doubling in size" is only the right answer from the right starting point.\n• Evaluation — demand is the binding question: the firm sells 38 000 units. Expansion is only worthwhile if the extra output can be sold, and selling substantially more is likely to require a price cut that offsets the cost saving. Cost data alone cannot justify an expansion decision.\n• Evaluation — the cheaper alternative: at 76% capacity utilisation the firm can raise output within its existing plant, spreading fixed costs with no capital outlay and no execution risk. This should be exhausted before new capacity is built.\n• Evaluation — the cost and risk of expanding: new plant requires capital that must be financed, takes time to build, and is sunk if demand disappoints. A firm that expands into a downturn carries high fixed costs into a period of low utilisation, which is how capital-intensive firms fail.\n• Evaluation — the directors\' incentives: managerial pay and status rise with firm size, and the practice data show directors\' pay rising from 4.1% to 21.7% of profit as scale increases. The recommendation to double in size may reflect the principal-agent problem rather than shareholders\' interests, which is a reason to scrutinise the case rather than accept it.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that the firm should grow, because it sits just below minimum efficient scale and the cost penalty of remaining there is large, but that the directors\' specific proposal to double in size is not supported by the evidence — the cost gain is exhausted at 40 000 to 80 000 units, the demand evidence is absent, and raising utilisation is the cheaper first step.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) extracts used throughout, diagram integrated, sustained evaluation distinguishing growth to MES from growth beyond it, with a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good use of extracts with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) some extract use, evaluation asserted; L2 (6-10) largely descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that technological change has reduced the importance of large scale production in most industries.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `• The case: digital technology has lowered the minimum efficient scale in many activities. Cloud computing replaces owned data centres with a variable cost; contract manufacturing lets a small firm buy someone else\'s scale; online platforms give access to a national market without a distribution network; and additive manufacturing makes short production runs viable.\n• Consequence for entry: where scale can be rented rather than built, the capital requirement falls and much of what remains is no longer sunk, so entry barriers fall and markets become more contestable. The growth of small firms serving niches is consistent with this.\n• Consequence for cost curves: the LRAC curve in many service industries is now flat over a wide range rather than steeply falling, so being small is no longer a serious cost handicap.\n• Evaluation — the opposite is true in the industries that matter most: semiconductor fabrication, pharmaceuticals, aerospace, battery manufacture and energy generation have seen minimum efficient scale rise sharply, because the fixed research and plant costs have grown faster than output. A leading-edge chip fabrication plant costs more in real terms than at any point in the industry\'s history.\n• Evaluation — digital industries have enormous scale economies of a different kind: near-zero marginal cost of reproduction means average cost falls continuously with users, and network effects mean value rises with scale too. These are more powerful than traditional plant economies, not less.\n• Evaluation — the evidence of concentration: if scale mattered less, concentration should be falling. Instead the CMA designated Google as having strategic market status in general search and search advertising in October 2025, and Apple and Google in mobile platforms later that month, with their platforms accounting for around 90 to 100% of UK mobile devices. That is the signature of increasing returns, not diminishing ones.\n• Evaluation — buying scale is not the same as not needing it: a small firm using cloud infrastructure and contract manufacturing is consuming someone else\'s economies of scale. Scale has been relocated up the supply chain and concentrated there, rather than eliminated.\n• Evaluation — it depends what "most industries" means: counted by number of industries, small-scale operation has become more viable; weighted by output or value added, production has become more concentrated. The two measures give opposite answers, and which is meant should be stated.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that technological change has reduced the scale an individual firm must achieve for itself in many service and retail activities, but has not reduced the importance of scale in the economy — it has shifted it to platform and infrastructure providers and increased it in research-intensive manufacturing, so the correct conclusion is relocation rather than reduction.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained evaluation with accurate dated evidence, the relocation point developed and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) one side developed; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that a firm\'s survival depends more on controlling its costs than on raising its revenue.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `• Framing: profit is total revenue minus total cost, so survival depends on both. The question is which margin a firm can more reliably act on, and which failure is more often fatal.\n• The case for costs: costs are largely within the firm\'s control. Productivity, procurement, process technology, scale and organisation are all internal decisions, whereas demand depends on consumers and rivals. A firm below minimum efficient scale carries a permanent handicap — the practice data show a 42.9% unit-cost disadvantage at 10 000 units against 40 000 — that no amount of selling effort can overcome.\n• Supporting point: in a competitive market the price is given, so the only route to profit is cost. Where the product is homogeneous, cost leadership is the whole of strategy, and the low-cost producer survives a price war that eliminates the others.\n• Supporting point on fixed costs: capital-intensive firms fail when utilisation collapses and fixed costs continue, which is a cost-structure failure rather than a revenue failure — and it explains why over-expansion at the wrong point in the cycle is so often fatal.\n• Assessment — revenue is the binding constraint more often than the model suggests: cost control has a floor. A firm cannot cut costs below the minimum of its LRAC curve, but revenue has no ceiling, so the larger opportunities usually lie on the revenue side. Differentiation, branding and new products shift the demand curve right and make it less elastic, raising both price and volume.\n• Assessment — the shut-down rule points at revenue: the short-run decision compares average revenue with average variable cost, and the practice data show a firm with AR of £14.00 surviving because revenue covers variable cost. Survival is defined in terms of revenue relative to cost, not cost alone.\n• Assessment — cost cutting can destroy revenue: reducing quality, service or marketing lowers cost and lowers willingness to pay, so the two are not independent. A cost-cutting strategy that erodes the product can be self-defeating.\n• Assessment — market structure decides which matters: in perfect competition the firm is a price taker and only cost matters. In monopolistic competition and oligopoly the firm has a downward-sloping demand curve, so differentiation and pricing strategy — revenue-side decisions — determine whether it survives at all. In digital markets with near-zero marginal cost, revenue and user growth are almost the entire question.\n• Assessment — the time horizon: cost control keeps a firm alive through a downturn; revenue growth is what makes it viable in the long run. Firms fail from cost shocks in the short run and from obsolete products in the long run.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that cost control is the more reliable determinant of short-run survival, because it is controllable and because unavoidable fixed costs are what actually kill firms, but that revenue is the more important determinant of long-run viability, since costs have a floor and demand does not; the correct answer therefore depends on the market structure and the horizon rather than being general.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained assessment linking the answer to market structure and time horizon, with a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) both sides described with limited assessment; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: `After considering Extract D, and the original evidence in Extracts A, B and C, would you recommend that this firm invest in a new plant taking its capacity to 160 000 units a year? Justify your recommendation.\n\nExtract D — written for this practice question\n"An independent appraisal found that the new plant would cost £48m and take three years to build, that total market demand is currently 310 000 units a year and has grown 1.4% a year for five years, that the firm's two largest rivals have each announced capacity expansions of 40 000 units, and that the firm's directors' bonuses are linked to revenue rather than to profit."`,
        marks: 25, command: 'Justify',
        markScheme: `• Restate the cost evidence: long-run average cost is £29.75 at 40 000 and 80 000 units but £32.20 at 160 000, so the proposed plant takes the firm beyond minimum efficient scale and into diseconomies of scale. On the cost evidence alone the proposal is wrong.\n• Extract D on demand: total market demand is 310 000 units growing at 1.4% a year, which adds about 4 340 units a year. Taking the firm from 38 000 to 160 000 units would require capturing over half the entire market, which market growth cannot supply. The output could only be sold by taking share from rivals, which means a price war.\n• Extract D on rivals: two rivals are each adding 40 000 units of capacity, 80 000 in total. Industry capacity is therefore rising by far more than demand, so the expected outcome is excess capacity, falling utilisation and downward pressure on the £31.00 price for everyone.\n• Extract D on the capital commitment: £48m sunk over three years is irreversible and arrives exactly when the rivals\' capacity comes on stream. High fixed costs carried into a period of low utilisation is the standard route to failure in a capital-intensive industry.\n• Extract D on incentives: directors\' bonuses are linked to revenue, not profit. A revenue-maximising manager expands beyond the profit-maximising output, and the earlier evidence shows directors\' pay rising with firm size. The proposal is consistent with the principal-agent problem rather than with shareholder value, which is a reason to discount the directors\' own case.\n• Weighing: Extracts A to C establish a genuine cost problem — the firm sits just below minimum efficient scale. Extract D shows that the proposed remedy massively overshoots what the cost data justify and what the market can absorb. The problem is real; the solution is not.\n• The recommendation and its conditions: a defensible recommendation is no to the 160 000-unit plant, and yes to a smaller expansion — raise utilisation from 76% within the existing plant first, then invest to reach the 40 000 to 80 000 range where average cost is at its minimum. Any capital commitment should be staged and conditional on demand evidence.\n• Counter-argument to address: if the rivals\' expansion succeeds in taking the market, a firm that does not expand may be left permanently sub-scale, and building later costs more and arrives too late. Pre-emptive capacity is a recognised strategy for deterring entry. A candidate who addresses this and still reaches a reasoned recommendation should be credited at the top.\n• Uncertainties in the evidence: the LRAC estimates are four points with nothing between 80 000 and 160 000; past demand growth of 1.4% need not continue; the rivals\' announcements may not be carried out; and the appraisal gives no estimate of the return on the £48m.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) all four extracts used, evidence weighed against evidence, a clear recommendation justified with conditions and the main counter-argument addressed; L4 (16-20) extracts used well with a justified recommendation; L3 (11-15) some use of extracts, recommendation asserted; L2 (6-10) descriptive use of extracts; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` }
    ]
  },
  '3.1.5': {
    name: 'Competitive & Concentrated Markets',
    questions: [
      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nMarket shares in the grocery market of Country X, 2026\n  Firm 1 = 27.3% ; Firm 2 = 15.1% ; Firm 3 = 13.7% ; Firm 4 = 10.2%\n  Firm 5 = 9.2% ; Firm 6 = 8.1% ; all other retailers = 16.4%\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the four-firm concentration ratio in this market.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: sum the market shares of the four largest firms = 27.3 + 15.1 + 13.7 + 10.2.\nAnswer: 66.30%.\n2 marks for 66.30%; 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for summing the wrong number of firms (e.g. CR5 = 75.50% or CR3 = 56.10%).` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nThree-firm concentration ratio in the grocery market of Country X\n  2017: Firm 1 = 28.1% ; Firm 2 = 16.4% ; Firm 3 = 15.3%\n  2026: Firm 1 = 27.3% ; Firm 2 = 15.1% ; Firm 3 = 13.7%\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the change in the three-firm concentration ratio between 2017 and 2026, in percentage points.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: CR3 in 2017 = 28.1 + 16.4 + 15.3 = 59.8%. CR3 in 2026 = 27.3 + 15.1 + 13.7 = 56.1%. Change = 56.1 - 59.8.\nAnswer: a fall of 3.70 percentage points.\n2 marks for -3.70 percentage points; 1 mark for both ratios correct without the difference, or for the correct method with an arithmetic slip. Do not credit "3.70%" alone if the answer treats it as a percentage change — the percentage change would be -6.19%.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA monopolist at its profit-maximising output\n  Price = £48.00 ; average total cost = £31.00 ; output = 240 000 units a year\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the monopolist's annual supernormal profit.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: supernormal profit = (AR - AC) × Q = (48.00 - 31.00) × 240 000 = 17.00 × 240 000.\nAnswer: £4 080 000.00.\n2 marks for £4 080 000.00; 1 mark for identifying the £17.00 profit per unit without multiplying by output, or for the correct method with an arithmetic slip.\nNote: normal profit is already included in average total cost, so the whole of this figure is profit above the minimum required to keep the firm in the industry.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA market in which marginal cost is constant at £31.00 per unit\n  Under monopoly:            price = £48.00 ; output = 240 000 units\n  Under perfect competition: price = £31.00 ; output = 320 000 units\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the deadweight welfare loss caused by the monopoly.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: the deadweight loss is the triangle between the demand curve and marginal cost over the units not produced = 0.5 × (48.00 - 31.00) × (320 000 - 240 000) = 0.5 × 17.00 × 80 000.\nAnswer: £680 000.00.\n2 marks for £680 000.00; 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for omitting the factor of one half (£1 360 000).\nNote: this measures the surplus lost on transactions that would have been mutually beneficial but do not take place — it is not a transfer to the monopolist, which is a separate rectangle.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA rail operator's revenue with and without price discrimination\n  Single price:      £48.00 × 200 000 passengers\n  Two prices:        £62.00 × 80 000 peak passengers  and  £41.00 × 140 000 off-peak passengers\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the percentage increase in total revenue from price discriminating.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: single-price revenue = 48.00 × 200 000 = £9 600 000. Discriminating revenue = (62.00 × 80 000) + (41.00 × 140 000) = £4 960 000 + £5 740 000 = £10 700 000.\nPercentage increase: (10 700 000 - 9 600 000) / 9 600 000 × 100 = 1 100 000 / 9 600 000 × 100.\nAnswer: 11.46% (11.4583...% to two decimal places).\n2 marks for 11.46%; 1 mark for both revenue figures correct but the percentage wrong, or for the correct method with an arithmetic slip.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA competition authority's penalty on one firm in a bid-rigging cartel\n  The firm's annual turnover = £412m\n  Penalty imposed            = £26.78m\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate, to two decimal places, the penalty as a percentage of the firm's annual turnover.`,
        marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: 26.78 / 412 × 100.\nAnswer: 6.50%.\n2 marks for 6.50%; 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic or rounding slip.\nContext: under UK competition law the maximum penalty for an infringement is 10% of a firm's worldwide turnover, so a figure of this size represents a serious infringement short of the statutory maximum.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nMarket shares in the grocery market of Country X, 2026\n  Firm 1 = 27.3% ; Firm 2 = 15.1% ; Firm 3 = 13.7% ; Firm 4 = 10.2%\n  Firm 5 = 9.2% ; Firm 6 = 8.1% ; all other retailers = 16.4%\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that this market has an oligopolistic structure.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• The four-firm concentration ratio is 27.3 + 15.1 + 13.7 + 10.2 = 66.30%, and the six largest firms account for 83.60% of the market. A small number of firms therefore supplies the large majority of output, which is the defining structural feature of oligopoly.\n• No firm is dominant: the largest holds 27.3%, well below the 40% threshold at which a competition authority would normally presume a position of dominance, so this is oligopoly rather than monopoly.\n• The remaining 16.4% is spread across many small retailers, so the market is not perfectly competitive either — entry at scale would require matching firms with two-figure shares.\n• Behavioural implication: with so few significant firms, each one's pricing, promotion and range decisions materially affect the others. That interdependence is what makes oligopoly behave differently from other structures and is the reason game theory is used to model it.\nLevels: 4 = the concentration ratio calculated correctly AND the structural and behavioural features of oligopoly both explained; 3 = ratio calculated with some explanation; 2 = shares described without calculation; 1 = a relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nMarket shares in the grocery market of Country X\n                      2017     2026\n  Firm 1              28.1%    27.3%\n  Firm 2              16.4%    15.1%\n  Firm 3              15.3%    13.7%\n  Discounters D and E  9.8%    18.3%\n  All other retailers  30.4%    25.6%\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that competition in this market has increased since 2017.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• The three-firm concentration ratio fell from 59.8% to 56.1%, a fall of 3.70 percentage points or 6.19%, so the largest incumbents supply a smaller share of the market than they did.\n• The combined share of the two discounters rose from 9.8% to 18.3%, an increase of 8.5 percentage points — they have nearly doubled their share, which is direct evidence of successful entry and expansion.\n• Explanation: each of the three largest firms lost share, so the gain is not a reshuffling between incumbents but a transfer to new competitors. Successful entry indicates that barriers to entry were not prohibitive.\n• Qualification that earns full marks: a lower concentration ratio is evidence of increased competition but not proof of it. The ratio says nothing about prices, margins or whether the remaining firms collude, so it is an indicator of structure rather than a measure of conduct.\nLevels: 4 = both changes calculated precisely AND the increase in competition explained with a qualification; 3 = changes calculated with some explanation; 2 = the direction of change identified without calculation; 1 = a relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA monopolist at its profit-maximising output\n  Price = £48.00 ; marginal cost = £31.00 ; average total cost = £31.00 ; output = 240 000 units\n  Minimum of the firm's average cost curve occurs at 310 000 units\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that this firm is both allocatively and productively inefficient.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Allocative inefficiency: price of £48.00 exceeds marginal cost of £31.00. Allocative efficiency requires P = MC, so that the value consumers place on the last unit equals the cost of producing it. Here consumers value extra units at more than they cost, so output is below the socially optimal level.\n• The gap is £17.00 per unit, and the resulting welfare loss on the units not produced is the deadweight loss triangle.\n• Productive inefficiency: the firm produces 240 000 units while its average cost is minimised at 310 000 units, so it is not producing at the lowest attainable unit cost. Productive efficiency requires output at the minimum of the average cost curve.\n• Explanation of why both follow from monopoly: the firm maximises profit where MC = MR, and because MR lies below AR for a price maker, this output is below both the allocatively and the productively efficient levels. Restricting output to raise price is the source of both inefficiencies.\nLevels: 4 = both conditions stated correctly AND the data used to show each is violated; 3 = both identified with some explanation; 2 = one form of inefficiency explained; 1 = a relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA rail operator's passengers and revenue\n  Single price of £48.00:  200 000 passengers ; revenue = £9 600 000\n  Peak £62.00 / off-peak £41.00: 80 000 peak and 140 000 off-peak passengers ; revenue = £10 700 000\n\nExplain how the data in Table 1 show that price discrimination has increased both the operator's revenue and the number of journeys made.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Revenue rose from £9 600 000 to £10 700 000, an increase of £1 100 000 or 11.46%.\n• Total passengers rose from 200 000 to 220 000, an increase of 10.00%, because the £41.00 off-peak fare brings in travellers who would not pay £48.00.\n• Explanation of the mechanism: the operator separates the market into a segment with relatively price inelastic demand — peak commuters with no alternative — and one with elastic demand, and charges each what it will bear. This converts consumer surplus into revenue in the peak segment and captures otherwise lost sales in the off-peak one.\n• Distributional point: peak passengers are worse off, paying £62.00 rather than £48.00, while off-peak passengers are better off. The total number of journeys rises, so output moves closer to the allocatively efficient level even though some consumers lose.\nLevels: 4 = both increases calculated correctly AND the elasticity-based mechanism explained with the distributional point; 3 = calculations with some explanation; 2 = one change calculated; 1 = a relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: `Extract B — written for this practice question\nAverage unleaded petrol prices at four forecourts on the same road, pence per litre\n  Day 1:  Firm A = 142.9 ; Firm B = 142.9 ; Firm C = 143.9 ; Firm D = 142.9\n  Day 2:  Firm A = 139.9 (price cut announced) ; Firm B = 142.9 ; Firm C = 143.9 ; Firm D = 142.9\n  Day 3:  Firm A = 139.9 ; Firm B = 139.9 ; Firm C = 139.9 ; Firm D = 139.9\n\nExplain how the data in Extract B show evidence of interdependence between these firms.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Prices are almost identical on Day 1, with three of the four firms at exactly 142.9p, despite no agreement being evident. Price similarity of this kind is the first indicator of interdependence.\n• When Firm A cut its price to 139.9p on Day 2, all three rivals matched it exactly within a day. Each firm's price therefore depends on what the others do rather than on its own costs alone.\n• Explanation: the product is homogeneous and the forecourts are on the same road, so cross elasticity of demand between them is very high. A firm that did not match would lose a large share of its sales; a firm that raised price alone would lose almost all of them.\n• Distinction that earns full marks: matching behaviour of this kind is consistent with tacit collusion but does not prove an agreement. Identical prices for a homogeneous good sold side by side are exactly what independent competition would also produce, which is why proving a cartel requires evidence of communication, not of parallel pricing.\nLevels: 4 = the matching pattern used precisely AND interdependence explained with the tacit-collusion caveat; 3 = the pattern identified with some explanation; 2 = the pattern described, explanation thin; 1 = one relevant observation only.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — written for this practice question\nA regional bus market over five years\n  Number of operators:              start = 3 ; end = 3\n  Firms entering during the period: 4\n  Firms exiting during the period:  4\n  Average length of time a new entrant remained in the market: 14 months\n  Share of an operator's capital that can be resold or redeployed: 91%\n\nExplain how the data in Table 2 show that this market is highly contestable despite having only three operators.`,
        marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• The number of firms is unchanged at three, so a structural measure such as the concentration ratio would classify the market as a tight oligopoly and suggest weak competition.\n• The flow data tell a different story: four firms entered and four exited over five years, with entrants staying an average of 14 months. That turnover is direct evidence that entry and exit are both feasible and frequent.\n• The key figure is that 91% of an operator's capital can be resold or redeployed, so sunk costs are very low. Buses can be sold or moved to another route, which makes hit-and-run entry viable.\n• Explanation: contestability depends on the height of entry and exit barriers, particularly sunk costs, rather than on the number of firms. The threat of entry alone disciplines incumbents, so the three operators are likely to price close to average cost even though there are only three of them.\nLevels: 4 = the entry/exit and sunk-cost data both used precisely AND contestability explained as distinct from structure; 3 = the data used with some explanation; 2 = the turnover noted without the sunk-cost point; 1 = one relevant figure quoted only.` },

      { q: 'With the help of a diagram, explain why a firm in perfect competition earns only normal profit in long-run equilibrium.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: two panels — the industry, with supply and demand determining price P1, and the firm, facing a horizontal AR = MR = price line tangent to the minimum of its average cost curve, with MC cutting AC at that point.\n• The assumptions that drive the result: many buyers and sellers, a homogeneous product, perfect information, and — crucially — freedom of entry and exit with no sunk costs. The firm is a price taker, so AR = MR = P.\n• Short run: if price exceeds average cost the firm earns supernormal profit, shown as the rectangle between AR and AC at the output where MC = MR.\n• The adjustment: supernormal profit is a signal. With free entry, new firms enter, industry supply shifts right and the market price falls. Entry continues until no supernormal profit remains.\n• Long-run equilibrium: price has fallen to the minimum of average cost, so AR = AC and only normal profit is earned. The same process works in reverse if firms are making losses, with exit raising the price until losses are eliminated.\n• Efficiency consequences that earn L3: at this point the firm is productively efficient, since it produces at the minimum of AC, and allocatively efficient, since P = MC. These two results are why perfect competition is used as the welfare benchmark even though no real market satisfies its assumptions.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate two-panel diagram with tangency at the AC minimum, the entry mechanism developed and both efficiency conditions identified; L2 (4-6) diagram with some explanation; L1 (1-3) assertion that competition removes profit, diagram missing or wrong.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 6-7) states that "the company has no significant competitor and has raised its prices every year for a decade". With the help of a diagram, explain how a profit-maximising monopolist determines its price and output, and show its supernormal profit.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: downward-sloping AR (demand) curve with MR below it and twice as steep, U-shaped AC and MC curves. Output Qm where MC = MR, price Pm read up to the AR curve, and supernormal profit shaded as the rectangle (Pm - AC) × Qm.\n• Why the firm faces a downward-sloping demand curve: as the sole supplier it is the industry, so to sell more it must lower the price. This is what distinguishes a price maker from a price taker.\n• Why MR lies below AR: the price cut needed to sell an extra unit applies to all units, so marginal revenue is the new price minus the revenue lost on previous units.\n• The profit-maximising rule: output where MC = MR, because below that output an extra unit adds more to revenue than to cost and above it the reverse. Price is then set by what the demand curve will bear at that output — the firm does not choose both independently.\n• Numerical illustration: with price £48.00, average cost £31.00 and output 240 000 units, supernormal profit is £17.00 × 240 000 = £4 080 000.\n• Why the profit persists: unlike in perfect competition, barriers to entry prevent new firms competing the profit away, so supernormal profit survives into the long run. That persistence, rather than its existence, is the defining feature of monopoly.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram with MR below AR, MC = MR output and profit correctly shaded, and the barriers point made; L2 (4-6) diagram with some explanation, profit shaded inaccurately; L1 (1-3) diagram missing or MR omitted.` },

      { q: 'With the help of a diagram, explain the deadweight welfare loss that arises when an industry is monopolised rather than perfectly competitive.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: a market demand curve with a horizontal (or upward-sloping) MC curve. Mark the competitive outcome where P = MC at Qc, and the monopoly outcome where MC = MR at Qm with price Pm. Shade the transfer rectangle from consumers to the monopolist and the deadweight loss triangle between Qm and Qc.\n• The competitive benchmark: with P = MC, every unit for which consumers' valuation exceeds the cost of production is produced, so the sum of consumer and producer surplus is maximised.\n• The monopoly outcome: because MR lies below AR, MC = MR occurs at a lower output. Price rises above marginal cost, so output is restricted below the efficient level.\n• The transfer: consumers pay more per unit on the units still bought, so surplus is transferred from consumers to the monopolist. This is a distributional change, not a welfare loss, since one party's gain is the other's loss.\n• The deadweight loss: on the units between Qm and Qc, consumers valued the good above its marginal cost but it is not produced. That surplus is lost to everyone, which is why it is a welfare loss rather than a transfer. In the practice data it equals 0.5 × £17.00 × 80 000 = £680 000.\n• Analytical qualification that earns L3: this comparison assumes identical cost curves. If the monopolist has lower costs through economies of scale, the MC curve shifts down and the welfare comparison can reverse; and the static loss ignores any dynamic efficiency gain from supernormal profit funding innovation.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram distinguishing the transfer from the deadweight loss, with the cost-curve qualification developed; L2 (4-6) diagram with some explanation, transfer and loss conflated; L1 (1-3) assertion that monopoly is bad for consumers.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 11-12) states that "it would make no sense to build a second network of water pipes alongside the first". With the help of a diagram, explain what is meant by a natural monopoly and why it presents a problem for regulators.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: a long-run average cost curve falling continuously across the whole range of market demand, with MC below AC throughout, and the demand curve cutting LRAC while it is still falling. Mark the unregulated monopoly output, the allocatively efficient output where P = MC, and the point where P = AC.\n• Definition: a natural monopoly exists where the minimum efficient scale exceeds total market demand, so one firm can supply the whole market at lower average cost than two or more could. Water networks, electricity transmission and rail track are the standard cases.\n• Why duplication is wasteful: the fixed cost of the network is enormous and largely sunk, while the marginal cost of supplying an extra customer is very small. Two networks would double the fixed cost and halve each firm's output, raising average cost for everyone.\n• The regulator's dilemma: allocative efficiency requires P = MC, but because AC is falling throughout, MC lies below AC. Pricing at marginal cost therefore means the firm makes a loss and cannot survive without a subsidy.\n• The practical compromise: regulators typically set price at average cost — allowing a normal return on capital — which is not allocatively efficient but is financially sustainable. RPI-X or CPI-X price controls do this while giving the firm an incentive to cut costs, since it keeps savings beyond X.\n• Further problems that earn L3: the regulator has worse information about costs than the firm does, so the allowed return may be too generous; and regulatory capture is a persistent risk where the regulator depends on the firm for information and expertise.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram with falling LRAC across demand and MC below AC, the P = MC loss problem explained and a regulatory response evaluated; L2 (4-6) natural monopoly explained with some regulatory discussion; L1 (1-3) definition only.` },

      { q: 'With the help of a diagram, explain how third-degree price discrimination raises a firm\'s total revenue and profit.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: two sub-market panels sharing a common marginal cost. In the inelastic sub-market, MC = MR gives a low quantity and a high price; in the elastic sub-market, a larger quantity and a lower price. A third panel or note shows the combined output.\n• Definition: third-degree price discrimination is charging different prices to different identifiable groups of consumers for the same good, where the price difference does not reflect a difference in cost.\n• The three necessary conditions: the firm must have price-setting power; it must be able to separate consumers into groups with different price elasticities of demand; and it must be able to prevent resale between the groups, or arbitrage would destroy the price difference.\n• The mechanism: the firm equates marginal cost with marginal revenue in each sub-market separately. Because MR differs between them at any single price, setting a higher price where demand is inelastic and a lower one where it is elastic raises total revenue for the same or greater output.\n• Numerical illustration: charging £62.00 to 80 000 peak and £41.00 to 140 000 off-peak passengers yields £10 700 000 against £9 600 000 at a single £48.00 price — an 11.46% increase — while total passengers rise from 200 000 to 220 000.\n• Welfare analysis that earns L3: consumer surplus is converted into producer surplus in the inelastic segment, so those consumers lose. But output rises, so some consumers who were priced out are now served, and in a natural monopoly or a firm with heavy fixed costs discrimination may be what makes provision viable at all. The welfare effect is therefore genuinely ambiguous.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate two-panel diagram, the three conditions stated, the MC = MR in each market mechanism explained and the ambiguous welfare effect discussed; L2 (4-6) conditions stated with some explanation; L1 (1-3) definition and an example only.` },

      { q: 'With the help of diagrams, explain why a firm in monopolistic competition earns supernormal profit in the short run but only normal profit in the long run.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagrams: short run — a downward-sloping AR with MR below it, output at MC = MR, price above AC and supernormal profit shaded. Long run — the AR curve has shifted left and become more elastic, and is tangent to the AC curve at the output where MC = MR, so AR = AC.\n• The model's assumptions: many firms, each small relative to the market; a differentiated product, which gives each firm a downward-sloping demand curve; and low barriers to entry and exit.\n• Short run: differentiation gives the firm some price-setting power, so it can charge above marginal cost and earn supernormal profit if demand is strong enough.\n• The adjustment: supernormal profit attracts entry. Because barriers are low, new firms enter with close substitutes, which takes demand away from each existing firm. Each firm's demand curve shifts left and becomes more elastic as more substitutes become available.\n• Long-run equilibrium: entry stops when AR is just tangent to AC, so only normal profit is earned. Tangency to a downward-sloping AR curve can only occur on the falling part of AC.\n• Efficiency consequences that earn L3: the firm is productively inefficient, since it produces below the minimum of AC — this is the excess capacity theorem — and allocatively inefficient, since P exceeds MC. The offsetting benefit is product variety, which the perfectly competitive benchmark does not provide; whether the variety is worth the excess capacity is a genuine trade-off, not a straightforward failure.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) both diagrams accurate with long-run tangency, the entry mechanism developed and the excess capacity trade-off discussed; L2 (4-6) both stages explained with partial diagrams; L1 (1-3) one stage only.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 15-16) states that "prices on the route have not moved in eighteen months, even though fuel costs have swung sharply". With the help of a diagram, explain the kinked demand curve model of oligopoly.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: a demand curve kinked at the prevailing price P1 — relatively elastic above the kink, relatively inelastic below it — with the corresponding MR curve showing a vertical discontinuity directly beneath the kink. Draw two MC curves both passing through the gap.\n• The behavioural assumption: each firm believes that if it raises its price rivals will not follow, but if it cuts its price rivals will match. This asymmetry of expected reactions is what produces the kink.\n• Above the kink: a price rise is not matched, so the firm loses a large share of its sales to rivals whose prices are now lower. Demand is elastic, and raising price reduces revenue.\n• Below the kink: a price cut is matched, so the firm gains little extra volume — only the small increase from the whole market expanding. Demand is inelastic, and cutting price reduces revenue too.\n• The discontinuity in MR: because the demand curve changes slope abruptly, marginal revenue jumps downward at the kink. Any marginal cost curve passing through that vertical gap gives the same profit-maximising output and price, so the price stays at P1 even when costs change substantially — which is exactly what the extract describes.\n• Evaluation that earns L3: the model explains price rigidity but not how the initial price was reached, and it fails to explain observed price wars and coordinated increases. Menu costs, long-term contracts and tacit collusion offer competing explanations of the same rigidity, so the model is a useful illustration of interdependence rather than a complete theory.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram with the MR discontinuity and both cost curves, the asymmetric reaction assumption explained and the model evaluated; L2 (4-6) diagram with some explanation, MR gap omitted; L1 (1-3) assertion that oligopoly prices are sticky.` },

      { q: 'Analyse, using game theory, why firms in an oligopoly may fail to cooperate even when cooperation would raise their joint profits.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `• The set-up: construct a two-firm payoff matrix in which each firm chooses a high price or a low price. Joint profits are highest when both choose the high price; each firm individually does best by undercutting while the other holds the high price; and both are worst off when both cut.\n• Illustrative payoffs (£m, Firm A first): both high (8, 8); A low, B high (11, 2); A high, B low (2, 11); both low (4, 4).\n• The dominant strategy: whatever Firm B does, Firm A earns more by choosing the low price — 11 against 8 if B prices high, 4 against 2 if B prices low. The same reasoning applies to B.\n• The Nash equilibrium: both choose the low price and earn 4 each, even though both would earn 8 by cooperating. Neither can improve by changing strategy unilaterally, so the outcome is stable despite being jointly inferior. This is the prisoners' dilemma.\n• Why this explains cartel instability: a cartel agreement puts the firms at the cooperative outcome, but each member has a standing incentive to cheat by secretly cutting price or exceeding its quota. The larger the number of members and the harder it is to observe each other's behaviour, the stronger that incentive.\n• Why cooperation is nonetheless observed: with repeated interaction, a firm that cheats can be punished by a price war in later periods. If the discounted future loss exceeds the one-off gain, cooperation becomes self-enforcing, which is why tacit collusion is most common in stable oligopolies with few firms and transparent prices.\n• Policy implication: competition authorities exploit the dilemma directly through leniency programmes, which offer immunity to the first cartel member to confess, deliberately strengthening each member's incentive to defect.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) a correct payoff matrix with the dominant strategy and Nash equilibrium identified, and the repeated-game or leniency extension developed; L2 (4-6) the dilemma explained with some analysis; L1 (1-3) description of collusion with no game-theoretic reasoning.` },

      { q: 'Analyse how the contestability of a market affects the behaviour of the firms already operating in it.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `• Definition: a market is contestable when entry and exit are cheap, in particular when sunk costs are low, so that a firm can enter, compete and leave without irrecoverable loss. Perfect contestability is the limiting case in which entry and exit are costless.\n• The central proposition: it is the threat of entry, not the number of firms, that disciplines incumbents. A monopolist in a perfectly contestable market must price close to average cost, because any supernormal profit invites hit-and-run entry.\n• Effect on pricing: incumbents forgo profit-maximising prices in favour of limit pricing — setting price low enough that entry is unattractive. Observed prices can therefore be close to competitive even in a highly concentrated market.\n• Effect on costs and efficiency: contestability removes the slack that market power permits, so X-inefficiency is squeezed out. Incumbents must operate at minimum average cost because a more efficient entrant could otherwise undercut them.\n• Effect on strategic behaviour: incumbents may instead invest in raising barriers — building brand loyalty through sunk advertising, locking customers into contracts, securing exclusive access to inputs or distribution, or creating excess capacity as a credible threat to flood the market if entry occurs.\n• Evidence from the practice data: a bus market with three operators saw four entries and four exits in five years, with 91% of capital recoverable. Structural measures would call this a tight oligopoly; the flow data show it behaving competitively.\n• Analytical limits: perfect contestability requires entrants to have identical costs and incumbents to be unable to respond before the entrant exits — both strong assumptions. Consumer loyalty, reputation and response speed all limit hit-and-run entry in practice, so contestability moderates incumbent behaviour rather than replicating perfect competition.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) the sunk-cost mechanism analysed, several behavioural effects developed and the limits of the theory discussed; L2 (4-6) contestability explained with some analysis; L1 (1-3) definition with an example.` },

      { q: 'With the help of a diagram, explain what is meant by X-inefficiency and why it is associated with firms that face little competition.',
        marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `• Diagram: a cost diagram showing the firm operating on an average cost curve AC2 that lies above the attainable minimum-cost curve AC1, with the vertical gap at the chosen output labelled as X-inefficiency.\n• Definition: X-inefficiency, identified by Leibenstein, is production at a cost above the minimum attainable for that output. It is not a movement along a cost curve but the firm operating above its own cost curve altogether.\n• Distinction that matters: productive inefficiency means being at the wrong point on the correct cost curve; X-inefficiency means being on the wrong cost curve. A firm can be X-inefficient at any output.\n• Why it arises without competition: supernormal profit protected by barriers to entry removes the penalty for waste. Managers face no threat of being undercut, so there is no pressure to negotiate hard with suppliers, eliminate overmanning, or resist cost-raising perquisites.\n• The link to the principal-agent problem: where ownership is divorced from control, managers derive utility from larger budgets, staff and expenditure. Competition normally constrains this by threatening the firm's survival; without competition the constraint is absent.\n• Consequences: the welfare cost of monopoly includes X-inefficiency as well as the deadweight loss from restricted output, and it may be the larger of the two, since it applies to every unit produced rather than only to the units forgone.\n• Evidence and remedies that earn L3: the cost reductions that typically follow deregulation, privatisation and the entry of low-cost competitors are consistent with substantial pre-existing X-inefficiency. Contestability, regulatory benchmarking and performance-linked pay all attack it.\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram distinguishing X-inefficiency from productive inefficiency, the competitive-pressure mechanism developed and a consequence or remedy discussed; L2 (4-6) definition with some explanation; L1 (1-3) assertion that monopolies are wasteful.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) and Extract B — written for this practice question\nThe grocery market of Country X\n                                   2017     2026\n  Three-firm concentration ratio    59.8%    56.1%\n  Combined share of two discounters  9.8%    18.3%\n  Average net profit margin, top 3   3.9%     2.8%\n  Real average grocery price index   100      96.4\n  Number of firms entering the market at national scale: 0\n\nExtract B states: "The discounters entered the market in the 1990s; no new national entrant has appeared since."\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data suggest that competition in this market has intensified since 2017?`,
        marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `• Evidence for intensification: the three-firm concentration ratio fell by 3.70 percentage points, or 6.19%; the discounters' combined share rose by 8.5 percentage points to 18.3%; net margins for the top three fell from 3.9% to 2.8%, a fall of 28.21%; and real prices fell by 3.6%.\n• Why the combination is persuasive: falling margins alongside falling real prices is the pattern competition predicts. A fall in concentration alone would prove little, since it says nothing about conduct, but margin and price data are measures of outcome rather than structure.\n• Evidence against: Extract B is decisive on one point — no new national entrant has appeared since the 1990s. The pressure comes from firms already present expanding, not from entry, so barriers to entry at national scale remain effectively prohibitive.\n• Further caution on the structural measure: CR3 of 56.1% still describes a tight oligopoly, and the fall of 3.70 percentage points over nine years is modest. A concentration ratio also ignores what happens in local markets, where a single store may face no competitor within a reasonable distance.\n• Alternative explanations of the margin and price data: margins can fall because of rising input costs rather than competition, and real prices can fall because of productivity gains or falling world commodity prices. The data do not isolate the competitive channel.\n• Limitations: two snapshots with no intervening years conceal the path; net margin is an accounting measure affected by depreciation and financing choices; and no data are given on quality, range or service, on which competition can also occur.\n• Judgement: the outcome data — falling margins and falling real prices — provide real support for intensified competition, and are stronger evidence than the structural measure. But the absence of any new national entrant since the 1990s shows the improvement came from expansion by existing firms rather than from a genuinely contestable market, so the intensification is real but bounded and could reverse if the discounters' growth stalls.\nLevels: L3 (8-10) figures calculated and used precisely, structure distinguished from conduct and outcome, limitations discussed and a supported judgement reached; L2 (4-7) relevant data used with some evaluation; L1 (1-3) data described without judgement.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — written for this practice question\nFour regional bus markets, most recent year\n  Market   Operators   Firms entering in 5 yrs   Recoverable share of capital   Average fare\n    P          1                  0                       34%                    £3.20\n    Q          3                  4                       91%                    £2.10\n    R          6                  1                       41%                    £2.95\n    S          2                  3                       88%                    £2.15\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data suggest that the number of firms in a market is a poor guide to how competitively it behaves?`,
        marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `• The pattern that supports the claim: Market Q has three operators and the second-lowest fare at £2.10; Market S has only two and a fare of £2.15. Market R has six operators — twice as many as Q — yet charges £2.95, 40.48% more than Q.\n• The variable that does track fares: the recoverable share of capital. The two markets with high recoverability (91% and 88%) have the two lowest fares and the most entry; the two with low recoverability (34% and 41%) have the two highest fares and almost no entry.\n• The theoretical interpretation: this is exactly what contestability theory predicts. Low sunk costs make hit-and-run entry credible, so incumbents price close to average cost regardless of how few of them there are. The number of firms measures structure; sunk costs measure the threat of entry.\n• Supporting evidence from the flows: four entries in Market Q and three in Market S against zero and one in the low-recoverability markets confirms that entry actually occurs where exit is cheap.\n• Limitations: four markets is far too small a sample to establish a relationship, and the correlation could be coincidental. Fares are not adjusted for route length, density, subsidy, vehicle age or service frequency, so they may not be comparable.\n• Further limitations: cost conditions may differ systematically — markets P and R may be rural with genuinely higher unit costs, which would explain both the higher fares and the low capital recoverability without any reference to competition. Causation could also run the other way, since profitable markets attract entrants.\n• Judgement: the data support the claim that firm numbers alone are a poor guide, since Market R has the most firms and the highest fare, and they point clearly towards sunk costs as the better indicator. But with four observations, no cost controls and no adjustment for route characteristics, the evidence illustrates the theory rather than testing it.\nLevels: L3 (8-10) the counter-example identified, the sunk-cost variable used, limitations developed and a supported judgement reached; L2 (4-7) relevant data used with some evaluation; L1 (1-3) description only.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main barriers to entry that can protect a firm from new competition.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Framing: a barrier to entry is anything that allows an incumbent to earn supernormal profit in the long run without attracting entry. Barriers may be structural, arising from the technology or the market, or strategic, created deliberately by the incumbent.\n• Economies of scale and minimum efficient scale: where MES is large relative to market demand, an entrant must either build at scale, adding enough output to depress the price, or accept a permanent unit-cost disadvantage. A natural monopoly is the extreme case, where MES exceeds total demand.\n• Sunk costs: contestability theory holds that irrecoverability, not size, is what deters entry. Heavy spending on brand advertising, specialised plant or market-specific research cannot be recovered on exit, so the entrant bears the whole risk.\n• Legal barriers: patents, copyright, licences, franchises and regulatory approval confer exclusive rights. Patent protection is deliberate policy, trading static inefficiency for dynamic efficiency by rewarding innovation.\n• Control of essential inputs or distribution: ownership of a scarce resource, of a network, or of shelf space and distribution channels can exclude rivals as effectively as any legal barrier.\n• Network effects and switching costs: where a product becomes more valuable as more people use it, an incumbent's installed base is self-reinforcing. Switching costs, whether contractual, technical or habitual, lock customers in. The Competition and Markets Authority's designation of Google in general search and search advertising, and of Apple and Google in mobile platforms, as having strategic market status in October 2025 rested on exactly these features, with the two mobile platforms accounting for around 90 to 100% of UK devices.\n• Brand loyalty and product differentiation: sustained advertising creates a perceived difference that an entrant must spend heavily to overcome, and that spending is sunk.\n• Strategic barriers: limit pricing sets price below the profit-maximising level to make entry unattractive; predatory pricing drives an entrant out by pricing below cost and is illegal for a dominant firm; excess capacity is a credible threat to expand output if entry occurs.\n• Organising judgement: the effectiveness of a barrier depends on whether it is credible and durable. Legal and network barriers tend to be the most durable, strategic barriers the least, since they cost the incumbent money to maintain.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several barriers explained with mechanisms, accurate dated examples and the structural/strategic distinction made; L2 (6-10) barriers listed with some explanation; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main types of economic efficiency and how each relates to market structure.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Productive efficiency: producing at the minimum of the average cost curve, so that no output could be produced more cheaply. On a PPF, any point on the frontier is productively efficient. Achieved in perfect competition in the long run, because entry drives price down to minimum AC; not achieved in monopoly or monopolistic competition, where output is below that point.\n• Allocative efficiency: producing the combination society values most, which requires price to equal marginal cost, so that the value of the last unit to consumers equals its cost. Achieved in perfect competition, where P = AR = MR = MC; not achieved wherever the firm faces a downward-sloping demand curve, since MR is then below AR and profit maximisation gives P above MC.\n• Dynamic efficiency: improvement over time through investment, research and innovation, which shifts cost curves downwards and creates new products. It requires the resources and the incentive to invest, so supernormal profit and some protection from imitation may be necessary — which is Schumpeter's argument that monopoly can be dynamically efficient even while being statically inefficient.\n• X-efficiency: operating on the lowest attainable cost curve rather than above it. Competitive pressure squeezes out organisational slack; market power permits it, which is why X-inefficiency is associated with protected firms.\n• Static versus dynamic: productive, allocative and X-efficiency are properties at a point in time; dynamic efficiency concerns change over time. A structure can score well on one and badly on the other, which is the central trade-off in competition policy.\n• Relation to structure, summarised: perfect competition achieves productive and allocative efficiency but generates no supernormal profit to fund innovation; monopoly fails both static tests but may fund dynamic efficiency; monopolistic competition fails both static tests and delivers variety instead; oligopoly is indeterminate, depending on whether firms compete or collude.\n• The policy consequence: because no structure delivers all four, competition policy weighs a static welfare loss against a possible dynamic gain, which is why merger assessment considers efficiencies as well as market shares.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) all four types defined precisely and related to structures with reasoning, and the static/dynamic trade-off developed; L2 (6-10) types defined with some linkage; L1 (1-5) definitions only.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main characteristics of an oligopolistic market and the main reasons why firms in such a market are interdependent.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Structural characteristics: a small number of firms supplies the large majority of output, so concentration ratios are high — the practice data show CR4 of 66.30%. Products may be homogeneous, as in petrol or cement, or differentiated, as in groceries or airlines.\n• Barriers to entry: substantial enough to prevent supernormal profit being competed away, whether through economies of scale, sunk advertising, control of distribution or brand loyalty.\n• Imperfect information and uncertainty: each firm must act without knowing how rivals will respond, which is what makes oligopoly analytically distinctive.\n• Why interdependence arises: with few firms, each is large relative to the market, so one firm's price or output decision materially changes the demand facing the others. Cross elasticity of demand between the firms is high, so a price cut takes customers directly from named rivals rather than from the market as a whole.\n• Consequence — strategic behaviour: each firm must anticipate rivals' reactions before acting. This is why game theory rather than a simple MC = MR rule is used, and why the prisoners' dilemma structure recurs.\n• Consequence — price rigidity: the kinked demand curve model explains why prices may remain unchanged even when costs move sharply, since a rise would not be matched and a cut would be. The practice petrol data show prices matched exactly within a day.\n• Consequence — non-price competition: because price cuts are matched and destroy margins for everyone, firms compete on advertising, branding, loyalty schemes, quality, range and service, where a lead is harder to copy immediately.\n• Consequence — collusion: firms recognise that joint profits are higher if they act as a monopolist. Overt collusion through a cartel is illegal in the UK, and the Competition and Markets Authority fined ten construction firms more than £59 million in 2026 for rigging bids on asbestos removal contracts. Tacit collusion, such as price leadership, is harder to prove because parallel pricing is also what independent competition produces.\n• Why collusion is unstable: each member gains by cheating, so cartels require monitoring and punishment; leniency programmes exploit that instability directly.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) structural and behavioural characteristics explained with the source of interdependence analysed and accurate dated examples; L2 (6-10) characteristics listed with some explanation; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main conditions necessary for a firm to practise price discrimination successfully, and the main forms it can take.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Definition: charging different prices to different consumers for the same good where the difference does not reflect a difference in the cost of supply. Charging more for an aisle seat is a price difference reflecting a different product; charging more to a commuter for the identical journey is discrimination.\n• Condition one — price-setting power: the firm must face a downward-sloping demand curve. A price taker cannot discriminate, since any attempt to charge above the market price loses all its customers.\n• Condition two — separable markets with different elasticities: the firm must be able to identify groups whose price elasticity of demand differs, and to tell which group a customer belongs to. Time of travel, age, student or membership status, geography and purchase history are the usual separators.\n• Condition three — no resale: arbitrage must be prevented, or those in the cheap market would buy and resell to those in the dear one, collapsing the price difference. This is why discrimination is most common in services, which are consumed at the point of sale, and why tickets are made non-transferable.\n• Condition four — the cost of separating the markets must be less than the extra revenue gained, otherwise the strategy is not worth pursuing.\n• First-degree discrimination: charging each consumer their maximum willingness to pay, capturing the whole of consumer surplus. Rare historically, but personalised algorithmic pricing using purchase data moves markets closer to it.\n• Second-degree discrimination: charging different prices according to the quantity or version bought — bulk discounts, two-part tariffs, block pricing, and deliberately degraded "basic" versions. The consumer self-selects rather than being identified by the firm.\n• Third-degree discrimination: charging different prices to identifiable groups — peak and off-peak, student and adult, domestic and export. The firm equates MC with MR in each sub-market separately, giving a higher price where demand is less elastic.\n• Effects worth noting: revenue and profit rise, output is often higher than under a single price, and in industries with heavy fixed costs discrimination may be what makes provision viable at all. Consumers in the inelastic segment lose surplus, so the welfare effect is ambiguous rather than simply negative.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) all conditions explained with the reason for each, and the three degrees distinguished with examples; L2 (6-10) conditions listed with some explanation; L1 (1-5) definition with an example.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main ways in which UK competition policy seeks to promote competitive markets.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• The institution: the Competition and Markets Authority, established in April 2014 to replace the Office of Fair Trading and the Competition Commission, is the UK's principal competition and consumer authority, alongside sector regulators such as Ofgem, Ofcom and Ofwat.\n• Prohibiting anti-competitive agreements: cartels — price fixing, market sharing, bid rigging and output restriction — are illegal. Penalties can reach 10% of worldwide turnover, and the CMA fined ten construction firms more than £59 million in 2026 for rigging bids on asbestos removal contracts. Leniency programmes offer immunity to the first firm to confess, deliberately exploiting the incentive to defect that game theory predicts.\n• Controlling abuse of a dominant position: a dominant firm may not use predatory pricing, refuse access to an essential facility, impose exclusive dealing or tie products to exclude rivals. Dominance itself is not unlawful; its abuse is.\n• Merger control: mergers meeting the thresholds are reviewed, and may be blocked or cleared subject to remedies such as divestment of stores or brands, where the CMA finds a substantial lessening of competition.\n• Market investigations: where a market appears to work badly without any single firm breaking the law, the CMA can investigate the market as a whole and impose remedies — mandating price comparison, capping charges, requiring the release of data or ordering structural separation.\n• Digital markets regime: under the Digital Markets, Competition and Consumers Act 2024 the CMA can designate firms as having strategic market status and impose tailored conduct requirements. Google was designated in general search and search advertising in October 2025, and Apple and Google in mobile platforms later that month.\n• Consumer protection: the CMA also enforces consumer law, since informed consumers are part of what makes competition work. It fined the AA £4.2 million in April 2026 over drip pricing in driving lessons and ordered refunds of more than £760 000 to over 80 000 learner drivers.\n• Supporting policies beyond competition law: deregulation and the removal of statutory entry barriers, privatisation with regulated price controls where natural monopoly persists, and trade liberalisation, which exposes domestic firms to foreign competition.\n• The organising judgement: the policies target conduct and structure separately, because size alone is not the problem — the aim is contestable markets rather than a particular number of firms.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several instruments explained with their purpose and accurate dated examples, and the conduct/structure distinction made; L2 (6-10) instruments listed with some explanation; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main characteristics of perfect competition, and why economists continue to use a model whose assumptions no real market satisfies.',
        marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `• Many buyers and sellers, each too small to affect the market price: the firm is a price taker, so its average revenue curve is horizontal and AR = MR = price.\n• A homogeneous product: consumers are indifferent between suppliers, so cross elasticity of demand between firms is infinite and no firm can charge above the market price.\n• Perfect information: all participants know prices, costs and product quality, so no firm can exploit ignorance and no persistent price differences can survive.\n• Freedom of entry and exit with no sunk costs: this is the assumption that drives the long-run result, because it is what competes supernormal profit away.\n• Profit maximisation and no externalities: firms produce where MC = MR, and private costs and benefits coincide with social ones.\n• The long-run result: entry continues while supernormal profit exists, so price falls to the minimum of average cost. The firm earns normal profit, is productively efficient at the minimum of AC, and allocatively efficient with P = MC.\n• Why the model is used despite its unrealism — it is a benchmark: it identifies the conditions under which a market maximises the sum of consumer and producer surplus, so every real market can be assessed by which assumption fails and by how much. Competition policy is organised around exactly these departures.\n• Why it is used — it isolates mechanisms: by assuming away market power, information failure and externalities, it shows what the price mechanism alone achieves, which is the necessary starting point for analysing what happens when each assumption is relaxed.\n• Why it is used — its predictions hold approximately in some markets: agricultural commodities, foreign exchange and some financial markets come close enough that the model predicts price and output responses well.\n• The honest limitation: the model delivers no variety, no advertising, no innovation and no economies of scale, because the product is identical and profit is competed away. It is therefore a standard for static efficiency, not a description of a desirable industry.\nLevels: L3 (11-15) characteristics explained precisely, the long-run efficiency result derived, and the benchmark justification developed; L2 (6-10) characteristics listed with some explanation; L1 (1-5) a list of assumptions.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that consumers always benefit more from perfect competition than from monopoly.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `• The static case for competition: in long-run equilibrium the competitive firm produces at the minimum of average cost and sells at a price equal to marginal cost, so it is both productively and allocatively efficient and consumer surplus is maximised. Show this alongside the monopoly outcome.\n• The static case against monopoly: profit maximisation at MC = MR with MR below AR gives a lower output and a higher price. Consumer surplus is reduced both by the transfer to the monopolist and by the deadweight loss — £680 000 in the practice data — on transactions that no longer occur.\n• Further costs of monopoly: X-inefficiency, because protected profit removes the penalty for waste; and the incentive to spend real resources defending the position through lobbying and litigation rather than on production.\n• Evaluation — economies of scale: the comparison above assumes identical costs. If a single large firm has substantially lower average cost than many small ones, the monopoly price can be below the competitive price even with a mark-up. In a natural monopoly, competition is simply not available at a lower cost.\n• Evaluation — dynamic efficiency: Schumpeter's argument is that supernormal profit funds research and that the prospect of temporary monopoly is what makes innovation worth undertaking. Patent protection embodies this reasoning as deliberate policy. A perfectly competitive firm earns only normal profit and has neither the funds nor the incentive to innovate.\n• Evaluation — the benchmark is unattainable: perfect competition requires a homogeneous product, perfect information and no sunk costs. No real market satisfies these, so the relevant comparison is not competition against monopoly but one imperfect structure against another.\n• Evaluation — what the homogeneity assumption costs consumers: perfect competition offers no variety, no branding and no product development, since the product is identical by assumption. Consumers value choice and quality improvement, which the model cannot deliver.\n• Evaluation — regulation changes the comparison: a regulated monopoly subject to a price control and quality standards may deliver outcomes closer to the competitive benchmark than an unregulated fragmented market with high costs.\n• Evaluation — the honest determinant: whether consumers gain depends on how large the cost advantage of scale is, how contestable the monopoly is, and how much innovation the profit actually buys. Where scale economies are small and the monopoly is entrenched, competition wins decisively; where scale economies are large or innovation is the main source of consumer gain, it need not.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that consumers benefit from competition in most markets and that "always" fails, because economies of scale, natural monopoly, dynamic efficiency and product variety each identify circumstances in which a monopolistic structure serves consumers better. The right policy conclusion is to attack the abuse of market power and to keep markets contestable, not to pursue a fragmented structure for its own sake.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained evaluation with diagrams, the static/dynamic trade-off developed and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) both structures described with limited evaluation; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the effectiveness of the Competition and Markets Authority in promoting competitive markets in the UK.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `• The remit: the CMA, established in April 2014, enforces the prohibitions on anti-competitive agreements and on abuse of dominance, reviews mergers, conducts market investigations and enforces consumer law, with penalties of up to 10% of worldwide turnover.\n• Evidence of effectiveness — cartels: the £59 million penalties imposed on ten construction firms in 2026 for rigging asbestos removal bids, and penalties of over £4 million on sports broadcasters for wage-fixing and no-poach agreements, show enforcement reaching beyond classic price fixing into labour markets.\n• Evidence of effectiveness — deterrence by design: the leniency programme exploits the prisoners' dilemma directly, offering immunity to the first cartel member to confess. Its value lies in the cartels never formed as well as the ones detected, which is real but unmeasurable.\n• Evidence of effectiveness — new powers used quickly: under the Digital Markets, Competition and Consumers Act 2024 the CMA designated Google as having strategic market status in general search and search advertising in October 2025, and Apple and Google in mobile platforms later that month, addressing a duopoly covering around 90 to 100% of UK mobile devices that traditional case-by-case enforcement had failed to touch.\n• Evidence of effectiveness — consumer protection: the £4.2 million fine on the AA in April 2026 over drip pricing, with over £760 000 refunded to more than 80 000 learner drivers, shows the authority acting on the exploitation of behavioural biases rather than only on classic competition harms.\n• Assessment — detection is the binding constraint: cartels are secret by design, and the ones uncovered are those that failed to conceal themselves. Fines below the statutory maximum may be absorbed as a cost of doing business if the probability of detection is low.\n• Assessment — speed and resources: investigations take years, during which the market harm continues. In fast-moving digital markets a remedy imposed three years after the conduct may be irrelevant, and the authority is heavily outresourced by the firms it investigates.\n• Assessment — merger control and remedies: predicting the competitive effect of a merger requires forecasting a counterfactual, and behavioural remedies in particular are hard to monitor. Decisions are open to appeal, which further delays outcomes.\n• Assessment — regulatory capture and information asymmetry: the authority depends on firms for the information it needs, and the firms know their own costs and strategies far better, which limits what any regulator can achieve.\n• Assessment — global firms and national jurisdiction: the largest market-power problems are transnational, and a UK authority acting alone has limited leverage, though the SMS regime represents a deliberate attempt to acquire it.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that the CMA is effective at sanctioning detected conduct and has moved decisively on digital market power since 2024, but that its effectiveness is bounded by detection rates, speed and information asymmetry rather than by its formal powers. Judged against the realistic alternative of no authority, it is clearly worthwhile; judged against the scale of market power in digital and infrastructure markets, it remains a partial remedy.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained assessment with accurate dated evidence on both sides and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) the CMA's role described with limited assessment; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Discuss the view that oligopoly always produces worse outcomes for consumers than monopolistic competition.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `• The structures: monopolistic competition has many firms, differentiated products and low barriers to entry, with long-run normal profit at tangency of AR to AC. Oligopoly has few firms, high barriers and interdependence, with supernormal profit sustainable in the long run.\n• The case against oligopoly: high concentration — CR4 of 66.30% in the practice data — allows tacit or overt collusion. A cartel behaves as a monopolist, restricting output and raising price, and the £59 million of penalties imposed on ten construction firms in 2026 for bid rigging shows the harm is not hypothetical.\n• Further points against oligopoly: the kinked demand curve model predicts prices that are rigid downwards; barriers to entry mean supernormal profit persists rather than being competed away; and limit and predatory pricing can exclude entrants who would have benefited consumers.\n• Discussion — the case for oligopoly: economies of scale are available at oligopolistic scale and not in monopolistic competition, so unit costs and therefore prices can be lower. Supernormal profit also funds research and development, giving dynamic efficiency — which is why pharmaceuticals, aerospace and semiconductors are oligopolies and why their products improve.\n• Discussion — non-price competition benefits consumers: because price cuts are matched, oligopolists compete on quality, range, service, innovation and loyalty schemes. That competition is real and delivers consumer benefit that a price comparison misses.\n• Discussion — oligopoly outcomes are indeterminate: game theory shows that the same structure can produce a collusive outcome or a competitive one depending on the number of firms, the transparency of prices, the frequency of interaction and the enforcement regime. A price war in an oligopoly produces lower prices than monopolistic competition ever does.\n• Discussion — the weaknesses of monopolistic competition: firms are productively inefficient because they operate below minimum average cost — the excess capacity theorem — and allocatively inefficient because P exceeds MC. Small scale forgoes economies of scale, and normal profit leaves nothing for research, so dynamic efficiency is weak.\n• Discussion — what monopolistic competition offers instead: variety and responsiveness, low barriers so that persistent poor service attracts entry, and no scope for sustained collusion given the number of firms.\n• Discussion — the decisive variable is contestability, not structure: the practice bus data show a three-firm market with lower fares than a six-firm market, because sunk costs were 91% recoverable in the first and 41% in the second. Structure alone predicts outcomes poorly.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that "always" is indefensible. Oligopoly is worse where collusion is sustained and barriers are high, and better where firms compete and scale economies or innovation are significant; monopolistic competition guarantees neither low prices nor innovation. The outcome turns on conduct and contestability, which is why competition policy targets behaviour rather than counting firms.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained discussion with diagrams, both structures evaluated on several efficiency criteria and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) both described with limited discussion; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that price discrimination by firms with market power should be prohibited.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `• The case for prohibition — surplus extraction: discrimination converts consumer surplus into producer surplus. In the practice data, peak passengers pay £62.00 rather than £48.00, a direct loss to consumers who have the least ability to avoid it.\n• The case for prohibition — it exploits inelasticity, which often means need: the consumers charged most are those with the fewest alternatives — commuters who must travel, patients who must buy a drug, travellers who must book late. Charging most to those with least choice is hard to defend on equity grounds.\n• The case for prohibition — it requires and reinforces market power: the ability to discriminate is itself evidence of a downward-sloping demand curve, and first-degree discrimination using personal data raises privacy and fairness concerns that the standard welfare analysis omits.\n• Assessment — output usually rises: the low price brings in consumers who would not buy at the single price. The practice data show passengers rising from 200 000 to 220 000, a 10.00% increase, so output moves towards the allocatively efficient level and some consumers gain who would otherwise be excluded.\n• Assessment — it can make provision viable: where fixed costs are heavy and marginal cost low — rail, airlines, software, pharmaceuticals — a single price covering average cost may exclude large numbers of consumers. Discrimination allows the fixed cost to be recovered from those who value the service most, and some routes or products would not exist at all without it.\n• Assessment — the distributional effect is not reliably regressive: student, senior and off-peak discounts transfer surplus towards groups with lower incomes and more flexible demand. Prohibiting discrimination would remove those discounts as well as the premiums.\n• Assessment — what prohibition would actually mean: a single price lying between the two. Peak consumers gain, off-peak consumers lose and some are priced out, and total output falls. Prohibition is therefore a redistribution between consumer groups, not a straightforward consumer gain.\n• Assessment — targeted rules work better than a ban: competition law already prohibits discrimination used to exclude rivals, and consumer law addresses practices that exploit bias — the CMA fined the AA £4.2 million in April 2026 over drip pricing. Regulating the abusive uses is more proportionate than banning the practice.\n• Assessment — the difficult case: algorithmic personalised pricing approaches first-degree discrimination and removes consumer surplus almost entirely, without the offsetting output gain being obvious. This is where a prohibition argument is strongest, and it is a different case from peak pricing.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that a general prohibition is not justified, because discrimination typically raises output, can make provision viable and is not reliably regressive; the defensible policy targets the specific uses that exclude competitors or exploit consumer biases, with personalised algorithmic pricing the case that most warrants fresh scrutiny.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained assessment distinguishing the welfare from the distributional effect, with a supported and appropriately qualified judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) effects described with limited assessment; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that the contestability of a market matters more than the number of firms operating in it.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `• The proposition: contestability theory holds that it is the threat of entry, particularly the height of sunk costs, that disciplines incumbents. In a perfectly contestable market even a single firm must price close to average cost, because any supernormal profit invites hit-and-run entry.\n• Evidence from the practice data: a bus market with three operators had a fare of £2.10 with 91% of capital recoverable, while a market with six operators charged £2.95 with only 41% recoverable. The market with twice as many firms charged 40.48% more. Firm numbers predicted the outcome badly; sunk costs predicted it well.\n• Supporting reasoning: concentration ratios measure structure at a point in time and say nothing about conduct. Two firms in a contestable market may behave more competitively than six in a protected one, which is why competition authorities examine barriers and conduct rather than counting firms.\n• Policy implication in favour: if contestability is what matters, the right policy is to remove entry barriers — deregulate, mandate access to essential facilities, require interoperability — rather than to break firms up.\n• Evaluation — perfect contestability rests on strong assumptions: entrants must have costs identical to incumbents, consumers must switch instantly, and the incumbent must be unable to cut price before the entrant exits. In reality brand loyalty, reputation and switching costs all blunt hit-and-run entry, and incumbents can respond within days.\n• Evaluation — incumbents can manufacture sunk costs: a firm facing a contestable market invests in brand advertising, exclusive contracts, loyalty schemes and excess capacity precisely to raise the sunk cost of entry. Contestability is therefore endogenous and can be destroyed by the incumbent's own strategy.\n• Evaluation — numbers still matter for collusion: the sustainability of tacit collusion depends directly on how many firms must coordinate and monitor one another. Two firms can reach a stable understanding without any communication; twenty cannot. The £59 million penalties on ten construction firms in 2026 show that coordination remains feasible at moderate numbers but it becomes harder as numbers rise.\n• Evaluation — where neither explains enough: in digital markets the decisive factors are network effects and data advantages, which are neither firm numbers nor conventional sunk costs. The CMA's strategic market status designations of Google in search and of Apple and Google in mobile platforms in October 2025 rested on ecosystem control that no entrant could replicate at any cost.\n• Evaluation — the two are complements: the best predictor of competitive conduct uses both, together with evidence on margins, prices and switching. A single-variable theory of competition is not available.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that contestability is the better single predictor of conduct and has rightly displaced pure structural analysis in competition policy, but that "matters more" overstates it — firm numbers determine whether collusion is sustainable, incumbents can make markets uncontestable at will, and in network industries neither variable is decisive.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained evaluation using the data, with the endogeneity of contestability and the collusion point developed, and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) theory explained with limited evaluation; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Discuss the view that cartels are inherently unstable and will always break down without government intervention.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `• The theoretical case for instability: a cartel puts its members at the jointly profitable outcome, but each member does better by cheating. Using a payoff matrix with both high (8, 8), unilateral defection (11, 2) and both low (4, 4), the low price is a dominant strategy and the Nash equilibrium is mutual defection.\n• Reinforcing pressures: cheating is profitable and hard to detect where prices are negotiated privately; members disagree about the allocation of quotas; a member with lower costs wants a larger share; and high cartel prices attract entry by outsiders who are bound by no agreement.\n• Empirical support: most known cartels have finite lives, and the discovery of long-running conspiracies is usually the result of a member defecting to the authorities rather than of independent detection.\n• Discussion — repeated interaction sustains cooperation: the one-shot dilemma is the wrong model for an ongoing market. If defection triggers a price war in later periods, the discounted future loss can exceed the one-off gain, making cooperation self-enforcing. Cartels lasting decades are documented, so "always" is empirically wrong.\n• Discussion — the conditions that make cartels stable: few members, so monitoring is feasible; a homogeneous product, so price comparison is easy; transparent prices, so cheating is visible; similar costs, so the agreed price suits everyone; high and durable entry barriers; and stable demand, since a downturn sharpens the incentive to cut price for volume.\n• Discussion — tacit collusion needs no agreement to break: price leadership and parallel pricing achieve much of the cartel outcome without any communication, so there is nothing to collapse. The practice petrol data show prices matched within a day, which competition and tacit collusion both predict.\n• Discussion — the role of intervention is precisely to exploit the instability: leniency programmes offer immunity to the first member to confess, converting a repeated game with self-enforcing cooperation back into a one-shot dilemma. That is why the policy works, and it shows the instability is real but insufficient on its own.\n• Discussion — evidence that intervention is needed: the CMA fined ten construction firms more than £59 million in 2026 for rigging bids on asbestos removal contracts worth over £150 million, and imposed over £4 million of penalties on sports broadcasters for wage-fixing and no-poach agreements. These arrangements did not collapse of their own accord; they were stopped.\n• Discussion — the cost of waiting: even a cartel that eventually collapses imposes higher prices and lower output while it lasts, and public procurement cartels transfer money directly from taxpayers. The relevant question is not whether it ends but what it costs before it does.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that the incentive to defect is real and makes cartels fragile, but "always break down" is wrong both theoretically, because repeated interaction supports cooperation, and empirically, because durable cartels exist. Government intervention is justified not because cartels never end but because they end too slowly and too expensively, and leniency policy works by deliberately amplifying the instability the theory identifies.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained discussion with correct game-theoretic reasoning, the repeated-game point developed and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) the dilemma explained with limited discussion; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that a natural monopoly should be publicly owned rather than privately owned and regulated.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `• The problem: where minimum efficient scale exceeds total market demand, long-run average cost falls across the whole range and one firm supplies most cheaply. Competition is not available at lower cost, so the question is how the single supplier should be owned and controlled. Use a natural monopoly diagram with falling LRAC and MC below AC.\n• The case for public ownership: the state can price at marginal cost, which is allocatively efficient, and fund the resulting loss from taxation — something a private firm cannot do. It can also pursue objectives the market ignores, such as universal service, network resilience and long-term investment beyond a commercial payback period.\n• Further points for public ownership: profits are retained rather than distributed to shareholders, and borrowing is cheaper at government rates, so the cost of the large capital base is lower. Regulation is also costly, adversarial and information-constrained, and public ownership removes that layer.\n• Assessment — the case for regulated private ownership: private firms face a profit incentive to control costs, whereas public ownership removes that discipline and invites X-inefficiency, overmanning and political interference in pricing and investment. Incentive regulation of the RPI-X or CPI-X kind allows the firm to keep savings beyond a target, deliberately reproducing that discipline.\n• Assessment — the information problem is shared: a regulator does not know the firm's true costs, so the allowed return may be too generous. But a government department running the industry has the same problem internally, and no external benchmark against which to test it.\n• Assessment — investment and political incentives: public ownership exposes capital spending to fiscal pressure, since network investment competes with health and education in the same budget and delivers benefits beyond an electoral cycle. Private ownership with a regulated return insulates investment, provided the regulatory settlement is credible.\n• Assessment — regulatory capture and the record: regulators depend on the firm for information and expertise and can come to adopt its perspective. The record of UK privatised utilities is mixed — substantial cost reductions and investment in some sectors, weak performance on environmental and service outcomes in others — so the evidence does not settle the question either way.\n• Assessment — the distinction that clarifies it: the natural monopoly is usually the network, not the whole industry. Separating the network from competitive activities that use it — generation from transmission, train operation from track — allows the monopoly element to be regulated or publicly owned while competition operates around it.\n• Assessment — what actually determines performance: the quality of the governance regime, the clarity of objectives, the strength of the incentives and the independence of the regulator matter more than the ownership label. Badly governed public and private monopolies both perform badly.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that neither model dominates. Public ownership is better where marginal cost pricing and universal service matter most and where private investment incentives are weak; regulated private ownership is better where cost control is the main problem and the regulatory framework is credible. The more productive reform is usually structural — separating the natural monopoly network from the contestable activities — rather than a change of owner.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained assessment with the diagram integrated, both models evaluated on efficiency and investment, and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) both described with limited assessment; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 22-23) states that "the two firms argue that the merger will cut their combined costs by 9% and that the savings will be passed on to shoppers".\n\nUsing the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that the competition authority should allow the merger of Firm 2 and Firm 4 in this grocery market.`,
        marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `• Establish the structural effect: Firm 2 holds 15.1% and Firm 4 holds 10.2%, so the merged firm would hold 25.3%, just below Firm 1's 27.3%. The four-firm concentration ratio would rise from 66.30% to include Firm 5's 9.2% in place of Firm 4, giving 27.3 + 25.3 + 13.7 + 9.2 = 75.50% — a rise of 9.20 percentage points.\n• The competition concern: a higher concentration ratio makes tacit collusion easier, because fewer firms must coordinate and monitor one another. Removing an independent competitor also reduces the number of alternatives available to consumers and to suppliers.\n• Local markets matter more than national shares: a national share of 25.3% can conceal local monopolies where the two firms' stores overlap. Competition authorities assess merger effects store by store, and divestment of overlapping stores is the standard remedy.\n• The efficiency defence: a 9% fall in combined costs is a real gain if it is achieved. Economies of scale in purchasing, distribution and overheads are genuine at grocery scale, and lower costs shift the supply curve right.\n• Evaluation — pass-through is the decisive question: cost savings only benefit consumers if competition forces them to be passed on. The merger itself reduces that competitive pressure, so the merger is simultaneously the source of the saving and the reason to doubt it will reach shoppers. A firm with more market power prices where MC = MR, capturing more of the saving as margin.\n• Evaluation — the evidence in the extracts cuts both ways: real prices fell 3.6% and top-three margins fell from 3.9% to 2.8% since 2017, which suggests competition is currently working and that pass-through has been occurring. But Extract B records no new national entrant since the 1990s, so the discipline comes from existing rivals — and the merger removes one of them.\n• Evaluation — countervailing power and the discounters: the discounters' combined share rose to 18.3%, and their continued expansion is a competitive constraint the merged firm would still face. Whether that is sufficient depends on whether they compete across the full range or only on price for a limited assortment.\n• Evaluation — remedies short of prohibition: the authority need not choose between blocking and clearing. It can clear subject to divestment of stores in overlapping local areas, which preserves most of the scale economies while removing the specific local harms.\n• Evaluation — the burden of proof: efficiency claims made by merging parties are self-interested, unverifiable in advance and frequently not realised. A merger is also very hard to undo, whereas a blocked merger can be revisited, which argues for caution where the evidence is finely balanced.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that the merger should not be cleared unconditionally. The 9.20 percentage point rise in concentration in a market with no new national entry for thirty years is a real risk to the competition that has delivered falling prices and margins, and the efficiency claim is unverified and least likely to be passed on precisely because the merger reduces the pressure to pass it on. Conditional clearance with local divestments is the proportionate response.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) extracts used throughout with calculation, the pass-through argument developed and a supported judgement that considers remedies; L4 (16-20) good use of extracts with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) some extract use, evaluation asserted; L2 (6-10) largely descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that the supernormal profit earned by firms with market power is justified by the innovation it funds.',
        marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `• The Schumpeterian argument: research and development is a large fixed cost incurred before any revenue, with an uncertain payoff. Only firms earning more than normal profit have the retained earnings to fund it and the prospect of a return large enough to justify the risk.\n• Supporting mechanism — appropriability: an innovator whose idea is immediately imitated cannot recover the development cost, so some protection from competition is a precondition for innovation. Patent law embodies this reasoning as deliberate policy, granting a temporary monopoly in exchange for disclosure.\n• Supporting evidence: the most research-intensive industries — pharmaceuticals, semiconductors, aerospace — are concentrated oligopolies with high minimum efficient scale, and their products improve continuously. A perfectly competitive firm earning only normal profit has neither the funds nor the incentive to innovate.\n• The dynamic gain can dwarf the static loss: the deadweight loss from monopoly pricing is a triangle on a diagram — £680 000 in the practice data — whereas a genuinely new product or a permanent fall in the cost curve delivers a recurring gain. If supernormal profit buys innovation, the trade is worth making.\n• Assessment — profit is necessary but not sufficient: the incentive to innovate comes from competitive pressure as much as from retained profit. A firm insulated from entry has a weaker reason to innovate, because it can earn its profit without doing so. X-inefficiency is the standard symptom, and cost reductions following deregulation are evidence of it.\n• Assessment — the profit may not be spent on research: supernormal profit can be distributed to shareholders, used for acquisitions, or spent on defending the market position through lobbying, litigation and brand advertising. Evidence that a firm earns high profit is not evidence that it innovates.\n• Assessment — innovation can be defensive: incumbents acquire potential rivals or patent around a technology to block competitors rather than to develop products. The CMA's designation of Google in search and of Apple and Google in mobile platforms as having strategic market status in October 2025 reflected concern that ecosystem control was being used to entrench position rather than to improve products.\n• Assessment — the profit and the innovation may have a common cause: a firm that innovates successfully earns supernormal profit as a result. Observing both together does not establish that the profit caused the innovation; the causation may run the other way.\n• Assessment — targeted alternatives exist: patents, research tax credits, public funding of basic science and prize mechanisms all reward innovation without requiring persistent market power, and they can be time-limited and conditional in a way that market power is not.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that the argument identifies something real — innovation requires appropriable returns, so some protection from immediate imitation is necessary — but that it does not justify supernormal profit in general. The defensible policy is to protect the returns to innovation directly and for a limited period, through patents and public funding, while attacking the market power that persists after the innovation has been made, which is precisely what competition policy attempts.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained assessment with the static/dynamic trade-off developed, the causation problem identified and a supported judgement; L4 (16-20) good analysis with developed evaluation; L3 (11-15) the argument described with limited assessment; L2 (6-10) descriptive; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: `After considering Extract D, and the original evidence in Extracts A, B and C, would you recommend that the competition authority open a full market investigation into this grocery market? Justify your recommendation.\n\nExtract D — written for this practice question\n"A preliminary review found that in 41% of postcode districts a single chain operates the only large store within a 20-minute drive, that prices in those districts average 4.8% above the national average, that the top three chains hold land banks of 380 undeveloped sites with restrictive covenants preventing their sale to rival grocers, and that the authority's last full market investigation took 27 months and cost £14m."`,
        marks: 25, command: 'Justify',
        markScheme: `• Restate the national picture from the earlier evidence: CR3 fell from 59.8% to 56.1%, the discounters' share rose to 18.3%, top-three margins fell from 3.9% to 2.8% and real prices fell 3.6%. On national indicators the market appears to be working better than it was, which is an argument against intervening.\n• What Extract D changes — the unit of analysis: 41% of postcode districts have only one large store within a 20-minute drive. Grocery competition is local, so a national concentration ratio is the wrong measure. Local monopoly can coexist with a moderate national share, and the data show it does.\n• What Extract D changes — evidence of harm: prices in those districts are 4.8% above the national average. This is an outcome measure, not a structural inference, and it is precisely the kind of evidence that distinguishes a theoretical concern from a demonstrated one.\n• What Extract D changes — an identified mechanism: 380 undeveloped sites held with covenants preventing sale to rival grocers is a deliberate strategic barrier to entry. It explains why Extract B records no new national entrant since the 1990s, and it is a practice a market investigation can remedy directly by ordering the covenants released.\n• Why a market investigation is the right instrument: no single firm need have broken the law. The harm arises from the structure of local markets and from a practice engaged in by several firms, which is exactly the situation the market investigation power exists to address, and its remedies — releasing covenants, ordering divestment, mandating access — are structural.\n• Weighing the cost: 27 months and £14m is a serious commitment, and national indicators are improving. Against that, a 4.8% price premium across 41% of districts is a large recurring consumer detriment, and the land banks mean the problem will not correct itself.\n• The recommendation and its conditions: a defensible recommendation is yes, open the investigation, but scoped narrowly — to local market structure and to the land bank covenants specifically, rather than a general review of the national market, so that the cost and duration are proportionate to the identified harm.\n• Counter-argument to address: the discounters' share is still rising and may erode local monopolies without intervention, and a 27-month investigation could be overtaken by events. A targeted enforcement action on the covenants alone, or a referral to planning policy, might achieve most of the benefit faster. A candidate who addresses this and still reaches a reasoned recommendation should be credited at the top.\n• Uncertainties in the evidence: the 4.8% premium is not adjusted for store size, format, rural cost differences or product mix; "20-minute drive" is one threshold among several and excludes online delivery; and the review is preliminary, with no confidence intervals given.\nLevels: L5 (21-25) all four extracts used, national and local evidence weighed against each other, a clear recommendation justified with scope conditions and the main counter-argument addressed; L4 (16-20) extracts used well with a justified recommendation; L3 (11-15) some use of extracts, recommendation asserted; L2 (6-10) descriptive use of extracts; L1 (1-5) fragmentary.` }
    ]
  },

'3.1.6': {
    name: 'The Labour Market',
    questions: [

      /* ─────────── Section A part (a): 2-mark calculations ─────────── */

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nOutput and price at Northvale Components Ltd\n  Price of output: £18 per unit (constant at all output levels)\n  4 workers employed: total weekly output = 64 units\n  5 workers employed: total weekly output = 79 units\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate the marginal revenue product of the fifth worker. Give your answer in pounds.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: MRP = marginal physical product x price of output (the firm is a price taker in its product market, so MR = price = £18).\nMPP of the fifth worker = 79 – 64 = 15 units.\nMRP = 15 x £18 = £270 per week.\n\n2 marks for £270. 1 mark for a correct method (MPP = 15, or multiplying a physical product figure by £18) spoiled by an arithmetic slip, or for an answer given without units. 0 marks for 79 x £18 = £1,422 (total revenue product, not marginal) unless the correct method is shown alongside.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nMedian gross hourly pay, full-time employees, Brackenshire\n  2025: £18.50\n  2026: £19.24\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate to two decimal places the percentage increase in median gross hourly pay between 2025 and 2026.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: percentage change = (change / original) x 100.\nChange = £19.24 – £18.50 = £0.74.\n0.74 / 18.50 x 100 = 4.00%.\n\n2 marks for 4.00% (the answer must be expressed to two decimal places as instructed). 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, for 0.04 without conversion to a percentage, or for 4% without the stated decimal places. Note for students: this is a NOMINAL increase — with CPI inflation of 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026, the real increase would be roughly 0.9%.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nSupply of labour to the Eastport warehousing sector\n  Hourly wage £12.00: 4 000 hours of labour supplied per week\n  Hourly wage £13.20: 4 300 hours of labour supplied per week\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate to two decimal places the elasticity of supply of labour to warehousing over this range.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: elasticity of labour supply = % change in quantity of labour supplied / % change in the wage rate.\n% change in quantity = 300 / 4 000 x 100 = 7.50%.\n% change in wage = 1.20 / 12.00 x 100 = 10.00%.\nElasticity = 7.50 / 10.00 = 0.75.\n\n2 marks for 0.75 (positive; supply of labour is upward sloping so a positive sign is expected). 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for inverting the fraction to give 1.33. Credit a comment that a value below 1 means supply is wage inelastic over this range, but do not require it.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — Annual Survey of Hours and Earnings, April 2025\nMedian gross hourly earnings excluding overtime, UK full-time employees\n  Men:   £20.27\n  Women: £18.87\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate to two decimal places the gender pay gap for full-time employees in April 2025, expressed as a percentage of men's median hourly pay.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: gender pay gap = (men's median – women's median) / men's median x 100.\nDifference = £20.27 – £18.87 = £1.40.\n1.40 / 20.27 x 100 = 6.9068… = 6.91%.\n\n2 marks for 6.91%. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for dividing by women's pay (7.42%) with method shown. The ONS published figure for April 2025 is 6.9%; the all-employee gap was larger, at 12.8%, because more women work part time and part-time hourly pay is lower.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question, using official 2025 proportions\nTrade union membership, UK employees\n  Employees in employment: 29.50 million\n  Employees who are trade union members: 6.60 million\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate to two decimal places trade union density among UK employees.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: union density = union members / employees in employment x 100.\n6.60 / 29.50 x 100 = 22.3729… = 22.37%.\n\n2 marks for 22.37%. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for an unconverted decimal (0.2237). The official figure for 2025 was 22.4% of employees, up from 22.0% in 2024 — density remains far higher in the public sector (48.5% in 2025) than in the private sector (12.1%).` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nLabour costs at Kestrel Foods, the only large employer in an isolated town\n  10 workers employed: weekly wage £400 each\n  11 workers employed: weekly wage £420 each (the firm must raise the wage for every worker)\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate the marginal cost of employing the eleventh worker. Give your answer in pounds.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: marginal cost of labour = change in the total wage bill.\nTotal wage bill with 10 workers = 10 x £400 = £4 000.\nTotal wage bill with 11 workers = 11 x £420 = £4 620.\nMCL of the eleventh worker = £4 620 – £4 000 = £620.\n\n2 marks for £620. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for an answer of £420 (the wage rather than the marginal cost) where the working shows the total-wage-bill method. The key economics: because a monopsonist faces the upward-sloping market supply curve of labour and cannot wage-discriminate, MCL (£620) exceeds the wage (£420).` },

      /* ─────────── Section A part (b): 4-mark data questions ─────────── */

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nEastport regional labour market\n  Unfilled vacancies (000s):        2024 = 58 ; 2026 = 91\n  Unemployed claimants (000s):      2024 = 74 ; 2026 = 49\n  Median hourly pay:                2024 = £13.60 ; 2026 = £15.28\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that the Eastport labour market has become tighter.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points — the answer must USE the figures, not merely describe them:\n• Vacancies rose from 58 000 to 91 000, an increase of 56.9%, while unemployed claimants fell from 74 000 to 49 000, a fall of 33.8% (1 mark for manipulating the data).\n• The vacancies-to-unemployed ratio moved from 0.78 in 2024 to 1.86 in 2026 — there are now almost two unfilled jobs per claimant, so employers are competing for a shrinking pool of workers (1 mark).\n• Excess demand for labour at the prevailing wage bids the wage up: median hourly pay rose from £13.60 to £15.28, a 12.35% increase (1 mark).\n• Explanation of the mechanism: a rightward shift of the demand for labour along an upward-sloping supply curve raises both the equilibrium wage and employment; the size of the wage rise relative to the quantity response suggests labour supply is relatively wage inelastic in Eastport (1 mark).\n\n4 marks: two figures manipulated AND the tightening mechanism explained. 3 marks: data used accurately with partial explanation. 2 marks: data quoted and a valid link asserted. 1 mark: figures simply lifted from the table with no explanation.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nMedian gross annual pay, selected occupations, 2026\n  Airline pilots:              £92 400\n  Secondary school teachers:   £45 900\n  Care workers:                £25 300\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that differences in the supply of labour help to explain wage differentials.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Quantify the differential: pilots earn 3.65 times care workers and 2.01 times teachers (1 mark for manipulation).\n• Supply of labour to piloting is highly restricted — long, expensive training, licensing, medical requirements — so supply is small and wage inelastic; a given demand meets that supply at a high equilibrium wage (1 mark).\n• Care work requires few formal qualifications, so the pool of potential entrants is very large and supply is wage elastic; the supply curve intersects demand at a low wage even though the work is socially valuable (1 mark).\n• Teaching sits between the two — a degree plus qualified teacher status restricts supply relative to care work but far less than piloting (1 mark).\n• Top-band answers note that the data alone cannot separate supply from demand: pilots also have a high MRP because the capital they operate is extremely valuable.\n\n4 marks: differential quantified AND supply conditions explained for at least two occupations. 3 marks: data used with partial explanation. 2 marks: valid link asserted with limited data use. 1 mark: figures repeated.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nMedian hourly pay by highest qualification, Brackenshire, 2026\n  Degree or above:        £21.40\n  A-levels, no degree:    £15.30\n  No qualifications:      £12.90\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that investment in human capital raises earnings.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Quantify the graduate premium: £21.40 against £15.30 is a premium of 39.87% over A-level holders, and £21.40 against £12.90 is 65.89% over those with no qualifications (1 mark).\n• Human capital theory: education and training raise the marginal physical product of a worker, so at any given output price the MRP curve — which is the firm\'s demand curve for that labour — lies further right, and firms will pay more (1 mark).\n• Qualifications also restrict supply to graduate occupations, so the higher demand meets a smaller and less wage-elastic supply, magnifying the differential (1 mark).\n• Evaluation of the data: the premium may partly reflect signalling rather than genuine productivity, and the table does not control for occupation, region, hours or ability — so it overstates the causal return to education (1 mark).\n\n4 marks: premium quantified AND the MRP/supply mechanism explained. Award the fourth mark generously for a valid caveat about causation.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — the National Living Wage figures are official; the median pay figures are written for this practice question\nThe "bite" of the National Living Wage, employees aged 21 and over\n  2021: NLW = £8.91  ; median hourly pay = £14.10\n  2026: NLW = £12.71 ; median hourly pay = £17.85\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that the National Living Wage now covers a larger part of the pay distribution.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Calculate the bite (NLW as a percentage of median pay): 8.91 / 14.10 = 63.19% in 2021; 12.71 / 17.85 = 71.20% in 2026 — a rise of 8.01 percentage points (2 marks for both calculations, 1 for one).\n• The NLW rose 42.65% over the period while median pay rose 26.60%, so the floor has risen faster than the middle of the distribution (1 mark).\n• Explanation: a higher bite means the statutory minimum binds for a larger share of employees, compressing the bottom half of the pay distribution and reducing measured earnings inequality — but it also raises the number of workers whose wage is set by law rather than by the market, so any employment effect is spread more widely (1 mark).\n\n4 marks: the bite calculated for both years AND its significance explained. 3 marks: both figures calculated, explanation thin. 2 marks: one figure calculated with a valid comment. 1 mark: data described only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nTwo UK regions, 2026\n  Region A: unemployment rate 7.8% ; vacancies per unemployed person 0.4 ; median house price £148 000\n  Region B: unemployment rate 3.1% ; vacancies per unemployed person 2.1 ; median house price £486 000\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that geographical immobility of labour is causing labour market failure.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Region A\'s unemployment rate is 4.7 percentage points higher than Region B\'s, and B has 5.25 times as many vacancies per unemployed person (1 mark for manipulation).\n• If labour were perfectly mobile, workers would move from A to B until the rates converged; the persistence of the gap is evidence that they cannot or will not move (1 mark).\n• The house price data give the mechanism: moving from A to B requires finding £338 000 more in housing costs, a 228% increase, which the wage gain is unlikely to cover — housing costs are the single largest barrier to internal migration in the UK (1 mark).\n• Consequence and why it is a market failure: unemployed resources in A coexist with unfilled vacancies in B, so the economy produces inside its production possibility boundary and the misallocation is not self-correcting (1 mark).\n\n4 marks: data manipulated AND immobility explained as a cause of allocative failure. Do not credit a general essay on unemployment that ignores the figures.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — union density figures are official 2025 data; pay figures are written for this practice question\n  Public sector:  union density 48.5% ; median hourly pay £19.10\n  Private sector: union density 12.1% ; median hourly pay £16.35\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) could be used to argue that trade union membership raises pay, and explain one reason why the data may not prove this.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Manipulate the data: public sector density is 4.01 times private sector density, and public sector median pay is 16.82% higher (1 mark).\n• The union mark-up argument: collective bargaining lets workers act as a monopoly seller of labour, raising the wage above the competitive level; where the employer is a monopsonist, a union can raise the wage towards the competitive outcome without reducing employment (1 mark).\n• Counter-argument on causation: the two sectors differ in occupational mix — the public sector employs a far higher share of graduates and professionals (teachers, doctors, nurses), whose pay would be higher with or without a union, so the raw gap confounds composition with union effects (1 mark).\n• Further caveat: causation may run the other way — unions form more easily in large, stable, high-paying workplaces; the data are also a snapshot and cannot show what public sector pay would be in the absence of unions (1 mark).\n\n4 marks: data used AND both the argument and a genuine limitation explained. Maximum 2 marks if the limitation is missing.` },

      /* ─────────── Section A part (c): 9-mark diagram/analysis questions ─────────── */

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 8–9) states: "The care home group is by far the largest employer of care workers in the district, and pay there has barely moved in five years." With the help of a labour market diagram, explain how a monopsony employer determines the wage it pays and the number of workers it employs.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: wage on the vertical axis, quantity of labour on the horizontal. Draw the upward-sloping market supply of labour curve (which is the monopsonist\'s average cost of labour, ACL) and the MCL curve above it and steeper, because hiring one more worker requires raising the wage for all existing workers. Add the downward-sloping MRP curve as the demand for labour.\n• Profit-maximising employment is where MCL = MRP, at Lm. The wage is then read DOWN to the supply curve at Wm, not off the MRP curve — the monopsonist pays the lowest wage at which Lm workers will supply their labour.\n• Compare with the competitive outcome where supply = MRP, giving Lc and Wc. Show clearly that Lm < Lc and Wm < Wc: the monopsonist restricts employment and pays below the competitive wage.\n• Welfare analysis: the area between MRP and supply between Lm and Lc is the deadweight welfare loss — mutually beneficial employment that does not take place. The wedge between MRP and Wm is monopsony exploitation.\n• Application: the care sector figure in the extract fits the model — a single large buyer of a geographically and occupationally immobile labour supply, with pay stagnating despite unfilled vacancies.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) accurate, fully labelled diagram with MCL correctly placed above supply, the MCL = MRP rule applied AND the wage read off the supply curve, with explicit application to the extract. L2 (4–6) diagram broadly correct but incompletely used, or the wage read off the MRP curve, or analysis not applied to the context. L1 (1–3) descriptive, diagram missing or seriously inaccurate.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 14–15) states: "From April 2026 the National Living Wage rose to £12.71 an hour, a 4.1% increase." With the help of a diagram, analyse the likely effect of this increase on employment in a competitive labour market.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: competitive labour market with downward-sloping demand (MRP) and upward-sloping supply, equilibrium at We, Le. Draw the NLW as a horizontal line above We. At the minimum wage, quantity supplied Qs exceeds quantity demanded Qd: employment falls from Le to Qd and excess supply (Qs – Qd) is the resulting unemployment.\n• Chain of reasoning: the wage is now above the MRP of the least productive workers, so profit-maximising firms hire until MRP = £12.71 and shed workers below that threshold; those affected are concentrated in low-paid, labour-intensive sectors such as hospitality, retail and social care.\n• Elasticity determines the size of the effect. The more wage elastic the demand for labour — labour a large share of total costs, capital easily substituted, output demand price elastic, long time period — the larger the fall in employment for a given wage rise. Where demand is inelastic, employment falls only slightly.\n• Alternative adjustment margins: firms may cut hours, reduce non-wage benefits, raise prices, accelerate automation, or accept lower profit margins rather than cut headcount, so the employment effect can be smaller than the diagram implies.\n• Application: at £12.71 the NLW binds for a large share of employees, so the effect operates on a much bigger group than a narrow minimum wage would.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) accurate labelled diagram showing the minimum wage above equilibrium and the excess supply, with a developed chain of reasoning applied to the 2026 rate. L2 (4–6) correct diagram with partial analysis, or analysis without a correct diagram. L1 (1–3) assertion that a minimum wage causes unemployment with no mechanism.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 3–4) states: "Where a single employer dominates a local labour market, a legal wage floor need not cost jobs at all." With the help of a diagram, explain how a national minimum wage can raise both the wage rate and employment in a monopsonistic labour market.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Start from the monopsony diagram: supply (ACL), MCL above it, MRP as demand. Unregulated outcome: employment Lm where MCL = MRP, wage Wm read off the supply curve, with Wm < MRP at that level.\n• Impose a minimum wage Wmin between Wm and the competitive wage Wc. The supply curve becomes horizontal at Wmin up to the point where it meets the original supply curve — and crucially, over that horizontal stretch MCL = Wmin, because the firm can now hire extra workers without raising the wage for everyone.\n• New profit-maximising employment is where the new MCL (the horizontal line) cuts MRP, giving employment greater than Lm. So the wage rises AND employment rises — the opposite of the competitive prediction.\n• Limit to the result: if the minimum wage is set above the competitive wage Wc, employment falls below Lc and the standard unemployment result returns. The employment gain therefore depends on the wage floor being set in the window between Wm and Wc.\n• Application and evaluation: this is the standard theoretical explanation for why UK minimum wage upratings since 1999 have produced far smaller employment effects than early competitive models predicted, and why the Low Pay Commission recommends upratings cautiously rather than ruling them out.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) correct monopsony diagram showing the kinked MCL under the wage floor and both the wage and employment increases identified, with the "only within a window" qualification. L2 (4–6) diagram correct but the kinked MCL not explained, or the employment result asserted rather than derived. L1 (1–3) description of monopsony with no diagrammatic derivation.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 21–22) states: "Union membership rose again in 2025, to 22.4% of employees." With the help of a diagram, analyse how a trade union can raise the wages of its members in an otherwise competitive labour market.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram route 1 — union as a monopoly seller of labour: draw competitive equilibrium at We, Le, then a horizontal union-negotiated wage Wu above We. Employment falls to the quantity demanded at Wu, and excess supply appears: insiders gain, outsiders lose.\n• Diagram route 2 — restricting supply: a closed shop, licensing or qualification requirement shifts the supply of labour curve left, raising the wage and reducing employment along an unchanged MRP curve.\n• Diagram route 3 — raising demand: productivity bargaining, or campaigning for demand for the final product, shifts the MRP curve right, raising both the wage and employment. This is the only route that avoids a trade-off.\n• Chain of reasoning on outcomes: the size of the wage gain and the employment cost depend on the wage elasticity of demand for labour and on union bargaining power — density, the employer\'s ability to substitute capital, the cost of a strike to the employer, and legal constraints such as statutory ballot thresholds.\n• Application: UK union density is now concentrated in the public sector (48.5% in 2025 against 12.1% in the private sector), so union effects on pay are much stronger in public services than in the private economy.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) at least one fully labelled diagram used to derive the wage increase, with an explicit chain of reasoning and contextual application. L2 (4–6) diagram present but only partly used, or two routes listed without development. L1 (1–3) description of what unions do, with no diagrammatic analysis.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 5–6) states: "Orders for new-build housing fell sharply, and bricklayers were laid off within weeks." With the help of a diagram, explain why the demand for labour is a derived demand.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Definition and mechanism: labour is not demanded for its own sake but for the output it produces. The demand curve for labour IS the marginal revenue product curve, MRP = MPP x MR, so anything that changes either the physical productivity of labour or the value of what it makes shifts the demand for labour.\n• Diagram: two panels, or one labour market diagram with a leftward shift. A fall in demand for new housing lowers the price of the output; since MRP = MPP x price for a price-taking firm, the whole MRP curve shifts left, cutting the equilibrium wage and employment for bricklayers from W1,L1 to W2,L2.\n• Chain of reasoning: the fall in employment is immediate because construction labour is hired on short contracts and labour is a large share of total cost, making demand for it wage elastic and responsive.\n• Why MRP slopes downwards in the first place: diminishing marginal returns mean each extra worker added to a fixed quantity of capital adds less physical output, so MRP falls as employment rises.\n• Extension: the same logic works in reverse — a housebuilding programme raises the derived demand for bricklayers, and because the supply of trained bricklayers is inelastic in the short run, wages rise sharply before employment can.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) derived demand explained through MRP with an accurate labelled diagram showing the shift, applied to the extract. L2 (4–6) correct idea with a partly used or partly inaccurate diagram. L1 (1–3) definition of derived demand only.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 17–18) states: "Some workers responded to higher hourly pay by working fewer hours, not more." With the help of a diagram, explain why an individual\'s supply curve of labour may bend backwards.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: wage rate on the vertical axis, hours supplied on the horizontal. The curve slopes upwards at low wages, reaches a maximum, then bends back to the left above some wage W*.\n• Substitution effect: a higher wage raises the opportunity cost of an hour of leisure, so the worker substitutes work for leisure — this pushes hours up and dominates at low wage rates.\n• Income effect: a higher wage means a given target income can be earned in fewer hours, and if leisure is a normal good the worker buys more of it — this pushes hours down. Above W*, the income effect outweighs the substitution effect and the curve bends back.\n• Chain of reasoning: the turning point differs between individuals and depends on preferences, household circumstances, fixed financial commitments such as a mortgage, and the tax and benefit system, which alters the net wage actually faced.\n• Reconciliation with the market: the MARKET supply curve of labour to an occupation normally still slopes upwards even where individual curves bend back, because a higher wage attracts new entrants from other occupations, other regions or outside the labour force.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) accurate labelled backward-bending diagram, both effects explained and correctly related to the turning point, plus the individual/market distinction. L2 (4–6) diagram correct but only one effect developed. L1 (1–3) diagram or description only, with no use of income and substitution effects.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 11–12) states: "Employers warned that the rise in employer National Insurance contributions from April 2025 would push them towards automation." With the help of a diagram, analyse the effect of a rise in non-wage labour costs on employment.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: labour market with demand (MRP) and supply. A rise in employer NICs raises the cost of employing a worker at every wage rate, so the demand for labour curve shifts DOWN/left by the amount of the extra cost. Equilibrium employment falls and the wage received by the worker falls, even though the cost to the firm rises.\n• Incidence: the split of the burden depends on the relative elasticities. Where the supply of labour is wage inelastic — which is typical, since most workers cannot easily withdraw from work — most of the cost is passed back to workers in lower wage growth rather than lost jobs.\n• Substitution towards capital: a rise in the relative price of labour makes capital relatively cheaper, so over time firms substitute self-service tills, warehouse robotics or software for labour. This effect is larger in the long run, which is why the demand for labour is more wage elastic over longer periods.\n• Where the wage cannot fall — because the worker is already on the £12.71 National Living Wage — the adjustment cannot come through lower pay and must come through hours, employment, prices or margins. That is why the combination of a higher wage floor and higher employer NICs bites hardest in low-paid sectors.\n• Context: employer NICs rose from 13.8% to 15% and the secondary threshold was cut from £9 100 to £5 000 from April 2025, so the change was concentrated on part-time and low-paid employment.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) accurate labelled diagram with the demand shift correctly derived, a developed chain on incidence or substitution, applied to the context. L2 (4–6) diagram correct with partial analysis. L1 (1–3) assertion that costs rise and jobs fall.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 26–27) states: "Net migration to the UK fell sharply from its 2023 peak, and some sectors reported severe recruitment difficulties." With the help of a diagram, explain the likely effects of a fall in net migration on wages and employment in a low-paid sector.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: labour market for the sector. A fall in net migration shifts the supply of labour curve left, raising the equilibrium wage from W1 to W2 and reducing equilibrium employment from L1 to L2.\n• Chain of reasoning: migrant workers are concentrated in particular sectors — agriculture, food processing, hospitality, social care and construction — so the supply shift is sector-specific rather than economy-wide, and the wage effect is correspondingly concentrated.\n• Size of the effect depends on elasticities: if the demand for labour is wage inelastic in the short run, because there is no ready capital substitute for a care worker, the wage rises a lot and employment falls little. Over time firms substitute capital or relocate production, making demand more elastic and the employment loss larger.\n• Counter-consideration: migrants are consumers as well as workers, so a fall in net migration also shifts the demand for labour left through lower aggregate demand for housing, retail and services. The net effect on wages is therefore theoretically ambiguous and empirically small for the average worker, even where it is larger at the bottom of the distribution.\n• Application: recruitment difficulties reported in the extract are consistent with excess demand for labour at the old wage — an unfilled vacancy is the visible form of a supply curve that has shifted left faster than wages have adjusted.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) accurate labelled diagram with a correct supply shift, developed reasoning including at least one qualification, applied to a named low-paid sector. L2 (4–6) diagram correct with limited development. L1 (1–3) assertion with no diagram or no mechanism.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 30–31) states: "Firms say they cannot find workers with the right skills, even where unemployment is high." With the help of a diagram, analyse how occupational immobility of labour causes labour market failure.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Definition: occupational immobility is the inability of workers to move between occupations because they lack the necessary skills, qualifications or licences. It makes the supply of labour to a skilled occupation highly wage inelastic in the short run.\n• Diagram: draw two markets side by side, or one skilled market with a near-vertical supply curve. A rightward shift in demand for the skilled occupation raises the wage sharply from W1 to W2 with almost no increase in employment, because the extra workers simply do not exist in the short run. In the declining occupation, demand shifts left and workers become structurally unemployed at the going wage.\n• Why this is a market failure: the price signal — a high and rising skilled wage — should reallocate labour, but the adjustment is blocked, so the economy operates inside its production possibility boundary. Unfilled vacancies and unemployed workers coexist.\n• Reinforcing failure: training has positive externalities and firms fear poaching, so private provision is below the socially optimal level. Individuals also under-invest because of imperfect information about future returns and credit constraints.\n• Policy response and its limits: apprenticeships, the apprenticeship levy, adult skills funding and vocational reform all target this, but retraining lags take years and are least effective for older displaced workers — which is why hysteresis can follow a structural shock.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) accurate diagram with inelastic supply used to derive the outcome, market failure explained rather than asserted, applied to the extract. L2 (4–6) partly developed analysis or a partly used diagram. L1 (1–3) definition plus examples only.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 19–20) states: "The median hourly pay of women working full time was 6.9% below that of men in April 2025." Analyse two possible economic explanations for the gender pay gap.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any two, each developed as a chain of reasoning:\n• Human capital and labour force interruption: career breaks for childcare reduce accumulated experience and firm-specific human capital, lowering measured MRP and therefore pay. Evidence for this mechanism is that the gap is small for workers in their twenties and widens sharply after the arrival of children — the "motherhood penalty".\n• Occupational and sectoral segregation: women are over-represented in occupations with lower average pay — care, retail, administration, primary teaching — and under-represented in high-paying ones such as finance and engineering. Because wages are set by MRP and supply conditions occupation by occupation, a composition difference produces an aggregate gap even where pay for the same job is equal.\n• Part-time work and the hours penalty: the all-employee gap (12.8% in April 2025) is far larger than the full-time gap (6.9%) precisely because part-time hourly pay is lower and a larger share of women work part time. Part-time roles are concentrated in jobs with flatter pay progression.\n• Discrimination: if employers systematically underestimate the MRP of one group, the perceived MRP curve lies below the true one, so they hire fewer and pay less than productivity warrants. This is productively inefficient as well as inequitable, since profitable hires are forgone; competitive pressure should erode it over time but monopsony power and imperfect information allow it to persist.\n• Monopsony and job search: women are on average less geographically mobile within a household, which reduces outside options and hence bargaining power — a monopsony explanation of the gap that does not require prejudice.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) two explanations, each developed into a full chain of reasoning with accurate use of the 2025 data or other dated evidence. L2 (4–6) two explanations with limited development, or one developed well. L1 (1–3) listed causes with no economic analysis. Note: only two explanations are required — reward the best two.` },

      /* ─────────── Paper 3 investigation: 10-mark data questions ─────────── */

      { q: `Extract D (Table 2) — written for this practice question; National Living Wage figures are official\nUK youth labour market indicators\n  National Living Wage (21+):                      2021 = £8.91 ; 2026 = £12.71\n  Employment rate, 18–24 year olds (%):            2021 = 60.4 ; 2026 = 57.1\n  Employment rate, 25–49 year olds (%):            2021 = 84.6 ; 2026 = 84.1\n  Share of 18–24s in full-time education (%):      2021 = 31.8 ; 2026 = 36.2\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data in Extract D (Table 2) suggest that increases in the National Living Wage have reduced employment among young workers?`, marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Evidence that they might: the NLW rose 42.65% while the 18–24 employment rate fell 3.3 percentage points, four times the 0.5 point fall for 25–49 year olds. Young workers are disproportionately low paid, so the wage floor binds hardest for them — consistent with the competitive prediction that a wage above MRP prices out the least experienced workers.\n• Evidence that they might not: the share of 18–24s in full-time education rose 4.4 percentage points over the same period. A student who is studying rather than working is not "priced out"; this alone could account for most or all of the employment rate fall, so the correlation does not establish causation.\n• Missing data: the table gives no information about hours worked, about unemployment as distinct from inactivity, about the 18–20 rate (which was lower than the 21+ rate throughout), or about the state of aggregate demand in either year — a cyclical downturn would also reduce youth employment.\n• Monopsony counter-argument: in low-paid sectors where a few large employers dominate, a wage floor set below the competitive wage raises employment. The direction of the effect is therefore theoretically ambiguous, not settled by theory.\n• Conclusion on extent: the data are consistent with a small negative effect but nowhere near sufficient to demonstrate one, because the education effect is an untested confounder and no counterfactual is available. A defensible judgement is "weak, suggestive evidence at most", with a statement of what further data would settle it.\n\nLevels: L3 (8–10) the data are used precisely BOTH for and against the proposition, alternative explanations are identified, and a supported judgement on the EXTENT is reached. L2 (4–7) some accurate use of the data on at least one side, limited alternative explanation, judgement asserted. L1 (1–3) data described with no evaluation of what they can support.` },

      { q: `Extract D (Table 2) — written for this practice question; union density figures follow the official 2025 pattern\nUK sectors, 2026\n  Sector 1 (public services):  union density 48.5% ; median hourly pay £19.10 ; graduate share of workforce 52%\n  Sector 2 (manufacturing):    union density 19.4% ; median hourly pay £17.20 ; graduate share of workforce 26%\n  Sector 3 (hospitality):      union density 3.6%  ; median hourly pay £12.95 ; graduate share of workforce 11%\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data in Extract D (Table 2) suggest that trade union membership is the main determinant of wage differentials between sectors?`, marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The apparent relationship: density and pay move together across all three sectors — a 44.9 percentage point density gap between Sectors 1 and 3 accompanies a 47.5% pay gap. The union mark-up story is that collective bargaining shifts the wage above the competitive level, or restores it towards the competitive level where the employer is a monopsonist.\n• The confounding variable is in the table: the graduate share moves in exactly the same direction, from 52% to 11%. Human capital theory predicts higher MRP and therefore higher pay for a more qualified workforce with or without a union, so the data cannot separate the two effects.\n• Other omitted determinants: capital intensity and therefore labour productivity, the price elasticity of demand for the final product, the degree of product market competition, regional composition, and hours and shift patterns. Hospitality also has a much larger share of workers on the £12.71 National Living Wage, which compresses pay from below regardless of unionisation.\n• Reverse causation: unions organise more easily where pay and job security are already high and workplaces are large, so high pay may cause high density rather than the other way round.\n• Judgement on extent: the data establish a strong association but cannot establish that union membership is the MAIN determinant; identifying that would need data comparing similar workers in unionised and non-unionised workplaces within the same sector. Reward candidates who say precisely what further evidence they would want.\n\nLevels: L3 (8–10) precise use of the figures, explicit identification of the confounder in the table, and a supported judgement on extent. L2 (4–7) the association is described and one limitation raised. L1 (1–3) the data are restated.` },

      /* ─────────── Section B part (a): 15-mark explain questions ─────────── */

      { q: 'Explain the determinants of the wage elasticity of demand for labour.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Availability and cost of substitutes for labour: the easier it is to replace workers with capital — self-service checkouts, robotic picking, AI-assisted software — the more elastic the demand. Where a task is physically or legally impossible to automate, such as much of social care, demand is inelastic.\n• Labour costs as a proportion of total costs: where wages are a large share of total costs, a given wage rise raises unit costs a lot, so firms respond strongly — demand is elastic. In capital-intensive industries such as oil refining, wages are a small share of cost and demand for labour is inelastic.\n• Price elasticity of demand for the final product: because the demand for labour is derived, a wage rise that is passed into prices costs the firm more sales where product demand is price elastic, so it cuts employment more. Highly competitive markets with substitutable products therefore have more elastic labour demand.\n• Time period: in the short run, capital is fixed and contracts, training and redundancy costs make adjustment slow, so demand is inelastic. In the long run firms can re-engineer processes, relocate or automate, so demand for labour is far more wage elastic. This is why announced wage-floor increases have larger employment effects than immediate measured ones.\n• Ease of substitution between types of labour: where skilled workers can be replaced by cheaper less-skilled workers plus supervision, demand for the skilled group is more elastic.\n• Useful diagram: two labour demand curves of different slope through the same equilibrium, showing the different employment loss from the same wage rise.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) at least three determinants explained with developed chains of reasoning and relevant application. L2 (6–10) determinants identified with some explanation; reasoning incomplete. L1 (1–5) a list with little or no explanation. Application to real labour markets is expected at L3.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main causes of wage differentials between different occupations in the UK.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Differences in marginal revenue product: pay is determined at the margin by MPP x MR, so occupations where workers are highly productive or where the output they produce commands a high price — a surgeon, a derivatives trader, a premier league footballer — attract high wages. Human capital accumulated through education, training and experience raises MPP and shifts the MRP curve right.\n• Differences in the elasticity and size of labour supply: occupations with long training periods, expensive qualifications, licensing or innate talent requirements have small, wage inelastic supply, so a given demand yields a high equilibrium wage. Occupations with low entry requirements have large, elastic supply and lower wages, as with care work.\n• Compensating wage differentials: unpleasant, dangerous, unsociable or insecure work must pay more to attract an equivalent worker, other things equal — offshore work, night shifts, the armed forces. Conversely, jobs with high non-monetary satisfaction can pay less.\n• Labour market imperfections: monopsony power holds pay below MRP; union bargaining power raises it above; imperfect information means workers do not know the best-paying alternative; geographical and occupational immobility prevent the arbitrage that would narrow the gaps.\n• Discrimination and segregation: systematic underestimation of one group\'s MRP, or crowding into a narrow range of occupations, produces persistent differentials — the full-time gender pay gap was 6.9% in April 2025 and the all-employee gap 12.8%.\n• Institutional floors: the National Living Wage at £12.71 from April 2026 compresses differentials at the bottom of the distribution by law rather than by market forces.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) at least three causes explained with full chains of reasoning, accurate terminology and dated UK evidence. L2 (6–10) causes identified, explanation partial. L1 (1–5) description or list only.` },

      { q: 'Explain the determinants of the supply of labour to a particular occupation.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The wage rate relative to other occupations: the fundamental determinant, producing movement along the supply curve. What matters is the net wage after tax, benefits withdrawal and travel costs, not the headline rate — which is why high effective marginal tax rates blunt supply responses.\n• Non-monetary characteristics: job security, pension provision, holiday entitlement, status, promotion prospects, hours flexibility, safety and job satisfaction all shift supply. A pleasant job attracts supply at a lower wage; an unpleasant one requires a compensating differential.\n• Qualifications, training time and barriers to entry: where entry requires a degree, professional examinations or licensing, supply in the short run is nearly fixed and highly wage inelastic. Supply becomes more elastic as the time period lengthens and new entrants can complete training.\n• Occupational and geographical mobility: the size of the pool of workers who could realistically switch into the occupation determines how far supply responds. Housing costs, family ties and non-transferable skills restrict it.\n• Demographic and participation factors: the size and age structure of the working population, migration policy, female participation, the state pension age, and childcare availability and cost all shift the supply curve.\n• Information and expectations: supply responds to expected future earnings, so a widely reported shortage can produce an overshoot of entrants several years later — the cobweb pattern seen in professions with long training pipelines.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) at least three determinants explained with developed reasoning, with the distinction between movement along and shifts of the supply curve handled correctly. L2 (6–10) determinants identified with partial explanation. L1 (1–5) listing.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main causes of the immobility of labour in the UK, and the main consequences for the efficient allocation of resources.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Geographical immobility — causes: large regional differences in house prices and rents, the transaction costs of moving, family and school ties, and imperfect information about vacancies elsewhere. Social housing tenancies are not portable, which locks lower-income households in place.\n• Occupational immobility — causes: skills that are specific to a declining industry, the time and cost of retraining, licensing and qualification requirements, and age — the returns to retraining fall as the remaining working life shortens.\n• Why training is under-supplied: positive externalities mean the social return exceeds the private return; firms fear that trained workers will be poached, so they under-invest; individuals face credit constraints and imperfect information about future returns.\n• Consequence 1 — structural unemployment coexisting with unfilled vacancies: resources are unemployed while demand goes unmet, so the economy operates inside its production possibility boundary. Chains of reasoning should link this to lost output, lower tax revenue and higher welfare spending.\n• Consequence 2 — persistent regional and occupational wage differentials that the market does not arbitrage away, widening income inequality and reinforcing regional divergence.\n• Consequence 3 — hysteresis: long spells out of work erode skills, confidence and employer perceptions, so a temporary shock permanently lowers the productive capacity of the economy, shifting LRAS left.\n• Policy relevance: this is the economic case for apprenticeships, adult skills funding, relocation support and housing supply reform — all supply-side interventions aimed at shifting LRAS right.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) causes AND consequences both explained with developed chains and accurate application. L2 (6–10) one side developed, the other listed. L1 (1–5) description of immobility only.` },

      { q: 'Explain how wages and employment are determined in a labour market in which a monopsony employer bargains with a trade union. Use a diagram to support your answer.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Establish the monopsony baseline: supply (ACL), MCL above and steeper, MRP as demand. Employment where MCL = MRP at Lm, wage Wm read off the supply curve, with Wm below both MRP and the competitive wage Wc — the wedge is monopsony exploitation.\n• Introduce the union: a union sets a floor wage Wu, which makes the effective supply curve horizontal at Wu up to the old supply curve, and makes MCL equal Wu over that range.\n• Case 1, Wm < Wu < Wc: employment rises above Lm and the wage rises. Both parties can be better off relative to the unregulated monopsony outcome — the union is offsetting monopsony power rather than creating distortion. This is the theoretical core of "countervailing power".\n• Case 2, Wu > Wc: employment falls below the competitive level and unemployment appears, the standard union-in-a-competitive-market result.\n• Bilateral monopoly and indeterminacy: with one buyer and one seller, theory alone does not pin down the wage. The outcome lies in a bargaining range between the monopsonist\'s preferred Wm and the union\'s maximum acceptable wage, and where it lands depends on relative bargaining power — union density, strike funds, the employer\'s ability to substitute capital or relocate, the cost of lost output, public sympathy and the legal framework governing ballots and industrial action.\n• Application: UK public services approximate this model closely — a near-single employer facing highly unionised staff (48.5% density in the public sector in 2025), with pay reviewed through pay review bodies rather than settled by a market-clearing wage.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) both parties\' effects derived, preferably diagrammatically, with the bargaining range and its determinants explained. L2 (6–10) monopsony or union analysed well but not the interaction. L1 (1–5) definitions only.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main causes of the gender pay gap in the UK and how government policy has attempted to reduce it.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Scale to anchor the answer: median hourly pay for full-time employees was 6.9% lower for women than men in April 2025 (down from 7.1% in 2024); the all-employee gap was 12.8%.\n• Cause 1 — the motherhood penalty: career interruptions reduce accumulated experience and firm-specific human capital, lowering measured MRP. The gap is near zero in the twenties and widens sharply after childbirth, which is why the gap is better described as a parenthood effect than a gender effect within a single job.\n• Cause 2 — occupational segregation: concentration in lower-paid sectors (care, retail, administration) and under-representation in higher-paying ones. Because pay reflects MRP and supply conditions occupation by occupation, composition alone generates an aggregate gap.\n• Cause 3 — part-time work: part-time hourly pay is lower and progression flatter, which is why the all-employee gap far exceeds the full-time gap.\n• Cause 4 — discrimination and unequal bargaining power: employers underestimating MRP, or monopsony power over a less geographically mobile workforce, hold pay below productivity. This is productively inefficient as well as inequitable.\n• Policy: the Equality Act 2010 makes unequal pay for equal work unlawful; mandatory gender pay gap reporting since 2017 for employers with 250 or more staff uses information provision rather than direct regulation; shared parental leave, the extension of funded childcare hours and flexible working rights target the underlying cause rather than the symptom.\n• Chain on policy effectiveness: reporting works through reputational pressure and is cheap but unenforced; childcare policy addresses the actual mechanism but is expensive and takes years to change participation patterns.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) at least three causes explained with developed reasoning, accurate dated data and specific policies. L2 (6–10) causes identified, policy thin or generic. L1 (1–5) assertion.` },

      /* ─────────── Section A (d) / Section B (b): 25-mark evaluation ─────────── */

      { q: 'Extract B (lines 24–25) states: "The National Living Wage rose to £12.71 an hour in April 2026, and now stands at over 70% of median pay for employees aged 21 and over." Using the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that further large increases in the National Living Wage would do more harm than good to low-paid workers.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The case for harm — competitive model: a wage floor above the market-clearing wage creates excess supply of labour. With a bite above 70%, the floor binds for a very large share of employees, so any disemployment effect operates on a wide group, concentrated in hospitality, retail and social care where labour is a large share of costs and product demand is price elastic. Diagram: minimum wage above equilibrium, excess supply Qs – Qd.\n• Reinforcing argument: the 2026 rise came on top of higher employer National Insurance contributions from April 2025 (rate up to 15%, secondary threshold cut to £5 000), so the total cost of employing a low-paid worker rose faster than the headline wage, accelerating substitution towards capital and self-service technology.\n• The case against harm — monopsony: where a few employers dominate a local low-wage market, the unregulated wage lies below both MRP and the competitive wage, and a floor within the window between them raises the wage AND employment. This is the standard explanation for why measured employment effects of UK upratings since 1999 have been small.\n• Wider benefits: higher pay reduces in-work poverty, raises the incentive to work rather than claim, may raise productivity through efficiency-wage effects and lower turnover, and injects spending into local economies since low-income households have a high marginal propensity to consume.\n• Costs beyond employment: differential compression demotivates supervisors; some firms respond through hours, contracted shift patterns and non-wage benefits rather than headcount, so measured employment understates the real effect; small firms with thin margins are hit hardest.\n• Distributional evaluation: the NLW is a blunt anti-poverty instrument because many low-paid workers live in higher-income households, so targeted transfers such as Universal Credit reach poor households more precisely — but they cost the Exchequer, while the NLW does not.\n• Judgement: the answer turns on how close the floor already is to the competitive wage. At a bite above 70% the monopsony window is narrowing, so the case for caution is stronger than it was at a 50% bite — a defensible conclusion is that continued increases should be evidence-led and gradual rather than large, with the Low Pay Commission\'s remit as the mechanism.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) sustained, balanced argument with accurate diagrams, precise use of the extracts and dated evidence, evaluation integrated throughout, and a conclusion that follows from the argument rather than being asserted. L4 (16–20) well-developed and mostly balanced with sound evaluation. L3 (11–15) some analysis and evaluation, balance uneven. L2 (6–10) largely descriptive, one-sided. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that trade unions are a source of labour market failure rather than a solution to it.', marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Unions as a source of failure: acting as a monopoly seller, a union that raises the wage above the competitive level creates excess supply and a deadweight loss. Insiders gain at the expense of outsiders — the young, the unemployed and those on insecure contracts. Restrictive practices and resistance to new working methods can hold productivity below its potential, and strikes impose costs on third parties, most obviously in transport, health and education.\n• Unions as a solution to failure: where the employer is a monopsonist, the unregulated wage is below MRP and employment is below the competitive level. A union restores the wage towards the competitive outcome and RAISES employment — countervailing power correcting an existing distortion rather than creating a new one. Diagram: monopsony with a union-set wage floor creating a horizontal MCL.\n• Information and contracting failures: individual workers negotiate with far less information about pay, and with far more to lose from a failed negotiation, than their employer. Collective bargaining reduces this asymmetry, and union involvement in health and safety, grievance procedures and training is associated with lower accident rates and turnover.\n• Efficiency-wage and voice effects: by providing a channel for "voice" rather than "exit", unions can reduce quit rates and raise firm-specific human capital, improving productivity — the empirical evidence here is genuinely mixed and should be presented as such.\n• Scale and context matters: with density at 22.4% of employees in 2025 and only 12.1% in the private sector, the aggregate distortionary power of UK unions is far smaller than in the 1970s, and the binding constraint on private-sector low pay is now the statutory floor, not collective bargaining. In the public sector, at 48.5% density, union effects remain substantial.\n• Legal framework: statutory ballot thresholds, notice requirements and minimum service levels constrain industrial action, so the question is partly about what unions are permitted to do rather than what they would do.\n• Judgement: whether unions are a failure or a fix depends on the market structure they operate in. In competitive labour markets the classical criticism holds; in monopsonistic ones the opposite is true — and since monopsony is pervasive in low-paid local labour markets, a blanket verdict either way is not supportable.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) both models handled accurately, evaluation of the conditions under which each applies, dated evidence, integrated judgement. L4 (16–20) well-developed and balanced. L3 (11–15) some analysis, balance uneven. L2 (6–10) one-sided description. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the usefulness of marginal revenue productivity theory in explaining wage differentials in the UK labour market.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• What the theory does well: it gives a rigorous account of the demand for labour as a derived demand, MRP = MPP x MR, and explains why the graduate premium exists, why productivity growth and real wage growth track each other over long periods, and why workers in capital-intensive, high-value industries earn more. It also predicts the profit-maximising hiring rule, hire until MRP = MCL, which is genuinely how firms behave at the margin.\n• Measurement problems: for most jobs the individual\'s marginal physical product is unobservable — what is the marginal output of a nurse, a teacher, a compliance officer? Where production is a team process, output cannot be attributed to individuals at all, so the theory cannot be applied directly.\n• Assumption problems: the model assumes profit maximisation, perfect information, homogeneous labour, perfectly competitive product and labour markets, and that labour is the only variable factor. Real labour markets have monopsony, unions, contracts, minimum wages, discrimination, imperfect information and search frictions, all of which break the link between pay and MRP.\n• Competing explanations with real force: compensating differentials explain pay gaps that MRP cannot; segmented labour market theory explains why primary and secondary sectors do not arbitrage; signalling theory suggests qualifications raise pay by conveying information rather than productivity; efficiency wage theory says firms deliberately pay above the market-clearing wage.\n• Institutional determinants: the National Living Wage at £12.71 from April 2026 sets the wage of a large share of the workforce by statute, and public sector pay is set by pay review bodies — in neither case does MRP determine the wage at the margin.\n• Evidence: the persistence of the gender pay gap at 6.9% for full-time employees in April 2025, and the failure of low-paid sector wages to rise to match reported vacancies, are both hard to explain on a pure MRP account.\n• Judgement: MRP is best understood as the underlying determinant of the DEMAND side — it sets a ceiling on what a profit-maximising employer will pay — while supply conditions, institutions and bargaining power determine where within that range the wage actually lands. A defensible conclusion is that MRP is necessary but nowhere near sufficient.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) the theory explained accurately, its assumptions probed, alternatives deployed, dated evidence used, and a judgement that distinguishes the demand side from the whole. L4 (16–20) well-developed and balanced. L3 (11–15) partial. L2 (6–10) description of the theory only. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Extract B (lines 9–10) states: "Employers report a shortage of skilled workers while unemployment among older men in former industrial areas remains stubbornly high." Using the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the effectiveness of government policies to reduce the immobility of labour.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagnose the failure first: occupational immobility makes the supply of skilled labour vertical in the short run, so demand increases raise wages without raising employment, while geographical immobility leaves vacancies and unemployed workers in different places. The result is production inside the PPF and a permanent loss of output.\n• Policy 1 — education and vocational training: apprenticeships, the apprenticeship levy, T-levels and adult skills funding directly raise the human capital of displaced workers, shifting the supply of skilled labour right and LRAS right. Evaluation: long time lags, high cost per place, poor completion rates on some programmes, and the returns to retraining fall sharply with age — which is precisely the group in the extract.\n• Policy 2 — housing and relocation: increasing housing supply in high-demand regions, reforming stamp duty (a transaction tax that directly discourages moving), and portable social tenancies. Evaluation: housing supply responds slowly, planning constraints bind, and relocation subsidies risk hollowing out already-declining areas.\n• Policy 3 — information and matching: Jobcentre Plus, national vacancy databases and careers advice reduce search frictions cheaply. Evaluation: addresses frictional rather than structural immobility, so it helps at the margin but not with the core skills mismatch.\n• Policy 4 — regional and place-based policy: investment in transport and infrastructure, enterprise zones, devolution of skills budgets to combined authorities. Evaluation: brings work to the worker rather than the worker to the work, but is expensive, slow, and vulnerable to displacement — jobs relocated rather than created.\n• Government failure considerations: information failure means the state may train people for the wrong skills, given how fast AI and automation are changing demand; administrative costs and political capture direct spending towards visible projects rather than effective ones; unintended consequences include subsidising training firms would have funded anyway.\n• Market alternatives: employer-led training, higher wage differentials doing the reallocation on their own, and immigration policy filling shortages quickly — each with its own distributional cost.\n• Judgement: the most defensible conclusion distinguishes by horizon and target group. Information and matching policies are cheap and fast but small; skills policy is the only route that addresses the underlying cause but works over a decade; housing policy is the highest-value geographical lever but the slowest politically. For older displaced workers specifically, expectations of retraining should be modest.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) several policies analysed with chains of reasoning, evaluated against cost, time lag, target group and government failure, using the extract, with a discriminating conclusion. L4 (16–20) well-developed, mostly balanced. L3 (11–15) policies described with some evaluation. L2 (6–10) list of policies. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Discuss the view that the growth of artificial intelligence and automation will permanently reduce the demand for labour in the UK.', marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The case for: automation substitutes capital for labour where tasks are routine and codifiable. As the relative price of labour rises — the National Living Wage at £12.71 from April 2026, employer NICs at 15% since April 2025 — the incentive to substitute strengthens, shifting the MRP-based demand curve for affected labour left and reducing employment and pay in those occupations. Unlike earlier waves, AI reaches cognitive and clerical tasks, so the displaced group includes graduates.\n• Distributional point: the effect is uneven. Polarisation means demand falls for middling routine roles while rising for both high-skill complementary roles and low-skill non-routine manual roles that are hard to automate, such as care. This widens wage differentials and worsens measured inequality even if total employment is unchanged.\n• The case against: technology raises labour productivity, so MPP and therefore MRP rise for workers who work WITH the new capital — the complementarity effect shifts their demand curve right. Historically, every previous wave of automation has changed the composition of employment rather than its total, and the UK employment rate stood at 75.1% in mid-2026 despite decades of automation.\n• The income effect: automation lowers costs and prices, raising real incomes and therefore aggregate demand, which creates derived demand for labour in entirely new sectors. The lump of labour fallacy — the assumption that there is a fixed quantity of work to be done — is the central error in the pessimistic case.\n• Adjustment costs are the real issue: even if long-run employment is unaffected, the transition imposes structural unemployment, hysteresis and regional concentration of losses on workers who cannot retrain quickly. The relevant policy question is therefore about the speed of adjustment, not the final level.\n• Policy response and evaluation: education and retraining (slow, costly, weaker returns for older workers), changes to how capital and labour are taxed (risks slowing productivity growth), and strengthened welfare during transition (raises the deficit, may weaken incentives).\n• Judgement: theory and history both point against a permanent fall in total labour demand, but a permanent change in its composition is very likely. The strongest answers conclude on the distributional and transitional question rather than on the aggregate one, and acknowledge genuine uncertainty about whether AI differs in kind from earlier automation.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) both sides developed with rigorous chains of reasoning, the lump of labour fallacy handled explicitly, dated evidence, integrated judgement. L4 (16–20) well-developed and balanced. L3 (11–15) some analysis, one side thin. L2 (6–10) assertion and description. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that monopsony power is the most important cause of low pay in the UK labour market.', marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Explain the mechanism: a monopsonist faces the upward-sloping market supply of labour, so MCL exceeds the wage. Hiring where MCL = MRP gives employment below, and a wage below, the competitive level, with the gap between MRP and the wage being monopsony exploitation. Diagram required for full credit.\n• Evidence that monopsony matters in the UK: many low-paid labour markets are local and thin — a single large care home group, supermarket, distribution centre or NHS trust in a travel-to-work area. Occupational and geographical immobility mean workers cannot credibly threaten to leave, which is what gives the employer wage-setting power. The small measured employment effects of successive minimum wage upratings since 1999 are consistent with monopsony rather than with a competitive market.\n• Alternative cause 1 — low productivity: on MRP theory, pay at the margin reflects productivity. The UK\'s weak productivity growth since 2008, low business investment and long tail of low-productivity firms would generate low pay even in a perfectly competitive labour market. This explanation places the cause on the demand side.\n• Alternative cause 2 — elastic labour supply at the bottom: occupations with low entry requirements have very large potential supply, so equilibrium pay is low even without any employer power. Migration and high participation reinforce this.\n• Alternative cause 3 — weak worker bargaining power beyond monopsony: private-sector union density of just 12.1% in 2025, insecure contracts, and a benefit system that makes leaving a job costly all reduce workers\' outside options.\n• Alternative cause 4 — human capital and educational inequality: a long tail of low basic skills limits the wage a worker can command in ANY market structure.\n• Policy implications differ by diagnosis, which is the strongest evaluative route: if monopsony is the cause, a higher wage floor and stronger collective bargaining raise pay without costing jobs. If low productivity is the cause, a wage floor above MRP does cost jobs, and the answer is investment, skills and competition policy. Getting the diagnosis wrong is costly in either direction.\n• Judgement: monopsony is plausibly a significant contributor in local low-wage markets, and the muted employment response to the NLW is real evidence for it — but productivity and skills explain the LEVEL of low pay across the whole economy in a way monopsony cannot. A defensible conclusion ranks monopsony as important but not the most important cause.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) monopsony modelled accurately, at least two serious alternatives developed, policy implications used as the evaluative device, dated evidence, clear judgement. L4 (16–20) well-developed and balanced. L3 (11–15) partial. L2 (6–10) description. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that the best way to raise the incomes of the lowest-paid workers in the UK is through education and training rather than through the National Living Wage.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The case for education and training: raising human capital raises MPP and therefore MRP, shifting the demand curve for that worker\'s labour right. This raises pay WITHOUT creating a wedge between the wage and productivity, so there is no theoretical disemployment effect — the worker is worth more, not just paid more. It also raises LRAS, so the gain is non-inflationary and compounds over a career.\n• Reinforcing point: training corrects an identified market failure. Positive externalities and the risk of poaching mean firms under-invest; credit constraints and imperfect information mean individuals do too. Government intervention therefore has an efficiency justification, not merely an equity one.\n• The case against relying on it: time lags run to years or decades; returns to retraining fall sharply with age; a large share of the low-paid are already in work and cannot easily study; and if everyone gains qualifications the relative advantage erodes — a fallacy of composition. Deadweight loss arises where the state funds training firms would have provided anyway, and government failure arises from training people for skills that AI then automates.\n• The case for the National Living Wage: it is immediate, costs the Exchequer nothing directly, reaches every low-paid employee, and where employers have monopsony power it can raise pay AND employment. At £12.71 from April 2026, with a bite above 70% of median pay, it is the single most powerful lever over low pay in the UK.\n• The case against the NLW: above the competitive wage it prices out the least productive workers; it is poorly targeted on poor HOUSEHOLDS, since many minimum wage earners are second earners or students; it interacts with Universal Credit taper rates so that a large share of the gross gain is withdrawn, leaving a much smaller net gain for exactly the households that need it most.\n• Third options that a top answer will raise: in-work benefits and lower taper rates target household poverty precisely; raising the personal allowance and NI thresholds raises net pay; competition policy and business investment raise productivity and hence sustainable pay; childcare provision raises hours and progression.\n• Judgement: these are complements operating on different curves and different timescales — the NLW sets a floor now, skills raise the ceiling later, and transfers do the household targeting neither can do. The strongest conclusions distinguish "raising the pay of the low-paid" from "raising the incomes of poor households", which is a different question with a different answer.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) both policies analysed with chains of reasoning, the targeting distinction drawn, third options considered, dated evidence, integrated judgement. L4 (16–20) well-developed and balanced. L3 (11–15) some evaluation, balance uneven. L2 (6–10) one-sided. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Extract B (lines 31–32) states: "Median hourly pay for women working full time was 6.9% below that of men in April 2025, down from 7.1% a year earlier." Using the extracts and your knowledge of economics, assess whether government intervention is the best way to close the gender pay gap.', marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Establish what the gap is and is not: the 6.9% full-time figure is a composition-adjusted-for-nothing average, not a measure of unequal pay for equal work, which has been unlawful since the Equal Pay Act 1970 and is now covered by the Equality Act 2010. The all-employee gap of 12.8% is larger because more women work part time. Getting this distinction right is a discriminator between levels.\n• The case that markets will close it: if discrimination means an employer underestimates a group\'s MRP, a rival that hires them at below their true productivity earns supernormal profit, so competitive pressure should erode the gap. The gap has narrowed steadily as participation, graduate shares and attitudes have changed — evidence that market and social forces are doing work without further intervention.\n• Why markets may not: monopsony power and search frictions reduce outside options; the gap arises largely from occupational segregation and career interruption, which no amount of product market competition addresses; and if employers bear the cost of statutory maternity provision, competition can entrench rather than erode differential treatment.\n• Intervention 1 — information: mandatory gender pay gap reporting for employers with 250 or more employees since 2017 is a low-cost, nudge-style correction of information failure. Evaluation: reputational pressure only, no enforcement, and firms can comply while changing nothing.\n• Intervention 2 — childcare and parental leave: funded childcare hours and shared parental leave attack the actual mechanism, the motherhood penalty, by raising participation, hours and continuity of experience. Evaluation: expensive, slow, and take-up of shared leave has been low — the policy only works if fathers use it.\n• Intervention 3 — regulation: pay transparency, quotas or bans on salary history questions. Evaluation: quotas address representation at the top but not the median; transparency can compress pay but may reduce pay flexibility.\n• Government failure: intervention aimed at an aggregate statistic can produce cosmetic compliance, or incentives to outsource low-paid work to shrink a reported gap without helping any worker.\n• Judgement: the best route depends on which part of the gap is targeted. Legal intervention has already dealt with unequal pay for equal work; the remaining gap is mostly about who does what job and who takes time out, so childcare and parental leave policy has the strongest claim, with reporting as a cheap complement — while markets alone are unlikely to close a gap that originates outside the workplace.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) the gap correctly characterised, market and interventionist cases both developed, specific dated policies evaluated, government failure considered, discriminating judgement. L4 (16–20) well-developed, mostly balanced. L3 (11–15) partial. L2 (6–10) description of the gap. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that the labour market for professional footballers, where a small number of players earn very large sums, can be fully explained by standard labour market theory.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The MRP explanation: a top player\'s marginal revenue product is genuinely enormous — broadcasting rights, matchday revenue, shirt sales, sponsorship and qualification for lucrative competitions all turn on marginal improvements in team performance. MRP = MPP x MR, and where MR per unit of performance is measured in tens of millions, a very high wage is exactly what the theory predicts.\n• The supply explanation: the supply of world-class players is extremely small and almost perfectly wage inelastic in the short run — talent cannot be manufactured by training alone. Demand shifts right against a near-vertical supply curve, so the entire increase shows up in price rather than quantity. Diagram: inelastic supply with a rightward demand shift.\n• Economic rent: because the supply is inelastic and the next-best alternative earnings of a top player are far lower, most of the wage is economic rent rather than transfer earnings. This is a clean application of a core concept and should be credited highly.\n• Superstar effects: in markets where output is broadcast at near-zero marginal cost to a global audience, small differences in quality produce very large differences in earnings, because consumers prefer the best and distribution is costless. This explains the extreme skew within the profession, which simple MRP on its own does not.\n• Where the theory struggles: MRP is hard to measure and is distributed across a team, so attributing revenue to an individual is partly guesswork; clubs are not always profit maximisers, with some pursuing trophies or owner prestige; bargaining, agents, transfer rules and financial regulations shape outcomes; and monopsony existed historically under the retain-and-transfer system, which suppressed pay far below MRP until it was struck down.\n• Wider evaluation: the same framework explains other winner-takes-all markets — chief executives, musicians, authors — and the fact that it does is evidence for the theory rather than against it. But it does not settle whether the outcome is equitable, which is a normative question the model cannot answer.\n• Judgement: standard theory, supplemented by economic rent and superstar effects, explains the LEVEL and the SKEW of footballers\' pay well — better than almost any other high-pay example — but the measurement problems and institutional influences mean "fully" is too strong.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) MRP, inelastic supply, economic rent and superstar effects all deployed accurately with a diagram, limitations evaluated, judgement on "fully". L4 (16–20) well-developed. L3 (11–15) MRP explained with limited evaluation. L2 (6–10) description. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Extract B (lines 4–5) states: "Union density among public sector employees was 48.5% in 2025, against 12.1% in the private sector." Using the extracts and your knowledge of economics, discuss the view that this difference means public sector pay is set inefficiently.', marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The inefficiency case: with density four times the private sector rate and the state as a near-monopsony employer, pay is settled by bargaining and pay review bodies rather than by a market-clearing wage. If the negotiated wage exceeds MRP, the taxpayer funds a wage above the value of output, resources are misallocated towards the public sector, and employment in it is lower than it would otherwise be for a given budget.\n• Reinforcing argument: public sector output is largely unpriced, so MRP cannot be observed. Without a market price for a teacher\'s or a nurse\'s output, there is no direct efficiency test of whether pay matches value — which is an information failure at the heart of the problem.\n• The counter-case — countervailing power: the state is a monopsonist in teaching, nursing and policing, since there is effectively one buyer. Unregulated monopsony pay would sit below MRP and below the competitive wage, producing under-employment and shortages. High union density restores the wage towards the competitive level, so high density is the correction, not the distortion.\n• Evidence route: persistent vacancies and recruitment and retention problems in nursing and teaching are more consistent with pay BELOW the market-clearing level than above it. Pay comparability with the private sector for equivalent qualifications is the relevant test, not the raw pay gap.\n• Composition caveat: the public sector employs a far higher share of graduates and professionals, so raw pay comparisons overstate any premium.\n• Wider efficiency considerations: pay review bodies attempt to replicate market outcomes using evidence on recruitment, retention and comparability — an institutional response to the missing price signal. Evaluation: they are advisory, their recommendations are constrained by fiscal policy, and settlements can lag conditions by a year or more.\n• Political economy: public sector pay disputes impose external costs on service users who are not party to the bargain, which is the strongest efficiency argument against pure bargaining and the justification for minimum service level requirements.\n• Judgement: the density difference alone proves nothing about efficiency, because efficiency depends on where pay sits relative to MRP and the alternative wage, and both models — union distortion and countervailing power — are consistent with the observed density. A defensible conclusion points to recruitment and retention data as the decisive evidence, and notes it currently points towards shortage rather than excess.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) both models developed, the missing-price-signal problem identified, the correct empirical test named, dated data used, discriminating judgement. L4 (16–20) well-developed and balanced. L3 (11–15) one model developed. L2 (6–10) description of density differences. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },
    ]
  },
  '3.1.7': {
    name: 'Income Distribution, Poverty & Inequality',
    questions: [

      /* ─────────── Section A part (a): 2-mark calculations ─────────── */

      { q: `Extract A (Figure 1) — written for this practice question\nA Lorenz curve has been drawn for Brackenshire. The area between the line of perfect equality and the Lorenz curve (area A) is measured as 0.165. The total area beneath the line of perfect equality is 0.5.\n\nUsing Extract A (Figure 1), calculate to two decimal places the Gini coefficient for Brackenshire.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: Gini coefficient = area A / (area A + area B), where A + B is the whole triangle beneath the line of perfect equality = 0.5.\n0.165 / 0.5 = 0.33.\n\n2 marks for 0.33 (accept 33% if the working shows the ratio). 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip. For context, the UK Gini coefficient for household disposable income was 33% before housing costs and 37% after housing costs in 2024/25, so this practice figure is realistic. Remind students that a Gini of 0 is perfect equality and 1 is perfect inequality.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nBrackenshire household incomes, 2026\n  Median equivalised weekly household disposable income: £620.00\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate to two decimal places the weekly relative low income threshold for Brackenshire, using the standard definition.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: the standard UK relative low income (relative poverty) threshold is 60% of contemporary median equivalised household disposable income.\n0.60 x £620.00 = £372.00 per week.\n\n2 marks for £372.00 to two decimal places. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for an answer given without the currency unit. Credit in a comment, but do not require, the point that because the threshold is a fixed proportion of the CURRENT median, it falls in a recession when median income falls — which is why measured relative poverty can improve while everyone is worse off.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nShare of total equivalised household disposable income by quintile, Brackenshire 2026\n  Bottom quintile: 7.5%\n  Second quintile: 12.0%\n  Third quintile:  16.5%\n  Fourth quintile: 23.5%\n  Top quintile:    40.5%\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate to two decimal places the quintile share ratio (the top quintile\'s share divided by the bottom quintile\'s share).`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: quintile share ratio = share of the top 20% / share of the bottom 20%.\n40.5 / 7.5 = 5.40.\n\n2 marks for 5.40. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for an answer expressed as a percentage rather than a ratio. Check that the shares sum to 100% (7.5 + 12.0 + 16.5 + 23.5 + 40.5 = 100) — a useful accuracy check students should be encouraged to make. Accept a comment that the ratio ignores everything happening inside the middle three quintiles, which the Gini coefficient at least partly captures.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nBrackenshire, 2026\n  Total households:                                   28.50 million\n  Households with income below 60% of the median:      4.30 million\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate to two decimal places the percentage of Brackenshire households in relative low income.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: headcount ratio = households below the threshold / total households x 100.\n4.30 / 28.50 x 100 = 15.0877… = 15.09%.\n\n2 marks for 15.09%. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for an unconverted decimal. Credit a comment that the headcount ratio says nothing about the DEPTH of poverty — a household £1 below the line and one £200 below it count identically — which is why the poverty gap is also published.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question; the inflation figure is official\n  Median equivalised household disposable income: 2025 = £34 500 ; 2026 = £35 880\n  CPI inflation over the same period: 3.1%\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate to two decimal places the percentage change in NOMINAL median household disposable income between 2025 and 2026.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: percentage change = (change / original) x 100.\nChange = £35 880 – £34 500 = £1 380.\n1 380 / 34 500 x 100 = 4.00%.\n\n2 marks for 4.00%. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for calculating the real rather than the nominal change when the question asked for nominal. Follow-up for discussion, not for marks here: with CPI inflation at 3.1% (the 12 months to August 2026), the approximate real increase is 4.00 – 3.10 = 0.90%, so nominal growth of 4% delivered less than 1% of extra purchasing power.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — the rates are the UK rates in force in 2026\nA Universal Credit claimant in work earns an extra £100 of gross pay, above the work allowance.\n  Income tax (basic rate):            20%\n  Employee National Insurance:         8%\n  Universal Credit taper rate:        55% of net earnings\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate the effective marginal deduction rate faced by this claimant, as a percentage of the extra £100 of gross pay.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: work through the deductions in order.\nIncome tax: 20% x £100 = £20. Employee NI: 8% x £100 = £8. Net earnings remaining = £72.\nUniversal Credit withdrawn: 55% x £72 = £39.60.\nTotal deducted = £20 + £8 + £39.60 = £67.60, so the effective marginal deduction rate = 67.60%.\n\n2 marks for 67.60% (accept "the claimant keeps £32.40 of the extra £100"). 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for simply adding 20 + 8 + 55 = 83% (which wrongly applies the taper to gross rather than net earnings). This calculation IS the poverty trap: the claimant faces a higher marginal deduction rate than an additional rate taxpayer.` },

      /* ─────────── Section A part (b): 4-mark data questions ─────────── */

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nShare of total household disposable income by quintile, Brackenshire\n  Bottom quintile: 2016 = 8.4% ; 2026 = 7.5%\n  Top quintile:    2016 = 38.2% ; 2026 = 40.5%\n  Gini coefficient: 2016 = 0.31 ; 2026 = 0.34\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that income inequality in Brackenshire has increased.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Manipulate the data: the top quintile\'s share rose by 2.3 percentage points while the bottom quintile\'s fell by 0.9 percentage points, so the quintile share ratio widened from 4.55 to 5.40 — an increase of 18.7% (1–2 marks for accurate manipulation).\n• The Gini coefficient rose from 0.31 to 0.34. Explain what this means: the Lorenz curve has bowed further away from the line of perfect equality, so area A relative to the whole triangle has grown (1 mark).\n• Explain the link: the two measures are consistent — a rise in the top share and a fall in the bottom share IS what makes the Lorenz curve bow outwards, so the Gini rise is not an independent piece of evidence but a summary of the same change (1 mark).\n• Caveat worth a mark if the other points are secure: these are shares, not levels. Real incomes in the bottom quintile could still have risen; inequality increasing does not by itself mean anyone is poorer.\n\n4 marks: figures manipulated AND the Gini/Lorenz relationship explained. 3 marks: accurate manipulation, explanation partial. 2 marks: figures quoted with a valid comment. 1 mark: figures restated.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nAverage annual household income per quintile, Brackenshire 2026\n  Bottom quintile: original income £6 200 ; final income £22 400\n  Top quintile:    original income £128 000 ; final income £89 600\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that the tax and benefit system redistributes income.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Manipulate: the bottom quintile gains £16 200, an increase of 261.3%; the top quintile loses £38 400, a fall of 30.0%. The ratio of top to bottom income falls from 20.65:1 on original income to 4.00:1 on final income (1–2 marks).\n• Explain the mechanism on the way up: original income is income from wages, self-employment, investments and private pensions before any state action. Cash benefits — Universal Credit, the state pension, disability benefits — are added to give gross income, and benefits in kind such as NHS treatment and state education are added at the end, which is why the bottom quintile\'s final income is nearly four times its original income (1 mark).\n• Explain the mechanism on the way down: direct taxes (income tax at 20/40/45% and employee National Insurance) are progressive and fall most heavily on the top quintile; indirect taxes such as VAT at 20% are regressive as a share of income and partly offset the redistribution (1 mark).\n• Evaluation for the fourth mark: benefits in kind are valued at what they cost the state to provide, not at what the household would pay, so the measured redistribution may be overstated.\n\n4 marks: both directions quantified AND the original-to-final income mechanism explained. Award no more than 2 marks if the answer simply says "the poor get more and the rich get less".` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nShare of total income and total wealth held by the top decile, Brackenshire 2026\n  Share of total household disposable income held by the richest 10%: 27%\n  Share of total household wealth held by the richest 10%:             57%\n  Share of total household wealth held by the poorest 30%:              2%\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that wealth is more unequally distributed than income.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Manipulate: the top decile holds 2.11 times as large a share of wealth as of income, and the poorest 30% hold just 2% of wealth — less than a fifteenth of a proportionate share (1 mark).\n• Explain why: wealth is a STOCK accumulated over a lifetime out of the FLOW of saved income, so differences in income compound over time. A household saving 30% of a high income accumulates wealth far faster than one that cannot save at all (1 mark).\n• Inheritance: wealth transfers between generations, so inequality persists across generations in a way income inequality does not. Income is at least partly re-earned each generation (1 mark).\n• Asset price effects and the life cycle: rising house and equity prices raise the wealth of existing owners without any effort or saving, and older households have simply had longer to accumulate, so part of the measured gap is a life-cycle effect rather than permanent inequality (1 mark).\n\n4 marks: comparison quantified AND at least two reasons explained. Do not credit an answer that only defines income and wealth.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question; the Gini figures follow the official 2024/25 pattern\n  Gini coefficient, before housing costs: 33%\n  Gini coefficient, after housing costs:  37%\n  Relative low income rate, before housing costs: 17%\n  Relative low income rate, after housing costs:  22%\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that housing costs worsen measured poverty and inequality.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Manipulate: the Gini is 4 percentage points higher after housing costs, a relative increase of 12.1%, and the relative low income rate is 5 percentage points higher, a relative increase of 29.4% (1–2 marks).\n• Explain the mechanism: housing costs absorb a much larger PROPORTION of a low income than of a high one, so deducting them compresses low incomes more than high ones and pushes more households below 60% of the median. Housing expenditure is therefore regressive in its effect on disposable income.\n• Add the ownership point: higher-income households are more likely to own outright, so their housing costs are low or zero, while lower-income households pay rent — which also means rising rents transfer income from poorer tenants to wealthier landlords (1 mark).\n• Explain why both measures are published: the AHC measure better reflects the resources a household actually has available for everything else, while the BHC measure is better for international comparison and for households whose housing costs reflect a choice of quality (1 mark).\n\n4 marks: both differences quantified AND the mechanism explained. 3 marks: quantified with a partial explanation. 1–2 marks: figures repeated.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nRelative low income rate by household type, Brackenshire 2026 (after housing costs)\n  Households where no adult works:        58%\n  Households with one adult in part-time work: 34%\n  Households where all adults work full time:  9%\n  Pensioner households:                   17%\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that worklessness is the strongest single predictor of poverty, and explain one limitation of drawing that conclusion.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Manipulate: workless households are 6.44 times more likely to be in relative low income than households where all adults work full time, a gap of 49 percentage points — much the largest gap in the table (1 mark).\n• Explain the mechanism: earnings are the dominant component of original income, so a household with no earner depends almost entirely on transfers, which are deliberately set below typical earnings to preserve work incentives — the unemployment trap (1 mark).\n• Limitation 1: the 9% figure shows that work is not a guarantee against poverty. In-work poverty is now a large share of total poverty because low hourly pay, insufficient hours and high housing costs can leave a full-time household below the threshold even at the £12.71 National Living Wage (1 mark).\n• Limitation 2 — causation: worklessness and poverty may share a common cause, such as disability, ill health or caring responsibilities, so the table cannot show that moving a household into work would lift it out of poverty by the full 49 points (1 mark).\n\n4 marks: data manipulated AND a genuine limitation explained. Maximum 2 marks where the limitation is missing or is simply "the data might be wrong".` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nGini coefficient for household disposable income, selected economies, 2026\n  Country W: 0.25\n  Country X: 0.33\n  Country Y: 0.41\n  Country Z: 0.48\n  Tax and social spending as a share of GDP:  W = 47% ; X = 38% ; Y = 32% ; Z = 26%\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show a relationship between the size of the state and income inequality, and explain one reason why the relationship may not be causal.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Describe and quantify the relationship: the Gini rises monotonically as tax and social spending as a share of GDP falls — a 21 percentage point difference in state size between W and Z accompanies a Gini difference of 0.23, or 92% higher inequality in Z (1–2 marks).\n• Explain the mechanism: progressive direct taxation and cash transfers move the distribution of final income closer to the line of perfect equality than the distribution of original income, so a larger tax-and-transfer system mechanically compresses the distribution (1 mark).\n• Non-causal explanation 1 — reverse causation: countries with more equal ORIGINAL income distributions, perhaps because of centralised wage bargaining or more equal education, may find it politically easier to sustain high social spending. The causation could run from equality to state size (1 mark).\n• Non-causal explanation 2 — omitted variables: differences in labour market institutions, union density, educational attainment, demography and the level of development affect both variables; and the Gini is a summary statistic that conceals differences in where in the distribution the inequality sits (1 mark).\n\n4 marks: relationship quantified AND a genuine non-causal explanation developed.` },

      /* ─────────── Section A part (c): 9-mark diagram/analysis questions ─────────── */

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 6–7) states: "The Gini coefficient for household disposable income was 33% before housing costs in 2024/25." With the help of a diagram, explain how the Gini coefficient is derived from a Lorenz curve.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: cumulative percentage of households ranked from poorest to richest on the horizontal axis, cumulative percentage of total income on the vertical axis, both running 0 to 100. Draw the 45-degree line of perfect equality, then the Lorenz curve bowing below and to the right of it.\n• Interpretation of the curve: a point on the curve reads "the poorest X% of households receive Y% of total income". Perfect equality would mean the poorest 20% receive 20% of income, which is the 45-degree line; the further the curve sags below it, the more unequal the distribution.\n• Derivation: label the area between the line of equality and the Lorenz curve as A, and the area beneath the Lorenz curve as B. The Gini coefficient = A / (A + B). Since A + B is the whole triangle beneath the 45-degree line, which has area 0.5, the Gini is also 2A.\n• Interpretation of values: 0 means perfect equality (the curve lies on the 45-degree line, so A = 0); 1 means one household receives all income. The UK value of 0.33 before housing costs, and 0.37 after, places it towards the more unequal end of comparable European economies.\n• Limitations to note briefly: two very different distributions can produce the same Gini because the curves may cross; the Gini is more sensitive to changes around the middle of the distribution than at the tails; and it captures income, not wealth, which is far more unequally held.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) accurate labelled Lorenz diagram with both axes cumulative, areas A and B identified, the formula correctly derived and interpreted, with application to the extract. L2 (4–6) diagram broadly correct but incompletely labelled or the formula asserted. L1 (1–3) description of inequality with a missing or inaccurate diagram.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 12–13) states: "The Chancellor announced a more progressive income tax structure." With the help of a Lorenz curve diagram, analyse the effect of a more progressive income tax system on the distribution of income.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Define progressivity precisely: a tax is progressive when the average rate of tax rises as income rises. The UK income tax structure is progressive through the £12,570 personal allowance and the 20%, 40% and 45% bands, with the personal allowance itself tapered away above £100,000.\n• Diagram: draw the Lorenz curve for original income, then a second curve for post-tax income lying closer to the line of perfect equality. Label the reduction in area A, and state that the Gini coefficient falls.\n• Chain of reasoning: raising the higher and additional rates, or raising the personal allowance, cuts the post-tax share of top earners relative to the bottom, so at every point the cumulative income share of the poorer part of the distribution rises. That is precisely what shifts the curve inwards.\n• Qualification 1 — behavioural response: higher marginal rates may reduce the incentive to work extra hours, encourage avoidance, or encourage emigration of highly mobile high earners. If the tax base shrinks, revenue and therefore redistribution may rise by less than intended (the Laffer curve argument).\n• Qualification 2 — coverage: income tax is only part of the system. Employee National Insurance is not progressive at the top because the rate falls to 2% above the upper earnings limit, indirect taxes such as VAT at 20% are regressive as a share of income, and the Lorenz curve for WEALTH is barely affected by an income tax at all.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) accurate Lorenz diagram with both curves and area A identified, a developed chain of reasoning, and at least one qualification. L2 (4–6) diagram present but only partly used, or reasoning asserted. L1 (1–3) definition of progressive tax with no diagrammatic analysis.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 20–21) states: "The richest tenth of households hold well over half of all household wealth." Analyse three reasons why wealth is more unequally distributed than income in the UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three, each developed:\n• Stocks versus flows: wealth is the accumulated stock of past saving, so differences in the ability to save compound year after year. A household with no surplus income accumulates nothing however long it works, while a high-income household saving a constant share sees its wealth grow geometrically. Small annual differences in saving therefore produce very large differences in stocks.\n• Inheritance: wealth is transferred at death, so advantage passes across generations. Inheritance tax is charged at 40% above the £325,000 nil-rate band, with a residence nil-rate band of up to £175,000 available where a home passes to direct descendants, but reliefs and lifetime gifting mean effective rates on the largest estates are far below 40%.\n• Asset price effects: house prices and equity prices have risen faster than earnings over recent decades, so those who already owned assets gained without saving anything extra. Quantitative easing after 2009 is frequently argued to have amplified this by raising asset prices. Those without assets received none of this gain.\n• Pension wealth and the life cycle: private pension wealth is the largest component of household wealth and accumulates only for those in schemes with employer contributions, which correlates strongly with earnings. Part of the measured gap is also a life-cycle effect — a 60-year-old has had 40 years to accumulate and a 25-year-old has not — so age structure inflates the measured Gini for wealth.\n• Access to returns: larger portfolios can access higher-return, less liquid assets and spread fixed costs of advice, so the RATE of return rises with wealth, widening the gap further.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) three reasons, each developed into a genuine chain of reasoning rather than stated, with accurate institutional detail. L2 (4–6) three reasons listed with partial development, or two developed. L1 (1–3) assertion. Only three are required — credit the best three.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 3–4) states: "A Universal Credit claimant who works an extra hour can lose nearly 70p of every extra pound earned." With the help of a diagram, explain how the poverty trap discourages low-income households from increasing their earnings.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Define the poverty trap: an in-work household increasing its gross earnings loses means-tested support at the same time as paying tax and National Insurance, so its NET income rises by much less than its gross income. This is distinct from the unemployment trap, which concerns the gap between out-of-work benefits and in-work income at the point of taking a job.\n• Quantify: on the 2026 rates, an extra £100 of gross pay above the work allowance loses £20 in income tax, £8 in employee National Insurance and then 55% of the remaining £72, or £39.60, to the Universal Credit taper — a total deduction of £67.60 and an effective marginal rate of 67.6%, higher than the 45% additional rate of income tax.\n• Diagram: net (post-tax, post-benefit) income on the vertical axis against gross earnings on the horizontal. Draw the 45-degree line for the no-tax, no-benefit case, then the actual schedule: it starts above the origin because of out-of-work support, then rises with a much shallower gradient over the taper range. The shallow section IS the trap, and its gradient is 1 minus the effective marginal deduction rate.\n• Chain of reasoning on incentives: the substitution effect of a low net return to an extra hour discourages extra work; the household may rationally choose to cap its hours where the taper bites. Over time this suppresses progression, experience and human capital accumulation, so the effect is larger than the static loss.\n• Evaluation: the single UC taper at 55% is a substantial improvement on the overlapping withdrawals of the legacy system, which produced effective rates above 90% for some households. Cutting the taper further improves incentives but costs revenue and extends means-tested support further up the income distribution — the classic trade-off between targeting and incentives.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) the trap defined and distinguished from the unemployment trap, an accurate budget-constraint diagram used, and the arithmetic applied. L2 (4–6) the idea explained with a partly used diagram. L1 (1–3) description of benefits with no analysis.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 15–16) states: "The National Living Wage rose to £12.71 an hour from April 2026." With the help of a diagram, analyse the likely effect of this increase on the distribution of income.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: a competitive labour market with the National Living Wage set above equilibrium, showing the gain to those who keep their jobs and the excess supply Qs – Qd. An alternative and equally creditable diagram is a Lorenz curve shifting inwards at the lower end of the distribution.\n• First-round effect on the distribution: the wage floor raises the earnings of workers at the bottom of the distribution and leaves those at the top unchanged, so the earnings distribution is compressed from below and measured earnings inequality falls. At a bite above 70% of median pay, this now affects a substantial share of employees.\n• Offsetting effect through employment: if the floor is above the competitive wage, some workers lose hours or jobs entirely, and they are precisely the workers at the bottom. A household that loses all earnings moves down the distribution, so the net effect on income inequality is smaller than the effect on the wages of those in work.\n• The household-level qualification, which is the key discriminator: the distribution of WAGES is not the distribution of household INCOME. Many minimum wage earners are second earners or students in middle-income or high-income households, so the gain is spread far more widely than across poor households alone.\n• Interaction with the tax and benefit system: for a household on Universal Credit, a rise in gross pay is taxed and then tapered at 55%, so roughly a third of the increase reaches the household. Much of the gross cost of the wage rise therefore accrues to the Exchequer rather than to the low-income household, which blunts its effect on the income distribution.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) accurate labelled diagram, both the compression and the offsetting effects analysed, and the wage/household income distinction made explicitly. L2 (4–6) compression explained with a partly used diagram and limited qualification. L1 (1–3) assertion that the NLW reduces inequality.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 24–25) states: "Measured relative poverty fell during the last recession." With the help of a diagram, explain why relative poverty can fall when the economy contracts.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The mechanism: the relative low income threshold is 60% of the CONTEMPORARY median equivalised household income. In a recession, median income falls, so the threshold itself falls. A household whose income is unchanged — a pensioner on an index-linked state pension, for instance — can therefore move from below the line to above it without gaining a penny.\n• Diagram: draw a frequency distribution of household incomes with a vertical line at 60% of the median. Show the whole distribution shifting left in the recession and the threshold line shifting left with it — the shaded area below the threshold can shrink even though everybody is poorer.\n• Reinforcing chain: recessions hit the middle of the distribution through job losses and reduced hours harder than the bottom, where income is largely benefit-based and indexed, so the distribution compresses from above and the measured Gini can fall.\n• Contrast with absolute poverty: the absolute low income measure holds the threshold constant in real terms (at 60% of a fixed base-year median, uprated by inflation), so it does NOT fall automatically in a recession and gives a more honest picture of living standards in a downturn. This is exactly why the UK publishes both.\n• Evaluation: the episode shows that relative measures capture inequality rather than deprivation. A complete assessment needs relative income, absolute income, material deprivation and persistent poverty measures together.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) the moving-threshold mechanism explained precisely, supported by an accurate diagram, with the absolute/relative contrast drawn. L2 (4–6) the mechanism asserted or the diagram incomplete. L1 (1–3) confusion between absolute and relative poverty.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 9–10) states: "In a free market, income is distributed according to what people own and what it earns." Analyse how the free market determines the distribution of income.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The core mechanism: in a market economy, household income is the sum of the returns to the factors of production the household owns — wages for labour, rent for land, interest for capital and profit for enterprise. Each factor price is set by the interaction of demand and supply in that factor market, with demand determined by marginal revenue product.\n• Chain of reasoning on labour income: differences in human capital raise MPP and therefore MRP, shifting the demand for that worker\'s labour right; restricted supply to skilled occupations raises the equilibrium wage further. The distribution of earnings therefore reflects the distribution of skills and of scarcity.\n• Chain of reasoning on capital income: the distribution of asset ownership is far more unequal than the distribution of skills, so once returns to capital are included the income distribution is substantially more unequal than the wage distribution alone. Where the return on capital exceeds the growth rate of the economy, capital income grows faster than average income and the distribution widens further.\n• The efficiency argument: unequal rewards are the signalling and incentive mechanism of a market economy. High returns attract labour and capital into activities consumers value most, and the prospect of profit motivates risk-taking and innovation. Removing the differential removes the signal.\n• The limits: market outcomes take the initial distribution of endowments as given and reproduce it, so inherited wealth and unequal access to education perpetuate the pattern. The market distributes according to marginal productivity and ownership, which has no necessary connection to need or to any concept of fairness — which is the entire case for government redistribution.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) factor markets and MRP used accurately, at least two developed chains, and the efficiency/equity tension drawn out. L2 (4–6) partial explanation. L1 (1–3) assertion that markets reward hard work.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 27–28) states: "Some economists argue that high inequality is itself a brake on growth." Analyse how a high level of income inequality can reduce an economy\'s rate of economic growth.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Aggregate demand channel: lower-income households have a higher marginal propensity to consume, so a transfer of income towards the top of the distribution reduces aggregate consumption for a given level of national income. AD grows more slowly and the multiplier effect of any injection is smaller. Diagram reference: a smaller rightward shift in AD.\n• Human capital channel: where access to education, training and healthcare depends on income, talented individuals from poor households do not reach their productive potential. The economy under-invests in its most important asset, so LRAS shifts right more slowly than it otherwise would. This is the most widely accepted mechanism.\n• Social and institutional channel: high inequality is associated with lower social mobility, higher crime, worse health outcomes and weaker trust, all of which raise the cost of doing business and divert public spending from investment to remediation.\n• Fiscal channel: greater inequality alongside persistent poverty raises demand for welfare spending while narrowing the effective tax base, worsening the fiscal position and potentially crowding out public investment.\n• Counter-arguments required for high marks: some inequality is necessary for incentives, since differential rewards direct labour and capital to their most valued uses and reward innovation and risk. High earners have a higher marginal propensity to save, which finances investment. And the relationship is contested empirically — fast-growing economies have often experienced widening inequality during the growth phase.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) at least two channels developed into full chains of reasoning, with the counter-argument acknowledged. L2 (4–6) channels identified with partial development. L1 (1–3) assertion that inequality is bad for growth.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 18–19) states: "Benefits in kind are worth more to poor households than any cash transfer." With the help of a diagram, explain how state provision of education and healthcare affects the distribution of final income.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Define the income chain: original income (from wages, self-employment, investment and private pensions) plus cash benefits = gross income; minus direct taxes = disposable income; minus indirect taxes = post-tax income; plus benefits in kind = final income. Benefits in kind are the last and, for the poorest households, one of the largest steps.\n• Diagram: a Lorenz curve for original income and a second curve for final income much closer to the line of perfect equality, with the reduction in area A labelled and the fall in the Gini stated.\n• Why the effect is progressive: state education and NHS treatment are provided free at the point of use and are allocated by need and family size rather than by income. Because the value is roughly similar across households but incomes differ enormously, the transfer is worth a far larger PERCENTAGE of a low income.\n• Long-run chain of reasoning: education is an investment in human capital, so free provision raises the future MRP of children from low-income households, shifting their future labour demand curves right. The effect on the distribution therefore compounds over a generation and raises social mobility, whereas a cash transfer affects only the current year.\n• Evaluation for top marks: benefits in kind are valued at cost of provision, not at what the household would willingly pay, which may overstate the redistribution. Access is also not equal in practice — better-informed, better-off households often use universal services more effectively, and catchment-area effects capitalise good schools into house prices, which excludes poorer families.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) the original-to-final income chain explained, an accurate Lorenz diagram used, and the long-run human capital point made with evaluation. L2 (4–6) the idea explained with a partly used diagram. L1 (1–3) description of the NHS and schools.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 30–31) states: "Median hourly pay for full-time employees was 6.9% lower for women than for men in April 2025." Analyse how differences in the labour market contribute to inequality in the distribution of household income.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Earnings are the dominant component of original income, so the shape of the earnings distribution largely determines the shape of the pre-redistribution income distribution. Wage differentials arising from differences in MRP, restricted supply to skilled occupations and compensating differentials therefore translate directly into income inequality.\n• Chain 1 — skill-biased technological change: technology has raised the MRP of high-skilled workers while substituting for routine middle-skill work, shifting labour demand towards the top and hollowing out the middle. This widens earnings dispersion without anyone\'s pay being cut.\n• Chain 2 — the household dimension, which is what the question turns on: households are formed of individuals, and assortative mating means high earners increasingly partner with other high earners. Two graduate salaries in one household and none in another widens household income inequality faster than individual earnings inequality. Worklessness concentrates in particular households for the same reason.\n• Chain 3 — hours and participation: the all-employee gender pay gap of 12.8% in April 2025 was nearly twice the full-time gap of 6.9% because part-time work is lower paid per hour and disproportionately done by women. Household income depends on hourly pay multiplied by hours, so participation and hours differences amplify hourly differences.\n• Chain 4 — institutional compression at the bottom: the National Living Wage at £12.71 sets a floor that compresses the bottom of the earnings distribution, so in recent years earnings inequality has narrowed at the bottom while widening at the top — the two ends of the distribution moving in opposite directions.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) at least two developed chains, with the individual-earnings/household-income distinction handled explicitly and dated evidence used. L2 (4–6) causes of wage differentials listed and linked loosely to income inequality. L1 (1–3) description.` },

      /* ─────────── Paper 3 investigation: 10-mark data questions ─────────── */

      { q: `Extract D (Table 2) — written for this practice question; the 2024/25 Gini figures follow the official pattern\nUK income distribution indicators\n  Gini coefficient, disposable income (BHC):      2010/11 = 34% ; 2024/25 = 33%\n  Share of income held by the top 1%:             2010/11 = 7.6% ; 2024/25 = 8.9%\n  Ratio of 90th to 10th percentile income:        2010/11 = 4.1 ; 2024/25 = 4.0\n  Share of total household wealth held by top 10%: 2010/11 = 53% ; 2024/25 = 57%\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data in Extract D (Table 2) suggest that inequality in the UK has increased since 2010/11?`, marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Evidence AGAINST an increase: the Gini for disposable income fell by 1 percentage point and the 90:10 ratio fell from 4.1 to 4.0. Both are the headline summary measures, and both point, very slightly, the other way.\n• Evidence FOR an increase: the top 1% share rose from 7.6% to 8.9%, a relative increase of 17.1%, and the wealth share of the top decile rose 4 percentage points. So the two measures that capture the very top of the distribution both rose.\n• Reconciling the two, which is the key analytical move: the Gini is relatively insensitive to changes in the extreme tails and most sensitive around the middle of the distribution. A distribution can therefore become more concentrated at the very top while the Gini is flat or falling — the 90:10 ratio has the same blind spot, since it ignores everything above the 90th percentile.\n• Income versus wealth: three of the four rows are income measures and one is wealth. Wealth inequality is much higher in level and is rising on this evidence, so a conclusion about "inequality" that rests only on the income Gini is incomplete.\n• What the data cannot tell us: nothing about before versus after housing costs, about the composition of top incomes, about persistence (are the same households at the bottom throughout?), or about regional differences. The table also gives only two points in time and cannot show the path between them.\n• Judgement on extent: on the summary income measures, inequality is essentially unchanged; on top-end concentration and on wealth, it has clearly increased. A defensible conclusion is that the honest answer depends on which measure is chosen, and that the data suggest a modest increase concentrated at the very top rather than a general widening.\n\nLevels: L3 (8–10) precise use of the figures on BOTH sides, explicit recognition that the measures disagree and why, and a supported judgement on extent. L2 (4–7) some accurate data use and one limitation. L1 (1–3) the data restated.` },

      { q: `Extract D (Table 2) — written for this practice question\nHousehold income by stage of the tax and benefit system, Brackenshire (annual averages)\n  Bottom quintile: original £6 200 ; gross £16 800 ; disposable £16 100 ; post-tax £13 400 ; final £22 400\n  Top quintile:    original £128 000 ; gross £130 200 ; disposable £94 500 ; post-tax £87 300 ; final £89 600\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data in Extract D (Table 2) suggest that indirect taxes undo the redistribution achieved by direct taxes and benefits?`, marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Identify the indirect tax stage precisely: it is the step from disposable to post-tax income. The bottom quintile loses £2 700, which is 16.77% of its disposable income; the top quintile loses £7 200, which is 7.62% of its disposable income (2 marks available for accurate manipulation).\n• The regressive finding: the bottom quintile pays more than twice as large a PROPORTION of its disposable income in indirect taxes, even though the top quintile pays 2.67 times as much in cash. This is the standard definition of a regressive tax — the average rate falls as income rises. VAT at 20%, plus duties on fuel, alcohol and tobacco, do the work.\n• Why: lower-income households spend a higher proportion of income (a higher average propensity to consume) and cannot save, so a larger share of income passes through a consumption tax. Duties on tobacco in particular fall on a good consumed disproportionately by lower-income households and with inelastic demand.\n• Evidence AGAINST the "undone" claim: the redistribution is far from undone. Original income ratio top:bottom is 20.65:1; final income ratio is 4.00:1. Even after indirect taxes, the bottom quintile\'s final income is 3.61 times its original income, while the top quintile\'s is 30.0% below its original income. Indirect taxes offset PART of the redistribution, not all of it.\n• Measurement caveats: indirect tax incidence measured against annual income overstates regressivity, because low annual income can be temporary — students, the recently unemployed and pensioners drawing down savings. Measured against lifetime income or expenditure, VAT looks much less regressive. Zero-rating of food, children\'s clothing and domestic fuel at 5% also blunts it.\n• Judgement on extent: the data clearly support the claim that indirect taxes are regressive and partially offset redistribution, but do NOT support the claim that they undo it. Note what further data would help: expenditure data and a lifetime rather than annual measure.\n\nLevels: L3 (8–10) the correct stage identified, proportions rather than cash amounts calculated, both sides of the claim tested, and a supported judgement on extent. L2 (4–7) some correct manipulation and a partial conclusion. L1 (1–3) cash amounts quoted with no proportional analysis.` },

      /* ─────────── Section B part (a): 15-mark explain questions ─────────── */

      { q: 'Explain the main causes of inequality in the distribution of income in the UK.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Wage differentials: pay is determined by marginal revenue product and by supply conditions occupation by occupation, so differences in human capital, in the scarcity of skills and in compensating differentials produce a wide earnings distribution. Skill-biased technological change has widened it further by raising demand for high-skilled labour while automating routine middle-skill work.\n• Unequal ownership of wealth: income from capital — rent, dividends, interest — accrues to the households that already hold assets, and wealth is far more unequally distributed than earnings. Adding capital income to earnings therefore widens the distribution substantially, and this component is growing as asset values rise relative to wages.\n• Differences in employment status and hours: worklessness, involuntary part-time work and insecure hours concentrate in particular households. Because households pool income, and because assortative mating means high earners partner with high earners, household income inequality exceeds individual earnings inequality.\n• Education and social mobility: unequal access to good schools, higher education and professional networks means human capital differences persist across generations, so the causes of today\'s inequality lie partly in the previous generation\'s distribution.\n• Demography and the life cycle: part of measured inequality reflects age — students and pensioners have low current incomes, mid-career workers high ones. This inflates the annual Gini relative to a lifetime measure.\n• Geography: large regional differences in productivity, wages and housing costs, reinforced by geographical immobility of labour, prevent the arbitrage that would otherwise narrow gaps.\n• The tax and benefit system as an offset: direct taxes and cash and in-kind benefits substantially narrow the distribution — in the UK, final income is far more equal than original income — but indirect taxes such as VAT at 20% are regressive and offset part of it.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) at least four causes explained with developed chains of reasoning and accurate UK application. L2 (6–10) causes identified with partial explanation. L1 (1–5) a list.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main causes of inequality in the distribution of wealth in the UK and the main difficulties in taxing wealth.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — causes:\n• Compounding of saved income: wealth is a stock accumulated from the flow of saved income, so income inequality compounds into much larger wealth inequality over a working life. Households with no surplus income accumulate nothing at all.\n• Inheritance and inter-generational transfer: wealth passes at death, so advantage persists across generations. Inheritance tax at 40% above the £325,000 nil-rate band (plus a residence nil-rate band of up to £175,000) is intended to check this, but lifetime gifting rules and reliefs mean effective rates on the largest estates are far lower.\n• Asset price inflation: house and equity price growth outpacing earnings growth enriches existing owners without any additional saving, and excludes non-owners from the gain entirely.\n• Pension wealth: the largest single component of household wealth, and accessible mainly through employment in schemes with employer contributions, which correlates with earnings.\n• Life cycle and age structure: older households have had longer to accumulate, so part of measured wealth inequality is not permanent inequality at all.\n\nDifficulties in taxing wealth:\n• Valuation: unlisted businesses, art, land and private pensions have no continuously observable market price, so an annual net wealth tax requires costly and contestable annual valuations.\n• Liquidity: wealth is not income. A household may hold a valuable asset with no cash flow — a farm, a family firm, a home owned by a retired person — so a tax on the stock may force a forced sale.\n• Mobility and avoidance: financial wealth and wealthy individuals are internationally mobile, so a national wealth tax risks capital flight and elaborate avoidance through trusts and offshore structures. Several European countries have abolished net wealth taxes for exactly this reason.\n• Administrative cost and yield: the cost of assessment can absorb a large share of the revenue, and the yield estimates are highly sensitive to behavioural response assumptions.\n• Alternatives that avoid some of these problems: reforming council tax bands, equalising capital gains tax with income tax rates, and tightening inheritance tax reliefs all tax wealth at the point of transaction or transfer, where a market price exists.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) causes AND difficulties both explained with developed chains and accurate institutional detail. L2 (6–10) one side developed. L1 (1–5) description.` },

      { q: 'Explain how the UK tax and benefit system redistributes income, and explain the difference between progressive, proportional and regressive taxes.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Definitions with the marginal/average distinction: a tax is progressive if the AVERAGE rate of tax rises as income rises; proportional if it stays constant; regressive if it falls. The marginal rate is the rate on the next pound; it is the average rate that determines progressivity.\n• Progressive example: UK income tax, through the £12,570 personal allowance and the 20%, 40% and 45% bands, with the personal allowance tapered away between £100,000 and £125,140 producing an effective marginal rate of 60% in that band. Because the first slice of income is untaxed, income tax is progressive even before the higher bands apply.\n• Regressive example: VAT at 20%, plus duties on fuel, alcohol and tobacco. The rate is the same for everyone, but lower-income households spend a higher proportion of income, so indirect taxes take a larger share of a low income. Zero-rating food and children\'s clothing and charging domestic fuel at 5% blunts but does not remove this.\n• The redistributive chain: original income + cash benefits = gross income; minus direct taxes = disposable income; minus indirect taxes = post-tax income; plus benefits in kind = final income. Final income is far more equally distributed than original income, and both ends of the chain — cash benefits at the bottom, and benefits in kind — do more of the work than direct taxation alone.\n• Cash benefits: Universal Credit, tapered at 55% of net earnings above a work allowance, replaced overlapping legacy benefits that could produce effective deduction rates above 90%. The state pension is the largest single item of benefit spending and is a major reason pensioner poverty is below child poverty.\n• Benefits in kind: NHS treatment and state education are allocated by need rather than income, so they are worth a far larger percentage of a low income.\n• The equity–efficiency trade-off: sharper means-testing targets support better but raises effective marginal deduction rates, worsening the poverty trap; universal provision preserves incentives but costs more and reaches households that do not need it.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) definitions precise, the redistributive chain explained with accurate UK detail, the trade-off drawn out. L2 (6–10) definitions correct, redistribution described. L1 (1–5) definitions only.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main causes of poverty in the UK.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Worklessness: earnings are the dominant component of original income, so a household with no earner depends on transfers set deliberately below typical earnings. Unemployment, disability, ill health and caring responsibilities are the main routes into worklessness, and long spells cause hysteresis — skills, confidence and employability erode, making the poverty persistent rather than temporary.\n• Low pay and insufficient hours: in-work poverty is now a large share of total poverty. A household can have a full-time worker at the £12.71 National Living Wage and still fall below 60% of median income if hours are short, there is only one earner, or there are several dependants. Equivalisation matters here — the threshold rises with household size.\n• Housing costs: the relative low income rate is substantially higher after housing costs than before, because rent absorbs a far larger share of a low income. Rising rents relative to earnings have pushed households below the line without any change in their earnings.\n• The poverty and unemployment traps: high effective marginal deduction rates — 67.6% for a basic-rate Universal Credit claimant on the 2026 rates — reduce the net return to extra hours, so households can become stuck at low earnings even when more work is available.\n• Low human capital and educational inequality: poor basic skills limit the wage a worker can command in any labour market and transmit disadvantage across generations, which is why poverty persists in particular families and places.\n• Geographical concentration: regional differences in productivity, employment and vacancies, combined with geographical immobility, concentrate poverty in particular areas and create a local multiplier working in reverse.\n• Demographic factors: larger families, lone parenthood and single-earner households face higher equivalised needs against the same income; disability raises the cost of achieving a given standard of living, which headline income measures do not capture.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) at least four causes explained with developed chains of reasoning, accurate use of dated UK data, and the distinction between poverty and inequality maintained. L2 (6–10) causes identified, explanation partial. L1 (1–5) a list.` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between absolute and relative poverty, and explain the main problems involved in measuring poverty.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — the distinction:\n• Absolute poverty is an inability to afford the minimum necessities for survival — food, shelter, clean water, basic clothing. In UK official statistics the "absolute low income" measure fixes the threshold at 60% of the median in a base year and uprates it by inflation, so it holds real living standards constant and can only fall if real incomes at the bottom rise.\n• Relative poverty is having an income below a fixed proportion of the contemporary median — in the UK, 60% of median equivalised household income. It measures the ability to participate in the normal life of the society, and so is really a measure of inequality in the lower half of the distribution.\n• The consequence of the difference: relative poverty can fall in a recession simply because the median, and therefore the threshold, falls. It can also fail to fall during strong growth if the median rises as fast as low incomes. Absolute poverty does not have these properties, which is why both are published.\n\nProblems of measurement:\n• The choice of threshold is arbitrary — 60% of the median is a convention, not a finding, and a small change to 50% or 70% changes the headcount substantially.\n• Equivalisation: incomes must be adjusted for household size and composition using a scale, and the choice of scale changes who counts as poor.\n• Before or after housing costs: the two give materially different rates and Ginis, and neither is obviously correct.\n• The headcount says nothing about DEPTH — a household £1 below the line counts the same as one £200 below it — which is why the poverty gap is also needed.\n• Income is not consumption: households can draw on savings, borrow, or receive help in kind, so low measured income need not mean low living standards. Students and the self-employed are particular problem cases.\n• Persistence: a snapshot cannot distinguish a temporary dip from entrenched poverty, so persistent poverty measures matter for policy.\n• Non-income deprivation: access to healthcare, education, housing quality and debt are not captured by any income line, which is why material deprivation indicators and multidimensional indices are used alongside.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) the distinction explained precisely with its consequences, and at least four measurement problems explained rather than listed. L2 (6–10) the distinction correct, problems listed. L1 (1–5) definitions only.` },

      { q: 'Explain the possible economic consequences for an economy of a high and rising level of income inequality.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — negative consequences:\n• Aggregate demand: lower-income households have a higher marginal propensity to consume, so a shift of income towards the top reduces total consumption for a given national income and weakens the multiplier. AD grows more slowly than it otherwise would.\n• Human capital and long-run supply: where access to education and healthcare depends on income, talent is wasted and the economy under-invests in its most valuable asset. LRAS shifts right more slowly, and social mobility falls, so the loss compounds across generations.\n• Fiscal consequences: higher demand for welfare spending alongside a narrower effective tax base worsens the public finances, potentially crowding out public investment.\n• Social consequences with economic costs: worse health outcomes, higher crime and lower social trust raise the cost of doing business and divert public spending to remediation rather than investment.\n• Financial stability: rising inequality alongside stagnant middle incomes can drive household borrowing to sustain consumption, raising leverage and the severity of any subsequent downturn.\n\nPositive or offsetting consequences — required for a balanced answer even on an "explain" question:\n• Incentives and allocation: differential rewards are the market\'s signalling mechanism. High returns attract labour and capital into the activities consumers value most, and the prospect of profit rewards risk-taking, entrepreneurship and innovation.\n• Saving and investment: higher-income households have a higher marginal propensity to save, which supplies loanable funds for investment.\n• Trickle-down is the contested version of this argument and should be presented as contested — the empirical evidence for it is weak, which is itself worth stating.\n• The Kuznets argument: inequality may rise during industrialisation and fall later, so a rising Gini during a growth phase is not necessarily evidence of a failing economy.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) at least four consequences explained with developed chains of reasoning, using AD/AS or PPF analysis where relevant, with offsetting effects acknowledged. L2 (6–10) consequences identified with partial explanation. L1 (1–5) assertion that inequality is harmful.` },

      /* ─────────── Section A (d) / Section B (b): 25-mark evaluation ─────────── */

      { q: 'Extract B (lines 7–8) states: "The Gini coefficient for UK household disposable income was 33% before housing costs and 37% after housing costs in 2024/25." Using the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that the government should do more to reduce income inequality in the UK.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The case for more action — market failure and efficiency: the market distributes income according to factor ownership and marginal productivity, which has no connection to need. Where unequal access to education wastes talent, redistribution is efficiency-improving as well as equitable, because it raises human capital and shifts LRAS right.\n• The case for more action — demand and stability: the higher marginal propensity to consume of poorer households means redistribution raises aggregate consumption and the multiplier, and reduces the household borrowing that inequality can drive.\n• The case for more action — the data: the after-housing-costs Gini of 37% is 4 points above the before-housing-costs figure, showing that the housing market is actively widening the distribution, and that a policy response targeting housing supply and rents would reduce inequality without touching tax rates at all.\n• The case against — incentives and the Laffer argument: higher marginal rates reduce the return to effort, risk and skill acquisition. Highly mobile top earners and mobile capital can relocate, so the tax base shrinks and the revenue gain is smaller than static calculations suggest. Sharper means-testing raises effective marginal deduction rates for the low-paid — already 67.6% for a basic-rate Universal Credit claimant on 2026 rates — worsening the poverty trap.\n• The case against — government failure: information failure means transfers reach households that do not need them and miss those that do; administrative costs absorb revenue; and policy responds to measured statistics rather than to deprivation, producing cosmetic improvements.\n• The measurement objection: the annual Gini overstates lifetime inequality because it counts students and pensioners as poor; and inequality is not the same as poverty, so a fall in the Gini does not necessarily improve anyone\'s living standards.\n• Alternative routes worth raising: predistribution through education, skills and competition policy changes the distribution of ORIGINAL income, avoiding the incentive costs of redistribution; housing supply reform addresses the AHC gap directly; and productivity growth raises real incomes across the distribution.\n• Judgement: the strongest answers distinguish between reducing inequality and reducing poverty, note that the UK already does a great deal — final income is far more equal than original income — and argue that the highest-return action is on the causes of unequal original income rather than on further transfer spending. Any judgement is creditable if supported.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) sustained balanced argument, precise use of the extract and dated evidence, evaluation integrated throughout, conclusion following from the analysis. L4 (16–20) well-developed and mostly balanced. L3 (11–15) some analysis and evaluation, balance uneven. L2 (6–10) one-sided description. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that an annual tax on net wealth would be the most effective way of reducing inequality in the UK.', marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The case for: wealth is far more unequally distributed than income — the richest tenth of households hold well over half of all household wealth against around a quarter of income — so a given revenue raised from wealth reduces inequality more than the same revenue raised from income. Wealth also generates income, so taxing the stock addresses the source of future income inequality rather than the symptom.\n• Efficiency argument for: a tax on the STOCK of wealth does not distort the decision to work or to take risk in the way an income tax does; it falls on holding, not on earning. Taxing unearned gains from asset price inflation and inheritance has less deadweight loss than taxing effort.\n• Practical objection 1 — valuation: unlisted businesses, land, art and private pensions have no continuously observed market price, so an annual tax requires costly, contestable annual valuations. Administration costs can absorb a large share of the yield.\n• Practical objection 2 — liquidity: wealth is not cash flow. A farmer, a family business owner or an asset-rich retired homeowner may have a large net wealth figure and little income, so an annual charge may force asset sales — politically explosive and economically disruptive.\n• Practical objection 3 — mobility and avoidance: financial wealth and wealthy individuals are internationally mobile. Most European countries that introduced net wealth taxes have since abolished them, largely because of avoidance, capital flight and disappointing yields. This comparative evidence is the strongest single argument against.\n• Alternatives that reach wealth where a price exists: aligning capital gains tax rates with income tax rates, reforming inheritance tax reliefs (the nil-rate band has been £325,000 since 2009, so fiscal drag has already widened its reach), revaluing council tax bands still based on 1991 values, and taxing land. Each avoids the valuation and liquidity problems at the cost of a narrower base.\n• Wider evaluation: reducing wealth inequality is not the same as reducing poverty. If the objective is to raise the living standards of the poorest, transfers, housing supply and skills policy may do more per pound than any wealth tax.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that the PRINCIPLE is strong but the INSTRUMENT is weak — that taxing wealth at the points of transaction and transfer, where valuation is already necessary and liquidity exists, achieves most of the distributional gain at a fraction of the administrative and behavioural cost.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) principle and practice both analysed, comparative evidence used, alternatives evaluated, discriminating judgement. L4 (16–20) well-developed and balanced. L3 (11–15) partial. L2 (6–10) one-sided. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Discuss whether means-tested benefits are the most effective way of reducing poverty in the UK.', marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The case for: means-testing concentrates a limited budget on the households with the lowest incomes, so the poverty reduction per pound of public spending is higher than for universal transfers. The same budget can therefore deliver a larger increase in the incomes of the poorest, or the same increase at lower cost to the taxpayer and with a smaller distortion elsewhere in the economy.\n• Supporting evidence: cash benefits do most of the heavy lifting in the UK redistributive chain — the gap between original and gross income for the bottom quintile is far larger than the gap between gross and disposable income for the top quintile.\n• The case against — the poverty trap: withdrawal of support as income rises produces high effective marginal deduction rates. On 2026 rates a basic-rate Universal Credit claimant faces 67.6% — higher than an additional rate taxpayer. This discourages extra hours and progression, so means-testing can entrench the low incomes it is designed to relieve.\n• The case against — take-up and stigma: means-tested benefits require claiming, which imposes information and administrative costs and carries stigma, so take-up is incomplete and is lowest among some of the households most in need. Universal benefits such as the state pension have near-perfect take-up, which is a large part of why pensioner poverty is lower than child poverty.\n• The case against — administration and error: assessing and reassessing millions of claims is costly and produces error and fraud in both directions, and the complexity itself deters claimants.\n• Alternative 1 — raising earnings: the National Living Wage at £12.71 from April 2026 costs the Exchequer nothing directly and reaches every low-paid employee, but is poorly targeted on poor HOUSEHOLDS and much of the gross gain is clawed back by tax and the 55% taper.\n• Alternative 2 — universal provision and UBI: removes the trap and the take-up problem entirely, but is extremely expensive per unit of poverty reduction and requires much higher tax rates elsewhere.\n• Alternative 3 — attacking causes: education and skills, childcare to enable work, and housing supply to reduce the after-housing-costs poverty rate all change original income rather than topping it up, but work only over years.\n• Judgement: means-testing is the most cost-effective way of raising incomes NOW, and the least effective way of ending poverty in the long run. The strongest conclusions separate the short-run relief objective from the long-run prevention objective and argue that the instruments should be matched to the objective rather than ranked against each other.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) both sides developed, alternatives evaluated against a clear criterion, dated evidence, integrated judgement. L4 (16–20) well-developed and balanced. L3 (11–15) partial. L2 (6–10) description. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Extract B (lines 16–17) states: "Raising the wage floor is the simplest anti-poverty policy available to any government." Using the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that a higher National Living Wage is the most effective way to reduce poverty in the UK.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The case for: at £12.71 from April 2026 and a bite above 70% of median pay for employees aged 21 and over, the NLW reaches a very large share of low-paid workers directly, immediately and at no direct cost to the Exchequer. In monopsonistic low-wage labour markets it can raise pay AND employment, so the standard employment objection need not apply.\n• Supporting chains: higher earnings reduce in-work poverty, strengthen the incentive to work rather than claim, and inject spending into local economies where the marginal propensity to consume is high.\n• Objection 1 — targeting: the NLW targets low-paid WORKERS, not poor HOUSEHOLDS. Many minimum wage earners are second earners or students in middle- or high-income households, while the poorest households typically contain no earner at all and receive nothing from a wage floor. This is the single strongest argument against and should be developed fully.\n• Objection 2 — clawback: for a household on Universal Credit, a rise in gross pay loses 20% to income tax, 8% to National Insurance and 55% of the remainder to the taper, so only around a third of the gross increase reaches the household. Much of the cost imposed on employers accrues to the Exchequer, not to the poor household.\n• Objection 3 — employment and hours: where the floor exceeds the competitive wage, the least productive workers lose hours or jobs, and they are the workers the policy is meant to help. The risk grows as the bite rises, and it interacts with the rise in employer National Insurance to 15% from April 2025. Diagram: minimum wage above equilibrium with excess supply.\n• Objection 4 — coverage: it does nothing for pensioners, the long-term sick, carers, the unemployed, or the self-employed — groups with among the highest poverty rates.\n• Alternatives evaluated: raising Universal Credit or cutting the taper targets poor households precisely but costs the Exchequer and risks weakening incentives; raising the personal allowance helps only those earning above it; childcare provision raises hours and earnings but works slowly; housing supply reform attacks the largest single driver of after-housing-costs poverty.\n• Judgement: the NLW is an effective anti-LOW-PAY policy and a blunt anti-POVERTY policy. A defensible conclusion is that it should be one component of a package, with transfers doing the household targeting and housing and skills policy addressing causes — and that "most effective" fails on the targeting argument alone.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) the workers/households distinction made explicitly and developed, alternatives evaluated, dated evidence, diagram used, clear judgement. L4 (16–20) well-developed and mostly balanced. L3 (11–15) some evaluation. L2 (6–10) one-sided. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that the Gini coefficient is the most useful measure of inequality available to economists.', marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The case for: the Gini reduces an entire distribution to one number between 0 and 1, which makes comparison across countries and over time straightforward; it is derived from the whole distribution rather than from two arbitrary points; it is scale-independent, so it is unaffected by inflation or by currency; and it has a clear geometric interpretation as twice the area between the Lorenz curve and the line of perfect equality. The UK figure of 33% before housing costs and 37% after in 2024/25 is instantly comparable with other economies.\n• Limitation 1 — information loss: two completely different distributions can produce the same Gini, because Lorenz curves can cross. A single number cannot say WHERE in the distribution the inequality sits, which is exactly what policy needs to know.\n• Limitation 2 — insensitivity at the tails: the Gini is most sensitive to changes around the middle of the distribution and relatively insensitive to changes at the very top. A period in which the top 1% share rises sharply while the Gini is flat is entirely possible — and is roughly what UK data since 2010 show.\n• Limitation 3 — it measures income, not wealth: wealth inequality is far higher in level and rising, so an income Gini alone gives a misleadingly reassuring picture.\n• Limitation 4 — annual snapshot: it counts students and pensioners drawing on savings as poor, so it overstates LIFETIME inequality and says nothing about mobility between deciles. A society with high annual inequality and high mobility differs profoundly from one with the same Gini and none.\n• Limitation 5 — before or after housing costs, before or after tax, and the equivalisation scale used all change the answer materially, so "the Gini coefficient" is not a single well-defined number.\n• Alternative measures and their own weaknesses: quintile or decile share ratios are transparent but ignore everything between the two points chosen; the Palma ratio (top 10% over bottom 40%) is deliberately sensitive to the parts of the distribution that change most, but is less familiar; the Theil index is decomposable between and within groups but has no intuitive interpretation; the Lorenz curve itself shows everything but cannot be ranked when curves cross; and the Human Development Index measures something different again.\n• Judgement: the Gini is the most USEFUL single summary precisely because it is a summary, and no single number can avoid information loss. The defensible conclusion is that it is indispensable as a headline but must be read alongside a Palma or top-share measure, a wealth measure and a mobility measure — that is, the question contains a false choice.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) strengths and at least three limitations developed, alternatives compared on their merits, the "single number" trade-off identified, judgement supported. L4 (16–20) well-developed and balanced. L3 (11–15) strengths and weaknesses listed with some development. L2 (6–10) definition plus assertion. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that raising the higher and additional rates of income tax is the best way to reduce inequality in the UK.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The mechanism: income tax is progressive through the £12,570 personal allowance and the 20%, 40% and 45% bands. Raising the top rates reduces the post-tax share of the highest earners, shifting the Lorenz curve for disposable income towards the line of perfect equality and lowering the Gini. Diagram: two Lorenz curves with area A shrinking.\n• Additional advantages: the machinery already exists, so administrative costs are near zero; the change is immediate; and the revenue can be directed to spending that raises the incomes or human capital of the poorest, doubling the distributional effect.\n• Objection 1 — behavioural response: higher marginal rates reduce the net return to effort, to risk-taking and to acquiring skills. High earners have the most scope to change the timing and form of income — converting it to capital gains, retaining it in a company, increasing pension contributions — so the taxable income elasticity is high at the top and revenue rises by far less than a static calculation implies. The Laffer curve argument is not that rates are necessarily beyond the peak, but that the response is real and must be estimated.\n• Objection 2 — international mobility: top earners and mobile capital can relocate, so a national rate increase in an open economy has a smaller yield than in a closed one, and may reduce the tax base permanently.\n• Objection 3 — the top is not where most inequality lives: raising the additional rate affects a small number of taxpayers. The 60% effective marginal rate created by the tapered personal allowance between £100,000 and £125,140 already distorts behaviour in that band, and adding to it worsens the distortion without touching the main body of the distribution.\n• Objection 4 — it does nothing about wealth: income tax does not reach capital held rather than earned, which is where inequality is greatest and growing.\n• Alternatives to compare: aligning capital gains tax with income tax rates closes the conversion route that blunts a rate rise; council tax revaluation and inheritance tax reform reach wealth; cutting the Universal Credit taper below 55% raises the incomes of the poorest directly; and education, childcare and housing policy change original income rather than redistributing it.\n• The equity-efficiency trade-off framed properly: the question is whether the deadweight loss from a higher rate exceeds the welfare gain from redistribution, given diminishing marginal utility of income. This is the analytical frame that separates a top-band answer.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that top-rate rises are a legitimate but limited tool — cheap to administer and quick, but with a small and uncertain yield and no effect on wealth — and that aligning capital taxation with income taxation is the higher-return reform.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) mechanism explained with a diagram, behavioural response analysed properly, alternatives evaluated, judgement supported. L4 (16–20) well-developed and balanced. L3 (11–15) partial. L2 (6–10) assertion. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Discuss the view that improving education is the most effective long-run policy for reducing inequality in the UK.', marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The mechanism: education raises human capital, which raises marginal physical product and therefore marginal revenue product, shifting the demand curve for that worker\'s labour to the right. Earnings rise because productivity rises, so the gain is sustainable and does not create a wedge between pay and productivity the way a wage floor can.\n• Why it changes ORIGINAL income, which is the key analytical point: transfers redistribute after the market has allocated; education changes what the market allocates in the first place, which avoids the incentive costs of redistribution and raises LRAS at the same time, so the policy is both equity- and efficiency-improving.\n• Market failure justification: education has positive externalities, individuals face credit constraints and imperfect information about returns, and children cannot choose their own schooling — so free market provision would be below the social optimum. Intervention is corrective, not merely redistributive.\n• Inter-generational effect: because parental income is one of the strongest predictors of a child\'s attainment, improving education for poorer children breaks the transmission mechanism, so the effect compounds across generations rather than needing to be repeated annually.\n• Objection 1 — time lags: the full return appears over 20 to 40 years. It does nothing for a household in poverty today, and no government can rely on the payoff arriving within its own term.\n• Objection 2 — diminishing and uncertain returns: UK participation in higher education is already high, so the marginal return to another year of average schooling is lower than in a developing economy. If qualifications work partly as a signal rather than through productivity, raising everyone\'s qualifications raises no one\'s relative position — a fallacy of composition.\n• Objection 3 — attainment gaps open before school and outside it: differences in early years development, housing quality, health and family income drive much of the attainment gap, so spending inside schools addresses a symptom. Early years and childcare intervention has a stronger evidence base than spending on later stages.\n• Objection 4 — it does nothing about wealth inequality, which is larger and rising, and nothing about the after-housing-costs gap of 4 Gini points that the housing market creates.\n• Government failure: skills funded today may be automated tomorrow; funding can be captured by already-advantaged schools and families; and catchment effects capitalise good schools into house prices, excluding poorer families from the very intervention meant to help them.\n• Judgement: education has the strongest claim to being the best LONG-RUN policy because it acts on original income and raises LRAS, but it cannot be the whole answer — the strongest conclusions pair it with early years intervention and housing policy, and note explicitly that transfers remain necessary in the meantime.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) mechanism developed, the original-income argument made explicitly, objections and government failure evaluated, judgement supported. L4 (16–20) well-developed and balanced. L3 (11–15) partial. L2 (6–10) description. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that economic growth does more to reduce absolute poverty than redistribution does.', marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Define the terms precisely and use the definition as the analytical spine: absolute poverty uses a threshold fixed in real terms, so it falls whenever real incomes at the bottom rise. Relative poverty uses 60% of the contemporary median, so growth that lifts everyone proportionately leaves it unchanged. The question is therefore much more favourable to growth than the equivalent question about relative poverty would be.\n• The case for growth: sustained growth raises real incomes, employment and tax revenue simultaneously. It is the only route that raises the incomes of the poor without reducing anyone else\'s, so it avoids the incentive costs and political resistance that redistribution faces. Historically it is the dominant explanation for falls in absolute poverty worldwide.\n• Chains of reasoning: growth raises labour demand, shifting the MRP curve right, which raises both employment and wages at the bottom; it expands the tax base, which funds transfers without higher rates; and it raises government revenue, allowing public services to expand.\n• The case against — distribution matters: growth reduces absolute poverty only if some of it reaches the bottom. If growth is concentrated in high-productivity sectors and regions, or accrues to capital rather than labour, the poorest may see little. UK real incomes at the bottom grew only slowly over the past decade despite positive GDP growth, and median income for the lowest tenth of households rose just £5 a week (2%) between 2023/24 and 2024/25.\n• The case against — some groups cannot benefit from growth: pensioners, the long-term sick and disabled, and carers are outside the labour market, so growth reaches them only through transfers or through the revenue that funds transfers.\n• Redistribution\'s strengths: it is immediate, precisely targetable, and the only tool that works for households with no earner. In the UK, final income is dramatically more equal than original income, which shows how much work transfers do.\n• Redistribution\'s costs: incentive effects through the 67.6% effective marginal deduction rate for a basic-rate Universal Credit claimant, administrative costs, take-up failure, and the risk that high taxation reduces the growth that funds the transfers.\n• The complementarity argument, which is the strongest judgement route: growth funds redistribution and redistribution can raise growth through human capital and higher consumption. They are not substitutes, and the framing of the question is partly false.\n• Judgement: for absolute poverty, growth plausibly does more over the long run; for households outside the labour market and for the immediate term, redistribution is indispensable. A supported conclusion that names the time horizon and the target group is what distinguishes L5.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) definitions used analytically, both sides developed, complementarity recognised, dated evidence, judgement conditional and supported. L4 (16–20) well-developed and balanced. L3 (11–15) partial. L2 (6–10) description. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the case for replacing means-tested benefits in the UK with a universal basic income.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Define: a universal basic income is an unconditional payment made to every individual regardless of income, wealth or employment status, replacing some or all means-tested support.\n• The case for — incentives: because it is not withdrawn as earnings rise, it eliminates the poverty trap. A Universal Credit claimant currently faces an effective marginal deduction rate of 67.6% on 2026 rates; under a pure UBI the deduction on an extra pound is the income tax and National Insurance rate alone, so the return to extra work rises sharply for exactly the households where it is now lowest.\n• The case for — take-up, administration and dignity: universality means take-up is complete, stigma disappears, and the enormous cost of assessing, reassessing and policing claims falls away. It also covers the self-employed, those with volatile hours and gig workers, whom a monthly means test handles badly.\n• The case for — security and flexibility: a floor under income supports risk-taking, retraining and caring work that the market does not pay for, which could raise productivity in a labour market being reshaped by automation and AI.\n• The case against — cost and the arithmetic: paying every adult a meaningful amount is extremely expensive. Funding it requires much higher tax rates across the distribution, and a higher basic rate reduces the net return to work for everyone, potentially offsetting the incentive gain at the bottom. This trade-off between adequacy, cost and incentives is the central problem and must be confronted for high marks.\n• The case against — targeting: a flat payment to everyone is by construction less targeted than means-testing, so poverty reduction per pound of spending is lower. Set at a level low enough to be affordable, it would leave the poorest worse off than under Universal Credit; set high enough to replace it, it is unaffordable at current tax rates.\n• The case against — special needs: means-tested systems can reflect disability, housing costs and family size. A flat payment cannot, so either a parallel means-tested system survives — losing the administrative saving — or those with the highest needs lose out.\n• Evidence: pilots in Finland and elsewhere found improvements in wellbeing but little measurable employment gain, and none tested a full nationwide replacement, so the evidence base is genuinely thin. Say so rather than overclaiming.\n• Intermediate options: a negative income tax, a much lower Universal Credit taper, a higher work allowance, or a universal payment for particular groups such as children capture part of the benefit at a fraction of the cost.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that UBI solves a real problem — the poverty trap — with an instrument whose cost scales with the whole population rather than with the problem, and that the intermediate options dominate on cost-effectiveness. Other supported conclusions are equally creditable.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) the adequacy-cost-incentives trilemma confronted with numbers, both sides developed, intermediate options evaluated, judgement supported. L4 (16–20) well-developed and balanced. L3 (11–15) partial. L2 (6–10) list of advantages and disadvantages. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Extract B (lines 22–23) states: "Housing costs push the measured poverty rate several percentage points higher and the Gini four points higher." Using the extracts and your knowledge of economics, discuss whether housing policy should be the government\'s main priority in reducing poverty in the UK.', marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Establish the scale from the data: with the Gini at 33% before housing costs and 37% after, and the relative low income rate several points higher after housing costs, the housing market is one of the largest single contributors to measured poverty and inequality in the UK. Any policy that reduced housing costs for low-income households would therefore move the headline measures more than most alternatives.\n• The mechanism: housing is a necessity with price-inelastic demand and highly price-inelastic supply in the short run, because of the time to build and planning constraints. Rising demand — from population growth, household formation, lower interest rates and investment demand — therefore raises prices and rents far more than quantity. Diagram: inelastic supply with a rightward demand shift.\n• Why the burden is regressive: housing costs take a far larger proportion of a low income than of a high one, and low-income households are disproportionately renters while high-income households are more likely to own outright. Rising rents transfer income from poorer tenants to wealthier landlords, worsening both income and wealth inequality at once.\n• Policy option 1 — increasing supply: planning reform, public housebuilding and infrastructure shift the supply curve right, lowering rents and prices in the long run. Strong on economics, but the lags are long, the political resistance is real, and existing owners lose wealth.\n• Policy option 2 — rent controls: a price ceiling below equilibrium raises quantity demanded and reduces quantity supplied, creating excess demand, deterioration of quality, and rationing by non-price means. Diagram: a maximum price with a shortage. Benefits existing tenants at the expense of would-be tenants — the standard analysis, and a useful discriminator.\n• Policy option 3 — housing benefit and the housing element of Universal Credit: immediate and targeted, but because short-run supply is inelastic a subsidy to demand is substantially capitalised into higher rents, so part of the spending accrues to landlords rather than to tenants.\n• The case for other priorities: worklessness remains the strongest single predictor of poverty, so employment and skills policy addresses more of the problem for households with no earner; raising Universal Credit or cutting the 55% taper raises incomes immediately; and early years and education policy attacks the causes across generations.\n• Measurement caution: after-housing-costs poverty is partly a measurement convention. Some housing costs reflect a choice of quality or location, so not every pound of the AHC gap is deprivation.\n• Judgement: housing has a strong claim because it is the largest contributor that current policy does least about, and because supply reform is one of the few interventions that reduces poverty AND raises growth. But the time lags mean it cannot be the ONLY priority, and the case for it rests on supply, not on rent controls or higher subsidies. A supported conclusion of any direction is creditable.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) the data used precisely, at least two policy options analysed with diagrams, alternatives to housing policy weighed, judgement supported. L4 (16–20) well-developed and mostly balanced. L3 (11–15) some analysis. L2 (6–10) description. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },
    ]
  },
  '3.1.8': {
    name: 'Market Failure & Government Intervention',
    questions: [

      /* ─────────── Section A part (a): 2-mark calculations ─────────── */

      { q: `Extract A (Figure 1) — written for this practice question\nThe market for a chemical whose production generates pollution\n  Free market output:              240 000 tonnes\n  Social optimum output:           200 000 tonnes\n  Marginal external cost per tonne (constant): £3.00\n\nUsing Extract A (Figure 1), calculate the welfare loss caused by the negative production externality. Give your answer in pounds.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: the welfare loss is the area of the triangle between MSC and MSB over the range of over-production.\nOver-production = 240 000 – 200 000 = 40 000 tonnes.\nWelfare loss = 1/2 x base x height = 1/2 x 40 000 x £3.00 = £60 000.\n\n2 marks for £60 000. 1 mark for the correct triangle method with an arithmetic slip, or for 40 000 x £3.00 = £120 000 (the rectangle rather than the triangle) where the method is shown. Remind students the triangle, not the rectangle, is the welfare loss, because the divergence between MSC and MSB is zero at the social optimum and only reaches £3.00 at the free market output.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — the levy rate is the UK rate in force from 1 April 2026\nSoft Drinks Industry Levy\n  Higher rate (8g of sugar per 100ml or more): 27.8p per litre\n  Volume of higher-rate drinks sold by one producer: 800 million litres a year\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate to one decimal place the annual levy liability of this producer. Give your answer in £ million.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: liability = rate per litre x volume.\n£0.278 x 800 000 000 = £222 400 000 = £222.4 million.\n\n2 marks for £222.4 million to one decimal place. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for a correct figure in pence with no conversion. Context: the UK Soft Drinks Industry Levy has two rates — 20.8p per litre for drinks with 5g to under 8g of sugar per 100ml and 27.8p for 8g or more, with drinks under 5g zero-rated. From January 2028 the lower threshold falls to 4.5g and milk-based drinks come into scope.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nCosts of producing one tonne of steel at a coastal plant\n  Marginal private cost: £9.80\n  Marginal social cost:  £14.50\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate the marginal external cost of producing one tonne of steel.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: marginal social cost = marginal private cost + marginal external cost, so MEC = MSC – MPC.\n£14.50 – £9.80 = £4.70 per tonne.\n\n2 marks for £4.70. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for adding rather than subtracting. This figure is also the size of the Pigouvian tax per tonne that would internalise the externality and move output to the social optimum, where MSC = MSB — credit that as a valid extension but do not require it.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA national emissions trading scheme\n  Total permits issued, 2024: 120 million tonnes of CO2 equivalent\n  Total permits issued, 2026:  96 million tonnes of CO2 equivalent\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate to two decimal places the percentage reduction in the emissions cap between 2024 and 2026.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: percentage change = (change / original) x 100.\nChange = 120 – 96 = 24 million tonnes.\n24 / 120 x 100 = 20.00%.\n\n2 marks for 20.00% (a reduction). 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for dividing by the new figure (25.00%). Extension for discussion: because the cap sets the quantity and the market sets the price, tightening the cap raises the permit price, which is precisely how a cap-and-trade scheme creates an incentive to abate.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nCost-benefit analysis of a proposed flood defence scheme\n  Present value of total social benefits: £4.80 billion\n  Present value of total social costs:    £3.20 billion\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate to two decimal places the benefit-cost ratio of the scheme.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: benefit-cost ratio = present value of benefits / present value of costs.\n4.80 / 3.20 = 1.50.\n\n2 marks for 1.50. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for calculating the net present value of £1.60 billion when the ratio was asked for (though the NPV is itself a correct and useful figure). A ratio above 1 means the scheme passes the cost-benefit test — but only on the valuations and discount rate chosen, both of which involve judgement, which is where the evaluation in longer questions lies.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nThe effect of a 20% ad valorem tax on a good\n  Price before the tax:                       £2.50\n  Price paid by consumers after the tax:      £2.90\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate to two decimal places the percentage of the tax borne by consumers.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: find the tax per unit, then the share of it paid by consumers.\nTax = 20% x £2.50 = £0.50 per unit.\nConsumer burden = £2.90 – £2.50 = £0.40 per unit.\nShare borne by consumers = 0.40 / 0.50 x 100 = 80.00%.\n\n2 marks for 80.00% (the remaining 20.00%, or £0.10, is borne by producers). 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for identifying the £0.40 consumer burden without converting it to a share. The economics: consumers bear 80% of the tax because demand is price inelastic relative to supply — which is exactly why indirect taxes on cigarettes and fuel raise so much revenue while changing quantity relatively little.` },

      /* ─────────── Section A part (b): 4-mark data questions ─────────── */

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question; the levy rates are the UK rates in force from 1 April 2026\nSoft drinks sold by one producer, by sugar content band\n  Under 5g per 100ml (zero-rated):        2018 = 380 million litres ; 2026 = 910 million litres\n  5g to under 8g per 100ml (20.8p/litre): 2018 = 210 million litres ; 2026 = 85 million litres\n  8g or more per 100ml (27.8p/litre):     2018 = 460 million litres ; 2026 = 105 million litres\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that the Soft Drinks Industry Levy has changed producer behaviour.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Manipulate the data: volume in the highest-taxed band fell 77.2% (from 460 to 105 million litres) while zero-rated volume rose 139.5% (from 380 to 910 million litres). Total volume rose only 1.8%, from 1 050 to 1 100 million litres, so this is substitution between bands, not a fall in sales (1–2 marks).\n• Explain the mechanism: the levy is a specific tax on the PRODUCER, banded by sugar content, so a producer can avoid it entirely by reformulating below 5g per 100ml. The tax therefore changes the relative profitability of recipes rather than simply raising price.\n• Link to the market failure: sugary drinks are demerit goods generating negative consumption externalities (NHS costs of obesity and type 2 diabetes) and information failure. Reformulation reduces sugar consumed per litre without requiring consumers to change behaviour at all, which is why the supply-side response matters more than the demand-side one here (1 mark).\n• Evaluation point worth the fourth mark: total volume barely changed, so if the externality depends on total consumption of sweetened drinks rather than sugar alone — through sweetener effects or taste formation — the levy has done less than the sugar figures suggest. The data also cannot separate the levy from wider changes in consumer taste.\n\n4 marks: substitution between bands quantified AND the producer-incentive mechanism explained. Maximum 2 marks for an answer that only notes that high-sugar sales fell.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nA national emissions trading scheme\n  Permits issued (Mt CO2e):     2021 = 140 ; 2026 = 96\n  Market permit price (£/tonne): 2021 = £42 ; 2026 = £78\n  Verified emissions (Mt CO2e): 2021 = 138 ; 2026 = 95\n  Investment in abatement technology by covered firms (£bn): 2021 = 1.8 ; 2026 = 4.6\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that the emissions trading scheme is working as intended.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Manipulate: the cap fell 31.4% and verified emissions fell 31.2%, so emissions tracked the cap almost exactly — which is the defining property of a quantity instrument (1 mark).\n• The price signal: the permit price rose 85.7%, from £42 to £78. Explain why — a tighter cap reduces the supply of permits, and with demand for emissions relatively inelastic in the short run, the price rises sharply (1 mark).\n• The incentive mechanism: a firm compares the permit price with its own marginal abatement cost. If abatement costs less than £78 a tonne it abates and sells its spare permits; if more, it buys permits. Abatement therefore happens wherever it is cheapest, which is why the scheme achieves the cap at lower total cost than uniform regulation would (1 mark).\n• Confirming evidence: abatement investment rose 155.6%, from £1.8bn to £4.6bn, which is the dynamic efficiency effect — a rising permit price makes low-carbon capital profitable (1 mark).\n\n4 marks: at least two figures manipulated AND both the price signal and the abatement incentive explained. Do not credit a general description of how permits work that ignores the table.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nCigarette consumption and taxation\n  Tax as a percentage of the retail price: 2010 = 77% ; 2026 = 88%\n  Adult smoking prevalence:                2010 = 20.1% ; 2026 = 10.4%\n  Estimated illicit market share of all cigarettes consumed: 2010 = 9% ; 2026 = 17%\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show both a success and an unintended consequence of using taxation to reduce smoking.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• The success, quantified: smoking prevalence roughly halved, falling 9.7 percentage points or 48.3%, while tax as a share of the retail price rose 11 percentage points (1 mark).\n• Explain the mechanism: tobacco generates negative consumption externalities (healthcare costs, passive smoking) and internalities from addiction and information failure, so a specific duty raises the private price towards the marginal social cost, reduces quantity demanded towards the social optimum and internalises the externality (1 mark).\n• The unintended consequence, quantified: the illicit share nearly doubled, rising 8 percentage points to 17% (1 mark).\n• Explain why this matters: smuggled cigarettes are untaxed, so the externality is not internalised for that share of consumption, government revenue is lost, and the product escapes safety and age-of-sale regulation. This is government failure arising from a well-intentioned intervention — the higher the tax, the greater the incentive to evade it (1 mark).\n• Caveat if the other points are secure: the fall in prevalence cannot be attributed to tax alone; advertising bans, the 2007 indoor smoking ban, plain packaging and the rise of vaping all contributed.\n\n4 marks: both effects quantified AND explained. Maximum 2 marks where only the success is addressed.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nCosts of a car journey into a city centre at peak time\n  Marginal private cost to the driver (fuel, wear, parking): £6.40\n  Marginal external cost (congestion delay to others):        £4.10\n  Marginal external cost (air pollution and noise):           £1.70\n  Marginal external cost (accident risk imposed on others):   £0.90\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that urban road use is over-consumed in a free market.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Manipulate: total marginal external cost = 4.10 + 1.70 + 0.90 = £6.70, so marginal social cost = 6.40 + 6.70 = £13.10. External costs are 51.1% of MSC and exceed the driver\'s own private cost (1–2 marks).\n• Explain the decision rule: a rational driver compares private benefit with the £6.40 private cost, ignoring the £6.70 imposed on others. Journeys are therefore taken whenever the private benefit exceeds £6.40, including journeys whose private benefit is below the £13.10 social cost (1 mark).\n• Result and welfare loss: output exceeds the social optimum where MSC = MSB, and the welfare loss is the triangle between MSC and MSB over the range of over-consumption. Congestion is the classic case because the externality is imposed by each driver on every other driver simultaneously (1 mark).\n• Extension worth the fourth mark: a congestion charge set at £6.70 would internalise the externality exactly and move consumption to the social optimum — which is the logic of the London congestion charge introduced in 2003, though setting the charge equal to the true MEC in practice requires information the authority does not have.\n\n4 marks: MSC calculated AND the divergence explained as the cause of over-consumption. 1–2 marks for figures repeated without the decision rule.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nChildhood vaccination in two districts\n  District P: no subsidy ; vaccination rate 71% ; measles cases per 100 000 = 34\n  District Q: full subsidy and school-based delivery ; vaccination rate 94% ; measles cases per 100 000 = 2\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that vaccination generates positive externalities of consumption.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Manipulate: a 23 percentage point difference in vaccination rates is associated with a 94.1% lower measles case rate — a 17-fold difference. The fall in cases is enormously more than proportionate to the rise in coverage (1–2 marks).\n• Explain the externality: the private benefit of vaccination is protection for the individual child. The external benefit is protection for everyone else, because a vaccinated child cannot transmit the disease. Marginal social benefit therefore exceeds marginal private benefit at every level of consumption.\n• Explain the non-proportionality, which is the discriminating point: herd immunity is a threshold effect. Once coverage passes the threshold, transmission chains break and cases collapse, which is why the case rate falls far faster than coverage rises (1 mark).\n• Explain the free market outcome: individuals decide on private benefit alone, so consumption settles where MPB = MPC, below the social optimum where MSB = MPC. There is under-consumption and a welfare loss, and a subsidy equal to the marginal external benefit internalises it — which is what District Q has done (1 mark).\n\n4 marks: data manipulated AND the MSB/MPB divergence explained, ideally with the threshold effect. Maximum 2 marks for "vaccination is good for everyone".` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nWaste disposal in one region since the introduction of a landfill tax\n  Landfill tax per tonne:     2000 = £11 ; 2026 = £126\n  Waste sent to landfill (Mt): 2000 = 7.9 ; 2026 = 1.4\n  Waste recycled (Mt):         2000 = 1.2 ; 2026 = 5.8\n  Reported incidents of illegal waste dumping: 2000 = 1 100 ; 2026 = 4 700\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that the landfill tax has both corrected and created a market failure.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Manipulate: the tax rose more than elevenfold, landfill volumes fell 82.3% and recycling rose 383.3%, while illegal dumping rose 327.3% (1–2 marks).\n• Explain the correction: landfill generates negative externalities — methane emissions, leachate, land use, local disamenity — so the private cost of disposal understates the social cost. A specific tax per tonne raises the private cost towards MSC, reduces landfill towards the social optimum and changes the relative price of recycling, which explains the substitution shown in the table (1 mark).\n• Explain the government failure: the tax also raises the private gain from evading it. Fly-tipping is untaxed, so a higher tax shifts waste towards an activity whose external costs (clean-up, contamination, disamenity) may be higher per tonne than landfill\'s. The intervention has created a new externality while correcting the original one (1 mark).\n• Evaluation for the fourth mark: the net welfare effect depends on the relative sizes of the two externalities and on enforcement. 3 600 extra dumping incidents against 6.5 million tonnes diverted from landfill suggests the correction dominates, but the data give no valuation of either, so the table cannot settle it.\n\n4 marks: both effects quantified AND explained. Maximum 2 marks where only one side is covered.` },

      /* ─────────── Section A part (c): 9-mark diagram/analysis questions ─────────── */

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 5–6) states: "The plant\'s discharges impose costs on fishermen and on households downstream." With the help of a diagram, explain how a negative externality of production leads to a misallocation of resources.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: price/cost on the vertical axis, quantity on the horizontal. Draw MPC and, above it, MSC, with the vertical gap between them equal to the marginal external cost. Draw MPB = MSB as the demand curve (there is no consumption externality here).\n• Free market outcome: production settles where MPC = MPB at Q1, because the firm maximises profit on the costs it actually pays. The social optimum is where MSC = MSB at Q*, which is lower. Shade the welfare loss triangle between MSC and MSB over the range Q* to Q1 and label it clearly.\n• Chain of reasoning: because the polluter does not bear the full cost of its activity, the price signal is wrong — the good appears cheaper to produce than it truly is, so too much is produced and too many resources are allocated to it. This is allocative inefficiency: society would gain by moving resources elsewhere.\n• Why the market does not solve it: pollution is not traded, so there is no price for it. Property rights over clean air and water are absent or unenforceable, so the affected parties cannot charge the polluter. This is the property rights explanation of the failure, and it points directly to the Coasean policy response.\n• Application and evaluation: the size of the welfare loss depends on the size of the marginal external cost and on the elasticities of demand and supply — where demand is price inelastic, a given divergence produces a smaller quantity distortion. Measuring the external cost in money terms is the central practical difficulty for any corrective policy.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) accurate, fully labelled diagram with MSC above MPC, both equilibria identified and the welfare loss triangle correctly shaded, with a developed chain of reasoning applied to the extract. L2 (4–6) diagram broadly correct but the welfare loss missing or misplaced, or analysis not applied. L1 (1–3) description of pollution with no diagrammatic analysis.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 11–12) states: "Vaccination protects the person who receives it and everyone they would otherwise have infected." With the help of a diagram, explain why a free market under-provides goods that generate positive externalities of consumption.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: MSB above MPB, with the vertical gap equal to the marginal external benefit; MSC = MPC as the supply curve. Free market consumption where MPB = MPC at Q1; social optimum where MSB = MSC at Q*, which is greater. Shade the welfare loss triangle between MSB and MSC over Q1 to Q*, representing the net benefit forgone by under-consumption.\n• Chain of reasoning: individuals weigh only their own benefit against the price, so they under-consume relative to the social optimum. The benefit that spills over to third parties is real but unpriced, so it does not enter anyone\'s decision.\n• Application: vaccination\'s external benefit is the interruption of transmission. Because herd immunity is a threshold effect, the marginal external benefit is very large near the threshold, which is why under-consumption by a small margin can have disproportionate social costs.\n• Policy implication derived from the diagram: a subsidy equal to the marginal external benefit shifts MPC down to MPC – subsidy, moving consumption to Q* and eliminating the welfare loss. Free provision is the limiting case where the subsidy equals the full price.\n• Evaluation: the government must know the size of the marginal external benefit to set the subsidy correctly — it does not, so the intervention may over- or under-shoot. Under-consumption may also stem from information failure or from behavioural factors rather than from the externality, in which case information provision or default enrolment may be cheaper than a subsidy.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) accurate labelled diagram with MSB above MPB, both equilibria and the welfare loss identified, developed reasoning and application. L2 (4–6) diagram present but incompletely used. L1 (1–3) definition of positive externality only.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 18–19) states: "Ministers are considering a tax set equal to the damage each tonne of emissions causes." With the help of a diagram, analyse how an indirect tax can correct a negative production externality.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: start with MPC, MSC above it, and MSB. Free market output Q1 where MPC = MSB; social optimum Q* where MSC = MSB. Now impose a specific tax per unit equal to the marginal external cost: the supply curve shifts up from MPC to MPC + tax, which coincides with MSC. The new market equilibrium is at Q*.\n• Chain of reasoning: the tax makes the producer pay for the damage it causes, so the external cost is internalised and the private decision now coincides with the socially optimal one. The price rises and quantity falls to the optimum; the welfare loss triangle is eliminated.\n• Revenue and hypothecation: the tax raises revenue equal to the tax per unit multiplied by Q*, which could fund clean-up, compensation or abatement subsidies — a second-round efficiency gain, though hypothecation reduces fiscal flexibility.\n• Incidence and elasticity: the split of the tax between producer and consumer depends on relative elasticities. Where demand is price inelastic, most of the tax is passed to consumers and the quantity reduction is small, so the tax raises revenue effectively but corrects the externality only weakly. A diagram showing an inelastic demand curve makes this point precisely.\n• Evaluation for the top band: the government must know the monetary value of the marginal external cost to set the rate correctly, and valuing health damage or the social cost of carbon involves contested judgement. Set too high, the tax creates a new welfare loss from under-production; too low and the externality persists. There are also international competitiveness effects where rival producers face no equivalent tax — the carbon leakage argument.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) accurate labelled diagram showing the supply shift to MSC, both equilibria identified, with elasticity or information-failure evaluation. L2 (4–6) correct diagram with limited analysis. L1 (1–3) assertion that a tax reduces output.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 22–23) states: "The scheme pays half the cost of a home insulation upgrade." With the help of a diagram, explain how a subsidy can correct the under-consumption of a merit good.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Define a merit good precisely: a good under-consumed in a free market because consumers undervalue its private benefits (information failure, myopia, high discounting of future gains) and because it generates positive externalities. Home insulation fits both — households underestimate lifetime fuel savings, and reduced energy use lowers emissions for everyone.\n• Diagram: MSB above MPB (external benefit), supply at MPC. Free market consumption Q1 where MPB = MPC; social optimum Q* where MSB = MSC. A subsidy shifts supply down from MPC to MPC – subsidy, cutting the price paid by consumers and raising consumption to Q*. Label the total cost of the subsidy as the rectangle (subsidy per unit x Q*).\n• Chain of reasoning: the subsidy lowers the price faced by the household, so quantity demanded rises along the demand curve. Where the under-consumption stems from information failure rather than from price, a subsidy alone may not close the gap — which is why insulation schemes combine a subsidy with advice and assessment.\n• Opportunity cost and funding: the subsidy must be financed by taxation or borrowing, so there is an opportunity cost in forgone spending elsewhere and a deadweight loss from the taxes that fund it.\n• Evaluation: government failure risks include setting the subsidy at the wrong level because the marginal external benefit is unknown; deadweight loss where households would have insulated anyway; supplier capture, where installers raise prices when a subsidy is announced, so part of the subsidy is captured by producers rather than reaching consumers — a real and recurrent problem with home improvement schemes.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) accurate diagram with the supply shift and the subsidy cost identified, merit good defined correctly, with genuine evaluation. L2 (4–6) diagram present but partly used. L1 (1–3) description of subsidies.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 28–29) states: "The scheme issues a fixed number of permits and lets firms trade them." With the help of a diagram, analyse how a system of tradeable pollution permits achieves a given reduction in emissions at least cost.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: permit price on the vertical axis, quantity of permits on the horizontal. Supply of permits is a vertical line at the cap set by the regulator — perfectly price inelastic. Demand for permits is downward sloping, derived from firms\' marginal abatement costs. The equilibrium permit price is where they cross. Show the effect of tightening the cap: the vertical supply line shifts left and the permit price rises.\n• The least-cost result, which is the heart of the answer: each firm compares the permit price P with its own marginal abatement cost. A firm whose MAC is below P abates and sells surplus permits at a profit; a firm whose MAC is above P buys permits instead. Trading continues until every firm\'s MAC equals P, which is the condition for the total cost of achieving the cap to be minimised. No regulator needs to know any firm\'s costs — the market reveals them.\n• Certainty over quantity: unlike a tax, the scheme fixes the environmental outcome. Emissions cannot exceed the cap whatever happens to abatement costs, which matters where a scientific threshold must be met.\n• Dynamic efficiency: a positive permit price makes low-carbon investment profitable, and a pre-announced tightening path raises expected future prices, encouraging investment today.\n• Evaluation: the price is volatile, because permit demand shifts with the business cycle against a fixed supply — a recession collapses the price and the abatement incentive with it. Over-allocation of permits in the early phases of the EU scheme is the standard example. Administration, monitoring and verification are costly, and carbon leakage occurs where covered firms relocate to uncovered jurisdictions — which is part of the rationale for the May 2025 agreement to link the UK and EU emissions trading systems.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) accurate permit market diagram with a vertical supply curve, the MAC-equalisation argument derived rather than asserted, and evaluation. L2 (4–6) description of how permits work with a partly used diagram. L1 (1–3) assertion.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 2–3) states: "No private firm would build a flood defence and expect to be paid for it." Analyse why the free market fails to provide pure public goods.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Define the two defining characteristics precisely. Non-excludability: once the good is provided, it is impossible or prohibitively costly to prevent anyone from consuming it. Non-rivalry: one person\'s consumption does not reduce the amount available to anyone else, so the marginal cost of an additional user is zero.\n• The free rider problem derived from non-excludability: a rational consumer knows they will receive the benefit whether or not they pay, so they understate their willingness to pay. If everyone reasons this way, effective demand is far below true valuation, no market demand curve can be constructed, and no profit-maximising firm supplies the good. Missing market, not merely under-provision.\n• The pricing problem derived from non-rivalry: allocative efficiency requires price equal to marginal cost, but the marginal cost of an extra user is zero. A firm charging zero earns no revenue and cannot cover the fixed cost of provision, so private supply is impossible even without free riding.\n• Application to the extract: a flood defence protects every property behind it regardless of who paid, and protecting one more house costs nothing extra. Both conditions hold, so private provision fails and public provision funded by general taxation is the standard response.\n• Refinement for the top band: most real goods are quasi-public rather than pure. Technology can change the classification — road pricing and satellite tolling made roads excludable, and encryption made broadcasting excludable. A road also becomes rival once congested. Note too that public provision brings its own difficulty: the government must estimate how much to provide without a market to reveal valuations, which is why cost-benefit analysis is used and why it is contestable.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) both characteristics defined and each used to derive a separate reason for failure, applied to the extract, with the quasi-public refinement. L2 (4–6) the free rider problem explained but non-rivalry under-used. L1 (1–3) definitions with examples only.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 33–34) states: "Scotland introduced a minimum price per unit of alcohol in May 2018." With the help of a diagram, analyse the likely effects of a minimum price on the consumption of a demerit good.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: a standard demand and supply diagram with a horizontal minimum price line above the free market equilibrium. Quantity demanded falls to Qd, quantity supplied rises to Qs, and excess supply (Qs – Qd) appears. Alternatively, and equally creditable, an MPB/MSB diagram showing the price floor moving consumption towards the social optimum.\n• Chain of reasoning on why intervention is justified: alcohol generates negative consumption externalities (healthcare, policing, lost output, harm to others) and internalities from addiction and information failure, so free market consumption exceeds the social optimum.\n• Why a minimum price rather than a tax: a minimum price bites hardest on the cheapest, strongest products, which are disproportionately consumed by the heaviest drinkers, whereas a per-unit duty raises the price of all alcohol. Targeting matters because the externality is concentrated in heavy consumption, not in average consumption.\n• Effectiveness and elasticity: demand for alcohol among dependent drinkers is highly price inelastic, so a given price rise reduces their consumption relatively little even though it is their consumption that generates most of the externality. The policy may therefore reduce total consumption mainly among moderate drinkers, who impose few external costs.\n• Evaluation: unlike a tax, the extra revenue accrues to retailers and producers, not to the government, so it cannot fund treatment — a significant design weakness. Regressive effects fall on low-income drinkers; cross-border and online purchasing undermines a policy applied in one nation only; and substitution towards illicit or home-produced alcohol is possible.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) accurate labelled diagram with the minimum price above equilibrium and excess supply shown, the targeting argument developed, and evaluation of elasticity and the revenue point. L2 (4–6) diagram correct with limited analysis. L1 (1–3) description of minimum pricing.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 38–39) states: "The seller knows far more about the car than the buyer ever will." Analyse how asymmetric information can cause a market to fail.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Define asymmetric information: one party to a transaction has more or better information than the other, so the price cannot reflect the true value of the good and the market outcome is allocatively inefficient.\n• Adverse selection, developed as a chain: buyers cannot distinguish good used cars from bad ones, so they offer only the average price. Owners of genuinely good cars find that price too low and withdraw them from the market. The average quality of cars remaining falls, so buyers lower their offers again, and the process repeats. In the limit only the worst cars are traded and the market for good used cars disappears — a missing market caused purely by information.\n• Second application to sharpen the analysis: in health or travel insurance, those most likely to claim are most likely to buy, so the pool worsens and premiums rise, pricing out low-risk customers. This is why insurers screen, why risk pooling must often be compulsory, and why a private market alone cannot insure everyone.\n• Moral hazard, distinguished clearly from adverse selection: adverse selection occurs BEFORE the contract, in who chooses to trade; moral hazard occurs AFTER, when a party behaves more riskily because they are protected from the consequences. An insured driver takes less care; a bank whose deposits are guaranteed lends more riskily.\n• Market and policy responses: warranties, service histories, brand reputation, independent inspection and third-party certification are private solutions that work by making quality credible. Regulation — consumer protection law, mandatory disclosure, professional licensing, compulsory insurance — is the public response. Evaluate: private signalling works where reputation is repeated and verifiable; regulation is needed where it is not, but brings compliance costs and the risk of regulatory capture.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) adverse selection developed as a full chain of reasoning, clearly distinguished from moral hazard, with applications and evaluated remedies. L2 (4–6) the idea explained with limited development or the two concepts conflated. L1 (1–3) definition only.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 42–43) states: "Rent controls were introduced to make housing affordable." With the help of a diagram, analyse how a maximum price can lead to government failure.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: demand and supply for rented housing with a maximum price (price ceiling) set below the market equilibrium. Quantity demanded rises to Qd, quantity supplied falls to Qs, and excess demand (Qd – Qs) is the shortage. Shade the welfare loss triangle between the demand and supply curves over the range Qs to the free market quantity.\n• Chain of reasoning on the intended effect: existing tenants who keep their tenancy pay less, which raises their real income and was the policy objective.\n• Chain of reasoning on government failure: the shortage means the good must be rationed by something other than price — queuing, waiting lists, discrimination by landlords, or informal side payments. Those rationed out are disproportionately new entrants, the young and the mobile, which is the opposite of the distributional intention.\n• Supply-side consequences over time: landlords reduce maintenance because they can let the property regardless of its condition, and new investment in rental housing falls. Supply is more price elastic in the long run, so the shortage grows over time — the policy gets worse, not better, the longer it runs.\n• Unintended consequences and secondary markets: a black market emerges at prices above the ceiling, and reduced mobility between properties worsens geographical immobility of labour. Both are classic signatures of government failure — an intervention whose net welfare cost exceeds the market failure it addressed.\n• Evaluation for balance: where landlords hold significant market power, or where tenants face high switching costs, a ceiling can transfer surplus without a large quantity effect, so the outcome depends on market structure and on the elasticity of supply. Increasing supply directly addresses the underlying problem without creating the shortage.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) accurate labelled diagram with the ceiling below equilibrium and the shortage shown, at least two government failure consequences developed, with the long-run elasticity point. L2 (4–6) diagram correct with limited analysis. L1 (1–3) assertion that rent controls cause shortages.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 46–47) states: "No one owns the fishery, so no one has an interest in preserving it." Analyse why common access resources are over-exploited, and how extending property rights could address this.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Define a common access resource: rival in consumption but non-excludable. One fisher\'s catch reduces the stock available to everyone else, yet no one can be prevented from fishing. This combination — rivalry without excludability — is what distinguishes it from a pure public good.\n• The tragedy of the commons as a chain of reasoning: each fisher compares their private marginal benefit from one more catch with their private marginal cost. The cost of depleting the stock is borne by all users, so each individual rationally ignores almost all of it. Every user reasoning this way produces exploitation far beyond the sustainable yield, and the resource collapses — a negative externality imposed by each user on every other user.\n• Why the market cannot correct it: there is no price for the stock because there is no owner to charge for access. The absence of property rights means the resource is under-priced at zero, so it is over-used.\n• The Coasean response: assign and enforce property rights, and the owner internalises the externality because depleting the stock now reduces their own future income. Individual transferable quotas in fisheries, ownership of forests, and land titling all work this way. Coase\'s further insight is that where property rights are clear and transaction costs are low, the parties can bargain to an efficient outcome regardless of who holds the right.\n• Evaluation: extending property rights works where the resource can be physically or legally divided and monitored. It is far harder for migratory fish stocks, the atmosphere or the deep ocean, where enforcement crosses jurisdictions and transaction costs are enormous. Distributional questions also arise — assigning rights hands a valuable asset to whoever receives it. Regulation, quotas and taxation are alternatives with their own information requirements.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) the rival/non-excludable combination used to derive the over-use result, Coase explained accurately with conditions, and limits evaluated. L2 (4–6) the tragedy of the commons described with limited analysis of the remedy. L1 (1–3) definition and example only.` },

      /* ─────────── Paper 3 investigation: 10-mark data questions ─────────── */

      { q: `Extract D (Table 2) — written for this practice question; the levy rates are the UK rates in force from 1 April 2026\nThe Soft Drinks Industry Levy\n  Average sugar content of taxed soft drinks (g per 100ml): 2016 = 7.6 ; 2026 = 3.9\n  Total soft drinks volume sold (billion litres):           2016 = 6.1 ; 2026 = 6.3\n  Total sugar sold in soft drinks (thousand tonnes):        2016 = 464 ; 2026 = 246\n  Adult obesity rate (%):                                   2016 = 26.2 ; 2026 = 27.1\n  Childhood obesity rate, age 10–11 (%):                    2016 = 20.0 ; 2026 = 21.3\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data in Extract D (Table 2) suggest that the Soft Drinks Industry Levy has been a successful policy?`, marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Success on the immediate target: average sugar content fell 48.7% and total sugar sold in soft drinks fell 47.0%, even though volume rose 3.3%. The levy was designed as a producer-facing, banded tax to induce reformulation, and on that measure it worked exactly as intended.\n• Failure on the ultimate objective: adult obesity rose 0.9 percentage points and childhood obesity rose 1.3 points over the same period. The policy objective was better health outcomes, not lower sugar in one product category, so on the ultimate measure the data show no success at all.\n• Explaining the gap — substitution: soft drinks are one source of dietary sugar among many. Consumers may have substituted towards confectionery, cakes or milk-based drinks, which is precisely why the announced 2028 reform brings milk-based drinks into scope and lowers the threshold from 5g to 4.5g per 100ml. Calorie intake may also have been maintained through sweeteners changing taste preferences.\n• Explaining the gap — counterfactual: obesity was rising before the levy. The right question is not whether obesity fell but whether it rose by LESS than it would have without the levy, and the table contains no counterfactual. A rising trend flattening is consistent with an effective policy.\n• Explaining the gap — other determinants: obesity depends on total calorie intake, physical activity, food environment, income and time costs. The levy addresses one input to one of these, so it could not plausibly reverse the trend on its own.\n• Missing data: nothing on total dietary sugar, on calorie intake, on distributional effects across income groups, on revenue raised, or on the cost of the intervention. A cost-benefit judgement is impossible from this table.\n• Judgement on extent: strongly successful as a reformulation instrument, unproven as an obesity policy. The strongest answers say what evidence would settle it — total dietary sugar and calorie data, and a credible counterfactual.\n\nLevels: L3 (8–10) the data used precisely on both sides, the intermediate-target/ultimate-objective distinction drawn explicitly, alternative explanations identified, judgement supported. L2 (4–7) accurate use on one side with some evaluation. L1 (1–3) the data restated.` },

      { q: `Extract D (Table 2) — written for this practice question\nTwo approaches to reducing industrial emissions in comparable economies\n  Economy J (carbon tax): tax £70/tonne in 2026 ; emissions fell 24% since 2018 ; tax revenue £9.1bn in 2026 ; abatement cost per tonne saved £61\n  Economy K (tradeable permits): permit price £78/tonne in 2026 ; emissions fell 31% since 2018 ; auction revenue £6.4bn in 2026 ; abatement cost per tonne saved £58\n  Both economies: manufacturing output growth since 2018 = 4% (J) and 3% (K)\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data in Extract D (Table 2) suggest that tradeable permits are a more effective instrument than a carbon tax?`, marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The case from the data: Economy K achieved a 31% emissions reduction against J\'s 24% — 7 percentage points more, or 29.2% more reduction — at a lower abatement cost per tonne (£58 against £61, 4.9% lower). On both the environmental outcome and the cost of achieving it, permits look better.\n• Theoretical support: a permit scheme fixes the QUANTITY of emissions, so the environmental outcome is certain, while trading equalises marginal abatement costs across firms and therefore achieves any given cap at least cost. A tax fixes the PRICE and leaves the quantity to be determined by firms\' abatement costs, which the regulator does not know.\n• The case against reading too much into the data: the permit price (£78) was 11.4% higher than the tax (£70), so K applied a stronger incentive. The comparison may therefore show the effect of a higher carbon price rather than of a better instrument — a confounder that a top answer must identify.\n• Further limitations: revenue was 29.7% lower under permits, which matters for funding abatement or compensating losers, and depends on how many permits were auctioned rather than issued free. Output growth differed slightly (4% against 3%), so emissions intensity is not being compared on a like-for-like basis. Two economies is not a sample, and the table gives no information on sectoral composition, energy mix, enforcement or the starting point in 2018.\n• The standard theoretical trade-off, worth credit: a tax gives price certainty, which helps firms invest, while a permit scheme gives quantity certainty but volatile prices, since permit demand shifts with the business cycle against a fixed supply. Which is preferable depends on whether the priority is meeting a scientific threshold or protecting firms from cost shocks.\n• Judgement on extent: the data are consistent with permits performing better, but because the carbon price differed they cannot isolate the instrument from the incentive strength. A defensible conclusion is that the evidence is suggestive but not decisive, and that the relevant comparison would hold the carbon price constant.\n\nLevels: L3 (8–10) accurate manipulation, the price-difference confounder identified, the theoretical price/quantity trade-off used, judgement supported. L2 (4–7) data described with one limitation. L1 (1–3) figures restated.` },

      /* ─────────── Section B part (a): 15-mark explain questions ─────────── */

      { q: 'Explain the main causes of market failure in a modern economy.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Externalities: where marginal social cost diverges from marginal private cost, or marginal social benefit from marginal private benefit, the price signal is wrong and the free market equilibrium is not the social optimum. Negative production externalities cause over-production; positive consumption externalities cause under-consumption. Each produces a measurable welfare loss triangle.\n• Public goods: non-excludability creates the free rider problem, so effective demand understates true valuation; non-rivalry means the efficient price is zero, which cannot fund provision. Together these produce a missing market rather than merely an inefficient one.\n• Common access resources: rival but non-excludable, so each user ignores the cost of depletion imposed on all other users. The tragedy of the commons over-exploits fisheries, aquifers and the atmosphere.\n• Information failure: asymmetric information causes adverse selection, which can destroy a market entirely, and moral hazard, which distorts behaviour after contracting. Imperfect information leads to the under-consumption of merit goods such as pensions, insurance and preventive healthcare, and the over-consumption of demerit goods where consumers discount future harm.\n• Market power: a monopolist restricting output to raise price produces allocative inefficiency, with price above marginal cost and a deadweight welfare loss. Monopsony in labour markets holds wages below MRP.\n• Factor immobility: geographical and occupational immobility prevent labour and capital moving in response to price signals, so unemployed resources coexist with unfilled demand and the economy produces inside its production possibility boundary.\n• Inequity: the market distributes income according to factor ownership and marginal productivity, which has no necessary relationship to need. This is a normative judgement rather than an efficiency failure, and the strongest answers say so explicitly rather than lumping it in with the others.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) at least four causes explained with developed chains of reasoning, correct use of MSC/MSB analysis, and the efficiency/equity distinction handled properly. L2 (6–10) causes identified with partial explanation. L1 (1–5) a list of types of market failure.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main methods a government can use to reduce the consumption of demerit goods.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Establish the failure first: demerit goods are over-consumed because consumers underestimate the private harm (information failure and present bias) and because consumption imposes external costs on others. MPB exceeds MSB, so free market consumption exceeds the social optimum.\n• Indirect taxation: a specific or ad valorem tax raises the price towards MSC, reducing quantity demanded towards the optimum and raising revenue that can fund treatment. Chain: effectiveness depends on the price elasticity of demand — for addictive goods, demand is inelastic, so the tax raises much revenue and cuts consumption little, and the incidence falls mainly on consumers. Tobacco duty is the clearest example: prevalence roughly halved over fifteen years, but the illicit trade grew.\n• Minimum pricing: a price floor targets the cheapest, strongest products, which are disproportionately consumed by the heaviest users. Scotland introduced minimum unit pricing for alcohol in May 2018. Unlike a tax, the revenue accrues to retailers rather than to the government, which is a genuine design weakness.\n• Regulation and bans: age-of-sale restrictions, advertising bans, indoor smoking bans, plain packaging. Chain: effective where enforcement is feasible and the behaviour is visible, and it changes social norms over time as well as prices. Evaluate: prohibition tends to create black markets, lose tax revenue and remove safety regulation from the product.\n• Information provision: health warnings, labelling, education campaigns. Chain: this addresses the information failure directly rather than overriding consumer choice, so it corrects the failure at its source. Evaluate: slow, and evidence suggests information alone changes behaviour much less than economists once assumed.\n• Behavioural nudges: default options, changes to product placement and choice architecture, restricting multi-buy promotions. Cheap and preserves choice, but the effects are often small and can fade.\n• The common evaluation thread: all these methods require the government to know the size of the external cost, all risk regressive distributional effects since demerit goods take a larger share of low incomes, and all can produce unintended consequences and government failure.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) at least four methods explained with chains of reasoning, elasticity used analytically, and real dated examples. L2 (6–10) methods listed with partial explanation. L1 (1–5) a list.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main causes of government failure.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Define precisely: government failure occurs where intervention intended to correct a market failure produces a net welfare loss — the costs of the intervention exceed the benefit of the correction. It is not simply a policy that fails to work perfectly.\n• Information failure: setting a Pigouvian tax correctly requires knowing the monetary value of the marginal external cost, which is unobservable. Valuing a life, a tonne of carbon or the disamenity of noise involves contested judgement. Set too high, the tax creates a new welfare loss from under-production; set too low, the externality persists. The same problem affects subsidies, quotas and cost-benefit analysis.\n• Unintended consequences: interventions change incentives in ways policymakers do not anticipate. A landfill tax raises fly-tipping; high tobacco duty grows the illicit trade; rent controls reduce maintenance and new supply; a demand-side housing subsidy is capitalised into higher rents where supply is inelastic.\n• Administrative and compliance costs: running, monitoring and enforcing a scheme absorbs real resources. Where these exceed the welfare gain, intervention is worse than doing nothing even if the mechanism works.\n• Regulatory capture: the regulator comes to serve the interests of the firms it regulates, because those firms hold the information and have a concentrated interest, while consumers are dispersed and individually have little at stake.\n• Political and electoral incentives: short political time horizons favour visible, immediate measures over interventions whose payoff is long-term, and concentrated well-organised interest groups can secure policies whose diffuse costs exceed their concentrated benefits.\n• Distortion of price signals: minimum and maximum prices, quotas and subsidies suppress the information that prices carry about scarcity and value, creating shortages, surpluses and misallocation — the Common Agricultural Policy surpluses of the 1980s are the standard example.\n• Conclusion required at the top band: the existence of market failure does not by itself justify intervention. The correct test is whether the intervention improves on the imperfect market outcome, not whether it achieves the theoretical optimum.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) at least four causes explained with developed chains and real examples, and the correct comparative test stated. L2 (6–10) causes identified, examples thin. L1 (1–5) a list.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main policies a government can use to reduce carbon emissions, and explain the economic arguments for each.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Establish the failure: greenhouse gas emissions are a negative externality of production and consumption, and the atmosphere is a global common access resource — rival in its capacity to absorb emissions, non-excludable in use. MSC exceeds MPC, so output exceeds the social optimum and a welfare loss results.\n• Carbon taxation: a Pigouvian tax set equal to the marginal external cost shifts the supply curve up to MSC and moves output to the optimum. Arguments for: price certainty helps firms plan investment; it raises revenue; it is administratively simpler than a trading scheme. Against: the government must know the social cost of carbon, and the emissions outcome is uncertain because it depends on unknown abatement costs.\n• Tradeable permits: a cap fixes the quantity of emissions, and trading equalises marginal abatement costs across firms, achieving the cap at least cost without the regulator knowing any firm\'s costs. The UK Emissions Trading Scheme operates this way, and the UK and EU agreed in May 2025 to link their schemes. Against: price volatility, because permit demand moves with the cycle against a fixed supply; over-allocation risk; administration and verification costs.\n• Regulation and standards: vehicle emissions standards, building regulations, bans on new fossil-fuel boilers, low emission zones such as the London-wide ULEZ from August 2023. Arguments for: certainty of outcome, effectiveness where consumers respond weakly to price, and simplicity of enforcement. Against: it imposes the same requirement regardless of abatement cost, so total compliance cost is higher than under a market instrument, and it gives no incentive to abate beyond the standard.\n• Subsidies for low-carbon alternatives: support for renewables, electric vehicles, heat pumps and insulation shifts supply of the clean substitute right and raises consumption towards the optimum. Against: opportunity cost of public funds, deadweight loss where the activity would have happened anyway, and producer capture of the subsidy.\n• Information and nudges: energy performance certificates, appliance labelling, default green tariffs. Cheap and choice-preserving, but the effects are small relative to the scale of the problem.\n• The international dimension, essential for top marks: emissions are a global externality, so unilateral action risks carbon leakage — production relocating to jurisdictions without a carbon price. This is the rationale for carbon border adjustment mechanisms and for international agreements, and it means no single country can solve the problem alone.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) at least four policies explained with the economic argument for each developed, accurate dated examples, and the leakage point made. L2 (6–10) policies listed with partial explanation. L1 (1–5) a list.` },

      { q: 'Explain how information failure causes market failure, and explain the policies available to correct it.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — types of information failure:\n• Imperfect information: consumers do not know the full costs or benefits of a good. Merit goods such as pensions, preventive healthcare and insulation are under-consumed because the benefits are distant, uncertain and hard to evaluate; demerit goods are over-consumed because harm is deferred and consumers discount the future heavily.\n• Asymmetric information: one party knows more than the other. Adverse selection — the used car and insurance examples — can shrink or destroy a market entirely, because the informed party\'s self-selection worsens the pool. Moral hazard changes behaviour after contracting, because one party no longer bears the full consequences of their actions.\n• Why this is allocative inefficiency: price is supposed to convey the value of a good. Where it cannot, resources flow to the wrong uses — too few pensions, too much borrowing, too little preventive care — and a welfare loss results even though no externality is present.\n\nPolicies:\n• Compulsory provision of information: labelling, energy performance certificates, health warnings, mandatory disclosure of charges, the requirement for financial products to state costs clearly. This corrects the failure at source and preserves choice. Evaluate: information only helps if people can process it, and more information can overwhelm rather than inform.\n• Regulation and licensing: professional standards for doctors, lawyers and financial advisers, product safety rules, consumer protection law. Evaluate: effective where quality genuinely cannot be assessed by a consumer, but compliance costs raise barriers to entry and can reduce competition.\n• Compulsion and defaults: automatic enrolment into workplace pensions since 2012 is the clearest UK example — it changes the default without removing the right to opt out, and raised participation far more than decades of information campaigns did. Compulsory insurance solves adverse selection by making the pool universal.\n• Direct state provision: free education and healthcare remove the need for the consumer to evaluate the benefit before consuming.\n• Private-sector solutions: warranties, brand reputation, independent certification, ratings and reviews all make quality credible without regulation. Evaluate: these work where transactions are repeated and reputation is verifiable, and fail where they are one-off.\n• Evaluation thread: information-based policies are cheap and preserve choice but often have small effects; compulsion and default-setting have much larger effects but raise a genuine question about paternalism and the value of consumer sovereignty.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) types of information failure explained and distinguished, at least three policies explained with evaluation, accurate dated examples. L2 (6–10) explanation partial. L1 (1–5) definitions.` },

      { q: 'Explain the difference between public, private and quasi-public goods, and explain why the distinction matters for government policy.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The two defining characteristics: excludability (can non-payers be prevented from consuming?) and rivalry (does one person\'s consumption reduce what is available to others?). Every good can be classified against these two dimensions, and the strongest answers set this out as a framework rather than listing examples.\n• Private goods: both excludable and rival — food, clothing, a cinema seat. Markets work: a price can be charged, willingness to pay is revealed, and the good is allocated to those who value it most. No intervention is required on these grounds alone.\n• Pure public goods: non-excludable and non-rival — national defence, flood defences, street lighting, a lighthouse. Non-excludability produces the free rider problem, so demand is understated and no profit-maximising firm supplies the good. Non-rivalry means the efficient price is zero, which cannot cover fixed costs. Result: a missing market, and provision funded through general taxation.\n• Quasi-public goods: they have the characteristics only partially, or lose them under certain conditions. An uncongested road is effectively non-rival, but once congested one driver\'s use imposes delay on others, so it becomes rival. A beach is non-excludable in practice but rival when crowded. Technology moves goods between categories — satellite tolling made roads excludable; encryption made broadcasting excludable.\n• Common access resources belong in the same framework: rival but non-excludable, producing the tragedy of the commons.\n\nWhy the distinction matters for policy:\n• It determines whether a market can exist at all. For a pure public good the policy question is how much to provide and how to fund it, not how to correct a price. For a quasi-public good, the question is whether excludability can be created cheaply enough to let a market work.\n• It determines the instrument. Road pricing works because technology made roads excludable; it could not work for national defence. Charging for a non-rival good is allocatively inefficient, because the marginal cost of an extra user is zero, so any positive price excludes someone whose benefit exceeds the cost of serving them.\n• It exposes the central difficulty of public provision: without a market to reveal valuations, the state must estimate the optimal quantity, which is why cost-benefit analysis is used for public projects and why its valuations are always contestable.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) the two-characteristic framework used analytically, all three categories explained with examples, and at least two clear policy implications developed. L2 (6–10) categories defined with examples, policy implications thin. L1 (1–5) definitions only.` },

      /* ─────────── Section A (d) / Section B (b): 25-mark evaluation ─────────── */

      { q: 'Extract B (lines 14–15) states: "Ministers must choose between taxing the polluter and simply telling firms what they may emit." Using the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the relative merits of taxation and regulation as methods of correcting negative externalities.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Set up the failure with a diagram: MSC above MPC, free market output above the social optimum, welfare loss triangle identified. Both instruments aim to move output to Q*.\n• The case for taxation — static efficiency: a Pigouvian tax equal to the marginal external cost shifts supply up to MSC and achieves the optimum exactly. Crucially, it allows each firm to decide whether to abate or to pay, so abatement happens wherever it is cheapest and the total cost of achieving a given reduction is minimised. Regulation imposes the same requirement on every firm regardless of its abatement cost.\n• The case for taxation — dynamic efficiency: a firm meeting a regulatory standard has no incentive to go further. A taxed firm pays on every remaining unit of pollution, so it has a continuing incentive to innovate. Over time this is the stronger argument.\n• The case for taxation — revenue: it raises revenue that can fund abatement, compensation or reductions in other distortionary taxes, which regulation does not.\n• The case for regulation — certainty of outcome: a tax fixes the price and leaves the quantity to be determined by unknown abatement costs. Where the externality involves a threshold — a toxin, a safety limit, an ecosystem tipping point — quantity certainty matters more than cost-efficiency, and a ban or a standard is the correct instrument.\n• The case for regulation — elasticity: where demand is highly price inelastic, a tax changes quantity very little while raising much revenue, so it internalises the externality without correcting it. Regulation acts directly on quantity regardless of elasticity.\n• The case for regulation — simplicity and information: a tax requires the government to value the externality in money; a standard requires only that it identify an acceptable level. Regulation is also easier to explain and enforce, and consumers often perceive it as fairer.\n• Distributional evaluation: indirect taxes on necessities such as fuel are regressive, taking a larger share of low incomes, which is politically and ethically significant. Regulation spreads the cost through prices too, but less visibly.\n• Government failure applies to both: valuation problems, unintended consequences (illicit trade, fly-tipping, carbon leakage), administration costs and regulatory capture.\n• Judgement: the instrument should be matched to the problem. Where the external cost can be valued and marginal abatement costs differ across firms, a tax or a permit scheme dominates; where a threshold must not be crossed, or demand is very inelastic, regulation is superior. In practice most effective policy combines them, and the strongest conclusions say so with reasons rather than sitting on the fence.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) accurate diagrams, both instruments analysed on static and dynamic efficiency, the elasticity and threshold arguments developed, distributional and government failure evaluation, conclusion following from the analysis. L4 (16–20) well-developed and mostly balanced. L3 (11–15) some analysis, balance uneven. L2 (6–10) list of advantages and disadvantages. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Extract B (lines 20–21) states: "The levy cut the sugar in soft drinks by almost half, but obesity has not fallen." Using the extracts and your knowledge of economics, assess the effectiveness of taxation in reducing obesity.', marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Establish the failure: obesity-related consumption generates negative externalities (healthcare costs borne by taxpayers, lost output) and internalities (present bias, imperfect information about long-run harm), so the free market over-consumes relative to the social optimum. Diagram: MPB above MSB, with over-consumption and a welfare loss.\n• The case for taxation — the reformulation channel: the UK Soft Drinks Industry Levy is banded by sugar content (20.8p per litre for 5g to under 8g per 100ml, 27.8p at 8g or more, zero below 5g), so it taxes the producer\'s RECIPE, not the consumer\'s choice. Producers reformulate to escape the levy, so sugar consumption falls without consumers changing behaviour at all — a design feature that explains why it outperformed a simple consumption tax.\n• The case for taxation — revenue and incentives: revenue can fund school sport and food programmes; the price signal discourages consumption at the margin; and the announced extension to milk-based drinks and the lower 4.5g threshold from January 2028 show the design being tightened in response to evidence.\n• The case against — substitution: consumers can switch to untaxed sources of calories such as confectionery, cakes or juices. Obesity depends on total calorie balance, so a tax on one category can leave it unchanged, which is consistent with the extract.\n• The case against — elasticity and targeting: demand for food is price inelastic overall, and the heaviest consumers may be the least responsive. A tax that changes moderate consumers\' behaviour but not heavy consumers\' misses the population generating most of the external cost.\n• The case against — distribution: food and drink taxes are regressive, taking a larger share of low incomes, and obesity prevalence is higher in lower-income groups, so the tax falls hardest on those least able to bear it. This is the strongest equity objection.\n• The counterfactual point: obesity was rising before the levy, so the right test is whether it rose by less than it would have. Absence of a fall is not evidence of no effect, and saying so is a mark of a high-level answer.\n• Alternatives evaluated: advertising restrictions (change demand rather than price, and reach children who do not respond to price at all); mandatory reformulation targets (certainty of outcome, but no flexibility on cost); calorie labelling and information (cheap, choice-preserving, small effects); subsidising fruit and vegetables (addresses the substitute rather than the target, and is progressive); planning restrictions on fast food outlets near schools.\n• Judgement: taxation is demonstrably effective at changing what producers make and much weaker at changing what consumers eat overall. A defensible conclusion is that a single-category tax cannot solve a whole-diet problem, that broadening the base is the logical next step, and that taxation belongs within a package rather than as the lead instrument.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) the failure modelled, the reformulation mechanism analysed precisely, substitution, elasticity, distribution and the counterfactual all evaluated, alternatives weighed, judgement supported. L4 (16–20) well-developed and balanced. L3 (11–15) some evaluation. L2 (6–10) one-sided. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Discuss whether tradeable pollution permits are a better instrument than a carbon tax for reducing greenhouse gas emissions.', marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Explain both instruments accurately, with diagrams: a tax shifts the supply curve up by the tax per unit, fixing the PRICE of emitting; a permit scheme sets a vertical supply of permits at the cap, fixing the QUANTITY and letting the market determine the price.\n• The case for permits — certainty of outcome: climate targets are quantity targets. A cap guarantees the environmental result whatever happens to abatement costs; a tax set too low simply raises revenue while emissions continue.\n• The case for permits — least-cost abatement without information: trading continues until every firm\'s marginal abatement cost equals the permit price, so any given cap is achieved at minimum total cost, and the regulator never needs to know any firm\'s costs. The UK Emissions Trading Scheme works this way, and the UK and EU agreed in May 2025 to link their schemes, which widens the market and improves cost-efficiency further.\n• The case for permits — political economy: free initial allocation buys the consent of incumbent emitters, which is why cap-and-trade has proved easier to introduce than an equivalent tax. Evaluate: free allocation also hands firms a valuable asset and forgoes revenue.\n• The case for a carbon tax — price certainty: firms making 20-year investment decisions need to know the future carbon price. Permit prices are volatile, because permit demand moves with the business cycle against a perfectly inelastic supply, so a recession collapses the price and the abatement incentive with it. The early EU scheme is the standard example of over-allocation destroying the price signal.\n• The case for a carbon tax — simplicity, coverage and revenue: a tax can cover millions of small emitters that a permit market cannot practically include, costs far less to administer, and raises revenue that can fund abatement or offset regressive effects.\n• The case for a carbon tax — no windfall: it avoids handing free permits to incumbents and avoids the gaming and monitoring problems of allocation.\n• Common weaknesses to evaluate: both require the government to choose a level — a tax rate or a cap — and both risk carbon leakage, where production relocates to jurisdictions without a carbon price. This is the argument for carbon border adjustment mechanisms and for international linkage, and it means neither instrument works well unilaterally.\n• Hybrid designs as the sophisticated answer: price floors and ceilings inside a permit scheme, or a tax with an emissions cap backstop, capture much of both. Most real schemes have converged on hybrids for exactly this reason.\n• Judgement: the choice turns on whether price certainty or quantity certainty matters more, which in turn depends on how steep the damage function is around the target. For a binding climate target, quantity certainty has the stronger claim; for broad coverage of small emitters, a tax does. A well-supported conclusion in either direction, or in favour of a hybrid, can reach L5.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) both instruments modelled accurately with diagrams, the price/quantity certainty trade-off developed, volatility and leakage evaluated, hybrid designs considered, judgement supported. L4 (16–20) well-developed and balanced. L3 (11–15) description with some evaluation. L2 (6–10) list. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that government intervention to correct market failure usually makes the allocation of resources worse rather than better.', marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Frame the question correctly, which is itself a high-level move: the test is not whether intervention achieves the theoretical optimum, but whether it improves on the imperfect market outcome. Comparing a real government with a perfect market, or a real market with a perfect government, is the error the question invites.\n• The case that intervention worsens allocation — information failure: setting a Pigouvian tax requires knowing the money value of the marginal external cost, which is unobservable. Valuing carbon, noise or a life involves contested judgement, so the instrument is almost certainly mis-set. A tax that is too high creates a new welfare loss from under-production.\n• Unintended consequences with evidence: a landfill tax raises fly-tipping; high tobacco duty grows an illicit trade that escapes both tax and safety regulation; rent controls reduce maintenance and new supply and worsen labour mobility; demand-side housing subsidies are capitalised into rents where supply is inelastic.\n• Administrative costs, capture and political incentives: monitoring and enforcement absorb real resources; regulators can be captured by the concentrated interests they regulate; short electoral horizons favour visible measures over effective ones; and concentrated lobbies can secure policies whose diffuse costs exceed their concentrated benefits.\n• Distortion of price signals: minimum and maximum prices and quotas suppress the information prices carry about scarcity, creating shortages, surpluses and misallocation — the Common Agricultural Policy surpluses are the standard illustration.\n• The case that intervention improves allocation — cases where the market produces nothing at all: pure public goods are a missing market, not an inefficient one. No amount of market refinement produces a flood defence, so intervention cannot make allocation worse than a market that does not exist.\n• Demonstrated successes: the Soft Drinks Industry Levy cut sugar in taxed drinks by around half through producer reformulation; automatic enrolment into workplace pensions since 2012 raised participation far more than information campaigns had; emissions trading has delivered measured reductions in line with tightening caps; the ban on leaded petrol and the 2007 indoor smoking ban produced large, measurable health gains at modest cost.\n• Design matters more than the principle: market-based instruments that work WITH the price mechanism — taxes, permits, subsidies, property rights, nudges — retain the information and incentive advantages of markets while correcting the divergence. Command-and-control instruments that override prices are the ones most prone to the failures above. This distinction is the strongest analytical route through the question.\n• Judgement: "usually" is the word to attack. The evidence supports the weaker claim — that intervention frequently falls short and sometimes does net harm — but not the stronger one that it usually makes things worse. A defensible conclusion is that the case for intervention is strongest where the market fails completely or where the externality is large and measurable, and weakest where the failure is small and the information requirements are severe.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) the comparative test stated explicitly, both sides developed with real dated examples, the instrument-design distinction drawn, judgement that engages with "usually". L4 (16–20) well-developed and mostly balanced. L3 (11–15) some analysis. L2 (6–10) list of government failures. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that the state should provide merit goods such as healthcare and education free at the point of use rather than subsidising private provision.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Establish why intervention of some kind is justified: healthcare and education are merit goods — under-consumed because of imperfect information about long-run benefits and present bias — and they generate substantial positive externalities. Diagram: MSB above MPB, free market consumption below the social optimum, welfare loss triangle.\n• The case for free direct provision — equity and access: free at the point of use removes price as a barrier entirely, so consumption does not depend on ability to pay. Because access to healthcare and education strongly affects lifetime earnings, this raises social mobility and reduces inequality of original income in the next generation, not just of disposable income now.\n• The case for free provision — information: the consumer of healthcare cannot evaluate what treatment they need, so asymmetric information between doctor and patient means a market with prices would produce supplier-induced demand. Removing the price removes the incentive to over-treat for profit.\n• The case for free provision — insurance market failure: private health insurance suffers adverse selection, so the highest-risk individuals are priced out. Universal provision solves this by making the pool universal.\n• The case for free provision — economies of scale and monopsony: a single national purchaser can negotiate lower prices for drugs and equipment, reducing cost.\n• The case against — no price signal: with a zero price, quantity demanded exceeds the quantity a fixed budget can supply, so the service must be rationed by waiting lists, clinical prioritisation or administrative rules rather than by price. Excess demand is not eliminated by making the good free; it is made invisible.\n• The case against — productive efficiency: a state monopoly faces no competitive pressure, so costs may drift above the minimum and innovation may be slower. Subsidising private provision preserves competition between providers while still correcting the externality.\n• The case against — opportunity cost and moral hazard: free provision is expensive, with a large opportunity cost in forgone spending elsewhere and deadweight loss from the taxes that fund it. Zero price may encourage over-use of services where consumption is discretionary.\n• Middle options that a strong answer will raise: state funding with plural provision, vouchers, co-payments with exemptions for low-income groups, and regulated competition. Each tries to combine the equity of universal funding with the efficiency incentives of competitive supply. Evaluate: co-payments deter low-value use but also deter necessary use by exactly the people the policy is meant to help.\n• Judgement: the strongest conclusions separate FUNDING from PROVISION. The equity and insurance arguments are arguments about funding, and are very strong; the efficiency arguments are arguments about provision, and point the other way. A defensible position is universal public funding with competitive or plural provision, subject to strong regulation — and the answer should say why, not merely assert it.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) the funding/provision distinction made and used, both sides developed with diagrams and chains of reasoning, middle options evaluated, judgement supported. L4 (16–20) well-developed and mostly balanced. L3 (11–15) partial. L2 (6–10) list. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Extract B (lines 27–28) states: "The charge applies to every vehicle entering the zone in the charging hours." Using the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the case for using road pricing to reduce urban congestion.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Establish the failure with a diagram: each driver bears the private cost of a journey but imposes delay, pollution, noise and accident risk on others. MSC exceeds MPC, so road use at peak times exceeds the social optimum and a welfare loss results. Road space at peak time is a common access resource — rival once congested, but non-excludable without charging technology.\n• The case for road pricing — it internalises the externality directly: a charge set equal to the marginal external cost raises the private cost of the journey to MSC, so journeys whose benefit is below the social cost no longer take place. Unlike a fuel duty, it can vary by time and place, which matters because the externality is entirely time- and location-specific.\n• The case for — allocative efficiency and rationing by value: road space is scarce at peak times. Without a price it is rationed by queuing, which wastes time and produces no revenue. A charge rations by willingness to pay, transferring the same scarcity into revenue that can fund public transport.\n• The case for — evidence: the London congestion charge, introduced in 2003, reduced traffic entering the zone and raised revenue hypothecated to transport. The London-wide ULEZ from August 2023 shows the same instrument applied to an air quality externality.\n• The case against — equity: willingness to pay reflects ability to pay, so a flat charge falls hardest on lower-income drivers, who may have least access to alternatives and least flexibility over working hours. Rationing by price is efficient but not equitable, and this tension is the core of the question.\n• The case against — availability of substitutes: the charge only reallocates journeys efficiently if a realistic alternative exists. Where public transport is inadequate, demand is price inelastic, so the charge raises revenue and imposes cost without cutting congestion much. Sequencing matters — invest first, charge second.\n• The case against — boundary and displacement effects: traffic diverts to roads just outside the zone, shifting the externality rather than removing it, and businesses just inside the boundary may lose trade.\n• The case against — information and administration: setting the charge equal to the true marginal external cost requires information the authority does not have, and camera and enforcement infrastructure is costly. Over-charging creates a new welfare loss from journeys that should have taken place.\n• Alternatives evaluated: investment in public transport capacity (addresses the substitute, but expensive and slow, and induced demand can refill the road space); workplace parking levies (cheaper to administer, blunter); bus lanes and road space reallocation (change relative costs without a charge); and remote working, which has reduced peak demand structurally.\n• Judgement: road pricing is the instrument that matches the failure most precisely, because the externality varies by time and place and only a variable charge can track it. The decisive qualifications are distributional and about substitutes, both of which are design problems rather than objections in principle — so a defensible conclusion supports road pricing with revenue recycling into public transport and exemptions or discounts for low-income and essential users.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) the externality modelled with a diagram, the time-and-place argument developed, equity and substitute availability evaluated, alternatives weighed, judgement supported. L4 (16–20) well-developed and mostly balanced. L3 (11–15) some evaluation. L2 (6–10) description. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that extending property rights is the most effective way to correct environmental market failure.', marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Explain the underlying diagnosis: environmental externalities exist because the resource is unowned and therefore unpriced. Nobody owns clean air, a fish stock or the atmosphere, so nobody can charge for damaging it, and the price signal that would ration its use is missing. On this analysis the failure is not "markets" but absent markets.\n• The Coase theorem: where property rights are clearly defined and transaction costs are low, the parties can bargain to an efficient outcome regardless of who initially holds the right — the polluter pays to pollute, or the victim pays the polluter to stop, but the efficient quantity is the same. The initial allocation determines the distribution of the gains, not the efficiency of the outcome. This is a genuinely powerful result and should be explained precisely, not asserted.\n• The case for — it uses the market rather than replacing it: no regulator needs to value the externality in money, because the bargaining parties reveal their valuations. This sidesteps the information problem that undermines Pigouvian taxes.\n• The case for — evidence: individual transferable quotas in fisheries have halted over-exploitation where they have been well enforced; tradeable emissions permits are property rights in emissions and achieve a cap at least cost; land titling, private ownership of forests and water abstraction rights all internalise depletion costs by giving one party a stake in the future value of the asset.\n• The case against — transaction costs: the Coase result depends on low transaction costs, and in most real environmental problems they are enormous. Climate change involves billions of emitters and billions of affected parties across every jurisdiction on earth; the parties cannot identify each other, let alone bargain.\n• The case against — enforcement and divisibility: rights are meaningless without enforcement. Migratory fish stocks, groundwater, the deep ocean and the atmosphere cross jurisdictions and cannot be physically divided or monitored cheaply. Where the state is weak, assigning a right on paper changes nothing.\n• The case against — distribution and legitimacy: assigning property rights hands a valuable asset to whoever receives it. Granting a firm the right to pollute, or enclosing a resource communities have used for generations, raises serious equity questions the efficiency argument does not address.\n• The case against — irreversibility and future generations: future people cannot bargain today, and extinction or ecosystem collapse cannot be undone, so a bargained outcome between present parties may be efficient for them and disastrous overall.\n• Alternatives compared: taxation (needs valuation, but scales to millions of emitters), regulation (certainty of outcome, higher total abatement cost), international agreement (the only route for global commons, but weakly enforced).\n• Judgement: extending property rights is the most effective instrument for LOCAL, DIVISIBLE, ENFORCEABLE resources — fisheries, forests, water, land — where the number of parties is small enough for bargaining or trading to work. It is close to useless for global, indivisible commons, which is where the largest environmental failures lie. A defensible conclusion therefore rejects "most effective" in general while defending it in a clearly specified class of cases.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) Coase explained accurately with its conditions, both the successful and the unworkable cases developed with real examples, alternatives compared, judgement that specifies the conditions. L4 (16–20) well-developed and mostly balanced. L3 (11–15) Coase described with limited evaluation. L2 (6–10) assertion. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Discuss whether cost-benefit analysis is a reliable basis for deciding whether a major public investment project should go ahead.', marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Explain the method: cost-benefit analysis identifies all private AND external costs and benefits of a project, attaches money values to them, discounts future flows to present values, and compares them. A project passes if the present value of benefits exceeds the present value of costs, or equivalently if the benefit-cost ratio exceeds 1.\n• The case for — it is the only systematic alternative to guesswork: public goods and major infrastructure have no market price to reveal social valuation, so without CBA the decision rests on political judgement alone. CBA forces every assumption to be stated, quantified and open to challenge, which is a discipline on decision-makers.\n• The case for — it captures externalities: a purely financial appraisal would ignore noise, emissions, landscape, journey-time savings and accident reductions. CBA counts them, so it aligns the decision with allocative efficiency rather than with the promoter\'s cash flow.\n• The case for — consistency: applying the same framework across competing projects allows scarce capital to be allocated to the highest-value uses, which is precisely the resource allocation question.\n• The case against — valuation of intangibles: what is the money value of a life, an hour of commuting time, an ancient woodland or a species? Values are imputed from revealed or stated preference studies, both of which are contestable, and small changes in these assumptions can flip a marginal project.\n• The case against — the discount rate: benefits of long-lived infrastructure accrue over decades while costs are immediate, so the result is extremely sensitive to the discount rate chosen. A higher rate systematically disadvantages long-term projects and, importantly, future generations — which is central to the appraisal of climate and flood defence projects.\n• The case against — optimism bias and strategic behaviour: promoters have an incentive to understate costs and overstate benefits to get a project approved, and the record of major infrastructure cost overruns internationally is consistent with this. Once a figure is required, it can be produced to order.\n• The case against — distribution: CBA aggregates gains and losses across people, so a project can pass even though the gains accrue to one group and the losses to another. Efficiency and equity are different tests, and CBA only performs one of them.\n• The case against — uncertainty: 60-year forecasts of demand, technology and costs carry error bars far wider than the margin by which most projects pass. Sensitivity analysis and scenario testing mitigate this but do not remove it.\n• Judgement: CBA is best understood as a structured way of organising the argument rather than as a machine that produces the answer. Its value lies in making assumptions explicit and comparable; its danger lies in the false precision of a single number. A defensible conclusion is that CBA should inform the decision alongside distributional analysis and sensitivity testing, and that a benefit-cost ratio close to 1 should be treated as no evidence either way.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) the method explained accurately, at least four limitations developed with reasoning, the discount rate and distribution points made, judgement that positions CBA rather than simply accepting or rejecting it. L4 (16–20) well-developed and balanced. L3 (11–15) method described with some limitations. L2 (6–10) list. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that behavioural "nudges" are a more effective form of government intervention than taxes and regulation.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Explain the behavioural diagnosis: conventional analysis assumes rational utility maximisers with full information. Behavioural economics identifies bounded rationality, present bias, anchoring, loss aversion, herd behaviour and status quo bias, which mean consumers systematically make choices they themselves would not endorse on reflection. Where the failure is a failure of DECISION-MAKING rather than of prices, changing choice architecture may correct it more directly than changing prices.\n• The case for nudges — evidence: automatic enrolment into workplace pensions from 2012 raised participation dramatically by changing the default while preserving the right to opt out. Decades of information campaigns and tax reliefs had achieved far less. This is the strongest single piece of evidence and should be used.\n• The case for nudges — cost: they are extraordinarily cheap relative to subsidies or enforcement, so even a small effect can have a very high benefit-cost ratio.\n• The case for nudges — libertarian paternalism: choice is preserved, so the policy avoids the welfare loss and political resistance of a ban, and avoids the regressive distributional effect of a tax on a necessity.\n• The case against — scale: nudge effects are typically small and sometimes fade once novelty passes. Where the market failure is very large, as with carbon emissions, a small behavioural shift cannot substitute for a price on the externality. Tax and permit instruments scale; nudges generally do not.\n• The case against — they do not internalise the externality: a nudge changes behaviour without changing relative prices, so the underlying divergence between MSC and MPC remains. If the nudge stops working, the failure returns in full. A tax changes the incentive permanently and raises revenue besides.\n• The case against — replication and measurement: several prominent behavioural findings have proved difficult to replicate, and evaluating a nudge requires a counterfactual that is often absent. Policy should be cautious about generalising from a single trial.\n• The ethical objection: nudges work partly because people do not notice them, which raises questions about transparency and manipulation that a visible tax does not. Defenders answer that some default must be chosen anyway, so the question is which default, not whether to have one.\n• The matching principle, which is the strongest analytical route: the instrument should match the failure. Information failure and inertia call for information, defaults and choice architecture. Externalities call for prices — taxes or permits — because only a price internalises a cost. Threshold risks call for regulation. Ranking the instruments in the abstract is the error the question invites.\n• Judgement: nudges are a valuable and cheap ADDITION to the toolkit and are frequently the best instrument for inertia-driven failures, but they cannot replace price-based correction of externalities. A defensible conclusion rejects the ranking and specifies which failures each instrument suits.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) behavioural concepts used accurately, real dated evidence deployed, limits of scale and of externality correction developed, the matching principle argued, judgement supported. L4 (16–20) well-developed and mostly balanced. L3 (11–15) nudges described with some evaluation. L2 (6–10) list of examples. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Extract B (lines 35–36) states: "Without a price on carbon, firms treat the atmosphere as a free dump." Using the extracts and your knowledge of economics, assess whether market-based instruments alone can deliver the UK\'s emissions targets.', marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Frame the failure: greenhouse gas emissions are a negative externality and the atmosphere is a global common access resource — rival in absorptive capacity, non-excludable in use. Without a price, each emitter ignores the cost imposed on everyone else, so emissions exceed the social optimum. Diagram: MSC above MPC with the welfare loss triangle.\n• The case that market-based instruments can do it — efficiency: a carbon price, whether a tax or a permit price, equalises marginal abatement costs across every emitter, so any target is met at the lowest possible total cost. The regulator needs no information about individual firms. The UK Emissions Trading Scheme delivers this for covered sectors, and the May 2025 agreement to link the UK and EU schemes widens the market and improves cost-efficiency further.\n• The case that they can do it — dynamic efficiency: a rising, credible carbon price makes low-carbon capital profitable and rewards innovation continuously, unlike a standard, which gives no incentive to go beyond compliance.\n• The case against — coverage: trading schemes cover large industrial and power installations. Household heating, private transport, agriculture and land use are enormous sources that a permit market cannot practically include, and where consumers respond weakly to price. Regulation — building standards, vehicle standards, boiler phase-outs, the London-wide ULEZ from August 2023 — reaches them directly.\n• The case against — inelastic demand and the absence of substitutes: a price only reallocates behaviour if an alternative exists. Where there is no low-carbon option — aviation, cement, steel — a carbon price raises costs without cutting emissions much, and simply relocates production. Public investment in the alternative technology must come first, and the market will not fund basic research because the returns are non-excludable: innovation itself is a public good.\n• The case against — infrastructure and coordination: the transition requires grid capacity, charging networks and hydrogen or carbon storage infrastructure, all of which have network externalities and coordination problems that no individual firm has an incentive to solve. This is a structural argument for public investment alongside pricing.\n• The case against — distribution and political sustainability: carbon pricing is regressive, since energy takes a larger share of low incomes. A policy that is not politically sustainable cannot deliver a target that requires decades of consistency, so revenue recycling and targeted support are not optional extras but conditions of success.\n• The case against — leakage: a national carbon price raises the incentive to relocate production to uncovered jurisdictions, which reduces measured domestic emissions without reducing global ones. Border adjustment mechanisms and international linkage are the responses, and both require regulation and diplomacy rather than markets.\n• Judgement: market-based instruments are necessary and are the most cost-effective core of any credible strategy, but they are not sufficient. The gaps — coverage of diffuse sources, absent substitutes, infrastructure coordination, innovation as a public good, and distributional sustainability — are precisely the areas where markets fail, so the answer is a package with a carbon price at its centre. Alternative supported conclusions are creditable.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) the failure modelled, market instruments analysed on static and dynamic efficiency, at least three substantive gaps developed, dated evidence, judgement following from the analysis. L4 (16–20) well-developed and mostly balanced. L3 (11–15) some analysis. L2 (6–10) description of policies. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },
    ]
  },
  '3.2.1': {
    name: 'Measurement of Macroeconomic Performance',
    questions: [

      /* ─────────── Section A part (a): 2-mark calculations ─────────── */

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\n  Nominal GDP, 2026:           £2 800 billion\n  GDP deflator, 2026 (2015 = 100): 116.0\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate to two decimal places real GDP in 2026 at 2015 prices. Give your answer in £ billion.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: real GDP = nominal GDP / (GDP deflator / 100).\n2 800 / 1.16 = 2 413.7931… = £2 413.79 billion.\n\n2 marks for £2 413.79 billion. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for multiplying rather than dividing by the deflator (£3 248 billion). The economics: the deflator of 116.0 means the general price level is 16% higher than in 2015, so roughly 16% of the nominal figure reflects price increases rather than extra output. Only the real figure measures the volume of goods and services actually produced.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\n  Consumer Prices Index, August 2025: 132.6\n  Consumer Prices Index, August 2026: 136.7\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate to two decimal places the rate of CPI inflation in the 12 months to August 2026.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: inflation rate = (change in the index / the earlier index) x 100.\nChange = 136.7 – 132.6 = 4.1.\n4.1 / 132.6 x 100 = 3.0920… = 3.09%.\n\n2 marks for 3.09%. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for subtracting the index values and calling 4.1 the inflation rate. The official figure was that CPI rose by 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026, up from 2.9% the previous month, with the largest upward contributions coming from petrol and diesel prices — so this practice calculation is realistic. Note that a falling inflation RATE still means prices are rising, only more slowly.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question, using the official mid-2026 pattern\n  People in employment:  33.38 million\n  People unemployed:      1.72 million\n  Economically inactive people of working age: 8.84 million\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate to two decimal places the unemployment rate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: the unemployment rate is unemployed people as a percentage of the LABOUR FORCE (employed + unemployed), NOT of the whole population. The economically inactive are excluded by definition.\nLabour force = 33.38 + 1.72 = 35.10 million.\n1.72 / 35.10 x 100 = 4.9003… = 4.90%.\n\n2 marks for 4.90%. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip. 0 marks for including the economically inactive in the denominator (1.72 / 43.94 = 3.91%) unless the correct method is shown alongside — this is the single most common error on this calculation. The official UK unemployment rate was 4.9% for May to July 2026, with employment at 75.1% and inactivity at 20.9%.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — the figures follow the official Quarter 4 2025 pattern\n  UK current account deficit (including precious metals): £18.4 billion\n  UK nominal GDP for the quarter:                        £766.7 billion\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate to two decimal places the current account deficit as a percentage of GDP.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: deficit as a share of GDP = deficit / GDP x 100.\n18.4 / 766.7 x 100 = 2.3999… = 2.40%.\n\n2 marks for 2.40% (accept 2.4%). 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip. Context: the UK current account deficit including precious metals widened by £7.7 billion to £18.4 billion, or 2.4% of GDP, in Quarter 4 2025, while the underlying deficit excluding precious metals narrowed to £8.4 billion, or 1.1% of GDP. Over the whole of 2024 the deficit was £63.2 billion, or 2.2% of GDP, down from 3.6% in 2023. Expressing the deficit as a share of GDP rather than in cash terms is essential for comparison over time and between countries.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\n  Real GDP, 2026:   £2 420 billion\n  Population, 2026:  69.2 million\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate to two decimal places real GDP per capita in 2026. Give your answer in pounds.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: GDP per capita = real GDP / population. Convert the units carefully — billions and millions.\n£2 420 000 000 000 / 69 200 000 = £34 971.0982… = £34 971.10.\n\n2 marks for £34 971.10. 1 mark for the correct method with a units error (a common slip is £34.97 or £34 971 000). The economics: GDP per capita is a better indicator of average living standards than total GDP, because a country whose GDP grows 2% while its population grows 2.5% has a FALLING average income despite positive headline growth. It still says nothing about distribution, which is the standard evaluation point.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\n  Index of export prices (2020 = 100), 2026: 108\n  Index of import prices (2020 = 100), 2026: 120\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate to two decimal places the terms of trade index for 2026.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: terms of trade = (index of export prices / index of import prices) x 100.\n108 / 120 x 100 = 90.00.\n\n2 marks for 90.00. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for inverting the ratio (111.11). Interpretation, creditable as a comment: the index has fallen from 100 to 90, a 10% DETERIORATION in the terms of trade. Each unit of exports now buys 10% fewer imports than in 2020, so a given volume of exports finances fewer imports — which worsens the trade balance in value terms even if export volumes are unchanged.` },

      /* ─────────── Section A part (b): 4-mark data questions ─────────── */

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\n  Nominal GDP (£bn):        2024 = 2 620 ; 2026 = 2 800\n  GDP deflator (2015 = 100): 2024 = 110.5 ; 2026 = 116.0\n  Population (millions):     2024 = 68.6  ; 2026 = 69.2\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that the headline growth in nominal GDP overstates the improvement in average living standards.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Nominal GDP rose 6.87% (from 2 620 to 2 800), which is the headline figure (1 mark).\n• Deflate both years: real GDP in 2024 = 2 620 / 1.105 = £2 371.04bn; in 2026 = 2 800 / 1.16 = £2 413.79bn. Real growth is therefore 1.80%, so more than three-quarters of the nominal increase was price rises, not extra output (1–2 marks for correct deflation).\n• Adjust for population: real GDP per capita rose from £34 563 to £34 881, a rise of just 0.92%. Population growth of 0.87% absorbed roughly half of the real growth (1 mark).\n• Explain the chain: nominal to real removes inflation, which measures the change in the VOLUME of output; real to real per capita removes population change, which measures the change in output available per person. Only the last figure bears on average living standards, and even it says nothing about distribution, leisure, environmental quality or unpaid work (1 mark).\n\n4 marks: deflation AND the population adjustment both carried out, with the chain explained. 3 marks: deflation correct, population point asserted. 2 marks: one adjustment done correctly. 1 mark: figures restated.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question; the headline rates are the official 2026 figures\nContributions to the annual CPI inflation rate, UK\n  CPI inflation, 12 months to June 2026:   2.6%\n  CPI inflation, 12 months to August 2026: 3.1%\n  Contribution of motor fuels (percentage points): June = –0.1 ; August = +0.5\n  Contribution of food and non-alcoholic drinks (pp): June = +0.4 ; August = +0.4\n  Contribution of services (pp):                      June = +1.6 ; August = +1.5\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that the rise in inflation between June and August 2026 was driven by one component.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Quantify the change: headline inflation rose 0.5 percentage points, from 2.6% to 3.1%, a relative increase of 19.2% (1 mark).\n• Identify the driver: the motor fuels contribution moved from –0.1 to +0.5 percentage points, a swing of 0.6 percentage points — larger than the entire change in the headline rate (1 mark).\n• Show the offsetting movement: services fell 0.1 points and food was unchanged, so the other components together subtracted 0.1 points. The arithmetic is therefore +0.6 – 0.1 = +0.5, which matches the headline change exactly and confirms fuel as the driver (1 mark).\n• Explain the economics: this is a cost-push contribution arising from a change in a global commodity price, not from domestic demand. It is a change in a relative price working through the index, and the effect drops out of the annual rate in twelve months unless prices keep rising — a base effect. Core inflation, which excludes energy and food, is the measure the Bank of England watches for exactly this reason (1 mark).\n\n4 marks: the driver identified using the contributions arithmetic AND the cost-push/base-effect economics explained. Maximum 2 marks for spotting fuel without the arithmetic.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nTwo measures of UK unemployment\n  Labour Force Survey unemployment: 1.72 million ; rate 4.9%\n  Claimant count:                   1.58 million\n  Economically inactive who want a job: 1.71 million\n  People working part time who want a full-time job: 0.94 million\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that the headline unemployment rate understates the amount of spare capacity in the labour market.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Manipulate: adding the 1.71 million inactive who want a job to the 1.72 million LFS unemployed more than doubles the figure, to 3.43 million. Adding the 0.94 million underemployed part-timers gives 4.37 million, which is 2.54 times the headline figure (1–2 marks).\n• Explain the LFS definition: to be counted as unemployed a person must be out of work, available to start within two weeks and have actively sought work in the past four weeks. Someone who has given up searching is classified as economically INACTIVE and disappears from both the numerator and the denominator, so discouraged workers lower the measured rate (1 mark).\n• Explain underemployment: a person working one hour a week counts as employed. Hours and pay are therefore not captured, so labour market slack can exist inside the employed count (1 mark).\n• Explain the claimant count difference: it records only those claiming unemployment-related benefits, so it excludes those who do not qualify or do not claim, and it changes whenever eligibility rules change — which is why it is not comparable over long periods, and why the LFS/ILO measure is used internationally (1 mark).\n• Caveat worth credit: the ONS has warned that Labour Force Survey estimates are less reliable than usual because of falling response rates, so both measures carry uncertainty.\n\n4 marks: figures combined AND at least two definitional reasons explained.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question, following the official 2025 pattern\nUK current account, one quarter (£ billion)\n  Trade in goods:   –55.5\n  Trade in services: +53.3\n  Primary income:    –2.7\n  Secondary income: –13.5\n  Current account balance: –18.4\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show which components are responsible for the UK current account deficit.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Check and use the arithmetic: –55.5 + 53.3 – 2.7 – 13.5 = –18.4, confirming the four components sum to the balance (1 mark).\n• Identify the dominant component: the goods deficit of £55.5bn is three times the size of the overall deficit. The services surplus of £53.3bn offsets 96.0% of it, leaving a total trade deficit of only £2.2bn (1 mark).\n• Explain the structural pattern: the UK has a comparative advantage in high-value services — finance, insurance, legal, education, business and professional services — and imports a large share of manufactured goods, energy and food. The current account deficit is therefore a structural feature of that specialisation, not a short-run aberration (1 mark).\n• Complete the picture: with trade nearly balanced, the deficit is explained mainly by the secondary income deficit of £13.5bn (transfers, including overseas aid and remittances) and the primary income deficit of £2.7bn (net investment income paid to foreign owners of UK assets). Many students wrongly assume a current account deficit means "we import too much"; these data show that is not the whole story (1 mark).\n\n4 marks: the components decomposed accurately AND the structural explanation given. Maximum 2 marks for identifying the goods deficit alone.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nFour economies, 2026\n  Country A: GDP per capita (PPP) $61 000 ; life expectancy 79 years ; mean years of schooling 13.4 ; HDI 0.930\n  Country B: GDP per capita (PPP) $58 000 ; life expectancy 84 years ; mean years of schooling 13.1 ; HDI 0.948\n  Country C: GDP per capita (PPP) $47 000 ; life expectancy 82 years ; mean years of schooling 12.8 ; HDI 0.931\n  Country D: GDP per capita (PPP) $72 000 ; life expectancy 76 years ; mean years of schooling 11.2 ; HDI 0.901\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that GDP per capita is an incomplete measure of development.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Identify the inconsistency precisely: Country D has the highest GDP per capita, 18.0% above A and 53.2% above C, but the LOWEST HDI of the four. Country C has 34.7% lower GDP per capita than D yet a higher HDI (1–2 marks).\n• Explain what the HDI adds: it combines income with life expectancy (a health dimension) and schooling (an education dimension), so it captures whether income has been converted into human development. D\'s life expectancy is 8 years below B\'s and its mean schooling 1.9 years below, which is why it ranks last despite its income.\n• Explain the economics: income is a MEANS to development, not development itself. How far income raises welfare depends on the distribution of that income and on whether it is spent on health and education — which GDP per capita cannot show (1 mark).\n• Complete the evaluation: the HDI is itself incomplete. It ignores inequality (the inequality-adjusted HDI was created for this), the environment, political freedom, gender inequality and subjective wellbeing, and its weighting of the three components is a value judgement rather than a finding (1 mark).\n\n4 marks: the inconsistency quantified AND both what HDI adds and what it still omits explained. Maximum 2 marks for "GDP does not measure happiness".` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question, following the official mid-2026 pattern\nUK labour market, percentages of the relevant population aged 16–64\n  Employment rate:           2025 = 75.2 ; 2026 = 75.1\n  Unemployment rate:         2025 = 4.7  ; 2026 = 4.9\n  Economic inactivity rate:  2025 = 21.0 ; 2026 = 20.9\n  Number unemployed (000s):  2025 = 1 637 ; 2026 = 1 720\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that the rise in unemployment came mainly from a fall in inactivity rather than from job losses.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditable points:\n• Manipulate: unemployment rose by 83 000, or 5.07%, and the unemployment rate rose 0.2 percentage points. But the employment rate fell only 0.1 percentage points, and the inactivity rate also fell, by 0.1 points (1–2 marks).\n• Explain the accounting identity: every person aged 16–64 is employed, unemployed or inactive, and the three rates are measured against different bases but must be consistent. If the inactivity rate falls while the employment rate is almost unchanged, people leaving inactivity have mostly joined the UNEMPLOYED, not the employed (1 mark).\n• Explain the economics and why it matters: someone moving from inactivity to active job search is counted as unemployed for the first time, so the measured unemployment rate rises even though no one lost a job. Rising participation is generally a sign of an improving labour market or of financial pressure to seek work, not of a deteriorating one — so the headline rate can move in the opposite direction to the underlying story (1 mark).\n• Caveat: the ONS has warned that Labour Force Survey estimates are currently less reliable than usual because of response rate problems and a data collection error in 2026, so small movements of 0.1 or 0.2 percentage points may not be statistically significant. A top answer says the data cannot bear the weight of a firm conclusion (1 mark).\n\n4 marks: the three rates reconciled AND the inactivity-to-unemployment flow explained, with the reliability caveat. Maximum 2 marks for restating the figures.` },

      /* ─────────── Section A part (c): 9-mark diagram/analysis questions ─────────── */

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 7–8) states: "CPI inflation rose to 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026, above the Bank of England\'s 2% target." With the help of an AD/AS diagram, explain how demand-pull inflation occurs.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: price level on the vertical axis, real GDP on the horizontal. Draw AD1 and an upward-sloping SRAS, with LRAS vertical at full capacity. Shift AD right to AD2. The equilibrium price level rises from P1 to P2 and real output rises from Y1 to Y2.\n• The crucial refinement: the steeper the AS curve, the more of the AD increase appears as inflation rather than output. Where the economy is well below capacity, AS is relatively flat and the effect is mostly on output; near or at full capacity, AS is steep or vertical and the whole effect is on the price level. The size of the inflationary effect therefore depends on the output gap.\n• Chain of reasoning on causes: AD = C + I + G + (X – M), so demand-pull inflation can originate in a consumption boom (rising confidence, higher real incomes, cheaper credit), an investment boom, a fiscal expansion, a depreciation raising net exports, or rapid monetary growth. With Bank Rate held at 3.75% in September 2026 while inflation ran at 3.1%, the real interest rate was around 0.65%, which is not restrictive.\n• Link to the extract with a qualification: the August 2026 rise was driven largely by petrol and diesel prices, which is a supply-side cost-push contribution, not demand-pull. A high-level answer distinguishes the two and notes that the diagrammatic signature differs — demand-pull raises the price level AND output, whereas cost-push raises the price level while output falls.\n• Second-round effects: if higher inflation raises expectations, wage bargaining adjusts, SRAS shifts left, and the initial demand-pull impulse becomes embedded — which is why central banks watch expectations as closely as the headline rate.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) accurate labelled AD/AS diagram with the shift correctly derived, the role of the slope of AS explained, applied to the extract with the demand-pull/cost-push distinction. L2 (4–6) diagram correct but incompletely used. L1 (1–3) definition with a missing or inaccurate diagram.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 11–12) states: "The largest upward contributions to inflation came from petrol and diesel prices." With the help of an AD/AS diagram, analyse the effects of cost-push inflation on an economy.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: AD, SRAS1 and LRAS. A rise in the price of an imported input shifts SRAS LEFT to SRAS2. The price level rises from P1 to P2 while real output FALLS from Y1 to Y2 — the signature of cost-push inflation, and the feature that distinguishes it from demand-pull on the diagram.\n• Chain of reasoning on causes: higher oil prices, higher imported raw material costs, a depreciation of sterling raising import prices, higher wage costs, higher indirect taxes, or higher employer National Insurance contributions all raise the cost of producing each unit of output.\n• Consequences — stagflation: inflation and falling output occur together, so unemployment rises at the same time as prices. This is the policy dilemma, because the conventional response to inflation (tighter monetary policy) worsens the output and employment effect, while supporting demand worsens the inflation.\n• Chain of reasoning on the policy response: the Bank of England held Bank Rate at 3.75% at its September 2026 meeting by a majority of 6–3, with three members voting for a rise to 4%. The split illustrates exactly this dilemma — whether to look through a commodity price shock that will drop out of the annual comparison, or to tighten because expectations may become unanchored.\n• Evaluation: a one-off increase in a price LEVEL is not the same as sustained inflation. Unless second-round effects raise wages and expectations, the fuel contribution falls out of the twelve-month comparison automatically. Core inflation, which strips out energy and food, is therefore the better guide to underlying pressure.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) accurate labelled diagram showing a leftward SRAS shift with both the price and output effects, the stagflation dilemma developed, applied to the September 2026 decision. L2 (4–6) diagram correct with limited analysis. L1 (1–3) assertion that costs rise and prices rise.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 15–16) states: "Output remains below the level implied by the economy\'s productive potential." With the help of a diagram, explain what is meant by a negative output gap and why it matters.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Define precisely: the output gap is the difference between actual real GDP and the trend or potential level of real GDP that the economy could sustain without accelerating inflation. A negative output gap means actual output is BELOW potential; a positive gap means it is above.\n• Diagram option 1: AD/AS with AD intersecting SRAS to the LEFT of LRAS, with the horizontal distance between the equilibrium output and LRAS labelled as the negative output gap. Diagram option 2: a time-series diagram with actual real GDP fluctuating around a rising trend line, with the gap shaded. Either is fully creditable if correctly labelled.\n• Why it matters for inflation: with spare capacity, firms can raise output without bidding up wages and input prices, so an increase in AD raises output more than prices. The AS curve is relatively flat in this region. This is the central input to monetary policy — the Bank of England is trying to judge whether inflation reflects excess demand or a supply shock.\n• Why it matters for unemployment: a negative gap implies cyclical unemployment, since firms need fewer workers than the capital stock and labour force could support. The economy produces inside its production possibility boundary and output is lost permanently — it cannot be recovered later.\n• Measurement difficulty, essential for the top band: potential output is not observable. It must be estimated from trends in the labour force, the capital stock and productivity, all of which are revised. Estimates of the output gap are therefore highly uncertain and frequently revised years after the event, which means policy is set on a number nobody can verify at the time.\n• Hysteresis: a prolonged negative gap can reduce potential output itself, as skills erode and investment is deferred, so LRAS shifts left and the gap closes for the wrong reason.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) definition precise, accurate labelled diagram, both the inflation and unemployment implications developed, with the measurement problem evaluated. L2 (4–6) definition and diagram correct, implications thin. L1 (1–3) definition only.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 20–21) states: "Sterling fell sharply against the dollar and the euro over the summer." With the help of a diagram, analyse how a depreciation of the exchange rate affects inflation and the current account.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Effect on inflation, with an AD/AS diagram: a depreciation raises the sterling price of imported raw materials, components, energy and food, which raises firms\' costs and shifts SRAS LEFT — imported cost-push inflation. Simultaneously, cheaper exports and dearer imports raise net exports, shifting AD RIGHT — demand-pull pressure. Both effects raise the price level, which is why depreciation is reliably inflationary.\n• Effect on the current account — the Marshall-Lerner condition: the trade balance improves only if the sum of the price elasticities of demand for exports and imports exceeds 1. Where demand is inelastic, the higher price of imports outweighs the reduced volume and the balance worsens.\n• The J-curve, which is the key dynamic point: in the short run, contracts are fixed and volumes respond slowly, so the value of imports rises immediately while export volumes have not yet grown — the current account deteriorates first. As elasticities rise over time, volumes adjust and the balance improves, tracing a J shape. Drawing the J-curve, with the current account balance on the vertical axis and time on the horizontal, is fully creditable as the diagram.\n• Application: the UK imports a high share of its energy, food and manufactured goods, and demand for these is price inelastic in the short run, so the inflationary effect of depreciation arrives faster and more certainly than the trade improvement.\n• Evaluation: the size of the effect depends on the degree of pass-through, on whether firms absorb costs in margins, and on how much of UK exports are themselves import-intensive, since imported inputs become dearer at the same time. A depreciation also raises the sterling value of overseas earnings, improving the primary income balance.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) both the inflation and current account effects analysed, an accurate diagram used, and the Marshall-Lerner condition or J-curve explained rather than named. L2 (4–6) one effect developed. L1 (1–3) assertion.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 25–26) states: "Households say prices have risen far more than the official inflation figure suggests." Analyse why the Consumer Prices Index may not accurately measure the change in a household\'s cost of living.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• How the index is built: the ONS collects around 180 000 prices each month for a basket of several hundred representative goods and services, weighted by their share in national household expenditure. The index measures the change in the cost of that fixed, weighted basket.\n• Reason 1 — the average household does not exist: weights reflect NATIONAL average spending patterns. A household spending a higher share of income on energy, food or rent — typically a low-income household — experiences a different inflation rate when those components rise faster than the average. Inflation is therefore distributionally uneven in a way one headline number conceals.\n• Reason 2 — substitution bias: because the basket is fixed between annual revisions, the index assumes consumers keep buying the same goods as relative prices change. In reality they substitute towards cheaper alternatives, so a fixed-basket index overstates the rise in the cost of maintaining a given standard of living.\n• Reason 3 — quality change and new goods: if a product improves while its price stays the same, the real cost of the service it provides has fallen. Hedonic adjustment attempts to correct for this but is inevitably imprecise, and new goods enter the basket only with a lag.\n• Reason 4 — coverage: CPI excludes owner-occupier housing costs, which are among the largest items of expenditure for many households. CPIH includes them through rental equivalence, which is itself an estimate, and RPI includes mortgage interest and uses a different averaging formula, which is why RPI typically prints higher than CPI.\n• Reason 5 — sampling and timing: prices are sampled, not censused; some are collected online and some in store; and the twelve-month comparison creates base effects, so the published rate can move sharply because of what happened a year ago rather than this month.\n• Judgement to include: these are reasons the index is an imperfect approximation of any individual household\'s experience, not reasons it is wrong. For measuring the general price level consistently over time, which is what monetary policy needs, it remains the best available instrument.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) at least three reasons developed into chains of reasoning, with accurate detail on construction and a judgement on how serious the limitations are. L2 (4–6) reasons listed with partial development. L1 (1–3) assertion that CPI is inaccurate.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 30–31) states: "National output can be measured by what is produced, what is earned or what is spent." Analyse why the three methods of measuring GDP should produce the same figure, and why in practice they do not.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The circular flow identity: in a closed economy with no government, the value of output produced equals the income paid to the factors that produced it, which equals the expenditure used to buy it. Every pound of output is a pound of someone\'s income and a pound of someone\'s spending. National output = national income = national expenditure is an accounting identity, true by construction.\n• Output method: sum the value added at each stage of production across all industries. Value added, not total sales, to avoid double counting the intermediate goods.\n• Income method: sum the incomes earned by factors of production — wages and salaries, rent, interest and profit — excluding transfer payments such as benefits and pensions, since these are not payments for current production.\n• Expenditure method: C + I + G + (X – M). Imports are subtracted because they are expenditure on output produced abroad.\n• Why they differ in practice — chain of reasoning: each is compiled from different sources (business surveys, tax records, household surveys, trade data), each of which samples imperfectly and is revised. The shadow economy — cash work, undeclared trade, illegal activity — is invisible to the income method but partly shows up in expenditure. Unpaid household work and volunteering appear in none of them. Timing differences, stock changes and imputations for owner-occupied housing add further discrepancies.\n• Consequence for policy: the ONS publishes a statistical discrepancy and balances the accounts, and early GDP estimates are revised substantially, sometimes changing the recorded direction of growth in a quarter. Policy is therefore set on provisional data, which is a substantive argument for caution in interpreting single quarters.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) the identity explained through the circular flow, all three methods described accurately, and at least two developed reasons for divergence with the policy implication. L2 (4–6) methods listed with a partial explanation of the identity. L1 (1–3) the three methods named only.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 35–36) states: "A current account deficit must be financed by a surplus elsewhere in the balance of payments." Analyse how a current account deficit is financed and what this implies for an economy.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The accounting identity: the balance of payments as a whole must balance, because every transaction has two entries. A current account deficit must therefore be matched by a combined surplus on the capital and financial accounts, plus or minus net errors and omissions.\n• How the financing occurs: the deficit is financed by net inflows on the financial account — foreign direct investment in UK companies and property, portfolio investment in UK equities and gilts, and banking flows — or by running down official reserves. In effect, the country is selling assets or borrowing from abroad to pay for the excess of imports over exports.\n• Chain of reasoning on why this can be benign: if the inflows are long-term foreign direct investment financing productive capital, the deficit is financing investment that raises future output and export capacity. A deficit driven by imports of capital goods is very different from one driven by imports of consumer goods. A deficit also reflects a strong currency and strong domestic demand, both of which have benefits.\n• Chain of reasoning on why it can be a problem: portfolio and banking flows are reversible — "hot money" that can leave quickly. Dependence on them makes the exchange rate and domestic interest rates hostage to foreign confidence. Selling assets also means future primary income outflows, as profits, dividends and interest accrue to foreign owners, which worsens the current account in later years — a self-reinforcing dynamic.\n• Application: the UK current account deficit was £63.2 billion, or 2.2% of GDP, in 2024, narrowing from 3.6% in 2023, and was 2.4% of GDP including precious metals in Quarter 4 2025. The UK has financed persistent deficits for decades without crisis, largely because sterling is a reserve currency and UK assets are attractive — which is itself the evaluation point: sustainability depends on the willingness of foreigners to keep lending, and that willingness is not guaranteed.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) the identity explained, financing mechanisms analysed, and both the benign and the risky interpretations developed with dated UK data. L2 (4–6) the identity stated with limited analysis. L1 (1–3) assertion.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 40–41) states: "Long spells out of work leave lasting scars on an economy\'s productive capacity." With the help of a diagram, explain the concept of hysteresis in the labour market.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Define hysteresis: the phenomenon by which a temporary shock has permanent effects — here, a cyclical rise in unemployment raising the NATURAL rate of unemployment and lowering the economy\'s productive potential even after demand recovers.\n• The mechanism, as a chain of reasoning: during a long spell of unemployment, skills depreciate and become out of date; the worker loses contact with workplace norms and networks; confidence and search intensity fall; and employers use duration of unemployment as a screening signal, so the long-term unemployed are passed over for the recently unemployed. Cyclical unemployment therefore converts into structural unemployment.\n• Diagram: an AD/AS diagram in which a leftward shift of AD lowers output, and then LRAS itself shifts left, so that when AD recovers the economy returns to a lower potential output at a higher price level. Alternatively, a labour market diagram showing the supply of effective labour shifting left. Either is creditable if correctly labelled.\n• Consequences: potential output is permanently lower, so the sustainable level of real GDP and living standards falls. Lower tax revenue and higher welfare spending worsen the public finances permanently, and inequality rises because the effects concentrate in particular groups and regions.\n• Policy implications, which lift an answer into the top band: hysteresis is the strongest argument for acting quickly and decisively in a downturn, because the cost of delay is permanent rather than temporary. It also strengthens the case for active labour market policy — retraining, job search support, wage subsidies — targeted specifically at duration of unemployment rather than at its level, since it is duration that does the damage.\n• Evaluation: the size of the effect is contested and depends on institutions. Economies with strong active labour market policies and flexible hiring show weaker hysteresis, which suggests it is a policy-dependent outcome rather than an inevitability.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) the mechanism explained as a chain rather than defined, an accurate diagram showing the LRAS effect, and policy implications drawn. L2 (4–6) definition with partial mechanism or an incomplete diagram. L1 (1–3) definition only.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 44–45) states: "Comparing GDP between countries requires converting it into a common currency." Analyse why purchasing power parity exchange rates are preferred to market exchange rates when comparing GDP per capita across countries.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The problem with market exchange rates: they are determined by the supply of and demand for currencies in foreign exchange markets, which reflect trade in TRADED goods and, far more importantly, capital flows, interest rate differentials and speculation. They are volatile — a currency can move 10% in a quarter — so a country\'s measured GDP in dollars can change substantially without any change in what it actually produces.\n• The core economic argument: a large share of consumption is of NON-TRADED goods and services — housing, haircuts, restaurant meals, domestic transport, healthcare. Their prices are not equalised by arbitrage across borders, and they are systematically cheaper in lower-income countries because wages are lower (the Balassa-Samuelson effect). Converting at market rates therefore understates the real volume of goods and services that an income buys in a poorer country.\n• What PPP does: a PPP exchange rate is the rate at which a common basket of goods and services costs the same in both countries. Converting at PPP measures the real purchasing power of income, which is what matters for comparing living standards.\n• Chain of reasoning on the consequence: PPP conversion typically raises the measured GDP per capita of lower-income countries substantially relative to market rate conversion, and narrows measured international inequality — which is why the choice of conversion method materially changes conclusions about global development.\n• Evaluation: PPP figures are estimates, produced through the International Comparison Program, and depend on the basket chosen. Consumption patterns differ between countries, so no single basket is representative of both; data collection in low-income and informal economies is difficult; and the figures are revised. Market rates remain the correct measure for anything involving international transactions — debt service, imports, trade flows.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) the non-traded goods argument developed as a chain, the effect on comparisons explained, and PPP\'s own limitations evaluated. L2 (4–6) the idea explained without the non-traded goods mechanism. L1 (1–3) assertion that PPP is more accurate.` },

      { q: 'Extract C (lines 48–49) states: "GDP counts a car crash as a contribution to output." Analyse the main limitations of using real GDP per capita as a measure of living standards.', marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Distribution: GDP per capita is a mean. An economy whose growth accrues entirely to the top decile records rising GDP per capita while median living standards stagnate. A mean cannot describe a distribution, and the UK Gini of 33% before housing costs in 2024/25 shows the distribution is far from equal.\n• What is omitted: unpaid household work, childcare and volunteering are excluded, so a country where more care work is done in the home records lower GDP for the same real activity. The shadow economy is largely missing. Leisure is not counted at all, so an economy that produces the same output in fewer hours shows no improvement.\n• What is counted that should not be — the extract\'s point: GDP counts expenditure regardless of whether it adds to welfare. Repairing accident damage, clearing up pollution, commuting costs and defensive expenditure on security all raise GDP while representing a cost of living rather than a benefit. These are "regrettables".\n• Externalities and sustainability: environmental degradation, resource depletion and carbon emissions do not appear as costs. An economy can raise GDP by depleting natural capital, which measured GDP treats as pure gain. Green GDP and natural capital accounting attempt to correct this.\n• Quality and composition: GDP measures quantity, not quality of life. Health, education, security, political freedom and social connection all determine welfare and none is captured, which is why the HDI and the ONS Measures of National Wellbeing were developed.\n• Measurement problems: deflating nominal GDP requires a price index subject to substitution and quality bias; early estimates are heavily revised; and public sector output is valued at input cost, so a more efficient public sector can appear to reduce GDP.\n• Balanced judgement for the top band: despite all this, real GDP per capita correlates strongly with life expectancy, literacy and most welfare indicators, is available consistently and frequently, and is comparable over time and across countries. It is a good proxy and a poor definition — which is the distinction that separates a high-level answer.\n\nLevels: L3 (7–9) at least four limitations developed with reasoning, illustrated, and a balanced judgement on GDP\'s usefulness. L2 (4–6) limitations listed with partial development. L1 (1–3) assertion that GDP does not measure happiness.` },

      /* ─────────── Paper 3 investigation: 10-mark data questions ─────────── */

      { q: `Extract D (Table 2) — written for this practice question; the CPI and labour market figures follow the official 2026 pattern\nUK macroeconomic indicators\n  CPI inflation (12 months to August): 2025 = 3.6% ; 2026 = 3.1%\n  Real GDP growth (annual):            2025 = 1.1% ; 2026 = 1.3%\n  Unemployment rate:                   2025 = 4.7% ; 2026 = 4.9%\n  Bank Rate (September):               2025 = 4.00% ; 2026 = 3.75%\n  Real household disposable income per head (index, 2019 = 100): 2025 = 101.4 ; 2026 = 102.1\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data in Extract D (Table 2) suggest that UK macroeconomic performance improved between 2025 and 2026?`, marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Evidence of improvement: inflation fell 0.5 percentage points, moving closer to the 2% target; real GDP growth rose from 1.1% to 1.3%, an 18.2% increase in the growth rate; and real household disposable income per head rose 0.69%, so average living standards improved slightly.\n• Evidence against: unemployment rose 0.2 percentage points and inflation at 3.1% remains 1.1 percentage points above target — and it rose between June (2.6%) and August 2026, so the direction of travel within the year was adverse. Growth of 1.3% is well below the UK\'s long-run average, so the economy is not making up lost ground.\n• The trade-off framing that lifts an answer: the four objectives — growth, low inflation, low unemployment and a sustainable external position — are not independent. A small rise in unemployment alongside falling inflation is consistent with a deliberate disinflation, so the two movements may be two sides of one policy rather than a success and a failure. The Phillips curve relationship is the analytical tool here.\n• Interpreting Bank Rate: the cut from 4.00% to 3.75% signals that the Monetary Policy Committee judged inflationary pressure to be easing, but the September 2026 decision to HOLD was taken by only 6–3, with three members voting for a rise. That split is evidence of genuine uncertainty rather than of confident improvement.\n• Missing data: nothing on the current account, on productivity, on the fiscal position, on investment, or on the distribution of the income gain. Macroeconomic performance is conventionally assessed against at least four objectives and the table covers three of them.\n• Data reliability: the ONS has warned that Labour Force Survey estimates are currently less reliable than usual, so the 0.2 point rise in unemployment may not be statistically significant.\n• Judgement on extent: modest improvement on inflation, growth and real incomes; deterioration on unemployment; and the evidence is too narrow and too uncertain to support a confident overall verdict. Say what further data would settle it.\n\nLevels: L3 (8–10) the data used precisely on both sides, trade-offs between objectives recognised, missing data and reliability identified, and a supported judgement on extent. L2 (4–7) some accurate data use with a partial conclusion. L1 (1–3) the figures restated.` },

      { q: `Extract D (Table 2) — written for this practice question\nFour economies, 2026\n  Economy P: real GDP per capita (PPP) $54 000 ; annual growth 1.2% ; Gini 0.29 ; life expectancy 83 ; CO2 per capita 4.9t\n  Economy Q: real GDP per capita (PPP) $54 000 ; annual growth 4.1% ; Gini 0.46 ; life expectancy 77 ; CO2 per capita 11.3t\n  Economy R: real GDP per capita (PPP) $31 000 ; annual growth 5.6% ; Gini 0.38 ; life expectancy 76 ; CO2 per capita 6.1t\n  Economy S: real GDP per capita (PPP) $49 000 ; annual growth 0.4% ; Gini 0.33 ; life expectancy 82 ; CO2 per capita 5.2t\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data in Extract D (Table 2) suggest that faster economic growth delivers higher living standards?`, marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The direct test: P and Q have IDENTICAL real GDP per capita, but Q grows 3.4 times faster while having a Gini 58.6% higher, life expectancy 6 years lower and CO2 emissions per head 2.31 times higher. On any welfare measure other than income growth, P is ahead — so faster growth has not delivered higher living standards here.\n• The counter-case: R has the fastest growth and the lowest income. At 5.6% a year, income per head doubles in about 13 years, so R is converging on the others rapidly. For a lower-income economy, growth is the dominant route to higher living standards, because the marginal utility of income is high when income is low. Growth also raises tax revenue to fund healthcare and education, which is why life expectancy typically follows income with a lag.\n• The distributional qualification: Q\'s high Gini means the mean is a poor guide to the median household\'s experience. Fast growth with rising inequality can leave the typical household little better off, so growth is a necessary but not sufficient condition for rising living standards.\n• The sustainability qualification: Q\'s CO2 per head suggests growth achieved by depleting natural capital, which GDP records as pure gain and makes no deduction for. Growth of this kind borrows living standards from the future.\n• What the data cannot show: no information on the starting point, on the composition of growth, on the counterfactual, on leisure and hours worked, or on political freedom and security. Four economies at one point in time is a snapshot, not evidence of a causal relationship, and the direction of causation is unestablished — better health raises productivity as well as the reverse.\n• Judgement on extent: the data suggest growth is strongly associated with rising living standards at LOW income levels (Economy R), and weakly or not at all at high income levels where distribution, health and sustainability dominate (P against Q). A conditional conclusion of that kind, rooted in the figures, is what reaches the top level.\n\nLevels: L3 (8–10) the P/Q comparison used as a controlled test, both sides argued from the data, limitations identified, and a conditional judgement supported. L2 (4–7) some accurate manipulation with a partial conclusion. L1 (1–3) the table described.` },

      /* ─────────── Section B part (a): 15-mark explain questions ─────────── */

      { q: 'Explain the main limitations of using real GDP per capita to compare living standards between countries and over time.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Distribution: GDP per capita is an arithmetic mean and says nothing about how output is shared. Two economies with identical GDP per capita can have completely different median living standards — the UK Gini for disposable income was 33% before housing costs and 37% after in 2024/25.\n• Omissions from the measure: unpaid household work, childcare and volunteering are excluded, and the shadow economy is largely missing, so countries with more informal or household production are understated. Leisure is not valued, so an economy producing the same output in fewer hours shows no gain.\n• Regrettables: GDP counts expenditure whether or not it adds to welfare. Clearing up pollution, repairing accident damage, commuting and defensive security spending all raise GDP while representing a cost of living.\n• Externalities and sustainability: environmental degradation and resource depletion are not deducted, so growth achieved by running down natural capital appears as pure gain. Green GDP and natural capital accounts exist precisely because of this.\n• Composition of output: an economy producing military equipment and one producing healthcare record the same GDP. What is produced matters for welfare and GDP is indifferent to it.\n• Problems specific to international comparison: converting at market exchange rates distorts comparisons, because non-traded goods and services are systematically cheaper in lower-income countries, so PPP conversion is required — and PPP figures are themselves estimates based on a basket that cannot be representative of both countries. Data collection quality also varies enormously between countries.\n• Problems specific to comparison over time: deflating nominal GDP requires a price index subject to substitution and quality bias; early GDP estimates are substantially revised; public sector output is valued at input cost, so efficiency gains can appear as output falls; and new goods and quality improvements are captured with a lag.\n• The balancing judgement expected at L3: real GDP per capita nonetheless correlates strongly with life expectancy, literacy and most welfare indicators, is collected consistently and frequently, and remains the best available single proxy. Alternatives such as the HDI, the Inequality-adjusted HDI and the ONS Measures of National Wellbeing each add a dimension and each carry their own weighting judgements.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) at least five limitations explained with developed reasoning, the international and over-time problems distinguished, and a balanced closing judgement. L2 (6–10) limitations identified with partial explanation. L1 (1–5) a list.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main causes of inflation in an economy.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Define first: inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level, measured in the UK by the annual change in the Consumer Prices Index, with a target of 2% set for the Bank of England. Distinguish it from a one-off change in a price level and from a change in relative prices.\n• Demand-pull inflation: excess aggregate demand relative to productive capacity. AD = C + I + G + (X – M), so the impulse can come from a consumption boom driven by confidence, real income growth or cheap credit; an investment boom; a fiscal expansion; or a depreciation raising net exports. Diagram: AD shifts right along an upward-sloping SRAS, raising both the price level and output. The steeper the AS curve — that is, the smaller the output gap — the more of the increase appears as inflation.\n• Cost-push inflation: a rise in the cost of producing each unit of output shifts SRAS left, raising the price level while output falls. Causes include higher commodity and energy prices, a depreciation raising import costs, higher wage costs not matched by productivity, higher indirect taxes, and higher employer National Insurance contributions. The rise in UK CPI inflation to 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026 was driven largely by petrol and diesel prices — a textbook cost-push contribution.\n• Monetary causes: on the quantity theory, MV = PQ, so a sustained increase in the money supply beyond the growth of real output raises the price level. Monetarists argue that all sustained inflation is ultimately monetary, because cost-push shocks cannot raise prices year after year unless accommodated by monetary growth. This is a genuine analytical distinction worth making.\n• Expectations and second-round effects: if households and firms expect higher inflation, they build it into wage claims and price setting. SRAS shifts left, the expectation is validated, and the process becomes self-sustaining — a wage-price spiral. This is why central banks treat the anchoring of expectations as their central task, and why the Monetary Policy Committee\'s September 2026 vote to hold Bank Rate at 3.75% was split 6–3.\n• Imported inflation: for an open economy importing a high share of energy, food and manufactured goods, world prices and the exchange rate feed directly into domestic prices with a short lag.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) demand-pull and cost-push both explained with accurate diagrams, expectations and monetary causes included, and dated UK evidence used. L2 (6–10) both main types identified with partial explanation. L1 (1–5) a list of causes.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main types of unemployment and their causes.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Measurement first: the internationally comparable Labour Force Survey / ILO measure counts those out of work, available to start within two weeks and actively seeking work in the past four weeks. The UK rate was 4.9% in May to July 2026, with employment at 75.1% and inactivity at 20.9%. The claimant count is an administrative alternative that changes with benefit eligibility rules.\n• Cyclical (demand-deficient) unemployment: caused by a fall in aggregate demand in a downturn. Since labour demand is derived from demand for output, a leftward shift of AD reduces derived demand for labour across the whole economy. Diagram: AD/AS with a negative output gap, or a labour market with demand shifting left where wages are downwardly sticky. Cured by recovery in AD, and the main target of expansionary fiscal and monetary policy.\n• Structural unemployment: a mismatch between the skills or location of workers and the requirements of available jobs, caused by long-run changes in the pattern of demand, by technology, or by international competition. It coexists with unfilled vacancies. Sub-types include regional and technological unemployment. Cured only by retraining, relocation support and education — which is why it is persistent.\n• Frictional unemployment: short-term unemployment while people move between jobs. It exists even at full employment, and some is efficient, since a good job match is worth searching for. Influenced by information about vacancies and by the generosity and conditionality of benefits.\n• Seasonal unemployment: predictable variation in demand for labour in agriculture, tourism, construction and retail.\n• Real wage (classical) unemployment: where the wage is held above the market-clearing level — by a wage floor such as the £12.71 National Living Wage, or by union bargaining — quantity of labour supplied exceeds quantity demanded. Note the qualification that in a monopsonistic labour market a wage floor can raise employment instead.\n• The natural rate: the sum of frictional, structural and real wage unemployment — the rate that persists when the economy is at potential output, so cyclical unemployment is zero. It can be reduced only by supply-side policy, not by demand management.\n• Hysteresis as the link between types: prolonged cyclical unemployment converts into structural unemployment as skills and employability erode, raising the natural rate. This is why the speed of the policy response to a downturn matters.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) at least four types explained with distinct causes and the appropriate policy response for each, with accurate dated data and the natural rate concept. L2 (6–10) types identified with partial explanation. L1 (1–5) a list of types.` },

      { q: 'Explain how the Consumer Prices Index is constructed and the main problems involved in measuring inflation accurately.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — construction:\n• A representative basket of several hundred goods and services is chosen from the Living Costs and Food Survey and other expenditure data, and is reviewed annually so that items entering and leaving typical spending are reflected.\n• Around 180 000 individual prices are collected each month from a sample of retailers and online, in locations across the UK.\n• Each category is weighted by its share of total household expenditure, so a 10% rise in the price of something that accounts for 3% of spending contributes 0.3 percentage points to the index.\n• The weighted prices are combined into an index number with a base year set to 100, and the published inflation rate is the percentage change in that index over twelve months.\n• Related measures: CPIH adds owner-occupier housing costs using rental equivalence and is the ONS\'s preferred measure; RPI includes mortgage interest payments and uses a different averaging formula, which typically produces a higher figure and is why RPI is no longer a National Statistic; core inflation strips out energy, food, alcohol and tobacco to reveal underlying pressure.\n\nProblems of measurement:\n• Substitution bias: the basket is fixed between revisions, so the index assumes consumers do not switch to cheaper alternatives as relative prices change. This overstates the rise in the cost of maintaining a given standard of living.\n• Quality change and new goods: if a product improves at an unchanged price, the true cost of the service it provides has fallen. Hedonic adjustment is an imperfect correction, and new goods enter the basket with a lag.\n• The average household does not exist: weights reflect national average spending. A household spending a higher share on energy, food or rent experiences a different inflation rate when those components move — so inflation is distributionally uneven and one headline number conceals it.\n• Coverage: CPI excludes owner-occupier housing costs, among the largest items of expenditure for many households.\n• Sampling and timing: prices are sampled rather than censused; and the twelve-month comparison creates base effects, so the rate can move sharply because of what happened a year earlier. UK CPI inflation rising from 2.6% in June 2026 to 3.1% in August 2026 partly reflects this.\n• Why it matters: CPI determines the Bank of England\'s 2% target and therefore monetary policy, the uprating of benefits and pensions, index-linked gilts, and wage bargaining. Measurement error therefore has large real consequences, which is the closing point expected at L3.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) construction explained accurately step by step, at least four measurement problems explained, and the consequences for policy drawn out. L2 (6–10) construction described, problems listed. L1 (1–5) a definition of CPI.` },

      { q: 'Explain the structure of the balance of payments and the main causes of a persistent current account deficit.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — structure:\n• The balance of payments records all transactions between residents of a country and the rest of the world over a period, and must balance overall because every transaction has two entries.\n• The CURRENT account has four components: trade in goods; trade in services; primary income (net investment income — profits, dividends and interest — plus compensation of employees); and secondary income (transfers such as overseas aid and remittances, for which nothing is received in return).\n• The CAPITAL account is small, covering capital transfers and the acquisition of non-produced non-financial assets.\n• The FINANCIAL account records transactions in assets and liabilities: foreign direct investment, portfolio investment, other investment including banking flows, and changes in official reserves.\n• The identity: a current account deficit must be matched by a net inflow on the capital and financial accounts, plus or minus net errors and omissions. A country running a current account deficit is, by construction, selling assets or borrowing from abroad.\n• UK application: the deficit was £63.2 billion, or 2.2% of GDP, in 2024, having narrowed from 3.6% of GDP in 2023, and stood at 2.4% of GDP including precious metals in Quarter 4 2025. The goods deficit is large and the services surplus offsets most of it.\n\nCauses of a persistent deficit:\n• Structural specialisation: the UK has a comparative advantage in high-value services and imports a high share of manufactured goods, energy and food. A persistent goods deficit partly offset by a services surplus is the arithmetic signature of that pattern.\n• Low relative international competitiveness: weak productivity growth raises unit labour costs relative to competitors, making exports dearer and imports more attractive. Non-price competitiveness — quality, design, reliability, delivery — matters as much as price.\n• A strong exchange rate: an overvalued currency makes exports dearer abroad and imports cheaper at home. Where sterling is supported by capital inflows rather than trade, the current account and financial account are two sides of one process.\n• The domestic growth rate relative to trading partners: if domestic income grows faster than that of export markets, imports rise faster than exports, since imports have a high income elasticity of demand. This is why a consumer-led boom typically widens the deficit.\n• Low national saving relative to investment: the current account balance equals national saving minus domestic investment, so a low household saving ratio or a large government deficit produces a current account deficit as an accounting matter. This is the most analytically powerful explanation and should be included at L3.\n• Deterioration in the terms of trade: if import prices rise faster than export prices, a given volume of exports finances fewer imports and the value balance worsens.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) all components of the structure explained accurately, the identity stated, and at least four causes explained with developed reasoning including the saving-investment identity. L2 (6–10) structure described, causes listed. L1 (1–5) a definition.` },

      { q: 'Explain the main costs of inflation and the main costs of deflation to an economy.', marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — costs of inflation:\n• Loss of purchasing power and arbitrary redistribution: unanticipated inflation transfers real wealth from lenders to borrowers and from savers to debtors, because it erodes the real value of nominal debts. Those on fixed nominal incomes lose most. With UK CPI inflation at 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026 against a 2% target, a nominal pay rise of 3% is a real pay CUT.\n• Distortion of the price mechanism: rapid or variable inflation makes it hard to distinguish a rise in a relative price, which carries information about scarcity, from a rise in the general price level. Resources are misallocated as a result.\n• Uncertainty and investment: volatile inflation raises the risk premium on long-term investment, so firms defer capital spending and LRAS grows more slowly. Variability matters more than the level for this cost.\n• International competitiveness: if domestic inflation exceeds that of trading partners with a fixed or slow-moving exchange rate, exports become dearer and imports cheaper, worsening the current account.\n• Menu costs and shoe-leather costs: the resources used in repricing, and the effort spent economising on cash holdings. These are real but small at moderate inflation rates — say so rather than overstating them.\n• Fiscal drag: where tax thresholds are not indexed, inflation moves taxpayers into higher bands without any rise in real income, which is a tax increase by stealth.\n• Wage-price spirals: if inflation becomes embedded in expectations, it accelerates and becomes much more costly to remove, since disinflation then requires a period of below-potential output.\n\nCosts of deflation:\n• Deferred consumption: if prices are expected to fall, rational consumers postpone purchases of durables, which reduces AD and causes prices to fall further — a self-reinforcing deflationary spiral.\n• Rising real debt burden: deflation raises the real value of nominal debts, so borrowers cut spending to service them. Debt deflation transmits the problem from households to banks and deepens downturns.\n• The zero lower bound: with nominal interest rates near zero, a central bank cannot cut the real interest rate further by conventional means, so monetary policy loses traction exactly when it is most needed. This is why an inflation target is set at 2% rather than 0% — it provides a buffer against deflation.\n• Real wage rigidity: nominal wages are resistant to falling, so deflation raises real wages above market-clearing levels and raises unemployment.\n• Crucial distinction for top marks: benign deflation driven by rising productivity and a rightward shift of LRAS raises real incomes and is not harmful; malign deflation driven by collapsing AD is extremely damaging. Which type it is determines whether it is a problem at all.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) at least four costs of each explained with developed reasoning, the benign/malign deflation distinction drawn, and dated evidence used. L2 (6–10) costs listed with partial explanation. L1 (1–5) a list.` },

      /* ─────────── Section A (d) / Section B (b): 25-mark evaluation ─────────── */

      { q: 'Extract B (lines 10–11) states: "GDP was never designed to measure welfare, yet it is the number governments are judged on." Using the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that GDP should be replaced by broader measures of economic welfare.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The case for replacement — what GDP misses: distribution (a mean cannot describe a distribution; the UK Gini was 33% before housing costs in 2024/25), unpaid household work and volunteering, leisure, the shadow economy, environmental degradation and resource depletion, and the composition of output. GDP also counts regrettables — pollution clean-up, accident repair, commuting, defensive spending — as positive contributions.\n• The case for replacement — perverse incentives: if governments are judged on GDP, they are incentivised towards policies that raise measured output even where welfare falls, such as growth achieved by depleting natural capital. What is measured is what is managed.\n• Alternatives evaluated individually rather than listed: the Human Development Index adds life expectancy and schooling and reveals cases where high income has not produced human development — but weights its three components by judgement, ignores inequality and the environment, and saturates at high income levels. The Inequality-adjusted HDI addresses the distribution gap. The Genuine Progress Indicator and green GDP deduct environmental costs, but rely on contested monetary valuations of non-market goods. The ONS Measures of National Wellbeing and subjective wellbeing surveys capture what people actually report, but are hard to compare across cultures and are sensitive to question wording.\n• The case against replacement — practical: GDP is collected consistently, frequently and comparably across almost every country, with a long back-run. Composite indices are published annually at best, are revised, and depend on weights that embed value judgements which are not economic findings. Replacing an imperfect but objective measure with a contested subjective one may reduce accountability rather than increase it.\n• The case against replacement — empirical: real GDP per capita correlates strongly with life expectancy, literacy, infant mortality and most welfare indicators. It is a good proxy even though it is a poor definition, and in low-income countries the correlation is very strong indeed.\n• The case against replacement — GDP\'s own job: GDP is the correct measure for the things it is designed for — the output gap, the tax base, debt sustainability ratios, productivity and monetary policy. No wellbeing index can tell a central bank whether there is spare capacity.\n• The dashboard argument, which is the strongest judgement route: the choice between one number and another is a false one. Using GDP alongside inequality, environmental, health and wellbeing indicators keeps GDP\'s precision for the questions it answers while making its omissions visible — which is precisely what the ONS now does.\n• Judgement: replace is the wrong verb; supplement is the right one. Any well-supported conclusion is creditable, but it must engage with what GDP is FOR rather than simply listing what it omits.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) alternatives evaluated individually, the proxy/definition distinction drawn, GDP\'s legitimate uses defended, dated evidence, judgement following from the analysis. L4 (16–20) well-developed and mostly balanced. L3 (11–15) limitations listed with some evaluation. L2 (6–10) one-sided. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Extract B (lines 18–19) states: "The UK current account deficit was 2.2% of GDP in 2024, down from 3.6% in 2023." Using the extracts and your knowledge of economics, assess whether a persistent current account deficit is a serious problem for the UK economy.', marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Establish the accounting: a current account deficit is necessarily matched by a net inflow on the capital and financial accounts. A country running a deficit is selling assets or borrowing from abroad — that is what financing means, and getting this right is the foundation of the answer.\n• The case that it IS a problem — sustainability: financing depends on the continued willingness of foreigners to hold UK assets. Portfolio and banking flows are reversible, so a loss of confidence can force a sharp depreciation, higher interest rates and an abrupt contraction in domestic demand.\n• The case that it IS a problem — compounding: selling assets abroad means future profits, dividends and interest accrue to foreign owners, worsening the primary income balance in later years. The deficit therefore has a tendency to reinforce itself.\n• The case that it IS a problem — what it signals: a persistent deficit may indicate weak international competitiveness, poor productivity growth, or an over-valued exchange rate. On the saving-investment identity, it reflects national saving falling short of domestic investment, which may point to an unsustainable consumption boom or a large fiscal deficit.\n• The case that it is NOT a problem — composition matters: a deficit financed by long-term foreign direct investment in productive capacity raises future output and export capability. Importing capital goods to expand supply is entirely different from importing consumer goods on credit, and the headline number does not distinguish them.\n• The case that it is NOT a problem — the UK\'s position: the UK has financed deficits for decades without crisis, because sterling is a reserve currency, UK assets are attractive, and the exchange rate floats — which provides an automatic adjustment mechanism a fixed-rate economy lacks. The deficit narrowed from 3.6% to 2.2% of GDP between 2023 and 2024 without policy intervention, and the underlying deficit excluding precious metals was just 1.1% of GDP in Quarter 4 2025. A deficit that self-corrects is evidence of adjustment, not of crisis.\n• The case that it is NOT a problem — measurement: the headline figure is distorted by precious metals trading, is heavily revised, and the errors and omissions item is large. A deficit that is 2.4% of GDP on one measure and 1.1% on another should not be the basis of a confident diagnosis.\n• Policy responses and their costs: expenditure reduction through tighter fiscal or monetary policy cuts imports but at the cost of output and jobs; expenditure switching through depreciation raises import prices and inflation, and works only if the Marshall-Lerner condition holds, with a J-curve delay; supply-side policy to raise productivity and non-price competitiveness addresses the cause but works over a decade.\n• Judgement: the size of the deficit matters far less than how it is financed, what it is spent on, and whether it is self-correcting. On current evidence the UK deficit is a symptom worth monitoring rather than a crisis — but the dependence on short-term flows is a genuine vulnerability, so complacency is not warranted either.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) the financing identity used analytically, both sides developed with the composition and sustainability distinctions, dated UK evidence used precisely, policy options evaluated, judgement supported. L4 (16–20) well-developed and balanced. L3 (11–15) some analysis. L2 (6–10) description. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Discuss whether low unemployment should be the government\'s most important macroeconomic objective.', marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Set out the objectives: sustainable economic growth, low and stable inflation (the 2% CPI target), low unemployment, and a sustainable balance of payments position — plus, increasingly, sound public finances, reduced inequality and environmental sustainability.\n• The case for prioritising unemployment — economic cost: unemployment is the clearest case of resources left idle, so the economy produces inside its production possibility boundary and the lost output can never be recovered. Tax revenue falls and welfare spending rises simultaneously, worsening the public finances.\n• The case for prioritising unemployment — hysteresis: prolonged unemployment erodes skills, confidence and employability, converting cyclical into structural unemployment and lowering potential output permanently. The cost of tolerating unemployment therefore compounds, which is the strongest analytical argument for prioritising it.\n• The case for prioritising unemployment — social and distributional: unemployment is concentrated in particular groups, regions and households, and is strongly associated with poverty, poor health and reduced life chances for children. At 4.9% in May to July 2026, UK unemployment represents around 1.7 million people.\n• The case against — the inflation trade-off: pushing unemployment below the natural rate through demand expansion raises inflation, as the short-run Phillips curve implies. If expectations adjust, the economy returns to the natural rate at a higher inflation rate — the long-run Phillips curve is vertical — so the gain is temporary and the inflation cost permanent. Diagram: SRPC and LRPC.\n• The case against — demand policy cannot cure the natural rate: only cyclical unemployment responds to demand management. Frictional, structural and real wage unemployment require supply-side policy — retraining, mobility support, education. Prioritising an unemployment target invites the wrong instrument for the majority of the problem.\n• The case against — price stability as a precondition: high and variable inflation distorts the price mechanism, deters investment, erodes competitiveness and redistributes arbitrarily. Low stable inflation is arguably a precondition for sustained employment rather than a rival to it — the Bank of England\'s September 2026 decision to hold Bank Rate at 3.75% by 6–3 with inflation at 3.1% illustrates the live nature of this judgement.\n• The case against — measurement: the headline unemployment rate omits the economically inactive who want work and the underemployed, and the ONS has warned that Labour Force Survey estimates are currently less reliable than usual. Targeting a number that imperfectly measures the thing you care about invites Goodhart\'s Law.\n• The case for growth as the priority instead: sustained growth in productive capacity is the only route that improves all the other objectives simultaneously, since it raises employment, tax revenue and real incomes without an inflation trade-off.\n• Judgement: the objectives conflict in the short run and are complementary in the long run. A defensible conclusion is that the correct priority depends on where the economy is — in a deep recession with a large negative output gap, unemployment dominates; with inflation above target and unemployment near the natural rate, price stability has the better claim. Naming the condition is what separates a top answer from a list.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) objectives and their trade-offs analysed with the Phillips curve, the natural rate argument used, measurement critiqued, judgement conditional and supported by dated evidence. L4 (16–20) well-developed and mostly balanced. L3 (11–15) partial. L2 (6–10) list of objectives. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the view that the Consumer Prices Index is an adequate basis for the Bank of England\'s inflation target.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Explain the institutional setting: the Bank of England has an operational target of 2% CPI inflation, with an open letter required if inflation deviates by more than one percentage point in either direction. CPI inflation was 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026 and Bank Rate was held at 3.75% in September 2026 by a 6–3 majority.\n• The case that CPI is adequate — consistency and comparability: CPI is constructed to a harmonised international standard, is published monthly with little delay, is rarely revised, and is understood by the public. A monetary target must be transparent, timely and hard to manipulate, and CPI is all three. Frequent revision — as with GDP — would make a target unworkable.\n• The case that CPI is adequate — anchoring expectations: the purpose of the target is to anchor inflation expectations. That depends far more on credibility and consistency than on the technical perfection of the index, so changing the measure carries a real cost in credibility.\n• The case against — coverage: CPI excludes owner-occupier housing costs, among the largest items of household expenditure. CPIH includes them through rental equivalence and is the ONS\'s preferred measure, so the target arguably uses the wrong index. Against this, rental equivalence is itself an imputation and house prices are an asset price, not a consumption price.\n• The case against — volatile components: energy and food prices are set globally and are outside the control of UK monetary policy. The August 2026 rise to 3.1% was driven largely by petrol and diesel. Targeting headline CPI means responding to shocks that will drop out of the annual comparison in twelve months, which risks tightening into a supply shock and deepening a downturn. Core inflation strips these out — but a core target is less credible with the public, who buy petrol and food.\n• The case against — it ignores asset prices: sustained increases in house and equity prices can build financial imbalances without appearing in CPI at all. The pre-2008 period is the standard illustration. This is why macroprudential policy now sits alongside the inflation target rather than inside it.\n• The case against — measurement bias: substitution bias, quality adjustment and the fixed basket mean CPI is an imperfect measure of the cost of living, and a systematically biased target implies systematically wrong policy.\n• The case against — distribution: households with different spending patterns face different inflation rates, so a single index conceals a very uneven experience, particularly when energy and food lead the increase.\n• Alternatives evaluated: a CPIH target (better coverage, less familiar), a core inflation target (less noise, less credibility), a price level target (corrects past misses but requires deliberate overshoots), or nominal GDP targeting (automatically accommodates supply shocks, but relies on a heavily revised statistic).\n• Judgement: CPI\'s adequacy rests on transparency and timeliness rather than on measurement perfection, and no alternative dominates it on all criteria. A defensible conclusion is that the index is adequate as a target but inadequate as a complete description of inflationary conditions, which is why the Monetary Policy Committee explicitly looks through temporary shocks and monitors core inflation and expectations alongside it.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) the purpose of a target analysed, multiple limitations developed, alternatives evaluated on the right criteria, dated evidence, judgement supported. L4 (16–20) well-developed and balanced. L3 (11–15) limitations listed with some evaluation. L2 (6–10) description of CPI. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that deflation is a more serious problem for an economy than inflation.', marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Define both and make the essential distinction early: inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level; deflation a sustained fall. Benign deflation, driven by rising productivity shifting LRAS right, raises real incomes and is not harmful. Malign deflation, driven by collapsing AD, is the dangerous case. The whole assessment turns on which type is meant, and saying so is the discriminator.\n• The case that deflation is worse — deferred consumption: if prices are expected to fall, consumers postpone purchases of durables, reducing AD and causing prices to fall further. The expectation is self-fulfilling, so deflation contains a positive feedback loop that moderate inflation does not.\n• The case that deflation is worse — debt deflation: falling prices raise the REAL value of nominal debts. Households and firms cut spending to service them, deepening the contraction and impairing bank balance sheets, which transmits the shock through the financial system.\n• The case that deflation is worse — the zero lower bound: with nominal rates near zero, a central bank cannot cut the real interest rate further by conventional means, so monetary policy loses traction exactly when it is most needed. This is the strongest single argument, and it is why the target is 2% rather than 0% — the buffer is deliberate.\n• The case that deflation is worse — real wage rigidity: nominal wages resist falling, so deflation raises real wages above market-clearing levels and raises unemployment. Japan after 1990 is the standard illustration of a prolonged deflationary trap.\n• The case that inflation is worse — it has no natural limit: deflation is bounded in practice by wage rigidity and by policy response, whereas inflation can accelerate without limit once expectations become unanchored. Hyperinflation destroys the monetary system entirely; there is no deflationary equivalent.\n• The case that inflation is worse — arbitrary redistribution and distortion: unanticipated inflation transfers wealth from savers and those on fixed incomes to borrowers; it obscures relative price signals; it raises uncertainty and deters long-term investment; and it erodes international competitiveness. With CPI at 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026, a 3% pay rise is a real pay cut.\n• The case that inflation is worse — the cost of removing it: once embedded in expectations, disinflation requires a period of below-potential output and higher unemployment, with hysteresis making some of that cost permanent. The sacrifice ratio is a real and measurable cost.\n• Symmetry of the policy problem: both are departures from price stability, which is why the target is symmetric and why an open letter is required for an undershoot as well as an overshoot. Neither is costless.\n• Judgement: malign deflation is more dangerous than MODERATE inflation because of the zero lower bound and the self-reinforcing dynamics, but less dangerous than HIGH inflation, which is unbounded. A defensible conclusion specifies the comparison rather than answering in the abstract — which is precisely what the word "more serious" requires.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) both phenomena analysed, the benign/malign distinction used, the zero lower bound and expectations arguments developed, judgement that specifies the comparison. L4 (16–20) well-developed and balanced. L3 (11–15) costs of each listed with some evaluation. L2 (6–10) one-sided. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate the usefulness of the Human Development Index compared with real GDP per capita for comparing the performance of different economies.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Explain both measures precisely: real GDP per capita measures the volume of output produced per person, converted at PPP for international comparison. The HDI is a composite index combining GNI per capita (PPP), life expectancy at birth and education (mean and expected years of schooling), each normalised and combined into a value between 0 and 1.\n• The case for the HDI — it measures ends, not just means: income is an input to development, not development itself. Two economies with identical income can differ greatly in life expectancy and schooling, and the HDI reveals whether income has been converted into human capability. It captures the substantial development gains achievable at low cost through public health and basic education.\n• The case for the HDI — it changes conclusions: oil-rich economies with high income but weak health and education outcomes rank far lower on the HDI than on income alone, which is diagnostically useful for policy.\n• The case against the HDI — arbitrary weighting: the three components are given equal weight by convention, not by any economic finding. Different weights produce different rankings, so the index embeds a value judgement presented as a measurement.\n• The case against the HDI — omissions: it ignores inequality (the Inequality-adjusted HDI was created for this reason), the environment, political freedom, gender inequality, security and subjective wellbeing. It also saturates — among high-income economies, life expectancy and schooling differ so little that the index collapses back towards being an income measure with compressed variation.\n• The case against the HDI — practical: published annually, with revisions and long lags, and dependent on data of uneven quality across countries. It cannot be used for anything requiring frequency or precision.\n• The case for GDP per capita — it does jobs the HDI cannot: it is the measure required for the output gap, tax capacity, debt sustainability ratios, productivity and monetary policy. No composite welfare index can tell a central bank whether there is spare capacity in the economy.\n• The case for GDP per capita — empirical strength: it correlates strongly with life expectancy, literacy and most welfare indicators, particularly at lower income levels, so it works well as a proxy even though it is poor as a definition of welfare.\n• Limitations shared by both: neither captures distribution within a country directly, neither values unpaid work or leisure, neither deducts environmental degradation, and both depend on PPP conversions that are estimates.\n• Judgement: the correct question is not which is better but which is better FOR WHAT. For comparing development and the effectiveness of public policy in converting income into capability, the HDI is more useful; for measuring economic activity, capacity and the tax base, GDP per capita is indispensable. The strongest conclusions state the purpose before ranking the measure, and note that both should be read alongside distributional and environmental indicators.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) both measures explained accurately, strengths and weaknesses of each developed, the fitness-for-purpose argument made explicitly, judgement supported. L4 (16–20) well-developed and balanced. L3 (11–15) both described with some evaluation. L2 (6–10) one-sided. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Extract B (lines 26–27) states: "Real GDP grew by 1.3% in 2026, but households say they feel no better off." Using the extracts and your knowledge of economics, discuss whether economic growth necessarily improves living standards.', marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Define terms and set up the analytical route: economic growth is an increase in real GDP; living standards depend on real income per head, its distribution, and non-material factors. The gap between them is where the whole answer lies.\n• Reason 1 the two can diverge — population: GDP growth of 1.3% with population growth of 0.9% leaves real GDP per capita growing only 0.4%, so headline growth can accompany almost static average incomes.\n• Reason 2 — distribution: GDP per capita is a mean. If growth accrues to the top of the distribution, the median household experiences no gain. With the UK Gini at 33% before housing costs and 37% after in 2024/25, and after-housing-costs inequality higher still, the typical household\'s experience can diverge sharply from the average.\n• Reason 3 — inflation and the composition of spending: with CPI inflation at 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026, driven largely by fuel, households whose spending is concentrated on energy, food and rent face an effective inflation rate above the headline, so their real income can fall while aggregate real income rises.\n• Reason 4 — the composition of output: growth in defence spending, pollution clean-up or commuting-related expenditure raises GDP without raising welfare. GDP counts regrettables as positives.\n• Reason 5 — externalities and sustainability: growth achieved by depleting natural capital or raising emissions imposes costs GDP does not deduct. Living standards later fall to pay for standards enjoyed now.\n• Reason 6 — leisure and hours: if output rises because people work longer hours, measured growth rises while welfare may not. GDP values production, not the time cost of producing it.\n• The case that growth DOES improve living standards: over any long period, growth is overwhelmingly the dominant source of rising real incomes, life expectancy and literacy. It expands the tax base, funding healthcare, education and transfers without higher tax rates. It raises labour demand, so employment and real wages rise. And the correlation between real GDP per capita and virtually every welfare indicator is strong, particularly at lower income levels.\n• Conditions that determine whether growth translates into living standards: whether it is per capita, whether it is distributed broadly, whether it is environmentally sustainable, whether it is export-led or debt-fuelled, and whether it is accompanied by rising productivity rather than simply longer hours. Listing these conditions is the route to a top-band judgement.\n• Judgement: growth is close to necessary but plainly not sufficient. The strongest conclusions answer the question as asked — "necessarily" is false, but growth remains the single most important determinant of living standards over the long run, and the policy question is about the TYPE of growth rather than whether to have it.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) at least four divergence mechanisms developed, the case for growth made with equal rigour, dated evidence used precisely, conditions specified, judgement engaging with "necessarily". L4 (16–20) well-developed and mostly balanced. L3 (11–15) some analysis. L2 (6–10) list. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Assess the view that the official statistics used to measure macroeconomic performance are too unreliable to be a sound basis for policy.', marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The case for unreliability — GDP: compiled from three different methods that do not agree, requiring a statistical discrepancy; early estimates are substantially revised, sometimes reversing the recorded direction of a quarter; the shadow economy is largely missing; public sector output is valued at input cost, so efficiency gains can register as output falls; and unpaid household work is excluded entirely.\n• The case for unreliability — the labour market: the ONS has warned that Labour Force Survey estimates are currently less reliable than usual, partly because of falling response rates and a data collection error in 2026. The headline unemployment rate also omits the inactive who want work and the underemployed, so it measures slack imperfectly by construction. Movements of 0.1 or 0.2 percentage points may not be statistically significant.\n• The case for unreliability — inflation: substitution bias, quality adjustment and a fixed basket mean CPI is an approximation of the cost of living; it excludes owner-occupier housing costs; and base effects move the headline rate for reasons unconnected with current conditions.\n• The case for unreliability — the balance of payments: large net errors and omissions, heavy revisions, and distortion by precious metals trading, which is why the Quarter 4 2025 deficit was 2.4% of GDP on one measure and 1.1% on another.\n• The case for unreliability — the output gap: potential output is not observable at all. It is estimated, and revised years later, yet it is central to judging whether inflation is demand-driven and to assessing the structural fiscal position.\n• The consequence — policy lags: monetary policy acts with a lag of up to two years, so decisions taken on provisional data affect the economy long after the data have been revised. Goodhart\'s Law also applies: once a statistic becomes a target, behaviour adjusts to it and it ceases to measure what it did.\n• The case against — the alternative is worse: policy must be made. Acting on no data, or on anecdote and political intuition, is plainly inferior to acting on imperfect measurement. The question is comparative, and saying so is a discriminator.\n• The case against — errors are known and manageable: revisions are systematic and their typical size is documented, so policymakers can and do apply confidence intervals. The Monetary Policy Committee publishes fan charts precisely to represent uncertainty rather than conceal it, and votes are split — 6–3 in September 2026 — reflecting genuine disagreement about what the data show.\n• The case against — triangulation: no serious policymaker relies on one series. Payroll data, tax receipts, business surveys, vacancy data and market prices are used alongside official statistics, so a single unreliable series does not drive a decision.\n• The case against — direction beats level: policy usually depends on the direction and approximate magnitude of change, which are far more reliably measured than levels. Inflation rising from 2.6% to 3.1% is informative even if neither figure is exact.\n• Judgement: the statistics are unreliable enough to require humility, wide confidence intervals and gradualism in policy, but not so unreliable as to be useless. A defensible conclusion is that the right response is better data collection and more explicit treatment of uncertainty, not abandoning evidence-based policy.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) reliability problems developed across several series with accurate dated examples, the comparative test applied, triangulation and uncertainty management analysed, judgement supported. L4 (16–20) well-developed and mostly balanced. L3 (11–15) problems listed with some evaluation. L2 (6–10) description. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Extract B (lines 32–33) states: "Inflation rose to 3.1% in August 2026 and the Monetary Policy Committee voted 6–3 to hold Bank Rate at 3.75%." Using the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that the rise in inflation shown in the extract should have prompted an immediate increase in interest rates.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Establish the framework: the Bank of England has an operational target of 2% CPI inflation. At 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026, inflation was 1.1 percentage points above target and rising from 2.6% in June 2026. With Bank Rate at 3.75%, the real interest rate was around 0.65%, which is not restrictive.\n• The case for raising rates — expectations: the central risk is that above-target inflation becomes embedded in wage bargaining and price setting, shifting SRAS left and making the inflation self-sustaining. Once expectations are unanchored, disinflation requires a period of below-potential output, so the cost of acting late exceeds the cost of acting early. This asymmetry is the strongest argument for the three dissenting members.\n• The case for raising rates — credibility: a central bank that tolerates a rising overshoot risks losing the credibility on which cheap inflation control depends. Credibility is an asset that is slow to build and fast to lose.\n• The case for raising rates — the transmission lag: monetary policy affects inflation with a lag of up to two years, so waiting for confirmation in the data guarantees acting too late.\n• The case against raising rates — the nature of the shock: the rise was driven largely by petrol and diesel prices. A commodity price shock is a change in a price LEVEL that drops out of the twelve-month comparison automatically unless it repeats. Diagram: a leftward SRAS shift raising the price level while output falls. Raising rates in response compounds the output loss without addressing the cause — the classic stagflation dilemma.\n• The case against raising rates — the real economy: unemployment had risen to 4.9% from 4.7% and real GDP growth was weak. Tightening into a slowing economy risks opening a negative output gap, with hysteresis making part of the resulting unemployment permanent.\n• The case against raising rates — core inflation: the services contribution eased between June and August 2026, which suggests underlying domestic inflationary pressure was not intensifying. Core inflation, not headline, is the better guide to what monetary policy can influence.\n• The case against raising rates — data reliability: the ONS has warned that Labour Force Survey estimates are currently less reliable than usual, and CPI is subject to base effects. Acting decisively on a single month\'s reading is imprudent.\n• Alternatives to an immediate rise: clear forward guidance that rates will rise if second-round effects appear achieves much of the expectations benefit at none of the output cost; gradual moves in later meetings preserve optionality. Fiscal and supply-side measures address cost pressures more directly than monetary policy can.\n• Judgement: the case turns on whether the shock is one-off or the start of a persistent process, and that is genuinely uncertain — which is exactly why the vote was 6–3 rather than unanimous. A defensible conclusion is that holding was reasonable given the supply-side origin of the shock and the weakening labour market, PROVIDED the Committee was prepared to act quickly if wage growth or expectations moved. Equally defensible is the opposite conclusion, argued from the asymmetry of the cost of acting late.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) the shock correctly diagnosed with a diagram, both sides developed with chains of reasoning, lags, expectations and data reliability all used, the split vote interpreted, and a conditional judgement supported. L4 (16–20) well-developed and mostly balanced. L3 (11–15) some analysis, one side thin. L2 (6–10) assertion. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },

      { q: 'Discuss whether the four main macroeconomic objectives can be achieved simultaneously.', marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Set out the four: sustainable economic growth, low and stable inflation at the 2% CPI target, low unemployment, and a sustainable balance of payments position. Note that governments now add sound public finances, reduced inequality and environmental sustainability, which multiplies the potential conflicts.\n• Conflict 1 — growth and inflation: expanding AD to raise output and employment raises the price level, and the closer the economy is to capacity the more of the expansion appears as inflation rather than output. Diagram: AD shifting right along a steepening SRAS. This is the trade-off facing the Monetary Policy Committee, which held Bank Rate at 3.75% in September 2026 with inflation at 3.1%.\n• Conflict 2 — unemployment and inflation: the short-run Phillips curve implies that reducing unemployment below the natural rate raises inflation. If expectations adjust, the economy returns to the natural rate at higher inflation — the long-run Phillips curve is vertical — so the trade-off is temporary but the inflation is not.\n• Conflict 3 — growth and the current account: faster domestic growth raises imports, since imports have a high income elasticity of demand, so a consumption-led expansion widens the current account deficit. The UK deficit was 2.2% of GDP in 2024.\n• Conflict 4 — growth and the environment, and growth and inequality: growth achieved by depleting natural capital, or accruing to the top of the distribution, conflicts with sustainability and equity objectives that are now part of the policy set.\n• Conflict 5 — objectives and the public finances: expansionary fiscal policy to support growth and employment worsens the deficit and debt ratio, constraining future policy.\n• The case that simultaneous achievement IS possible — supply-side policy: policies that shift LRAS right — investment in infrastructure, education and skills, competition policy, innovation support, reducing labour immobility — raise output and employment WITHOUT raising the price level. Diagram: LRAS shifting right, with the price level falling and output rising. Because supply-side improvement also raises productivity and international competitiveness, it improves the current account too. This is the only route that improves all four together, and identifying it is the central analytical move.\n• The case that it is possible — the "non-inflationary consistent expansion" experience: periods of low inflation combined with steady growth and falling unemployment have occurred, often assisted by favourable global conditions such as cheap imports, so the conflicts are not inevitable at every moment.\n• Limitations of the supply-side answer: it works over years or decades, not within an electoral cycle; it has an upfront fiscal cost that worsens the deficit in the short run; the returns are uncertain; and some measures, such as deregulation or greater labour market flexibility, may worsen inequality even as they raise output.\n• Judgement: the objectives conflict in the SHORT run, where only AD can be moved quickly, and are complementary in the LONG run, where AS can be raised. A defensible conclusion is that simultaneous achievement is possible only through supply-side improvement and only over a long horizon, which is precisely why short-term demand management always involves choosing between them — and why policy assignment matters, with monetary policy assigned to inflation and supply-side policy to capacity.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) at least three conflicts analysed with accurate diagrams, the supply-side resolution developed and its limits evaluated, the short-run/long-run distinction used as the spine, dated evidence, judgement supported. L4 (16–20) well-developed and mostly balanced. L3 (11–15) conflicts listed with some analysis. L2 (6–10) objectives described. L1 (1–5) fragmentary.` },
    ]
  },

'3.2.2': {
    name: 'How the Macroeconomy Works',
    questions: [
      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nComponents of aggregate demand in an economy, £bn at constant prices\n  Household consumption (C):   1 742\n  Investment (I):                386\n  Government spending (G):       635\n  Exports (X):                   842\n  Imports (M):                   971\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate the level of aggregate demand. Give your answer in £bn to the nearest £bn.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `AD = C + I + G + (X - M).\nAD = 1 742 + 386 + 635 + (842 - 971)\nAD = 1 742 + 386 + 635 - 129 = £2 634bn.\n\n2 marks for £2 634bn. 1 mark for correct method (adding C, I and G and adding net trade) with an arithmetic slip, or for a correct answer with no working where the working is asked for. A candidate who adds imports rather than subtracting them (£2 892bn) earns 1 mark for method only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (line 6) — written for this practice question\n"In this economy the marginal propensity to consume out of additional income is estimated at 0.72."\n\nUsing Extract A, calculate the value of the simple national income multiplier. Give your answer to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Simple multiplier k = 1 / (1 - MPC) = 1 / (1 - 0.72) = 1 / 0.28 = 3.5714...\nAnswer: 3.57 (to two decimal places).\n\n2 marks for 3.57. 1 mark for the correct formula stated or for 1/0.28 shown with an arithmetic or rounding error (e.g. 3.6 or 3.58). No marks for 1/0.72 = 1.39, which inverts the MPS.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — written for this practice question\nEstimated marginal propensities for an open economy with a government sector\n  Marginal propensity to save (MPS):   0.12\n  Marginal propensity to tax (MPT):    0.28\n  Marginal propensity to import (MPM): 0.20\n\nUsing Extract B (Table 2), calculate the value of the national income multiplier. Give your answer to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `k = 1 / (MPS + MPT + MPM) = 1 / (0.12 + 0.28 + 0.20) = 1 / 0.60 = 1.6666...\nAnswer: 1.67 (to two decimal places).\n\n2 marks for 1.67. 1 mark for correctly summing the withdrawals to 0.60 but failing to invert, or for inverting with a rounding error. Accept 1 / (1 - MPC) where the candidate first derives MPC = 0.40 from 1 - 0.60.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nReal household disposable income and consumption, £bn\n                         Year 1     Year 2\n  Disposable income:      1 480      1 520\n  Consumption:            1 310      1 336\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate the marginal propensity to consume between Year 1 and Year 2. Give your answer to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `MPC = change in consumption / change in disposable income.\nChange in consumption = 1 336 - 1 310 = £26bn. Change in disposable income = 1 520 - 1 480 = £40bn.\nMPC = 26 / 40 = 0.65.\n\n2 marks for 0.65. 1 mark for correctly identifying both changes (£26bn and £40bn) without dividing, or for dividing the wrong way round (40/26 = 1.54). Note that the AVERAGE propensity to consume (1 336/1 520 = 0.88) is not creditworthy here.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 11-12) — written for this practice question\n"The national income multiplier in this economy is estimated at 1.67. The government has announced an additional £9bn of capital spending."\n\nUsing Extract C, calculate the eventual change in real national income. Give your answer in £bn to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Change in national income = multiplier x initial injection = 1.67 x £9bn = £15.03bn.\n\n2 marks for £15.03bn. 1 mark for the correct method (multiplier x injection) with an arithmetic slip. Candidates who add the injection again (£24.03bn) have double-counted: 1 mark for method only.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — written for this practice question\nHousehold sector accounts, £bn\n  Gross disposable income:  1 530\n  Household saving:           108\n\nUsing Extract B (Table 2), calculate the household saving ratio. Give your answer as a percentage to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Saving ratio = saving / gross disposable income x 100 = 108 / 1 530 x 100 = 7.0588...\nAnswer: 7.06%.\n\n2 marks for 7.06%. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic or rounding error, or for an answer given as a decimal (0.0706) without converting to a percentage.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nComponents of aggregate demand, £bn at constant prices\n  Household consumption (C):   1 742\n  Investment (I):                386\n  Government spending (G):       635\n  Net trade (X - M):            -129\n  Aggregate demand:            2 634\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that household consumption dominates aggregate demand in this economy.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• Consumption is £1 742bn out of £2 634bn, which is 66.1% of aggregate demand - roughly two thirds (1 mark for using the figure, a further mark for converting it to a share).\n• It is more than four times investment (1 742/386 = 4.5 times) and nearly three times government spending (1 mark).\n• Net trade is negative at -£129bn, so it subtracts from AD rather than adding to it, which raises the relative weight of consumption further (1 mark).\n• Implication explained: because C is so large a share, a given percentage change in consumption moves AD by far more than the same percentage change in investment - a 1% fall in C is £17.4bn, a 1% fall in I only £3.9bn (1 mark for this developed explanation).\n\nPoints: 4 marks for two figures used accurately AND an explanation of what they show; 3 marks for accurate data use with thinner explanation; 2 marks for data quoted with minimal explanation; 1 mark for a relevant assertion with no data.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Figure 1) — written for this practice question\nAnnual percentage change in real household consumption and real business investment\n              2022    2023    2024    2025    2026\n  Consumption: +2.1    +0.4    +0.8    +1.1    +0.9\n  Investment:  +7.8    -3.2    -2.4    +4.6    -1.8\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Figure 1) show that investment is more volatile than consumption.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• Investment growth ranges from +7.8% to -3.2%, a spread of 11.0 percentage points, while consumption ranges from +2.1% to +0.4%, a spread of only 1.7 percentage points (2 marks for the comparison of ranges with figures).\n• Investment changes sign four times over the period; consumption is positive in every year (1 mark).\n• Explanation of why: investment is a discretionary, forward-looking decision driven by expected profit and "animal spirits", and it can be postponed, whereas much consumption is of necessities smoothed against permanent income (1 mark).\n• Consequence: a given shock to confidence produces a much larger proportionate swing in I than in C, so investment contributes disproportionately to the trade cycle even though it is the smaller component (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Figure 2) — real data\nUK real GDP, quarterly growth and sector detail (ONS first quarterly estimate, published 13 August 2026)\n  Q1 2026 real GDP growth:   +0.6%\n  Q2 2026 real GDP growth:   +0.4%\n  Q2 2026, services:         +0.5%\n  Q2 2026, production:        0.0%\n  Q2 2026, construction:     +0.3%\n  Q2 2026 growth on the same quarter a year earlier: +1.2%\n\nExplain how the data in Extract B (Figure 2) show that UK growth slowed in the first half of 2026 and was narrowly based.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• Quarterly growth fell from 0.6% in Q1 2026 to 0.4% in Q2 2026, a slowdown of 0.2 percentage points - growth continued but at a slower rate, so this is a fall in the rate of growth, not a fall in output (2 marks: one for the figures, one for the correct distinction).\n• Growth was narrowly based because services, about four fifths of UK output, grew by 0.5% while production recorded no growth at all (0.0%) and construction grew by only 0.3% (1 mark).\n• Developed point: with production flat, the whole of the quarter's expansion is attributable to services, so the economy is exposed to any shock that hits that one sector (1 mark).\n• Annual growth of 1.2% is close to most estimates of the UK trend rate, so the data are consistent with an economy growing at about capacity rather than one in recession (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nHouse prices, household wealth and the saving ratio\n              Year 1    Year 2    Year 3\n  House price index (Year 1 = 100):  100      93       91\n  Household saving ratio (%):        5.4      7.8      8.6\n  Real consumption growth (%):      +1.9     -0.3     +0.2\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show a possible wealth effect on consumption.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• House prices fall by 9% over the period (index 100 to 91) while the saving ratio rises by 3.2 percentage points, from 5.4% to 8.6% (2 marks for accurate, paired use of both series).\n• Consumption growth turns negative in Year 2 (-0.3%) in the same year as the sharpest fall in house prices (1 mark).\n• Explanation of the mechanism: housing is the largest single asset of most UK households, so a fall in its value reduces perceived lifetime wealth and the collateral available for equity withdrawal, so households rebuild savings and cut discretionary spending (1 mark).\n• Qualification worth crediting: the data show correlation, not causation - the same fall in confidence, or a rise in interest rates, could be driving both series (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract C (Table 3) — written for this practice question\nExports and imports of goods and services, £bn at constant prices\n            Year 1    Year 2    Year 3\n  Exports:    805       824       842\n  Imports:    880       921       971\n\nExplain how the data in Extract C (Table 3) show that net trade was a drag on aggregate demand.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• Net trade is negative throughout: -£75bn, -£97bn and -£129bn (1 mark for calculating at least two of the three).\n• The deficit widens by £54bn over the period because imports grow faster than exports: imports rise by 10.3% (880 to 971) while exports rise by only 4.6% (805 to 842) (2 marks for the growth-rate comparison with figures).\n• Explanation: since AD = C + I + G + (X - M), a widening trade deficit means the (X - M) term subtracts more each year, so net trade reduces AD and, through the marginal propensity to import, reduces the size of the multiplier on any domestic injection (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — written for this practice question\nEstimated marginal propensities in two economies\n                     Country A     Country B\n  Marginal propensity to save:   0.10        0.14\n  Marginal propensity to tax:    0.25        0.30\n  Marginal propensity to import: 0.15        0.36\n\nExplain how the data in Extract B (Table 2) show that a fiscal stimulus would raise national income by more in Country A than in Country B.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• Total marginal withdrawals: Country A = 0.10 + 0.25 + 0.15 = 0.50; Country B = 0.14 + 0.30 + 0.36 = 0.80 (1 mark).\n• Multipliers: A = 1/0.50 = 2.00; B = 1/0.80 = 1.25 (2 marks for both correct).\n• Explanation: the multiplier depends on how much of each round of extra income is passed on as further domestic spending. In Country B four fifths of any extra income leaks out into saving, tax and imports, so the successive rounds die away quickly; in Country A only half leaks, so the process runs further (1 mark).\n• Development worth crediting: Country B's very high MPM of 0.36 means much of any stimulus raises output abroad rather than at home (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 4-5) states: "Survey measures of consumer confidence fell sharply during the year, and households began to rebuild their savings."\n\nWith the help of an AD/AS diagram, explain the likely effect of a fall in consumer confidence on real output and the price level.`, marks: 9, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: AD/AS with AD shifting left from AD1 to AD2; equilibrium moves from Y1/P1 to Y2/P2. Label axes "real output (real GDP)" and "average price level" - not "quantity" and "price".\n• Chain of reasoning: lower confidence about job security and future income raises precautionary saving, so the marginal propensity to consume falls and planned consumption falls at every price level. Because consumption is roughly two thirds of UK AD, a small percentage fall in C is a large absolute fall in AD.\n• The multiplier magnifies the initial fall: lower spending becomes lower income for firms and their workers, who cut spending in turn, so the final fall in real output exceeds the initial fall in consumption - a negative multiplier.\n• The split between output and price effects depends on the slope of SRAS at the starting point. On a Keynesian AS curve with a large negative output gap, output falls and the price level barely moves; near full capacity the price level falls more and output less.\n• Dated application: UK real GDP growth slowed from 0.6% in Q1 2026 to 0.4% in Q2 2026 (ONS, 13 August 2026), and the UK unemployment rate had risen to 4.9% in May-July 2026 (ONS, September 2026), a labour market backdrop that makes precautionary saving more likely.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate labelled diagram plus a sustained chain of analysis linking confidence to C, to AD, to output and prices; L2 (4-6) diagram and some analysis, or good analysis with a flawed diagram; L1 (1-3) description or definition only, diagram missing or seriously mislabelled.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 9-10) states: "Protracted conflict in the Middle East has pushed crude oil prices higher and more volatile, and firms report rising energy and freight costs."\n\nWith the help of an AD/AS diagram, analyse the effect of a sustained rise in oil prices on a national economy.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: SRAS shifts left (up) from SRAS1 to SRAS2 with AD unchanged; the price level rises from P1 to P2 and real output falls from Y1 to Y2 - the classic stagflation result of higher prices with lower output.\n• Chain of reasoning: oil is a near-universal input, directly through fuel and petrochemicals and indirectly through freight and utilities. Higher input costs raise firms' costs of production at every price level, so less is supplied at any given price.\n• Second-round effects: if workers bargain for higher nominal wages to protect real pay, SRAS shifts left again, embedding the shock; this is precisely the "second-round effects" risk the MPC identified when it held Bank Rate at 3.75% on 16 September 2026, with three of nine members voting for 4%.\n• An oil-importing economy also suffers a demand-side effect: the higher import bill is a withdrawal from the circular flow and worsens the terms of trade, shifting AD left as well, which deepens the output loss but partly offsets the price rise.\n• Dated application: UK CPI inflation rose to 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026, up from 2.9% in July, with the largest upward contribution from transport, where prices rose 4.6% over the year, driven by motor fuels (ONS).\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram plus developed analysis of both the cost mechanism and the output/price consequences; L2 (4-6) diagram with limited analysis; L1 (1-3) assertion that oil prices raise costs, with no diagram or an incorrect one.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 2-3) states: "Economists disagree about the shape of the long-run aggregate supply curve, and that disagreement shapes what they expect demand management to achieve."\n\nWith the help of a diagram, analyse the difference between the classical and the Keynesian view of long-run aggregate supply.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: either two panels or one composite. Classical LRAS is a vertical line at the full-employment level of output Yfe. The Keynesian AS curve is horizontal (or very shallow) at low output, upward sloping as spare capacity is used up, and vertical at full capacity.\n• Classical analysis: markets clear because prices and wages are flexible, so output returns to Yfe regardless of AD. An increase in AD therefore raises only the price level in the long run - demand management is inflationary and ineffective, and only supply-side reform can raise output.\n• Keynesian analysis: wages are sticky downwards, so an economy can settle at an under-full-employment equilibrium and remain there. On the horizontal range an increase in AD raises output with little price effect, so demand management works.\n• The decisive analytical point is that the two models give the same answer near full capacity and opposite answers in a deep recession - so the policy disagreement is really about how much spare capacity exists, which is why output-gap estimation matters so much.\n• Application: with UK annual growth of 1.2% in Q2 2026 and unemployment at 4.9% in May-July 2026, the UK looks close to, rather than far below, capacity - which weakens the case for pure demand expansion.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) both curves drawn accurately and the policy implication of each analysed; L2 (4-6) both described with limited analysis of consequences; L1 (1-3) one model only, or a diagram without explanation.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 14-15) states: "The government has announced a multi-year programme of investment in transport, energy and digital infrastructure."\n\nWith the help of a diagram, analyse why public investment in infrastructure may shift both aggregate demand and long-run aggregate supply.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: AD shifts right from AD1 to AD2 AND LRAS shifts right from LRAS1 to LRAS2; output rises from Y1 to Y2 with the price level rising by less than it would if AD alone had shifted (or unchanged if the shifts are matched).\n• Demand-side chain: infrastructure spending is government spending (G) and stimulates private investment (I) in the supply chain; the initial injection is multiplied through the circular flow as contractors' wages become consumption, so the final rise in AD exceeds the spending.\n• Supply-side chain: better transport cuts journey times and firms' logistics costs, reliable energy and digital networks raise capital productivity, and the improved capital stock raises the productive potential of the economy - the LRAS shift.\n• Timing is the key analytical distinction: the AD effect arrives during construction, the LRAS effect only when the asset is in use and may take a decade. In the interval the economy gets the inflationary effect without the disinflationary one.\n• Evaluation of the mechanism worth crediting: the LRAS shift depends on the projects being well chosen; crowding out is a risk if the economy is near capacity, and UK 30-year gilt yields around 5.9% in early September 2026, their highest since the late 1990s, make debt-financed capital spending more costly.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) both shifts drawn and both chains of reasoning developed, with the timing distinction; L2 (4-6) one shift analysed well or both asserted; L1 (1-3) description of infrastructure spending with no model.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 6-7) states: "Officials estimate that each £1bn of extra public spending eventually raises national income by rather more than £1bn."\n\nAnalyse how the multiplier process transmits an initial increase in government spending through the circular flow of income.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Definition and formula: the multiplier is the ratio of the final change in national income to the initial change in injections, k = 1/(1 - MPC) in a closed economy with no government, or 1/(MPS + MPT + MPM) in an open economy with a government sector.\n• The process round by round: the government pays contractors (round 1); the contractors' wages are income for households, who spend the fraction MPC of it (round 2); that spending is income for other firms, who pay wages that are spent again (round 3), and so on. Each round is smaller than the last because of withdrawals.\n• Worked illustration: with MPS 0.12, MPT 0.28 and MPM 0.20, withdrawals total 0.60 and k = 1.67, so £9bn of extra capital spending eventually raises national income by £15.03bn.\n• Analysis of what determines the size: the larger the leakages into saving, taxation and imports, the smaller the multiplier. A large marginal propensity to import means much of the stimulus raises foreign output.\n• Capacity matters: the multiplier raises REAL output only if there is spare capacity. At full capacity the extra spending raises the price level instead, and the real multiplier collapses towards zero.\n• Time lags: the rounds take quarters, not weeks, so the full effect arrives well after the decision - a reason fiscal fine-tuning is difficult.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) formula, round-by-round mechanism, determinants of size and at least one qualification; L2 (4-6) formula and mechanism with limited analysis; L1 (1-3) definition or formula alone.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 1-2) states: "The aggregate demand curve slopes downwards, but not for the same reasons as the demand curve for a single good."\n\nWith the help of a diagram, analyse why the aggregate demand curve slopes downwards from left to right.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: AD curve downward sloping, axes "average price level" (vertical) and "real output" (horizontal). Best answers also mark two price levels and the corresponding output levels.\n• Why the micro explanation does NOT apply: for a single good, a price fall makes consumers substitute towards it from other goods. At the aggregate level there is nothing outside the economy to substitute towards, so a different explanation is required.\n• Real balance (wealth) effect: a higher price level reduces the real value of money-denominated wealth, so households feel poorer and consume less.\n• Interest rate effect: a higher price level raises the nominal demand for money for transactions; given the money supply this raises interest rates, which reduces interest-sensitive consumption and investment.\n• International competitiveness (net exports) effect: a higher domestic price level with a given nominal exchange rate makes domestic goods dearer relative to foreign goods, so exports fall and imports rise and (X - M) falls.\n• Analysis worth crediting: the three effects differ in strength between economies - the net-export effect is strong for a very open economy with a high MPM, weak for a large closed one.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) diagram plus all three effects explained as chains of reasoning; L2 (4-6) diagram plus two effects, or three effects listed without development; L1 (1-3) assertion that lower prices mean more demand.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 8-9) states: "Firms invest to expand capacity, so investment depends less on the level of national income than on the rate at which it is changing."\n\nAnalyse how the accelerator and the multiplier can interact to amplify fluctuations in national income.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The accelerator: net investment depends on the RATE OF CHANGE of output, because firms add capital only when existing capacity is insufficient. If the capital-output ratio is, say, 2, then an increase in expected output of £1bn requires £2bn of new capital.\n• Consequence: output growth can slow while still positive, and net investment then falls absolutely. This is why investment turns negative in years when consumption is still growing (see the volatility of investment relative to consumption in the data).\n• The multiplier: any change in investment changes income by a multiple of itself, k x change in I.\n• The interaction: a rise in AD raises output; accelerating output raises net investment; higher investment raises AD again through the multiplier; that raises output faster still. The same loop works in reverse in a downturn, producing a sharp, self-reinforcing contraction.\n• Analysis of limits: the upswing is capped by full capacity and the availability of finance; the downswing is floored because gross investment cannot fall below zero and replacement investment continues. Expectations and "animal spirits" can break the mechanical link in either direction.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) both mechanisms explained and the interaction traced through a full cycle, with limits; L2 (4-6) both defined with a partial link; L1 (1-3) one concept defined only.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 3-4) states: "Households responded to uncertainty by saving more, and the economy ended the year poorer than it began."\n\nWith the help of a diagram, analyse the paradox of thrift.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: either an AD/AS diagram with AD shifting left as consumption falls, or an injections/withdrawals diagram in which the S+T+M line pivots upwards and equilibrium national income falls from Y1 to Y2, with the level of saving at the new equilibrium no higher than before.\n• The paradox: saving is virtuous for an individual household, since it raises that household's future consumption. If every household saves more simultaneously, aggregate consumption falls, AD falls, and national income falls by a multiple of the initial fall.\n• Because saving is a function of income, the fall in income reduces saving again. At the new, lower equilibrium, planned saving must once more equal planned investment, so the attempt to save more can leave total saving unchanged - or lower, if lower income and confidence also cut investment.\n• Analysis of the fallacy of composition: what is true of the part is not true of the whole, because one household's spending is another's income. This is the central Keynesian objection to treating the national budget like a household budget.\n• Qualification worth crediting: the paradox bites in a demand-deficient economy with spare capacity. In a supply-constrained economy higher saving finances investment and raises long-run growth, which is the classical view.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram, the circular chain of reasoning and the fallacy of composition made explicit, with a qualification; L2 (4-6) the paradox described with a partial chain; L1 (1-3) definition of saving or assertion only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 10-11) states: "The Monetary Policy Committee voted by a majority of 6-3 to maintain Bank Rate at 3.75% at its meeting ending on 16 September 2026."\n\nAnalyse the channels through which a change in Bank Rate is transmitted to aggregate demand.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Market rates channel: Bank Rate is the rate the Bank of England pays on commercial banks' reserves, so it anchors money-market rates and feeds into mortgage, loan and deposit rates - though with a lag, since most UK mortgages are now fixed for two or five years, so a change reaches households only as fixes expire.\n• Cost of borrowing / reward for saving: a higher rate raises the cost of servicing debt and of new borrowing and raises the return on saving, so interest-sensitive consumption (cars, housing, consumer durables) and business investment fall. AD falls.\n• Asset prices and the wealth effect: higher rates raise the discount rate applied to future income, lowering bond, equity and house prices; lower wealth lowers consumption.\n• Exchange rate channel: higher UK rates relative to abroad attract capital inflows, raising demand for sterling. An appreciation lowers import prices and raises export prices, cutting net exports and also cutting imported inflation directly.\n• Expectations and confidence: the decision itself signals the Bank's judgement. With CPI inflation at 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026 and rising, holding rather than cutting was a signal about the Bank's tolerance for second-round effects.\n• Analysis of strength and lag: the Bank's own working assumption is that policy takes up to two years to have its full effect on inflation, so the channels operate on the economy of 2027-28, not of today.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) at least three channels each developed as a chain of reasoning, plus lags or relative strength; L2 (4-6) two or three channels asserted with limited development; L1 (1-3) "higher rates reduce spending" with no mechanism.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 16-17) states: "Sterling weakened against the euro over the year, trading around 1.16 euros in September 2026."\n\nWith the help of an AD/AS diagram, analyse the effect of a depreciation of the exchange rate on real output and the price level.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: AD shifts right (higher net exports) AND, in the strongest answers, SRAS shifts left (dearer imported inputs). Output rises from Y1 to Y2 and the price level rises from P1 to P2 - the price effect is unambiguous, the output effect is not.\n• Demand-side chain (SPICED in reverse): a weaker pound makes exports cheaper in foreign currency and imports dearer in sterling, so export volumes rise and import volumes fall, raising (X - M) and shifting AD right, magnified by the multiplier.\n• Supply-side chain: the UK imports energy, food and components, so a depreciation raises firms' input costs, shifting SRAS left and raising the price level while reducing output - a partial offset to the demand-side gain.\n• The Marshall-Lerner condition: net exports improve only if the sum of the price elasticities of demand for exports and imports exceeds one. In the short run both are low, so the value of the trade balance can initially worsen - the J-curve.\n• Dated application: sterling traded around 1.16 euros in September 2026 after the Bank of England held Bank Rate at 3.75% on 17 September while the ECB had raised its deposit rate to 2.50% on 10 September - a narrowing interest differential is a standard depreciation mechanism.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) both shifts drawn or the SRAS offset explicitly analysed, with elasticity conditions; L2 (4-6) AD shift only, correctly analysed; L1 (1-3) "a weaker pound helps exporters" with no model.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nContributions to the change in real aggregate demand, percentage points\n                        Year 1   Year 2   Year 3\n  Consumption:            +0.7     -0.2     +0.1\n  Investment:             +0.6     -0.3     -0.2\n  Government spending:    +0.4     +0.5     +0.6\n  Net trade:              -0.3     -0.4     -0.1\n  Total change in AD:     +1.4     -0.4     +0.4\n\nExtract B (lines 5-6) — written for this practice question\n"Survey measures of consumer confidence fell to their lowest level for three years in Year 2, and import prices rose by 6% over the same period."\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data suggest that the contraction of aggregate demand in Year 2 was caused by weaker household confidence rather than by dearer imports?`, marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Support for the confidence explanation: consumption swings from +0.7 to -0.2 percentage points, a deterioration of 0.9 points, the largest single swing in the table, and Extract B dates the three-year low in confidence to the same year. Investment also swings by 0.9 points, consistent with a general collapse in "animal spirits" affecting households and firms together.\n• Support for the import-price explanation: net trade deteriorates from -0.3 to -0.4 points, and Extract B reports a 6% rise in import prices. Dearer imports also reduce real disposable income, so part of the consumption fall may itself be an import-price effect operating through real incomes rather than through sentiment.\n• Against the import explanation: the net trade contribution worsens by only 0.1 percentage points, one ninth of the consumption swing, so on the arithmetic of the table net trade cannot account for the contraction. Note also that a rise in import PRICES with unchanged volumes reduces the value of net trade but need not reduce real AD by much.\n• Limitations of the data: contributions are not causes - they are accounting shares. Consumption and confidence may both be responding to a third factor such as higher interest rates or rising unemployment. The extract gives no data on interest rates, incomes or the labour market.\n• Only three years are shown, there is no indication of whether Year 2 was a one-off or a trend, and confidence is a survey measure with known bias towards over-reacting to news.\n• Judgement: the data are consistent with confidence being the larger proximate cause, because the consumption and investment swings dwarf the net trade swing, but they cannot establish causation and dearer imports plausibly operate THROUGH consumption rather than separately from it.\n\nLevels: L3 (8-10) uses the data quantitatively on both sides, recognises what the data cannot show, and reaches a supported judgement; L2 (4-7) uses some data and offers a partial judgement; L1 (1-3) asserts an answer or describes the table without using it. A response that reaches a conclusion without reference to the data cannot exceed L1.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nEstimated national income multipliers, selected studies\n  Study 1 (tax cut, economy near capacity):          0.4\n  Study 2 (tax cut, economy in deep recession):      0.9\n  Study 3 (public investment, near capacity):        0.6\n  Study 4 (public investment, deep recession):       1.6\nMarginal propensity to import, UK economy: 0.30\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data suggest that fiscal stimulus is an effective way of raising real output?`, marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Evidence that it can be effective: the public investment multiplier in a deep recession is 1.6, so £10bn of spending raises national income by £16bn - the injection more than pays for itself in output terms and, through higher tax receipts, partly finances itself.\n• Evidence that it is often ineffective: three of the four estimates are BELOW one, meaning the stimulus raises national income by less than it costs. Near capacity the tax-cut multiplier is only 0.4, so 60p in every pound leaks away into saving, tax and imports or is crowded out.\n• The decisive pattern in the data is the state-dependence: the same instrument has a multiplier four times larger in a deep recession (1.6) than near capacity (0.6). This is exactly what the Keynesian AS curve predicts - on the flat range extra AD raises output, near the vertical range it raises prices.\n• Composition matters too: investment multipliers exceed tax-cut multipliers in both states, because a tax cut is partly saved whereas public investment is spent in full at the first round and also raises LRAS.\n• Limitations: four point estimates from unnamed studies, no confidence intervals, no indication of country, period or method; multiplier estimates in the literature range very widely. The MPM of 0.30 is given but not linked to the estimates.\n• Application: UK annual growth of 1.2% in the year to Q2 2026 and unemployment of 4.9% in May-July 2026 suggest an economy close to capacity rather than in deep recession - on these data, the relevant multipliers are the low ones.\n• Judgement: the data suggest fiscal stimulus is effective in a deep recession and weak otherwise, so the answer is "it depends on the output gap and on what the money is spent on" rather than a general yes or no.\n\nLevels: L3 (8-10) quantitative use of the data, recognition of their limits, and a supported conditional judgement; L2 (4-7) partial use with a thin judgement; L1 (1-3) assertion. A response that reaches a conclusion without reference to the data cannot exceed L1.` },

      { q: `Explain the main determinants of the level of household consumption in an economy.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Real disposable income is the dominant determinant. The consumption function C = a + bYd makes consumption the sum of autonomous consumption and the marginal propensity to consume times disposable income, so any change in wages, employment or the tax and benefit system moves consumption directly. The UK unemployment rate rose to 4.9% in May-July 2026 with payrolled employees down 145,000 on the year (ONS), which reduces aggregate disposable income.\n• Interest rates. Higher rates raise the cost of servicing and taking on debt and raise the reward for saving, cutting interest-sensitive consumption. Bank Rate stood at 3.75% in September 2026. The transmission is slowed in the UK because most mortgages are fixed, so the effect arrives as fixes roll off.\n• Wealth and asset prices. Housing is the largest asset of most households; rising house prices raise perceived lifetime wealth and the collateral available for borrowing, so consumption rises. Falling asset prices work in reverse.\n• Consumer confidence and expectations. Expected future income, job security and expected inflation all shift the consumption function independently of current income. Expectations of higher future inflation can bring purchases forward; expectations of unemployment raise precautionary saving.\n• Availability of credit. Consumption depends not only on the price of credit but on whether banks will lend. Tighter lending standards or loan-to-value limits cut consumption even at unchanged interest rates.\n• Distribution of income. Lower-income households have a higher MPC, so a given national income distributed more equally supports more consumption than the same income concentrated at the top.\n• Theoretical framing worth crediting: the permanent income and life-cycle hypotheses argue that households smooth consumption against expected LIFETIME income, so temporary income changes (a one-off tax rebate) have a much smaller effect than permanent ones.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several determinants developed as chains of reasoning, with theory and dated application; L2 (6-10) determinants explained with limited development or application; L1 (1-5) a list of factors with little explanation.` },

      { q: `Explain the main determinants of the level of investment in an economy.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Expected rate of return relative to the cost of borrowing. A project is undertaken if its expected yield exceeds the interest rate, so the marginal efficiency of capital schedule slopes downwards against the interest rate. With Bank Rate at 3.75% in September 2026 and 10-year gilt yields around 5.2% in early September 2026, the hurdle rate facing UK firms is materially higher than in the 2010s.\n• Business confidence and "animal spirits". Keynes's point is that returns are estimated over years that cannot be known, so investment depends on sentiment, which is volatile and self-reinforcing. This is the main reason investment is the most volatile component of AD.\n• The rate of change of demand - the accelerator. Firms invest to add capacity, so net investment depends on how fast output is growing, not on its level. Output growth that slows from 0.6% to 0.4% a quarter can therefore be associated with falling net investment.\n• Corporate taxation and allowances. Corporation tax, capital allowances and R&D tax credits change the post-tax return on a project and so shift the whole MEC schedule.\n• Availability and cost of finance. Retained profits are the main source of UK corporate investment finance; credit conditions, risk premia and equity market valuations determine the rest.\n• Technological change and the price of capital goods. A new technology that raises expected productivity raises the MEC at every interest rate; cheaper capital goods do the same.\n• Regulation, planning and policy uncertainty. Long-lived assets are exposed to policy risk, so uncertainty about tax, trade or planning rules raises the effective hurdle rate and delays projects - the option value of waiting.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several determinants developed with a clear distinction between what moves along the MEC schedule (interest rates) and what shifts it; L2 (6-10) determinants explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Explain the main factors that determine the size of the national income multiplier.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The formula frames the answer: k = 1/(MPS + MPT + MPM). Anything that raises marginal withdrawals shrinks the multiplier, because each round of spending passes on less to the next.\n• Marginal propensity to save. Cautious households with high precautionary saving break the chain earlier. Saving behaviour itself depends on confidence and on the age structure of the population.\n• Marginal propensity to tax. A more progressive tax system raises the marginal tax rate and lowers the multiplier - which is also why progressive taxes act as automatic stabilisers, damping the cycle in both directions.\n• Marginal propensity to import. The more open the economy, the more of each round of spending goes abroad. This is a significant constraint for the UK, which ran a current account deficit of £15.1bn, 1.9% of GDP, in Q2 2026.\n• The amount of spare capacity. The multiplier raises REAL output only if firms can supply more. At full capacity the extra demand raises the price level, and the REAL multiplier falls towards zero even if the nominal one does not.\n• The monetary policy reaction. If the central bank responds to a fiscal expansion by raising interest rates to hold inflation at target, the induced fall in C and I offsets part of the stimulus, so the effective multiplier is smaller. At the zero lower bound, when no offset occurs, multipliers are estimated to be much larger.\n• Crowding out. Government borrowing that raises gilt yields reduces private investment, again shrinking the effective multiplier - a live constraint with 30-year gilt yields near 5.9% in early September 2026.\n• What the stimulus is spent on. Spending directed at low-income households, who have a high MPC, or at domestically produced capital goods, generates larger multipliers than a tax cut that is largely saved.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) the formula plus several determinants developed, including at least one that is not a leakage (capacity, policy reaction, crowding out); L2 (6-10) leakages explained only; L1 (1-5) the formula stated with little explanation.` },

      { q: `Explain the main causes of a rightward shift in the long-run aggregate supply curve.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• LRAS represents the productive potential of the economy, so it shifts only when the quantity or quality of the factors of production changes - not when demand or prices change.\n• Quantity of labour. Net inward migration, a rising participation rate or a falling economic inactivity rate raise the labour supply. The UK inactivity rate was 20.9% in May-July 2026, down 0.1 percentage points on the year (ONS) - a small rightward influence.\n• Quality of labour (human capital). Education, vocational training and apprenticeships raise output per worker; the effect is large but arrives over decades.\n• Quantity of capital. Net investment adds to the capital stock. Public infrastructure investment raises the productivity of private capital as well as adding to it directly.\n• Technological progress and innovation. The main long-run driver of productivity growth; supported by R&D tax credits, patent protection and a research base. Its effect is to raise output from unchanged inputs.\n• Institutional and efficiency improvements. Competition policy, deregulation of product markets, improved labour market flexibility and lower search frictions reduce the natural rate of unemployment and raise allocative and productive efficiency.\n• Discovery of natural resources or improved access to them, and gains from trade and specialisation according to comparative advantage, which raise the output attainable from a given endowment.\n• Analysis worth crediting: most of these are slow and uncertain, which is why the UK's productivity problem has proved so persistent - and why classical economists argue that only LRAS-shifting policy can raise output sustainably, while a demand stimulus merely raises the price level.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several causes developed with clear reasoning about the mechanism and its timescale; L2 (6-10) causes explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list of supply-side factors.` },

      { q: `Explain the main causes of a fall in aggregate demand in an open economy.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• A fall in consumption, the largest component. Causes include falling real disposable income, rising unemployment, higher interest rates raising the cost of debt service, falling asset prices reducing wealth, and a collapse in consumer confidence raising precautionary saving.\n• A fall in investment. Higher interest rates or gilt yields raise the hurdle rate; weaker expected demand cuts the accelerator term; policy or geopolitical uncertainty raises the option value of waiting. Investment is small but highly volatile, so it contributes disproportionately to falls in AD.\n• A fall in government spending or a rise in taxation. Fiscal consolidation to meet a fiscal rule reduces G directly and reduces disposable income through higher taxes, so it hits both G and C.\n• A fall in net exports. An appreciation of the exchange rate, a recession among trading partners, or protectionist measures abroad all reduce X. The sharp rise in US tariffs since 2025 - the US average effective tariff rate reached 18.2% by August 2025, the highest in about 90 years - is a live example of an external demand shock.\n• A rise in the price level, which moves the economy ALONG the AD curve rather than shifting it, through the real balance, interest rate and international competitiveness effects. Strong answers make this distinction explicitly.\n• A tightening of credit conditions. A banking crisis or a rise in risk premia cuts lending to households and firms even at unchanged policy rates.\n• The multiplier magnifies every one of these: an initial fall in any injection is transmitted round the circular flow, so the final fall in national income is a multiple of the initial one, and the accelerator can add a further contraction in investment.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several causes developed with mechanisms, including the shift/movement distinction and the multiplier; L2 (6-10) causes explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list of AD components.` },

      { q: `Explain the main factors that determine the position and slope of the short-run aggregate supply curve.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Why SRAS slopes upwards: in the short run some input prices, above all nominal wages, are fixed by contract. A higher output price with fixed input costs raises profit margins, so firms expand output. Rising marginal costs as capacity is approached reinforce this.\n• Wage costs shift the curve. A rise in nominal wages, the National Living Wage, or employers' National Insurance raises unit labour costs at every price level and shifts SRAS left. UK CPI services inflation was 3.4% in the 12 months to August 2026, a common proxy for domestically generated wage-driven cost pressure.\n• Raw material and energy prices. Oil is the classic case: the MPC noted in September 2026 that conflict in the Middle East had pushed crude and refined energy prices higher and more volatile.\n• The exchange rate. A depreciation raises the sterling cost of imported energy, food and components, shifting SRAS left, and vice versa. Sterling traded around 1.16 euros in September 2026.\n• Indirect taxes and subsidies. VAT, duties and green levies raise costs at every price level; subsidies lower them.\n• Productivity. Higher output per worker lowers unit costs and shifts SRAS right - the only one of these shifts that also moves LRAS.\n• Regulation and non-wage costs, such as compliance and reporting requirements, which raise costs without raising output.\n• Analysis of the slope itself: SRAS becomes steeper as spare capacity is used up, because bottlenecks and overtime premia raise marginal cost sharply. This is why the same rise in AD is more inflationary in a boom - the crucial link to the output gap.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) the reason for the slope explained AND several shift factors developed, with the distinction between the two kept clear; L2 (6-10) shift factors only, or the slope only; L1 (1-5) a list of costs.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 18-19) states: "Some economists argue that the Keynesian aggregate supply curve, with its long flat range, describes the modern UK economy better than the vertical classical curve."\n\nUsing the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that the Keynesian model of aggregate supply is more useful than the classical model for analysing the modern UK economy.`, marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For the Keynesian model: it allows an under-full-employment equilibrium that can persist, which matches the UK's experience after 2008, when output stayed below the pre-crisis trend for more than a decade. Downward nominal wage rigidity is well evidenced - firms cut hours and hiring rather than pay.\n• For the Keynesian model: it explains why demand management works in a slump. With a large negative output gap, an AD expansion raises real output with little price effect, which justifies automatic stabilisers and discretionary stimulus.\n• For the classical model: the UK in 2026 does not look demand-deficient. Annual growth was 1.2% in the year to Q2 2026, unemployment was 4.9% in May-July 2026 - low by historical standards - and CPI inflation was 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026 and rising. An economy with above-target inflation and near-trend growth is behaving as the classical model predicts, with AD increases feeding into prices.\n• For the classical model: the UK's central problem since 2008 has been productivity, which is an LRAS problem no amount of demand can solve. The classical model's policy implication - supply-side reform - addresses the actual constraint.\n• Synthesis: the two are not rivals over the same range. The Keynesian curve IS the classical curve on its vertical section; the disagreement is empirical, about how much spare capacity exists. The practical difficulty is that the output gap is unobservable and revised heavily after the event, so policymakers cannot reliably tell which range they are on.\n• Evaluation - it depends on the question being asked: for analysing a recession, the Keynesian model; for analysing the inflation of 2022-26 or the long-run growth rate, the classical model. A model that is useful for one purpose is not thereby useful for all.\n• Evaluation - the modern consensus embeds both: central banks operate a short-run Phillips curve trade-off (Keynesian) around a long-run vertical supply constraint (classical), which is precisely what the MPC's September 2026 decision to hold at 3.75% while three members voted for 4% reflects - a judgement about how much slack remains.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer takes a clear position and defends it against the strongest opposing argument, most persuasively by arguing that usefulness is state-dependent and giving a criterion for deciding which state the UK is in.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, analysis and evaluation integrated, conclusion follows from the argument; L4 (16-20) well-developed and mostly balanced with sound evaluation; L3 (11-15) some analysis and evaluation, uneven balance; L2 (6-10) largely descriptive, one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 7-8) states: "Estimates of the fiscal multiplier vary from 0.4 to 1.6 depending on the state of the economy and on what the money is spent on."\n\nUsing the extracts and your knowledge of economics, assess the view that the multiplier makes fiscal expansion an effective way to raise real output.`, marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For: the multiplier means an injection raises national income by more than itself, so a given output gap can be closed with less borrowing than the gap implies. Where the multiplier exceeds one, higher activity raises tax receipts and lowers benefit spending, so the stimulus partly finances itself.\n• For: public investment multipliers are the largest in the estimates, and infrastructure spending also shifts LRAS right, so it raises both actual and potential output - a double dividend that tax cuts do not deliver.\n• Against - leakages: with UK marginal propensities to save, tax and import that are substantial, and an economy that imports enough to run a current account deficit of 1.9% of GDP in Q2 2026, a large share of each round leaks abroad or into the Exchequer rather than circulating domestically.\n• Against - capacity: the real multiplier depends on spare capacity. With UK unemployment at 4.9% and annual growth of 1.2% in the year to Q2 2026, the economy is near trend, so extra AD is more likely to raise the price level than real output - especially with CPI inflation already at 3.1% in the year to August 2026.\n• Against - the monetary offset: an independent MPC targeting 2% inflation will tighten in response to a fiscal expansion. With three of nine members already voting for 4% in September 2026, a large stimulus would very likely be met with higher Bank Rate, cancelling much of the effect.\n• Against - crowding out and the fiscal constraint: UK public sector net debt was 93.8% of GDP at the end of August 2026 and 30-year gilt yields reached about 5.9% in early September 2026, their highest since the late 1990s. Extra borrowing at those yields raises debt interest and can raise the cost of capital for private firms.\n• Against - lags: recognition, decision and implementation lags mean discretionary stimulus frequently arrives after the downturn it was meant to offset, and can then be pro-cyclical.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer argues conditionally and specifies the conditions - the multiplier makes fiscal expansion effective when there is a large negative output gap, monetary policy is at the lower bound and the spending is on domestically produced investment; it makes it largely ineffective when, as in the UK in 2026, the economy is near capacity, inflation is above target and borrowing costs are high.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated evaluation with a conclusion that follows; L4 (16-20) well-developed, mostly balanced; L3 (11-15) some evaluation, uneven; L2 (6-10) descriptive, one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Discuss the view that changes in consumer confidence are the most important determinant of aggregate demand in the UK.`, marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For: consumption is about two thirds of UK aggregate demand, so anything that moves the consumption function moves AD more than an equal proportionate change in any other component. Confidence shifts the function independently of current income, through precautionary saving.\n• For: confidence is a common factor. The same news that makes households cautious makes firms cautious, so a confidence shock hits C and I together and the multiplier-accelerator interaction amplifies it - which is why AD swings can be so much larger than the shock that started them.\n• For: confidence is self-fulfilling. Expectations of recession cause the spending cuts that cause the recession, which is Keynes's point about animal spirits and the reason expectations management (forward guidance, fiscal credibility) is a policy instrument in its own right.\n• Against - income and interest rates dominate: the consumption function makes real disposable income the main determinant, and confidence usually MOVES WITH income rather than independently of it. Rising unemployment (4.9% in May-July 2026, up 0.2 percentage points on the year) lowers both income and confidence, so attributing the fall to confidence may be double counting.\n• Against - volatility is concentrated in investment: investment swings by far more in percentage terms than consumption, so the marginal contribution of investment to changes in AD is larger than its 15% or so share suggests.\n• Against - policy and external shocks: Bank Rate moved from 0.1% in 2020 to 5.25% in 2023 and back to 3.75% by September 2026; the US average effective tariff rate reached 18.2% by August 2025. Shocks of that scale move AD regardless of sentiment.\n• Against - measurement: confidence indices are surveys of stated feeling, not of behaviour, and are known to over-react to news headlines; the relationship between the index and actual spending is loose.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer distinguishes the largest component (consumption) from the most volatile determinant (investment and confidence) and from the most powerful policy lever (interest rates), and concludes that confidence is best understood as an amplifier of other shocks rather than an independent cause - or defends the opposite with equal rigour.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed, mostly balanced; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 20-21) states: "Conflict in the Middle East has pushed energy prices higher and more volatile, and UK inflation rose again in August 2026."\n\nUsing the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that supply-side shocks are now a more important source of macroeconomic instability than demand-side shocks.`, marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For: the inflation of 2021-23 and the renewed rise of 2026 are both cost-push in origin. UK CPI inflation rose to 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026 from 2.9% in July, with the largest upward contribution from transport, where prices rose 4.6% over the year on the back of motor fuels (ONS).\n• For: supply shocks are the harder problem because they move output and prices in OPPOSITE directions, so a central bank faces a genuine dilemma - tightening to stop second-round effects deepens the output loss. Demand shocks move both in the same direction, so the policy response is unambiguous.\n• For: the structural sources of supply shocks have multiplied - geopolitical conflict, the fragmentation of supply chains, climate-related events and the sharp rise in trade barriers (the US average effective tariff rate reached 18.2% by August 2025, the highest in about 90 years).\n• Against: the deepest UK contractions of the last two decades - 2008-09 and 2020 - were, respectively, a financial and demand collapse and a shock that was both, and the demand side did the lasting damage through unemployment and hysteresis.\n• Against: demand policy remains the dominant driver of the cycle. Bank Rate moved from 0.1% to 5.25% and back to 3.75% by September 2026, and fiscal consolidation to meet the stability rule is itself a demand shock of significant scale.\n• Against: supply shocks are usually temporary unless demand validates them. Core CPI was 2.6% in the 12 months to August 2026, well below headline 3.1%, which suggests the shock is concentrated in energy rather than generalised - so its inflationary effect should fade unless expectations shift.\n• Evaluation - the distinction is not clean: a tariff is a supply shock to the importing economy and a demand shock to the exporting one; an oil price rise raises costs AND transfers income to oil producers, cutting demand in importers.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer argues from the criterion it chooses - frequency, persistence, or difficulty of policy response - and is explicit about which. The most defensible conclusion is that supply shocks have become more frequent and are harder to respond to, while demand shocks remain capable of doing more lasting damage through hysteresis.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated, with a criterion-based conclusion; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Assess the view that the wealth effect is the most powerful channel through which monetary policy affects aggregate demand in the UK.`, marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For: housing is the largest asset most UK households hold, and UK owner-occupation is widespread, so interest-rate-driven changes in house prices change perceived wealth and collateral for a very large share of the population. Asset price effects arrive faster than income effects because markets reprice immediately.\n• For: the wealth channel is the main route by which quantitative easing worked. QE raised bond prices and lowered yields, pushing investors into equities and property; with the Bank still holding a gilt portfolio of around £488bn as at September 2026, the stock effect on asset prices persists.\n• Against - the market rates channel is more direct: Bank Rate at 3.75% feeds into mortgage and loan rates and into the reward for saving. This changes cash flow, not just perceived wealth, and cash-flow effects are more reliably spent.\n• Against - the UK's mortgage structure blunts the wealth channel and the cash-flow channel alike: most UK mortgages are now fixed for two or five years, so rate changes reach households only as fixes expire, spreading and delaying every channel.\n• Against - the exchange rate channel may matter more for inflation: higher UK rates relative to abroad raise sterling and cut import prices directly. With the ECB deposit rate at 2.50% from 10 September 2026 against UK Bank Rate of 3.75%, the differential is substantial, and sterling around 1.16 euros in September 2026 reflects it.\n• Against - distribution: wealth effects are concentrated among older, asset-rich households with a low marginal propensity to consume out of wealth, whereas interest-rate effects on cash flow hit younger, indebted households with a high MPC. The channel that reaches high-MPC households does more to AD.\n• Against - expectations: much of the effect of a policy decision comes through what it signals. The 6-3 vote to hold in September 2026, with three members voting for 4%, moved expectations of future rates and therefore long rates, independently of any wealth change.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer ranks the channels explicitly and justifies the ranking, most persuasively by noting that the channels are not independent - the wealth effect is itself a consequence of the discount rate applied to future income, so it is a transmission OF the interest rate channel rather than a rival to it.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Figure 1) shows that investment fell by 1.8% in 2026 while consumption grew by 0.9%.\n\nUsing the extracts and your knowledge of economics, discuss the view that investment is the most important component of aggregate demand for macroeconomic stability.`, marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - volatility: in the extract data investment ranges from +7.8% to -3.2% while consumption ranges from +2.1% to +0.4%. Because the swings are so much larger, investment contributes far more to CHANGES in AD than its share of the level suggests.\n• For - the accelerator: net investment depends on the rate of change of output, so it turns negative while output is still growing, giving the cycle its turning points. Combined with the multiplier it produces the self-reinforcing loop that generates booms and slumps.\n• For - the supply side: investment is the only component of AD that also raises LRAS, so a shortfall in investment damages both the demand side today and the productive potential of the economy tomorrow. The UK's weak productivity growth since 2008 is widely attributed to persistently low business investment.\n• Against - size: investment is roughly 15% of UK AD against about two thirds for consumption. A 1% change in consumption is worth more in absolute terms than a 4% change in investment, so consumption shocks dominate the aggregate.\n• Against - net trade and policy: an open economy is exposed to external shocks regardless of domestic investment. The rise in US tariffs since 2025 and the UK's current account deficit of 1.9% of GDP in Q2 2026 are sources of instability that investment policy cannot address.\n• Against - government spending is the stabiliser: automatic stabilisers and discretionary fiscal policy act precisely to offset instability in the other components, so G is the component that determines whether instability elsewhere is transmitted or damped.\n• Evaluation - "important for stability" is ambiguous: investment is the most important SOURCE of instability but consumption is the most important DETERMINANT of the level of AD and government spending the most important INSTRUMENT for stabilising it. Strong answers separate these.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer resolves the ambiguity explicitly and reaches a conclusion consistent with the definition it has chosen, supported by both the share and the volatility evidence.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 13-14) states: "Households raised their saving ratio sharply during the year, and some commentators welcomed the return of prudence."\n\nUsing the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that an increase in the household saving ratio is damaging to an economy.`, marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - the paradox of thrift: if all households save more simultaneously, consumption falls, AD shifts left and national income falls by a multiple of the initial fall. Because saving depends on income, the attempt to save more can leave aggregate saving no higher, and can reduce it if firms also cut investment.\n• For - the mechanism is strongest in a slump: on the flat range of the Keynesian AS curve, the fall in AD is almost entirely a fall in real output rather than prices, so higher saving translates directly into lost output and unemployment, with hysteresis risks if the downturn persists.\n• For - the fallacy of composition: what is prudent for one household is destructive for all of them together, because one household's spending is another's income. This is the core Keynesian objection to treating the nation's finances like a household's.\n• Against - the classical view: saving is the source of loanable funds. Higher saving lowers the equilibrium interest rate, raises investment and so raises the capital stock and LRAS. On this view higher saving raises long-run growth, and the UK's low saving and low investment are two sides of the same problem.\n• Against - it depends on the state of the economy: in 2026 the UK was near capacity, with CPI inflation at 3.1% in the year to August 2026 and unemployment at 4.9%. A higher saving ratio in those conditions relieves inflationary pressure rather than causing lost output, and reduces the need for the MPC to keep Bank Rate at 3.75%.\n• Against - the external balance: higher domestic saving reduces the excess of investment over saving that is the counterpart of a current account deficit. The UK deficit narrowed from 2.4% of GDP in Q1 2026 to 1.9% in Q2 2026; a sustained rise in saving would reduce reliance on foreign capital inflows.\n• Against - the composition of saving matters: saving held as bank deposits is intermediated into lending, saving held as gilts finances government borrowing, and precautionary hoarding does neither - the damage depends on where the money goes.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer argues explicitly that the answer depends on the output gap and on whether saving is intermediated into investment, and applies that test to the UK in 2026 rather than answering in the abstract.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Assess the view that the effect of an increase in aggregate demand on an economy depends principally on the amount of spare capacity in that economy.`, marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For: the shape of the AS curve is the whole of the answer in the standard model. On the flat Keynesian range, extra AD raises real output with almost no price effect; as spare capacity is used the curve steepens and the split shifts towards prices; on the vertical range the entire increase is inflationary.\n• For: this is why the output gap is the central concept of stabilisation policy, and why the MPC's decisions turn on judgements about slack. Holding Bank Rate at 3.75% in September 2026 with a 6-3 split reflected exactly such a disagreement about how much restraint the economy still needed.\n• For: the same policy has produced opposite results in different states - the fiscal expansion of 2020, with output far below capacity, raised output; expansion in a near-capacity economy raises prices.\n• Against - the composition of demand matters: an increase in AD driven by investment shifts LRAS right as well, so it raises capacity at the same time as using it. Demand met by imports raises foreign output, not domestic, however much slack there is.\n• Against - expectations matter: if firms and workers expect the stimulus to be inflationary, wages and prices rise even with spare capacity, because SRAS shifts left in anticipation. This is the expectations-augmented argument, and it is why credibility of the inflation target matters.\n• Against - measurement: spare capacity is unobservable. The output gap is estimated, revised heavily and often revised in the opposite direction after the event, so it may be the right theoretical answer and a useless practical one. Answers may note that apparent slack before 2008 was later found not to exist.\n• Against - sectoral bottlenecks: aggregate slack can coexist with acute shortages in particular sectors or skills, so an aggregate measure can conceal inflationary pressure - a point made repeatedly about the UK labour market despite unemployment of 4.9% in May-July 2026.\n• Against - the policy reaction function: what actually happens depends on how the central bank responds, not only on the AS curve. A credible inflation-targeting MPC will offset a demand increase whether or not there is slack.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer accepts the theoretical primacy of spare capacity while arguing that composition, expectations and measurement determine what happens in practice - or argues the reverse, provided both sides are addressed.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Discuss the view that the circular flow of income model is too simple to be a useful guide to macroeconomic policy.`, marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For the model - it establishes the accounting identity that national income, national output and national expenditure are three measures of the same flow, which is the foundation of the national accounts and therefore of every macroeconomic statistic policy uses.\n• For the model - it locates the injections (I, G, X) and withdrawals (S, T, M) that policy can move, and shows that equilibrium requires J = W. That immediately generates the multiplier and explains why a rise in the marginal propensity to import shrinks it - the UK ran a current account deficit of 1.9% of GDP in Q2 2026, so this leakage is material.\n• For the model - it delivers the paradox of thrift and the fallacy of composition, insights that are not obvious and that continue to be got wrong in public debate about the national budget.\n• Against - it has no supply side: the model determines output entirely from demand, so it cannot explain the 2021-26 inflation, in which CPI rose to 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026 largely because of energy and transport costs, nor the UK's productivity problem.\n• Against - no price level, no expectations and no financial sector. It cannot model the transmission of Bank Rate at 3.75%, nor the role of gilt yields near 5.9% in early September 2026, nor a banking crisis.\n• Against - it assumes fixed marginal propensities. In practice the MPC, MPS and MPM all change with income, confidence, credit conditions and the distribution of income, so multiplier estimates derived from it are unreliable - estimates in the literature range from about 0.4 to 1.6.\n• Evaluation - the model's job: it is a first approximation whose purpose is to make the identity and the leakages visible. No one uses it for forecasting; central banks use large structural models. A model can be useful as a teaching and framing device without being useful as a forecasting tool.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer distinguishes usefulness as a framework from usefulness as a forecasting model and concludes accordingly, rather than simply listing the model's omissions.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 22-23) states: "Business investment fell for the second year in three, even as consumer spending continued to grow."\n\nUsing the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that the accelerator explains fluctuations in national income better than changes in consumption do.`, marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For the accelerator: it explains precisely the pattern in the extract - investment falling while consumption grows - because net investment depends on the RATE OF CHANGE of output. When growth slows from 0.6% to 0.4% a quarter, as UK GDP did between Q1 and Q2 2026, the accelerator predicts falling net investment even though output is still rising.\n• For the accelerator: it generates turning points endogenously. A model driven by consumption alone needs an external shock to explain why a boom ends; the accelerator explains the end of the boom from the arithmetic of capacity itself.\n• For the accelerator: combined with the multiplier it produces amplification, which matches the observed fact that output swings far exceed the shocks that appear to start them.\n• Against - size: consumption is about two thirds of AD and investment roughly 15%. Even a large proportionate swing in investment can be outweighed by a modest swing in consumption, so in accounting terms consumption changes dominate the aggregate.\n• Against - the accelerator is mechanical: it assumes a fixed capital-output ratio, no spare capacity, and that firms can obtain finance instantly. In practice firms run existing capital harder before investing, so the relationship is loose and lagged. Keynes's animal spirits break the link entirely.\n• Against - consumption theories do explain fluctuations: the permanent income hypothesis, the wealth effect and credit conditions together explain the consumption collapse of 2008-09 and the saving surge of 2020-21 far better than the accelerator explains anything.\n• Evaluation - they are complements: the accelerator explains the amplitude and timing of fluctuations, consumption explains their aggregate scale, and the multiplier connects the two. Asking which is "better" without specifying the aspect of fluctuation being explained is the wrong question.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer specifies the criterion - explaining turning points versus explaining magnitude - and concludes accordingly, using the extract data to support the claim about investment volatility.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

    ]
  },
  '3.2.3': {
    name: 'Economic Performance',
    questions: [
      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — index numbers written for this practice question, consistent with the ONS outturn\nUK Consumer Prices Index, all items (2015 = 100)\n  August 2025:  138.4\n  August 2026:  142.7\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate the rate of CPI inflation in the 12 months to August 2026. Give your answer as a percentage to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Inflation rate = (change in index / original index) x 100 = (142.7 - 138.4) / 138.4 x 100\n= 4.3 / 138.4 x 100 = 3.1069...\nAnswer: 3.11%.\n\n2 marks for 3.11%. 1 mark for the correct method (change over the ORIGINAL index) with an arithmetic or rounding slip, or for 4.3 identified as the change without converting to a percentage. Dividing by the later index (4.3/142.7 = 3.01%) is a method error: 1 mark maximum. The ONS reported CPI inflation of 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — real data, ONS Labour Market Overview, September 2026 (May to July 2026)\n  People aged 16+ in employment:  34.48 million\n  People aged 16+ unemployed:      1.78 million\n\nUsing Extract B (Table 2), calculate the UK unemployment rate. Give your answer as a percentage to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Labour force (economically active) = employed + unemployed = 34.48 + 1.78 = 36.26 million.\nUnemployment rate = 1.78 / 36.26 x 100 = 4.909...\nAnswer: 4.9%.\n\n2 marks for 4.9%. 1 mark for correctly forming the labour force (36.26m) without completing the calculation, or for the correct method with a rounding slip. Dividing by the employed only (1.78/34.48 = 5.2%) or by the whole population is a method error: 1 mark maximum. The ONS reported an unemployment rate of 4.9% for May to July 2026.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\n  Actual real GDP:      £2 485bn\n  Estimated potential real GDP:  £2 510bn\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate the output gap as a percentage of potential real GDP. Give your answer to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Output gap = (actual - potential) / potential x 100 = (2 485 - 2 510) / 2 510 x 100\n= -25 / 2 510 x 100 = -0.9960...\nAnswer: -1.00% (a NEGATIVE output gap of 1.00% of potential output).\n\n2 marks for -1.00% with the sign or the word "negative". 1 mark for the correct method with a sign or rounding error, or for identifying the -£25bn gap without expressing it as a percentage. Dividing by actual output rather than potential is a method error: 1 mark maximum.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 3-4) — written for this practice question\n"Nominal GDP rose by 4.6% over the year, while the GDP deflator rose by 3.4%."\n\nUsing Extract C, calculate the approximate rate of real GDP growth. Give your answer as a percentage to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Real growth is approximately nominal growth minus inflation = 4.6 - 3.4 = 1.2%.\nAnswer: 1.2%.\n\n2 marks for 1.2%. 1 mark for a correct statement of the relationship without completing the arithmetic. Accept the exact calculation (1.046/1.034 - 1 = 1.16%, so 1.2% to one decimal place) for full marks. Adding the two figures (8.0%) earns no marks.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 8-9) — written for this practice question, using the ONS CPI outturn for August 2026\n"Average weekly earnings grew by 4.5% over the year, while CPI inflation was 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026."\n\nUsing Extract B, calculate the approximate growth in real average weekly earnings. Give your answer as a percentage to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Real earnings growth is approximately nominal earnings growth minus the inflation rate = 4.5 - 3.1 = 1.4%.\nAnswer: 1.4%.\n\n2 marks for 1.4%. 1 mark for a correct method statement without the arithmetic, or for a correct answer expressed the wrong way round (-1.4%). Accept the exact calculation (1.045/1.031 - 1 = 1.36%, so 1.4% to one decimal place).` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\n  Real GDP:      £2 485bn\n  Population:    69.0 million\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate real GDP per capita. Give your answer in pounds to the nearest pound.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Real GDP per capita = real GDP / population = £2 485 000 000 000 / 69 000 000\n= £36 014.49...\nAnswer: £36 014.\n\n2 marks for £36 014. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip or a units error (for example £36 014 million, or 36.01). Candidates must handle the billions-to-millions conversion: 2 485 / 69 = 36.0145, so the answer is £36 014 per head, not £36bn.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Figure 1) — real data, ONS Consumer Price Inflation, August 2026\nAnnual CPI inflation rates, 12 months to August 2026\n  CPI, all items:                          3.1%   (July 2026: 2.9%)\n  Core CPI (excluding energy, food, alcohol and tobacco): 2.6%   (July 2026: 2.6%)\n  CPI services:                            3.4%   (July 2026: 3.4%)\n  CPI goods:                               2.7%   (July 2026: 2.2%)\n  Transport:                               4.6%   (July 2026: 3.6%)\n  Food and non-alcoholic beverages:        1.3%   (July 2026: 1.3%)\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Figure 1) show that the rise in UK inflation in August 2026 was concentrated in a narrow part of the basket.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• Headline CPI rose by 0.2 percentage points, from 2.9% to 3.1%, but core CPI was unchanged at 2.6% - so the increase came entirely from the items core excludes, namely energy, food, alcohol and tobacco (2 marks for pairing the two series and drawing the inference).\n• Transport inflation rose by a full percentage point, from 3.6% to 4.6%, the largest movement in the table, and the ONS attributes this mainly to motor fuels (1 mark).\n• Services inflation was unchanged at 3.4% and food inflation unchanged at 1.3%, so two large parts of the basket contributed nothing to the increase (1 mark).\n• Goods inflation rose from 2.2% to 2.7% while services was flat, which is the signature of an imported cost shock rather than domestically generated inflation (1 mark for this developed inference).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — real data, ONS Labour Market Overview, September 2026\n  Unemployment rate (May to July 2026):   4.9%   (up 0.2 percentage points on the year)\n  Number unemployed:                      1.78 million   (up 83 000 on the year)\n  Employment rate, 16-64 (May to July 2026): 75.1%   (75.2% a year earlier)\n  Economic inactivity rate:               20.9%   (down 0.1 percentage points on the year)\n  Payrolled employees, August 2026:       30.2 million   (down 145 000, or 0.5%, on the year)\n\nExplain how the data in Extract B (Table 2) show that the UK labour market weakened in the year to mid-2026.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• Unemployment rose on both measures: the rate up 0.2 percentage points to 4.9% and the level up 83 000 to 1.78 million (1 mark).\n• Demand for labour fell: payrolled employees were down 145 000, or 0.5%, on the year to 30.2 million in August 2026 - a direct measure of jobs rather than of self-reported status (1 mark).\n• The employment rate fell from 75.2% to 75.1%, so the rise in unemployment was not simply a matter of more people entering the labour market (1 mark).\n• Developed inference: inactivity FELL by 0.1 percentage points, so some of the rise in measured unemployment reflects people moving from inactivity into job search. That partly offsets the negative reading, but with payrolled employment and the employment rate both falling, the overall picture remains one of weakening demand for labour (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract C (Table 3) — written for this practice question\nActual and estimated potential real GDP, £bn\n              Year 1    Year 2    Year 3\n  Actual:      2 418     2 441     2 485\n  Potential:   2 460     2 485     2 510\n\nExplain how the data in Extract C (Table 3) show that this economy had a persistent negative output gap.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• Actual output is below potential in every year: gaps of -£42bn, -£44bn and -£25bn (1 mark for at least two correct).\n• Expressed as percentages of potential: -1.71%, -1.77% and -1.00% (2 marks for at least two correct percentages; 1 mark for one).\n• The gap narrows in Year 3, because actual output grew by 1.80% (2 441 to 2 485) while potential grew by only 1.01% (2 485 to 2 510) - so spare capacity is being used up but has not been eliminated (1 mark).\n• Explanation of significance: a persistent negative output gap implies deficient aggregate demand, downward pressure on inflation and cyclical unemployment - conditions in which demand-side policy raises real output rather than prices (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Figure 1) — real data, ONS\nUK real GDP growth\n  Q1 2026 (on the previous quarter):   +0.6%\n  Q2 2026 (on the previous quarter):   +0.4%\n  Q2 2026 (on the same quarter a year earlier): +1.2%\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Figure 1) show that the UK experienced a slowdown in actual growth rather than a recession in the first half of 2026.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• Growth was positive in both quarters, at 0.6% and 0.4%, so real GDP rose in each quarter (1 mark).\n• The technical definition of a recession is two consecutive quarters of NEGATIVE growth; neither quarter was negative, so the definition is not met (1 mark).\n• What fell was the RATE of growth, by 0.2 percentage points between the quarters - a deceleration, not a contraction. Candidates must distinguish a fall in the rate of increase from a fall in the level (1 mark).\n• Annual growth of 1.2% is broadly in line with most estimates of the UK trend rate of growth, so the economy grew at roughly its long-run average over the year as a whole (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained. An answer that treats slower growth as "output falling" cannot score above 2.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — written for this practice question\n              Year 1    Year 2    Year 3    Year 4\n  Inflation rate (%):   2.0      6.8       4.2       3.1\n  Unemployment rate (%): 3.8      4.1       4.6       4.9\n\nExplain how the data in Extract B (Table 2) show that the simple Phillips curve relationship did not hold over this period.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• The simple Phillips curve predicts an INVERSE relationship: inflation and unemployment should move in opposite directions (1 mark for stating the prediction).\n• Between Year 1 and Year 2 both rise together - inflation from 2.0% to 6.8% and unemployment from 3.8% to 4.1% - which contradicts the prediction (1 mark for the paired data).\n• Between Year 2 and Year 4 inflation falls from 6.8% to 3.1% while unemployment continues to rise from 4.1% to 4.9%, so the two move in the same direction again (1 mark).\n• Explanation: simultaneous rises are the signature of a supply-side shock, which shifts SRAS left and raises prices while reducing output and employment - stagflation. A single downward-sloping curve cannot generate this, which is why the curve is now treated as shifting with expected inflation (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract C (Table 3) — written for this practice question\n              Year 1    Year 5\n  Real GDP (£bn):              2 290     2 485\n  Population (millions):        66.4      69.0\n  Real median household income (£):  33 100    33 450\n\nExplain how the data in Extract C (Table 3) show that economic growth did not translate into a proportionate rise in typical living standards.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• Real GDP grew by 8.52% ((2 485 - 2 290)/2 290 x 100) (1 mark).\n• Real GDP per capita grew by far less: 2 290/66.4 = £34 488 and 2 485/69.0 = £36 014, a rise of 4.42% - roughly half the headline growth rate, because population grew by 3.92% (2 marks).\n• Real median household income grew by only 1.06% (33 100 to 33 450), far below even per capita GDP growth, which implies that the gains were unevenly distributed - the mean rose much faster than the median (1 mark).\n• Explanation: GDP growth can reflect a larger population or gains concentrated at the top of the distribution rather than a better standard of living for the typical household (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 5-6) states: "For a generation policymakers assumed they could buy lower unemployment at the cost of a little more inflation."\n\nWith the help of a Phillips curve diagram, analyse the short-run trade-off between inflation and unemployment.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: short-run Phillips curve, downward sloping, with inflation on the vertical axis and the unemployment rate on the horizontal axis; a movement along the curve from a high-unemployment, low-inflation point to a low-unemployment, higher-inflation point.\n• Chain of reasoning: an increase in aggregate demand raises output, so firms hire more workers and unemployment falls. Competition for a shrinking pool of available labour bids up nominal wages; wages are a large share of costs, so prices rise. Hence lower unemployment is associated with higher inflation.\n• The link to AD/AS: the short-run Phillips curve is the same information as the upward-sloping SRAS curve read on different axes. Moving up SRAS is moving down the Phillips curve.\n• The trade-off is a policy menu only in the short run and only for demand shocks: a demand expansion moves the economy ALONG the curve. A supply shock SHIFTS the curve outwards, so both variables rise together - which is why the relationship broke down in the 1970s and again during the energy shocks of the 2020s.\n• Dated application: UK CPI inflation was 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026 while unemployment was 4.9% in May to July 2026 and rising - both moving in the same direction, consistent with a shifted rather than a stable curve.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) correctly labelled diagram plus a full chain from AD to the labour market to wages to prices, with the shift/movement distinction; L2 (4-6) diagram plus partial analysis; L1 (1-3) the relationship asserted, diagram missing or mislabelled.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 10-11) states: "Monetarists argued that any attempt to hold unemployment below its natural rate would simply produce ever-faster inflation."\n\nWith the help of a diagram, analyse why the long-run Phillips curve is vertical at the natural rate of unemployment.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: a vertical long-run Phillips curve at the natural rate (NAIRU), crossed by a family of short-run Phillips curves each drawn for a different expected rate of inflation; the adjustment path shown as a move along SRPC1, then a shift to SRPC2, and so on.\n• Chain of reasoning: a demand expansion raises actual inflation above expected inflation. Nominal wages were set on the old expectation, so real wages fall and firms hire more; unemployment falls below the natural rate - a movement along SRPC1.\n• Workers then discover that their real wages have fallen and bargain to restore them. Expected inflation rises to match actual inflation, the short-run curve shifts up, and unemployment returns to the natural rate at a HIGHER rate of inflation.\n• Repeating the attempt repeats the cycle, so holding unemployment below the natural rate requires continuously ACCELERATING inflation - hence the alternative name, the non-accelerating inflation rate of unemployment.\n• Policy implication analysed: demand management cannot permanently reduce unemployment. Only supply-side measures that reduce frictional and structural unemployment - improved skills, better job matching, lower search costs - can shift the vertical curve left.\n• Qualification worth crediting: the natural rate is unobservable and itself moves, for example through hysteresis after a long recession, so the vertical line is not a fixed target policymakers can see.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram with at least two short-run curves and the full expectations adjustment traced; L2 (4-6) vertical curve drawn with partial explanation; L1 (1-3) assertion that the curve is vertical.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 1-2) states: "When an economy is producing above its sustainable capacity, the pressure shows up in prices."\n\nWith the help of an AD/AS diagram, analyse how a positive output gap generates demand-pull inflation.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: AD/AS with AD shifting right so that equilibrium output exceeds the full-employment level Yfe (marked by the LRAS line); the SRAS curve is steep or vertical over this range, so the price level rises sharply from P1 to P2 while output rises little.\n• Chain of reasoning: AD exceeds the economy's productive potential. Firms cannot raise output much because capital is fully utilised and suitable labour is scarce, so they respond to the excess demand by raising prices.\n• Labour market mechanism: with unemployment below the natural rate, firms bid against each other for workers, raising nominal wages faster than productivity. Unit labour costs rise and are passed on in prices - the demand-pull shock becomes a cost-push one at the second round.\n• Expectations: if the excess demand persists, expected inflation rises, wage settlements build it in and the SRAS curve shifts left, so inflation can persist after the original excess demand has gone.\n• Application: the MPC held Bank Rate at 3.75% at its meeting ending on 16 September 2026, with three of nine members voting for 4%, and warned that the risk of second-round effects was growing while CPI inflation stood at 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026.\n• Measurement qualification: the output gap is an estimate, heavily revised, so policymakers may act on a positive gap that later proves not to have existed.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) diagram showing output beyond Yfe, the excess demand mechanism and the wage/expectations second round; L2 (4-6) diagram with a partial mechanism; L1 (1-3) "too much demand causes inflation" with no model.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 12-13) states: "Higher energy prices left the economy with faster inflation and weaker output at the same time."\n\nWith the help of a diagram, analyse how a supply-side shock can produce stagflation.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: AD/AS with SRAS shifting left from SRAS1 to SRAS2, AD unchanged; the price level rises from P1 to P2 while real output falls from Y1 to Y2. A Phillips curve diagram showing the short-run curve shifting outwards is an equally valid alternative.\n• Definition: stagflation is the simultaneous occurrence of stagnant or falling output, rising unemployment and rising inflation.\n• Chain of reasoning: a rise in the price of a universal input such as energy raises firms' costs of production at every price level. Firms restrict output and raise prices, so the economy moves to a point with both higher prices and lower output.\n• Why it is a policy dilemma: a demand contraction to control the inflation deepens the output loss; a demand expansion to support output worsens the inflation. Unlike a demand shock, there is no single direction of policy that improves both.\n• Second-round effects: if workers bargain to restore real wages, SRAS shifts left again and the shock becomes embedded in expectations - the central reason central banks emphasise credibility.\n• Dated application: the MPC noted in September 2026 that conflict in the Middle East had raised crude and refined energy prices, and UK transport inflation rose to 4.6% in the 12 months to August 2026 while unemployment rose to 4.9% and GDP growth slowed from 0.6% to 0.4% between Q1 and Q2 2026.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram plus the cost mechanism AND the policy dilemma analysed; L2 (4-6) diagram with a partial mechanism; L1 (1-3) definition of stagflation only.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 2-3) states: "Unemployment rises in a downturn and falls in an upswing, but not in step with output."\n\nWith the help of a trade cycle diagram, analyse the relationship between the output gap and cyclical unemployment.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: real output on the vertical axis against time on the horizontal, with an upward-sloping trend line and actual output oscillating around it; the four phases labelled (boom, downturn/slowdown, recession/slump, recovery), and the vertical distance between actual and trend marked as the positive or negative output gap.\n• Chain of reasoning: labour is a derived demand. In a downturn, actual output falls below trend, so firms need fewer workers; demand-deficient (cyclical) unemployment rises. In a boom, a positive output gap draws workers back in and cyclical unemployment falls towards zero, leaving only frictional and structural unemployment - the natural rate.\n• Why the relationship is not one-for-one: labour hoarding means firms retain trained workers through a shallow downturn because hiring and training costs are high, so unemployment rises by less than output falls. Firms also cut hours and overtime first.\n• Lags: unemployment is a lagging indicator. Firms delay both redundancies and hiring until they are confident the change in demand is permanent, so unemployment continues rising after output has turned.\n• Hysteresis: a deep or long recession can convert cyclical unemployment into structural unemployment as skills and work habits decay, raising the natural rate and shifting the trend line itself downwards.\n• Dated application: UK growth slowed from 0.6% in Q1 2026 to 0.4% in Q2 2026 while unemployment rose to 4.9% in May to July 2026, up 0.2 percentage points on the year - unemployment rising as growth decelerates, consistent with the lagged relationship.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram with phases and the gap marked, plus analysis of derived demand, lags and labour hoarding; L2 (4-6) diagram plus a simple inverse relationship; L1 (1-3) description of the cycle only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 14-15) states: "Long spells out of work leave scars that outlast the recession that caused them."\n\nAnalyse how hysteresis effects can raise an economy's natural rate of unemployment.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Definition: hysteresis is the persistence of an effect after the cause has been removed - here, unemployment remaining high after the demand deficiency that caused it has gone, so that a temporary shock permanently raises the natural rate.\n• Skills depreciation: technical and job-specific skills decay out of work and are not updated with technological change, so the long-term unemployed become progressively less employable even when vacancies return.\n• Employer screening: firms use the duration of unemployment as a signal of unobserved quality, so long spells reduce the probability of an interview independently of actual skill - a self-reinforcing trap.\n• Loss of attachment and motivation: search intensity falls, some workers move into inactivity or long-term sickness, and the effective labour supply shrinks. This shifts LRAS left as well as raising the natural rate, so potential output falls.\n• Insider-outsider effects: those still in work bargain for their own wages without regard to the unemployed, so wages do not fall enough to clear the market.\n• Capital scrapping: in a long recession firms scrap capital and do not replace it, so even when demand returns there is less equipment for workers to use.\n• Policy implication analysed: if hysteresis is real, allowing a demand-deficient recession to run its course is not costless, because the supply side is permanently damaged. That strengthens the case for prompt demand-side intervention and for active labour market policy - training, subsidised employment, job-search support.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) at least three mechanisms developed as chains of reasoning, plus the policy implication; L2 (4-6) two mechanisms with limited development; L1 (1-3) definition only.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 7-8) states: "Tax receipts fell and benefit spending rose during the downturn without any decision being taken."\n\nAnalyse how automatic stabilisers reduce the amplitude of the trade cycle.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Definition: automatic stabilisers are features of government spending and taxation that move counter-cyclically without any discretionary decision, so they act with no recognition, decision or implementation lag.\n• In a downturn: incomes, profits and spending fall, so receipts from income tax, National Insurance, corporation tax and VAT fall automatically and by proportionately more than income where the tax system is progressive. Disposable income therefore falls by less than national income, so consumption falls by less.\n• Also in a downturn: unemployment-related benefits and means-tested support rise automatically as more people qualify, injecting spending into the circular flow at exactly the point at which private demand is weakest, and directed at households with a high marginal propensity to consume.\n• In a boom the mechanism reverses: fiscal drag pulls earnings into higher tax bands and benefit spending falls, withdrawing demand and damping inflationary pressure.\n• Link to the multiplier: a higher marginal propensity to tax reduces the multiplier, which is precisely why the stabiliser works - it shrinks the amplification of any shock in either direction.\n• Analysis of limits: stabilisers damp the cycle but cannot close a large output gap on their own; they operate on the CYCLICAL deficit only, so a structural deficit is untouched; and a government bound by a fiscal rule may be forced to offset them with discretionary tightening, which is pro-cyclical. The UK's stability rule requires the current budget to be in surplus by 2029-30.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) both sides of the cycle traced, the link to the multiplier made, and limits analysed; L2 (4-6) one side explained with some development; L1 (1-3) examples listed without mechanism.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 16-17) states: "A falling general price level might sound welcome to consumers, but economists regard sustained deflation as a serious problem."\n\nAnalyse two reasons why deflation can damage an economy.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content - two developed reasons required:\n• Deferred consumption: if households expect prices to keep falling, the rational response is to postpone purchases of durables, since waiting makes them cheaper. Deferred spending reduces AD, which pushes prices down further and validates the expectation - a self-reinforcing deflationary spiral.\n• Rising real debt burden (debt deflation): debt contracts are fixed in nominal terms, so a falling price level raises the real value of what must be repaid while nominal incomes stagnate or fall. Households and firms cut spending to service debt, and default risk rises, damaging bank balance sheets.\n• Real interest rates rise: with the nominal rate floored near zero, a negative inflation rate makes the real interest rate positive and rising exactly when the economy needs stimulus. Conventional monetary policy is disarmed, which is why central banks target a small positive number (2% CPI in the UK) rather than zero.\n• Real wage rigidity: nominal wages are sticky downwards, so falling prices raise real wages, which raises firms' real costs and causes redundancies rather than pay cuts - deflation raises unemployment.\n• Delayed investment: falling output prices with fixed nominal costs squeeze margins and reduce expected returns, so investment falls, damaging LRAS as well as AD.\n• Distinction worth crediting: benign deflation, caused by a rightward shift in AS from technological progress, comes with rising output and is not damaging. The malign case above is caused by a leftward shift in AD. Strong answers make this distinction.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) two reasons each developed as a full chain of reasoning, ideally with the benign/malign distinction; L2 (4-6) two reasons asserted or one developed; L1 (1-3) definition of deflation only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 18-19) states: "Output per hour worked in the UK has grown far more slowly since 2008 than in the decade before it."\n\nWith the help of a diagram, analyse how an increase in labour productivity affects an economy's potential output and its rate of inflation.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: AD/AS with LRAS shifting right from LRAS1 to LRAS2 (and SRAS shifting right too, since unit costs fall); with AD unchanged, output rises from Y1 to Y2 and the price level falls from P1 to P2. A PPF shifting outwards is an acceptable alternative for the potential-output part.\n• Potential output: productivity is output per unit of input, so higher productivity means more output from the same labour and capital. That is a direct increase in the economy's productive potential and shifts LRAS right.\n• Inflation: higher output per worker lowers unit labour costs for any given wage, so firms can pay higher nominal wages without raising prices. Non-inflationary growth in nominal wages equals productivity growth plus target inflation.\n• The trend rate of growth rises, so a given level of AD growth becomes less inflationary and the economy can sustain faster growth without a positive output gap - the natural rate of unemployment may also fall as firms become more willing to hire.\n• Analysis of the UK case: with productivity growth weak since 2008, the trend rate of growth is lower, so the UK reaches capacity constraints at a lower rate of expansion. UK annual growth of 1.2% in the year to Q2 2026 coincided with CPI inflation of 3.1% in the year to August 2026 - modest growth alongside above-target inflation is what a low trend rate implies.\n• Qualification: productivity gains can in the short run reduce employment in the affected sector, and the LRAS shift takes years to appear in the data.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram, both the potential-output and the unit-cost/inflation chains developed; L2 (4-6) one chain developed, diagram present; L1 (1-3) definition of productivity with no model.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 20-21) states: "The Committee judged that domestic activity and tight financial conditions were restraining inflation, while warning that the risk of second-round effects was growing."\n\nAnalyse the conflict facing UK policymakers in 2026 between reducing inflation and supporting economic growth.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The conflict stated: reducing inflation requires restraining aggregate demand, but restraining demand reduces output and raises unemployment. The short-run Phillips curve and the upward-sloping SRAS curve both express this.\n• The 2026 position: CPI inflation was 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026, above the 2% target and up from 2.9% in July; GDP growth had slowed from 0.6% in Q1 2026 to 0.4% in Q2; unemployment had risen to 4.9% in May to July 2026. Inflation and unemployment were rising together.\n• Why this is harder than a demand shock: the source was substantially a supply shock - transport inflation of 4.6% driven by motor fuels, with core inflation unchanged at 2.6%. Tightening policy to suppress an energy price rise imposes the whole cost on output, because it cannot lower the oil price.\n• The MPC's actual response: Bank Rate held at 3.75% on 16 September 2026 by 6 votes to 3, with three members voting for 4%. The majority argued that existing restraint was sufficient; the minority feared that second-round effects would embed the shock in expectations.\n• The case for tolerating the overshoot: an energy shock is temporary if expectations stay anchored, so tightening further would sacrifice output for an inflation outcome that would improve anyway. Core inflation at 2.6% supports this reading.\n• The case for tightening: if wage settlements build in the higher headline rate, the short-run Phillips curve shifts out and the eventual cost of disinflation is larger. Services inflation at 3.4% is a warning sign.\n• The fiscal dimension: the government cannot offset monetary tightening with fiscal expansion, because public sector net debt was 93.8% of GDP at the end of August 2026 and 30-year gilt yields reached about 5.9% in early September 2026.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) the conflict analysed as a genuine trade-off with dated UK evidence on both sides; L2 (4-6) the conflict described with limited evidence; L1 (1-3) assertion that the objectives conflict.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — real data, ONS, August-September 2026\n  CPI, all items, 12 months to August 2026:   3.1%  (July 2026: 2.9%)\n  Core CPI, 12 months to August 2026:         2.6%  (July 2026: 2.6%)\n  CPI services:                               3.4%  (unchanged)\n  Transport:                                  4.6%  (July 2026: 3.6%)\n  UK unemployment rate, May to July 2026:     4.9%  (up 0.2 percentage points on the year)\n  UK real GDP growth, Q2 2026:                +0.4% (Q1 2026: +0.6%)\n\nExtract B (lines 4-5) — real data\n"The Monetary Policy Committee noted that protracted conflict in the Middle East had contributed to further increases in crude and refined energy prices."\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data suggest that UK inflation in August 2026 was cost-push rather than demand-pull?`, marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Support for cost-push: headline CPI rose 0.2 percentage points to 3.1% while core CPI was unchanged at 2.6%, so the entire increase came from items core excludes - energy, food, alcohol and tobacco. Transport inflation rose by a full percentage point to 4.6%, and Extract B dates the cause to Middle East conflict raising energy prices. This is the textbook signature of a leftward shift in SRAS.\n• Support for cost-push: output and employment moved the WRONG way for demand-pull. Growth slowed from 0.6% to 0.4% and unemployment rose to 4.9%. Demand-pull inflation is accompanied by falling unemployment and a positive output gap, not by both deteriorating.\n• Support for demand-pull, or at least for domestically generated inflation: services inflation of 3.4% is well above the 2% target and above goods inflation. Services prices are wage-intensive and largely domestically determined, so persistent services inflation points to domestic cost or demand pressure rather than imported costs.\n• Support for demand-pull: core inflation at 2.6% is still above target, so even stripping out energy the economy was not at price stability. Three of nine MPC members voted to raise Bank Rate to 4%, which implies a judgement that demand pressure remained.\n• Limits of the data: a single month's figures, with no wage data, no output gap estimate and no measure of inflation expectations - and expectations are what determine whether a cost shock becomes persistent. The categories also overlap: higher energy prices raise transport costs, which feed into services prices, so the cost-push shock partly shows up in the "domestic" measure.\n• Judgement: the data strongly suggest that the INCREASE in August 2026 was cost-push, because core was flat and transport did the work. They do not show that inflation as a whole was purely cost-push, since core and services both remained above target - the most defensible conclusion is a cost-push shock on top of residual domestic inflation.\n\nLevels: L3 (8-10) uses the data quantitatively on both sides, recognises what the data cannot show and reaches a supported judgement; L2 (4-7) partial data use and a thin judgement; L1 (1-3) assertion. A conclusion reached without reference to the data cannot exceed L1.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — real data, ONS Labour Market Overview, September 2026\n  Unemployment rate, May to July 2026:      4.9%  (up 0.2 percentage points on the year)\n  Number unemployed:                        1.78 million (up 83 000 on the year)\n  Employment rate, 16-64:                   75.1% (75.2% a year earlier)\n  Economic inactivity rate:                 20.9% (down 0.1 percentage points on the year)\n  Payrolled employees, August 2026:         30.2 million (down 145 000, or 0.5%, on the year)\n  Real GDP growth, year to Q2 2026:         +1.2%\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data suggest that the UK economy moved into a period of demand-deficient unemployment during 2026?`, marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Support: unemployment rose on both the rate (4.9%, up 0.2 percentage points) and the level (1.78 million, up 83 000). Payrolled employees fell by 145 000 on the year, a direct measure of jobs lost rather than of self-reported status, and the employment rate fell from 75.2% to 75.1%. Falling employment alongside rising unemployment is consistent with weakening demand for labour.\n• Support: the fall in payrolled employment has been running for around two years according to the ONS, so this is a trend rather than a one-month movement.\n• Against: real GDP still grew by 1.2% over the year to Q2 2026, roughly the UK trend rate. Demand-deficient unemployment implies a negative output gap, but an economy growing at trend does not obviously have one.\n• Against: the inactivity rate FELL by 0.1 percentage points, so part of the rise in measured unemployment is people moving from inactivity into active job search. Those people are newly counted as unemployed without any job having been lost - a labour supply effect, not a demand deficiency.\n• Against: an unemployment rate of 4.9% is low by historical UK standards and may be at or near the natural rate, in which case the unemployment is frictional and structural rather than cyclical.\n• Against: the data contain no direct evidence on the CAUSE. Rising unemployment is equally consistent with a supply shock raising costs, with higher employment taxes, or with automation - all of which raise unemployment without any deficiency of aggregate demand.\n• What is missing: vacancy data, the vacancies-to-unemployment ratio, long-term unemployment shares, an output gap estimate and redundancy figures would all be needed to distinguish cyclical from structural unemployment.\n• Judgement: the data show a labour market weakening on every measure, which is consistent with rising cyclical unemployment, but growth at trend and falling inactivity mean they fall well short of establishing demand deficiency.\n\nLevels: L3 (8-10) quantitative use of both sides, explicit recognition of the limits of the data, supported judgement; L2 (4-7) partial use with a thin judgement; L1 (1-3) assertion. A conclusion reached without reference to the data cannot exceed L1.` },

      { q: `Explain the main causes of demand-pull inflation in an economy.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Definition and framing: demand-pull inflation arises when aggregate demand grows faster than aggregate supply, so that AD shifts right along a steepening SRAS curve and the price level rises. It is most powerful where the economy is at or beyond full capacity.\n• Loose monetary policy: low interest rates raise interest-sensitive consumption and investment and lower the exchange rate. Bank Rate stood at 0.1% from March 2020 and reached 5.25% by August 2023, falling back to 3.75% by September 2026 - the 2020-21 period of very cheap money is a standard example.\n• Expansionary fiscal policy: higher government spending or lower taxation raises AD directly and, through the multiplier, by more than the initial injection. The effect is more inflationary the smaller the output gap.\n• Rapid credit growth: easier lending standards allow households to spend beyond current income, and commercial bank credit creation expands the broad money supply. The quantity theory, MV = PQ, frames the monetarist version of this cause.\n• A weaker exchange rate: a depreciation raises net exports and so raises AD (as well as raising import costs, which is a cost-push channel, so a depreciation works through both).\n• Strong external demand: faster growth among trading partners raises exports.\n• Rising consumer and business confidence: a fall in precautionary saving or a rise in animal spirits shifts the consumption and investment functions right independently of income.\n• Essential qualification: none of these is inflationary if there is substantial spare capacity, because output rather than prices adjusts. The cause of demand-pull inflation is therefore always excess demand RELATIVE TO capacity, not the level of demand itself - so a fall in potential output can cause demand-pull inflation with no increase in AD at all.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several causes developed with mechanisms, and the relative-to-capacity point made explicitly; L2 (6-10) causes explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Explain the main causes of cost-push inflation in an economy.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Definition and framing: cost-push inflation arises when firms' costs of production rise at every price level, shifting SRAS left, so the price level rises and real output falls - the stagflation combination.\n• Commodity and energy prices: oil and gas are inputs to almost everything directly or through freight and utilities. The MPC noted in September 2026 that Middle East conflict had raised crude and refined energy prices, and UK transport inflation rose to 4.6% in the 12 months to August 2026.\n• Wage costs: nominal wage growth in excess of productivity growth raises unit labour costs. This is the main domestic source, and is why services inflation - wage-intensive - was 3.4% in the year to August 2026 while goods inflation was 2.7%.\n• Import prices and the exchange rate: a depreciation raises the sterling price of imported energy, food and components. The UK imports a high share of both food and energy, so the pass-through is significant.\n• Indirect taxes and regulatory costs: rises in VAT, excise duties, employers' National Insurance or environmental levies raise costs at every price level. These produce a one-off rise in the price level rather than sustained inflation unless they recur.\n• Supply chain disruption and trade barriers: tariffs, quotas and logistical bottlenecks raise landed costs. The US average effective tariff rate reached 18.2% by August 2025, the highest in about 90 years, and trade-policy activity in early 2026 ran at nearly double 2024 levels according to WTO-IMF tracking.\n• Falling productivity: if output per worker falls, unit costs rise even with unchanged wages.\n• The wage-price spiral: the crucial analytical point is that a cost shock becomes SUSTAINED inflation only if it is validated - by workers successfully bargaining to restore real wages, by firms restoring margins, and by monetary policy accommodating the higher price level. Otherwise it is a one-off change in the price level that drops out of the annual comparison after twelve months.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several causes developed with mechanisms, plus the one-off versus sustained distinction; L2 (6-10) causes explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Explain the main determinants of an economy's long-run rate of economic growth.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Framing: long-run growth is growth in POTENTIAL output - a rightward shift of LRAS or an outward shift of the PPF - as distinct from actual growth, which is the use of existing capacity. The determinants are therefore the quantity and quality of the factors of production and the efficiency with which they are combined.\n• Investment and the capital stock: net investment adds to the stock of capital per worker (capital deepening), raising output per hour. Weak UK business investment since 2008 is the most commonly cited explanation of weak UK productivity growth.\n• Human capital: education, training and health raise output per worker. The effects are large but arrive over decades, and depend on skills matching what employers need.\n• Technological progress and innovation: the principal long-run driver, because it raises output from unchanged inputs rather than requiring more of them. Supported by R&D spending, patent protection, university research and the diffusion of best practice between firms.\n• Labour supply: the size of the working-age population, net migration, and participation. The UK economic inactivity rate was 20.9% in May to July 2026; reducing it raises the labour force and potential output.\n• Institutions and economic efficiency: competition policy, secure property rights, the rule of law, an effective financial sector that allocates capital to its best use, and product and labour market flexibility that speeds the reallocation of resources.\n• Infrastructure: transport, energy and digital networks raise the productivity of private capital, though with very long lags between spending and effect.\n• Trade and openness: specialisation according to comparative advantage raises the output attainable from a given endowment, and exposure to foreign competition and technology raises productivity. The UK joined the CPTPP on 15 December 2024 and it was in force with all eleven other members by 1 September 2026.\n• Constraints worth crediting: natural resources, environmental limits and the sustainability of the growth achieved.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several determinants developed, with the actual/potential distinction sustained and dated application; L2 (6-10) determinants explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Explain the main causes of structural unemployment in an advanced economy.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Definition and framing: structural unemployment is a mismatch between the skills, location or characteristics of the unemployed and the requirements of available vacancies. It persists even when aggregate demand is adequate, so it forms part of the natural rate and is not curable by demand management.\n• Technological change and automation: capital substitutes for routine labour, destroying jobs in some occupations while creating them in others that require different skills. The displaced workers cannot immediately fill the new vacancies.\n• Changing patterns of demand and international competition: the long decline of UK manufacturing employment and the growth of services relocated jobs between sectors and regions. Shifts in comparative advantage do this continuously; the rise in global trade barriers since 2025 is now doing it in the opposite direction.\n• Occupational immobility: retraining is costly, slow and uncertain, and skills acquired over a career are not transferable. Older displaced workers face the sharpest barrier.\n• Geographical immobility: housing costs in areas with vacancies, family ties, and the transaction costs of moving all prevent workers relocating to where the jobs are. Regional differences in UK unemployment rates are persistent for this reason.\n• Information failure: workers and employers do not know about each other, so vacancies and unemployed workers coexist. This shades into frictional unemployment.\n• Institutional causes: minimum wages set above the market-clearing wage in low-productivity regions, replacement ratios that reduce the incentive to accept available work, and employment protection that raises the cost of hiring.\n• Hysteresis: cyclical unemployment that persists becomes structural, as skills and employer perceptions decay - so a demand shock can permanently raise structural unemployment.\n• Application: the UK unemployment rate was 4.9% in May to July 2026 with payrolled employment falling by 145 000 over the year, and inactivity at 20.9%. Some of this is cyclical, but the persistence of high inactivity points to structural and health-related causes.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several causes developed, with the natural-rate framing and the policy implication that demand management cannot cure it; L2 (6-10) causes explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list of unemployment types.` },

      { q: `Explain the main costs to an economy of a high and unstable rate of inflation.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Loss of purchasing power and real income: if nominal wages lag prices, real incomes fall. With CPI inflation at 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026, nominal pay growth below that rate means falling real pay.\n• Distributional effects: inflation redistributes from savers and creditors to borrowers and debtors, because the real value of nominal debts falls. Those on fixed nominal incomes and those without index-linked assets lose most, so inflation acts as an arbitrary and regressive tax.\n• Uncertainty and lower investment: UNSTABLE inflation is the more damaging of the two. Firms cannot forecast real returns, so the risk premium on long-lived projects rises and investment falls, damaging LRAS and long-run growth.\n• The price mechanism works less well: relative price changes carry the signals that allocate resources. When the general price level is moving fast, firms cannot distinguish a change in relative demand from general inflation, so resources are misallocated - a loss of allocative efficiency.\n• Menu costs and shoe-leather costs: the administrative cost of repricing, and the cost of the effort households and firms devote to minimising cash holdings.\n• International competitiveness: if domestic inflation exceeds that of trading partners with a fixed or slow-moving exchange rate, exports become dearer and imports cheaper, worsening the current account. The UK ran a current account deficit of 1.9% of GDP in Q2 2026.\n• Wage-price spirals and expectations: once higher inflation is built into expectations, the short-run Phillips curve shifts out and disinflation becomes costly in lost output - the "sacrifice ratio". This is the risk the MPC identified in September 2026 when warning about second-round effects while holding Bank Rate at 3.75%.\n• Fiscal effects: fiscal drag raises real tax burdens without legislation, and index-linked debt raises the cost of servicing the national debt - relevant with UK public sector net debt at 93.8% of GDP at the end of August 2026.\n• Qualification worth crediting: low, stable, positive inflation is desirable. It allows real wages to adjust downwards without nominal pay cuts and keeps the economy away from the zero lower bound - which is why the target is 2% and not 0%.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several costs developed with mechanisms, the stability/level distinction made, and the qualification included; L2 (6-10) costs explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Explain the main conflicts that can arise between an economy's macroeconomic policy objectives.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The objectives: stable and sustainable growth, low and stable inflation (the UK target is 2% CPI), low unemployment, a satisfactory balance of payments position on current account, and increasingly a sustainable fiscal position and an equitable distribution of income.\n• Inflation versus unemployment: the short-run Phillips curve trade-off. Restraining demand to bring inflation to target raises cyclical unemployment; expanding demand to cut unemployment below the natural rate raises inflation. In 2026 the UK faced the harder version of this, with CPI inflation at 3.1% in the year to August 2026 and unemployment rising to 4.9%.\n• Growth versus the current account: faster growth raises real incomes, and with a positive marginal propensity to import this sucks in imports and widens the current account deficit. The UK deficit was 1.9% of GDP in Q2 2026.\n• Growth versus inflation: if AD grows faster than potential output, the output gap turns positive and demand-pull inflation follows. The lower the trend rate of growth, the tighter this constraint.\n• Growth versus the environment: faster output growth raises energy use, emissions and resource depletion unless growth is decoupled from them, so the current generation's consumption may reduce the next generation's capacity - the sustainability conflict.\n• Growth versus equity: market-based supply-side policies that raise efficiency - deregulation, lower marginal tax rates, welfare reform - can widen the distribution of income, while redistribution can weaken incentives.\n• Reducing the deficit versus supporting demand: fiscal consolidation to meet the stability rule (current budget in surplus by 2029-30) withdraws demand and, through the multiplier, reduces national income and tax receipts, so the consolidation partly undoes itself.\n• Where conflicts disappear: successful supply-side policy raises output AND reduces inflation AND reduces unemployment simultaneously, because it shifts LRAS right rather than moving along a trade-off. This is the strongest argument for the supply side, and strong answers make it.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several conflicts developed with the mechanism in each, plus recognition that supply-side improvement can dissolve them; L2 (6-10) conflicts explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list of objectives.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 22-23) states: "Core inflation was unchanged in August 2026, while transport costs rose sharply on the back of higher fuel prices."\n\nUsing the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that UK inflation in 2026 was primarily a cost-push phenomenon that monetary policy could do little about.`, marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For: the composition of the August 2026 data points overwhelmingly to costs. Headline CPI rose to 3.1% from 2.9% while core was flat at 2.6%; transport inflation rose from 3.6% to 4.6%, driven by motor fuels; the MPC attributed higher energy prices to Middle East conflict.\n• For: output and employment moved in the wrong direction for demand-pull - growth slowed from 0.6% to 0.4% between Q1 and Q2 2026 and unemployment rose to 4.9%. This is a leftward SRAS shift, not a rightward AD shift.\n• For: monetary policy cannot lower the world oil price. Raising Bank Rate to suppress an imported cost shock imposes the entire adjustment on domestic output and employment, so the sacrifice ratio is high and the policy is poorly targeted.\n• Against: services inflation of 3.4% is domestically generated and wage-intensive, and well above the 2% target. Core inflation of 2.6% is also above target. Stripping out energy does not leave price stability.\n• Against: the distinction blurs at the second round. A cost shock becomes persistent inflation only if wages and expectations validate it, and expectations are precisely what monetary policy CAN influence. This is why three of nine MPC members voted to raise Bank Rate to 4% in September 2026 and why the majority warned about second-round effects.\n• Against: the counterfactual matters. Bank Rate at 3.75%, well above the 0.1% of 2020-21, is itself restraining demand - the MPC judged that domestic activity and tight financial conditions were restraining inflation. Inflation of 3.1% may be the result of monetary policy working, not of its impotence.\n• Evaluation - the policy question is not "can policy remove the shock" but "should policy accommodate it". Looking through a temporary energy shock is right if expectations are anchored and wrong if they are not, and the anchoring itself depends on the central bank's credibility, which is a product of past policy.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer accepts the cost-push diagnosis of the AUGUST INCREASE while arguing that the inflation as a whole had a domestic component that monetary policy both could and should address, and that credibility is the mechanism by which policy acts on a supply shock. The opposite conclusion is equally creditworthy if defended against these points.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, analysis and evaluation integrated, conclusion follows from the argument; L4 (16-20) well-developed and mostly balanced; L3 (11-15) some evaluation, uneven balance; L2 (6-10) descriptive and one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Assess the view that economic growth should be the UK government's principal macroeconomic objective.`, marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For: growth is the only sustainable route to higher living standards, and compounding makes small differences in the trend rate enormous over a generation. Real GDP per capita of about £36 000 in 2026 would take roughly 35 years to double at 2% growth but 70 years at 1%.\n• For: growth relieves every other constraint. Higher output raises tax receipts at unchanged rates, which is the only comfortable way to reduce public sector net debt from 93.8% of GDP (end-August 2026) and to meet the stability rule requiring a current budget surplus by 2029-30.\n• For: growth reduces unemployment through derived demand, and reduces the political intensity of distributional conflict because incomes can rise for everyone at once.\n• Against - growth is not welfare: GDP omits unpaid work, leisure, health and environmental depletion, and says nothing about distribution. In the practice data, real GDP grew 8.5% over five years while real median household income grew 1.1% - growth that did not reach the typical household.\n• Against - sustainability: growth that depletes natural capital or raises emissions borrows from future output. Sustainable growth requires decoupling, which has only partly been achieved.\n• Against - stability may matter more: an economy growing fast and unstably is worse for investment than one growing steadily, because uncertainty raises risk premia. This is the argument for the inflation target having primacy, and for the MPC's independence.\n• Against - the objectives conflict: prioritising growth worsens the current account (1.9% of GDP deficit in Q2 2026) and, once capacity is reached, raises inflation - which was 3.1% in the year to August 2026.\n• Against - a government cannot simply choose a growth rate. The trend rate is determined by productivity, investment and demographics, which policy influences slowly and unreliably. Making growth the principal objective risks demand stimulus that raises only the price level.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer distinguishes growth as an OBJECTIVE from growth as a MEANS, and reaches a defensible position - for example that the right objective is sustainable and inclusive growth in real income per head, which preserves the substance of the case while meeting the distributional and environmental objections.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Discuss the view that the Phillips curve is no longer a useful guide for macroeconomic policy.`, marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Against the curve: the simple relationship has failed repeatedly. In 2021-23 and again in 2026 UK inflation and unemployment rose together - inflation of 3.1% in the year to August 2026 alongside unemployment rising to 4.9%. A stable downward-sloping curve cannot generate that.\n• Against: the relationship appeared to flatten in the 2010s, with unemployment falling to historic lows without generating inflation, and then steepened abruptly. A relationship whose slope is unstable cannot be used to set policy.\n• Against: the natural rate itself is unobservable and moves - through hysteresis, migration, participation and changing labour market institutions - so even the expectations-augmented version gives policymakers a target they cannot locate. Estimates of the NAIRU are revised heavily.\n• Against - the Lucas critique: if policymakers try to exploit the trade-off, agents change their expectations and the relationship shifts, so the curve ceases to hold precisely when it is used.\n• For the curve: the expectations-augmented version is not refuted by stagflation - it PREDICTS it, as a shift of the short-run curve caused by higher expected inflation or a supply shock. What failed was the naive 1960s version, not the theory.\n• For: the vertical long-run curve remains the central organising insight of modern macroeconomic policy. It is why inflation targeting exists, why central bank independence was granted, and why the MPC's September 2026 discussion centred on second-round effects - that is an expectations argument in Phillips curve terms.\n• For: the short-run trade-off is real and is used every time a central bank decides how fast to return inflation to target. The MPC's 6-3 split in September 2026 was precisely a disagreement about the size of the short-run output cost of faster disinflation.\n• Evaluation: the curve is useless as a menu of policy choices and indispensable as a framework for thinking about the relationship between slack, expectations and inflation. Judging it by the first use and discarding it fails to engage the second.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer separates the naive from the expectations-augmented version, and the curve's role as a forecasting tool from its role as a framework, reaching a conclusion that distinguishes them.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 24-25) states: "Unemployment rose to 4.9% in the three months to July 2026, while inflation remained above the Bank of England's target."\n\nUsing the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that reducing unemployment should take priority over controlling inflation.`, marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For prioritising unemployment: the costs fall on identifiable people and are severe - lost income, lost skills, worse health outcomes and higher benefit spending. The costs of moderate inflation are diffuse and partly nominal.\n• For: hysteresis makes unemployment costs permanent. Long spells out of work raise the natural rate and reduce potential output, so tolerating unemployment today lowers the economy's capacity tomorrow - the supply side is damaged by a demand-side failure.\n• For: the inflation in question is substantially imported. Core CPI was unchanged at 2.6% in the year to August 2026 while transport inflation rose to 4.6%, so tightening imposes an output cost without addressing the cause.\n• Against prioritising unemployment: inflation is cumulative and self-reinforcing, unemployment is not. Once expectations shift, the short-run Phillips curve moves out and the eventual output cost of disinflation is larger than the output saved by delaying - the argument that led to the 1997 grant of operational independence to the Bank of England.\n• Against: in the long run the choice does not exist. The long-run Phillips curve is vertical, so demand policy cannot hold unemployment below the natural rate; attempting it delivers accelerating inflation and the same unemployment.\n• Against: unemployment at 4.9% is low by historical standards and may be close to the natural rate, in which case demand expansion would raise inflation without reducing unemployment. Falling inactivity (20.9%, down 0.1 points) suggests part of the rise is labour supply, not demand deficiency.\n• Against: the institutional point. The MPC has a statutory 2% CPI remit, with support for growth and employment subject to that. Reversing the priority would require changing the remit and would risk the credibility that anchors expectations - and with public sector net debt at 93.8% of GDP and 30-year gilt yields near 5.9% in early September 2026, a loss of credibility would be expensive.\n• Evaluation of the framing: the two are not independent. Price stability is the precondition for the stable investment that generates employment, so "prioritising employment" by tolerating inflation may reduce employment over a decade.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer identifies the time horizon as decisive - the case for prioritising unemployment is strong in a deep demand-deficient recession and weak when unemployment is near the natural rate and inflation is above target, as in the UK in 2026 - and defends that conclusion against the hysteresis argument.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Assess the view that deflation is more damaging to an economy than moderate inflation.`, marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For: deflation triggers a self-reinforcing spiral. Expected price falls cause consumers to defer purchases, which cuts AD, which cuts prices further - a mechanism with no natural stopping point. Moderate inflation has no equivalent spiral.\n• For - debt deflation: nominal debts are fixed, so a falling price level raises their real value while nominal incomes fall. With UK household and government debt both large, and public sector net debt at 93.8% of GDP at the end of August 2026, deflation would sharply raise real debt burdens and default risk.\n• For - monetary policy is disarmed: with the nominal rate floored near zero, negative inflation makes the real interest rate positive and rising exactly when stimulus is needed. Japan's experience after 1990 is the standard case. This is why the target is 2%, not 0%.\n• For - real wage rigidity: nominal wages are sticky downwards, so falling prices raise real wages, raising firms' real costs and producing redundancies rather than pay cuts.\n• Against - it depends on the cause: benign deflation, produced by a rightward shift in AS from technological progress or falling commodity prices, comes with RISING output and rising real incomes. It is not damaging at all, which shows that the damage comes from the demand deficiency, not the falling prices.\n• Against - moderate inflation has real costs too: it redistributes arbitrarily from savers to borrowers, erodes fixed incomes, creates menu and shoe-leather costs, blurs relative price signals, and can accelerate. UK inflation of 3.1% in the year to August 2026 means a real pay cut for anyone whose nominal pay rose by less.\n• Against - high or unstable inflation is unambiguously worse than mild deflation: the comparison in the question is with MODERATE inflation, and the honest answer depends on the numbers. Deflation of 0.5% caused by cheaper technology is less damaging than inflation of 10%.\n• Against - practical experience: sustained deflation is rare in modern economies precisely because central banks act to prevent it, whereas inflation episodes are common, so the realised costs of inflation have been larger historically even if the theoretical costs of deflation are more severe.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer makes the benign/malign distinction the centre of the argument and concludes that malign, demand-driven deflation is indeed more damaging than moderate inflation because it is self-reinforcing and disarms policy, while noting that this is a statement about demand deficiency rather than about falling prices as such.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) shows real GDP rising by 8.52% over five years while real median household income rose by 1.06%.\n\nUsing the extracts and your knowledge of economics, discuss the view that economic growth is a poor indicator of improvements in living standards.`, marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For: the extract data make the point directly. Real GDP grew 8.52% while real GDP per capita grew only 4.42%, because population grew 3.92%; and real median household income grew just 1.06%, so the gains went disproportionately to the top of the distribution.\n• For - what GDP omits: unpaid household and voluntary work, the value of leisure, health, life expectancy, education quality, crime and the quality of the environment. Output that repairs damage (pollution clean-up, accident costs) adds to GDP while representing no gain in welfare.\n• For - sustainability: GDP does not deduct the depletion of natural capital, so growth financed by resource depletion or emissions overstates the improvement and may reduce future living standards.\n• For - the informal and digital economy: unmeasured output, and free digital services with a consumer surplus not captured in GDP, mean the measure is increasingly imperfect in both directions.\n• Against: GDP per capita correlates strongly across countries with almost every direct welfare indicator - life expectancy, infant mortality, literacy, access to clean water. The correlation is high enough that it remains the single most informative number available.\n• Against: it is the only measure available consistently, quarterly, comparably across countries and over decades. Alternatives such as the Human Development Index or measures of national well-being are annual, partly subjective and not designed for short-run policy.\n• Against: growth finances the public services that raise living standards, because it raises tax receipts at unchanged rates - which is precisely the constraint facing a UK government with public sector net debt at 93.8% of GDP at the end of August 2026.\n• Against - the criticisms are really about which measure of growth: most are answered by using real GDP per capita, or median rather than mean income, rather than by abandoning growth as an indicator. The extract's contrast between 8.52% and 1.06% is itself made using GDP data.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer concludes that headline real GDP growth is a poor indicator of TYPICAL living standards while real GDP per head, read alongside distributional and environmental measures, remains indispensable - and supports that with the arithmetic in the extract.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Evaluate the view that a recession has permanent effects on an economy's productive potential.`, marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - hysteresis in the labour market: long spells of unemployment destroy skills, weaken attachment and cause employer screening on duration. Cyclical unemployment becomes structural, raising the natural rate and shifting LRAS left, so the recession permanently lowers potential output.\n• For - the capital stock: in a recession firms scrap capital and cancel investment. Capital not installed in 2009 or 2020 is missing from the capital stock permanently, and the accelerator means investment falls further than output does.\n• For - innovation and intangibles: R&D and training budgets are cut first in a downturn, and firm-specific know-how is lost with redundancies. These are the inputs to long-run productivity growth.\n• For - the UK evidence: output has never returned to the pre-2008 trend path, and productivity growth has been persistently weaker since then. Real GDP grew 1.2% in the year to Q2 2026, well below pre-crisis norms.\n• Against - recessions can raise potential output: a downturn forces the exit of the least productive firms and the reallocation of labour and capital to more productive uses - Schumpeter's creative destruction. Measured productivity often rises in recessions for this reason.\n• Against - attribution: the post-2008 productivity slowdown is common to almost all advanced economies and began in several before the crisis, so it may reflect the exhaustion of a technological wave rather than the recession itself. Correlation is not causation.\n• Against - policy can prevent the damage: furlough-type schemes preserved employment relationships through 2020, and active labour market policy shortens unemployment spells. Hysteresis is a consequence of the policy response, not an inevitable consequence of recession.\n• Against - measurement: potential output is unobservable. The "permanent loss" is measured against an extrapolated pre-crisis trend that may itself have been unsustainable - pre-2008 output may have been above potential, financed by a credit boom, in which case less was lost than it appears.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer accepts that the mechanisms of hysteresis are real while arguing that the size of the permanent effect depends on the depth and length of the recession and on the policy response - and notes that the measurement problem makes the empirical claim harder to settle than the theoretical one.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Assess the view that the output gap is too unreliable a concept to be used as the basis for macroeconomic policy.`, marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - potential output is unobservable: it must be estimated by statistical filtering or by production-function methods, both of which impose assumptions. Different methods give materially different answers for the same year.\n• For - revisions: estimates of the gap are revised heavily as GDP data are revised and as the trend is re-estimated, and revisions often change the SIGN. Policy set on a real-time estimate can turn out to have been set on the wrong side of zero.\n• For - the pre-2008 failure: output before the financial crisis was later judged to have been above potential, financed by unsustainable credit growth. At the time the gap appeared benign, so policy was looser than it should have been - a documented case of the concept misleading policymakers.\n• For - aggregation conceals: an economy can show aggregate slack alongside acute shortages of particular skills or capacity, so an aggregate number gives a false reading of inflationary pressure.\n• Against - policy needs some measure of slack: the question of whether extra demand will raise output or prices is unavoidable, and refusing to estimate the gap does not make the question go away - it just means answering it implicitly and without discipline.\n• Against - it can be cross-checked: survey measures of capacity utilisation, the vacancies-to-unemployment ratio, core and services inflation, and wage growth relative to productivity all bear on the same question. The MPC's September 2026 judgement rested on a range of such indicators, not on a single gap estimate.\n• Against - uncertainty argues for caution, not abandonment: the appropriate response to an unreliable estimate is to move policy gradually and to attach wide confidence intervals - which is what inflation-targeting central banks do, and what the 6-3 split at the September 2026 meeting reflects.\n• Against - the alternatives are worse: targeting the money supply failed in the 1980s because velocity proved unstable; a fixed exchange rate outsources policy; and a pure rule ignores shocks. Every practical framework needs a judgement about slack somewhere.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer accepts the measurement critique in full and argues that it establishes a case for humility, gradualism and multiple indicators rather than for abandoning the concept, since no framework can avoid the underlying question.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Discuss the view that continued economic growth is incompatible with environmental sustainability.`, marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For: growth in output has historically required growth in energy and materials use, and the atmosphere's capacity to absorb emissions is finite. If growth compounds indefinitely and resource use is proportional to output, the constraint binds eventually.\n• For - negative externalities: the market price of output excludes the marginal external cost of emissions and depletion, so the free market over-produces relative to the socially optimal level. Growth measured by GDP therefore overstates the welfare gain because it omits the environmental cost.\n• For - intergenerational equity: current consumption of non-renewable resources and of the carbon budget reduces the productive potential available to future generations, so it is not growth at all but a transfer across time.\n• For - the rebound effect: efficiency improvements lower the effective price of energy services, which raises demand for them, offsetting part of the saving.\n• Against - decoupling: several advanced economies including the UK have reduced territorial greenhouse gas emissions while real GDP has grown, which is direct evidence that output and emissions can move in opposite directions.\n• Against - growth in what: services, software and knowledge-intensive output have far lower material intensity than manufacturing, so the composition of growth matters as much as its rate. The UK is a services-dominated economy - services accounted for the whole of the 0.4% growth in Q2 2026, with production flat.\n• Against - growth funds the transition: renewable generation, grid investment and carbon capture are capital-intensive, and only a growing economy generates the savings, tax receipts and investment to pay for them. Zero growth would make decarbonisation harder, not easier.\n• Against - the real problem is pricing, not growth: carbon taxes, tradable permits and regulation internalise the externality, so output grows in the direction of low-carbon activity. On this view the incompatibility is a consequence of a market failure that policy can correct.\n• Qualification worth crediting: territorial emissions measures exclude the emissions embodied in imports, so apparent UK decoupling may partly reflect offshoring production rather than reducing it.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer distinguishes growth in measured output from growth in resource use, argues that the compatibility depends on the rate of decoupling relative to the rate of growth, and handles the consumption-versus-territorial emissions objection.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Evaluate the view that the UK's trend rate of economic growth has fallen permanently since the financial crisis of 2008.`, marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For: UK output has not returned to the pre-2008 trend path, and productivity growth - the main determinant of the trend rate - has been far weaker since than before. Real GDP grew 1.2% in the year to Q2 2026, at or below most current estimates of trend.\n• For - investment: business investment has been persistently low as a share of GDP, so capital per worker has grown slowly. The accelerator and the high cost of capital reinforce this: 10-year gilt yields were around 5.2% and 30-year yields around 5.9% in early September 2026, a materially higher hurdle rate than in the 2010s.\n• For - hysteresis and labour supply: economic inactivity remained high at 20.9% in May to July 2026, and payrolled employment fell by 145 000 over the year to August 2026. A smaller effective labour force lowers potential output.\n• For - structural change: reduced trade openness raises costs and reduces the gains from specialisation. The UK's trading arrangements changed substantially after 2020, and world trade barriers have risen sharply since 2025, with the US average effective tariff rate reaching 18.2% by August 2025.\n• Against - measurement: potential output and the trend rate are unobservable and estimated with wide error. Pre-2008 growth may have been above trend, inflated by an unsustainable credit boom, in which case the "fall" is partly a correction of a mismeasured starting point.\n• Against - it may not be permanent: the slowdown is common to almost all advanced economies, which points to a global technological pause rather than a UK-specific permanent impairment. Diffusion of artificial intelligence and automation could raise measured productivity substantially.\n• Against - policy can change it: the determinants of the trend rate - investment, skills, infrastructure, competition, planning - are all policy-sensitive. Calling the fall permanent is a statement about expected policy, not about an economic law.\n• Against - mismeasurement of output: the growing share of intangible investment and of free digital services means measured GDP may understate real output growth, so part of the apparent slowdown may be a statistical artefact.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer accepts the evidence that the realised trend rate has been lower since 2008 while arguing that "permanently" is not established, and identifies investment and productivity as the variables on which the answer turns.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated, conclusion follows from the argument; L4 (16-20) well-developed and mostly balanced; L3 (11-15) some evaluation, uneven balance; L2 (6-10) descriptive and one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },
    ]
  },
  '3.2.4': {
    name: 'Financial Markets & Monetary Policy',
    questions: [
      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nAn undated government bond\n  Nominal (par) value:      £100\n  Annual coupon payment:    £4.00\n  Current market price:     £64.00\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate the current yield on the bond. Give your answer as a percentage to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Current yield = annual coupon / market price x 100 = 4.00 / 64.00 x 100 = 6.25%.\nAnswer: 6.25%.\n\n2 marks for 6.25%. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for dividing the coupon by the PAR value (4/100 = 4.00%), which gives the coupon rate rather than the yield - method error, 1 mark maximum. Note that the yield exceeds the coupon rate precisely because the bond trades below par.` },

      { q: `Extract B (line 7) — written for this practice question\n"Commercial banks in this economy hold reserves equal to 8% of their deposits."\n\nUsing Extract B, calculate the value of the credit (money) multiplier. Give your answer to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Credit multiplier = 1 / reserve ratio = 1 / 0.08 = 12.5.\nAnswer: 12.50.\n\n2 marks for 12.50. 1 mark for the formula stated correctly without the arithmetic, or for an answer of 8 or 0.08 that shows the ratio has been identified but not inverted. Candidates may note that this is a theoretical maximum: in practice banks hold excess reserves and lending is constrained by capital requirements and loan demand, so the realised multiplier is smaller.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 2-3) — real data\n"Bank Rate was maintained at 3.75% at the Monetary Policy Committee meeting ending on 16 September 2026. CPI inflation was 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026."\n\nUsing Extract A, calculate the approximate real rate of interest. Give your answer as a percentage to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Real interest rate is approximately the nominal rate minus the rate of inflation = 3.75 - 3.10 = 0.65.\nAnswer: 0.65%.\n\n2 marks for 0.65%. 1 mark for a correct statement of the Fisher relationship without the arithmetic, or for the calculation performed the wrong way round (-0.65%). Accept the exact Fisher calculation (1.0375/1.031 - 1 = 0.63%) for full marks. Adding the two figures earns no marks.` },

      { q: `Extract C (Table 3) — written for this practice question\n  Money supply (M):            £2 400bn\n  Velocity of circulation (V):  1.10\n  Real output (Q):             £2 485bn\n\nUsing Extract C (Table 3) and the equation of exchange MV = PQ, calculate the implied price level P as an index number. Give your answer to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `MV = PQ, so P = MV / Q.\nMV = 2 400 x 1.10 = 2 640.\nP = 2 640 / 2 485 = 1.06237...\nAs an index number: 106.24.\n\n2 marks for 106.24 (accept 1.06 expressed as a ratio with the working shown). 1 mark for correctly calculating MV = 2 640 without completing the division, or for the correct method with a rounding error. Dividing Q by MV is a method error: 1 mark maximum.` },

      { q: `Extract B (line 11) — real data\n"Sterling traded at around 1.16 euros in September 2026."\n\nA UK exporter sells machinery priced at £2 400. Using Extract B, calculate the price of the machinery in euros, to the nearest euro.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Price in euros = price in sterling x exchange rate = 2 400 x 1.16 = 2 784.\nAnswer: 2 784 euros.\n\n2 marks for 2 784 euros. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for dividing rather than multiplying (2 400/1.16 = 2 069 euros), which is the calculation a UK importer buying a euro-priced good would perform - method error, 1 mark maximum.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nSterling effective exchange rate index (2005 = 100)\n  January:   82.4\n  December:  79.6\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate the percentage change in the sterling effective exchange rate index over the year. Give your answer to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Percentage change = (79.6 - 82.4) / 82.4 x 100 = -2.8 / 82.4 x 100 = -3.3980...\nAnswer: -3.40% (a depreciation of 3.40%).\n\n2 marks for -3.40% with the sign or the word "depreciation"/"fall". 1 mark for the correct method with a sign, arithmetic or rounding error, or for identifying the fall of 2.8 index points without expressing it as a percentage. Dividing by the final value is a method error: 1 mark maximum.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\n  Bank Rate (%):                        0.75   3.00   5.25   4.25   3.75\n  Average new 2-year fixed mortgage rate (%): 1.60   3.40   5.90   5.20   4.60\n  Average outstanding mortgage rate, all borrowers (%): 2.10   2.40   3.10   3.70   3.90\n  (five successive years, earliest first)\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that changes in Bank Rate are transmitted to UK households with a lag.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• New fixed mortgage rates track Bank Rate closely and almost immediately: both peak in the same year, at 5.25% and 5.90%, and both fall thereafter (1 mark).\n• The average rate paid by ALL borrowers behaves quite differently: it continues rising from 3.10% to 3.90% for two years AFTER Bank Rate has peaked and started to fall (2 marks for identifying the divergence with figures).\n• Explanation: most UK mortgages are fixed for two or five years, so a change in Bank Rate reaches a household only when its fix expires. Borrowers who fixed at low rates are still rolling onto higher ones long after policy has begun to loosen (1 mark).\n• Consequence: the effective stance of monetary policy on households continues tightening even as the MPC eases, which is one reason the Bank works on the assumption that policy takes up to two years to have its full effect (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — written for this practice question\nA government bond paying an annual coupon of £4 on a nominal value of £100\n  Market price:   £125.00   £100.00   £80.00   £64.00\n  Current yield:  3.20%     4.00%     5.00%    6.25%\n\nExplain how the data in Extract B (Table 2) show the relationship between bond prices and bond yields.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• The relationship is inverse: as the price falls from £125 to £64 the yield rises from 3.20% to 6.25% (1 mark).\n• Verification with a calculation: 4/125 = 3.20% and 4/64 = 6.25% - the coupon is fixed in cash terms, so the yield is determined entirely by the price paid (2 marks for showing at least one calculation and explaining the fixed coupon).\n• At par (£100) the yield equals the coupon rate of 4.00%; above par the yield is below the coupon rate, below par it is above (1 mark).\n• Significance explained: because the relationship is mechanical, a rise in market interest rates depresses the prices of existing bonds, imposing capital losses on holders - which is how monetary policy transmits to bank and pension fund balance sheets and to asset prices generally (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract C (Table 3) — written for this practice question\n              Year 1   Year 2   Year 3   Year 4\n  Central bank asset purchases, cumulative (£bn):   200      375      445      445\n  Narrow money (monetary base), annual growth (%):  +42.0    +31.5    +9.8     +1.2\n  Broad money (M4), annual growth (%):              +3.8     +2.1     +1.4     +0.9\n  Bank lending to households and firms, annual growth (%): +0.4  -0.6   +0.3    +0.8\n\nExplain how the data in Extract C (Table 3) suggest that quantitative easing raised the monetary base by far more than it raised bank lending.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• Cumulative purchases rise from £200bn to £445bn, and narrow money grows by 42.0% and 31.5% in the first two years - the direct effect of the central bank crediting commercial banks' reserve accounts (1 mark).\n• Broad money grows by only 3.8% and 2.1% over the same years, an order of magnitude less (1 mark).\n• Bank lending grows by 0.4% and then FALLS by 0.6%, so the asset purchases were not passed on as credit to households and firms at anything like the same rate (1 mark).\n• Explanation: the textbook money multiplier assumes banks lend out reserves in excess of a fixed ratio. In practice banks were rebuilding capital, borrowers were deleveraging and loan demand was weak, so the extra reserves sat on balance sheets. The realised multiplier was far below its theoretical value (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 5-6) — real data\n"The European Central Bank raised its deposit rate to 2.50% on 10 September 2026. The Bank of England held Bank Rate at 3.75% on 17 September 2026. Sterling traded at around 1.16 euros in September 2026, having reached around 1.18 euros in July."\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A show how interest rate differentials may influence the sterling-euro exchange rate.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• The differential in September 2026 was 1.25 percentage points in sterling's favour (3.75% less 2.50%), which is a positive return to holding sterling-denominated deposits (1 mark for the calculation).\n• Mechanism: a higher relative return attracts short-term capital ("hot money") inflows. Investors must buy sterling to buy sterling assets, so demand for sterling on the foreign exchange market rises and the currency appreciates (1 mark).\n• But sterling FELL from about 1.18 euros in July to about 1.16 euros in September even though the UK rate remained the higher of the two (1 mark for noting that the data do not straightforwardly support the mechanism).\n• Explanation of the apparent contradiction: what moves the exchange rate is the CHANGE in the expected differential, not its level. The ECB raised while the Bank of England held, so the differential narrowed, and markets price expectations rather than current rates. Other influences - rising UK government borrowing costs, the current account deficit and risk sentiment - also bear on the rate (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained. Credit candidates who recognise that the data are consistent with a narrowing differential rather than contradicting the theory.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — written for this practice question\n              Year 1   Year 2   Year 3   Year 4\n  Bank Rate (%):        0.10     0.10     1.75     5.25\n  CPI inflation (%):    0.9      2.6      9.1      6.8\n\nExplain how the data in Extract B (Table 2) show that the real rate of interest was negative for much of this period.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• The real interest rate is approximately the nominal rate minus inflation (1 mark for stating the relationship).\n• Calculations: Year 1 = 0.10 - 0.9 = -0.8%; Year 2 = 0.10 - 2.6 = -2.5%; Year 3 = 1.75 - 9.1 = -7.35%; Year 4 = 5.25 - 6.8 = -1.55% (2 marks for at least three correct).\n• The real rate is negative in every year, and most sharply negative in Year 3 at -7.35%, even though the NOMINAL rate was rising fastest that year (1 mark).\n• Explanation: a negative real rate means savers lose purchasing power and borrowers repay in money worth less than they borrowed, so the stance of policy was still stimulative in real terms despite nominal tightening. Nominal rates alone are a poor guide to how restrictive policy is (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract C (Figure 3) — real data, ONS and Bank of England\n  CPI inflation, 12 months to August 2026:   3.1%   (July 2026: 2.9%)\n  Core CPI, 12 months to August 2026:        2.6%\n  CPI services, 12 months to August 2026:    3.4%\n  Bank of England CPI inflation target:      2.0%\n  Bank Rate, September 2026:                 3.75%\n\nExplain how the data in Extract C (Figure 3) show that the Bank of England was not meeting its inflation target in August 2026.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• CPI inflation of 3.1% is 1.1 percentage points above the 2% target (1 mark).\n• The overshoot exceeds one percentage point, which triggers the requirement for the Governor to write an open letter to the Chancellor explaining the deviation and the action being taken (1 mark).\n• Inflation rose between July and August, from 2.9% to 3.1%, so it was moving away from target rather than towards it (1 mark).\n• Developed point: the overshoot was not confined to volatile items. Core inflation of 2.6% and services inflation of 3.4% are both above target, so the deviation is not purely an energy effect - though core being much closer to target than headline indicates that the recent increase was concentrated in items core excludes (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained. Candidates should recognise that the target is symmetric: undershooting by more than one percentage point requires a letter too.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 1-2) states: "The Monetary Policy Committee voted by a majority of 6-3 to maintain Bank Rate at 3.75%; three members voted to increase it to 4%."\n\nWith the help of an AD/AS diagram, analyse how an increase in Bank Rate is transmitted to the rate of inflation.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: AD/AS with AD shifting left from AD1 to AD2; the price level falls from P1 to P2 (or rises more slowly) and real output falls from Y1 to Y2. Strong answers mark the starting point near or beyond Yfe so that the price effect dominates.\n• Market rates channel: Bank Rate is the rate paid on commercial banks' reserves at the Bank of England, so it anchors money market rates and feeds through into mortgage, overdraft, business loan and deposit rates.\n• Cost of borrowing and reward for saving: dearer credit and a better return on saving reduce interest-sensitive consumption - housing, cars, consumer durables - and reduce business investment by raising the hurdle rate above the marginal efficiency of capital. C and I both fall, and the multiplier magnifies the fall.\n• Asset prices and wealth: a higher discount rate lowers bond, equity and house prices. The inverse bond price-yield relationship is mechanical: a fixed coupon on a lower price is a higher yield. Lower wealth lowers consumption.\n• Exchange rate: a higher relative return attracts capital inflows and appreciates sterling, which lowers the sterling price of imports directly and reduces net exports.\n• Expectations: the decision signals the Bank's tolerance for inflation. Anchored expectations keep wage settlements moderate, which holds SRAS in place.\n• Lags analysed: the Bank works on the assumption that policy takes up to two years to have its full effect, and the UK's fixed-rate mortgage market lengthens the lag further - which is why the MPC must act on a forecast rather than on the current 3.1% outturn.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram plus at least three channels developed as chains of reasoning, with lags; L2 (4-6) diagram plus two channels with limited development; L1 (1-3) "higher rates reduce spending" with no mechanism or model.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 8-9) states: "Gilt prices fell sharply during the year, and the yield on the 30-year gilt reached about 5.9% in early September 2026, its highest since the late 1990s."\n\nAnalyse the relationship between bond prices and bond yields, and why that relationship matters for government borrowing.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The mechanical relationship: a conventional gilt pays a coupon fixed in cash terms at issue. The current yield is coupon divided by market price, so price and yield are inversely related by construction. A £4 coupon on a £100 bond yields 4.00%; on a £64 bond it yields 6.25%.\n• Why prices move: bond prices are the present value of a fixed future cash flow. When expected future interest rates rise, the discount rate rises, the present value falls, and the yield rises to match. Expectations of inflation do the same, because they erode the real value of fixed coupons.\n• Supply and demand: heavy gilt issuance to finance a deficit raises supply; quantitative tightening removes the central bank as a buyer and returns stock to the market. Both push prices down and yields up.\n• Consequence for government borrowing analysed: the yield is the interest rate the government pays on NEW issuance and on refinancing maturing debt. With public sector net debt at £2 985.5bn, or 93.8% of GDP, at the end of August 2026, a sustained rise in yields raises debt interest spending substantially, crowding out other spending within a fixed budget.\n• Consequence for fiscal rules: higher projected debt interest reduces the headroom against the stability rule, which requires the current budget to be in surplus by 2029-30. Under the March 2026 OBR forecast that headroom was about £23.6bn - a figure that higher yields erode directly.\n• Wider consequence: the gilt yield is the benchmark risk-free rate, so it raises the cost of capital for private borrowers too, reducing investment.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) the inverse relationship explained with a calculation or present-value reasoning AND the fiscal consequences analysed; L2 (4-6) the relationship stated with partial development; L1 (1-3) assertion that prices and yields move in opposite directions.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 3-4) states: "Between 2009 and 2021 the Bank of England purchased hundreds of billions of pounds of government bonds in an attempt to support demand."\n\nWith the help of a diagram, analyse how quantitative easing is intended to raise aggregate demand.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: either an AD/AS diagram with AD shifting right from AD1 to AD2, or a bond market supply-and-demand diagram in which central bank purchases shift demand for gilts right, raising the price and lowering the yield. The strongest answers use the bond market diagram to derive the AD shift.\n• Mechanism step 1: the Bank creates central bank reserves electronically and uses them to buy gilts from non-bank financial institutions in the secondary market. It does not lend directly to government.\n• Step 2 - the portfolio rebalancing channel: buying gilts raises their price and lowers their yield. Sellers hold cash they must reinvest, and with gilt yields depressed they move into corporate bonds, equities and property, raising those prices too and lowering the cost of capital for firms.\n• Step 3 - the wealth effect: higher asset prices raise household wealth, which raises consumption.\n• Step 4 - the credit and exchange rate channels: lower market interest rates should encourage bank lending, and lower yields reduce the return on sterling assets, depreciating the currency and raising net exports.\n• Step 5: C, I and (X - M) all rise, so AD shifts right, raising output and, on a steep SRAS, the price level - which was the point, since QE was deployed when inflation was below target.\n• Analysis of why the effect may be weak: if banks hoard reserves rather than lending, and if firms will not invest because expected demand is weak, the transmission stops at asset prices. The practice data showing narrow money growing at 42% while lending fell illustrate exactly this.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram plus the portfolio rebalancing chain developed to AD, with a limitation; L2 (4-6) diagram and a partial mechanism; L1 (1-3) "the Bank prints money to boost spending".` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 10-11) states: "With Bank Rate at 0.1%, the Committee judged that conventional policy had reached its practical limit."\n\nWith the help of a diagram, analyse why monetary policy may become ineffective at very low interest rates.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: liquidity preference diagram with the demand for money curve becoming horizontal at a very low interest rate (the liquidity trap); an increase in the money supply from MS1 to MS2 leaves the rate of interest unchanged. An acceptable alternative is an AD/AS diagram showing AD failing to shift.\n• The zero lower bound: nominal rates cannot fall far below zero because holders of deposits can switch into physical cash, which pays zero. Some central banks have set mildly negative policy rates, but the scope is limited by the cost of storing cash.\n• The liquidity trap: at very low rates, bond prices are correspondingly high and investors expect them to fall, so they prefer to hold any extra money as cash rather than buy bonds. Demand for money becomes perfectly elastic, and further increases in the money supply cannot lower the interest rate.\n• Even if rates fall, the interest-elasticity of investment may be low in a slump: firms facing weak expected demand will not invest whatever the cost of borrowing, because the accelerator term dominates. Banks may also refuse to lend to riskier borrowers.\n• Policy consequence analysed: the central bank must turn to unconventional tools - quantitative easing, forward guidance, funding-for-lending schemes - or the burden falls on fiscal policy, whose multiplier is at its largest precisely when the monetary offset is absent.\n• Dated context: Bank Rate was 0.1% from March 2020 and stood at 3.75% in September 2026, so the UK currently has substantial conventional room - but the episode is the reason the inflation target is 2% rather than 0%, since a positive target keeps nominal rates away from the bound.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram plus the liquidity trap and the investment-elasticity arguments developed, with the policy consequence; L2 (4-6) one argument developed with a diagram; L1 (1-3) "rates cannot go below zero" alone.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 12-13) states: "The interest rate differential between the United Kingdom and the euro area stood at 1.25 percentage points in September 2026."\n\nWith the help of a foreign exchange market diagram, analyse how a rise in UK interest rates relative to those abroad affects the value of sterling.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: foreign exchange market for sterling, with the exchange rate (euros or dollars per pound) on the vertical axis and the quantity of sterling on the horizontal. Demand for sterling shifts right from D1 to D2 (and/or supply shifts left as UK residents keep funds at home), raising the rate from e1 to e2.\n• Chain of reasoning: a higher UK rate relative to abroad raises the return on sterling-denominated deposits and short-term assets. Foreign investors must buy sterling before they can buy those assets, so demand for the currency rises. These short-term speculative flows are "hot money" and can be very large relative to trade flows.\n• Expectations matter more than levels: what moves the rate is the change in the EXPECTED future differential. Rates already anticipated are priced in, which is why sterling can fall on an unchanged rate if the other central bank tightens - as when the ECB raised its deposit rate to 2.50% on 10 September 2026 while the Bank of England held at 3.75% on 17 September, and sterling traded around 1.16 euros against about 1.18 euros in July.\n• Consequences analysed: an appreciation lowers the sterling price of imports, which reduces imported inflation and is part of the monetary transmission mechanism; it also makes exports dearer abroad, reducing net exports and AD.\n• Qualifications: other determinants can dominate - the current account deficit (1.9% of GDP in Q2 2026), relative inflation rates, political risk and global risk sentiment. Higher rates that reflect fears about inflation or fiscal sustainability can weaken rather than strengthen a currency.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) correctly labelled foreign exchange diagram, the hot money chain developed and the expectations qualification made; L2 (4-6) diagram plus a simple flow argument; L1 (1-3) "higher rates mean a stronger pound" with no diagram or mechanism.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 14-15) states: "Most of the money in a modern economy is created not by the central bank but by commercial banks making loans."\n\nAnalyse how commercial banks create credit, and why the credit multiplier overstates the amount of money created in practice.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The mechanism: when a bank makes a loan it credits the borrower's deposit account with a matching sum. The loan is the bank's asset and the deposit its liability, so the act of lending creates a new bank deposit - which is money - rather than lending out an existing one.\n• The textbook multiplier: if banks keep a fixed fraction of deposits as reserves, an initial deposit of £1 000 with a reserve ratio of 8% supports total deposits of £1 000/0.08 = £12 500, since each round is re-lent less the reserve.\n• Why the multiplier overstates the outcome - capital, not reserves, is the binding constraint: post-crisis regulation requires banks to hold capital against risk-weighted assets, so the ability to lend depends on equity and on the risk weight of the loan, not on the reserve ratio.\n• Why it overstates - banks hold excess reserves: after quantitative easing, banks held reserves far above any required level. The practice data show narrow money growing 42% in a year while bank lending grew 0.4% and then fell 0.6%.\n• Why it overstates - demand-determined lending: banks cannot force borrowers to borrow. In a downturn households and firms deleverage and creditworthy demand collapses, so the constraint is on the demand side.\n• Why it overstates - leakages: cash withdrawn from the banking system and money moved abroad both break the chain, as do banks' own liquidity buffers.\n• Analysis of the policy implication: because money is created endogenously by lending decisions, the central bank influences the money supply indirectly, by setting the price of credit and through macroprudential tools such as capital requirements and loan-to-income limits, rather than by controlling a monetary base.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) the credit creation mechanism explained accurately AND at least two reasons the multiplier overstates it, developed; L2 (4-6) the multiplier explained with one limitation; L1 (1-3) the formula or a description of lending only.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 16-17) states: "The central bank stands ready to lend to solvent banks that cannot obtain funds elsewhere."\n\nAnalyse the lender of last resort function of a central bank and the moral hazard it can create.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The function: a central bank lends to solvent but illiquid banks against good collateral, usually at a penalty rate. The classic formulation is Bagehot's - lend freely, to solvent institutions, against good collateral, at a high rate.\n• Why it is needed - maturity transformation: banks borrow short (deposits repayable on demand) and lend long (mortgages, business loans). No bank can repay all depositors at once, so a loss of confidence can cause a run on an institution that is fundamentally sound.\n• Why it is needed - contagion and systemic risk: banks are connected through the interbank market and through common exposures, so one failure can cascade. The social cost of a systemic banking crisis far exceeds the private cost to shareholders, which is a market failure justifying intervention.\n• Moral hazard analysed: if banks expect to be rescued, the expected cost of failure falls, so the privately optimal level of risk rises. Banks hold thinner capital and liquidity buffers, lend to riskier borrowers and rely more on wholesale funding than they otherwise would - profits stay private while losses are socialised.\n• Compounding factor: depositors and wholesale creditors have less incentive to monitor a bank they believe to be protected, so market discipline weakens too. Deposit insurance has the same double-edged effect.\n• How the hazard is mitigated: the penalty rate; solvency and collateral requirements; capital and liquidity regulation; resolution regimes that impose losses on shareholders and bondholders; ring-fencing of retail from investment banking; and macroprudential oversight by the Financial Policy Committee.\n• Analysis of the residual trade-off: the tools reduce moral hazard but cannot eliminate it, because a central bank that credibly refuses to act in a genuine systemic crisis would impose enormous costs - the "too big to fail" problem is a time-inconsistency problem.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) the function and the moral hazard both developed, with mitigation and the residual trade-off; L2 (4-6) both defined with partial development; L1 (1-3) one term defined.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 18-19) states: "Sterling appreciated against the currencies of the United Kingdom's main trading partners over the first half of the year."\n\nWith the help of an AD/AS diagram, analyse the effects of an appreciation of sterling on the UK economy.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: AD shifts left from AD1 to AD2 as net exports fall, and SRAS shifts right as imported input costs fall; output falls from Y1 to Y2 and the price level falls from P1 to P2. The downward pressure on prices is reinforced from both directions; the output effect is negative.\n• Demand-side chain: an appreciation makes exports dearer in foreign currency and imports cheaper in sterling. Export volumes fall and import volumes rise, so (X - M) falls, AD shifts left and the multiplier magnifies the fall in output and employment, particularly in exporting regions.\n• Supply-side chain: the UK imports energy, food, components and capital equipment, so a stronger pound lowers firms' input costs at every price level, shifting SRAS right. This is disinflationary and partly offsets the output loss.\n• Inflation: the appreciation lowers imported inflation directly - a significant channel for a country importing a high share of its food and energy - which is why the exchange rate is treated as part of the monetary transmission mechanism.\n• Current account: whether the balance improves or worsens depends on elasticities. By the Marshall-Lerner condition, an appreciation worsens the trade balance only if the sum of the price elasticities of demand for exports and imports exceeds one; in the short run elasticities are low, so the effect is muted.\n• Analysis of what matters more - the CAUSE: an appreciation driven by higher productivity and stronger competitiveness has very different consequences from one driven by hot money attracted by high interest rates, which can leave exporters uncompetitive without any underlying improvement.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) both shifts drawn or the SRAS offset explicitly analysed, with elasticities or the cause of the appreciation; L2 (4-6) AD shift only, correctly analysed; L1 (1-3) "a strong pound hurts exporters" with no model.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 20-21) states: "The Bank of England was granted operational independence in 1997 and its Committee publishes minutes explaining each decision."\n\nAnalyse how central bank independence and forward guidance are intended to make monetary policy more effective.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The time-inconsistency problem: a government controlling interest rates has an incentive to engineer a pre-election boom, and the public knows it. Expected inflation therefore rises, and the government must either validate it or accept a recession - so discretionary political control delivers higher average inflation for no gain in output.\n• Independence as a solution: delegating the instrument to a committee with a statutory 2% CPI remit removes the electoral incentive. Because agents believe the target will be pursued, expected inflation anchors at the target, the short-run Phillips curve sits lower, and the output cost of maintaining price stability falls.\n• Accountability mechanisms analysed: the target is set by the Chancellor, not the Bank, so the objective remains democratic while the instrument is technical. Minutes and voting records are published - the September 2026 decision to hold at 3.75% was recorded as a 6-3 vote - and an overshoot of more than one percentage point requires an open letter, as CPI inflation of 3.1% in the year to August 2026 did.\n• Forward guidance: communicating the likely future path of policy works because spending decisions depend on expected rates over the life of a mortgage or an investment project, not on today's overnight rate. Guidance therefore moves long rates immediately, without any change in Bank Rate.\n• Why guidance can fail: it is only as good as its credibility. If the Bank departs from guidance when circumstances change, agents discount future guidance; if it honours guidance when circumstances have changed, it makes a policy error. Publishing a divided vote conveys genuine uncertainty but weakens the signal.\n• Analysis of limits: independence cannot address supply shocks, and a central bank can lose credibility through forecast errors. It also places significant power in unelected hands, which becomes contentious when the distributional consequences - through asset prices and mortgage costs - are large.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) the time-inconsistency argument AND the expectations mechanism of guidance both developed, with limits; L2 (4-6) independence described with partial analysis; L1 (1-3) "the Bank is independent of politicians" alone.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 22-23) states: "In September 2026 the Committee voted unanimously to reduce the Bank's stock of government bonds to zero over a multi-year period, at an average pace of about £46bn a year."\n\nWith the help of a diagram, analyse the likely effects of quantitative tightening on gilt yields and on the wider economy.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: the gilt market with price on the vertical axis and quantity on the horizontal. Quantitative tightening either shifts supply to the private market right (active sales) or shifts demand left (the central bank ceasing to reinvest maturing holdings). Either way the price falls and, by the inverse relationship, the yield rises.\n• The mechanism: QT is the reversal of QE. Gilts return to private hands, so private investors must absorb more duration risk, and the term premium they require rises. The portfolio rebalancing channel runs in reverse - investors sell other assets to buy gilts, so equity and corporate bond prices also weaken.\n• Effect on the wider economy: higher gilt yields raise the benchmark risk-free rate, so the cost of capital rises for firms and mortgage rates rise for households. C and I fall, AD shifts left - QT is therefore a tightening of monetary policy operating alongside Bank Rate.\n• Effect on the public finances: higher yields raise the cost of new and refinanced government borrowing, with public sector net debt at 93.8% of GDP at the end of August 2026 and 30-year yields around 5.9% in early September 2026. Crystallised losses on the Asset Purchase Facility are also met by the Treasury.\n• Analysis of the September 2026 design: the MPC scrapped sales of long-dated gilts, holding around £120bn maturing in 2049 or later against future banknote issuance, and slowed the overall pace from the £70bn targeted in the previous twelve months to about £46bn. The purpose was to avoid adding to pressure at the long end of the curve, where demand is thinnest - an illustration that the COMPOSITION of QT matters as much as its pace.\n• Uncertainty: the effect of QT is harder to estimate than that of Bank Rate, because it works through quantities and term premia rather than a posted price, so the MPC treats Bank Rate as the active instrument and QT as a background process.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram, the yield mechanism and at least two wider consequences analysed, with dated detail; L2 (4-6) the reversal of QE described with partial analysis; L1 (1-3) "the Bank sells bonds" alone.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — real data, September 2026\n  Bank Rate:                                     3.75%\n  MPC vote, meeting ending 16 September 2026:    6 to hold, 3 to raise to 4%\n  CPI inflation, 12 months to August 2026:       3.1%   (target 2.0%)\n  Core CPI, 12 months to August 2026:            2.6%\n  UK unemployment rate, May to July 2026:        4.9%   (up 0.2 percentage points on the year)\n  UK real GDP growth, Q2 2026:                   +0.4%  (Q1 2026: +0.6%)\n  Payrolled employees, August 2026:              down 145 000 on the year\n  Quantitative tightening, from September 2026:  stock to fall to zero by 2034, average pace about £46bn a year\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data suggest that UK monetary policy was too tight in September 2026?`, marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Support for "too tight": every real-economy indicator is deteriorating. Growth slowed from 0.6% to 0.4%, unemployment rose 0.2 percentage points to 4.9%, and payrolled employment fell by 145 000 over the year. Bank Rate of 3.75% against core inflation of 2.6% is a positive real rate of about 1.15 percentage points, which is restrictive.\n• Support for "too tight": quantitative tightening is tightening policy independently of Bank Rate, so the overall stance is more restrictive than the headline rate implies - and gilt yields near 5.9% at the long end were already raising the cost of capital.\n• Support for "too tight": the inflation overshoot is concentrated in energy and transport, which monetary policy cannot influence. Core at 2.6% is far closer to target than headline at 3.1%.\n• Against: inflation was 3.1% and RISING, 1.1 percentage points above target and more than a percentage point above it, which triggers an explanatory letter. Services inflation of 3.4% is domestically generated. Policy that leaves inflation above target and rising is not obviously too tight.\n• Against: three of nine Committee members voted to raise Bank Rate to 4%, so a third of the Committee judged policy to be too LOOSE. The 6-3 split is evidence of genuine uncertainty, not of a clear error.\n• Against: policy acts with a lag of up to two years, so the current stance is aimed at inflation in 2028. Judging it by today's output and employment data is judging it by the wrong variables.\n• Limits of the data: no estimate of the output gap, no wage growth, no inflation expectations measure, no forecast - all of which the MPC had and none of which is shown here. Unemployment at 4.9% may be at or near the natural rate, in which case there is no excess restraint to remove.\n• Judgement: the real-economy data are consistent with a restrictive stance, but with inflation above target and rising, and a third of the Committee wanting more restraint, the data fall well short of showing that policy was too tight. The most defensible conclusion is that the data show policy was tight, which is what it was intended to be.\n\nLevels: L3 (8-10) quantitative use of the data on both sides, explicit recognition of what the data cannot show, supported judgement; L2 (4-7) partial data use with a thin judgement; L1 (1-3) assertion. A conclusion reached without reference to the data cannot exceed L1.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\n              Year 1   Year 2   Year 3   Year 4\n  Cumulative central bank asset purchases (£bn):  200   375    445     445\n  Equity price index (Year 1 = 100):              100   128    147     151\n  House price index (Year 1 = 100):               100   109    118     121\n  Bank lending to households and firms, annual growth (%):  +0.4  -0.6  +0.3  +0.8\n  Real business investment, annual growth (%):    -2.1   +0.9  +1.4    +1.1\n  Share of household wealth held by the wealthiest fifth (%): 62   64     66      67\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data suggest that quantitative easing raised asset prices rather than productive investment?`, marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Support: asset prices rose far faster than anything in the real economy. Equities rose 51% and house prices 21% over the period, while real business investment rose by a cumulative total of about 1.3% after a fall of 2.1% in Year 1.\n• Support: bank lending, the channel through which QE was supposed to reach firms, grew by 0.4%, then FELL by 0.6%, and never exceeded 0.8%. The portfolio rebalancing channel clearly operated; the credit channel barely did.\n• Support: the wealth share of the top fifth rose from 62% to 67%, consistent with the criticism that QE is regressive because asset ownership is concentrated - and the wealthiest have the lowest marginal propensity to consume, so the wealth effect on AD is weaker than the asset price rise suggests.\n• Against: investment did turn positive from Year 2, exactly as purchases accelerated, and the counterfactual is unknown. QE may have prevented a far worse outcome rather than producing a good one - the question is not whether investment was strong but whether it would have been weaker without QE.\n• Against: rising asset prices are part of the intended transmission, not a side effect. Higher equity prices lower firms' cost of equity capital and higher house prices support consumption, so the two columns are not alternatives.\n• Against: correlation is not causation. Equity prices respond to global earnings expectations and house prices to supply constraints and demographics; neither is driven solely by UK asset purchases.\n• Limits: no control group, no counterfactual, no data on the cost of capital, and inequality may have been rising for unrelated reasons. Four years is too short to judge an investment response that operates with long lags.\n• Judgement: the data show a very large divergence between asset prices and productive investment, which supports the criticism. They cannot establish that QE caused the divergence, nor that investment would not have been worse without it.\n\nLevels: L3 (8-10) quantitative use of both sides, explicit treatment of the counterfactual problem, supported judgement; L2 (4-7) partial use with a thin judgement; L1 (1-3) assertion. A conclusion reached without reference to the data cannot exceed L1.` },

      { q: `Explain the main channels through which a change in monetary policy is transmitted to the rate of inflation.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Framing: the Bank of England sets Bank Rate - 3.75% in September 2026 - and this affects inflation only indirectly, through several channels that operate with long and variable lags. The Bank works on the assumption that policy takes up to two years to have its full effect.\n• Market rates channel: Bank Rate is the rate paid on commercial banks' reserves, so it anchors money market rates, which feed into mortgage, loan, overdraft and deposit rates. The UK's predominantly fixed-rate mortgage market lengthens this lag considerably.\n• Cost of borrowing and reward for saving: higher rates raise the cost of debt service and of new borrowing and improve the return on saving, so interest-sensitive consumption and business investment fall. C and I are components of AD, so AD shifts left.\n• Asset prices and wealth: a higher discount rate lowers bond, equity and house prices - the bond price-yield relationship is mechanical. Lower wealth reduces consumption and reduces collateral for borrowing.\n• Cash flow: for households and firms with variable-rate debt, a rate rise reduces disposable income immediately. This channel reaches high-MPC borrowers and so is disproportionately powerful relative to its size.\n• Exchange rate: a higher relative return attracts capital inflows and appreciates sterling, which lowers the sterling price of imports directly - the fastest-acting of all the channels for a country importing much of its food and energy - and reduces net exports.\n• Expectations: the decision signals the Bank's commitment to the 2% target. Anchored expectations restrain wage settlements and price-setting, holding SRAS in place. This is why the MPC's September 2026 warning about second-round effects mattered as much as the rate decision itself.\n• Credit availability: banks tighten lending standards as well as raising prices, so quantities as well as prices adjust.\n• The aggregate effect: AD shifts left relative to AS, the output gap narrows or turns negative, unemployment rises towards or above the natural rate, wage growth moderates, and inflation falls - a chain with several links, each of which can fail.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several channels developed as full chains of reasoning, with lags and dated application; L2 (6-10) channels explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Explain the main functions of financial markets in a modern economy.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• To facilitate saving: financial markets provide households with assets - deposits, bonds, equities, pensions - in which to store purchasing power for the future, and so make saving possible on any scale.\n• To lend to businesses and individuals: banks and bond markets channel funds from surplus units to deficit units, allowing investment to exceed a firm's own retained profit and allowing households to buy housing.\n• To facilitate the exchange of goods and services: the payments system - current accounts, card networks, clearing - is what makes a modern division of labour possible. Its failure would halt the real economy, which is why banks are treated as systemically important.\n• To provide forward markets in currencies and commodities: forwards, futures and options allow firms to hedge the risk of adverse price movements, so an exporter can fix the sterling value of a future receipt.\n• To provide a market for equities: primary issuance raises new capital for firms, and a liquid secondary market makes the primary market possible, because investors will only buy what they can later sell.\n• Maturity transformation: banks borrow short and lend long, reconciling savers' preference for liquidity with borrowers' need for long-term funds. This is genuinely valuable and is simultaneously the source of bank fragility.\n• Risk transformation and pooling: insurance and diversified funds allow risks that would be ruinous individually to be spread across many holders.\n• Price discovery and the allocation of capital: market prices aggregate dispersed information about expected returns and risks. Gilt yields - around 5.2% at ten years in early September 2026 - are the benchmark risk-free rate against which every other project is assessed, so they determine which investments proceed.\n• Analysis worth crediting: each function can fail. Asymmetric information produces adverse selection and moral hazard, speculation can detach prices from fundamentals, and externalities mean private risk-taking imposes systemic costs - which is the justification for regulation.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several functions explained with the economic reasoning behind each, not merely named; L2 (6-10) functions described with limited explanation; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Explain the main causes of market failure in financial markets.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Asymmetric information - adverse selection: borrowers know more about their own riskiness than lenders do. If lenders raise the interest rate to cover average risk, safe borrowers withdraw and the pool worsens, so credit may be rationed rather than priced. The same problem afflicts the sale of complex securities whose risks the seller understands better than the buyer.\n• Asymmetric information - moral hazard: once insured or rescued, an institution takes more risk than it otherwise would. Deposit insurance and the lender of last resort function both reduce the private cost of failure, so banks economise on capital and liquidity. "Too big to fail" is this problem at systemic scale.\n• Negative externalities and systemic risk: a bank's failure imposes costs on other banks through interbank exposures and on the whole economy through the payments system and credit supply. The private calculation of risk omits these costs, so privately optimal risk-taking is socially excessive.\n• Principal-agent problems: traders and executives whose bonuses depend on short-term profit have an incentive to take risks whose losses fall on shareholders, depositors or taxpayers later. The asymmetry between bonus and downside is the mechanism.\n• Speculation and market bubbles: if buyers purchase an asset because they expect its price to rise rather than for its yield, price becomes self-referential. Herding and extrapolative expectations sustain bubbles, and their collapse transmits into the real economy through wealth effects and bank balance sheets.\n• Market rigging and abuse of market power: concentration in particular markets and the manipulation of benchmark rates distort the prices on which countless contracts depend.\n• Bounded rationality and consumer inertia: financial products are complex and infrequently purchased, so consumers misjudge risk, under-save for retirement and fail to switch - a form of information failure with large welfare costs.\n• The policy response follows from the causes: capital and liquidity requirements internalise systemic externalities; the Financial Policy Committee conducts macroprudential oversight; conduct regulation addresses information failure; resolution regimes and ring-fencing attack moral hazard.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several causes developed with the economic mechanism in each, linked to the regulatory response; L2 (6-10) causes explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list of terms.` },

      { q: `Explain the main determinants of the exchange rate in a floating exchange rate system.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Framing: in a floating system the rate is the price of one currency in terms of another, set by demand and supply on the foreign exchange market. Anything that changes demand for or supply of the currency changes the rate.\n• Relative interest rates: a higher return on sterling assets attracts short-term capital inflows, raising demand for sterling. The UK-euro area differential was 1.25 percentage points in September 2026, with Bank Rate at 3.75% and the ECB deposit rate at 2.50%. What matters is the CHANGE in the expected differential, since current rates are already priced in.\n• Relative inflation rates: purchasing power parity implies that a currency with persistently higher inflation must depreciate to keep traded goods prices comparable. This dominates over years rather than months.\n• The current account and trade flows: exports generate demand for sterling and imports generate supply of it. A persistent deficit - the UK's was 1.9% of GDP in Q2 2026 - means a structural excess supply of the currency, which must be financed by capital inflows.\n• Capital flows and foreign direct investment: inward FDI and portfolio investment raise demand for the currency. These flows are far larger than trade flows in a financial centre such as the UK.\n• Speculation and expectations: the great majority of foreign exchange turnover is speculative. If traders expect a currency to fall they sell it now, which causes the fall - expectations are self-fulfilling in the short run.\n• Relative economic performance and risk sentiment: expected growth, political stability and perceived fiscal sustainability all affect willingness to hold a currency. Rising government borrowing costs - 30-year gilt yields near 5.9% in early September 2026 - can weaken a currency even as they raise the return on its assets, if they signal fiscal risk.\n• Government and central bank intervention: even in a floating system, reserves can be used to influence the rate, and monetary policy decisions affect it as a by-product.\n• Analysis worth crediting: these determinants can pull in opposite directions, which is why sterling fell from about 1.18 euros in July 2026 to about 1.16 in September despite the UK retaining the higher policy rate.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several determinants developed with mechanisms and the short-run/long-run distinction; L2 (6-10) determinants explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Explain the main ways in which a central bank can influence the supply of money and credit in an economy.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Framing: most money in a modern economy is bank deposits created when commercial banks lend, so the central bank influences the money supply mainly by changing the price and the conditions of credit rather than by controlling a quantity directly.\n• The policy rate: Bank Rate, 3.75% in September 2026, is the rate paid on commercial banks' reserves. It anchors money market rates and therefore the rates at which banks lend. A higher rate reduces the demand for credit and so reduces the creation of new deposits.\n• Open market operations: buying or selling short-term securities adjusts the quantity of reserves in the system and keeps market rates at the policy rate. This is the day-to-day operational tool rather than a separate policy.\n• Quantitative easing: large-scale purchases of government bonds with newly created reserves expand the monetary base directly and depress yields along the curve, encouraging portfolio rebalancing into riskier assets. The Bank's gilt portfolio stood at around £488bn as at September 2026.\n• Quantitative tightening: the reverse. In September 2026 the MPC voted unanimously to reduce the stock to zero by 2034 at an average pace of about £46bn a year, combining maturing holdings with around £20bn of annual sales, having dropped sales of long-dated gilts.\n• Reserve requirements and liquidity regulation: requiring banks to hold more reserves or high-quality liquid assets reduces the volume of lending a given balance sheet can support.\n• Capital requirements and macroprudential tools: the Financial Policy Committee can vary the countercyclical capital buffer and impose limits on loan-to-value or loan-to-income ratios, which restrain credit growth in particular markets without changing the policy rate for the whole economy.\n• Funding schemes and forward guidance: term funding schemes lower banks' cost of funds conditional on their lending, and guidance about the future path of rates moves longer-term rates and therefore credit conditions today.\n• Analysis worth crediting: the central bank can restrict credit far more reliably than it can expand it. Reserves can be created but banks cannot be compelled to lend, nor borrowers to borrow - the practice data of narrow money growing 42% while lending fell make the point.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several instruments explained with mechanisms, including the endogenous-money framing; L2 (6-10) instruments listed with some explanation; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Explain the main reasons why governments regulate the financial system.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• To limit systemic risk and negative externalities: the cost of a banking crisis falls on the whole economy - lost output, unemployment, and fiscal cost - not only on the bank's owners. Because private decisions omit these costs, unregulated risk-taking is socially excessive. Capital and liquidity requirements force institutions to internalise part of the cost.\n• To address moral hazard: deposit insurance and the lender of last resort function are necessary to prevent runs, but they reduce the private cost of failure and so encourage risk-taking. Regulation, resolution regimes that impose losses on shareholders and bondholders, and ring-fencing of retail from investment banking are the offsetting measures.\n• To correct information asymmetry: consumers cannot assess the risk of complex products, and depositors cannot audit a bank. Disclosure requirements, conduct regulation, suitability rules and compensation schemes substitute for information the consumer cannot obtain.\n• To protect consumers from market power and abuse: concentration, benchmark manipulation and mis-selling all impose costs that competition alone has not prevented.\n• To maintain confidence in the payments system: the payments system is close to a public good, and its failure would halt real economic activity, so its continuity is a public interest that private institutions will not fully price.\n• To restrain excessive credit growth: credit booms precede most financial crises, so macroprudential tools - the countercyclical capital buffer, loan-to-income limits, stress testing - lean against the cycle in a way monetary policy cannot do without affecting the whole economy.\n• To prevent financial crime: anti-money-laundering and sanctions enforcement pursue objectives that are not economic efficiency at all but which the financial system is uniquely placed to serve.\n• The costs of regulation, which a strong answer weighs: compliance costs fall disproportionately on smaller institutions and raise barriers to entry; higher capital requirements raise the cost of credit and can reduce lending to small firms; and regulatory arbitrage shifts activity into less-regulated shadow banking, where the risk is harder to see. Regulatory capture and the risk that rules fight the last crisis are further limitations.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several reasons developed from the underlying market failure, with the costs of regulation weighed; L2 (6-10) reasons explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 24-25) states: "With Bank Rate at 3.75% and public sector net debt at 93.8% of GDP, the government has little room to support demand through borrowing."\n\nUsing the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that monetary policy is now a more effective instrument than fiscal policy for managing the UK macroeconomy.`, marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For monetary policy - speed and flexibility: the MPC meets eight times a year and can change Bank Rate immediately, as on 16 September 2026. Fiscal changes require a Budget - the next set for 28 October 2026 - and legislation, so the decision and implementation lags are far longer.\n• For monetary policy - freedom from the political cycle: operational independence since 1997 removes the incentive to engineer pre-election booms, which anchors expectations and lowers the output cost of price stability. Fiscal policy is made by politicians facing elections.\n• For monetary policy - it is currently the instrument with room to move. Bank Rate at 3.75% can be cut substantially; fiscal space is constrained by public sector net debt at 93.8% of GDP (end-August 2026), by 30-year gilt yields around 5.9% in early September 2026, and by the stability rule requiring a current budget surplus by 2029-30 with headroom of roughly £23.6bn on the March 2026 OBR forecast.\n• Against - monetary policy is blunt and slow-acting: it takes up to two years to have its full effect, it cannot be targeted at particular sectors or regions, and the UK's fixed-rate mortgage market delays and disperses the transmission unpredictably.\n• Against - it is powerless at the zero lower bound and in a liquidity trap, precisely when it is most needed. Bank Rate sat at 0.1% from March 2020, and the unconventional substitutes worked mainly through asset prices.\n• Against - monetary policy cannot address supply shocks. The August 2026 inflation increase came from transport and energy, with core flat at 2.6%; raising Bank Rate cannot lower the oil price and imposes the whole cost on output and employment.\n• Against - fiscal policy is better targeted and can act on the supply side. Public investment raises AD now and LRAS later; tax and benefit changes can be directed at high-MPC households; and the fiscal multiplier is largest precisely when monetary policy is at its limit.\n• Against - distributional consequences: monetary policy operates through asset prices and mortgage rates, so it redistributes between generations and between debtors and creditors without democratic decision. Fiscal policy makes those choices explicitly.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer argues that effectiveness is state-dependent - monetary policy dominates in normal conditions and fiscal policy at the lower bound or when the shock is structural - and applies that framework to the UK in 2026, where above-target inflation and constrained fiscal space both point to monetary policy as the active instrument.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, analysis and evaluation integrated, conclusion follows from the argument; L4 (16-20) well-developed and mostly balanced; L3 (11-15) some evaluation, uneven; L2 (6-10) descriptive, one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Assess the case for maintaining the operational independence of the Bank of England.`, marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - time inconsistency: a government controlling interest rates has an incentive to inflate before an election, and because the public anticipates this, expected inflation is higher on average with no gain in output. Delegating the instrument removes the incentive and anchors expectations, which lowers the sacrifice ratio.\n• For - credibility and the evidence: UK inflation averaged far lower in the two decades after 1997 than in the two before. Anchored expectations are also what allowed the MPC to look through parts of the 2021-26 energy shocks without a wage-price spiral.\n• For - technical expertise and continuity: the MPC's nine members can specialise and act on a two-year forecast horizon rather than an electoral one.\n• For - transparency and accountability are preserved: the 2% target is set by the Chancellor, not the Bank, so the objective is democratic; minutes and voting records are published - the September 2026 hold was recorded as 6-3 - and an overshoot of more than one percentage point requires an open letter, which inflation of 3.1% in the year to August 2026 required.\n• Against - democratic legitimacy: unelected officials make decisions with enormous distributional consequences, between borrowers and savers, young and old, and asset owners and wage earners. Quantitative easing in particular had large effects on wealth distribution with no democratic mandate.\n• Against - the record is not unblemished: inflation reached over 11% in 2022 despite independence, and the Bank has acknowledged forecasting errors. Independence guarantees insulation from politics, not competence.\n• Against - coordination failure: monetary and fiscal policy affect the same variables. An independent central bank tightening while the Treasury consolidates can over-tighten, and neither institution is responsible for the combined stance - a live risk in 2026 with Bank Rate at 3.75%, quantitative tightening under way, and fiscal rules requiring a current budget surplus by 2029-30.\n• Against - mission creep: independence granted for interest rates now extends to a balance sheet of around £488bn in gilts, whose unwinding to 2034 has direct fiscal consequences borne by the Treasury.\n• Against - independence cannot address supply shocks, so it may guarantee only that the wrong instrument is applied skilfully.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer accepts the time-inconsistency case as strong while arguing that its strength depends on the shocks being demand shocks, and concludes on whether the accountability mechanisms are adequate to the powers the Bank now exercises.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Discuss the view that quantitative easing did more harm than good to the UK economy.`, marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Harm - distributional effects: QE works through asset prices, and assets are concentrated. Rising equity and house prices benefit existing owners and price out younger non-owners, widening wealth inequality with no democratic decision having been taken about it.\n• Harm - weak transmission to the real economy: the intended credit channel largely failed. In the practice data narrow money grew 42% in a year while bank lending grew 0.4% and then fell 0.6%; business investment recovered only weakly.\n• Harm - moral hazard and misallocation: years of depressed yields allowed unprofitable firms to survive on cheap credit, encouraged leveraged financial engineering over productive investment, and pushed pension funds into riskier assets to meet return targets.\n• Harm - the exit cost: the portfolio must be unwound. In September 2026 the MPC set a course to reduce the stock to zero by 2034 at about £46bn a year, and the Treasury bears realised losses on the Asset Purchase Facility. QE's costs arrive long after its benefits.\n• Good - the counterfactual: the relevant comparison is not with a healthy economy but with the depression that conventional policy could not prevent at a Bank Rate of 0.1%. Preventing a deflationary spiral, with the debt-deflation and zero-lower-bound dynamics that entails, is a large benefit even if unmeasurable.\n• Good - it worked through the channels it was designed for: it lowered gilt yields and the whole term structure, lowered the cost of government borrowing during the period when deficits were largest, and supported asset prices and therefore consumption.\n• Good - it preserved market functioning: the March 2020 intervention restored orderly trading in the gilt market at a moment of acute dysfunction, a financial stability objective distinct from the demand objective.\n• Evaluation - attribution: separating QE's effects from everything else happening in 2009-21 is close to impossible, so both the claimed benefits and the claimed harms rest on contested counterfactuals. Estimates of QE's effect on output vary very widely.\n• Evaluation - it was an instrument of last resort. Judging it against an ideal policy is the wrong test; the right test is against the alternatives actually available, which were fiscal expansion in a period when governments were consolidating, or nothing.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer is explicit about the counterfactual problem and reaches a conclusion that distinguishes QE's emergency deployment in 2009 and 2020, where the case is strong, from its prolonged continuation through the 2010s, where the distributional and misallocation criticisms bite hardest.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 26-27) states: "Three members, voting in the minority, judged that the risk of second-round effects justified an immediate increase in Bank Rate to 4%."\n\nUsing the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that the Monetary Policy Committee was right to hold Bank Rate at 3.75% in September 2026.`, marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For holding - the shock was a supply shock: CPI inflation rose to 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026 from 2.9%, but core CPI was unchanged at 2.6% and transport inflation rose from 3.6% to 4.6% on motor fuels. Raising Bank Rate cannot lower the oil price; it can only suppress domestic demand, so the output cost would be borne for no gain against the cause.\n• For holding - the real economy was already weakening: growth slowed from 0.6% in Q1 2026 to 0.4% in Q2, unemployment rose to 4.9% in May to July 2026, and payrolled employees fell by 145 000 over the year. Policy acts with a lag of up to two years, so tightening into a slowdown risks overshooting on the downside in 2028.\n• For holding - the overall stance was already restrictive: Bank Rate of 3.75% against core inflation of 2.6% is a positive real rate, quantitative tightening is removing accommodation independently, and gilt yields near 5.9% at the long end were tightening financial conditions further.\n• Against holding - the target is 2% and inflation was 3.1% and RISING, an overshoot large enough to require an explanatory letter. A central bank whose credibility rests on the target cannot indefinitely explain why it is not being met.\n• Against holding - domestically generated inflation was not under control: services inflation of 3.4%, unchanged, is wage-intensive and largely domestic. Core at 2.6% is still above target.\n• Against holding - second-round effects: the majority itself warned that this risk was growing. If wage settlements build in 3.1%, the short-run Phillips curve shifts out and the eventual cost of disinflation is larger than the cost of acting now. Acting early is cheaper than acting late.\n• Against holding - sterling: the ECB raised its deposit rate to 2.50% on 10 September 2026 and sterling fell from about 1.18 euros in July to about 1.16 in September. A weaker pound raises import prices and adds to the very inflation in question.\n• Evaluation of the decision procedure: a 6-3 split is itself informative. It signals genuine uncertainty, which is honest but weakens the forward guidance signal; and it makes a November move more likely, so the question may be one of timing rather than direction. The next decision was due on 5 November 2026.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer decides on a stated criterion - whether inflation expectations were anchored - and recognises that the case for holding rests entirely on that judgement, which the data in the extracts cannot settle. Either conclusion is creditworthy if argued against the strongest opposing point.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated, conclusion follows; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Assess the view that macroprudential regulation is more important than monetary policy in maintaining financial stability.`, marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For regulation - the instruments are targeted: capital requirements, the countercyclical capital buffer, loan-to-income limits and stress testing act directly on the leverage and credit growth that precede crises, without imposing a cost on the whole economy. Interest rates are a blunt instrument by comparison.\n• For regulation - monetary policy has one instrument and two objectives: Bank Rate must be set for the 2% inflation target. If stability requires tighter credit while inflation requires looser policy, one objective must be sacrificed. Tinbergen's rule says each objective needs its own instrument, which is exactly the case for a separate macroprudential toolkit.\n• For regulation - the evidence: the 2008 crisis occurred with inflation at target. Price stability did not deliver financial stability, which is why the Financial Policy Committee was created.\n• For regulation - raising Bank Rate enough to stop a credit boom would require a recession. Using the policy rate to lean against asset prices means accepting large output costs for an uncertain benefit.\n• Against - monetary policy shapes the environment: prolonged very low rates drive the search for yield, leverage and risk-taking that regulation then has to contain. Bank Rate of 0.1% from 2020 and around £488bn of gilt purchases had far larger effects on risk appetite than any buffer setting.\n• Against - regulation is porous: activity migrates to less-regulated shadow banking, where leverage is harder to see. The 2022 UK gilt market episode involving liability-driven investment strategies showed systemic risk emerging outside the regulated banking core.\n• Against - regulation fights the last crisis: rules are written after the event, calibrated against known failure modes, and subject to regulatory capture and arbitrage. Monetary policy at least responds to conditions in real time.\n• Against - regulation has costs: higher capital requirements raise the cost of credit and can reduce lending to small firms, and compliance costs raise barriers to entry, reducing competition.\n• Evaluation of the framing: the two are complements operating on different margins - regulation on the resilience of the system, monetary policy on the aggregate quantity of credit and risk appetite. Quantitative tightening, which the MPC set on a course to 2034 in September 2026, is itself both.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer applies Tinbergen's rule explicitly, concluding that macroprudential tools are the appropriate INSTRUMENT for financial stability while monetary policy remains a powerful determinant of the CONDITIONS regulation must manage - so the two cannot be ranked without specifying which margin is in question.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Discuss the view that the United Kingdom should use the exchange rate as an instrument of macroeconomic policy.`, marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - competitiveness: a lower exchange rate makes exports cheaper abroad and imports dearer, raising net exports and AD. With a current account deficit of 1.9% of GDP in Q2 2026 and a persistent goods deficit, this is a plausible target for policy.\n• For - it is already an instrument: the exchange rate is one of the channels of the monetary transmission mechanism, so a central bank changing Bank Rate is changing the exchange rate whether it says so or not. Targeting it explicitly would at least be transparent.\n• For - a fixed or managed rate imports credibility: pegging to a low-inflation anchor constrains domestic policy and can break an inflationary spiral, which is why some economies use exchange rate targets.\n• Against - the impossible trinity: a country cannot simultaneously have a fixed exchange rate, free capital movement and an independent monetary policy. Targeting the rate means surrendering Bank Rate as an instrument for domestic inflation and output - a very high price when domestic inflation is 3.1% in the year to August 2026 and above target.\n• Against - the ERM experience: the UK's attempt to hold a rate within the Exchange Rate Mechanism ended on 16 September 1992, when reserves and sharply higher interest rates could not defend the parity against speculative pressure. Reserves are trivial relative to daily foreign exchange turnover.\n• Against - a depreciation may not improve the current account: the Marshall-Lerner condition requires the sum of export and import demand elasticities to exceed one. In the short run both are low, so the J-curve means the balance worsens first. UK imports of energy, food and components are price-inelastic.\n• Against - a depreciation is inflationary: it raises the sterling cost of imports directly, which is the last thing an economy with above-target inflation needs. It also reduces real incomes, so it is a real wage cut achieved by stealth.\n• Against - competitive devaluation invites retaliation and does not raise world demand. With the US average effective tariff rate reaching 18.2% by August 2025 and trade-policy activity running at nearly double 2024 levels in early 2026, the retaliation risk is real.\n• Against - the durable solution is supply-side: competitiveness gained by depreciation is temporary and erodes as import costs feed into domestic prices; competitiveness gained by higher productivity is permanent.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer makes the impossible trinity the organising argument, concluding that for an open economy with free capital movement and an inflation target the exchange rate cannot be a separate instrument - only an outcome - and that the competitiveness objective is better pursued through the supply side.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Evaluate the view that a symmetric 2% CPI inflation target remains the right objective for UK monetary policy.`, marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - clarity and accountability: a single number is verifiable, so the public and Parliament can judge performance. The requirement for an open letter when inflation deviates by more than one percentage point - triggered by the 3.1% outturn for the 12 months to August 2026 - makes accountability concrete.\n• For - it anchors expectations: the whole benefit of independence flows through expectations. A known, stable target keeps wage settlements and price-setting moderate, which flattens the short-run Phillips curve and lowers the output cost of disinflation.\n• For - 2% rather than 0%: a small positive rate keeps nominal interest rates away from the zero lower bound, leaving room to cut in a downturn; it allows real wages to fall without nominal pay cuts, which matters because nominal wages are sticky downwards; and it provides a buffer against measurement bias in the index, which tends to overstate inflation.\n• For - symmetry: undershooting is treated as seriously as overshooting, which prevents the deflationary bias that an asymmetric ceiling would create.\n• Against - a single target ignores other objectives: financial stability, asset price inflation, employment and the distributional consequences of policy are all outside the remit. The 2008 crisis occurred with inflation at target.\n• Against - the target cannot handle supply shocks well: when inflation is driven by energy, as the increase to 3.1% in August 2026 largely was with core flat at 2.6%, hitting the target quickly requires a recession. The remit's flexibility about the horizon helps but is a matter of judgement rather than rule.\n• Against - alternatives exist: a higher target, say 3% or 4%, would give more room above the zero lower bound; nominal GDP targeting would automatically accommodate supply shocks while restraining demand shocks; average inflation targeting would allow overshoots to offset past undershoots.\n• Against - changing the target is costly: credibility is the asset the target exists to create, and raising it when inflation is above it would be read as capitulation, unanchoring the expectations the whole framework depends on. The costs of a change are largest precisely when a change looks attractive.\n• Against - measurement: CPI excludes owner-occupiers' housing costs, which CPIH includes, so the targeted index omits a major element of household cost of living.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer weighs the theoretical case for an alternative against the practical cost of changing the anchor, and concludes - most defensibly - that the target's imperfections are real but that the credibility cost of changing it, particularly during an overshoot, outweighs them.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Assess the view that the Bank of England should slow or abandon its programme of quantitative tightening.`, marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For slowing - the effect on gilt yields: returning stock to private investors raises the term premium. UK 30-year gilt yields reached about 5.9% in early September 2026, the highest since the late 1990s, and the ten-year yield was around 5.2%. QT is one contributor among several, but it operates precisely where demand is thinnest.\n• For slowing - fiscal cost: with public sector net debt at £2 985.5bn, 93.8% of GDP, at the end of August 2026, higher yields raise debt interest on new and refinanced borrowing and erode the headroom against the stability rule, which was about £23.6bn on the March 2026 OBR forecast. Realised losses on the Asset Purchase Facility are also met by the Treasury.\n• For slowing - it is an additional tightening whose size is unknown: QT tightens financial conditions alongside Bank Rate of 3.75%, but its quantitative effect is far harder to estimate than a policy rate change, so the combined stance is uncertain. Tightening by an unmeasurable amount into a slowing economy - growth of 0.4% in Q2 2026, unemployment at 4.9% - is a risk.\n• For slowing - the Bank has already acted on this logic: in September 2026 the MPC scrapped sales of long-dated gilts, keeping around £120bn maturing in 2049 or later matched against banknote issuance, and reduced the overall pace from the £70bn targeted over the previous twelve months to about £46bn a year - an implicit acknowledgement that the long end was under strain.\n• Against - normalisation is necessary: a balance sheet of around £488bn in gilts limits the Bank's capacity to respond to the next crisis with asset purchases. Restoring headroom is itself a stability objective.\n• Against - moral hazard and fiscal dominance: a central bank that slows QT because the government's borrowing costs are high looks like one financing the deficit. The perception of fiscal dominance would raise the inflation risk premium and could push yields higher, defeating the purpose.\n• Against - inflation is above target: CPI was 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026 and rising, with three of nine MPC members voting to raise Bank Rate to 4%. Loosening one arm of policy while the other is being debated for tightening is incoherent.\n• Against - attribution: gilt yields are driven mainly by expected policy rates, inflation risk and the scale of government issuance. QT's contribution is estimated to be modest, so slowing it would deliver little relief while incurring the credibility cost.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer distinguishes the PACE and the COMPOSITION of QT from the principle of normalisation, and concludes - as the Committee's own September 2026 decision implies - that adjusting composition to avoid the long end is defensible while abandoning the programme would risk the more expensive charge of fiscal dominance.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Discuss the view that changes in the money supply are the principal cause of changes in the price level.`, marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - the quantity theory: MV = PQ. If the velocity of circulation V is stable and real output Q is determined by the supply side, then P is proportional to M, so inflation is "always and everywhere a monetary phenomenon" in Friedman's formulation.\n• For - the historical evidence: hyperinflations are invariably associated with very rapid monetary expansion to finance government deficits, and the long-run cross-country correlation between money growth and inflation is strong.\n• For - the theoretical point survives even if the mechanism differs: a sustained rise in the price level requires accommodating money, because without it the real money supply falls, interest rates rise and demand is choked off. A cost shock cannot become sustained inflation unless it is monetarily validated.\n• Against - velocity is not stable: the 1980s monetary targeting experiments failed because V moved unpredictably with financial innovation and changing payment habits. If V varies, the equation is an identity with no predictive content.\n• Against - money is endogenous: most money is created when commercial banks lend, so the money supply responds to the demand for credit rather than being set by the central bank. Causation may run from prices and output to money rather than the reverse.\n• Against - the QE evidence: in the practice data narrow money grew 42% in a year while broad money grew 3.8% and bank lending fell. The UK's monetary base expanded enormously after 2009 with inflation below target for most of the following decade - the clearest modern counter-example.\n• Against - the 2021-26 inflation does not fit: the increase to 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026 came overwhelmingly from transport and energy, with core CPI unchanged at 2.6%. The proximate cause was a supply shock, whatever the monetary preconditions.\n• Against - modern policy frameworks abandoned money targets for exactly this reason: the MPC targets inflation directly using Bank Rate, with no monetary aggregate in the remit.\n• Evaluation - the horizon is decisive: over decades, sustained inflation and money growth move together, so the monetarist proposition has long-run content. Over the horizon on which policy is made, two years, velocity and endogeneity make monetary aggregates useless as a guide.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer separates the long-run proposition, which is defensible, from the short-run policy claim, which the evidence does not support, and is explicit that a claim true "in the long run" may be useless to a policymaker.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Evaluate the view that financial markets allocate capital efficiently in a modern economy.`, marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - price discovery: market prices aggregate dispersed information about expected returns and risks more effectively than any planner could. Gilt yields - around 5.2% at ten years in early September 2026 - provide the risk-free benchmark against which every project is assessed, so capital flows towards the highest risk-adjusted returns.\n• For - the range of functions performed: maturity transformation reconciles savers' liquidity preference with borrowers' need for long-term funds; risk pooling and diversification make risks bearable that would be ruinous individually; forward and futures markets let firms hedge; equity markets provide permanent capital and a liquid secondary market that makes primary issuance possible.\n• For - dynamic efficiency: venture capital and equity markets fund innovation that banks would not lend against, because equity holders share the upside.\n• Against - asymmetric information: adverse selection can cause credit rationing rather than pricing, so profitable projects go unfunded, particularly for small firms with no track record. Moral hazard causes excessive risk-taking once losses are socialised.\n• Against - externalities and systemic risk: a bank's risk decisions impose costs on the whole economy that its private calculation omits, so privately efficient allocation is socially inefficient. The 2008 crisis is the demonstration.\n• Against - speculation and bubbles: when assets are bought for expected price appreciation rather than yield, prices detach from fundamentals, capital is misallocated on a large scale, and the correction transmits into the real economy.\n• Against - short-termism and principal-agent problems: quarterly reporting pressure and remuneration linked to short-run profit bias firms against long-lived investment - a plausible contributor to persistently weak UK business investment and therefore to weak productivity growth.\n• Against - the composition of UK lending: a large share of bank lending is secured against existing property rather than financing new productive capital, which inflates asset prices without adding to the capital stock.\n• Evaluation - the counterfactual: the alternative to market allocation is administrative allocation, which has its own and generally worse information problems and is vulnerable to political capture. The question is not perfection but comparison.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer concludes that financial markets allocate capital more efficiently than any available alternative while failing systematically in identifiable ways - information asymmetry, externalities and short-termism - which is precisely why regulation exists, and judges efficiency against the realistic alternative rather than an ideal.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, analysis and evaluation integrated, conclusion follows from the argument; L4 (16-20) well-developed and mostly balanced; L3 (11-15) some evaluation, uneven; L2 (6-10) descriptive, one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },
    ]
  },
  '3.2.5': {
    name: 'Fiscal & Supply-Side Policies',
    questions: [
      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\n  Total public sector receipts:        £1 145bn\n  Total managed expenditure:           £1 268bn\n  Nominal GDP:                         £2 485bn\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate the budget deficit as a percentage of GDP. Give your answer to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Budget deficit = expenditure - receipts = 1 268 - 1 145 = £123bn.\nAs a percentage of GDP = 123 / 2 485 x 100 = 4.9497...\nAnswer: 4.95%.\n\n2 marks for 4.95%. 1 mark for correctly calculating the £123bn deficit without expressing it as a percentage, or for the correct method with an arithmetic or rounding slip. Subtracting the wrong way round and reporting a surplus is a method error: 1 mark maximum.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — written for this practice question\n  Total budget deficit:                £123bn\n  Of which, estimated cyclical component: £28bn\n  Nominal GDP:                         £2 485bn\n\nUsing Extract B (Table 2), calculate the structural budget deficit as a percentage of GDP. Give your answer to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Structural deficit = total deficit - cyclical deficit = 123 - 28 = £95bn.\nAs a percentage of GDP = 95 / 2 485 x 100 = 3.8229...\nAnswer: 3.82%.\n\n2 marks for 3.82%. 1 mark for identifying the £95bn structural component without converting to a percentage, or for the correct method with a rounding slip. Using the total deficit (4.95%) does not answer the question set: no marks for the final answer, 1 mark for method if the subtraction is attempted.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\n  Gross annual income:  £50 000    Income tax paid:  £7 486\n  Gross annual income:  £55 000    Income tax paid:  £9 486\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate the marginal rate of income tax over this range of income. Give your answer as a percentage to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Marginal rate = change in tax paid / change in income x 100.\nChange in tax = 9 486 - 7 486 = £2 000. Change in income = 55 000 - 50 000 = £5 000.\nMarginal rate = 2 000 / 5 000 x 100 = 40.00%.\n\n2 marks for 40.00%. 1 mark for identifying both changes without dividing, or for the correct method with an arithmetic slip. Calculating an AVERAGE rate (9 486/55 000 = 17.25%) answers a different question: 1 mark maximum, and only if the method is otherwise sound.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 5-6) — written for this practice question\n"The Treasury estimates the fiscal multiplier on capital spending at 1.4. The Budget raised public investment by £14bn."\n\nUsing Extract C, calculate the eventual change in national income. Give your answer in £bn to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Change in national income = multiplier x change in injections = 1.4 x £14bn = £19.6bn.\nAnswer: £19.60bn.\n\n2 marks for £19.60bn. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip. Adding the injection a second time (£33.60bn) double-counts, because the multiplier already includes the initial round: 1 mark for method only.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — written for this practice question\n  Total public sector receipts:   £1 145bn\n  Debt interest spending:           £112bn\n\nUsing Extract B (Table 2), calculate debt interest spending as a percentage of total receipts. Give your answer to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Debt interest as a share of receipts = 112 / 1 145 x 100 = 9.7816...\nAnswer: 9.78%.\n\n2 marks for 9.78%. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic or rounding slip, or for an answer expressed as a decimal (0.0978) without converting to a percentage. Dividing receipts by debt interest is a method error: 1 mark maximum.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 1-2) — real data, ONS Public Sector Finances, August 2026\n"Public sector net debt was provisionally estimated at £2 985.5bn at the end of August 2026, £78.5bn more than a year earlier."\n\nUsing Extract A, calculate the percentage increase in public sector net debt over the year to August 2026. Give your answer to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Debt a year earlier = 2 985.5 - 78.5 = £2 907.0bn.\nPercentage increase = 78.5 / 2 907.0 x 100 = 2.7004...\nAnswer: 2.70%.\n\n2 marks for 2.70%. 1 mark for correctly deriving the earlier figure of £2 907.0bn without completing the calculation, or for the correct method with a rounding error. Dividing by the CURRENT stock (78.5/2 985.5 = 2.63%) is a method error, since a percentage increase is measured against the original value: 1 mark maximum.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — real data, ONS Public Sector Finances, August 2026\n  Public sector net debt, end-August 2026:          £2 985.5bn\n  Change on a year earlier:                         +£78.5bn\n  Public sector net debt as a percentage of GDP:    93.8%\n  Change in that ratio on a year earlier:           -1.3 percentage points\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that the national debt can rise while the debt-to-GDP ratio falls.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• The LEVEL of debt rose by £78.5bn, an increase of 2.70% on the £2 907.0bn a year earlier, so the government continued to borrow (1 mark).\n• The RATIO nonetheless fell by 1.3 percentage points to 93.8%, so debt fell relative to the size of the economy (1 mark).\n• Explanation: the ratio is debt divided by nominal GDP. If nominal GDP - real growth plus inflation - grows faster than the debt stock, the ratio falls even though the numerator is rising. Nominal GDP must therefore have grown by more than 2.70% over the year (2 marks for the full reasoning).\n• Significance explained: sustainability depends on the ratio, not the level, because GDP is the tax base from which debt is serviced. This is why inflation and real growth both reduce the debt burden, and why the UK's investment rule is framed in terms of liabilities as a share of GDP rather than in pounds (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — written for this practice question\n              Year 1   Year 2   Year 3   Year 4   Year 5\n  Output gap (% of potential GDP):   +0.8    -0.2    -1.8    -0.6    +0.3\n  Total budget deficit (% of GDP):    3.9     4.4     6.1     5.2     4.6\n  Estimated cyclical component (% of GDP): -0.3   +0.1   +0.9    +0.3   -0.1\n\nExplain how the data in Extract B (Table 2) show that this country's budget deficit was mainly structural.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• Structural deficit = total deficit less the cyclical component: 4.2%, 4.3%, 5.2%, 4.9% and 4.7% of GDP across the five years (2 marks for at least three correct).\n• In every year the structural component is far larger than the cyclical one - for example in Year 3, 5.2 percentage points of the 6.1% deficit are structural and only 0.9 points cyclical (1 mark).\n• In Year 5 the output gap is POSITIVE at +0.3%, so the economy is operating above potential, yet the deficit is still 4.6% of GDP. A deficit that persists in a boom cannot be explained by the cycle (1 mark for this developed inference).\n• Explanation of significance: a cyclical deficit closes automatically as the economy recovers, through automatic stabilisers. A structural deficit does not, so it requires discretionary tax rises or spending cuts - which is why fiscal rules are framed around the cyclically adjusted position (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract C (Table 3) — written for this practice question\nAnnual income, income tax paid, and VAT paid on spending, three households\n                        Household A   Household B   Household C\n  Gross income (£):          22 000       55 000       140 000\n  Income tax paid (£):        1 886        9 486        45 703\n  VAT paid (£):               2 420        4 400         7 000\n\nExplain how the data in Extract C (Table 3) show that income tax is progressive while VAT is regressive.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• Income tax as a share of gross income: A = 8.57%, B = 17.25%, C = 32.65%. The share RISES with income, which is the definition of a progressive tax (2 marks for at least two correct percentages and the definition applied).\n• VAT as a share of gross income: A = 11.00%, B = 8.00%, C = 5.00%. The share FALLS as income rises, which is the definition of a regressive tax (1 mark).\n• Explanation of why VAT is regressive despite a single rate: lower-income households spend a higher proportion of their income and save less, so a tax levied on spending takes a larger share of their income even though every household pays the same rate on the same goods (1 mark).\n• Developed point: judged on the whole tax system, Household A pays 19.57% of income in these two taxes and Household C 37.65%, so the combined system is still progressive - the regressivity of one tax does not make the system regressive (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\n              Year 1   Year 2   Year 3   Year 4\n  Total public sector receipts (£bn):   985     1 042    1 096    1 145\n  Debt interest spending (£bn):          42       68        94      112\n  Spending on public investment (£bn):   96      101        99       94\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) show that rising debt interest may crowd out other public spending.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• Debt interest rose by 166.7% over the period, from £42bn to £112bn, while receipts rose by only 16.2%, from £985bn to £1 145bn (2 marks for both growth rates).\n• As a share of receipts, debt interest rose from 4.26% to 9.78%, so more than twice as large a slice of every pound of tax revenue is now pre-committed (1 mark).\n• Public investment rose from £96bn to £101bn and then FELL to £94bn, ending below its starting level, at the same time as debt interest was rising fastest (1 mark).\n• Explanation: debt interest is a non-discretionary commitment. Within a fixed envelope, every additional pound of it must be met by higher taxation, higher borrowing, or lower spending elsewhere - and capital spending, having no immediate constituency, is typically cut first (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained. Credit candidates who note the data show correlation and that other factors could explain the fall in investment.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — real data, Office for Budget Responsibility, March 2026 Economic and Fiscal Outlook\n  Headroom against the stability rule (current budget in surplus by 2029-30):   about £23.6bn\n  Headroom against the investment rule (PSNFL falling as a share of GDP):        about £27.1bn\n  Public sector net financial liabilities, projected for 2028-29:                82.2% of GDP\n  UK 30-year gilt yield, early September 2026:                                   about 5.9% (highest since the late 1990s)\n\nExplain how the data in Extract B (Table 2) show that the government's ability to meet its fiscal rules is vulnerable to movements in gilt yields.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• The headroom figures are small relative to the public finances as a whole: £23.6bn is under 1% of a GDP of roughly £3 trillion, and under 2.5% of annual receipts (1 mark).\n• Mechanism: gilt yields determine the interest the government pays on new and refinanced borrowing. Higher yields raise projected debt interest, which is day-to-day spending, so it counts against the STABILITY rule - the rule requiring the current budget to be in surplus by 2029-30 (2 marks for linking yields to the rule through debt interest).\n• With the 30-year yield at about 5.9% in early September 2026, its highest since the late 1990s, a sustained rise of even a fraction of a percentage point across the curve can absorb a large share of £23.6bn of headroom (1 mark).\n• Developed point: PSNFL of 82.2% of GDP in 2028-29 must be FALLING as a share of GDP, so the investment rule depends on nominal GDP growth outpacing the growth of liabilities - and higher yields raise liabilities while doing nothing for GDP (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract C (Table 3) — written for this practice question\n              Year 1   Year 3   Year 5   Year 7\n  Public spending on skills and apprenticeships (£bn):  4.2   5.6    6.8     7.1\n  Business R&D spending (% of GDP):                     1.5   1.6    1.8     1.9\n  Output per hour worked, index (Year 1 = 100):        100.0  100.9  102.4   104.1\n  Real business investment, index (Year 1 = 100):      100.0  103.0  107.5   110.2\n\nExplain how the data in Extract C (Table 3) show that supply-side policy affects productivity only slowly.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• Skills spending rose by 69.0% over the period, from £4.2bn to £7.1bn, and R&D spending by 0.4 percentage points of GDP, but output per hour rose by only 4.1% in seven years - roughly 0.6% a year (2 marks for the contrast, with figures).\n• The productivity response is back-loaded: output per hour rises by just 0.9% over the first two years but by 1.7% between Year 5 and Year 7, so the gains accelerate long after the spending began (1 mark).\n• Business investment rose earlier and faster than productivity (3.0% by Year 3 against 0.9%), which is consistent with capital being installed before it raises measured output per hour (1 mark).\n• Explanation: skills take years to acquire and longer to be matched to jobs; R&D must be commercialised and diffused; and infrastructure raises productivity only once in use. This long lag is the central weakness of supply-side policy as a response to a current problem, and it also makes attribution difficult - other factors over seven years could explain the change (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 7-8) states: "The Budget raised public investment by £14bn, financed by additional borrowing."\n\nWith the help of an AD/AS diagram, analyse the likely effects of expansionary fiscal policy on real output and the price level.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: AD shifts right from AD1 to AD2; equilibrium moves from Y1/P1 to Y2/P2. The split between output and price depends on the slope of AS at the starting point, so the strongest answers mark Yfe and discuss the position relative to it.\n• Chain of reasoning: government spending is a component of AD, so the £14bn injection raises AD directly. Through the multiplier - estimated at 1.4 on capital spending in the extract - the eventual rise in national income is £19.6bn, because contractors' wages become consumption which becomes other firms' income.\n• Employment: higher output raises the derived demand for labour, so cyclical unemployment falls and incomes rise further, reinforcing the expansion.\n• The output/price split analysed: with a large negative output gap the AS curve is shallow, so most of the effect is on real output. Near capacity - and with UK unemployment at 4.9% in May to July 2026 and CPI inflation at 3.1% in the year to August 2026 - the curve is steep and much of the effect is on prices.\n• Offsets: crowding out, if extra borrowing raises gilt yields (about 5.9% at 30 years in early September 2026) and reduces private investment; the monetary offset, if the MPC responds by keeping Bank Rate at 3.75% or raising it; and import leakage, since part of the spending goes abroad.\n• Supply-side dimension worth crediting: because this is CAPITAL spending, LRAS should also shift right in the long run, which would raise output without the price effect - but only years later.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram plus the multiplier chain AND the capacity-dependence of the outcome, with at least one offset; L2 (4-6) diagram with a partial chain; L1 (1-3) "more spending raises demand" with no model.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 9-10) states: "Ministers argue that reform of planning, skills and competition policy will raise the economy's productive potential."\n\nWith the help of a diagram, analyse how successful supply-side policies affect an economy's output and price level.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: AD/AS with LRAS shifting right from LRAS1 to LRAS2 (and SRAS shifting right as unit costs fall). With AD unchanged, real output rises from Y1 to Y2 and the price level FALLS from P1 to P2 - the distinctive feature of supply-side policy, that it improves output and inflation together.\n• Chain of reasoning for market-based policy: lower marginal tax rates raise the return to work and to enterprise; deregulation and competition policy reduce barriers to entry, forcing incumbents to cut costs; labour market flexibility speeds the matching of workers to vacancies, reducing frictional and structural unemployment and so raising the effective labour supply.\n• Chain of reasoning for interventionist policy: education and training raise human capital and output per worker; infrastructure raises the productivity of private capital; R&D support corrects the positive externality in innovation, since firms cannot appropriate the full social return on research.\n• Why the policy conflict disappears: because LRAS shifts right rather than the economy moving along a trade-off, growth, employment and inflation all improve simultaneously. This is the strongest argument for the supply side, and a genuine contrast with demand management.\n• Analysis of limits: the lags are very long - in the practice data, seven years of rising skills and R&D spending raised output per hour by 4.1% in total. The outcome is uncertain, the opportunity cost of the spending is immediate, and market-based measures may worsen the distribution of income.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram plus at least two chains of reasoning and the output-and-inflation point made explicitly, with limits; L2 (4-6) diagram plus a list of policies with some explanation; L1 (1-3) policies named with no model.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 11-12) states: "Some economists warn that additional government borrowing simply displaces private sector investment."\n\nWith the help of a diagram, analyse how crowding out may reduce the effectiveness of expansionary fiscal policy.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: the loanable funds market, with the real interest rate on the vertical axis and the quantity of funds on the horizontal. Government borrowing shifts the demand for loanable funds right, raising the equilibrium interest rate from r1 to r2 and reducing the quantity of private borrowing. An acceptable alternative is an AD/AS diagram showing AD shifting right by less than the initial injection.\n• Financial crowding out: the government competes with private borrowers for a limited pool of savings. Higher demand for funds raises the interest rate, which raises firms' hurdle rate above the marginal efficiency of some projects, so private investment falls and partly offsets the rise in G.\n• Resource crowding out: at full employment the economy cannot supply more real resources. Extra public demand for construction workers, steel and land bids up their prices and diverts them from private uses, so real private output falls one-for-one and the fiscal multiplier tends towards zero.\n• Dated application: UK 30-year gilt yields reached about 5.9% in early September 2026, their highest since the late 1990s, with public sector net debt at 93.8% of GDP at the end of August 2026 - conditions in which additional issuance plausibly raises the whole yield curve.\n• Analysis of the Keynesian counter-argument: in a recession with a large negative output gap, there are idle resources and an excess of desired saving over investment, so the interest rate need not rise and the multiplier is large. Crowding out is therefore a claim about the state of the economy, not a general law.\n• Further counter-arguments worth crediting: crowding IN, where public investment raises expected demand and the productivity of private capital, so private investment rises; and the fact that a central bank targeting inflation, not the interest rate, can accommodate government borrowing.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram, both financial and resource crowding out analysed, and the state-dependence recognised; L2 (4-6) one mechanism with a diagram; L1 (1-3) assertion that government borrowing raises interest rates.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 13-14) states: "Supporters of tax reduction argue that cutting rates can raise the total revenue collected."\n\nWith the help of a diagram, analyse the Laffer curve and what it implies for tax policy.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: Laffer curve with the tax rate (0-100%) on the horizontal axis and total tax revenue on the vertical, an inverted-U shape rising from zero at a 0% rate to a maximum at some rate t*, then falling back to zero at 100%.\n• The two endpoints and why: at a 0% rate no revenue is raised because there is no tax. At a 100% rate no revenue is raised because there is no incentive to undertake taxed activity at all. Between them revenue must rise and then fall, so a revenue-maximising rate exists.\n• The mechanisms on the downward-sloping section: the substitution effect, as higher marginal rates reduce the return to an extra hour of work and encourage leisure; reduced incentives to take risk or start a business; emigration of highly mobile high earners; and increased avoidance, evasion and shifting of income into untaxed forms.\n• Policy implication analysed: if the current rate is to the RIGHT of t*, a cut raises revenue; if it is to the LEFT, a cut reduces revenue. So the Laffer curve does not by itself justify tax cuts - it makes the argument entirely empirical.\n• Critical analysis: t* is unknown and differs by tax, by group and over time. Most estimates put the revenue-maximising top income tax rate well above current UK rates, and most economies are believed to be on the upward-sloping section. The income effect also cuts the other way, since a tax rise can make people work MORE to maintain a target income.\n• Further limitations: revenue is not the only objective - equity and public service provision matter too - and short-run responses differ from long-run ones, since the immediate revenue effect of a cut may be a loss that recovers only slowly, if at all.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram plus the incentive mechanisms AND the "which side of the peak" point made explicitly; L2 (4-6) diagram with a partial explanation; L1 (1-3) "lower taxes raise revenue" asserted.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 15-16) states: "The deficit widened sharply during the downturn, but the Office for Budget Responsibility judged that most of it would remain when the economy recovered."\n\nAnalyse the distinction between a structural and a cyclical budget deficit and why it matters for policy.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Definitions: the cyclical deficit is the part attributable to the position of the economy in the trade cycle, arising from automatic stabilisers. The structural deficit is the part that would remain if the economy were operating at potential output, so it reflects a permanent gap between the government's spending commitments and its tax base.\n• How the cyclical part arises: in a downturn, incomes, profits and spending fall, so receipts from income tax, National Insurance, corporation tax and VAT fall - by more than proportionately where the system is progressive - while spending on unemployment-related and means-tested benefits rises automatically.\n• Why the distinction matters analytically: a cyclical deficit self-corrects as the economy recovers, and attempting to close it is actively harmful because it removes the automatic stabiliser and deepens the downturn. A structural deficit does not self-correct, so closing it requires discretionary tax rises or spending cuts.\n• Why it matters for fiscal rules: the UK's stability rule requires the CURRENT budget - receipts less day-to-day spending - to be in surplus by 2029-30, and once that year becomes the third year of the rolling forecast the rule becomes a rolling third-year test, with balance permitting a deficit of up to 0.5% of GDP. Framing the rule at a forecast horizon is an attempt to target the structural position rather than the cyclical one.\n• Analysis of the measurement problem: the split depends entirely on an estimate of potential output, which is unobservable and heavily revised. Pre-2008 deficits were judged cyclical at the time and structural in retrospect, because potential output had been overestimated - so the distinction is theoretically essential and empirically fragile.\n• Consequence analysed: policy set on a mistaken split is set wrongly in both directions. Treating a structural deficit as cyclical delays necessary consolidation; treating a cyclical deficit as structural produces pro-cyclical austerity that deepens a recession.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) both concepts defined accurately, the policy implication of each developed, and the measurement problem analysed; L2 (4-6) both defined with a partial policy implication; L1 (1-3) definitions only.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 17-18) states: "Estimates of the fiscal multiplier vary widely, and the Treasury's own assumptions differ by type of spending."\n\nAnalyse why the size of the fiscal multiplier is important for the design of fiscal policy.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• What the multiplier is: the ratio of the eventual change in national income to the initial change in the fiscal injection, k = 1/(MPS + MPT + MPM). A multiplier of 1.4 on £14bn of capital spending delivers £19.6bn of national income.\n• Why the size determines the cost of a stimulus: if k exceeds one, the injection raises income by more than it costs, and the induced rise in receipts and fall in benefit spending means the stimulus partly finances itself - so the debt-to-GDP ratio may fall even as borrowing rises, because the denominator grows.\n• Why the size determines the cost of a CONSOLIDATION: the same arithmetic in reverse. A large multiplier means spending cuts reduce national income by more than they save, so the deficit falls by less than intended and the debt ratio can rise - consolidation that is partly self-defeating.\n• Composition: multipliers differ by instrument. Capital spending and transfers to low-income households with a high MPC have larger multipliers than tax cuts for high earners, much of which are saved. A government choosing between instruments of equal cost should choose on the multiplier.\n• State dependence: multipliers are larger when there is a negative output gap and when the central bank cannot or will not offset the stimulus with higher rates. With Bank Rate at 3.75% in September 2026 and inflation at 3.1%, a monetary offset is likely, so the effective multiplier is smaller than in a zero-lower-bound episode.\n• Analysis of the uncertainty: estimates in the literature range from well below one to well above it, which means the expected effect of a fiscal package is genuinely uncertain. That argues for gradualism and for choosing instruments whose multipliers are least disputed.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) the concept explained plus at least three developed reasons why its size matters, including state dependence; L2 (4-6) the formula and one or two reasons; L1 (1-3) the formula alone.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 19-20) states: "A reduction in the marginal rate of income tax is intended to strengthen the incentive to work."\n\nWith the help of a diagram, analyse why a cut in income tax may not increase the supply of labour.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: individual labour supply with the (post-tax) wage rate on the vertical axis and hours supplied on the horizontal, showing a backward-bending supply curve; or an income-leisure choice diagram with budget constraints before and after the tax cut. The key is to show hours that could rise OR fall.\n• The substitution effect: a lower marginal rate raises the post-tax return to an extra hour of work, so the opportunity cost of leisure rises and the worker substitutes work for leisure. Hours rise. This is the intended effect.\n• The income effect: the same tax cut raises income for any given number of hours. If leisure is a normal good, the worker can now afford more of it and reaches their target income with fewer hours. Hours FALL.\n• Net effect: the two pull in opposite directions, so the outcome is theoretically indeterminate and empirically depends on the worker. Evidence generally finds the substitution effect dominating at low incomes and for second earners, and the income effect strengthening at high incomes - which is what produces the backward bend.\n• Institutional constraints analysed: most employees cannot choose their hours. Contracts are fixed, so any response is confined to overtime, second jobs or participation decisions, not marginal hours.\n• Other reasons the policy may fail: the marginal rate faced by low earners depends on the withdrawal of benefits as well as on tax, so a tax cut may barely change the effective marginal rate; and if the cut is financed by borrowing at gilt yields near 5.9%, or by cuts to training and childcare, the effect on effective labour supply can be negative.\n• Link to the Laffer curve: if labour supply does not respond, the economy is on the upward-sloping section, so the tax cut simply reduces revenue.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram plus both effects developed and the institutional constraints analysed; L2 (4-6) both effects named with partial development; L1 (1-3) the substitution effect only.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 21-22) states: "The yield on the 30-year gilt reached about 5.9% in early September 2026, and public sector net debt stood at 93.8% of GDP at the end of August."\n\nAnalyse how high government borrowing costs constrain a government's fiscal policy choices.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The direct mechanism: the gilt yield is the interest rate the government pays on new issuance and on refinancing maturing debt. With net debt at £2 985.5bn at the end of August 2026, even a small rise in the average rate translates into billions of pounds of additional debt interest.\n• Debt interest is day-to-day spending: it therefore counts against the stability rule, which requires the current budget to be in surplus by 2029-30. Headroom against that rule was about £23.6bn on the March 2026 OBR forecast - small enough that a modest sustained rise in yields can absorb it.\n• The squeeze on discretionary spending: debt interest is non-discretionary, so within a fixed envelope every extra pound of it must be met by higher taxation, more borrowing or cuts elsewhere. Capital spending is typically cut first, which damages LRAS and future growth - the constraint compounds itself.\n• Index-linked debt: a significant share of UK gilts is index-linked, so debt interest rises with inflation as well as with yields. CPI inflation of 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026 therefore raises servicing costs directly.\n• The credibility loop: markets price fiscal risk. A government that responds to high yields by borrowing more may face higher yields still, so the constraint is self-tightening. This is why the rules exist and why the Budget, due on 28 October 2026, is accompanied by an independent OBR forecast.\n• Consequence for stabilisation policy: with little fiscal space, the burden of responding to a downturn falls on monetary policy - Bank Rate at 3.75% in September 2026 - which is blunt and slow. An economy with high debt has fewer instruments.\n• Qualification worth crediting: the constraint is about the RATIO, not the level. If nominal GDP grows faster than debt, the ratio falls, as it did by 1.3 percentage points in the year to August 2026 despite debt rising by £78.5bn - so growth-enhancing borrowing may loosen the constraint rather than tightening it.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) at least three mechanisms developed with dated evidence, including the qualification; L2 (4-6) two mechanisms with limited development; L1 (1-3) "high debt means less room to borrow" asserted.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 23-24) states: "Successive governments transferred state-owned industries to the private sector and removed restrictions on entry into regulated markets."\n\nAnalyse how privatisation and deregulation are intended to improve economic efficiency.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The profit motive and productive efficiency: private owners bear the cost of waste and keep the gains from cost reduction, so they have an incentive to minimise average cost. State-owned firms facing soft budget constraints do not, because losses are met from taxation.\n• Shareholder monitoring and the market for corporate control: underperforming private managers face removal, takeover or the collapse of the share price - disciplines that have no counterpart in a state monopoly, where the sanction is political rather than commercial.\n• Deregulation and allocative efficiency: removing legal barriers to entry exposes incumbents to competition, so price is driven towards marginal cost, choice widens and X-inefficiency is squeezed out. The threat of entry alone can discipline an incumbent in a contestable market.\n• Dynamic efficiency: competition rewards innovation, and private firms can raise capital on commercial terms without competing against health and education for a place in the public spending envelope.\n• Fiscal effects: sale proceeds and subsequent corporation tax receipts improve the public finances, and the state no longer funds losses - relevant with public sector net debt at 93.8% of GDP at the end of August 2026.\n• Analysis of why the gains may not materialise: where the industry is a natural monopoly, privatisation converts a public monopoly into a private one with a stronger incentive to exploit market power, which is why regulators were created alongside the sales. Deregulation of financial markets is widely argued to have contributed to the 2008 crisis, since the externalities were not internalised.\n• Further limitations: privatised firms may under-invest in long-lived assets whose returns fall beyond the investors' horizon, may cut unprofitable but socially valuable services, and may reduce employment and worsen the distribution of income. The efficiency gain also depends on whether competition actually follows the sale.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) at least three mechanisms developed AND the natural monopoly or externality limitation analysed; L2 (4-6) mechanisms asserted with limited development; L1 (1-3) definitions of privatisation and deregulation.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 25-26) states: "The government announced an expansion of apprenticeships and of adult retraining in sectors reporting persistent skill shortages."\n\nWith the help of a diagram, analyse how investment in education and training can reduce the natural rate of unemployment and raise potential output.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: AD/AS with LRAS shifting right from LRAS1 to LRAS2, output rising and the price level falling with AD unchanged. An equally valid alternative is a long-run Phillips curve shifting left, showing a lower natural rate of unemployment at any rate of inflation.\n• Mechanism for structural unemployment: the natural rate consists of frictional and structural unemployment. Retraining directly attacks the structural component by equipping workers displaced from declining sectors with the skills the expanding sectors demand, so the occupational mismatch narrows.\n• Mechanism for frictional unemployment: better information and shorter retraining periods reduce the time between jobs, so the flow of workers through unemployment is faster even if the number of transitions is unchanged.\n• Mechanism for potential output: human capital raises output per worker. Higher productivity lowers unit labour costs at any given wage, so it shifts SRAS right as well, which is why the policy improves output and inflation together rather than trading them off.\n• Hysteresis: active labour market policy shortens unemployment spells, which prevents cyclical unemployment hardening into structural unemployment through skill decay and employer screening.\n• Dated application: UK economic inactivity stood at 20.9% in May to July 2026 and unemployment at 4.9%, with payrolled employment down 145 000 on the year - a labour market in which the structural and participation margins matter.\n• Analysis of limits: the lags are very long, training may not match what employers actually need, there is a significant opportunity cost when public spending is constrained by debt at 93.8% of GDP, and firms may free-ride on publicly funded training or poach trained workers, which is itself the externality justifying public provision.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram plus the structural-unemployment and productivity chains developed, with limits; L2 (4-6) diagram plus one chain; L1 (1-3) "training makes workers better" asserted.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — real data, ONS and OBR\n  Public sector net debt, end-August 2026:                    £2 985.5bn (93.8% of GDP)\n  Change in the debt-to-GDP ratio on a year earlier:          -1.3 percentage points\n  Change in the level of debt on a year earlier:              +£78.5bn\n  Headroom against the stability rule, March 2026 forecast:   about £23.6bn in 2029-30\n  Headroom against the investment rule, March 2026 forecast:  about £27.1bn\n  PSNFL projected for 2028-29:                                82.2% of GDP\n  UK 30-year gilt yield, early September 2026:                about 5.9%\n  UK real GDP growth, year to Q2 2026:                        +1.2%\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data suggest that the UK's fiscal position was sustainable in 2026?`, marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Support for sustainability: the debt-to-GDP ratio FELL by 1.3 percentage points to 93.8%, which is the measure that matters, since GDP is the tax base from which debt is serviced. Nominal GDP therefore grew faster than the 2.70% increase in the debt stock.\n• Support: the government was projected to meet both fiscal rules, with headroom of about £23.6bn against the stability rule and £27.1bn against the investment rule on the March 2026 forecast, and PSNFL falling as a share of GDP.\n• Support: the UK retains the capacity to borrow at long maturities in its own currency, and real GDP grew by 1.2% over the year to Q2 2026, so the denominator of the ratio is still growing.\n• Against: debt rose in cash terms by £78.5bn, so the government is still borrowing heavily, and a ratio of 93.8% is at a level the ONS notes was last seen in the early 1960s.\n• Against: the headroom is very small - £23.6bn is well under 1% of GDP, and the OBR's forecasts have been substantially revised in the past. Headroom of that size is within the margin of forecast error.\n• Against: 30-year gilt yields of about 5.9%, the highest since the late 1990s, raise the cost of new and refinanced borrowing, feed directly into debt interest, which is day-to-day spending, and therefore erode the stability-rule headroom.\n• Limits of the data: sustainability depends on the relationship between the interest rate on debt and the nominal growth rate of the economy, and the table gives neither the average effective interest rate on the whole stock nor nominal GDP growth. There is no information on the ageing-related spending pressures that dominate long-run projections, nor on contingent liabilities.\n• Judgement: on the narrow test of the rules and the direction of the ratio, the data are consistent with sustainability; on the tests of headroom, borrowing costs and the level of the ratio, the position is fragile. The most defensible conclusion is that it was sustainable but with very little margin for error.\n\nLevels: L3 (8-10) quantitative use of the data on both sides, recognition of what the data cannot show, supported judgement; L2 (4-7) partial use with a thin judgement; L1 (1-3) assertion. A conclusion reached without reference to the data cannot exceed L1.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\n              Year 1   Year 3   Year 5   Year 7\n  Public spending on skills and apprenticeships (£bn):  4.2    5.6     6.8      7.1\n  Business R&D spending (% of GDP):                     1.5    1.6     1.8      1.9\n  Output per hour worked, index (Year 1 = 100):        100.0  100.9   102.4    104.1\n  Real business investment, index (Year 1 = 100):      100.0  103.0   107.5    110.2\n  Unemployment rate (%):                                 5.4    5.1     4.8      4.7\n  Economic inactivity rate (%):                         21.8   21.5    21.1     20.9\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data suggest that supply-side policy succeeded in raising this economy's productive potential?`, marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Support: every indicator moves in the intended direction. Output per hour rose 4.1%, business investment 10.2%, unemployment fell by 0.7 percentage points and inactivity by 0.9 points - the combination of higher productivity and higher participation is precisely a rightward shift of LRAS.\n• Support: the gains accelerate over time, with output per hour rising 0.9% in the first two years and 1.7% between Year 5 and Year 7, which is the pattern supply-side theory predicts given the lags in skills formation and capital installation.\n• Against - the scale is modest: 4.1% over seven years is about 0.6% a year, while skills spending rose 69.0%. Whether that represents value for money cannot be judged without knowing the opportunity cost of the £2.9bn of additional annual spending.\n• Against - attribution: seven years is long enough for many other things to have changed. Falling unemployment is equally consistent with a cyclical recovery in aggregate demand, which would raise measured productivity through better capacity utilisation rather than through any change in potential output.\n• Against - reverse causation: rising business investment could be the cause of the productivity gain rather than a result of policy, and investment responds to expected demand through the accelerator.\n• Against - no counterfactual: there is no comparison economy and no estimate of what would have happened without the policy. The data show a correlation across six series, not a causal effect.\n• What is missing: the output gap, which would distinguish actual from potential growth; data on the quality and take-up of the training; and any measure of whether the skills matched the vacancies.\n• Judgement: the data are consistent with the policy having worked, and the acceleration over time is suggestive, but they cannot separate a supply-side improvement from a cyclical recovery. A supported conclusion states this limitation rather than asserting success.\n\nLevels: L3 (8-10) quantitative use on both sides, explicit treatment of attribution and the actual/potential distinction, supported judgement; L2 (4-7) partial use with a thin judgement; L1 (1-3) assertion. A conclusion reached without reference to the data cannot exceed L1.` },

      { q: `Explain the main causes of a rise in a country's national debt.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Framing: the national debt is a STOCK, the accumulation of past annual deficits (a flow) less any repayments. It rises whenever the government spends more than it receives, so the causes of a rising debt are the causes of persistent deficits.\n• Cyclical causes: in a downturn, automatic stabilisers cut receipts from income tax, National Insurance, corporation tax and VAT while raising benefit spending. This is a cause of a rising debt that is self-correcting when the economy recovers.\n• Discretionary responses to shocks: the financial crisis of 2008-09 and the pandemic of 2020 both produced very large one-off increases in borrowing to support incomes and the financial system.\n• Structural causes - demographics: an ageing population raises spending on pensions, health and social care while the share of the population paying income tax falls. This is the dominant long-run driver in most advanced economies and does not reverse.\n• Structural causes - a mismatch between commitments and the tax base: if governments repeatedly promise services that the tax system does not fund, a structural deficit persists even at full employment. In the practice data, a deficit of 4.6% of GDP persisted with a POSITIVE output gap.\n• Debt interest and compounding: once the stock is large, interest on it is itself a major spending item - in the practice data, 9.78% of total receipts. With 30-year gilt yields around 5.9% in early September 2026, refinancing maturing debt at higher rates raises the deficit automatically.\n• Weak growth: slow real growth and low inflation mean nominal GDP grows slowly, so the ratio rises even at a constant deficit. Conversely the UK ratio FELL by 1.3 percentage points in the year to August 2026 despite the level of debt rising by £78.5bn.\n• Public investment: borrowing to invest raises the debt but also raises the asset side of the public balance sheet and, if well chosen, future output - which is why the UK's investment rule is framed around net financial liabilities rather than gross debt.\n• Other causes worth crediting: bank bailouts and other contingent liabilities crystallising; tax avoidance and evasion narrowing the base; and war or emergency spending.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several causes developed, with the stock/flow and cyclical/structural distinctions sustained; L2 (6-10) causes explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Explain the main supply-side policies a government could use to raise productivity.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Framing: productivity is output per unit of input, and raising it shifts LRAS right, so output rises and the price level falls - the reason supply-side policy escapes the trade-offs demand management faces. Policies divide into market-based, which work by improving incentives and competition, and interventionist, which work by providing what the market under-supplies.\n• Education and training (interventionist): raising human capital raises output per worker. Apprenticeships, adult retraining and improvements in basic numeracy address both productivity and the structural mismatch behind an inactivity rate of 20.9% in May to July 2026. Long lags are the main drawback.\n• Infrastructure investment (interventionist): transport, energy and digital networks raise the productivity of private capital. Effects are large but arrive a decade after the spending, and public investment is the first item cut when debt interest squeezes budgets.\n• Support for research and development (interventionist): innovation generates positive externalities, since firms cannot appropriate the full social return, so the free market under-invests. R&D tax credits and public research funding correct this.\n• Lower marginal tax rates (market-based): raising the post-tax return to work and enterprise strengthens the substitution effect towards work and risk-taking - though the income effect works the other way, so the net effect is uncertain.\n• Labour market flexibility (market-based): reforming employment protection, trade union law and benefit withdrawal rates speeds the reallocation of labour and reduces the natural rate of unemployment. The distributional cost is that flexibility is experienced as insecurity.\n• Competition policy and deregulation (market-based): removing barriers to entry forces incumbents to reduce X-inefficiency and innovate. Planning reform is the current UK example, since restrictions on land use raise costs across the whole economy.\n• Privatisation (market-based): the profit motive and the market for corporate control impose disciplines absent under state ownership - subject to regulation where the industry is a natural monopoly.\n• Openness to trade and investment: specialisation according to comparative advantage and exposure to foreign competition and technology both raise productivity. The UK joined the CPTPP on 15 December 2024, in force with all eleven other members by 1 September 2026.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several policies developed with the mechanism in each, the market-based/interventionist distinction sustained, and lags or costs acknowledged; L2 (6-10) policies described with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Explain the main economic effects of an increase in the rate of income tax.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Effect on aggregate demand: higher income tax reduces disposable income, so consumption falls at every price level and AD shifts left. The multiplier magnifies the fall, and a higher marginal propensity to tax also reduces the multiplier itself, so the economy becomes less responsive to any subsequent shock.\n• Effect on the public finances: receipts rise, reducing the deficit and slowing the growth of debt - relevant with public sector net debt at 93.8% of GDP at the end of August 2026 and headroom against the stability rule of about £23.6bn on the March 2026 OBR forecast. But the yield is less than the static calculation implies, because the tax base itself shrinks with activity.\n• Effect on incentives - the substitution effect: a higher marginal rate lowers the post-tax return to an extra hour of work, raising the relative attractiveness of leisure, so hours supplied may fall.\n• Effect on incentives - the income effect: the same rise reduces income at any given number of hours, so a worker with a target income may work MORE. The two effects oppose one another, so the net effect on labour supply is theoretically indeterminate and empirically modest for most employees, who cannot vary their hours.\n• Effect on the supply side more broadly: sustained high marginal rates may reduce entrepreneurship and risk-taking, encourage avoidance and evasion, and prompt emigration among the most mobile high earners. The Laffer curve formalises this, though the question of whether an economy is to the left or right of the revenue-maximising rate is empirical, and most estimates place advanced economies to the left.\n• Effect on inflation: lower AD reduces demand-pull pressure, so the price level rises more slowly - which with CPI inflation of 3.1% in the year to August 2026 would be a policy benefit, and would reduce the burden on Bank Rate, held at 3.75% in September 2026.\n• Effect on equity: income tax is progressive - in the practice data, 8.57% of income for a household on £22 000 and 32.65% for one on £140 000 - so a rise in rates narrows the post-tax distribution of income, particularly if concentrated at the top.\n• Effect on the current account: weaker domestic demand reduces imports, so the current account deficit, 1.9% of GDP in Q2 2026, would tend to narrow.\n• The size of all these effects depends on WHICH rate rises: a rise in the basic rate has large AD and revenue effects and small incentive effects; a rise in the additional rate has the reverse.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several effects developed across demand, supply, public finances and equity, with the substitution/income distinction; L2 (6-10) effects explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Explain the main reasons why governments adopt fiscal rules.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• To solve a time-inconsistency problem: governments face an electoral incentive to cut taxes and raise spending before elections and to defer the correction. A rule pre-commits the government, removing the incentive in the same way that central bank independence removes it for monetary policy.\n• To maintain credibility with lenders: a government that must refinance a large stock of debt depends on investors' willingness to hold it. A credible rule lowers the risk premium in gilt yields, which matters when net debt stood at £2 985.5bn, 93.8% of GDP, at the end of August 2026 and 30-year yields at about 5.9% in early September 2026.\n• To restrain the growth of debt interest: without a constraint, deficits compound into a stock whose servicing pre-empts discretionary spending - 9.78% of total receipts in the practice data.\n• To protect capital spending: the UK's two rules are designed to do exactly this. The stability rule requires the CURRENT budget - receipts less day-to-day spending - to be in surplus by 2029-30, and then on a rolling third-year basis, with balance permitting a deficit of up to 0.5% of GDP. Because it applies only to day-to-day spending, the government may still borrow to invest, which protects investment from being the first casualty of consolidation.\n• To measure the public balance sheet properly: the investment rule requires public sector net financial liabilities to be falling as a share of GDP by 2029-30 and then by the third year of the rolling forecast. PSNFL includes funded public sector pension schemes and illiquid assets such as student loans, so it nets financial assets against liabilities - a broader measure than public sector net debt.\n• To ensure intergenerational fairness: borrowing for day-to-day consumption shifts the cost onto future taxpayers who receive no asset in return; borrowing for investment leaves them the asset as well as the debt.\n• To preserve room for stabilisation: a government that enters a downturn with a sound structural position can let automatic stabilisers operate and add discretionary support. One that does not, cannot.\n• Independent verification: rules require an independent assessor to be credible. The Office for Budget Responsibility, created in 2010, produces the official forecasts; under the Charter for Budget Responsibility passed in February 2026 it assesses performance against the rules once a year at the Budget - the next due on 28 October 2026 - while still publishing a second five-year forecast each spring.\n• Limitations worth crediting: rules framed on forecasts several years ahead can be met on paper by back-loaded measures; small headroom, about £23.6bn on the March 2026 forecast, is within forecast error; and a binding rule can force pro-cyclical tightening in a downturn.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several reasons developed, with accurate description of the two UK rules and at least one limitation; L2 (6-10) reasons explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Explain the main causes of weak productivity growth in the UK economy since the financial crisis.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Framing: productivity is output per hour worked, and its growth is the main determinant of the trend rate of growth and of real wages. UK growth of 1.2% in the year to Q2 2026 is modest by pre-2008 standards, and the proximate arithmetic is weak productivity.\n• Low business investment: UK business investment has been persistently low as a share of GDP, so capital per worker has grown slowly. Causes include policy uncertainty, short-termism in financial markets, and a high cost of capital - 10-year gilt yields were around 5.2% and 30-year yields around 5.9% in early September 2026, a materially higher hurdle rate than through the 2010s.\n• Weak public investment: infrastructure spending has been squeezed by rising debt interest within a constrained envelope, and poor transport and digital networks lower the productivity of private capital. Planning constraints compound this by raising the cost of building anything.\n• A long tail of low-productivity firms: the gap between the UK's most and least productive firms is wide, and the diffusion of best practice from frontier firms to the rest has been slow - a management and skills problem as much as a technology one.\n• Skills and human capital: weaknesses in technical and vocational education, and in basic numeracy, limit the workforce's capacity to use new technology. An economic inactivity rate of 20.9% in May to July 2026 also suggests unused labour capacity.\n• Composition of output and employment: employment growth has been concentrated in relatively low-productivity service sectors, and cheap labour may have substituted for capital investment at the margin.\n• Financial sector effects: bank balance sheet repair after 2008 restricted lending to small firms, and a large share of lending is secured against existing property rather than financing new productive capital.\n• Trade and openness: reduced trade openness raises costs and reduces the gains from specialisation and from exposure to foreign competition and technology. Rising global trade barriers since 2025 work in the same direction.\n• Measurement: part of the slowdown may be statistical, since intangible investment and free digital services are imperfectly captured in GDP. This is a genuine caveat, not an explanation of the whole gap, and the slowdown is common to most advanced economies, which suggests a global component.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several causes developed with mechanisms, including the measurement caveat and the international comparison; L2 (6-10) causes explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Explain the main ways in which a government could reduce a budget deficit.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Framing: the deficit is the excess of spending over receipts, so it can be reduced by raising receipts, cutting spending, or raising the growth of the tax base. Each has different effects on AD, on equity and on the supply side.\n• Raising direct taxes: income tax, National Insurance and corporation tax raise substantial revenue and are progressive in the case of income tax, but reduce disposable income and therefore AD, and may weaken work and investment incentives.\n• Raising indirect taxes: VAT and duties are harder to avoid and collected at lower administrative cost, but they are regressive - in the practice data VAT took 11.00% of the income of a household on £22 000 and 5.00% of one on £140 000 - and they raise the price level directly, which is unwelcome with CPI inflation at 3.1% in the year to August 2026.\n• Fiscal drag: freezing tax thresholds while nominal incomes rise raises the average tax rate without legislating a rate increase. It raises large sums quietly, which is why it is used, but it is less transparent than an explicit rise.\n• Cutting current spending: reductions in departmental budgets, benefits or public sector pay reduce spending immediately, but fall on those with the highest marginal propensity to consume, so the multiplier effect on output is large, and they raise inequality.\n• Cutting capital spending: politically the easiest cut because no service is withdrawn today, but it damages LRAS and future growth and therefore the future tax base - the most self-defeating option in the long run, and the reason the UK's stability rule applies only to day-to-day spending.\n• Raising the rate of economic growth: a larger tax base raises receipts at unchanged rates and reduces benefit spending. This is the least painful route and the slowest, and it is why the debt-to-GDP ratio fell by 1.3 percentage points in the year to August 2026 despite the level of debt rising by £78.5bn.\n• Asset sales and one-off measures: these improve the headline figures in a single year but do not change the structural position, and they forgo the future income the asset would have produced.\n• Analysis of the essential distinction: only the STRUCTURAL deficit requires discretionary action. Trying to close a cyclical deficit removes the automatic stabilisers and deepens a downturn, reducing the tax base and so partly undoing the consolidation - the larger the fiscal multiplier, the more self-defeating it is.\n• Timing: consolidating in a recovery, when the multiplier is smaller and the monetary authority can offset the demand withdrawal, costs far less output than consolidating in a downturn.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several routes developed with their AD, equity and supply-side consequences, plus the structural/cyclical distinction; L2 (6-10) routes listed with some explanation; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 27-28) states: "Public sector net debt stood at 93.8% of GDP at the end of August 2026, a level last seen in the early 1960s."\n\nUsing the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that a national debt of this size is a serious problem for the UK economy.`, marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - debt interest crowds out other spending: interest is non-discretionary, so within a fixed envelope it displaces public services and investment. In the practice data it rose from 4.26% to 9.78% of receipts while public investment fell.\n• For - vulnerability to interest rates: with 30-year gilt yields at about 5.9% in early September 2026, the highest since the late 1990s, refinancing maturing debt raises costs automatically. A significant share of UK gilts is index-linked, so inflation of 3.1% in the year to August 2026 raises servicing costs too.\n• For - lost fiscal space: headroom of about £23.6bn against the stability rule on the March 2026 OBR forecast is under 1% of GDP and well within forecast error. A government with no fiscal space cannot respond to the next shock, so the burden falls on monetary policy alone.\n• For - intergenerational equity: borrowing for day-to-day spending transfers the cost to future taxpayers who receive no asset in return.\n• Against - the ratio is falling: it declined by 1.3 percentage points in the year to August 2026 even though the level of debt rose by £78.5bn, because nominal GDP grew faster than the debt stock. Sustainability depends on the relationship between the interest rate and the nominal growth rate, not on the level.\n• Against - historical and international comparison: the UK carried far higher ratios after both world wars and reduced them through growth and inflation rather than repayment. Several comparable economies carry higher ratios without crisis.\n• Against - the composition matters: the UK borrows in its own currency at long average maturity, so it faces no currency mismatch and limited refinancing risk in any single year, and the Bank of England holds a substantial share.\n• Against - what the borrowing bought: debt incurred to finance investment raises the asset side of the balance sheet and future output. This is precisely why the investment rule targets public sector net financial liabilities, projected at 82.2% of GDP in 2028-29, rather than gross debt.\n• Against - the alternative has costs: consolidating rapidly reduces AD, and with a multiplier above one it reduces national income by more than it saves, raising the ratio through the denominator.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer makes the r-versus-g relationship the organising criterion, concluding that the level is not in itself the problem but that the combination of high yields, thin headroom and a large share of borrowing financing current rather than capital spending makes the position fragile - a serious constraint rather than a crisis.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, analysis and evaluation integrated, conclusion follows from the argument; L4 (16-20) well-developed and mostly balanced; L3 (11-15) some evaluation, uneven; L2 (6-10) descriptive, one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Assess the view that supply-side policies are more effective than demand-side policies in raising an economy's rate of economic growth.`, marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - only supply-side policy raises POTENTIAL output: LRAS is vertical in the classical model, so demand management changes the price level in the long run and nothing else. Sustainable growth requires more or better factors of production.\n• For - it avoids the trade-offs: a rightward shift in LRAS raises output AND lowers inflation AND reduces unemployment simultaneously, whereas demand expansion trades them against each other. This matters with UK inflation at 3.1% in the year to August 2026, above the 2% target.\n• For - the UK's binding constraint is the supply side: unemployment of 4.9% and growth of 1.2% in the year to Q2 2026 do not describe a demand-deficient economy. The problem is productivity, which demand cannot fix.\n• For - fiscal space: with net debt at 93.8% of GDP at the end of August 2026 and headroom of about £23.6bn, there is little room for demand stimulus, while some supply-side reform - planning, competition, regulation - costs nothing to legislate.\n• Against - lags and uncertainty: in the practice data, seven years of rising skills and R&D spending raised output per hour by 4.1% in total. Education and infrastructure take a decade or more, and the LRAS shift may not materialise if the projects are badly chosen.\n• Against - the supply side needs demand: investment is the main route to a larger capital stock, and through the accelerator it depends on expected demand. Firms will not build capacity for output they do not expect to sell, so suppressing demand suppresses the investment that shifts LRAS.\n• Against - hysteresis: a demand-deficient recession left unaddressed permanently raises the natural rate as skills and attachment decay, so demand management protects the supply side. The two are complements, not substitutes.\n• Against - distributional costs: market-based supply-side policies concentrate their costs on identifiable groups - welfare reform, deregulation, labour market flexibility - which limits how far they can be pursued and may reduce the political capacity to sustain them.\n• Against - some demand-side policy IS supply-side policy: public investment raises AD now and LRAS later, so the categories overlap. The relevant choice is between instruments, not between categories.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer distinguishes the time horizons - demand management stabilises around the trend, supply-side policy raises the trend - and concludes with reference to the UK's 2026 conditions, where near-capacity operation, above-target inflation and thin fiscal space all point towards supply-side priority, while noting that a future recession would reverse the ranking.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 29-30) states: "The stability rule requires the current budget to be in surplus by 2029-30, while the investment rule requires public sector net financial liabilities to be falling as a share of GDP."\n\nUsing the extracts and your knowledge of economics, discuss the view that the UK should relax its fiscal rules in order to borrow more for public investment.`, marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For relaxation - the returns may exceed the cost: if a project's social rate of return exceeds the government's cost of borrowing, borrowing to fund it raises future output and the future tax base, so the debt-to-GDP ratio can fall as a result of the borrowing rather than despite it.\n• For relaxation - the UK's problem is investment: business investment has been persistently weak, infrastructure quality constrains productivity, and public investment is the item most often cut. In the practice data, public investment FELL from £96bn to £94bn while debt interest rose from £42bn to £112bn.\n• For relaxation - the rules already recognise the argument: the stability rule applies only to DAY-TO-DAY spending, so the government may already borrow to invest, and the investment rule uses PSNFL, which nets financial assets against liabilities. The existing framework is closer to the case for relaxation than it appears.\n• For relaxation - thin headroom forces short-termism: with about £23.6bn against the stability rule on the March 2026 forecast, governments manage to the forecast rather than to the economy, back-loading measures and cancelling capital projects to hit a number three years out.\n• Against - the cost of borrowing is high: 30-year gilt yields of about 5.9% in early September 2026, the highest since the late 1990s, raise the hurdle rate a project must clear. Borrowing at 5.9% for a project returning less destroys value.\n• Against - credibility and the market reaction: the rules exist to reassure lenders. Relaxing them when yields are already elevated risks a rise in the risk premium that raises the cost of ALL borrowing, so the government could end up able to invest less, not more.\n• Against - the definition problem: if "investment" is loosely defined, a relaxation becomes an incentive to reclassify current spending as capital. The discipline of the rule depends on the boundary being credible.\n• Against - public investment is not automatically productive: the return depends on project selection, and appraisal is subject to political pressure. Poorly chosen capital spending adds debt without adding output.\n• Against - crowding out: with unemployment at 4.9% and the economy near capacity, additional public demand for construction labour and materials may displace private projects rather than adding to total investment.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer argues that the case turns on whether the expected social return exceeds the cost of borrowing and on the credibility of the definition of investment, and concludes on that basis - most defensibly that the framework's treatment of investment is already broadly right and that the real problem is the thinness of the headroom and the quality of project selection rather than the rules themselves.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Evaluate the view that fiscal consolidation during a downturn is self-defeating.`, marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - the multiplier arithmetic: if the fiscal multiplier exceeds one, a £10bn spending cut reduces national income by more than £10bn. Receipts fall and benefit spending rises as a result, so the deficit falls by less than the cut, and the debt-to-GDP ratio can RISE because the denominator shrinks faster than the numerator.\n• For - multipliers are largest in a downturn: with a negative output gap, idle resources and a central bank at or near the lower bound unable to offset the contraction, estimates of the multiplier are well above one. Consolidating when the multiplier is at its maximum is the worst possible timing.\n• For - hysteresis: consolidation that prolongs a downturn converts cyclical into structural unemployment through skill decay and employer screening, and causes firms to scrap capital. Potential output falls, so the future tax base is permanently smaller.\n• For - capital spending is cut first: it is politically the easiest reduction and economically the most damaging, since it directly reduces LRAS - exactly the pattern in the practice data, where public investment fell while debt interest rose.\n• For - removing automatic stabilisers: deliberate tightening in a downturn works against the stabilisers that would otherwise damp the cycle, so it is pro-cyclical by construction.\n• Against - the distinction between structural and cyclical: only the CYCLICAL deficit self-corrects. A structural deficit persists at full employment - in the practice data, 4.7% of GDP with a positive output gap - and delaying its correction simply accumulates debt at compound interest.\n• Against - credibility and the market constraint: a government that cannot convince lenders it will stabilise its debt faces higher yields on all its borrowing. With 30-year yields near 5.9% in early September 2026 and net debt at 93.8% of GDP, the option of postponing indefinitely may not exist.\n• Against - composition matters more than timing: consolidation through capital cuts is self-defeating; consolidation through closing tax reliefs or reducing transfers to high-saving households has a small multiplier and need not be. The claim is about the instrument, not about consolidation as such.\n• Against - the monetary offset: if the central bank has room to cut rates, it can offset the demand withdrawal, and the effective multiplier falls sharply. Bank Rate at 3.75% in September 2026 leaves considerable room.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer concludes conditionally and states the conditions - consolidation is self-defeating when the multiplier is large, monetary policy cannot offset it, and the cuts fall on investment; it is necessary when the deficit is structural and credibility is at risk - and applies that test rather than answering in the abstract.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Assess the view that reducing taxation is the most effective way of raising an economy's long-run rate of growth.`, marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - incentives to work: lower marginal rates raise the post-tax return to an extra hour, so through the substitution effect labour supply and participation rise, raising potential output. This is most powerful where effective marginal rates are highest, which for low earners is where benefit withdrawal compounds tax.\n• For - incentives to invest and take risk: lower corporation tax and capital allowances raise the post-tax return on a project, shifting the marginal efficiency of capital schedule right, so more projects clear the hurdle rate. Capital is internationally mobile, so relative tax rates affect where firms locate.\n• For - the Laffer argument: if the economy is to the right of the revenue-maximising rate, a cut raises revenue, so growth and the public finances improve together.\n• For - allocative efficiency: taxes create deadweight loss by driving a wedge between the price the buyer pays and the price the seller receives. Lower rates reduce that distortion.\n• Against - the income effect opposes the substitution effect: a tax cut raises income at any given hours, so a worker with a target income may work less. The net effect on labour supply is empirically modest for most employees, whose hours are fixed by contract.\n• Against - the Laffer argument is empirical, not theoretical: most estimates place advanced economies to the LEFT of the revenue-maximising rate, in which case a cut simply reduces revenue. The curve establishes that a peak exists, not where it is.\n• Against - the opportunity cost: a tax cut must be financed. If it is financed by borrowing at 30-year gilt yields of about 5.9%, it raises debt interest and erodes headroom of about £23.6bn against the stability rule. If it is financed by cuts to education, training or infrastructure, it removes the supply-side spending that raises productivity - so the net effect on LRAS can be negative.\n• Against - the binding constraints may lie elsewhere: the UK's productivity problem is widely attributed to weak investment, infrastructure, planning constraints, skills and the diffusion of best practice, none of which a tax cut directly addresses.\n• Against - distribution and public goods: taxation funds the legal system, education, health and infrastructure that growth depends on. Below some level, further cuts reduce growth. Tax cuts concentrated at the top also worsen the distribution of income with little demand effect, since high earners have a low MPC.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer argues that "most effective" requires a comparison, and compares tax reduction directly against public investment, skills and planning reform - concluding most defensibly that tax structure matters at the margin while the UK's binding constraints are investment and productivity, so tax reduction is neither the only nor the most powerful instrument.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Discuss the view that market-based supply-side policies are preferable to interventionist supply-side policies.`, marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For market-based - incentives and information: lower marginal tax rates, deregulation, privatisation and labour market flexibility work by changing the incentives facing millions of decision-makers who have information no government possesses. Competition forces cost reduction continuously, without anyone having to identify where the waste is.\n• For market-based - fiscal cost: planning reform, competition policy and deregulation are largely costless to the Exchequer, which matters with public sector net debt at 93.8% of GDP at the end of August 2026 and about £23.6bn of headroom against the stability rule.\n• For market-based - government failure: interventionist policy requires the state to pick sectors, projects and skills. Information failures, political capture and short electoral horizons mean it frequently picks wrongly, and the cost of the mistake is borne by taxpayers.\n• For interventionist - market failure is the reason the policies exist: education, training, infrastructure and R&D all generate positive externalities, so the free market under-provides them. Firms under-invest in transferable training because trained workers can be poached; firms under-invest in research because they cannot appropriate the full social return. No amount of deregulation corrects an externality.\n• For interventionist - public goods and coordination: transport and energy networks require coordination and scale that no single firm will finance, and their benefits accrue across the whole economy.\n• For interventionist - the evidence on the UK's constraints: weak productivity is attributed to low investment, poor infrastructure and skills gaps, all of which are under-provision problems rather than incentive problems.\n• Against market-based - distributional costs: flexibility experienced as insecurity, deregulation producing the financial instability of 2008, and tax cuts concentrated at the top all impose costs on identifiable groups, which limits political sustainability.\n• Against interventionist - lags and measurement: in the practice data, seven years of rising skills and R&D spending raised output per hour by 4.1%, and attribution to the policy is impossible to establish.\n• Evaluation of the framing: the two are complements addressing different failures. Privatisation of a natural monopoly requires a regulator; training provision requires that firms have an incentive to hire; infrastructure requires a planning system that permits building. The most effective programmes combine both.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer rejects the either/or framing with an argument rather than an assertion - showing that the right instrument depends on whether the problem is a market failure (interventionist) or a distortion of incentives (market-based) - and applies that test to specific UK constraints.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Evaluate the view that a government should aim to balance its budget over the economic cycle.`, marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - it permits stabilisation while controlling debt: balancing over the CYCLE rather than each year allows automatic stabilisers to run, so deficits in downturns are offset by surpluses in booms and the debt stock does not drift upwards.\n• For - intergenerational fairness: borrowing to fund current consumption shifts the cost to future taxpayers who receive nothing in return. Balancing the current budget confines borrowing to purposes that leave an asset.\n• For - credibility: a clear rule reassures lenders and lowers the risk premium in gilt yields, which matters with net debt at 93.8% of GDP at the end of August 2026 and 30-year yields near 5.9% in early September 2026.\n• For - it preserves fiscal space: a government entering a recession with a sound structural position can respond; one that does not, cannot.\n• Against - the cycle cannot be identified in real time: potential output is unobservable and estimates are revised heavily, so a government cannot know where it is in the cycle. Pre-2008 deficits were judged cyclical at the time and structural afterwards, which meant the rule was met on figures that proved wrong.\n• Against - it excludes investment: a strict balanced-budget rule would prevent borrowing for projects whose returns exceed the cost of borrowing, which is economically irrational. This is precisely why the UK's stability rule applies only to the CURRENT budget and a separate investment rule governs PSNFL, projected at 82.2% of GDP in 2028-29.\n• Against - it can force pro-cyclical policy: if a downturn is deeper than forecast, meeting the rule requires tightening into the downturn, which is self-defeating where the multiplier exceeds one.\n• Against - forecast-based rules invite gaming: with headroom of about £23.6bn on the March 2026 OBR forecast, governments can meet the rule by back-loading tax rises and cancelling capital projects in the final forecast year, changes that may never be delivered.\n• Against - debt sustainability does not require balance: it requires nominal GDP growth to exceed the effective interest rate on debt. The UK ratio fell by 1.3 percentage points in the year to August 2026 while debt rose by £78.5bn - so a persistent deficit is consistent with a falling ratio.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer argues that the sustainable target is a stable or falling debt-to-GDP ratio rather than a balanced budget, that the current/capital distinction is essential, and that an independent forecaster and transparent rules matter more than the precise numerical target.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Assess the view that the Office for Budget Responsibility has improved the conduct of UK fiscal policy.`, marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - independence removes optimism bias: before 2010 the Treasury produced its own forecasts, and governments had an incentive to assume growth that would make the figures work. The OBR, created in 2010, produces the official economic and fiscal forecasts, so the numbers against which policy is judged are not chosen by the people being judged.\n• For - transparency and accountability: publishing a five-year forecast alongside each Budget, and judging policy against explicit rules, makes the cost of announcements visible at the moment they are made. The March 2026 Economic and Fiscal Outlook, published on 3 March 2026, set out the central forecast and uncertainties to 2030-31.\n• For - credibility with lenders: an independent assessor lowers the risk premium that lenders require, which is worth real money on a debt stock of £2 985.5bn.\n• For - it makes the rules meaningful: a rule is only as credible as its assessor. The stability and investment rules depend on someone independent judging whether they are met, and on published headroom figures - about £23.6bn and £27.1bn respectively on the March 2026 forecast.\n• For - self-correction: the OBR publishes forecast evaluation reports assessing its own errors, most recently in June 2026, which is a discipline the Treasury never applied to itself.\n• Against - forecasting is inherently unreliable: five-year forecasts carry very wide margins of error. Headroom of £23.6bn is well inside that margin, so policy is being calibrated to a number more precise than the forecast can support.\n• Against - errors have consequences: the OBR acknowledged and apologised for the seriousness of an error on 26 November 2025 and the disruption it caused to government and Parliament. An institution whose authority rests on accuracy is damaged by such failures.\n• Against - it can distort policy: rules assessed against a forecast horizon encourage governments to meet the rule on paper through back-loaded measures and cancelled capital projects rather than through substantive consolidation - a form of gaming the OBR cannot prevent.\n• Against - it shifts power to unelected officials: judgements about potential output and the output gap are contestable, and a small change in those judgements changes how much a government may spend. That is a large influence over democratic choices.\n• Against - institutional churn: the framework has already been changed in response to these problems. Under the Charter for Budget Responsibility passed on 24 February 2026, the OBR assesses performance against the fiscal rules only once a year at Budgets - the next due on 28 October 2026 - while still publishing a second five-year forecast each spring, following the recommendations of its 2025 external review.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer distinguishes the OBR's success in removing optimism bias and improving transparency, which is well supported, from the separate question of whether forecast-based rules are a good way to run fiscal policy, where the criticisms are strongest - and concludes that the weaknesses lie mainly in the rules rather than in the institution.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Evaluate the view that increasing public investment is the most effective way of raising UK productivity.`, marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - the diagnosis fits: the UK's productivity problem is widely attributed to a low capital stock per worker and to infrastructure quality. Public investment raises the productivity of private capital directly, and it is the component of public spending most often cut - in the practice data it fell from £96bn to £94bn while debt interest rose from £42bn to £112bn.\n• For - it works on both sides: infrastructure spending raises AD now and LRAS later, so it addresses a cyclical slowdown and a structural constraint with the same instrument, and the multiplier on capital spending is among the largest.\n• For - it crowds in private investment: better transport, energy and digital networks raise the expected return on private projects, so private investment rises alongside. The UK's investment rule, framed on PSNFL rather than gross debt, was designed to recognise that public investment creates an asset.\n• For - market failure justifies it: networks require coordination and scale that no single firm will finance, and their benefits spill over to firms that did not pay for them.\n• Against - the lags are very long: benefits arrive a decade or more after the spending, so public investment cannot address a current productivity problem. In the practice data, seven years of higher skills and R&D spending raised output per hour by 4.1% in total.\n• Against - project selection: the return depends entirely on choosing well, and appraisal is subject to political pressure and optimism bias about costs and benefits. Badly chosen capital spending adds debt without adding output.\n• Against - the cost of financing: with 30-year gilt yields near 5.9% in early September 2026 and headroom of about £23.6bn against the stability rule, the hurdle rate a project must clear is high, and additional borrowing risks a higher risk premium on all government debt.\n• Against - rival explanations of weak productivity: planning constraints, which cost nothing to reform; the slow diffusion of best practice from frontier to laggard firms, which is a management and skills issue; weak business investment, which responds to expected demand and to the cost of capital; and reduced trade openness. None is addressed by public capital spending.\n• Against - crowding out: with unemployment at 4.9% in May to July 2026, additional public demand for construction labour and materials may displace private projects rather than add to total investment.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer compares public investment explicitly against the named alternatives rather than assessing it in isolation, and concludes with a criterion - most defensibly that public investment is necessary but not sufficient, because the constraints that cost nothing to fix, such as planning, offer a higher return per pound and act faster.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Discuss the view that the burden of UK taxation should be shifted away from incomes and towards consumption and wealth.`, marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - incentives: taxes on income distort the choice between work and leisure and between consuming now and saving. Taxing consumption rather than income leaves the return to saving and investment undistorted, which should raise the capital stock and therefore productivity and long-run growth.\n• For - a broader and harder-to-avoid base: consumption taxes are collected at the point of sale, so they are harder to avoid than income taxes, and a broad base allows a lower rate for the same revenue, reducing deadweight loss.\n• For - wealth is lightly taxed relative to its size: property and inherited wealth have grown far faster than incomes, partly because of the asset price effects of a decade of very low interest rates and quantitative easing. In the practice data the share of wealth held by the wealthiest fifth rose from 62% to 67%.\n• For - property taxes are efficient: land cannot be moved or hidden, so a tax on land values has minimal distortionary effect - one of the oldest results in public finance.\n• For - intergenerational fairness: income taxes fall on workers, who are disproportionately young, while wealth is held disproportionately by older households who benefited from asset price appreciation.\n• Against - consumption taxes are regressive: in the practice data VAT took 11.00% of the income of a household on £22 000 and 5.00% of one on £140 000, because lower-income households spend a higher proportion of their income. Shifting the burden this way worsens the distribution unless compensated.\n• Against - inflation: raising VAT or duties raises the price level directly. With CPI inflation at 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026 and above target, that is poorly timed, and it would raise the cost of index-linked government debt.\n• Against - wealth taxes are hard to administer: valuation of illiquid assets is contentious and costly, liquidity problems arise for asset-rich but income-poor households, and internationally mobile capital can relocate. Several countries have introduced and then abandoned annual wealth taxes for these reasons.\n• Against - the incentive gains may be small: most employees cannot vary their hours, so the labour supply response to income tax rates is empirically modest. The efficiency gain from the switch may therefore be less than the theory suggests.\n• Against - the practical obstacle: reform creates identifiable losers immediately and diffuse gains slowly, which is why property revaluation in particular has repeatedly been postponed.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer separates the efficiency case, which is strong for land and property and weaker for consumption taxes once regressivity is priced in, from the practical and distributional obstacles - concluding on whether compensation through the benefit system could reconcile the two.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, analysis and evaluation integrated, conclusion follows from the argument; L4 (16-20) well-developed and mostly balanced; L3 (11-15) some evaluation, uneven; L2 (6-10) descriptive, one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },
    ]
  },
  '3.2.6': {
    name: 'The International Economy',
    questions: [
      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\n  Index of average export prices (base year = 100):  106.4\n  Index of average import prices (base year = 100):  112.0\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate this country's terms of trade index. Give your answer to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Terms of trade = (index of export prices / index of import prices) x 100\n= (106.4 / 112.0) x 100 = 95.00.\n\n2 marks for 95.00. 1 mark for the correct formula stated without the arithmetic, or for inverting the ratio (112.0/106.4 x 100 = 105.26), which is a method error. Candidates may add that a figure below 100 means the terms of trade have DETERIORATED relative to the base year: a given volume of exports now buys fewer imports.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — written for this practice question\nCurrent account of the balance of payments, £bn\n  Balance on trade in goods:      -190\n  Balance on trade in services:   +142\n  Primary income balance:          -20\n  Secondary income balance:        -27\n\nUsing Extract B (Table 2), calculate the current account balance. Give your answer in £bn.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Current account = trade in goods + trade in services + primary income + secondary income\n= -190 + 142 - 20 - 27 = -£95bn (a deficit of £95bn).\n\n2 marks for -£95bn, or £95bn identified as a DEFICIT. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic or sign slip. An answer of -£48bn, which nets only the two trade balances, omits the income accounts: 1 mark maximum.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 3-4) — real data, ONS\n"The UK current account deficit was £15.1bn in Quarter 2 2026. GDP at current prices for the quarter was £795bn (figure supplied for this calculation)."\n\nUsing Extract A, calculate the current account deficit as a percentage of GDP. Give your answer to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Deficit as a percentage of GDP = 15.1 / 795 x 100 = 1.8994...\nAnswer: 1.90%.\n\n2 marks for 1.90% (accept "a deficit of 1.90% of GDP"). 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic or rounding slip, or for an answer left as a decimal without converting to a percentage. The ONS reported the Q2 2026 deficit as 1.9% of GDP, down from 2.4% in Q1 2026.` },

      { q: `Extract C (Table 3) — written for this practice question\n  Value of imported steel before the tariff:   £8.4bn\n  Ad valorem tariff imposed:                   12.5%\n(Assume for this calculation that the volume of imports does not change.)\n\nUsing Extract C (Table 3), calculate the tariff revenue the government would collect. Give your answer in £bn to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Tariff revenue = tariff rate x value of imports = 0.125 x £8.4bn = £1.05bn.\n\n2 marks for £1.05bn. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for an answer expressed in the wrong units. Candidates may note that this overstates the true revenue, because in practice the tariff raises the domestic price, import volumes fall, and the revenue rectangle shrinks - the assumption of unchanged volume is stated only to make the arithmetic definite.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — written for this practice question\nMaximum output per worker per year\n              Wheat (tonnes)   Cloth (units)\n  Country A:        120              40\n  Country B:         60              30\n\nUsing Extract B (Table 2), calculate the opportunity cost to Country A of producing one unit of cloth, in tonnes of wheat. Give your answer to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Opportunity cost of 1 unit of cloth in Country A = wheat forgone / cloth gained = 120 / 40 = 3.00 tonnes of wheat.\n\n2 marks for 3.00 tonnes. 1 mark for the correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for calculating the reciprocal (0.33, the opportunity cost of one tonne of wheat in units of cloth) - method understood but the wrong question answered. Candidates may note that the same calculation for Country B gives 60/30 = 2.00 tonnes, so Country B has the comparative advantage in cloth.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nSterling-dollar exchange rate\n  1 January:    1.3420 dollars per pound\n  31 December:  1.2985 dollars per pound\n\nUsing Extract A (Table 1), calculate the percentage change in the sterling-dollar exchange rate over the year. Give your answer to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Percentage change = (1.2985 - 1.3420) / 1.3420 x 100 = -0.0435 / 1.3420 x 100 = -3.2414...\nAnswer: -3.24% (a depreciation of 3.24%).\n\n2 marks for -3.24% with the sign or the word "depreciation"/"fall". 1 mark for the correct method with a sign, arithmetic or rounding error, or for identifying the fall of 0.0435 without expressing it as a percentage. Dividing by the final value is a method error: 1 mark maximum.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nUK trade balances, £bn\n              Year 1   Year 2   Year 3   Year 4\n  Trade in goods:     -172     -181     -186     -190\n  Trade in services:  +118     +127     +136     +142\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) suggest that the UK has a comparative advantage in services rather than in goods.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• The UK runs a deficit on trade in goods in every year, widening from £172bn to £190bn, and a surplus on trade in services in every year, rising from £118bn to £142bn (1 mark for identifying the contrast with figures).\n• The services surplus grew by 20.3% over the period while the goods deficit widened by 10.5%, so the specialisation is becoming more pronounced, not less (2 marks for the growth-rate comparison).\n• Explanation: a country exports what it can produce at a lower opportunity cost than its trading partners. A persistent surplus in services - financial and professional services, insurance, education - indicates that the UK's endowment of skilled labour and institutions gives it that advantage, while it imports goods more cheaply than it could make them (1 mark).\n• Qualification worth crediting: trade balances reflect demand conditions, exchange rates and trade barriers as well as relative costs, so they are evidence of REVEALED comparative advantage rather than a direct measurement of opportunity cost (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Table 2) — written for this practice question\n              Year 1   Year 2   Year 3   Year 4\n  Index of export prices:   100.0    102.8    104.6    106.4\n  Index of import prices:   100.0    106.2    109.5    112.0\n\nExplain how the data in Extract B (Table 2) show that this country's terms of trade deteriorated.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• Terms of trade = export price index / import price index x 100. The values are 100.00, 96.80, 95.53 and 95.00 (2 marks for at least two correct calculations).\n• The index falls in every year, so the terms of trade deteriorate continuously - import prices rose by 12.0% while export prices rose by only 6.4% (1 mark).\n• Explanation: a given volume of exports now buys fewer imports, so real national income falls for any given volume of trade. The country must export more to obtain the same quantity of imports (1 mark).\n• Developed point worth crediting: a deterioration is not unambiguously bad. If it results from a depreciation, export volumes may rise enough for the VALUE of exports to increase, so the trade balance can improve even as the terms of trade worsen - provided the Marshall-Lerner condition holds (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract C (Figure 3) — real data, ONS\n  UK current account deficit, Q1 2026:   £18.2bn   (2.4% of GDP)\n  UK current account deficit, Q2 2026:   £15.1bn   (1.9% of GDP)\n\nExplain how the data in Extract C (Figure 3) show that the UK current account position improved between the first and second quarters of 2026.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• The deficit fell by £3.1bn, from £18.2bn to £15.1bn, a reduction of 17.0% in a single quarter (2 marks for the change and the percentage).\n• As a share of GDP it fell by 0.5 percentage points, from 2.4% to 1.9% (1 mark).\n• The share of GDP is the more meaningful measure, because it shows the deficit relative to the size of the economy and therefore relative to the country's capacity to finance it. The ratio fell by more than the cash figure would suggest on its own, which implies nominal GDP also grew (1 mark).\n• Qualification worth crediting: this is a single quarter, the current account is volatile and subject to substantial revision, and an improvement in the deficit tells us nothing about its cause - a narrowing deficit caused by weaker domestic demand is not the same as one caused by stronger exports (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained. A response that treats the fall in the deficit as a surplus cannot score above 2.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nTrade balance following a 10% depreciation of the currency at the end of Quarter 0, £bn\n  Quarter 0:  -38\n  Quarter 1:  -44\n  Quarter 2:  -46\n  Quarter 3:  -41\n  Quarter 4:  -33\n  Quarter 5:  -28\n\nExplain how the data in Extract A (Table 1) are consistent with the J-curve effect.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• The deficit initially WIDENS after the depreciation, from £38bn to £46bn over two quarters, before improving to £28bn by Quarter 5 - a deterioration followed by an improvement, which traces the shape of a J (2 marks for both phases with figures).\n• Explanation of the initial worsening: in the short run, contracts are already signed and volumes barely respond, so the main effect is that each unit of imports costs more in domestic currency. Price elasticities of demand for exports and imports are low, so the VALUE of imports rises while export volumes have not yet adjusted (1 mark).\n• Explanation of the subsequent improvement: over time buyers respond to the changed relative prices. Export volumes rise because exports are cheaper abroad and import volumes fall because imports are dearer at home, so the Marshall-Lerner condition - that the sum of the two elasticities exceeds one - comes to be satisfied and the balance improves (1 mark).\n• By Quarter 5 the deficit is £10bn smaller than before the depreciation, so the eventual net effect is an improvement (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract B (Figure 2) — real data\n  US average effective tariff rate, August 2025:     18.2% (highest in about 90 years)\n  US average effective tariff rate, 8 April 2026:    11.8% (highest since the early 1940s), subsequently easing towards about 7%\n  WTO-IMF trade-policy activity index, first five months of 2026: nearly double 2024 levels\n  UK accession to the CPTPP:                         15 December 2024; in force with all eleven other members by 1 September 2026\n\nExplain how the data in Extract B (Figure 2) show that the world trading system became more protectionist and more volatile after 2024.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• The level of protection rose sharply: a US average effective tariff rate of 18.2% by August 2025 is described as the highest for about 90 years, so this is a departure from decades of liberalisation (1 mark).\n• The VOLATILITY is as striking as the level: the rate fell to 11.8% by April 2026 and then eased further towards about 7%. Swings of that size within a year mean firms cannot plan, which raises the effective barrier to trade above what any single rate implies (2 marks for identifying volatility as distinct from level and explaining why it matters).\n• Trade-policy activity - new tariffs, subsidies and safeguards combined - ran at nearly double 2024 levels in the first five months of 2026, so the change is broad rather than confined to tariffs (1 mark).\n• Counter-evidence worth crediting: the UK's accession to the CPTPP, in force with all eleven other members by 1 September 2026, is liberalisation running in the opposite direction, so the picture is one of fragmentation into blocs rather than uniform protectionism (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract C (Table 3) — written for this practice question\nMaximum output per worker per year\n              Wheat (tonnes)   Cloth (units)\n  Country A:        120              40\n  Country B:         60              30\n\nExplain how the data in Extract C (Table 3) show that Country B has a comparative advantage in cloth even though it has an absolute advantage in neither good.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Creditworthy points:\n• Absolute advantage: Country A produces more of BOTH goods per worker (120 against 60 tonnes of wheat, 40 against 30 units of cloth), so it has an absolute advantage in both (1 mark).\n• Opportunity cost of one unit of cloth: in Country A, 120/40 = 3.00 tonnes of wheat; in Country B, 60/30 = 2.00 tonnes of wheat (2 marks for both).\n• Country B gives up less wheat to produce a unit of cloth, so its opportunity cost is lower and it holds the comparative advantage in cloth. Conversely Country A's opportunity cost of one tonne of wheat is 0.33 units of cloth against Country B's 0.50, so A has the comparative advantage in wheat (1 mark).\n• Explanation of significance: trade is mutually beneficial whenever opportunity cost ratios differ, regardless of absolute productivity. Any exchange rate between 2 and 3 tonnes of wheat per unit of cloth leaves both countries better off than in autarky (1 mark).\n\nPoints: 4/3/2/1 according to how fully the data are used AND explained.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 5-6) states: "The government has imposed a 12.5% tariff on imported steel in response to what it describes as unfair foreign competition."\n\nWith the help of a diagram, analyse the effects of a tariff on domestic consumers, domestic producers and the government.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: domestic supply and demand for steel with a horizontal world supply line at Pw and a second horizontal line at Pw + tariff. Mark domestic output before and after, total consumption before and after, the tariff revenue rectangle, and the two deadweight loss triangles (production and consumption).\n• Consumers lose: the domestic price rises from Pw to Pw + tariff, so they pay more and consume less. Consumer surplus falls by the whole area between the two price lines up to the original quantity demanded. The burden is regressive if the good is a necessity, and it falls on downstream firms too, since steel is an input.\n• Domestic producers gain: at the higher price, domestic output expands along the supply curve and producer surplus rises. Employment in the protected industry is preserved - the visible, concentrated benefit that makes tariffs politically attractive.\n• Government gains: revenue equal to the tariff per unit multiplied by the remaining volume of imports - £1.05bn on imports of £8.4bn at 12.5%, before allowing for the fall in volume.\n• Net welfare: the two deadweight loss triangles are the loss not recovered by anyone. The production triangle represents resources drawn into a use in which the country does not have comparative advantage; the consumption triangle represents consumption forgone.\n• Wider analysis: retaliation is likely, so export industries lose; and the gains are concentrated while the losses are spread thinly across consumers, which explains why protection persists despite a net welfare loss. The rise in the US average effective tariff rate to 18.2% by August 2025, the highest in about 90 years, and trade-policy activity at nearly double 2024 levels in early 2026, show the mechanism operating at scale.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram with the areas identified plus all three groups analysed and the net welfare loss explained; L2 (4-6) diagram with partial analysis, or good analysis with a flawed diagram; L1 (1-3) assertion that tariffs raise prices.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 7-8) states: "Ricardo showed that two countries can both gain from trade even when one of them is more efficient at producing everything."\n\nWith the help of a production possibility diagram, analyse how comparative advantage generates gains from specialisation and trade.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: two production possibility frontiers, one per country, with the same two goods on the axes and different slopes. The slope IS the opportunity cost ratio, so different slopes are the source of the gain. Best answers mark a post-trade consumption point beyond each country's own PPF.\n• The numerical case: with Country A producing 120 tonnes of wheat or 40 units of cloth per worker, and Country B 60 wheat or 30 cloth, A has an absolute advantage in both. But A's opportunity cost of a unit of cloth is 3.00 tonnes of wheat while B's is 2.00, so B has the comparative advantage in cloth and A in wheat.\n• The chain of reasoning: if each country specialises where its opportunity cost is lower, total world output of both goods rises from the same resources. Trading at any rate between the two domestic opportunity cost ratios - here between 2 and 3 tonnes of wheat per unit of cloth - leaves both countries able to consume outside their own PPFs.\n• Why this is the core result: it shows that the basis for trade is relative, not absolute, efficiency, so even the least productive country has something to gain by specialising.\n• Analysis of the assumptions, which is what separates the top band: constant opportunity costs (in reality increasing costs limit specialisation); no transport costs or trade barriers; perfect factor mobility within each country; and no account of how the gains are distributed. Adjustment costs fall on the workers in the contracting sector, which is why liberalisation can be resisted even when the country gains overall.\n• Further limitation: complete specialisation creates dependence and exposure to shocks, and for a developing country specialising in primary products it can mean a deteriorating long-run terms of trade.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram plus the opportunity cost reasoning worked through with numbers and the assumptions analysed; L2 (4-6) the theory explained with a diagram but assumptions unexamined; L1 (1-3) absolute and comparative advantage confused or merely defined.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 9-10) states: "Markets moved to price in a narrower interest rate differential between the United Kingdom and the euro area."\n\nWith the help of a foreign exchange market diagram, analyse how a fall in UK interest rates relative to those abroad affects the value of sterling.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: the market for sterling, with the exchange rate (euros per pound) on the vertical axis and the quantity of sterling on the horizontal. Demand for sterling shifts left from D1 to D2, and/or supply shifts right as UK residents move funds abroad, so the rate falls from e1 to e2.\n• Chain of reasoning: a lower relative return on sterling deposits reduces the incentive for foreign investors to hold sterling-denominated assets. They demand less sterling and UK residents supply more as they buy foreign assets, so the currency depreciates. These short-term flows are "hot money" and dwarf trade flows in volume.\n• Expectations: what moves the rate is the change in the EXPECTED differential, since current rates are already priced in. The ECB raised its deposit rate to 2.50% on 10 September 2026 while the Bank of England held Bank Rate at 3.75% on 17 September, narrowing the differential; sterling traded around 1.16 euros in September 2026 having reached about 1.18 euros in July.\n• Consequences analysed: a depreciation raises the sterling price of imports, adding to inflation - unwelcome with CPI at 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026 - and lowers the foreign price of exports, so net exports and AD may rise. It also raises the sterling value of foreign-currency assets and liabilities.\n• Qualification: the trade effect depends on elasticities. The Marshall-Lerner condition requires the sum of the price elasticities of demand for exports and imports to exceed one; in the short run it typically does not, producing the J-curve.\n• Further qualification: interest rates are only one determinant. Relative inflation, the current account deficit (1.9% of GDP in Q2 2026), fiscal credibility and global risk sentiment can all dominate, so a currency can fall despite a favourable differential.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) correctly labelled foreign exchange diagram, the capital flow chain developed, and the expectations or elasticity qualification made; L2 (4-6) diagram plus a simple mechanism; L1 (1-3) "lower rates weaken the pound" with no diagram.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 11-12) states: "The trade deficit widened for two quarters after the currency fell before beginning to improve."\n\nWith the help of a diagram, analyse the J-curve effect following a depreciation.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: time on the horizontal axis, the current account or trade balance on the vertical, with a curve that dips below its starting level immediately after the depreciation and then rises above it - the shape of a J.\n• The initial deterioration explained: the depreciation changes prices immediately but volumes only slowly. Import contracts are already signed and priced, and domestic buyers cannot switch suppliers at once, so the same volume of imports costs more in sterling. Export volumes have not yet responded, so the value of exports rises little. The balance worsens.\n• Why volumes respond slowly: consumers and firms take time to notice and act on relative price changes; contracts run for months; exporters need time to expand capacity, find distributors and win new customers; and supply chains cannot be re-sourced quickly.\n• The subsequent improvement: over one to two years the price elasticities of demand for exports and imports rise as buyers adjust. Once the Marshall-Lerner condition is satisfied - the sum of the two elasticities exceeding one - the volume effects outweigh the price effect and the balance improves beyond its original level.\n• Illustrative data: a trade deficit moving from £38bn to £44bn and £46bn in the two quarters after a depreciation, then to £41bn, £33bn and £28bn, traces exactly this path.\n• Analysis of what can prevent the recovery: if exporters have no spare capacity, or if export prices are set in foreign currency so that exporters take higher margins rather than cutting prices, volumes will not rise. If the depreciation feeds into domestic inflation - which is more likely when inflation is already above target, as at 3.1% in the year to August 2026 - the competitiveness gain erodes and the J never completes.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram plus both phases explained with the elasticity reasoning and a limitation; L2 (4-6) the shape described with partial explanation; L1 (1-3) "the deficit gets worse then better" asserted.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 13-14) states: "Whether a devaluation improves the trade balance depends on how responsive buyers are to the change in prices."\n\nAnalyse the significance of the Marshall-Lerner condition for a country considering a devaluation of its currency.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Statement of the condition: a depreciation or devaluation improves the trade balance only if the sum of the price elasticity of demand for exports and the price elasticity of demand for imports, in absolute terms, is greater than one (PEDx + PEDm > 1).\n• The reasoning behind it: a depreciation has two opposing effects. Export volumes rise and import volumes fall - both improve the balance - but each unit of imports now costs more in domestic currency, which worsens it. The condition is precisely the point at which the volume effects outweigh the price effect.\n• Worked intuition: if both elasticities are 0.3, summing to 0.6, a 10% depreciation raises export volumes by 3% and cuts import volumes by 3% while import prices rise 10% - so the value of imports rises and the balance worsens.\n• Why it matters for policy: a devaluation is not a reliable instrument for correcting a deficit. A country whose imports are energy, food and components - price-inelastic necessities with no domestic substitute, which describes much of UK import demand - may find the condition fails in the short run.\n• Link to the J-curve: elasticities rise with time, so the condition typically fails immediately and holds after one to two years. The J-curve is the time path implied by the condition, not a separate phenomenon.\n• Further analysis: even if the condition holds, the improvement may be temporary. The depreciation raises import costs and therefore domestic inflation, which erodes the competitiveness gain unless domestic costs are restrained. And a devaluation invites retaliation, which is a live risk with trade-policy activity at nearly double 2024 levels in early 2026.\n• Analysis of the alternative: because the elasticity conditions are so demanding, the durable route to a smaller deficit is improved non-price competitiveness - productivity, quality and innovation - rather than a cheaper currency.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) the condition stated accurately, the underlying reasoning explained, and the policy implications and time dimension analysed; L2 (4-6) the condition stated with partial reasoning; L1 (1-3) the condition quoted with no explanation.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 15-16) states: "Instead of a tariff, the government has considered restricting the quantity of imported steel that may enter the country."\n\nWith the help of a diagram, analyse how the effects of a quota differ from those of a tariff.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: domestic supply and demand with the world price Pw. Under a quota, the supply available to the domestic market becomes domestic supply plus the fixed quota quantity, so the effective supply curve shifts right by the quota amount and the domestic price rises to Pq. A tariff diagram with the Pw + tariff line may be drawn alongside for comparison.\n• Similarities: both raise the domestic price above the world price, both expand domestic production and contract domestic consumption, both transfer surplus from consumers to domestic producers, and both create the same two deadweight loss triangles.\n• Key difference - who gets the revenue: a tariff generates government revenue equal to the tariff multiplied by the remaining import volume. Under a quota, the equivalent rectangle is a QUOTA RENT that accrues to whoever holds the import licence - often the foreign exporter, who now sells a restricted quantity at a higher price. So the same restriction can transfer income abroad rather than to the Exchequer.\n• Key difference - certainty: a quota fixes the QUANTITY of imports, so the protection is guaranteed regardless of what happens to foreign costs. A tariff fixes the PRICE wedge, so if foreign producers cut costs they can still take market share. A quota therefore offers domestic producers more certain protection and is correspondingly more distorting.\n• Key difference - the effect of demand growth: if domestic demand rises, a tariff leaves imports free to expand, whereas a quota holds them fixed, so the domestic price rises further and the protection becomes progressively tighter.\n• Competition effects: a quota can convert a competitive domestic industry into an effective monopoly for the domestic producers, since the maximum competing supply is capped - a further loss of allocative efficiency that a tariff does not produce.\n• Administration: quotas require licences to be allocated, which creates scope for rent-seeking and corruption; tariffs are collected at the border.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram plus at least three genuine differences analysed, including the destination of the rent; L2 (4-6) similarities and one or two differences; L1 (1-3) definitions of tariff and quota.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 17-18) states: "The share of world output that crosses a border has risen dramatically over the past half-century."\n\nAnalyse the main causes of globalisation.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Falling transport costs: containerisation from the 1950s onwards and cheaper air freight cut the cost of moving goods so far that distance ceased to be the binding constraint on where production takes place. This made it viable to separate production from consumption geographically.\n• Falling communication and information costs: telecommunications and then the internet made it possible to coordinate a supply chain spread across continents in real time, and made services - software, professional services, call centres - tradable for the first time.\n• Trade liberalisation: successive GATT rounds and then the WTO reduced average tariffs substantially from the 1940s onwards, and regional agreements such as the single market and the CPTPP went further. The UK joined the CPTPP on 15 December 2024, in force with all eleven other members by 1 September 2026.\n• Liberalisation of capital movements: the removal of exchange controls and the deregulation of financial markets allowed capital to flow to the highest return anywhere, which is what makes foreign direct investment and global supply chains financeable.\n• The growth of multinational corporations: firms with the capability to organise production across borders both respond to and drive the process, locating each stage where its costs are lowest.\n• The entry of large economies into the world trading system: China's WTO accession in 2001 and the opening of the former centrally planned economies roughly doubled the effective global labour force.\n• Analysis of the reversal now under way: the same forces can run backwards. The US average effective tariff rate reached 18.2% by August 2025, the highest in about 90 years, and WTO-IMF tracking found trade-policy activity in the first five months of 2026 running at nearly double 2024 levels. Geopolitical risk and pandemic experience have also pushed firms towards resilience over least cost, so supply chains are shortening.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) at least three causes developed as chains of reasoning, with the recent reversal analysed; L2 (4-6) causes listed with some explanation; L1 (1-3) a list of factors.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 19-20) states: "A major trading partner has imposed sharply higher tariffs on imports from all sources."\n\nWith the help of an AD/AS diagram, analyse the likely effects on the UK economy of a rise in tariffs imposed by a major trading partner.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diagram: AD shifts left from AD1 to AD2 as exports fall; strong answers also shift SRAS left if the tariffs raise the cost of imported inputs through disrupted supply chains, giving a larger fall in output and an ambiguous price effect.\n• Demand-side chain: a tariff imposed abroad raises the price of UK exports in that market, so export volumes fall. Since AD = C + I + G + (X - M), a fall in X shifts AD left, and the multiplier magnifies the fall in national income and employment, concentrated in exporting sectors and regions.\n• Investment channel: exporters facing an uncertain market defer capital spending, so I falls as well as X, and the accelerator amplifies this. Uncertainty itself is damaging - the US average effective tariff rate moved from 18.2% in August 2025 to 11.8% by April 2026 and then towards about 7%, and swings of that size make investment planning impossible.\n• Trade diversion channel: goods excluded from the protected market are redirected elsewhere, so UK producers face intensified competition in third markets even where no tariff applies to them.\n• Exchange rate offset: if sterling depreciates in response, part of the competitiveness loss is recovered - but only if the Marshall-Lerner condition holds, and the depreciation raises import prices and therefore inflation, already 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026.\n• Supply-side damage: tariffs reduce specialisation according to comparative advantage, so world output falls and unit costs rise. Over time this lowers productivity and shifts LRAS left, which is the more serious and more permanent effect.\n• Qualification worth crediting: the UK's trade is heavily weighted towards services, which are less exposed to goods tariffs - the practice data show a services surplus of £142bn against a goods deficit of £190bn - so the direct exposure may be smaller than for a goods exporter.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) accurate diagram plus at least three channels developed, including a qualification; L2 (4-6) diagram plus the export channel developed; L1 (1-3) "tariffs are bad for exports" asserted.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 21-22) states: "Joining a customs union means adopting a common external tariff as well as removing tariffs between members."\n\nAnalyse the difference between trade creation and trade diversion when a country joins a customs union.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Definitions: trade creation occurs when the removal of internal tariffs shifts consumption from a higher-cost domestic producer to a lower-cost producer within the union - production moves towards comparative advantage, so it raises welfare. Trade diversion occurs when the common external tariff shifts consumption from a lower-cost producer OUTSIDE the union to a higher-cost producer inside it - production moves away from comparative advantage, so it lowers welfare.\n• Worked illustration: suppose a good costs £100 domestically, £85 from a union partner and £75 from a non-member. With a uniform 40% tariff before entry, the domestic producer supplies at £100 against £119 and £105 for the imports. After entry, the partner's price falls to £85 while the non-member still faces the tariff at £105. Consumption shifts from the £100 domestic producer to the £85 partner - trade creation, a genuine saving. But the £75 non-member, the genuinely cheapest supplier, is excluded - trade diversion.\n• The analytical point: joining a customs union is liberalisation towards members and protection against everyone else, so the net welfare effect is ambiguous and must be assessed empirically.\n• What determines the balance: trade creation dominates when members' economies are similar in structure but differ in costs, when the union is geographically close so transport costs are low, when pre-entry tariffs were high and the common external tariff is low, and when the union is large enough to contain the world's low-cost producers.\n• Further effects worth crediting: dynamic gains from a larger market - economies of scale, greater competition, more investment - may exceed the static welfare calculation. Against that, a customs union removes the freedom to negotiate independent trade agreements, which is why membership of one precludes joining another. The UK joined the CPTPP, a free trade area rather than a customs union, on 15 December 2024, in force with all eleven other members by 1 September 2026.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) both concepts defined and distinguished with a worked or numerical illustration, plus the determinants of the balance; L2 (4-6) both defined with limited development; L1 (1-3) one concept only, or the two confused.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 23-24) states: "A multinational corporation has announced a large new manufacturing plant employing three thousand people."\n\nAnalyse the likely effects of inward foreign direct investment on a host economy.`, marks: 9, command: 'Analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Aggregate demand and employment: the construction and operation of the plant is investment expenditure, so AD shifts right, and the multiplier spreads the effect through the local economy as wages are spent. Three thousand direct jobs generate further indirect employment in suppliers and services.\n• Aggregate supply: the plant adds to the capital stock, so LRAS shifts right. If the multinational brings superior technology and management practice, and if these diffuse to domestic firms through supply chains and labour turnover, the productivity gain extends beyond the firm itself - a positive externality and the main long-run argument for attracting FDI.\n• Human capital: training raises the skills of the local workforce, and those skills are partly transferable, which is another spillover the investing firm cannot fully appropriate.\n• Balance of payments: the initial inflow is a credit on the financial account, and if the plant exports, the trade balance improves. But profits repatriated to the parent company are a DEBIT on the primary income account, so over the life of the investment the current account effect can turn negative - which is part of why the UK's primary income balance is negative in the practice data.\n• Public finances: corporation tax and the income tax and National Insurance paid by employees raise receipts, although multinationals' capacity to shift profits between jurisdictions limits the corporation tax yield.\n• Costs analysed: domestic competitors may be driven out, so some of the employment is displaced rather than created; the multinational may use imported components, limiting the local multiplier; and it may extract subsidies and tax concessions from the host government in a competitive bidding process that transfers rent from taxpayers to shareholders.\n• Vulnerability: a footloose multinational can relocate when costs rise or subsidies expire, leaving a region dependent on a single employer - so the benefit is contingent on the investment being durable.\n\nLevels: L3 (7-9) at least three effects developed as chains of reasoning, covering both benefits and costs, with the income account point; L2 (4-6) benefits listed with limited development; L1 (1-3) "it creates jobs" asserted.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — real data\n  US average effective tariff rate, August 2025:    18.2%  (highest in about 90 years)\n  US average effective tariff rate, 8 April 2026:   11.8%, subsequently easing towards about 7%\n  WTO-IMF trade-policy activity index, first five months of 2026:  nearly double 2024 levels\n  UK trade in goods balance (practice data, £bn):   Year 1 -172; Year 4 -190\n  UK trade in services balance (practice data, £bn): Year 1 +118; Year 4 +142\n  UK current account deficit:  Q1 2026 £18.2bn (2.4% of GDP); Q2 2026 £15.1bn (1.9% of GDP)\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data suggest that the rise in global trade barriers since 2025 has damaged the UK economy?`, marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Support for damage: the scale of the change is very large. A US average effective tariff rate of 18.2% by August 2025 is the highest for about 90 years, and trade-policy activity across all instruments ran at nearly double 2024 levels in the first five months of 2026. Higher barriers reduce specialisation according to comparative advantage, so world output falls and UK exporters face a smaller market.\n• Support: the VOLATILITY may matter more than the level. A rate moving from 18.2% to 11.8% and then towards 7% within a year makes investment planning impossible, so firms defer capital spending - a channel that damages LRAS as well as AD.\n• Support: the UK goods deficit widened from £172bn to £190bn over the practice period, consistent with UK goods exporters losing ground.\n• Against: the UK current account deficit NARROWED between the quarters shown, from £18.2bn (2.4% of GDP) to £15.1bn (1.9%), which is the opposite of what a trade shock would produce - so the aggregate external position improved, not deteriorated.\n• Against: the UK's comparative advantage is in services, where the surplus grew 20.3% over the practice period to £142bn. Goods tariffs largely do not apply to services, so the UK's direct exposure is smaller than for a goods-exporting economy.\n• Against: the tariffs are US measures, and the data give no figure at all for the share of UK exports going to the US, nor any UK export volume series. The causal link is asserted, not shown.\n• Limits of the data: two of the six series are labelled practice data on an unspecified period; the current account comparison is a single quarter and highly volatile; and there is no counterfactual for what UK trade would have done without the tariff increases. Exchange rate movements, which would show up in all these series, are absent.\n• Judgement: the data establish that global trade policy became markedly more restrictive and more volatile, and the theory is clear about the direction of the effect, but the UK-specific series shown point in conflicting directions and cannot demonstrate damage to the UK economy.\n\nLevels: L3 (8-10) quantitative use of the data on both sides, explicit recognition of the missing UK-specific evidence, and a supported judgement; L2 (4-7) partial use with a thin judgement; L1 (1-3) assertion. A conclusion reached without reference to the data cannot exceed L1.` },

      { q: `Extract A (Table 1) — written for this practice question\nTrade balance following a 10% depreciation of sterling at the end of Quarter 0, £bn\n  Quarter 0: -38 ; Quarter 1: -44 ; Quarter 2: -46 ; Quarter 3: -41 ; Quarter 4: -33 ; Quarter 5: -28\n\nExtract B (Table 2) — written for this practice question\n  Estimated price elasticity of demand for UK exports, after one quarter:  -0.3\n  Estimated price elasticity of demand for UK imports, after one quarter:  -0.4\n  Estimated price elasticity of demand for UK exports, after two years:    -0.8\n  Estimated price elasticity of demand for UK imports, after two years:    -0.7\n\nTo what extent, if at all, do the data suggest that a depreciation of sterling is an effective way of reducing a current account deficit?`, marks: 10, command: 'To what extent',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Support: by Quarter 5 the deficit is £28bn against £38bn before the depreciation, an improvement of £10bn, or 26.3%. The elasticities after two years sum to 1.5, which exceeds one, so the Marshall-Lerner condition is satisfied in the long run and the improvement is exactly what theory predicts.\n• Against - the short run: the elasticities after one quarter sum to only 0.7, so the condition FAILS initially. The data confirm this: the deficit widens from £38bn to £46bn over two quarters before it begins to improve. A government seeking a quick correction would get the opposite of what it wanted.\n• Against - the timescale: the improvement takes more than a year to exceed the starting point, and the full elasticity response takes two years. Any policy whose benefit arrives that late is exposed to other shocks in the interval.\n• Against - what the data omit: there is no information on domestic inflation. A depreciation raises import prices, and with UK CPI inflation already at 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026 the competitiveness gain could be eroded by higher domestic costs before the volume effects mature. The elasticity estimates implicitly assume it is not.\n• Against - no counterfactual: the improvement from Quarter 3 could reflect a recovery in world demand, a fall in commodity prices or weaker UK domestic demand reducing import volumes, none of which is shown. A deficit that narrows because the domestic economy is weakening is not a policy success.\n• Against - elasticities are estimates: point estimates with no confidence intervals, and elasticities change with the composition of trade. UK imports of energy, food and components are price-inelastic with no immediate domestic substitute.\n• Judgement: the data support the conclusion that depreciation works eventually and perversely at first. That makes it a poor instrument for correcting a deficit in any horizon a government cares about, and it says nothing about the primary and secondary income accounts, which are part of the current account and are unaffected by competitiveness.\n\nLevels: L3 (8-10) uses both tables quantitatively, applies the Marshall-Lerner condition to the elasticity figures explicitly, recognises the limits and reaches a supported judgement; L2 (4-7) partial use with a thin judgement; L1 (1-3) assertion. A conclusion reached without reference to the data cannot exceed L1.` },

      { q: `Explain the main causes of globalisation over the past fifty years.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Definition and framing: globalisation is the increasing integration of national economies through trade, capital flows, migration and the transfer of technology. Its causes are the things that reduced the cost of operating across borders.\n• Containerisation and cheaper transport: standardised containers from the 1950s onwards, larger ships and cheaper air freight cut the cost of moving goods so far that it ceased to be a binding constraint on where production takes place.\n• The communications and information revolution: telecommunications and the internet made it possible to coordinate production spread across continents in real time, and made services - software, professional services, finance, education - tradable for the first time, which matters particularly for the UK, whose services surplus reached £142bn in the practice data.\n• Trade liberalisation: successive GATT rounds and then the WTO cut average tariffs substantially, and regional agreements went further. The UK joined the CPTPP on 15 December 2024, in force with all eleven other members by 1 September 2026.\n• Liberalisation of capital movements: the removal of exchange controls and the deregulation of financial markets allowed capital to seek the highest return anywhere, which is what makes foreign direct investment and globally financed supply chains possible.\n• The growth of multinational corporations: firms organised to exploit differences in factor costs across countries both respond to and drive integration, slicing production into stages located wherever each stage is cheapest.\n• Entry of large economies into the trading system: China's accession to the WTO in 2001 and the opening of the former centrally planned economies roughly doubled the effective global labour supply and transformed relative factor prices.\n• Political and institutional change: the end of the Cold War, the spread of market-oriented policy, and the growth of international institutions providing a rules-based framework.\n• The reversal now under way, which a strong answer notes: the US average effective tariff rate reached 18.2% by August 2025, the highest in about 90 years; WTO-IMF tracking found trade-policy activity at nearly double 2024 levels in early 2026; and the pandemic and geopolitical risk have pushed firms towards resilience over least cost. The causes above are reversible.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several causes developed with mechanisms and the recent reversal recognised; L2 (6-10) causes explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Explain the main determinants of a country's current account balance.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Structure of the account: the current account comprises trade in goods, trade in services, primary income (investment income and compensation of employees) and secondary income (transfers). The practice data show a UK pattern of a large goods deficit (£190bn) partly offset by a services surplus (£142bn), with both income balances negative.\n• Relative price competitiveness: the exchange rate and relative inflation determine the price of a country's exports abroad and of imports at home. A depreciation improves the balance only if the Marshall-Lerner condition holds, so the effect is slow and uncertain.\n• Non-price competitiveness: quality, design, reliability, after-sales service and brand determine export performance independently of price. This is the more durable determinant, and it depends on productivity and innovation.\n• Relative rates of economic growth: a country growing faster than its trading partners sucks in imports through the marginal propensity to import while its export markets grow more slowly, so the balance worsens. This is the standard growth-versus-current-account policy conflict.\n• The domestic saving-investment balance: the current account balance is identically equal to national saving less domestic investment. A deficit therefore means the country is investing more than it saves and is financing the difference by borrowing from abroad - which is why a fiscal deficit can show up as a current account deficit.\n• Commodity prices and the terms of trade: for an energy importer, higher oil prices worsen the balance directly. The practice terms of trade index falling from 100.00 to 95.00 means a given volume of exports buys fewer imports.\n• Structure of the economy and comparative advantage: a country specialised in services exports and dependent on imported manufactures will show exactly the UK's pattern of balances.\n• Primary income flows: profits, interest and dividends paid to and received from abroad depend on the stock of foreign assets and liabilities and on relative interest rates. Repatriated profits from inward FDI are a persistent debit.\n• Trade policy at home and abroad: tariffs, quotas and non-tariff barriers change both export volumes and import volumes. The rise in the US average effective tariff rate to 18.2% by August 2025 affects the balances of every economy trading with it.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several determinants developed, including the saving-investment identity; L2 (6-10) determinants explained with limited development; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Explain the main arguments used to justify protectionism.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The infant industry argument: a new industry in a developing economy cannot initially compete with established foreign producers that have already descended their long-run average cost curve. Temporary protection allows it to reach the scale at which it is competitive, after which protection is removed. The difficulty is that "temporary" rarely is, because the protected industry acquires a political constituency.\n• The sunset or adjustment argument: a declining industry protected temporarily allows workers and capital to be reallocated gradually rather than through a sudden regional collapse. The economic case rests on the social costs of rapid structural unemployment, which the market does not price.\n• Anti-dumping: if a foreign producer sells below cost to drive out domestic competitors and then raises prices, the long-run outcome is a monopoly and a net welfare loss. Tariffs to offset dumping are permitted under WTO rules, though the accusation is frequently made where it is not justified.\n• Strategic and national security industries: steel, semiconductors, energy and food are argued to require domestic capacity regardless of cost, because dependence on a potentially hostile supplier is a risk that price does not capture. The pandemic and subsequent geopolitical conflict strengthened this argument considerably.\n• Correcting an unfair playing field: foreign producers may face lower environmental or labour standards, so their lower costs reflect externalities they are not paying for rather than genuine comparative advantage. A border adjustment restores the comparison.\n• Correcting a current account deficit: restricting imports improves the trade balance directly. The counter-argument is that this addresses the symptom rather than the saving-investment imbalance that causes it, and invites retaliation.\n• Employment and the terms of trade: protection preserves jobs in the protected sector, and a large country can improve its terms of trade with an optimum tariff by forcing foreign suppliers to absorb part of it.\n• Revenue: tariffs are administratively simple to collect and matter for governments with limited capacity to levy income taxes.\n• The counter-argument a strong answer supplies: nearly all of these can be met more efficiently by targeted subsidies, regulation or retraining, which achieve the objective without the consumption distortion. And retaliation is the standard response - trade-policy activity ran at nearly double 2024 levels in the first five months of 2026.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several arguments explained with the economic reasoning behind each and the counter-arguments acknowledged; L2 (6-10) arguments listed with some explanation; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Explain the main benefits to a country of joining a customs union.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Definition: a customs union removes tariffs and quotas between members AND adopts a common external tariff against non-members, which distinguishes it from a free trade area such as the CPTPP, where each member keeps its own external tariffs.\n• Trade creation: the removal of internal tariffs shifts consumption from higher-cost domestic producers to lower-cost producers within the union, so production moves towards comparative advantage and real income rises. This is the primary static gain.\n• Economies of scale: access to a much larger tariff-free market allows firms to increase output and descend their long-run average cost curve, lowering unit costs. This is a dynamic gain and can exceed the static one.\n• Increased competition: domestic firms face producers from every member state, which squeezes X-inefficiency, narrows margins and lowers prices for consumers - an allocative and productive efficiency gain.\n• Greater choice and lower prices for consumers: the direct welfare gain from the removal of internal barriers.\n• Inward investment: a common external tariff makes the union an attractive location for multinationals wanting tariff-free access to the whole bloc, so FDI rises, adding to AD and, through technology transfer, to LRAS.\n• No rules of origin within the union: because the external tariff is common, goods do not need to be checked for their origin when crossing internal borders, which removes an administrative cost that a free trade area cannot avoid.\n• Bargaining power: the union negotiates external trade agreements as a single entity, so it has more leverage than any member would alone - significant in a world where the US average effective tariff rate reached 18.2% by August 2025.\n• Costs a strong answer weighs: trade diversion, where the common external tariff shifts purchasing from a genuinely cheaper non-member to a dearer member, reducing welfare; the loss of the freedom to negotiate independent trade deals, which is why membership of a customs union precludes joining another; adjustment costs concentrated in sectors that contract; and the loss of tariff policy as an instrument.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several benefits developed with mechanisms, the customs union/free trade area distinction made, and costs acknowledged; L2 (6-10) benefits listed with some explanation; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Explain the main barriers to economic development facing low-income countries.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• The savings gap and the poverty trap: low incomes mean low saving, so there are few domestic funds to finance investment; low investment means a small capital stock and low productivity, which keeps incomes low. The circle is self-reinforcing and is the central structural problem.\n• Human capital: poor health, low school completion and limited technical training keep output per worker low. Investment in human capital competes with immediate consumption needs, so it is systematically under-provided.\n• Infrastructure deficits: unreliable power, poor transport and limited digital connectivity raise the cost of every transaction and deter both domestic and foreign investment. These are public goods requiring coordination and scale that private firms will not supply.\n• Dependence on primary product exports: commodity prices are volatile and their long-run trend relative to manufactures has often been declining, so the terms of trade deteriorate - meaning a given volume of exports buys progressively fewer imports. Export revenue volatility also makes planning impossible.\n• Institutional weaknesses: insecure property rights, weak contract enforcement, corruption and political instability all raise the risk premium on investment and encourage capital flight. Without an effective state there is no reliable tax base either.\n• Debt burdens: countries that borrowed in foreign currency face servicing costs that rise when their own currency depreciates, and debt service pre-empts spending on health, education and infrastructure.\n• Trade barriers in richer markets: tariff escalation - low tariffs on raw materials and high tariffs on processed goods - discourages moving up the value chain, and agricultural subsidies in developed economies undercut producers who hold the genuine comparative advantage. Rising global protectionism compounds this: the US average effective tariff rate reached 18.2% by August 2025, and WTO-IMF tracking found trade-policy activity at nearly double 2024 levels in early 2026.\n• Demographic and geographic factors: rapid population growth spreads a given capital stock more thinly; landlocked geography raises transport costs; and climate vulnerability destroys capital repeatedly.\n• Capital flight and the brain drain: the most mobile capital and the best-educated workers leave for higher returns elsewhere, removing exactly the resources development requires.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several barriers developed with mechanisms, including the self-reinforcing nature of the poverty trap; L2 (6-10) barriers listed with some explanation; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Explain the main policies a government could use to reduce a current account deficit.`, marks: 15, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Framing: policies divide into expenditure-reducing, which cut total demand and so cut imports, and expenditure-switching, which change relative prices so that spending moves from imports to domestic output. Each has different costs.\n• Expenditure-reducing - contractionary fiscal policy: higher taxation or lower government spending reduces disposable income, so import demand falls through the marginal propensity to import. The cost is lower output and higher unemployment, so the deficit is reduced by making the country poorer.\n• Expenditure-reducing - tighter monetary policy: a higher Bank Rate reduces interest-sensitive consumption and investment and so reduces imports. But it also appreciates the currency by attracting capital inflows, which works against the objective - the two effects pull in opposite directions.\n• Expenditure-switching - depreciation or devaluation: a weaker currency makes exports cheaper abroad and imports dearer. It works only if the Marshall-Lerner condition holds, so the J-curve means the balance worsens for a year or more first, and the depreciation raises imported inflation, which is unwelcome with CPI inflation at 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026.\n• Expenditure-switching - protectionism: tariffs and quotas reduce import volumes directly. The costs are higher prices for consumers, deadweight welfare loss, and retaliation, which reduces exports - a serious risk with trade-policy activity at nearly double 2024 levels in early 2026.\n• Supply-side policy to raise non-price competitiveness: investment in skills, infrastructure, innovation and product quality raises the attractiveness of exports independently of price. This is slow but is the only route that reduces the deficit while raising living standards rather than lowering them.\n• Attracting inward investment: FDI both finances the deficit on the financial account and, if it produces for export, improves the trade balance. The offsetting consideration is that repatriated profits are a debit on the primary income account.\n• Addressing the saving-investment balance: since the current account balance equals national saving less domestic investment, policies that raise domestic saving - pension reform, reducing the fiscal deficit - reduce the external deficit by construction.\n• The prior question a strong answer raises: whether the deficit needs correcting at all. The UK deficit was 1.9% of GDP in Q2 2026, down from 2.4% in Q1, and a deficit financed by long-term investment inflows in a country borrowing in its own currency is far less problematic than one financed by volatile short-term flows.\n\nLevels: L3 (11-15) several policies developed with mechanisms and costs, using the expenditure-reducing/switching framework; L2 (6-10) policies listed with some explanation; L1 (1-5) a list.` },

      { q: `Extract A (lines 25-26) states: "For four decades the share of world output crossing a border rose almost without interruption. Since 2025 that trend has reversed sharply."\n\nUsing the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that globalisation has benefited the UK economy.`, marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - gains from specialisation: trade according to comparative advantage raises the output attainable from a given endowment. The UK specialises in services, where it holds a clear advantage, running a surplus that grew to £142bn in the practice data while importing manufactures it would produce at higher opportunity cost.\n• For - lower prices and wider choice: access to low-cost imports raised real incomes, particularly for lower-income households spending a high share of income on goods, and the entry of China and the former planned economies into the trading system held goods prices down for two decades.\n• For - inward investment and technology: the UK has been among the largest recipients of FDI in Europe, bringing capital, management practice and technology, with spillovers to domestic firms through supply chains and labour mobility.\n• For - competition and productivity: exposure to foreign competition squeezes X-inefficiency and forces innovation. The UK's financial and professional services sectors are globally competitive partly because they compete globally.\n• Against - distributional effects: the gains are aggregate, the losses concentrated. Manufacturing regions lost employment that services growth in London and the South East did not replace, and the adjustment costs fell on workers with the least capacity to bear them. Aggregate gain is compatible with large groups losing permanently.\n• Against - vulnerability: long supply chains optimised for cost proved fragile in 2020-22, and the UK's dependence on imported energy and food transmits world price shocks directly into domestic inflation - transport inflation of 4.6% in the 12 months to August 2026 is the mechanism in action.\n• Against - external imbalance: persistent current account deficits, 1.9% of GDP in Q2 2026, mean the UK relies on continuing capital inflows, and the negative primary income balance reflects returns flowing abroad on foreign-owned UK assets.\n• Against - tax base erosion: multinationals' ability to shift profits between jurisdictions reduces corporation tax yields.\n• The reversal, which the extract dates: the US average effective tariff rate reached 18.2% by August 2025, the highest in about 90 years, and trade-policy activity ran at nearly double 2024 levels in early 2026. Evaluating globalisation now requires judging what happens as it unwinds, and higher barriers raise costs and reduce specialisation for everyone.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer separates the aggregate efficiency gain, which is well supported, from its distribution and from the resilience costs, and concludes on a stated criterion - most defensibly that the UK gained in aggregate while failing to compensate the losers, which is a failure of domestic policy rather than of trade.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, analysis and evaluation integrated, conclusion follows from the argument; L4 (16-20) well-developed and mostly balanced; L3 (11-15) some evaluation, uneven; L2 (6-10) descriptive, one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Extract B (lines 27-28) states: "The UK current account deficit was £15.1bn in Quarter 2 2026, or 1.9% of GDP, down from 2.4% in the previous quarter."\n\nUsing the extracts and your knowledge of economics, assess the view that a persistent current account deficit is a serious problem for the UK economy.`, marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - it must be financed: a current account deficit requires a matching financial account surplus, so the UK depends on continuing willingness of foreigners to buy UK assets. If that willingness falters, sterling must fall or interest rates rise, and the adjustment is not chosen but imposed.\n• For - the composition of the financing matters: if the deficit is financed by short-term portfolio flows rather than long-term FDI, the funding can reverse quickly. With 30-year gilt yields around 5.9% in early September 2026 and public sector net debt at 93.8% of GDP, the UK relies on foreign appetite for gilts at a time when its fiscal position is under scrutiny.\n• For - the structural signal: a persistent deficit indicates that national saving falls short of domestic investment, and in the UK's case it reflects a large goods deficit - £190bn in the practice data - rooted in weak manufacturing competitiveness and low productivity.\n• For - the income account: a growing stock of foreign-owned UK assets generates a growing stream of primary income debits, so today's deficit makes tomorrow's larger, other things equal.\n• Against - the deficit is modest and narrowing: 1.9% of GDP in Q2 2026, down from 2.4%, is far from crisis territory and is within the range the UK has sustained for decades without difficulty.\n• Against - the accounting: the balance of payments as a whole always balances, so a current account deficit is by construction a capital account surplus - inward investment. A deficit reflecting foreigners choosing to invest in a country is not obviously a problem.\n• Against - the UK borrows in its own currency: there is no currency mismatch of the kind that produces emerging market crises, and sterling floats, so adjustment can occur through the exchange rate rather than through reserves running out.\n• Against - what the deficit finances: a deficit that finances investment raising future output is sustainable in a way that one financing consumption is not. The relevant question is the use, not the existence, of the borrowing.\n• Against - the self-correcting mechanism: a floating rate depreciates as the deficit widens, which improves competitiveness - subject to the Marshall-Lerner condition and the J-curve, so the correction is slow and unreliable.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer makes the composition of the financing and the use of the borrowing the decisive criteria rather than the size of the deficit, and concludes that the UK's position is a symptom of weak goods competitiveness and low saving rather than a problem in itself - with the caveat that the reliance on foreign purchases of gilts at a time of fiscal strain is a genuine vulnerability.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Extract C (lines 29-30) states: "The United States average effective tariff rate reached 18.2% by August 2025, the highest in about 90 years."\n\nUsing the extracts and your knowledge of economics, discuss the view that protectionism can never be economically justified.`, marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Against protectionism - the standard welfare analysis: a tariff raises the domestic price, so consumer surplus falls by more than producer surplus and government revenue rise. The two deadweight loss triangles are a pure loss, representing resources drawn into a use where comparative advantage does not lie, and consumption forgone.\n• Against - retaliation and escalation: trade wars shrink markets for everyone. WTO-IMF tracking found trade-policy activity in the first five months of 2026 running at nearly double 2024 levels, so the measures provoked responses rather than remaining isolated.\n• Against - the dynamic cost: protection removes the competitive pressure that drives productivity growth, so the protected industry becomes progressively less competitive - the opposite of the infant industry intention.\n• Against - volatility: the US rate moved from 18.2% in August 2025 to 11.8% by April 2026 and then towards about 7%. Unpredictability of that scale deters investment more than a stable high tariff would, because firms cannot appraise projects at all.\n• For protectionism - the infant industry case: a new industry cannot descend its long-run average cost curve while competing with established foreign producers. Temporary protection is a second-best response to a genuine capital market failure, and several successful industrialisations used it.\n• For - correcting externalities: if foreign producers face weaker environmental or labour standards, their apparent cost advantage reflects costs they do not pay. A border adjustment makes the comparison honest rather than distorting it.\n• For - strategic capacity: semiconductors, energy, food and pharmaceuticals involve a security externality that price does not capture. The pandemic and subsequent conflicts made the cost of dependence explicit, and a country may rationally pay an insurance premium for domestic capacity.\n• For - adjustment costs: rapid liberalisation concentrates losses on regions and workers who cannot relocate, and those social costs are real even though they do not appear in the welfare triangles. Temporary protection to phase the adjustment can raise total welfare if it prevents hysteresis.\n• Evaluation - the second-best objection: almost every legitimate objective above is achieved more efficiently by a targeted instrument - a subsidy, regulation, retraining, or a carbon price - which meets the aim without distorting consumption. The case against protectionism is therefore usually a case for a better instrument, not a denial that the objective is valid.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer distinguishes the validity of the OBJECTIVE from the efficiency of the INSTRUMENT, concluding that protectionism is rarely the best available tool while the objectives it is used to pursue are sometimes legitimate - and tests that conclusion against the strategic and infant industry cases.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Assess the view that a depreciation of sterling would improve the UK current account.`, marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For: a depreciation makes UK exports cheaper in foreign currency and imports dearer in sterling. Export volumes should rise and import volumes fall, improving the trade balance and, through the multiplier, raising output and employment.\n• For - the long-run elasticity evidence: in the practice data, elasticities after two years sum to 1.5, comfortably satisfying the Marshall-Lerner condition, and the trade deficit improves from £38bn before the depreciation to £28bn by Quarter 5.\n• For - the automatic stabiliser argument: under a floating rate, a widening deficit tends to weaken the currency, which is a self-correcting mechanism requiring no policy action.\n• Against - the short-run elasticities: after one quarter they sum to only 0.7, so the condition fails and the balance WORSENS. The practice data show the deficit widening from £38bn to £46bn over two quarters - the J-curve. Any government seeking a correction within an electoral horizon would be disappointed.\n• Against - the composition of UK imports: energy, food and intermediate components are price-inelastic with no immediate domestic substitute, so import volumes respond little while the sterling cost rises immediately.\n• Against - inflation: a depreciation raises import prices directly. With CPI inflation at 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026, above the 2% target and rising, additional imported inflation would be unwelcome and could force the MPC to raise Bank Rate from 3.75%, contracting demand.\n• Against - the competitiveness gain erodes: if higher import costs feed into wages and domestic prices, the real exchange rate returns to where it started and the gain is lost. This is why depreciation cannot substitute for productivity.\n• Against - supply capacity: exporters can only sell more if they can produce more. With UK unemployment at 4.9% in May to July 2026 and an economy near trend, spare capacity is limited, so the volume response may be constrained regardless of price.\n• Against - the current account is more than trade: primary and secondary income are components too, and they respond to asset stocks and interest differentials rather than to competitiveness. A depreciation raises the sterling value of foreign-currency income both ways.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer concludes conditionally on the elasticities and the time horizon - the depreciation worsens the balance for around a year and improves it thereafter, provided domestic costs do not rise to match - and identifies non-price competitiveness as the durable alternative.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Evaluate the view that free trade agreements such as the CPTPP significantly benefit the UK economy.`, marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - market access: the UK joined the Comprehensive and Progressive Agreement for Trans-Pacific Partnership on 15 December 2024, and it was in force between the UK and all eleven other members by 1 September 2026. The bloc includes several of the fastest-growing economies, so the agreement is oriented towards where future demand growth is expected.\n• For - services and investment provisions: modern agreements cover services, digital trade, investment protection and public procurement, not just goods tariffs. That matters disproportionately for the UK, whose comparative advantage is in services - a surplus of £142bn against a goods deficit of £190bn in the practice data.\n• For - diversification and resilience: with the US average effective tariff rate reaching 18.2% by August 2025 and trade-policy activity at nearly double 2024 levels in early 2026, having multiple trading relationships reduces exposure to any single partner's policy.\n• For - dynamic gains: access to larger markets supports economies of scale, and exposure to competition raises productivity - the channel most relevant to the UK's central economic problem.\n• Against - gravity: trade falls sharply with distance. The CPTPP members are on the other side of the world, and official estimates of the long-run GDP gain from accession have been very small - a fraction of one per cent - because the UK already had bilateral agreements with most members.\n• Against - tariffs were already low: on most goods traded with these partners the pre-existing tariffs were modest, so the marginal liberalisation is limited. The binding barriers on services are regulatory, and those are much harder to remove by treaty.\n• Against - trade diversion: preferential agreements shift purchasing towards members and away from genuinely lower-cost non-members, so part of any increase in trade is diversion rather than creation, which reduces welfare.\n• Against - regulatory and standards concerns: joining requires accepting rules on investor protection, procurement and standards, which constrains domestic policy, and agricultural market opening is contested on animal welfare and environmental grounds.\n• Against - the counterfactual: the relevant comparison is with the closer, larger markets that account for the bulk of UK trade. An agreement that adds a fraction of a per cent to GDP does not offset changes in access to a much larger nearby market.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer weighs the modest quantified gains against the strategic and diversification arguments, concluding on a stated criterion - most defensibly that the direct trade effect is small, that the services and digital provisions are where the value lies, and that the benefit is better understood as optionality in a fragmenting trading system than as a measurable boost to GDP.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Discuss the view that the theory of comparative advantage remains a valid guide to trade policy.`, marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - the core insight is robust: mutual gains arise whenever opportunity cost ratios differ, regardless of absolute productivity. In the practice data, Country A is absolutely more productive in both goods yet both countries gain by specialising, because A's opportunity cost of cloth is 3.00 tonnes of wheat against B's 2.00.\n• For - it explains observed patterns: the UK's services surplus and goods deficit are precisely what the theory predicts for an economy with a comparative advantage in skill-intensive services.\n• For - the policy implication is clear and has been vindicated: the post-war liberalisation that the theory justified coincided with the largest sustained rise in world living standards in history.\n• Against - the assumptions are unrealistic: constant opportunity costs, perfect factor mobility within countries, no transport costs, no trade barriers, and full employment. Relaxing factor mobility in particular changes the policy conclusion, because workers displaced from a contracting sector do not costlessly move to the expanding one.\n• Against - distribution: the theory establishes that the COUNTRY gains, not that everyone within it does. Adjustment costs are concentrated on identifiable regions and workers, which is both a welfare problem and the political reason liberalisation is resisted.\n• Against - most trade is not between dissimilar countries: intra-industry trade between similar advanced economies is a large share of world trade, and it is driven by economies of scale, product differentiation and consumer preference for variety - explanations the Ricardian model does not contain.\n• Against - comparative advantage is not fixed: it is created by investment in skills, technology and infrastructure, so a country can choose its future advantage. This is the infant industry argument, and it means that specialising according to today's advantage may lock a country into low-value production.\n• Against - the theory omits strategic and security considerations, environmental externalities, and the risk of supply chain disruption, all of which have risen in salience. A policy of pure least-cost sourcing proved fragile in 2020-22.\n• Evaluation - what a theory is for: comparative advantage establishes a presumption in favour of trade and identifies the burden of proof that protection must meet. It was never intended to determine the optimal tariff on steel. Judged as a framework it remains valid; judged as a complete guide to policy it was always insufficient.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer distinguishes the validity of the core proposition from the adequacy of the model's assumptions and from the questions it does not address, and concludes that the theory remains the right starting point while the distributional, strategic and dynamic considerations determine what policy should actually do.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Assess the view that a floating exchange rate system is preferable to a fixed exchange rate system for the UK.`, marks: 25, command: 'Assess',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For floating - monetary policy independence: the impossible trinity says a country cannot have a fixed rate, free capital movement and independent monetary policy simultaneously. A floating rate preserves the Bank of England's ability to set Bank Rate for domestic conditions - 3.75% in September 2026, with CPI inflation at 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026 requiring domestic restraint.\n• For floating - automatic adjustment: a deficit tends to weaken the currency, improving competitiveness without any policy action. The UK deficit of 1.9% of GDP in Q2 2026 adjusts partly through the exchange rate rather than through deflation of domestic demand.\n• For floating - no reserves required: defending a peg requires reserves that are trivial relative to daily foreign exchange turnover. The UK's exit from the Exchange Rate Mechanism on 16 September 1992 demonstrated that a determined speculative attack cannot be resisted.\n• For floating - shock absorption: a floating rate allows the economy to absorb an external shock through the currency rather than through output and employment.\n• For fixing - certainty for trade and investment: exporters and importers can plan without hedging costs, which supports trade and long-term investment. Sterling moving from about 1.18 euros in July 2026 to about 1.16 in September, and a practice sterling-dollar depreciation of 3.24% over a year, illustrate the uncertainty firms face.\n• For fixing - imported credibility and inflation discipline: pegging to a low-inflation anchor removes the option of inflating, which can break an entrenched inflationary expectation.\n• For fixing - it removes destabilising speculation: floating rates are driven overwhelmingly by speculative capital flows rather than trade fundamentals, and those flows can push a currency far from any level justified by the real economy.\n• Against fixing - the adjustment burden falls on the real economy: with the nominal rate fixed, an uncompetitive economy must restore competitiveness through lower wages and prices, which means recession and unemployment. This is the euro area periphery's experience after 2010.\n• Against fixing - choosing the right parity: a peg set too high destroys competitiveness and one set too low imports inflation, and the correct rate changes over time.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer makes the impossible trinity the organising framework and concludes for an economy of the UK's type - large, open to capital, with its own inflation problem and a services-based comparative advantage - that monetary independence is worth more than exchange rate certainty, while acknowledging that a small economy heavily dependent on one trading partner might rationally choose otherwise.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Evaluate the view that investment by multinational corporations benefits developing countries.`, marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - it closes the savings gap: low-income countries cannot finance investment domestically because low incomes generate little saving. FDI supplies capital without creating external debt, since the returns are contingent on the project succeeding rather than being fixed interest obligations.\n• For - technology and management transfer: multinationals bring production techniques and management practices that domestic firms can imitate. Where the spillovers occur through supply chains and labour turnover, they raise the productivity of the whole economy - a positive externality the investing firm cannot appropriate and therefore under-supplies.\n• For - employment, human capital and the tax base: direct and indirect employment, on-the-job training that raises transferable skills, and corporation tax plus income tax receipts that fund public services.\n• For - export capacity and infrastructure: production for export earns foreign exchange and improves the current account, and investment in supporting infrastructure benefits other users.\n• Against - profit repatriation: returns flow to the parent company as a debit on the primary income account, so over the life of the investment the current account effect can turn negative. The initial inflow is a one-off; the outflow is permanent.\n• Against - transfer pricing and tax avoidance: multinationals can shift profits to low-tax jurisdictions, so the corporation tax yield is far below what the activity would suggest, and developing countries have the least administrative capacity to prevent it.\n• Against - enclave economies: if the plant imports its inputs and exports its output, the local multiplier is small and the linkages to the domestic economy minimal, so the benefits are confined to wages.\n• Against - bargaining asymmetry and the race to the bottom: countries compete for investment by offering tax holidays, subsidies and weaker labour or environmental standards, so a substantial part of the gain is competed away before it arrives.\n• Against - market power and displacement: a large multinational can drive out domestic competitors, so employment is displaced rather than created, and dependence on a single footloose employer leaves a region exposed when the firm relocates.\n• Against - resource extraction: investment concentrated in primary products entrenches dependence on volatile commodity exports and a deteriorating long-run terms of trade.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer argues that the benefit is contingent rather than automatic, and specifies the conditions - local sourcing and linkages, enforceable tax and labour standards, and domestic absorptive capacity to capture the spillovers - concluding that the host country's institutions determine whether FDI develops the economy or merely operates within it.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Discuss the view that the World Trade Organization is no longer an effective institution.`, marks: 25, command: 'Discuss',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For ineffectiveness - rules are being broken openly: the sharp rise in US tariffs since 2025, taking the average effective rate to 18.2% by August 2025, is difficult to reconcile with the most-favoured-nation principle at the heart of the system, and the measures were imposed without WTO authorisation.\n• For - limited enforcement: the WTO has no power to compel compliance. Its only sanction is to authorise retaliation by the injured party, which is useless for a small economy against a large one and escalatory between large ones.\n• For - the dispute settlement problem: the Appellate Body has been unable to function since appointments to it were blocked, so rulings can be appealed "into the void" and never take effect. A rules-based system without a functioning court is a system of voluntary compliance.\n• For - negotiating paralysis: the Doha Round was never concluded, and consensus among a membership of well over 150 states with sharply divergent interests has proved unattainable. The action has moved to regional and bilateral agreements - the UK joined the CPTPP on 15 December 2024 - which fragments rather than unifies the system.\n• For - the volume of new measures: WTO-IMF tracking found trade-policy activity in the first five months of 2026 running at nearly double 2024 levels, which is evidence that restraint has broken down.\n• Against - the system persists: the great majority of world trade still moves under WTO tariff schedules and rules, and most members continue to comply. Bound tariff commitments and the transparency obligations continue to constrain behaviour that would otherwise be worse.\n• Against - retaliation has been limited: even where the new tariffs violate MFN, the expected cascade of retaliation has been smaller than feared, which suggests the norms retain some force.\n• Against - the monitoring function: the WTO's trade policy reviews and its joint tracking with the IMF supply the information on which any assessment, including this one, depends. An institution that makes policy visible constrains it.\n• Against - the counterfactual: the alternative to an imperfect rules-based system is bilateral bargaining in which power decides, which is worse for every small and medium-sized economy - including the UK, whose trade is dominated by partners larger than itself.\n• Evaluation - what "effective" means: judged against its founding ambition of progressive liberalisation through negotiated rounds, the WTO has failed. Judged as a floor that prevents a return to 1930s conditions, it is still functioning.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer is explicit about the standard it applies and concludes accordingly - most defensibly that the negotiating and adjudication functions have broken down while the rule-setting and monitoring functions still constrain behaviour, so reform rather than replacement is the implication.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, integrated; L4 (16-20) well-developed; L3 (11-15) some evaluation; L2 (6-10) one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: `Evaluate the view that trade liberalisation is the most effective route to development for low-income countries.`, marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For - comparative advantage and market size: specialising according to comparative advantage raises real income from unchanged resources, and access to world markets removes the constraint of a small, poor domestic market. A low-income country with abundant labour can specialise in labour-intensive production and grow far faster than autarky would allow.\n• For - technology, capital and competition: openness brings FDI, imported capital equipment and exposure to international best practice, and competition disciplines domestic firms. Several of the fastest sustained growth episodes in history were export-led.\n• For - it costs nothing fiscally: removing one's own trade barriers requires no public spending, which matters for a government with a narrow tax base and heavy debt service obligations.\n• Against - the barriers to development are largely domestic: the savings gap, weak human capital, absent infrastructure, insecure property rights and corruption are not removed by opening to trade. A country cannot export if it has no reliable power supply or port.\n• Against - the infant industry argument: exposing a nascent industry to established foreign competitors prevents it ever reaching the scale at which it is viable. Several successful industrialisations used substantial protection during the transition, then liberalised.\n• Against - the terms of trade: specialising in primary products exposes a country to volatile prices and a long-run relative decline against manufactures. Tariff escalation in rich countries - low tariffs on raw materials and high ones on processed goods - actively discourages moving up the value chain.\n• Against - the system is not symmetric: liberalisation by low-income countries is not matched by access to rich markets. The US average effective tariff rate reached 18.2% by August 2025, and trade-policy activity ran at nearly double 2024 levels in early 2026. Agricultural subsidies in developed economies undercut producers who hold the genuine advantage.\n• Against - adjustment and distribution: rapid liberalisation destroys existing employment before new activity replaces it, and countries with weak social protection cannot cushion the transition, so the poorest can lose absolutely.\n• Against - sequencing: the evidence suggests that liberalisation delivers where complementary conditions exist - infrastructure, education, macroeconomic stability, institutional quality. Opening first and building capacity later has a poor record.\n\nJudgement: a Level 5 answer argues that trade liberalisation is a necessary but not sufficient condition, and that sequencing and complementary domestic investment determine whether it delivers - concluding explicitly against the "most effective route" framing by identifying which constraint binds first.\n\nLevels: L5 (21-25) sustained, balanced, analysis and evaluation integrated, conclusion follows from the argument; L4 (16-20) well-developed and mostly balanced; L3 (11-15) some evaluation, uneven balance; L2 (6-10) descriptive, one-sided; L1 (1-5) fragmentary. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },
    ]
  }
};

if (typeof module !== 'undefined' && module.exports) {
  module.exports = { ECONOMICS_AQA_PRACTICE };
}
